create temp table if not exists rationale_report(day int primary key, questions_updated int, rows_changed int);

-- ===================== DAY 1 =====================
-- Nursing Exam Room: full rationales for CCN Mock Exam — Day 1 (200 questions)
-- Run this once in the Supabase SQL Editor. It is safe to run again.
--
-- Each explanation now says: the correct answer and why, why each other option is wrong,
-- and a key point to remember. The same questions in the practice app get the new explanation too.
-- Questions are found by their exact wording and correct answer. Where the old answer key was wrong,
-- the correct option is also fixed (listed at the top of the file).


with r(n, q, answer, exp, fixed_answer) as (values
(1, $x$The source of pulmonary surfactant is:$x$, $x$Great alveolar (type II) cells$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Great alveolar (type II) cells
Surfactant is a mixture of phospholipids (mainly dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine) and proteins made by the type II alveolar cells, also called great alveolar cells or type II pneumocytes. It lines the inside of each alveolus and lowers surface tension, so the small alveoli do not collapse at the end of expiration and the lungs are easier to inflate (better compliance). Type I cells, by contrast, are thin, flat cells that form the gas-exchange surface.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The visceral pleura — The visceral pleura is the membrane covering the outside of the lung; it makes pleural fluid for smooth gliding, not surfactant.
• Tracheal glands — Tracheal glands make mucus that traps dust and germs in the airways; they do not make surfactant.
• Alveolar capillaries — Alveolar capillaries carry blood for gas exchange; they do not secrete anything into the alveoli.

⭐ Key point: Type II pneumocytes make surfactant. Preterm babies (before about 34 weeks) lack it, causing respiratory distress syndrome; antenatal steroids speed up its production.$x$, null),
(2, $x$Normal arterial partial pressure of oxygen (PaO2) in a healthy adult breathing room air is approximately:$x$, $x$80-100 mmHg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 80-100 mmHg
In a healthy adult breathing room air (FiO₂ 21%) at sea level, the normal PaO₂ is 80–100 mmHg (10.7–13.3 kPa). It falls slightly with age. PaO₂ is the pressure of oxygen dissolved in arterial blood, and it drives oxygen onto haemoglobin. Below 80 mmHg is hypoxaemia; below 60 mmHg (8 kPa) is respiratory failure, because the oxygen–haemoglobin curve becomes steep and saturation falls quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 40-45 mmHg — 40–45 mmHg is close to the normal VENOUS PO₂ (about 40 mmHg), not arterial.
• 60-65 mmHg — 60–65 mmHg is hypoxaemia; 60 mmHg is the cut-off for respiratory failure.
• 120-140 mmHg — 120–140 mmHg is higher than normal and is only seen when breathing extra oxygen.

⭐ Key point: Normal PaO₂ 80–100 mmHg. PaO₂ below 60 mmHg = respiratory failure (type 1 if PaCO₂ is normal or low; type 2 if PaCO₂ is above 45 mmHg).$x$, null),
(3, $x$In a healthy person, the primary physiological stimulus that drives minute-to-minute ventilation is:$x$, $x$A rise in arterial carbon dioxide tension (PaCO2)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A rise in arterial carbon dioxide tension (PaCO2)
Breathing is controlled mainly by central chemoreceptors in the medulla, which respond to CO₂. CO₂ crosses into the cerebrospinal fluid, forms carbonic acid and lowers the pH there. Even a small rise in PaCO₂ quickly increases the rate and depth of breathing to blow off the extra CO₂. This is the main minute-to-minute drive in a healthy person.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A rise in arterial oxygen tension (PaO2) — A rise in PaO₂ would reduce, not drive, breathing. Low oxygen (below about 60 mmHg) is only a back-up drive, sensed by the carotid and aortic bodies.
• A fall in blood glucose — Blood glucose does not directly control breathing.
• A rise in serum calcium — Serum calcium affects nerves and muscles but is not a breathing stimulus.

⭐ Key point: CO₂ is the main drive to breathe. In chronic CO₂ retainers (some COPD patients), the hypoxic drive matters more, so give controlled oxygen with a target SpO₂ of 88–92%.$x$, null),
(4, $x$Which of the following causes a leftward shift of the oxyhaemoglobin dissociation curve (increased affinity for oxygen)?$x$, $x$Decreased temperature, decreased 2,3-DPG, and alkalosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Decreased temperature, decreased 2,3-DPG, and alkalosis
A LEFT shift means haemoglobin holds oxygen more tightly (higher affinity), so it picks up O₂ easily in the lungs but releases less to the tissues. Causes of a left shift: LOW temperature, LOW 2,3-DPG, ALKALOSIS (high pH), LOW CO₂, and also carbon monoxide and fetal haemoglobin. This option lists three left-shift causes together, so it is correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increased temperature — Increased temperature shifts the curve to the RIGHT (hot, working tissues need more oxygen released).
• Decreased pH (acidosis) — Acidosis (low pH) shifts the curve to the RIGHT (the Bohr effect).
• Increased 2,3-DPG — Increased 2,3-DPG shifts the curve to the RIGHT (as in anaemia and high altitude).

⭐ Key point: Remember "CADET, face Right": CO₂, Acid, 2,3-DPG, Exercise and Temperature all shift the curve to the RIGHT (more O₂ released). The opposite of each shifts it LEFT.$x$, null),
(5, $x$Which patient can safely receive AB-negative blood?$x$, $x$AB-negative$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: AB-negative
AB-negative red cells carry the A antigen, the B antigen, and no D (Rh) antigen. They can only be given safely to someone whose plasma has NO anti-A and NO anti-B antibodies, which means a group AB recipient. Because the cells are Rh-negative, they can go to an AB-negative (or AB-positive) patient. Among the options, only the AB-negative patient can safely receive them.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• O-negative — An O-negative patient has both anti-A and anti-B, which would destroy AB cells (a haemolytic reaction).
• B-negative — A B-negative patient has anti-A, which would attack the A antigen on AB cells.
• A-negative — An A-negative patient has anti-B, which would attack the B antigen on AB cells.

⭐ Key point: Red cells: O-negative is the universal donor and AB-positive is the universal recipient. Plasma is the reverse: AB plasma is the universal plasma donor.$x$, null),
(6, $x$The average lifespan of a red blood cell is approximately:$x$, $x$120 days$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 120 days
Red blood cells live for about 120 days. They have no nucleus, so they cannot repair themselves. As they age, they become stiff and are removed by macrophages in the spleen (and the liver). The haem is turned into bilirubin, and the iron is recycled to make new red cells in the bone marrow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10 days — 10 days is about the lifespan of platelets (7–10 days).
• 40 days — 40 days is too short for a red cell.
• 300 days — 300 days is far too long; no blood cell lives this long in the circulation.

⭐ Key point: Red cells live 120 days, platelets 7–10 days, and neutrophils only hours to days. Stored red cells for transfusion last 35–42 days in the fridge.$x$, null),
(7, $x$Haemophilia A results from a deficiency of which clotting factor?$x$, $x$Factor VIII$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Factor VIII
Haemophilia A is an X-linked inherited bleeding disorder caused by a lack of clotting factor VIII. It mostly affects males; females are usually carriers. Patients bleed into joints (haemarthrosis) and muscles. The blood test shows a prolonged APTT with a normal PT and platelet count. Treatment is factor VIII concentrate (or desmopressin in mild cases).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Factor IX — Factor IX deficiency is haemophilia B (Christmas disease).
• Factor XI — Factor XI deficiency is haemophilia C, a milder form.
• Factor XIII — Factor XIII deficiency is a rare disorder of clot stabilisation, not haemophilia A.

⭐ Key point: Haemophilia A = factor VIII; B = factor IX. Avoid IM injections, aspirin and NSAIDs, and give the factor BEFORE procedures.$x$, null),
(8, $x$The AV node's primary function in the cardiac conduction system is to:$x$, $x$Briefly delay the electrical impulse, allowing the atria to fully contract before ventricular contraction$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Briefly delay the electrical impulse, allowing the atria to fully contract before ventricular contraction
The AV (atrioventricular) node sits between the atria and ventricles. Its main job is to DELAY the impulse for about 0.1 second. This gives the atria time to finish contracting and fill the ventricles before the ventricles contract. The delay appears on the ECG as the PR interval (normal 0.12–0.20 s). The AV node also protects the ventricles from very fast atrial rates, as in AF.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Generate the primary pacemaker impulse — Generating the primary pacemaker impulse is the job of the SA node (60–100/min).
• Directly cause ventricular contraction without delay — The impulse must be delayed, not passed straight to the ventricles; the bundle of His and Purkinje fibres conduct it rapidly afterwards.
• Regulate coronary blood flow — Coronary blood flow is controlled by the coronary arteries and local metabolites, not the AV node.

⭐ Key point: SA node = pacemaker; AV node = delay (PR interval). If the SA node fails, the AV junction can take over at 40–60/min.$x$, null),
(9, $x$Which nephron segment is primarily responsible for the countercurrent multiplier system that concentrates urine?$x$, $x$Loop of Henle$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Loop of Henle
The loop of Henle (in the medulla) is the countercurrent multiplier. The descending limb lets water out; the thick ascending limb pumps sodium and chloride out but is impermeable to water. This builds a very salty (hyperosmolar) medulla. Then, under ADH, water leaves the collecting ducts into the salty medulla and the urine becomes concentrated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bowman's capsule — Bowman's capsule collects the filtrate from the glomerulus; it does not concentrate urine.
• Proximal convoluted tubule — The proximal convoluted tubule reabsorbs about 65% of filtered sodium and water and all glucose, but the fluid stays isotonic.
• Renal pelvis — The renal pelvis is a collecting funnel that carries urine to the ureter.

⭐ Key point: Loop of Henle = concentrates urine. Loop diuretics (furosemide) block the Na-K-2Cl pump in the thick ascending limb, which is why they are so powerful.$x$, null),
(10, $x$Antidiuretic hormone (ADH, vasopressin) is synthesized in the hypothalamus and released into the circulation from the:$x$, $x$Posterior pituitary gland$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Posterior pituitary gland
ADH (vasopressin) is MADE in the hypothalamus (supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei). It travels down nerve fibres to the POSTERIOR pituitary, where it is stored and released into the blood. ADH makes the collecting ducts keep water, so it raises blood volume and concentrates the urine. Oxytocin is handled the same way.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anterior pituitary gland — The anterior pituitary makes its own hormones (GH, TSH, ACTH, FSH, LH, prolactin), not ADH.
• Adrenal medulla — The adrenal medulla releases adrenaline and noradrenaline.
• Thyroid gland — The thyroid gland releases T3, T4 and calcitonin.

⭐ Key point: Too little ADH = diabetes insipidus (large volumes of dilute urine). Too much ADH = SIADH (low sodium, concentrated urine). Both are common after head injury and brain surgery.$x$, null),
(11, $x$The normal glomerular filtration rate (GFR) in a healthy adult is approximately:$x$, $x$120-125 mL/min$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 120-125 mL/min
Normal GFR is about 120–125 mL/min (about 180 L/day) in a healthy young adult. Almost all of this filtrate (about 99%) is reabsorbed, leaving 1–2 L of urine a day. GFR is the best overall measure of kidney function; it is estimated (eGFR) from serum creatinine, age and sex.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 20-30 mL/min — 20–30 mL/min is severely reduced (stage 4 CKD).
• 500 mL/min — 500 mL/min is about the renal plasma flow, not the GFR.
• 1000 mL/min — 1000 mL/min is close to total renal BLOOD flow (about 20% of cardiac output), not the filtration rate.

⭐ Key point: GFR normal ~120 mL/min. Stage 5 CKD (kidney failure) is GFR below 15. Many drugs (vancomycin, gentamicin, enoxaparin) need dose changes when GFR is low.$x$, null),
(12, $x$Cerebrospinal fluid circulates within the$x$, $x$subarachnoid space$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: subarachnoid space
CSF flows in the SUBARACHNOID space, between the arachnoid mater and the pia mater, and in the ventricles and central canal. It is made mainly by the choroid plexus (about 500 mL/day; about 150 mL present at a time) and absorbed by the arachnoid villi into the venous sinuses. It cushions the brain and removes waste. A lumbar puncture samples CSF from this space below L1–L2.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• epidural space — The epidural space lies outside the dura and contains fat and veins; epidural anaesthesia goes here, not CSF.
• subdural space — The subdural space lies between the dura and arachnoid; a subdural haematoma collects here.
• potential space between periosteum and dura — The space between the periosteum and dura is the epidural space in the skull, where an extradural haematoma forms.

⭐ Key point: Subarachnoid = CSF. A subarachnoid haemorrhage puts blood into the CSF, giving a thunderclap headache and neck stiffness.$x$, null),
(13, $x$A patient presents with ptosis, a pupil that is fixed and dilated, and the eye deviated "down and out." Which cranial nerve palsy best explains this presentation?$x$, $x$Cranial nerve III (oculomotor)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Cranial nerve III (oculomotor)
The oculomotor nerve (CN III) moves most eye muscles, lifts the eyelid (levator palpebrae) and carries parasympathetic fibres that constrict the pupil. When it is paralysed, the unopposed lateral rectus (CN VI) and superior oblique (CN IV) pull the eye "down and out". The lid droops (ptosis), and the pupil is fixed and dilated. In head injury, a new dilated pupil means the uncus is herniating and pressing on CN III.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cranial nerve IV (trochlear) — A CN IV (trochlear) palsy causes vertical double vision, especially going downstairs, without ptosis or a dilated pupil.
• Cranial nerve VI (abducens) — A CN VI (abducens) palsy stops the eye moving outwards; the eye turns in.
• Cranial nerve VII (facial) — A CN VII (facial) palsy causes facial weakness and inability to close the eye, not ptosis with a dilated pupil.

⭐ Key point: A fixed dilated pupil on one side after head injury is a NEUROSURGICAL EMERGENCY (raised ICP and herniation). Report it at once.$x$, null),
(14, $x$Beta cells of the pancreatic islets of Langerhans secrete:$x$, $x$Insulin$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Insulin
In the islets of Langerhans, BETA cells make insulin, which lowers blood glucose by moving it into cells and storing it as glycogen and fat. Beta cells are the most common islet cells (about 70%). They are destroyed in type 1 diabetes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Glucagon — Glucagon is made by ALPHA cells; it raises blood glucose.
• Somatostatin — Somatostatin is made by DELTA cells; it inhibits insulin and glucagon release.
• Pancreatic polypeptide exclusively — Pancreatic polypeptide is made by PP (F) cells, not beta cells.

⭐ Key point: Beta = insulin (lowers glucose). Alpha = glucagon (raises glucose). Glucagon 1 mg IM is used for severe hypoglycaemia when there is no IV access.$x$, null),
(15, $x$Intrinsic factor, secreted by gastric parietal cells, is essential for absorption of which nutrient?$x$, $x$Vitamin B12, absorbed in the terminal ileum$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Vitamin B12, absorbed in the terminal ileum
Gastric parietal cells make intrinsic factor (as well as hydrochloric acid). Intrinsic factor binds vitamin B12, and the complex is absorbed in the TERMINAL ILEUM. Without intrinsic factor (pernicious anaemia, gastrectomy) or without the terminal ileum (Crohn's disease, resection), B12 is not absorbed. The result is macrocytic anaemia and nerve damage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Iron, absorbed in the duodenum — Iron is absorbed mainly in the duodenum, helped by stomach acid and vitamin C, but it does not need intrinsic factor.
• Calcium, absorbed in the jejunum — Calcium is absorbed in the duodenum and jejunum with the help of vitamin D, not intrinsic factor.
• Vitamin K, absorbed in the colon — Vitamin K is partly made by gut bacteria and absorbed with fats; it does not need intrinsic factor.

⭐ Key point: No intrinsic factor = no B12 = pernicious anaemia, which is treated with lifelong IM vitamin B12 (hydroxocobalamin) injections.$x$, null),
(16, $x$What is the normal range for HCO3-?$x$, $x$22-26 mEq/L$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 22-26 mEq/L
Normal serum bicarbonate (HCO₃⁻) is 22–26 mEq/L (mmol/L). Bicarbonate is the main base (alkali) buffer in the blood, and the kidneys control it. Below 22 = metabolic acidosis (or compensation for respiratory alkalosis). Above 26 = metabolic alkalosis (or compensation for respiratory acidosis).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 18-22 mEq/L — 18–22 is low (acidosis range).
• 26-30 mEq/L — 26–30 is high (alkalosis or compensation).
• 30-34 mEq/L — 30–34 is clearly raised, as in vomiting or chronic CO₂ retention with compensation.

⭐ Key point: ABG normals: pH 7.35–7.45, PaCO₂ 35–45 mmHg, HCO₃⁻ 22–26 mmol/L. Remember "the lungs control CO₂ (acid), the kidneys control HCO₃⁻ (base)".$x$, null),
(17, $x$An ABG result of pH 7.51, PaCO2 35 mmHg, and HCO3- 30 mEq/L indicates:$x$, $x$Metabolic alkalosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Metabolic alkalosis
Step 1: pH 7.51 is above 7.45, so the patient has alkalosis. Step 2: PaCO₂ 35 is normal, so the lungs are not the cause. Step 3: HCO₃⁻ 30 is above 26, so there is too much base. The bicarbonate moves in the same direction as the pH (both up), which means a METABOLIC cause: uncompensated metabolic alkalosis. ROME: Metabolic = Equal (pH and HCO₃⁻ move the same way).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would need a LOW pH and a LOW HCO₃⁻.
• Respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would need a high pH with a LOW PaCO₂ (below 35, from hyperventilation).
• Respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis would need a LOW pH with a HIGH PaCO₂.

⭐ Key point: Causes of metabolic alkalosis: vomiting or NG suction (loss of stomach acid), diuretics, low potassium and too much bicarbonate. Treat the cause and replace chloride and potassium.$x$, null),
(18, $x$In a patient with respiratory failure, the nurse correctly interprets a high PaCO₂ value and a low pH from an ABG. This indicates:$x$, $x$Respiratory acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Respiratory acidosis
A LOW pH means acidosis. A HIGH PaCO₂ (above 45 mmHg) means CO₂, the respiratory acid, is building up. pH and PaCO₂ move in OPPOSITE directions, so the cause is respiratory. Together they mean respiratory acidosis, which is the typical result of hypoventilation in respiratory failure (type 2): COPD, opioid overdose, exhaustion, neuromuscular weakness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis would show a HIGH pH and a HIGH HCO₃⁻.
• Respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would show a HIGH pH and a LOW PaCO₂ (hyperventilation).
• Metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would show a low pH but a LOW HCO₃⁻ as the main change, with a normal or low PaCO₂.

⭐ Key point: Respiratory acidosis is treated by improving ventilation: open the airway, reverse sedatives (naloxone), use NIV (BiPAP), or intubate and ventilate. The kidneys raise HCO₃⁻ to compensate over 2–5 days.$x$, null),
(19, $x$What is a cause of metabolic alkalosis?$x$, $x$Vomiting$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Vomiting
Vomiting (and NG suction) removes hydrochloric acid (HCl) from the stomach. Losing acid leaves extra bicarbonate behind, which raises the pH. Loss of fluid and chloride makes the kidneys hold on to more bicarbonate, and potassium is lost too. The result is a hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis, as in pyloric stenosis in babies.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diarrhea — Diarrhoea causes metabolic ACIDOSIS, because bowel fluid is rich in bicarbonate, which is lost.
• Renal failure — Renal failure causes metabolic ACIDOSIS, because the kidneys cannot excrete acid or make bicarbonate.
• Hypoventilation — Hypoventilation causes RESPIRATORY acidosis (CO₂ builds up).

⭐ Key point: Above the stomach (vomiting) = alkalosis; below the stomach (diarrhoea) = acidosis. Treat with 0.9% saline and potassium.$x$, null),
(20, $x$The modified Allen's test is performed before arterial blood gas sampling primarily to:$x$, $x$Assess collateral circulation to the hand via the ulnar artery before radial artery puncture$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Assess collateral circulation to the hand via the ulnar artery before radial artery puncture
The radial artery is the usual ABG site. Before puncturing it, the modified Allen test checks that the ULNAR artery can supply the hand on its own if the radial artery is damaged or clotted. Method: press both arteries, ask the patient to clench the fist until the hand blanches, then release only the ulnar artery. Colour should return within 5–15 seconds. If it doesn't, choose another site.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Measure blood pressure — Blood pressure is measured with a cuff or arterial line, not the Allen test.
• Assess for infection at the puncture site — Infection at the site is checked by looking for redness, swelling and pus.
• Determine the patient's pain tolerance — Pain tolerance is not what the Allen test measures.

⭐ Key point: A negative (abnormal) Allen test means don't use that radial artery. After an ABG, press firmly for at least 5 minutes (longer if the patient is on anticoagulants).$x$, null),
(21, $x$A patient develops fever with chills and rigors shortly after a blood transfusion is started post-operatively. What is the priority nursing action?$x$, $x$Stop the transfusion immediately, keep the IV line open with normal saline, notify the physician, and send the blood/tubing for investigation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Stop the transfusion immediately, keep the IV line open with normal saline, notify the physician, and send the blood/tubing for investigation
Fever, chills and rigors soon after starting blood may be the first sign of a serious reaction: an acute haemolytic reaction (wrong blood), bacterial contamination, or a febrile reaction. The nurse cannot tell which at the bedside, so the SAFEST first action is to STOP the transfusion at once. Keep the vein open with a new giving set and 0.9% saline, check vital signs, inform the doctor, recheck the patient ID against the bag, and return the bag and tubing with blood and urine samples to the lab.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No action is needed if the temperature is below 38.5 degrees C — Waiting for the temperature to reach a certain level could let a fatal haemolytic reaction continue.
• Increase the transfusion rate to finish it quickly — Speeding up the transfusion gives MORE of the possibly harmful blood.
• Give an antipyretic and continue the transfusion at the same rate — An antipyretic can hide the fever, and continuing the transfusion is unsafe until the cause is known.

⭐ Key point: Any reaction means STOP the blood, keep the line open with saline, call the doctor, check the ID, and send the bag back. Most acute haemolytic reactions are caused by giving blood to the wrong patient.$x$, null),
(22, $x$Massive blood transfusion is defined as:$x$, $x$Replacement of the patient's total blood volume within 24 hours$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Replacement of the patient's total blood volume within 24 hours
Massive transfusion is usually defined as replacing the patient's whole blood volume (about 70 mL/kg; roughly 10 units of red cells in an adult) within 24 hours. Other definitions include 50% of blood volume within 3 hours, or more than 4 units within 1 hour. It is used in major trauma, PPH and GI bleeding, with a balanced ratio of red cells, FFP and platelets.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 350 mL in 5 minutes — 350 mL in 5 minutes is a rapid infusion, but it is not the definition.
• 500 mL in 5 minutes — 500 mL in 5 minutes is not a recognised definition.
• 1 litre in 5 minutes — 1 litre in 5 minutes is very fast, but the definition is based on blood volume over time.

⭐ Key point: Watch for the complications of massive transfusion: hypothermia, LOW calcium (citrate), HIGH potassium, acidosis and clotting problems. Use a blood warmer and give calcium.$x$, null),
(23, $x$A patient is admitted to ICU with diagnosis of vaso-occlusive crisis, which of this management will you start with?$x$, $x$Rehydrate with normal saline$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Rehydrate with normal saline
A sickle cell vaso-occlusive (pain) crisis happens when sickled cells block small vessels. Dehydration makes the blood thicker and causes more sickling, so the first step is IV REHYDRATION (with normal saline). Rehydration improves blood flow and stops the cycle. Strong pain relief (often opioids) within 30 minutes, oxygen if SpO₂ is low, warmth, and treatment of infection go alongside it.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Transfuse with fresh whole blood — Transfusion is not first-line for an uncomplicated pain crisis; it is used for acute chest syndrome, stroke, or severe anaemia (sequestration). Fresh whole blood is rarely used.
• Order for narcotics and administer — Pain relief is essential and urgent, but rehydration is the first treatment this question is testing.
• Give oral rehydration solution — Oral rehydration solution is too slow and not suitable for an ICU patient in crisis.

⭐ Key point: Sickle crisis care: fluids, oxygen, warmth, pain relief within 30 minutes, and antibiotics for infection. Avoid cold, dehydration and hypoxia, because each one triggers more sickling.$x$, null),
(24, $x$Laboratory findings consistent with disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) include:$x$, $x$Prolonged PT/aPTT, thrombocytopenia, low fibrinogen, and elevated D-dimer$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Prolonged PT/aPTT, thrombocytopenia, low fibrinogen, and elevated D-dimer
In DIC, small clots form throughout the body. These use up platelets and clotting factors, so the patient then BLEEDS from everywhere: line sites, gums, wounds. Laboratory findings: PT and APTT PROLONGED (factors used up), platelets LOW, fibrinogen LOW, and D-dimer HIGH (clot breakdown). Causes include sepsis, trauma, placental abruption, amniotic fluid embolism and HELLP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Shortened PT/aPTT and elevated platelets — Shortened clotting times with high platelets do not fit DIC, which consumes clotting factors.
• Normal fibrinogen and normal D-dimer — Normal fibrinogen and D-dimer rule DIC out.
• Isolated elevated hemoglobin — Haemoglobin usually FALLS in DIC (bleeding and haemolysis); a raised haemoglobin alone is unrelated.

⭐ Key point: Treat the cause. Replace with platelets, FFP and cryoprecipitate (for low fibrinogen) if the patient is bleeding.$x$, null),
(25, $x$Which of the following pathogens is a common cause of ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP) in the ICU?$x$, $x$Pseudomonas aeruginosa$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Pseudomonas aeruginosa
Pseudomonas aeruginosa is one of the commonest causes of VAP, especially late VAP (after 5 or more days on the ventilator). It is a Gram-negative bacterium that lives in moist places (sinks, ventilator circuits, humidifiers) and is often resistant to many antibiotics. Other VAP germs: Staphylococcus aureus (including MRSA), Klebsiella, Acinetobacter, and E. coli.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Common cold rhinovirus only — Rhinovirus causes the common cold, not typical VAP.
• Malaria parasite — The malaria parasite infects red blood cells and liver cells, not the lungs via the ventilator.
• Tapeworm — Tapeworm is a gut parasite and has nothing to do with VAP.

⭐ Key point: Prevent VAP with the bundle: head up 30–45°, daily sedation hold, chlorhexidine mouth care, subglottic suction, cuff pressure 20–30 cmH₂O, and hand hygiene.$x$, null),
(26, $x$Autoclaving is a sterilisation method that primarily uses:$x$, $x$Pressurised steam at high temperature$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Pressurised steam at high temperature
An autoclave sterilises with STEAM UNDER PRESSURE. Pressure raises the boiling point, so steam reaches 121 °C (held for 15 minutes) or 134 °C (held for 3 minutes). Moist heat kills ALL microorganisms, including bacterial spores, by denaturing their proteins. It is the commonest, cheapest and most reliable method for metal instruments, linen and dressings.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ultraviolet light only — Ultraviolet light only reduces germs on surfaces and in air; it does not sterilise instruments.
• Cold chemical soaking only, with no heat — Cold chemical soaking (such as 2% glutaraldehyde) is high-level disinfection, used for heat-sensitive items, not autoclaving.
• Dry air at room temperature — Dry air at room temperature does not kill microorganisms.

⭐ Key point: Autoclave: 121 °C for 15 minutes or 134 °C for 3 minutes. Clean items first; check indicator tape, and use biological indicators (spore tests) to prove sterility.$x$, null),
(27, $x$To reduce contamination risk, blood cultures should ideally be drawn:$x$, $x$From two separate sites, ideally before starting antibiotics$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: From two separate sites, ideally before starting antibiotics
Taking blood cultures from TWO separate sites (two sets) helps tell a true infection from skin contamination. If the same germ grows from both sites, it is real; if only one bottle grows a skin germ, it is probably a contaminant. Cultures must be taken BEFORE antibiotics, because even one dose can stop the germs growing in the bottle and give a false negative. Clean the skin with 2% chlorhexidine in 70% alcohol and let it dry.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• From a single site only, after antibiotics are given — Taking cultures after antibiotics, or from only one site, makes the results unreliable.
• Only from a central line hub — Using only a central line hub increases contamination (unless line infection is suspected, and then a peripheral set is also taken).
• Without skin antisepsis — Skipping skin antisepsis guarantees contamination with skin bacteria.

⭐ Key point: In sepsis: cultures FIRST (two sets), then antibiotics within 1 hour. Never delay antibiotics for long just to get cultures.$x$, null),
(28, $x$What are the two key pharmacological antidotes used in the management of organophosphate poisoning?$x$, $x$Atropine and pralidoxime$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Atropine and pralidoxime
Organophosphates (insecticides such as Sniper/DDVP) block the enzyme acetylcholinesterase, so acetylcholine builds up. This causes SLUDGE signs (salivation, lacrimation, urination, diarrhoea, GI cramps, emesis), small pupils, slow pulse, bronchorrhoea (wet chest), and muscle twitching and weakness. ATROPINE blocks the muscarinic effects and dries the chest (give it until the chest is clear). PRALIDOXIME re-activates the enzyme (helps the muscle weakness) if given early.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Naloxone and flumazenil — Naloxone and flumazenil reverse opioids and benzodiazepines, not organophosphates.
• Atropine and naloxone — Naloxone has no role here; only atropine is correct in that pair.
• Pralidoxime and N-acetylcysteine — N-acetylcysteine is the antidote for paracetamol; only pralidoxime is correct in that pair.

⭐ Key point: Organophosphate care: remove contaminated clothes, wash the skin (wear PPE yourself), protect the airway, then give large doses of atropine plus pralidoxime.$x$, null),
(29, $x$Protamine sulphate is antidote of$x$, $x$Heparin$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Heparin
Protamine sulphate reverses HEPARIN. Protamine is strongly positive (basic) and heparin is strongly negative (acidic), so they bind and neutralise each other. 1 mg of protamine reverses about 100 units of heparin given in the last 2–3 hours. It only partly reverses LMWH (enoxaparin). Give it slowly IV; fast injection causes low BP, slow pulse and anaphylaxis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ephedrine — Ephedrine is a vasopressor for low BP during anaesthesia; it has no protamine antidote.
• Pethadine — Pethidine is an opioid; its antidote is naloxone.
• Scoline — Scoline (suxamethonium) is a muscle relaxant; it has no specific antidote (prolonged block is managed by ventilation).

⭐ Key point: Heparin → protamine. Warfarin → vitamin K plus PCC or FFP. Opioids → naloxone. Benzodiazepines → flumazenil.$x$, null),
(30, $x$Specific antidote in paracetamol poisoning is$x$, $x$Acetyl cysteine$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Acetyl cysteine
N-acetylcysteine (NAC, Parvolex) is the specific antidote for paracetamol overdose. In overdose, a toxic metabolite (NAPQI) builds up and destroys liver cells. NAC refills glutathione, which neutralises NAPQI. It works best within 8 hours of ingestion but is still given later. Treatment is guided by the paracetamol blood level at 4 hours or more after ingestion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Desferroxamine — Desferrioxamine is the antidote for iron poisoning.
• Naloxone — Naloxone is the antidote for opioids.
• Activated charcoal — Activated charcoal reduces absorption of many drugs within 1 hour, but it is not a specific antidote.

⭐ Key point: Paracetamol liver damage may not show for 24–72 hours, so start NAC early, check LFTs and INR, and watch for low glucose and liver failure.$x$, null),
(31, $x$First drug used to treat bradycardia:$x$, $x$Atropine$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Atropine
For symptomatic bradycardia (low BP, chest pain, shock, syncope, heart failure), the first drug is ATROPINE 500 micrograms IV, repeated up to a total of 3 mg. Atropine blocks the vagus nerve, which speeds up the SA node and AV conduction. If atropine fails, use transcutaneous pacing or adrenaline/isoprenaline infusions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Epinephrine — Epinephrine (adrenaline) is a second-line infusion if atropine fails, or for cardiac arrest.
• Amiodarone — Amiodarone treats FAST rhythms (VT, AF) and can slow the heart further.
• Deltacortil — Deltacortil (prednisolone) is a steroid and has no role in bradycardia.

⭐ Key point: Atropine may not work in a heart transplant patient (no vagal supply) or in complete heart block with wide QRS; prepare for pacing.$x$, null),
(32, $x$A patient on a high-dose, prolonged sodium nitroprusside infusion develops altered mental status and unexplained metabolic acidosis. This should raise suspicion for:$x$, $x$Cyanide toxicity from accumulation of nitroprusside metabolites$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Cyanide toxicity from accumulation of nitroprusside metabolites
Sodium nitroprusside contains cyanide. When high doses run for a long time, especially with kidney or liver problems, cyanide (and thiocyanate) builds up. Cyanide stops cells using oxygen, causing confusion, metabolic (lactic) acidosis, a rising mixed venous oxygen level (SvO₂), arrhythmias and eventually death. Stop the infusion and give an antidote (hydroxocobalamin or sodium thiosulphate).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Serotonin syndrome — Serotonin syndrome is caused by serotonergic drugs (SSRIs, tramadol, MDMA), not nitroprusside.
• Malignant hyperthermia — Malignant hyperthermia is caused by volatile anaesthetics and suxamethonium.
• Digoxin toxicity — Digoxin toxicity causes nausea, visual changes and arrhythmias, not this picture with nitroprusside.

⭐ Key point: Protect nitroprusside from light (cover the syringe and line), use the lowest dose for the shortest time, and check lactate and acid–base status.$x$, null),
(33, $x$The objective of medical management of angina is to:$x$, $x$Decrease myocardial oxygen demand and increase supply$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Decrease myocardial oxygen demand and increase supply
Angina is chest pain caused by the heart muscle needing more oxygen than the narrowed coronary arteries can supply. Treatment aims to REDUCE oxygen DEMAND and INCREASE oxygen SUPPLY. Beta-blockers slow the heart rate and nitrates reduce preload (lower demand); nitrates and calcium channel blockers widen the coronary arteries (more supply); aspirin and statins prevent clots and plaque growth.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase myocardial oxygen demand and increase supply — Increasing demand would make the imbalance and the pain worse.
• Increase demand and decrease supply — Increasing demand and decreasing supply is the exact cause of ischaemia, not its treatment.
• Maintain the general circulation — Maintaining the general circulation is too vague and doesn't describe the specific aim.

⭐ Key point: For angina, rest plus GTN sublingual. If pain lasts more than 20 minutes or doesn't respond, treat it as ACS: ECG, troponin and aspirin.$x$, null),
(34, $x$Which of these drugs is used as an antidote for narcotics$x$, $x$Nalaxone$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Nalaxone
Naloxone is the specific antagonist (antidote) for opioids (narcotics such as morphine, heroin, pethidine, fentanyl and tramadol). It blocks opioid receptors and reverses respiratory depression, sedation and small pupils within 1–2 minutes when given IV. Dose: 0.4 mg IV, repeated every 2–3 minutes. Smaller doses (40–100 mcg) are used in post-op patients so pain relief is not completely reversed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Black charcoal — Activated charcoal reduces gut absorption of many oral poisons but does not reverse opioids.
• Atropine — Atropine is the antidote for organophosphates and treats bradycardia.
• Cimetidine — Cimetidine is an H₂ blocker that lowers stomach acid; it is not an antidote.

⭐ Key point: Naloxone works for a SHORTER time (30–90 minutes) than many opioids, so the patient can become sedated again (re-narcotisation). Monitor closely and repeat doses or start an infusion.$x$, null),
(35, $x$The preferred muscle relaxant in a patient with liver or renal failure is:$x$, $x$Atracurium$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Atracurium
Atracurium is broken down in the blood by Hofmann elimination (a spontaneous chemical breakdown at normal body temperature and pH) and by ester hydrolysis, NOT by the liver or kidneys. Its action therefore stays predictable in liver or kidney failure. Cisatracurium works the same way and is often preferred in ICU.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Rocuronium — Rocuronium is cleared mainly by the liver (and kidneys), so it can last longer in liver failure.
• Pancuronium — Pancuronium is mainly cleared by the kidneys and is very long-acting in renal failure.
• Vecuronium — Vecuronium depends on the liver and kidneys, and its active metabolite builds up in organ failure.

⭐ Key point: Atracurium and cisatracurium are the muscle relaxants of choice in liver or kidney failure. Note that atracurium can release histamine (rash, low BP).$x$, null),
(36, $x$A patient sedated with a continuous propofol infusion for several days develops metabolic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, and cardiac dysfunction. The nurse should suspect:$x$, $x$Propofol Infusion Syndrome, a rare but serious complication of prolonged high-dose propofol use$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Propofol Infusion Syndrome, a rare but serious complication of prolonged high-dose propofol use
Propofol infusion syndrome (PRIS) is a rare but often fatal complication of high-dose (above 4 mg/kg/h) propofol given for more than 48 hours, especially in children, sepsis, head injury and with steroids or catecholamines. It disrupts how the mitochondria burn fat. Signs: unexplained metabolic (lactic) acidosis, rhabdomyolysis (high CK), high potassium, high triglycerides, kidney failure, arrhythmias and heart failure. Stop propofol at once and give supportive care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal, expected sedative effect with no concern — These are dangerous abnormalities, not a normal sedative effect.
• A simple allergic reaction to propofol — An allergic reaction causes rash, wheeze and low BP, not acidosis with rhabdomyolysis.
• Improved sedation with no adverse effect — This picture is harmful; it does not mean better sedation.

⭐ Key point: With long propofol infusions, monitor triglycerides, lactate, CK and ECG, and keep the dose below 4 mg/kg/h where possible.$x$, null),
(37, $x$A patient with a major burn receives morphine IV. The nurse prioritizes monitoring for:$x$, $x$Respiratory depression$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Respiratory depression
Morphine, like all opioids, depresses the respiratory centre in the brainstem, causing slow, shallow breathing, CO₂ retention, low oxygen and apnoea. This is the most dangerous side effect and can be fatal, especially with IV doses, in burns patients who may also have smoke inhalation, and when combined with sedatives. The nurse monitors respiratory rate, depth, SpO₂ and sedation level and keeps naloxone ready.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypertension — Morphine is more likely to cause LOW blood pressure (vasodilation), not high.
• Urinary retention — Urinary retention is a side effect but not life-threatening.
• Constipation — Constipation is common but not the priority in the acute setting.

⭐ Key point: Opioid danger signs are a respiratory rate below 8–10, increasing sleepiness and pinpoint pupils. Sedation usually comes before respiratory depression, so check the sedation score.$x$, null),
(38, $x$A patient is prescribed dopamine 5 mcg/kg/min. The patient weighs 70 kg and the infusion is mixed as 400 mg in 250 mL D5W. What is the infusion rate in mL/hr?$x$, $x$13.1 mL/hr$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 13.1 mL/hr
Step 1, dose per minute: 5 mcg × 70 kg = 350 mcg/min. Step 2, dose per hour: 350 × 60 = 21,000 mcg/h = 21 mg/h. Step 3, concentration: 400 mg ÷ 250 mL = 1.6 mg/mL. Step 4, rate: 21 mg/h ÷ 1.6 mg/mL = 13.1 mL/h. Shortcut formula: (dose mcg/kg/min × weight × 60) ÷ concentration in mcg/mL = (5 × 70 × 60) ÷ 1600 = 13.1 mL/h.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10.5 mL/hr — 10.5 mL/h comes from a calculation error, such as using the wrong weight or concentration.
• 21.9 mL/hr — 21.9 mL/h comes from mixing up mg and mL or using the wrong concentration.
• 26.3 mL/hr — 26.3 mL/h is about double the right answer, a common error from using a weaker concentration.

⭐ Key point: Always convert to the SAME units (mcg or mg) before dividing. Have a second nurse check every vasoactive infusion calculation.$x$, null),
(39, $x$A litre of Normal Saline is ordered to run over 8 hours via a gravity administration set with a drop factor of 15 gtt/mL. What is the correct flow rate in drops per minute?$x$, $x$31 gtt/min$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 31 gtt/min
Formula: drops per minute = (volume in mL × drop factor) ÷ time in MINUTES. Volume 1000 mL; drop factor 15 gtt/mL; time 8 hours = 480 minutes. (1000 × 15) ÷ 480 = 15,000 ÷ 480 = 31.25, which rounds to 31 drops per minute.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 21 gtt/min — 21 gtt/min would give too little fluid; it comes from a wrong drop factor or time.
• 42 gtt/min — 42 gtt/min is the answer for a 20 gtt/mL set (1000 × 20 ÷ 480 = 41.7), not 15.
• 15 gtt/min — 15 gtt/min just repeats the drop factor and is not a calculation.

⭐ Key point: Standard giving sets are 20 drops/mL (adult crystalloid), blood sets 15 drops/mL, and paediatric (burette/micro-drip) sets 60 drops/mL. With a 60-drop set, drops per minute = mL per hour.$x$, null),
(40, $x$Activated charcoal is most effective in acute poisoning management when given:$x$, $x$Early after ingestion (typically within about 1 hour)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Early after ingestion (typically within about 1 hour)
Activated charcoal binds (adsorbs) many drugs in the gut so they are not absorbed. It works best when given EARLY, ideally within 1 hour of swallowing a poison, because after that much of the drug has already been absorbed. The adult dose is 50 g orally (or via NG tube) and the child dose is 1 g/kg. The patient must have a protected airway or be awake, because aspiration is dangerous.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• More than 24 hours after ingestion — After 24 hours almost all of the drug is absorbed, so charcoal does little (except for some slow-release drugs).
• Only intravenously — Charcoal is given by MOUTH or NG tube, never IV.
• Only for iron overdose — Charcoal does NOT bind iron (or lithium, alcohol, acids or alkalis).

⭐ Key point: Don't use charcoal for iron, lithium, alcohols, corrosives or hydrocarbons such as kerosene, or in a patient who cannot protect their airway.$x$, null),
(41, $x$A classic chest X-ray sign of pleural effusion is:$x$, $x$Blunting of the costophrenic angle$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Blunting of the costophrenic angle
Fluid in the pleural space collects at the lowest point first. On an erect chest X-ray, the sharp costophrenic angle (where the diaphragm meets the ribs) becomes blunted, often with a curved upper edge (meniscus sign). At least about 200–300 mL is needed to show on a PA film. A large effusion makes the whole lower zone white and can push the trachea and mediastinum to the opposite side.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperinflated lung fields — Hyperinflated (black, enlarged) lung fields suggest COPD or asthma, not effusion.
• A widened mediastinum — A widened mediastinum suggests aortic injury or dissection.
• Free air under the diaphragm — Free air under the diaphragm means a perforated bowel or stomach.

⭐ Key point: An effusion is dull to percussion, with reduced breath sounds and reduced vocal resonance. Large ones are drained by thoracentesis or a chest drain.$x$, null),
(42, $x$A key chest X-ray sign of pneumothorax is:$x$, $x$Absence of lung markings peripheral to a visible pleural line$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Absence of lung markings peripheral to a visible pleural line
In a pneumothorax, air enters the pleural space and the lung collapses inwards. On X-ray you see a thin white line (the visceral pleural edge), and OUTSIDE it there are NO lung markings (black space with no blood vessels). On a supine ICU film, look for the deep sulcus sign (a deep, dark costophrenic angle).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increased lung markings throughout — Increased lung markings suggest fluid, infection or fibrosis, not pneumothorax.
• Bilateral diffuse infiltrates — Bilateral diffuse infiltrates suggest ARDS, pulmonary oedema or pneumonia.
• Widened mediastinum — A widened mediastinum suggests aortic injury.

⭐ Key point: TENSION pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis (distress, low BP, trachea deviated away, absent breath sounds). Decompress with a needle immediately; don't wait for an X-ray.$x$, null),
(43, $x$On a plain abdominal X-ray, multiple dilated bowel loops with air-fluid levels on an erect film are classic findings of:$x$, $x$Intestinal obstruction$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Intestinal obstruction
In intestinal obstruction, gas and fluid build up above the blockage. The bowel loops stretch (dilated loops), and on an ERECT film, gas sits on top of fluid, forming flat lines called air–fluid levels (like a glass of water). Small bowel loops are central with folds that cross the whole width (valvulae conniventes); large bowel loops are peripheral with folds that cross only partly (haustra).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal bowel gas pattern — A normal bowel gas pattern does not show multiple dilated loops with fluid levels.
• Chronic constipation only — Constipation shows faecal loading, not multiple air–fluid levels.
• Gallstones — Gallstones are often not visible on X-ray (only about 10–15% contain calcium); they don't cause this pattern.

⭐ Key point: Signs of obstruction: colicky pain, vomiting, distension, absolute constipation. Treatment is "drip and suck": IV fluids plus NG tube on free drainage, nil by mouth.$x$, null),
(44, $x$Which nursing intervention is most effective in preventing contrast-induced nephropathy in an at-risk patient before a CT scan with IV contrast?$x$, $x$Ensuring adequate IV isotonic fluid hydration before and after the procedure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Ensuring adequate IV isotonic fluid hydration before and after the procedure
Contrast can damage the kidney tubules and narrow the kidney blood vessels, causing a rise in creatinine 24–72 hours later. The most effective preventive step is good IV HYDRATION with isotonic fluid (0.9% saline or Hartmann's) before and after the scan. This keeps urine flowing, dilutes the contrast and protects the kidneys. Also use the lowest dose of low-osmolar contrast, stop nephrotoxic drugs (NSAIDs), and hold metformin if the GFR is low.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administering prophylactic antibiotics — Antibiotics have no role in preventing contrast kidney injury.
• Withholding all oral fluids for 12 hours before the scan — Withholding fluids causes dehydration, which INCREASES the risk.
• Administering a loop diuretic prior to the scan — Loop diuretics can dehydrate the patient and increase the risk.

⭐ Key point: High-risk patients are those with CKD, diabetes, dehydration, heart failure and older age. Check creatinine at 48–72 hours after contrast.$x$, null),
(45, $x$Which chest X-ray finding is characteristic of cardiogenic pulmonary oedema?$x$, $x$Bilateral perihilar 'bat-wing' infiltrates with Kerley B lines and cardiomegaly$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Bilateral perihilar 'bat-wing' infiltrates with Kerley B lines and cardiomegaly
In cardiogenic pulmonary oedema, the failing left ventricle backs up pressure into the lungs. On X-ray you see: an enlarged heart (cardiomegaly), fluid around the central airways in a "bat-wing" (butterfly) pattern, Kerley B lines (short horizontal lines at the lung edges, from swollen lymphatics), upper-lobe vessel diversion, and often pleural effusions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A single well-circumscribed nodule in the right upper lobe — A single nodule suggests a tumour or granuloma, not pulmonary oedema.
• Complete opacification of one hemithorax with mediastinal shift away from it — Complete whiteness of one side with shift AWAY from it suggests a massive pleural effusion.
• A normal chest X-ray in all cases — The X-ray is usually abnormal in pulmonary oedema, not normal.

⭐ Key point: Pulmonary oedema care: sit the patient upright, oxygen or CPAP, GTN (if BP allows), and IV furosemide. In ARDS, the heart size is usually normal.$x$, null),
(46, $x$A Processor acts like a/an$x$, $x$BRAIN$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: BRAIN
The processor (CPU, central processing unit) is the "brain" of the computer. It carries out instructions, does calculations and controls all other parts, just as the brain controls the body. Its speed is measured in gigahertz (GHz).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• HEART — The heart pumps blood; in a computer, the power supply is a better comparison.
• ARM — An arm is like an output device (it carries out actions), not the processor.
• KIDNEY — The kidney filters waste; there is no CPU comparison with the kidney.

⭐ Key point: CPU = brain, RAM = short-term memory (working desk), hard disk = long-term storage (filing cabinet), and input/output devices = the senses and hands.$x$, null),
(47, $x$What is the main function of RAM (Random Access Memory) in a computer?$x$, $x$To temporarily store data that the CPU is actively using$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: To temporarily store data that the CPU is actively using
RAM (random access memory) is the computer's TEMPORARY working memory. It holds the programs and data the CPU is using right now, so the computer can work quickly. RAM is volatile: everything in it is lost when the power is switched off. More RAM lets more programs run smoothly at the same time.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• To store data permanently — Permanent storage is the job of the hard disk, SSD or flash drive.
• To store the operating system — The operating system is stored permanently on the hard disk; it is only loaded into RAM while running.
• To provide an internet connection — Internet connection comes from the network card or modem, not RAM.

⭐ Key point: RAM is temporary and erased when off. ROM (read-only memory) is permanent and holds the start-up instructions.$x$, null),
(48, $x$What is the purpose of an Electronic Health Record (EHR)?$x$, $x$To create a digital version of a patient's chart$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: To create a digital version of a patient's chart
An electronic health record (EHR) is a digital (computer) version of the patient's chart. It holds the full health information — history, diagnoses, drugs, allergies, lab results, imaging, vital signs, nursing notes and care plans — and is available to authorised staff wherever needed. It improves legibility, speeds sharing of information, supports alerts (allergies, drug interactions) and reduces errors.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• To store only the patient's demographic information — An EHR stores the FULL record, not only demographic details.
• To serve as a paper-based filing system — An EHR replaces paper files; it is not a paper system.
• To replace the need for any patient-nurse interaction — An EHR supports care; it never replaces the nurse–patient relationship.

⭐ Key point: EHRs must be protected: use your own login, never share passwords, log out after use, and respect confidentiality.$x$, null),
(49, $x$Telemedicine refers to:$x$, $x$The use of telecommunications technology to deliver healthcare services remotely$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The use of telecommunications technology to deliver healthcare services remotely
Telemedicine means using telecommunication technology — phone, video calls, internet and data links — to give healthcare at a DISTANCE: consultations, monitoring, diagnosis and education. In critical care, "tele-ICU" allows specialists to support teams in remote hospitals and review monitors and images from afar. It improves access in rural areas and saves travel time.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A type of medical imaging equipment — Imaging equipment (X-ray, CT) is a diagnostic device, not telemedicine itself.
• A brand of hospital software only used for billing — Billing software is administrative and is not what telemedicine means.
• A paper-based referral system — A paper referral system does not use telecommunication.

⭐ Key point: Telemedicine needs patient consent, privacy and secure systems, the same as face-to-face care.$x$, null),
(50, $x$A cardiac monitor and pulse oximeter used in the ICU are examples of which category of electronic devices in healthcare?$x$, $x$Monitoring devices$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Monitoring devices
A cardiac monitor and a pulse oximeter continuously WATCH the patient's vital signs (heart rhythm, heart rate, oxygen saturation) and raise alarms when values go outside the set limits. That makes them monitoring devices. They do not treat the patient or make the diagnosis on their own; they alert the nurse to changes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diagnostic devices — Diagnostic devices are used mainly to find a diagnosis, such as X-ray machines, CT scanners and lab analysers.
• Treatment devices — Treatment (therapeutic) devices deliver therapy, such as ventilators, infusion pumps and defibrillators.
• Storage devices — Storage devices hold data, such as hard drives and USB drives.

⭐ Key point: Set alarm limits for each patient, never switch alarms off, and always check the PATIENT, not just the monitor.$x$, null),
(51, $x$A classic clinical feature of acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema is:$x$, $x$Pink, frothy sputum with severe dyspnoea and orthopnoea$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Pink, frothy sputum with severe dyspnoea and orthopnoea
In acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema, the left ventricle fails and pressure backs up into the lung capillaries. Fluid leaks into the alveoli and mixes with air, producing PINK (blood-stained), FROTHY sputum. The patient is severely breathless, cannot lie flat (orthopnoea), is sweaty and anxious, and has widespread crackles, often with wheeze ("cardiac asthma").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Painless haematuria — Painless haematuria is a urinary sign (kidney or bladder tumour, stones), not pulmonary oedema.
• Unilateral leg swelling with a normal respiratory rate — One swollen leg with normal breathing suggests a DVT, not pulmonary oedema.
• Dry cough with no dyspnoea — A dry cough without breathlessness does not match the severe distress of acute pulmonary oedema.

⭐ Key point: Treat by sitting the patient upright with legs down, giving oxygen or CPAP, GTN (if SBP is above 90–100) and IV furosemide, and monitoring urine output.$x$, null),
(52, $x$Drug therapy for pulmonary oedema includes the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Haematinics$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Haematinics
The question asks which drug is NOT used. Haematinics (iron, folic acid, vitamin B12) treat anaemia; they do nothing for fluid in the lungs. All the other options can be used: diuretics remove fluid, morphine reduces anxiety and preload, and digitalis strengthens contraction and controls AF rate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diuretics — Diuretics (furosemide) ARE used: they remove excess fluid and quickly dilate the veins.
• Morphine — Morphine IS used (now more carefully): it eases breathlessness and anxiety and lowers preload.
• Digitalis — Digitalis (digoxin) IS used in some patients, especially with AF, to slow the rate and improve pumping.

⭐ Key point: Remember "LMNOP" for pulmonary oedema: Lasix (furosemide), Morphine (with caution), Nitrates, Oxygen, Position (sit up).$x$, null),
(53, $x$A patient develops sudden dyspnoea, pleuritic chest pain, and tachycardia after prolonged bed rest post-surgery. The nurse should have a high index of suspicion for:$x$, $x$Pulmonary embolism$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Pulmonary embolism
After surgery and bed rest, blood flows slowly in the leg veins (stasis), surgery makes the blood clot more easily, and veins may be damaged. These three factors (Virchow's triad) cause a DVT. A piece of clot can travel to the lungs as a pulmonary embolism, causing SUDDEN breathlessness, sharp chest pain worse on breathing in (pleuritic), fast pulse, low oxygen, and sometimes haemoptysis or collapse.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple anxiety — Anxiety should never be assumed until serious causes like PE are excluded.
• Common cold — A common cold causes runny nose and sore throat, not sudden pleuritic pain.
• Gastritis — Gastritis causes epigastric burning, not sudden breathlessness with tachycardia.

⭐ Key point: Prevent PE with early mobilisation, leg exercises, compression stockings and LMWH. Suspect PE with any sudden breathlessness after surgery.$x$, null),
(54, $x$CT pulmonary angiography is the imaging investigation of choice to diagnose:$x$, $x$Pulmonary embolism$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Pulmonary embolism
CT pulmonary angiography (CTPA) injects contrast into a vein and scans the chest as the contrast passes through the lung arteries. A clot shows as a dark "filling defect" in a bright vessel. It is fast, widely available and accurate, so it is the first-choice test for PE. A V/Q scan is used when contrast cannot be given (kidney failure, contrast allergy, pregnancy in some centres).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple rib fracture — A rib fracture is seen on a plain X-ray or a normal CT; contrast angiography is not needed.
• Peptic ulcer disease — Peptic ulcer is diagnosed by endoscopy (OGD).
• Osteoarthritis — Osteoarthritis is diagnosed clinically and with plain X-rays.

⭐ Key point: Use the Wells score and D-dimer first in stable patients. A low score with a normal D-dimer makes PE unlikely.$x$, null),
(55, $x$A patient with severe emphysema is classically described as a 'pink puffer.' This term reflects:$x$, $x$A thin body habitus, pursed-lip breathing, and relatively preserved oxygenation until late disease$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A thin body habitus, pursed-lip breathing, and relatively preserved oxygenation until late disease
In emphysema, the alveolar walls are destroyed and the lungs over-inflate. The patient works very hard to breathe: they are thin (they burn many calories breathing), barrel-chested, use accessory muscles, and breathe out through pursed lips to keep the airways open. Because they breathe so hard, oxygen levels stay fairly normal until late, so they look "pink" and "puff". This is the opposite of the "blue bloater".

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cyanosis with peripheral oedema — Cyanosis with peripheral oedema describes the "blue bloater" (chronic bronchitis with right heart failure).
• Obesity with cor pulmonale — Obesity with cor pulmonale also fits the blue bloater, not the pink puffer.
• A productive cough with copious purulent sputum — A productive cough with lots of sputum is typical of chronic bronchitis (blue bloater).

⭐ Key point: Pink puffer = emphysema (thin, breathless, near-normal O₂). Blue bloater = chronic bronchitis (overweight, cyanosed, oedema, CO₂ retention).$x$, null),
(56, $x$For a patient with an acute COPD exacerbation and a history of chronic CO2 retention, which initial oxygen delivery approach is most appropriate?$x$, $x$Controlled oxygen via Venturi mask titrated to a target SpO2 of about 88-92%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Controlled oxygen via Venturi mask titrated to a target SpO2 of about 88-92%
Patients with COPD who retain CO₂ may depend partly on low oxygen to drive their breathing. Too much oxygen can raise CO₂ further (through less breathing drive, V/Q mismatch and the Haldane effect), causing CO₂ narcosis, acidosis and drowsiness. A Venturi mask gives an EXACT, fixed oxygen percentage (24% or 28% to start), titrated to a target SpO₂ of 88–92%. Check an ABG within 30–60 minutes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Non-rebreather mask at 15 L/min regardless of saturation — A non-rebreather at 15 L/min gives very high oxygen and risks CO₂ narcosis; it is for other emergencies.
• Room air only with no supplemental oxygen under any circumstance — Withholding oxygen completely is dangerous; hypoxia kills faster than CO₂ retention.
• Nasal cannula at 6 L/min titrated to SpO2 100% — Aiming for 100% saturation in a CO₂ retainer causes oxygen-induced hypercapnia.

⭐ Key point: COPD target SpO₂ is 88–92%. If pH is below 7.35 with a high PaCO₂ despite treatment, start BiPAP (NIV).$x$, null),
(57, $x$How is a tension pneumothorax causing PEA arrest managed?$x$, $x$Immediate needle decompression followed by chest tube insertion$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Immediate needle decompression followed by chest tube insertion
Tension pneumothorax causes PEA by trapping air that crushes the lung and pushes the heart and great veins aside, so blood cannot return to the heart. It is one of the reversible "4 Ts". The cause must be relieved IMMEDIATELY with needle decompression (2nd ICS mid-clavicular line, or 4th–5th ICS mid-axillary line in adults), followed by a chest drain. Continue CPR while this is done.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Defibrillation — Defibrillation does not work for PEA; it is only for VF and pulseless VT.
• IV fluids alone — IV fluids alone cannot overcome the blockage to blood return.
• Atropine administration — Atropine is no longer recommended for PEA and does not treat the cause.

⭐ Key point: The reversible causes of cardiac arrest are the 4 Hs (Hypoxia, Hypovolaemia, Hypo/Hyperkalaemia, Hypothermia) and the 4 Ts (Tension pneumothorax, Tamponade, Toxins, Thrombosis).$x$, null),
(58, $x$Which site is commonly used for chest tube insertion?$x$, $x$5th intercostal space, mid-axillary line$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 5th intercostal space, mid-axillary line
The usual chest drain site is the "safe triangle": 4th–5th intercostal space in the MID-AXILLARY line (lateral edge of pectoralis major in front, lateral edge of latissimus dorsi behind, the level of the nipple below). This avoids the internal mammary artery, the heart, the liver and spleen, and large muscles. The drain goes over the TOP of the rib, because the intercostal vessels and nerve run along the bottom edge.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Midline of the abdomen — The midline of the abdomen is for abdominal procedures (such as a laparotomy), not chest drains.
• Clavicular notch — The clavicular notch is not a chest drain site; it is near large vessels.
• 4th intercostal space, midclavicular line — The 4th ICS mid-clavicular line is too close to the heart and breast tissue; the 2nd ICS mid-clavicular line is used for NEEDLE decompression.

⭐ Key point: The patient should sit up with the arm raised behind the head. After insertion, check for swinging and bubbling and get a chest X-ray.$x$, null),
(59, $x$Type II (hypercapnic) respiratory failure is defined by:$x$, $x$A PaCO2 greater than 50 mmHg with accompanying respiratory acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A PaCO2 greater than 50 mmHg with accompanying respiratory acidosis
Type II (hypercapnic or ventilatory) respiratory failure means the lungs cannot remove CO₂: PaCO₂ above 45–50 mmHg (above 6 kPa), usually with a low PaO₂ and a low pH (respiratory acidosis) when it is acute. The cause is HYPOventilation: COPD, exhausted asthma, opioid overdose, neuromuscular disease (GBS, myasthenia), obesity and chest wall problems.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A PaO2 less than 60 mmHg alone — A PaO₂ below 60 mmHg alone (normal or low CO₂) is TYPE I (hypoxaemic) failure.
• A normal PaCO2 with severe hypoxemia — A normal PaCO₂ with severe hypoxaemia is also type I failure.
• Elevated bicarbonate with normal pH — A raised bicarbonate with normal pH is a compensated or metabolic picture, not the definition.

⭐ Key point: Type 1 = low O₂ only (oxygenation problem: pneumonia, oedema, ARDS). Type 2 = low O₂ + HIGH CO₂ (ventilation problem). Type 2 needs ventilation support (NIV or intubation), not just oxygen.$x$, null),
(60, $x$A key clinical benefit of BiPAP in a patient with a COPD exacerbation and respiratory failure is that it:$x$, $x$Reduces the work of breathing by providing inspiratory pressure support while maintaining alveolar recruitment with EPAP$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Reduces the work of breathing by providing inspiratory pressure support while maintaining alveolar recruitment with EPAP
BiPAP gives two pressures. IPAP (inspiratory pressure) pushes air in on each breath, helping the tired breathing muscles, increasing tidal volume and blowing off CO₂. EPAP (expiratory pressure, which acts like PEEP) keeps the small airways and alveoli open at the end of each breath, so less work is needed for the next breath. In COPD exacerbations it lowers CO₂, corrects the pH and reduces the need for intubation and death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Eliminates the need for any pharmacologic bronchodilator therapy — BiPAP does not replace bronchodilators; nebulisers, steroids and antibiotics are still needed.
• Cures the underlying airway obstruction — BiPAP supports breathing while the exacerbation is treated; it does not cure the disease.
• Requires no patient cooperation or spontaneous breathing effort — BiPAP needs a cooperative, spontaneously breathing patient who can protect their airway.

⭐ Key point: Start NIV in COPD when pH is 7.25–7.35 with a high PaCO₂. Check the ABG at 1 hour; no improvement means the patient may need intubation.$x$, null),
(61, $x$What PaO2/FiO2 ratio threshold, with PEEP of at least 5 cmH2O, defines severe ARDS under the Berlin definition?$x$, $x$100 or less$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 100 or less
The Berlin definition grades ARDS by the PaO₂/FiO₂ (P/F) ratio, with PEEP or CPAP of at least 5 cmH₂O. SEVERE is P/F of 100 or less, moderate is above 100 to 200, and mild is above 200 to 300. Example: PaO₂ 70 mmHg on FiO₂ 0.8 gives 70 ÷ 0.8 = 87.5, which is severe ARDS. Severe ARDS has the highest death rate and needs prone positioning and sometimes ECMO.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 300 or less — 300 or less is the upper cut-off for MILD ARDS (the threshold for any ARDS).
• 200 or less — 200 or less is the cut-off for MODERATE ARDS.
• 150 or less — 150 or less is the cut-off used to start PRONE positioning, not the Berlin severe category.

⭐ Key point: P/F ratio = PaO₂ (mmHg) ÷ FiO₂ (as a decimal). Normal is about 400–500 (100 ÷ 0.21 ≈ 476).$x$, null),
(62, $x$What is the normal recommended tidal volume range for lung-protective ventilation in ARDS, based on predicted body weight?$x$, $x$6-8 mL/kg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 6-8 mL/kg
Lung-protective ventilation uses SMALL tidal volumes of 6–8 mL/kg PREDICTED body weight (starting at 6 mL/kg in ARDS), with a plateau pressure of 30 cmH₂O or less. ARDS lungs are "baby lungs": only a small part works, so large breaths over-stretch and damage it (volutrauma). The ARDSNet trial showed that 6 mL/kg instead of 12 mL/kg reduced deaths.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10-12 mL/kg — 10–12 mL/kg was the old practice and increases lung injury and death.
• 15-18 mL/kg — 15–18 mL/kg is dangerously large and would cause barotrauma and volutrauma.
• 2-4 mL/kg — 2–4 mL/kg is too small for normal ventilation (only used in special settings like ECMO).

⭐ Key point: Use PREDICTED body weight (from height and sex), not actual weight. An obese patient's lungs are no bigger than a thin patient's of the same height.$x$, null),
(63, $x$A patient's serum sodium is measured at 118mmol/L with confusion and lethargy. The nurse understands that correction of severe hyponatraemia must be done:$x$, $x$Gradually and carefully, since overly rapid correction risks osmotic demyelination syndrome$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Gradually and carefully, since overly rapid correction risks osmotic demyelination syndrome
In chronic low sodium, brain cells adapt by losing solutes to avoid swelling. If sodium is raised too FAST, water rushes out of the brain cells and damages the myelin in the pons, causing osmotic demyelination syndrome (central pontine myelinolysis). This leads to difficulty speaking and swallowing, quadriplegia, "locked-in" syndrome or death. Correction must therefore be gradual: no more than 8–10 mmol/L in 24 hours, with frequent sodium checks (every 2–4 hours).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• As rapidly as possible regardless of rate — Correcting as fast as possible is exactly what causes osmotic demyelination.
• Only with oral salt tablets, never IV — IV treatment (including 3% saline for severe symptoms such as seizures) is used when needed; oral salt alone is not the rule.
• With no monitoring required — Close monitoring of sodium, fluid balance and neurological status is essential.

⭐ Key point: For severe symptomatic hyponatraemia (seizures, coma), give 3% saline 150 mL boluses to raise sodium by 4–6 mmol/L quickly, then slow down to stay within 8–10 mmol/L per 24 hours.$x$, null),
(64, $x$Which ventricle is most sensitive to an increase in afterload?$x$, $x$A failing ventricle with poor contractility$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A failing ventricle with poor contractility
Afterload is the resistance the ventricle must pump against. A healthy heart can keep its stroke volume even when afterload rises. A FAILING ventricle with weak contractility cannot overcome extra resistance, so its stroke volume falls sharply when afterload rises. That is why lowering afterload (ACE inhibitors, nitrates, nitroprusside, IABP) improves output in heart failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A normal ventricle — A normal ventricle can compensate well, so its stroke volume changes little.
• A hypertrophied athletic ventricle — A hypertrophied athletic heart is strong and handles extra afterload well.
• None, afterload never affects stroke volume — Afterload DOES affect stroke volume; it matters most in the failing heart.

⭐ Key point: In heart failure, reduce afterload with vasodilators and avoid high blood pressure. In septic shock, afterload (SVR) is LOW and vasopressors raise it.$x$, null),
(65, $x$A patient reports substernal chest pressure radiating to the jaw during exertion, relieved within 5 minutes of rest. The nurse should classify this as most consistent with:$x$, $x$Stable angina$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Stable angina
STABLE angina is chest pain brought on by a predictable amount of exertion (walking, climbing stairs) or emotion. It lasts a few minutes (usually under 10–15 minutes) and goes away with REST or GTN. It is caused by a fixed narrowing of the coronary artery. Here, the pain comes on with exertion and is relieved within 5 minutes of rest, which is the classic stable pattern.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Unstable angina — Unstable angina is pain at rest, new pain, or pain that is getting worse (crescendo); it is an emergency (ACS).
• Acute MI — An acute MI causes pain lasting more than 20 minutes, not relieved by rest, with ECG changes and raised troponin.
• Pericarditis — Pericarditis causes sharp pain, worse lying down and on breathing, better sitting forward, not related to exertion.

⭐ Key point: Any change in the pattern of angina (at rest, more often, longer, less relief) is unstable angina. Treat it as ACS.$x$, null),
(66, $x$Which cardiac biomarker is most specific for myocardial injury and remains elevated for 7-14 days after MI?$x$, $x$Troponin I/T$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Troponin I/T
Troponin I and T are proteins found only in heart muscle, so they are the most SPECIFIC markers of heart muscle damage. Levels rise 3–6 hours after the injury, peak at 12–24 hours, and stay raised for 7–14 days. This makes troponin useful to diagnose MI even if the patient comes late. High-sensitivity troponin tests at 0 and 1–3 hours are now standard.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CK-MB — CK-MB is less specific (some is in skeletal muscle) and returns to normal in 2–3 days, so it is used to detect REINFARCTION.
• Myoglobin — Myoglobin rises earliest (1–2 hours) but is found in all muscle, so it is not specific.
• LDH — LDH rises late and is non-specific (blood, liver, muscle); it is no longer used for MI.

⭐ Key point: Troponin can also rise in sepsis, PE, kidney failure, myocarditis and after CPR or defibrillation, so interpret it with the ECG and symptoms.$x$, null),
(67, $x$A patient post-myocardial infarction develops new-onset crackles bilaterally, a third heart sound (S3), and worsening dyspnoea. This most likely indicates:$x$, $x$Acute left ventricular failure/pulmonary oedema$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Acute left ventricular failure/pulmonary oedema
After an MI, the damaged left ventricle may fail to pump forward. Blood backs up into the lungs (pulmonary oedema), causing new bilateral crackles and worsening breathlessness. An S3 (third heart sound, "Ken-tuc-ky") comes from blood rushing into a stiff, overfilled ventricle, a classic sign of LV failure. This is acute left ventricular failure (Killip class II–III).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple anxiety — Anxiety does not produce crackles and an S3.
• Normal post-MI recovery — These are signs of a complication, not normal recovery.
• Gastro-oesophageal reflux — Reflux causes heartburn, not crackles and an S3.

⭐ Key point: Post-MI complications include arrhythmias, heart failure and cardiogenic shock, papillary muscle rupture (a new murmur), ventricular rupture, and pericarditis (including Dressler's syndrome).$x$, null),
(68, $x$The most common cause of right-sided heart failure is:$x$, $x$Chronic left-sided heart failure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Chronic left-sided heart failure
The commonest cause of right heart failure is LEFT heart failure. When the left side fails, pressure backs up into the lungs (pulmonary hypertension). The right ventricle then has to pump against this high pressure and eventually fails too. Signs of right heart failure: raised JVP, swollen ankles (pitting oedema), enlarged liver, ascites.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Aortic stenosis — Aortic stenosis affects the left ventricle first; it can lead to right failure, but it is not the commonest cause.
• Hyperthyroidism — Hyperthyroidism can cause high-output failure but is not the commonest cause of right failure.
• Anemia — Anaemia can cause high-output failure but is uncommon as a cause of right failure.

⭐ Key point: Right heart failure caused by LUNG disease (COPD, PE, pulmonary hypertension) is called cor pulmonale.$x$, null),
(69, $x$One of these is an indication for a permanent pacemaker$x$, $x$Complete heart block$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Complete heart block
Complete (third-degree) heart block means NO impulses pass from the atria to the ventricles. The ventricles beat on their own slow escape rhythm (often 30–40/min), causing dizziness, fainting (Stokes–Adams attacks), heart failure or sudden death. Because it rarely recovers (except after an inferior MI or from drugs), a PERMANENT pacemaker is the definitive treatment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tachyarrthymias — Tachyarrhythmias are usually treated with drugs, ablation or an ICD, not a standard pacemaker.
• Carotid sinus syndrome — Carotid sinus syndrome can need a pacemaker in severe cases, but complete heart block is the classic, definite indication.
• Myocardial infarction — An MI alone is not an indication; temporary pacing may be needed for some blocks after MI.

⭐ Key point: Indications for a permanent pacemaker: complete heart block, Mobitz II block, symptomatic sick sinus syndrome, and symptomatic bradycardia without a reversible cause.$x$, null),
(70, $x$a 62 years old male patient has an ECG done that shows PR interval to be fixed, but drop QRS randomly. It indiciates?$x$, $x$second degree type-II block$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: second degree type-II block
Second-degree TYPE II (Mobitz II) heart block: the PR interval stays the SAME (fixed) for each conducted beat, but suddenly a P wave is NOT followed by a QRS (a dropped beat), without warning. The block is usually below the AV node (in the His–Purkinje system), so it can suddenly progress to complete heart block. It needs pacing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• first degree block — First-degree block has a long PR (above 0.20 s) but NO dropped beats.
• second degree type-I block — Second-degree type I (Wenckebach) has a PR that gets LONGER and LONGER until a beat drops, then the cycle repeats.
• third degree block — Third-degree block has NO relationship between P waves and QRS complexes (they march independently).

⭐ Key point: Wenckebach = "longer, longer, longer, drop" (usually benign). Mobitz II = "fixed PR, sudden drop" (dangerous; prepare for pacing).$x$, null),
(71, $x$A patient with a permanent pacemaker shows a rhythm strip with pacer spikes not followed by a QRS complex. This indicates:$x$, $x$Failure to capture — the pacemaker impulse is not depolarising the myocardium$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Failure to capture — the pacemaker impulse is not depolarising the myocardium
Each pacing spike should be immediately followed by a QRS complex (ventricular pacing) or a P wave (atrial pacing). This means the impulse has "captured" the heart. A spike with NO QRS after it is FAILURE TO CAPTURE: the electrical output is not making the heart muscle contract. Causes: lead displacement, a lead fracture, energy output too low, scar tissue at the lead tip, low battery, high potassium, or drugs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal pacemaker function — Normal function means every spike is followed by a complex.
• Battery is fully charged with no issue — A battery problem is one possible CAUSE of failure to capture; the strip does not show a healthy battery.
• Over-sensing with no clinical relevance — Over-sensing means the pacemaker detects signals it shouldn't and fails to pace (no spikes when needed); it is clinically important.

⭐ Key point: Pacemaker problems: failure to PACE (no spike), failure to CAPTURE (spike with no complex), and failure to SENSE (spikes in the wrong place). Check the patient's pulse and BP first, then call for help.$x$, null),
(72, $x$It is a flexible catheter, inserted into the femoral artery and passed into the descending aorta 1-2 cm below the left subclavian artery and slightly above the renal arteries.$x$, $x$Intra-aortic balloon pump$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Intra-aortic balloon pump
The intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP) is a long balloon catheter inserted through the femoral artery into the descending thoracic aorta. The tip sits just below the left subclavian artery, and the balloon ends above the renal arteries. It INFLATES in diastole (pushing blood back into the coronary arteries, improving heart muscle oxygen) and DEFLATES just before systole (lowering afterload so the heart pumps more easily). It is used in cardiogenic shock and severe ischaemia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pulmonary Artery Wedge Pressure — PAWP is a pressure measurement, not a device.
• Pulmonary Artery Catheter — A pulmonary artery catheter goes through a vein into the right heart and pulmonary artery, not into the aorta.
• None of the above — One of the options (IABP) does fit the description, so "none" is wrong.

⭐ Key point: IABP nursing: check leg pulses, colour and warmth (limb ischaemia); check the LEFT radial pulse (the tip may block the left subclavian); check urine output (it may block the renal arteries); keep the leg straight; watch for bleeding.$x$, null),
(73, $x$Which one of the following is NOT a function of pulmonary arterial catheter (PAC) which is also called Swan GANZ catheter?$x$, $x$Measurement of right atrium pressure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Measurement of right atrium pressure
The pulmonary artery catheter is placed mainly to measure pressures in the pulmonary artery, the wedge pressure (which estimates left ventricular filling) and cardiac output. The question's answer key marks "measurement of right atrium pressure" as NOT its function. The reason: right atrial pressure (CVP) is usually measured with an ordinary central venous catheter, and measuring it is not the reason for placing a Swan-Ganz. (Strictly, the PAC's proximal port can show RA pressure too, so read questions like this carefully.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Continuous cardiac output monitoring — Continuous cardiac output monitoring IS a PAC function (thermodilution).
• Measurement of pulmonary artery pressure — Measuring pulmonary artery pressure IS the main PAC function.
• Estimation of diastole filling of left ventricle — Estimating left ventricular diastolic filling IS a PAC function, through the wedge pressure (PAWP).

⭐ Key point: PAC measures PA pressure (15–25/8–15), PAWP (6–12, left heart filling), cardiac output (4–8 L/min) and SvO₂ (60–80%).$x$, null),
(74, $x$The first-line vasopressor of choice in septic shock is:$x$, $x$Norepinephrine$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Norepinephrine
Noradrenaline (norepinephrine) is the first-line vasopressor in septic shock (Surviving Sepsis Campaign). Septic shock is a VASODILATED shock (low SVR). Noradrenaline mainly stimulates alpha-1 receptors, tightening blood vessels and raising MAP, with less effect on heart rate. The target is MAP of 65 mmHg or more after fluids. Ideally it runs through a central line, but it can be started peripherally in a big vein for a short time.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dopamine — Dopamine causes more arrhythmias and higher death rates than noradrenaline; it is no longer first-line.
• Adrenaline — Adrenaline is added as a second or third agent (or for low cardiac output), not first-line.
• Vasopressin — Vasopressin is ADDED to noradrenaline (0.03 units/min) to reduce the noradrenaline dose, not used first.

⭐ Key point: Sepsis Hour-1 bundle: lactate, blood cultures, antibiotics, 30 mL/kg crystalloid for low BP, and noradrenaline for MAP below 65.$x$, null),
(75, $x$A trauma patient in hypovolaemic shock has lost an estimated 35% of their circulating blood volume. According to classification of haemorrhagic shock, this places them in:$x$, $x$Class III$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Class III
Blood loss classes (ATLS): Class I up to 15%, Class II 15–30%, Class III 30–40%, Class IV over 40%. A loss of 35% falls into CLASS III (about 1500–2000 mL in a 70 kg adult). In Class III, the pulse is above 120, systolic BP FALLS, breathing is fast, urine output is low (5–15 mL/h) and the patient is anxious or confused. They need blood transfusion, not just crystalloid.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Class I — Class I (under 15%) has almost normal vital signs.
• Class II — Class II (15–30%) has tachycardia and a narrow pulse pressure, but the BP is usually still normal.
• Class IV — Class IV (over 40%) is immediately life-threatening, with very low BP, lethargy and almost no urine.

⭐ Key point: A falling BP is a LATE sign of blood loss (Class III). Tachycardia, narrowed pulse pressure, cool skin and slow capillary refill come first.$x$, null),
(76, $x$Early (warm) septic shock differs from hypovolaemic shock by all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Tachycardia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Tachycardia
The question asks which feature does NOT differ. Both early (warm) septic shock and hypovolaemic shock cause TACHYCARDIA, so it does not help tell them apart. What differs is the skin: in warm sepsis, the blood vessels are dilated, so the skin is warm, flushed and well-perfused. In hypovolaemic shock, the vessels are clamped down, so the skin is cold, pale and clammy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Warm extremities — Warm extremities ARE different: warm in sepsis, cold in hypovolaemia.
• Flushed skin — Flushed skin IS different: flushed in sepsis, pale in hypovolaemia.
• Well-perfused extremities — Well-perfused extremities ARE different (sepsis) from poorly perfused ones (hypovolaemia).

⭐ Key point: Warm shock (low SVR) = septic, anaphylactic and neurogenic. Cold shock (high SVR) = hypovolaemic, cardiogenic and obstructive. Late septic shock can also become cold.$x$, null),
(77, $x$What is a common cause of acute kidney injury (AKI) in the ICU?$x$, $x$All of the above$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: All of the above
All three are common causes of AKI in ICU, and often several act together. Sepsis is the commonest cause: it causes low BP, inflammation and microcirculation damage. Dehydration or hypovolaemia (bleeding, vomiting, diuretics) reduces kidney blood flow (pre-renal AKI). Nephrotoxic drugs damage the tubules: aminoglycosides (gentamicin), vancomycin, NSAIDs, IV contrast, amphotericin and ACE inhibitors in dehydration.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sepsis — Sepsis alone is a cause, but not the only one listed.
• Dehydration — Dehydration alone is a cause, but not the only one listed.
• Nephrotoxic drugs — Nephrotoxic drugs alone are a cause, but not the only one listed.

⭐ Key point: Prevent AKI: keep MAP of 65 or more, keep the patient well hydrated, avoid or monitor nephrotoxic drugs (check trough levels), and watch urine output hourly (aim for at least 0.5 mL/kg/h).$x$, null),
(78, $x$Kidney failure is a potential postoperative complication in the ICU. Which laboratory test would be most indicative of kidney function?$x$, $x$Creatinine levels$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Creatinine levels
Creatinine is a waste product of muscle that the kidneys filter at a steady rate. When kidney function falls, creatinine RISES in the blood. It is the standard simple test of kidney function and is used to estimate GFR and to diagnose AKI (KDIGO: a rise of 26.5 µmol/L or more within 48 hours, or 1.5 times baseline, or urine below 0.5 mL/kg/h for 6 hours).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Glucose levels — Glucose measures diabetes control, not kidney function.
• Potassium levels — Potassium often rises in kidney failure and is important to monitor, but it is not the best measure of kidney function itself.
• Sodium levels — Sodium reflects water balance, not kidney filtration.

⭐ Key point: Creatinine rises LATE (24–48 hours after injury). Falling urine output is often the EARLIEST sign of AKI, so measure it hourly.$x$, null),
(79, $x$A patient with hyperkalemia has peaked T waves. After calcium gluconate, which drug shifts potassium intracellularly?$x$, $x$Albuterol$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Albuterol
After calcium gluconate protects the heart, the next step is to SHIFT potassium from the blood into the cells. Albuterol (salbutamol), a beta-2 agonist, does this by stimulating the sodium-potassium pump, lowering K⁺ by about 0.5–1 mmol/L within 30 minutes. It is given by nebuliser (10–20 mg). Insulin with glucose works the same way and is the most reliable shifting drug.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sodium polystyrene — Sodium polystyrene (Kayexalate/calcium resonium) REMOVES potassium through the gut, slowly (hours); it doesn't shift it.
• Furosemide — Furosemide REMOVES potassium in the urine; it doesn't shift it into cells.
• Fludrocortisone — Fludrocortisone increases potassium loss over days; it is not used acutely.

⭐ Key point: Order for high potassium: 1) PROTECT the heart (calcium gluconate 10%), 2) SHIFT K⁺ into cells (insulin and glucose, salbutamol), 3) REMOVE K⁺ (dialysis, resins, diuretics).$x$, null),
(80, $x$In Arteriovenous fistula:$x$, $x$There is mixture of arterial and venous blood$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: There is mixture of arterial and venous blood
An arteriovenous (AV) fistula is a surgical joining of an ARTERY directly to a VEIN, usually in the forearm (radial artery to cephalic vein). Arterial blood at high pressure flows straight into the vein, so the vein contains a mixture of arterial and venous blood. The vein enlarges and its wall thickens ("matures" over 6–12 weeks), so it can be needled for haemodialysis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• There is mixture of arterial blood — It is not only arterial blood; the blood goes into a vein.
• There is mixture of venous blood — It is not only venous blood; arterial blood enters the vein directly.
• There is mixture of artery and capillary blood — Capillaries are bypassed in a fistula; there is no artery–capillary mixing.

⭐ Key point: Fistula care: feel for the THRILL (buzz) and listen for the BRUIT (whoosh) daily. Never take BP, put a cannula in, or take blood from the fistula arm.$x$, null),
(81, $x$A hemodialysis patient develops sudden hypotension, nausea, headache, and confusion approximately 3 hours into a first dialysis session. This presentation is most consistent with:$x$, $x$Dialysis disequilibrium syndrome$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Dialysis disequilibrium syndrome
Dialysis disequilibrium syndrome happens during or just after the FIRST dialysis sessions, especially when urea is very high and is removed too quickly. Urea falls fast in the blood but slowly in the brain, so water moves INTO brain cells (cerebral oedema). Signs: headache, nausea, vomiting, restlessness, confusion, hypotension, seizures, even coma. Prevent it with short, slow first sessions (low blood flow) and a gradual urea reduction.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anaphylaxis to the dialyzer membrane — Dialyser anaphylaxis happens within MINUTES of starting, with wheeze, urticaria and itching.
• Air embolism — Air embolism causes sudden breathlessness, chest pain and collapse, not slowly developing headache and confusion.
• Hyperkalemic cardiac arrest — Hyperkalaemia improves during dialysis (K⁺ is removed), so cardiac arrest from high K⁺ is unlikely 3 hours in.

⭐ Key point: Treat by slowing or stopping dialysis and giving osmotic agents (mannitol or hypertonic saline) if severe. Monitor neuro status during first sessions.$x$, null),
(82, $x$Hypothermia is a common complication of CRRT because:$x$, $x$Extracorporeal blood is exposed to room-temperature tubing, filters, and replacement fluids, causing heat loss$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Extracorporeal blood is exposed to room-temperature tubing, filters, and replacement fluids, causing heat loss
In CRRT, blood leaves the body and runs through long tubing and a filter at room temperature, and large volumes of replacement fluid or dialysate (often several litres per hour) are added. The blood loses heat outside the body and on mixing with cooler fluid, so the patient's temperature falls. Hypothermia can also HIDE a fever, so infection may be missed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CRRT machines actively cool the blood intentionally at all times — CRRT machines do not deliberately cool the blood.
• Patients are typically febrile at baseline — Patients may or may not have fever; this does not explain the cooling.
• The dialysate is always heated above body temperature — Dialysate is warmed TO body temperature (not above it) to reduce heat loss.

⭐ Key point: Prevent CRRT hypothermia with the fluid warmer and blankets or forced-air warmers. Monitor temperature hourly, and remember a normal temperature on CRRT does not rule out infection.$x$, null),
(83, $x$A patient opens their eyes only to painful stimuli, makes incomprehensible sounds, and withdraws from pain. What is the calculated GCS score?$x$, $x$8$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 8
Add up the three parts of the GCS. EYES open only to pain = E2. VERBAL: incomprehensible sounds (moaning, groaning, no words) = V2. MOTOR: withdraws from pain (pulls the limb away) = M4. Total = 2 + 2 + 4 = 8. A GCS of 8 is a severe brain injury, and the airway may need protection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 6 — 6 would mean a lower motor or verbal score (for example abnormal flexion, M3).
• 10 — 10 would need higher scores, such as confused words (V4) or localising pain (M5).
• 12 — 12 would need much better scores, such as eyes opening to voice and localising pain.

⭐ Key point: GCS: Eyes 1–4, Verbal 1–5, Motor 1–6 (total 3–15). Motor: 6 obeys, 5 localises, 4 withdraws, 3 abnormal flexion (decorticate), 2 extension (decerebrate), 1 none.$x$, null),
(84, $x$A GCS below what value is generally used as an indication for intubation?$x$, $x$<8$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: <8
A GCS of 8 or less is the general sign that the patient cannot protect their own airway. The tongue may fall back and block the airway, the gag and cough reflexes are weak, and the patient may aspirate vomit. Intubation protects the airway, prevents aspiration, and allows control of oxygen and CO₂ (important in head injury). Say "GCS less than 8, intubate".

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• <12 — Below 12 is moderate impairment; many patients can still protect their airway.
• <10 — Below 10 is too high a threshold for routine intubation.
• <5 — Below 5 is too low; waiting this long risks aspiration and hypoxic brain damage.

⭐ Key point: Also intubate for a rapidly falling GCS, poor oxygenation or ventilation, seizures that can't be controlled, or before transfer of a deteriorating head-injured patient.$x$, null),
(85, $x$The following statement regarding extradural haematoma are true EXCEPT$x$, $x$Prognosis is poor$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Prognosis is poor
The question asks which statement is NOT true. Extradural haematoma usually has a GOOD prognosis if it is found and drained quickly, because the brain beneath is often not badly damaged. The statement "prognosis is poor" is therefore false. Delay is what makes it fatal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It is often caused by a skull fracture with laceration of the middle meningeal artery — It IS usually caused by a skull fracture tearing the middle meningeal artery (true).
• It commonly occurs at the pterion — It DOES commonly occur at the pterion, the thin part of the temporal skull (true).
• There is often a lucid interval — There IS often a lucid interval: knocked out, wakes up and talks, then quickly deteriorates (true).

⭐ Key point: Extradural = arterial bleed, lens-shaped (biconvex) on CT, lucid interval, emergency surgery. Subdural = venous bleed, crescent-shaped, often in elderly and alcoholics.$x$, null),
(86, $x$Cushing's triad, a late sign of rising intracranial pressure, consists of which three findings?$x$, $x$Hypertension with widened pulse pressure, bradycardia, and irregular respirations$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hypertension with widened pulse pressure, bradycardia, and irregular respirations
Cushing's triad is a LATE sign of raised ICP and approaching brainstem herniation. 1) HYPERTENSION with a WIDE pulse pressure: the body raises BP to push blood into the brain against high pressure. 2) BRADYCARDIA: a reflex response to the high BP and brainstem compression. 3) IRREGULAR breathing (Cheyne–Stokes, apnoea): compression of the breathing centre in the brainstem.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypotension, tachycardia, and tachypnea — Low BP, fast pulse and fast breathing describe SHOCK, not raised ICP.
• Hypertension with tachycardia and hyperthermia — High BP with fast pulse and fever does not fit; the pulse is SLOW in Cushing's triad.
• Hypotension, bradycardia, and apnea — Low BP, slow pulse and apnoea is a pre-terminal picture, not Cushing's triad.

⭐ Key point: Cushing's triad means herniation is near. Call for help at once and prepare for urgent ICP-lowering treatment (osmotherapy, neurosurgery).$x$, null),
(87, $x$The Kellie-Monro (Monro-Kellie) Doctrine states which of the following?$x$, $x$The skull is a rigid, non-expansile compartment; an increase in volume of one intracranial component requires a reciprocal decrease in another to keep ICP constant$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The skull is a rigid, non-expansile compartment; an increase in volume of one intracranial component requires a reciprocal decrease in another to keep ICP constant
The Monro–Kellie doctrine says the skull is a rigid, closed box containing three things: brain (about 80%), blood (about 10%) and CSF (about 10%). The total volume must stay constant. If one part increases (a tumour, a bleed, swelling), another must decrease, for example CSF is pushed into the spinal canal or venous blood leaves. When these compensations run out, ICP rises steeply.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The brain can expand infinitely without pressure changes — The brain cannot expand freely inside the rigid skull.
• CSF volume is fixed and cannot change — CSF volume does change; displacing CSF is the first compensation.
• Cerebral blood flow is independent of ICP — Cerebral blood flow depends on CPP (MAP − ICP), so it is not independent of ICP.

⭐ Key point: The ICP volume–pressure curve is flat at first, then steep. Once compensation is used up, a small extra volume (coughing, suction) causes a big rise in ICP.$x$, null),
(88, $x$Signs of raised intracranial pressure include all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Loss of hearing$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Loss of hearing
The question asks for the sign that does NOT belong. Loss of hearing is not a sign of raised ICP. Raised ICP classically causes headache that is worse in the morning (CO₂ rises during sleep and on lying down), nausea and vomiting (often projectile), papilloedema (swollen optic discs), visual changes, a falling GCS, pupil changes and Cushing's triad late.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Early-morning headache — Early-morning headache IS a classic sign of raised ICP.
• Nausea and vomiting — Nausea and vomiting ARE classic signs of raised ICP.
• Papilloedema — Papilloedema IS a classic sign of raised ICP.

⭐ Key point: The EARLIEST and most sensitive sign of raised ICP is a change in the level of consciousness (restlessness, confusion, drowsiness).$x$, null),
(89, $x$The signs of an upper motor neuron lesion include all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Fibrillations$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fibrillations
The question asks for the sign that does NOT belong. Fibrillations (tiny spontaneous twitches of single muscle fibres, seen on EMG) and fasciculations are signs of a LOWER motor neurone lesion, not an upper one. Upper motor neurone lesions (brain or spinal cord: stroke, MS, spinal injury) cause spasticity, increased (brisk) reflexes, clonus and a positive Babinski sign, with little muscle wasting.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Muscular spasticity — Spasticity IS an upper motor neurone sign.
• Brisk deep tendon reflexes — Brisk deep tendon reflexes ARE an upper motor neurone sign.
• Positive Babinski response — A positive Babinski sign (big toe goes up) IS an upper motor neurone sign.

⭐ Key point: UMN = spastic, brisk reflexes, upgoing toe. LMN = floppy (flaccid), lost reflexes, wasting, fasciculations and fibrillations (as in GBS, polio and peripheral nerve injury).$x$, null),
(90, $x$A Licox brain tissue oxygen monitor measures which of the following?$x$, $x$Brain tissue oxygen tension (PbtO2)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Brain tissue oxygen tension (PbtO2)
The Licox probe is placed in the brain tissue (white matter) and measures the partial pressure of oxygen there (PbtO₂), and also brain temperature. It shows whether the brain is getting enough oxygen. Normal PbtO₂ is about 20–35 mmHg; below 20 needs treatment and below 10–15 means serious brain hypoxia with a poor outcome.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Intracranial pressure only — ICP is measured by a separate probe or an EVD; Licox measures oxygen.
• Cerebral blood flow velocity — Cerebral blood flow velocity is measured by transcranial Doppler.
• Serum oxygen saturation — Serum (arterial) oxygen saturation is measured by an ABG or pulse oximeter.

⭐ Key point: To improve low PbtO₂: raise FiO₂, raise CPP (MAP), treat high ICP, correct anaemia, avoid hyperventilation (low CO₂), and treat fever and seizures.$x$, null),
(91, $x$Causes of Brain death are EXCEPT$x$, $x$Good brain oxygenation.$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Good brain oxygenation.
The question asks which is NOT a cause of brain death. Good brain oxygenation keeps brain cells alive; it is the opposite of what causes brain death. Brain death happens when the whole brain, including the brainstem, is irreversibly destroyed, usually by severe injury, swelling or loss of blood and oxygen supply.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An acute catastrophic brain injury. — An acute catastrophic brain injury (trauma, a huge bleed) IS a cause.
• Abrupt loss of cerebral perfusion — Abrupt loss of cerebral perfusion (very high ICP stopping blood flow) IS a cause.
• Extracranial brain injury, as seen in patients following cardiopulmonary arrest — Brain injury from outside the skull, such as lack of oxygen after cardiac arrest (hypoxic-ischaemic injury), IS a cause.

⭐ Key point: Before testing for brain death, exclude reversible causes: sedative drugs, hypothermia, severe metabolic or endocrine problems, and neuromuscular blockers.$x$, null),
(92, $x$Brudzinski's sign is positive when which of the following occurs?$x$, $x$Involuntary flexion of the hips and knees when the examiner passively flexes the patient's neck$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Involuntary flexion of the hips and knees when the examiner passively flexes the patient's neck
Brudzinski's sign tests for meningeal irritation (meningitis, subarachnoid haemorrhage). With the patient lying flat, the examiner gently bends the patient's head forward toward the chest. If the patient's hips and knees bend up on their own (involuntarily), the sign is positive. Bending the neck stretches the inflamed meninges, and the body bends the legs to reduce the stretch.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pain on hip flexion with knee extension — Pain on hip flexion with the knee straight describes a different test (Kernig's sign checks pain or resistance when straightening the knee with the hip bent).
• Dilation of the pupils with light exposure — Pupils dilating with light is abnormal but has nothing to do with Brudzinski's sign (pupils normally constrict).
• Rigid extension of the leg — A rigid straight leg is not the Brudzinski response.

⭐ Key point: Meningitis signs: neck stiffness, photophobia, fever, headache, Kernig's and Brudzinski's signs, and a purpuric rash in meningococcal disease. Give antibiotics without delay and use droplet precautions.$x$, null),
(93, $x$What is the recommended treatment for a patient with Guillain-Barré syndrome?$x$, $x$Plasmapheresis or immunoglobulin therapy$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Plasmapheresis or immunoglobulin therapy
Guillain–Barré syndrome is an autoimmune attack on peripheral nerves, often after an infection. Proven treatments that shorten the illness and improve recovery are PLASMA EXCHANGE (plasmapheresis, which removes the harmful antibodies) or IV IMMUNOGLOBULIN (IVIG, 0.4 g/kg/day for 5 days). Both work equally well; they are not given together. Supportive ICU care (breathing, autonomic monitoring, DVT prevention, pain control, nutrition) is essential.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Antibiotics — Antibiotics do not treat the immune nerve damage (the triggering infection has usually passed).
• Hospitalization for observation — Observation alone is not enough for significant weakness; specific treatment should start early.
• No treatment is required — GBS can paralyse the breathing muscles, so leaving it untreated is dangerous.

⭐ Key point: Steroids do NOT help GBS. Monitor FVC every 4 hours: below 20 mL/kg needs ICU; below 15 mL/kg usually needs intubation.$x$, null),
(94, $x$What are common initial presenting symptoms of Myasthenia Gravis?$x$, $x$Ptosis and diplopia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Ptosis and diplopia
Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disease in which antibodies block the acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction. Muscles tire quickly with use and recover with rest. The EYE muscles are usually affected first: drooping eyelids (PTOSIS) and double vision (DIPLOPIA) are the commonest first symptoms (in about 2 out of 3 patients). Weakness is typically worse at the end of the day.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Foot drop — Foot drop is more typical of a peripheral nerve problem (such as common peroneal nerve palsy).
• Urinary retention — Urinary retention is not a feature of myasthenia (smooth muscle is spared).
• Severe headache — Severe headache is not a feature of myasthenia.

⭐ Key point: In a myasthenic crisis (breathing or swallowing failure), monitor FVC, protect the airway, and give IVIG or plasma exchange. Avoid aminoglycosides and magnesium.$x$, null),
(95, $x$A tetanus patient should ideally be nursed in an environment that is:$x$, $x$Quiet and dark, avoiding unnecessary stimulation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Quiet and dark, avoiding unnecessary stimulation
In tetanus, the toxin (tetanospasmin) blocks the nerves that normally relax muscles. Any stimulus — noise, light, touch, even a draught — can trigger painful, violent muscle spasms, including laryngospasm, which can stop breathing. The patient is therefore nursed in a QUIET, DARK (dim) room, with minimal handling and procedures grouped together, usually with sedation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bright and stimulating — A bright, stimulating environment would trigger spasms.
• Noisy, for constant communication — Noise triggers spasms and must be avoided.
• Cold, to reduce metabolic rate — Cold can trigger spasms and does not help; temperature should be kept comfortable.

⭐ Key point: Tetanus treatment: wound debridement, tetanus immunoglobulin, metronidazole, benzodiazepines and magnesium for spasms, ventilation if needed, and the tetanus vaccine (the illness does not give immunity).$x$, null),
(96, $x$What is the first-line pharmacologic treatment for status epilepticus?$x$, $x$IV benzodiazepine (e.g., lorazepam)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: IV benzodiazepine (e.g., lorazepam)
Status epilepticus (a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, or repeated seizures without recovery) is a medical emergency. The FIRST-line drug is an IV BENZODIAZEPINE, for example lorazepam 4 mg IV, which can be repeated once after 5–10 minutes. Alternatives without IV access: buccal midazolam or rectal diazepam. Benzodiazepines boost the calming GABA system and stop most seizures quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral phenytoin — Oral phenytoin is too slow; phenytoin (or levetiracetam or valproate) is given IV as SECOND-line.
• IV mannitol — Mannitol lowers ICP but does not stop seizures.
• IV vancomycin — Vancomycin is an antibiotic; it might be used for meningitis but does not stop seizures.

⭐ Key point: The steps are: ABC, oxygen, check glucose (give dextrose if low), benzodiazepine, then a second-line IV drug, then anaesthesia (propofol or thiopentone) with intubation if seizures continue past 20–30 minutes.$x$, null),
(97, $x$Which sedative agent is preferred over benzodiazepines in mechanically ventilated ICU patients, in part due to its lower associated delirium risk?$x$, $x$Dexmedetomidine$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Dexmedetomidine
Dexmedetomidine is an alpha-2 agonist that gives light, rousable sedation: the patient is calm but can wake and follow commands. It does not depress breathing. Studies show less delirium and shorter ventilation than with benzodiazepines (MENDS, SEDCOM). Guidelines prefer propofol or dexmedetomidine over benzodiazepines for ventilated ICU patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lorazepam — Lorazepam is a benzodiazepine that increases delirium.
• Midazolam — Midazolam is a benzodiazepine that builds up (especially in kidney failure) and increases delirium.
• Diazepam — Diazepam is a long-acting benzodiazepine that also increases delirium.

⭐ Key point: Dexmedetomidine side effects are bradycardia and hypotension. Benzodiazepines are mainly used for seizures and alcohol withdrawal.$x$, null),
(98, $x$Non-pharmacological management of delirium focuses on risk factors such as:$x$, $x$All of the above$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: All of the above
Delirium usually has several causes at once, so non-drug prevention tackles all the risk factors together. That includes treating dehydration and constipation, protecting sleep (night-time quiet, dim lights, clustered care), reorienting the patient (clock, calendar, glasses, hearing aids, family), communicating clearly, early mobilisation, good pain control, adequate nutrition, and removing catheters and lines early. All the groups listed are correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dehydration, constipation and sleep deprivation — This group is part of the answer but not all of it.
• Reorientation, clear communication and immobility — This group is part of the answer but not all of it.
• Pain control, nutrition and early removal of catheters — This group is part of the answer but not all of it.

⭐ Key point: A useful memory aid for the causes of delirium is "PINCH ME": Pain, Infection, Nutrition, Constipation, Hydration, Medication, Environment. Screen every shift with CAM-ICU.$x$, null),
(99, $x$How is TENS distinguished from Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)?$x$, $x$Percentage of body surface area with epidermal detachment (TEN greater than 30%, SJS less than 10%)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Percentage of body surface area with epidermal detachment (TEN greater than 30%, SJS less than 10%)
SJS and TEN are the same disease at different severities, so the difference is the PERCENTAGE of body surface area where the epidermis peels off. SJS is less than 10%, the SJS/TEN overlap is 10–30%, and TEN is more than 30%. Both are usually drug-induced, with fever, painful skin, mucosal ulcers (mouth, eyes, genitals) and a positive Nikolsky sign. TEN has a much higher death rate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Causative organism — The causative agent is usually the same type (drugs such as carbamazepine, phenytoin, allopurinol, sulphonamides, nevirapine), so it doesn't separate them.
• Patient age only — Both can occur at any age.
• Presence of fever — Fever occurs in both, so it doesn't distinguish them.

⭐ Key point: Stop the causing drug immediately. Manage like a major burn: warm room, fluids, non-stick dressings, eye and mouth care, nutrition and pain relief, ideally in a burns unit or ICU.$x$, null),
(100, $x$How is Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis Syndrome (TENS) best classified?$x$, $x$A severe, life-threatening mucocutaneous drug reaction with widespread epidermal detachment$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A severe, life-threatening mucocutaneous drug reaction with widespread epidermal detachment
Toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN) is a severe, life-threatening drug reaction that affects the skin AND mucous membranes. The top layer of skin (epidermis) dies and peels off in sheets over more than 30% of the body, leaving raw areas like a scald burn. The patient loses fluid, protein and heat, and is at high risk of infection and sepsis. The death rate is 25–35% or more.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A viral exanthem — It is not a viral rash (exanthem), although infections can occasionally trigger it.
• A bacterial skin infection — It is not a bacterial skin infection; it is an immune reaction to a drug.
• A chronic autoimmune skin condition unrelated to medications — It is an acute reaction, usually caused by a medication, not a chronic autoimmune disease.

⭐ Key point: Use the SCORTEN score to predict death risk. Care includes barrier nursing, warming, fluids, eye care by an ophthalmologist, and stopping all non-essential drugs.$x$, null),
(101, $x$A patient is on a ventilator for respiratory failure. The nurse knows that a high pressure alarm on the ventilator may indicate:$x$, $x$A kinked endotracheal tube or the patient is coughing$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A kinked endotracheal tube or the patient is coughing
The high-pressure alarm sounds when the pressure needed to deliver the breath rises above the set limit. Something is blocking or resisting airflow: a kinked or bitten ETT, secretions (needs suction), coughing, the patient fighting the ventilator, bronchospasm, a pneumothorax, or water in the tubing. Check the patient first, then the tube, then the circuit.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A leak in the circuit — A leak in the circuit causes a LOW-pressure alarm (pressure can't build up).
• The patient is disconnected from the ventilator — Disconnection causes a LOW-pressure or low-volume alarm.
• The patient's lung has re-expanded — Re-expansion of the lung would improve compliance and lower pressure, not trigger a high-pressure alarm.

⭐ Key point: HIGH pressure = something blocking (kink, secretions, cough, biting, bronchospasm, pneumothorax). LOW pressure = something leaking (disconnection, cuff leak, circuit leak).$x$, null),
(102, $x$End-tidal CO2 (capnography) waveform loss or a sudden flat waveform in an intubated patient most urgently suggests:$x$, $x$Tube displacement/dislodgement (e.g., esophageal intubation or extubation) or complete obstruction$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Tube displacement/dislodgement (e.g., esophageal intubation or extubation) or complete obstruction
Waveform capnography shows CO₂ in every breath. A normal square waveform proves the tube is in the trachea and the patient is being ventilated. A SUDDEN loss or flat line in an intubated patient most urgently means: the tube has come out or moved into the oesophagus (no CO₂ from the lungs), the tube is completely blocked, the circuit has disconnected, or cardiac arrest. Check the patient at once ("no trace = wrong place").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal ventilator function — Normal ventilator function would show a regular square waveform, not a flat line.
• Improved lung compliance — Better lung compliance would still show a normal waveform.
• Adequate ventilation — Adequate ventilation shows CO₂ on every breath; a flat line means none is reaching the sensor.

⭐ Key point: Respond with DOPE (Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment): check the tube position, the patient's chest movement and the pulse, and hand-ventilate with 100% O₂.$x$, null),
(103, $x$When intubating a patient with suspected cervical spine injury, the recommended technique to protect the spine is:$x$, $x$Manual in-line stabilization with the anterior portion of the cervical collar removed during the procedure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Manual in-line stabilization with the anterior portion of the cervical collar removed during the procedure
In suspected cervical spine injury, the head and neck must not move during intubation. An assistant holds the head in line with the body, using both hands on each side (manual in-line stabilisation, MILS). The FRONT of the hard collar is opened or removed so the mouth can open wide enough for the laryngoscope. Video laryngoscopes help get a good view with less neck movement.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Full neck extension for optimal glottic view — Full neck extension could worsen a spinal injury and cause paralysis.
• Flexion of the neck — Flexing the neck could also move an unstable fracture.
• Leaving the patient fully upright — Sitting the patient upright does not protect the spine and makes intubation harder.

⭐ Key point: Treat every major trauma patient as having a C-spine injury until it is cleared. Use jaw thrust (not head tilt) to open the airway.$x$, null),
(104, $x$Signs of weaning intolerance during a trial include all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Improved mentation and stable heart rate$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Improved mentation and stable heart rate
The question asks which is NOT a sign of failing a weaning trial. Improved mentation (more alert) with a stable heart rate is a GOOD sign that the patient is tolerating the trial. Signs of failure include fast breathing (RR above 35), accessory muscle use, see-saw (paradoxical) breathing, sweating, anxiety or agitation, a rise in HR or BP of more than 20%, falling SpO₂ (below 90%) and rising CO₂.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Use of accessory muscles and paradoxical (abdominal) breathing — Accessory muscle use and paradoxical breathing ARE signs of failure (respiratory muscle fatigue).
• Diaphoresis and anxiety — Sweating (diaphoresis) and anxiety ARE signs of failure.
• Significant increase in heart rate or blood pressure — A significant rise in HR or BP IS a sign of failure.

⭐ Key point: If a spontaneous breathing trial fails, put the patient back on comfortable support, look for the reason (fluid overload, secretions, weakness, low phosphate, sedation), and try again the next day.$x$, null),
(105, $x$If a ventilated patient acutely desaturates and the cause is not immediately identified, an appropriate first-line nursing action while troubleshooting is to:$x$, $x$Disconnect from the ventilator and manually ventilate with a bag-valve device on 100% oxygen while assessing for the cause$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Disconnect from the ventilator and manually ventilate with a bag-valve device on 100% oxygen while assessing for the cause
When a ventilated patient suddenly desaturates and you don't know why, take the ventilator out of the problem. DISCONNECT the patient and hand-ventilate with a bag-valve device on 100% oxygen. This checks whether the problem is the ventilator or the patient. Squeezing the bag also tells you about the airway: a hard bag suggests blockage or pneumothorax. Then work through DOPE and call for help.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Leave the patient on the ventilator and wait — Waiting while the patient's oxygen falls can lead to cardiac arrest.
• Immediately extubate — Extubating without a clear reason may remove a working airway and make things worse.
• Turn off all alarms to focus — Alarms are important information; turning them off is unsafe.

⭐ Key point: Keep a bag-valve-mask connected to oxygen at EVERY ventilated patient's bedside, always checked and ready.$x$, null),
(106, $x$Diffusion hypoxia after discontinuing nitrous oxide anaesthesia is best prevented by:$x$, $x$Administering 100% oxygen for several minutes at the end of the case before returning to room air$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Administering 100% oxygen for several minutes at the end of the case before returning to room air
Nitrous oxide is very soluble in blood. When it is stopped at the end of anaesthesia, large amounts rush OUT of the blood into the alveoli and dilute the oxygen there. If the patient breathes room air at that moment, the alveolar oxygen can drop dangerously, causing hypoxia (diffusion hypoxia, the Fink effect). Giving 100% oxygen for 5–10 minutes after stopping nitrous oxide prevents this.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Rapidly increasing volatile agent concentration — Increasing the volatile agent deepens anaesthesia and doesn't solve the oxygen dilution.
• Hyperventilating the patient with room air — Hyperventilating with room air would still give low oxygen.
• Administering naloxone — Naloxone reverses opioids; it doesn't prevent diffusion hypoxia.

⭐ Key point: Nitrous oxide also expands gas-filled spaces, so avoid it in pneumothorax, bowel obstruction, middle-ear surgery and air embolism.$x$, null),
(107, $x$A patient develops rapidly rising temperature, severe muscle rigidity, tachycardia, and hypercarbia shortly after receiving succinylcholine. The priority treatment is:$x$, $x$Dantrolene, which reduces calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Dantrolene, which reduces calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum
These signs (rapidly rising temperature, muscle rigidity including the jaw, fast heart rate, rising EtCO₂ and acidosis) after suxamethonium or a volatile gas are MALIGNANT HYPERTHERMIA. It is an inherited muscle disorder in which calcium floods out of the muscle stores, causing uncontrolled contraction and heat production. DANTROLENE 2.5 mg/kg IV (repeated as needed) is the specific treatment; it stops the calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Naloxone — Naloxone reverses opioids and has no effect here.
• Neostigmine — Neostigmine reverses non-depolarising muscle relaxants and does not treat MH.
• Flumazenil — Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines and has no effect here.

⭐ Key point: MH management: stop the trigger, call for help, give 100% oxygen at high flow, give dantrolene, cool the patient, treat high potassium and acidosis, and monitor urine (rhabdomyolysis). Warn the family about genetic testing.$x$, null),
(108, $x$What is Mendelson's syndrome?$x$, $x$Chemical pneumonitis resulting from aspiration of acidic gastric contents into the lungs$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Chemical pneumonitis resulting from aspiration of acidic gastric contents into the lungs
Mendelson's syndrome is a CHEMICAL pneumonitis caused by inhaling acidic stomach contents into the lungs, classically during general anaesthesia in obstetrics (Curtis Mendelson, 1946). Stomach acid (especially with pH below 2.5 and volume over 25 mL) burns the lung lining, causing wheeze, breathlessness, hypoxia and ARDS within hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bacterial pneumonia acquired during mechanical ventilation — VAP is a bacterial infection after 48 hours of ventilation, not chemical aspiration.
• An allergic reaction to inhalational anaesthetic agents — An allergic reaction to anaesthetic gases is a different problem.
• Pulmonary embolism occurring after prolonged bed rest — PE after bed rest is a blood clot, not aspiration.

⭐ Key point: Prevent aspiration with fasting, antacids or H₂ blockers (ranitidine or sodium citrate in obstetrics), rapid sequence induction, and extubating only when the patient is awake.$x$, null),
(109, $x$Pulmonary oxygen toxicity is most likely to develop with which exposure pattern?$x$, $x$FiO2 greater than about 60% sustained for more than 24-48 hours$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: FiO2 greater than about 60% sustained for more than 24-48 hours
Oxygen toxicity damages the lung lining when a high oxygen concentration (FiO₂ above about 60%) is breathed for a long time (more than 24–48 hours). Free radicals injure the alveoli, causing inflammation, fluid leak and eventually fibrosis, much like ARDS. Absorption collapse (atelectasis) can also happen, because nitrogen is washed out of the alveoli.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Brief exposure to 100% oxygen for less than 5 minutes during pre-oxygenation — A brief exposure to 100% oxygen (such as pre-oxygenation for intubation) is safe.
• FiO2 of 21% (room air) for any duration — Room air (21%) does not cause oxygen toxicity.
• Intermittent low-flow nasal cannula oxygen at 2 L/min — Low-flow 2 L/min nasal oxygen gives only about 28% and does not cause toxicity.

⭐ Key point: Give the LOWEST FiO₂ that achieves the target SpO₂ (usually 94–98%, or 88–92% in CO₂ retainers). Use PEEP to allow a lower FiO₂ in ventilated patients.$x$, null),
(110, $x$What is the currently recommended target temperature range for targeted temperature management after cardiac arrest?$x$, $x$32 to 36 degrees C$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 32 to 36 degrees C
After return of circulation following cardiac arrest, patients who remain unconscious are kept at a controlled temperature to protect the brain. Current guidance is a target between 32 and 36 °C for at least 24 hours, and above all to AVOID FEVER (above 37.7 °C) for at least 72 hours. Fever after cardiac arrest worsens brain damage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 40 to 42 degrees C — 40–42 °C is hyperthermia, which harms the brain.
• 20 to 25 degrees C — 20–25 °C is severe hypothermia and causes arrhythmias and cardiac arrest.
• 38 to 39 degrees C — 38–39 °C is fever, which is exactly what should be prevented.

⭐ Key point: During cooling, watch for shivering (treat with sedation or paralysis), bradycardia, low potassium (which rises on rewarming), high glucose, bleeding and infection. Rewarm slowly, at about 0.25–0.5 °C per hour.$x$, null),
(111, $x$Which parameter setting is primarily responsible for oxygenation rather than ventilation (CO2 removal)?$x$, $x$FiO2 and PEEP$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: FiO2 and PEEP
Oxygenation (getting O₂ into the blood) is controlled mainly by FiO₂ (the oxygen concentration) and PEEP (keeps the alveoli open for gas exchange and increases surface area). Ventilation (removing CO₂) is controlled by MINUTE ventilation: respiratory rate × tidal volume. So to raise a low PaO₂, increase FiO₂ or PEEP; to lower a high PaCO₂, increase the rate or tidal volume.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory rate — Respiratory rate mainly changes CO₂ removal (ventilation).
• Tidal volume — Tidal volume mainly changes CO₂ removal (ventilation).
• I:E ratio — I:E ratio has some effect on oxygenation but is not the main control; it is adjusted for special cases.

⭐ Key point: O₂ problem → change FiO₂ or PEEP. CO₂ problem → change rate or tidal volume. Memorise this for ventilator questions.$x$, null),
(112, $x$What is the standard adult IV/IO dose of epinephrine during cardiac arrest, repeated every 3 to 5 minutes?$x$, $x$1 mg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 1 mg
In adult cardiac arrest, the dose of adrenaline (epinephrine) is 1 mg IV or IO (10 mL of 1:10,000), repeated every 3–5 minutes (every other 2-minute CPR cycle). In non-shockable rhythms (asystole, PEA) give it as soon as possible. In VF/pulseless VT, give it after the 3rd shock. Flush with 20 mL of saline.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.1 mg — 0.1 mg is too small for an adult; doses of about this size are used for children (10 mcg/kg).
• 10 mg — 10 mg is ten times too high and dangerous.
• 100 mcg given only once — 100 mcg given once is too small and is not repeated as recommended.

⭐ Key point: Adrenaline 1 mg every 3–5 minutes. Amiodarone 300 mg after the 3rd shock, then 150 mg after the 5th. Anaphylaxis is different: 0.5 mg IM of 1:1000.$x$, null),
(113, $x$Which is a qSOFA criterion for sepsis?$x$, $x$All of the above$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: All of the above
qSOFA (quick SOFA) is a bedside screen outside ICU for patients with suspected infection who are at risk of poor outcomes. It has three items, scoring 1 each: respiratory rate of 22/min or more, systolic BP of 100 mmHg or less, and altered mental state (GCS below 15). A score of 2 or more suggests a higher risk of death and needs urgent review. All three listed criteria are part of qSOFA.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• RR >22/min — Fast breathing alone is only one of the three criteria.
• SBP <100 mmHg — Low systolic BP alone is only one of the three criteria.
• Altered mental status — Altered mental status alone is only one of the three criteria.

⭐ Key point: Remember qSOFA as "HAT": Hypotension (SBP ≤100), Altered mentation, Tachypnoea (RR ≥22). Do not wait for a high score to give antibiotics if sepsis is suspected.$x$, null),
(114, $x$What is the typical initial fluid resuscitation approach for a patient in hypovolemic shock?$x$, $x$A rapid bolus of isotonic crystalloid solution while closely monitoring the hemodynamic response$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A rapid bolus of isotonic crystalloid solution while closely monitoring the hemodynamic response
Hypovolaemic shock means there is not enough fluid in the blood vessels. The first treatment is to replace volume quickly with an isotonic crystalloid (0.9% saline or Hartmann's), for example a 500 mL bolus over 15 minutes (or 20 mL/kg), then reassess HR, BP, capillary refill, urine output and mental state. Repeat if needed. If the cause is bleeding, give BLOOD early and stop the bleeding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Withholding fluids until blood pressure normalizes spontaneously — Waiting for the BP to recover by itself lets organs suffer and the patient may die.
• Administering diuretics to reduce circulating volume — Diuretics REMOVE fluid and would make hypovolaemia worse.
• A slow maintenance-rate infusion of hypotonic fluid — A slow drip of hypotonic fluid (5% dextrose) does not stay in the vessels and is far too slow.

⭐ Key point: Use two large-bore cannulas (14–16 G). In trauma, limit crystalloid and give blood products early (permissive hypotension, except in head injury).$x$, null),
(115, $x$A patient with multiple organ dysfunction syndrome (MODS) following sepsis requires nursing care primarily focused on:$x$, $x$Comprehensive, systematic monitoring and support of all affected organ systems$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Comprehensive, systematic monitoring and support of all affected organ systems
In MODS, two or more organ systems fail together (such as lungs, kidneys, liver, heart, blood clotting and brain), usually after sepsis or major trauma. Each failing organ affects the others, so care must be COMPREHENSIVE and SYSTEMATIC: assess and support every system ("head to toe" or "A to E") — ventilation, haemodynamics, kidney replacement, nutrition, glucose control, infection control, skin, and family support.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A single-organ focus only, ignoring other systems — Focusing on one organ misses the others failing alongside it.
• No specific monitoring beyond routine vital signs — Routine vital signs alone are not enough; MODS needs invasive monitoring and lab checks.
• Withholding all interventions — Withholding care is not appropriate unless a decision about end-of-life care has been made with the family.

⭐ Key point: Mortality rises with each failing organ. Use the SOFA score to track the organs, and treat the cause (source control of infection).$x$, null),
(116, $x$A patient with pelvic fracture is at risk for:$x$, $x$A and B$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A and B
Pelvic fractures cause both. MASSIVE HAEMORRHAGE: the pelvis has large veins and arteries, and the pelvic space can hold several litres of blood. Unstable fractures (open book) can cause rapid shock and death. FAT EMBOLISM: fat from the bone marrow of large fractured bones (pelvis, femur) can enter the bloodstream and travel to the lungs, brain and skin, usually 24–72 hours later.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fat embolism — Fat embolism alone is only part of the answer.
• Massive hemorrhage — Massive haemorrhage alone is only part of the answer.
• Neurogenic shock — Neurogenic shock comes from spinal cord injury, not pelvic fracture.

⭐ Key point: Fat embolism triad: breathing difficulty (hypoxia), confusion, and a petechial rash (armpits, chest, conjunctiva). Early fixation of fractures reduces the risk.$x$, null),
(117, $x$A patient with multiple trauma has a pelvic fracture suspected on examination. The nurse should avoid which action that could worsen bleeding?$x$, $x$Repeatedly 'springing' or manually testing pelvic stability, which can dislodge clots and worsen haemorrhage$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Repeatedly 'springing' or manually testing pelvic stability, which can dislodge clots and worsen haemorrhage
Repeatedly pressing or "springing" the pelvis to check if it is stable can move the broken bone ends, tear vessels and dislodge clots that are slowing the bleeding. This can turn a controlled bleed into a fatal one. If a pelvic fracture is suspected (from the mechanism or signs), apply a pelvic binder and get imaging; avoid repeated examinations.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Applying a pelvic binder as indicated — Applying a pelvic binder at the level of the greater trochanters is CORRECT; it reduces pelvic volume and bleeding.
• Monitoring vital signs closely — Monitoring vital signs is correct and essential.
• Preparing for possible blood transfusion — Preparing blood products is correct, because large blood loss is expected.

⭐ Key point: Also avoid log-rolling patients with a suspected pelvic fracture more than needed, and do not insert a urinary catheter if there is blood at the urethral meatus (urethral injury) until it is checked.$x$, null),
(118, $x$A patient s/p TURP has pink-tinged urine and bladder spasms. This is:$x$, $x$Normal$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Normal
After transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP), PINK-tinged urine (light bleeding) and BLADDER SPASMS (from the catheter balloon and the raw area) are expected in the first days. Continuous bladder irrigation through a three-way catheter keeps clots from forming, and anticholinergic drugs (such as oxybutynin) ease the spasms.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Indicative of infection — Infection would cause fever, cloudy smelly urine and rising inflammatory markers, not just pink urine.
• Sign of hemorrhage — Haemorrhage would show bright red urine with clots, falling BP and rising HR; pink urine is not haemorrhage.
• Urinary retention — Retention would cause a full painful bladder with little drainage; check the catheter is draining.

⭐ Key point: After TURP, report bright red urine or clots, a blocked catheter (no drainage with pain), and signs of TURP syndrome (confusion, low sodium, low BP).$x$, null),
(119, $x$A patient with facial burns, singed nasal hair, and hoarseness of voice after a house fire should be closely monitored for:$x$, $x$Inhalational airway injury, with high risk of rapid airway obstruction$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Inhalational airway injury, with high risk of rapid airway obstruction
Facial burns, singed nasal hairs, soot in the mouth, a hoarse voice, cough with black sputum, and a history of a fire in an ENCLOSED space all suggest INHALATION INJURY. Heat and smoke cause swelling of the upper airway that can rapidly block it within hours, especially once IV fluids start. These patients need early assessment by an anaesthetist and often EARLY intubation before the airway closes. Also check for carbon monoxide and cyanide poisoning.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No specific concern beyond the visible burns — There IS a specific concern: the airway can be lost quickly.
• Simple anxiety — Anxiety does not explain singed nasal hair and hoarseness.
• Only skin infection risk — Infection is a later risk; the immediate danger is airway obstruction.

⭐ Key point: Give 100% oxygen (also treats CO poisoning; SpO₂ is falsely normal in CO poisoning). Watch for stridor, which is a late, dangerous sign.$x$, null),
(120, $x$To prevent pressure ulcers, a critically ill patient should be turned every:$x$, $x$2 hours$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 2 hours
Turning the patient every 2 hours relieves pressure on bony areas (sacrum, heels, hips, back of the head) before the skin and tissue are damaged by lack of blood flow. Pressure ulcers can start within 1–2 hours of unrelieved pressure in critically ill patients, who are immobile, poorly perfused and often on vasopressors. Turn the patient alternately (left 30°, back, right 30°), use pressure-relieving mattresses, and check the skin each time.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 30 minutes — Every 30 minutes is impractical and would disturb rest and sleep.
• 1 hour — Every hour is more often than usually needed (though some very high-risk patients need it).
• 6 hours — Every 6 hours is far too long; ulcers would develop.

⭐ Key point: Use the Braden scale (a score of 18 or less means at risk), keep the skin clean and dry, ensure good nutrition, and remember devices (ETT, NG tube, masks) can also cause pressure ulcers.$x$, null),
(121, $x$The general adaptation syndrome of stress includes all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$A period of fast recovery$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A period of fast recovery
Hans Selye's general adaptation syndrome has THREE stages: 1) ALARM (fight or flight: adrenaline and cortisol released, fast heart and breathing), 2) RESISTANCE (the body adapts and copes with the ongoing stressor), 3) EXHAUSTION (resources run out, leading to illness, burnout or even death if the stress continues). "A period of fast recovery" is not one of the stages.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An alarm reaction (fight-or-flight) — The alarm reaction IS the first stage.
• A resistance phase — The resistance phase IS the second stage.
• A period of exhaustion — The exhaustion phase IS the third stage.

⭐ Key point: Critical illness keeps patients in prolonged stress (high cortisol, high glucose, muscle breakdown). Staff are also at risk of exhaustion and burnout.$x$, null),
(122, $x$A critically ill patient in septic shock remains hypotensive despite adequate fluid resuscitation and high-dose vasopressors. Which endocrine abnormality should be considered and evaluated?$x$, $x$Critical illness-related corticosteroid insufficiency (relative adrenal insufficiency)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Critical illness-related corticosteroid insufficiency (relative adrenal insufficiency)
In severe sepsis, the adrenal glands may not produce enough cortisol for the body's needs, or the tissues may not respond to it. This is called critical illness-related corticosteroid insufficiency (relative adrenal insufficiency). Without enough cortisol, the blood vessels respond poorly to vasopressors and BP stays low. Low-dose hydrocortisone (200 mg/day) is recommended in septic shock needing ongoing vasopressors.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Primary hyperaldosteronism — Primary hyperaldosteronism causes HIGH BP and low potassium, not refractory shock.
• Hyperthyroidism — Hyperthyroidism causes fast heart rate and high output, but it is not the classic cause here.
• Growth hormone excess — Growth hormone excess (acromegaly) is a chronic condition, not a cause of shock.

⭐ Key point: Signs that point to adrenal insufficiency: low BP not responding to fluids and vasopressors, low sodium, high potassium, low glucose and a history of steroid use.$x$, null),
(123, $x$The following are indication of monitoring intra-abdominal pressure except:$x$, $x$Laparotomy closed with normal tension$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Laparotomy closed with normal tension
The question asks which is NOT an indication for monitoring intra-abdominal pressure. Measuring IAP (via the bladder) is recommended for patients at risk of intra-abdominal hypertension: shock needing large-volume resuscitation, damage control laparotomy, major burns (above 25% TBSA), severe pancreatitis, massive transfusion, and abdominal surgery closed under tension. A laparotomy closed with NORMAL tension is low risk, so it is not an indication.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Shock requiring resuscitation. — Shock needing large fluid resuscitation IS an indication (the gut swells).
• Damage control laparotomy — Damage control laparotomy IS an indication (packs, swollen bowel).
• Major burns \> 25% — Major burns above 25% IS an indication (huge fluid volumes cause gut and wall oedema).

⭐ Key point: Normal IAP is 5–7 mmHg. IAH is 12 mmHg or more. Abdominal compartment syndrome is above 20 mmHg with new organ failure, which needs decompression.$x$, null),
(124, $x$Which of the following best describes the purpose of stress-ulcer prophylaxis (e.g. proton pump inhibitors) in critically ill patients?$x$, $x$To reduce the risk of stress-related gastrointestinal bleeding in high-risk ICU patients$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: To reduce the risk of stress-related gastrointestinal bleeding in high-risk ICU patients
Critically ill patients, especially those ventilated for more than 48 hours or with clotting problems, can develop stress ulcers in the stomach because blood flow to the stomach lining is reduced. These can bleed. Stress ulcer prophylaxis with a PPI (omeprazole, pantoprazole) or H₂ blocker reduces stomach acid and lowers the risk of significant upper GI bleeding. Early enteral feeding also helps protect the stomach.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• To treat active pneumonia — Stress ulcer drugs do not treat pneumonia; reducing stomach acid may even slightly increase pneumonia risk.
• To lower blood glucose — PPIs do not lower blood glucose.
• To prevent deep vein thrombosis — DVT is prevented with LMWH or heparin and mechanical devices, not PPIs.

⭐ Key point: Review stress ulcer prophylaxis daily and stop it when the patient no longer has risk factors; long-term use increases C. difficile and pneumonia risk.$x$, null),
(125, $x$Hemorrhagic discoloration of the flanks is known as$x$, $x$Grey-Turner's sign$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Grey-Turner's sign
Grey-Turner's sign is bruising (bluish discolouration) of the FLANKS. Blood from the retroperitoneum tracks along the tissue planes to the skin of the sides, typically 24–48 hours after the bleed. It is seen in severe (haemorrhagic) acute pancreatitis, ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm, and retroperitoneal bleeding (including ruptured ectopic pregnancy).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Grey-Prumer's sign — "Grey-Prumer's sign" does not exist; it is a made-up distractor.
• Cullen's sign — Cullen's sign is bruising around the UMBILICUS (navel), not the flanks.
• Kullen's sign — "Kullen's sign" is a misspelling distractor of Cullen's sign.

⭐ Key point: Grey-Turner = flanks; Cullen = umbilicus. Both suggest bleeding in the abdomen or retroperitoneum (pancreatitis, ruptured ectopic, AAA).$x$, null),
(126, $x$A hallmark early clinical clue suggesting necrotising fasciitis, distinguishing it from simple cellulitis, is:$x$, $x$Pain out of proportion to visible skin findings$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Pain out of proportion to visible skin findings
Necrotising fasciitis is a fast-spreading bacterial infection of the deep tissues (fascia) that destroys tissue under the skin before the skin itself looks very bad. The early classic clue is severe PAIN OUT OF PROPORTION to what you can see on the skin. Later signs include rapid spread, tense swelling, dusky or purple skin, blisters, crepitus (gas under the skin), numbness and sepsis. It is a surgical emergency.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mild, well-localised redness with minimal pain — Mild, well-localised redness with little pain fits simple cellulitis, not necrotising fasciitis.
• Slow onset over several weeks — Necrotising fasciitis spreads over HOURS, not weeks.
• Complete absence of systemic symptoms — Patients quickly become very unwell (fever, fast pulse, low BP, confusion); systemic symptoms are usually present.

⭐ Key point: Treatment is urgent surgical debridement (removing dead tissue) plus broad-spectrum IV antibiotics and sepsis care. Delay increases death and amputation.$x$, null),
(127, $x$The 'Golden Hour' concept in trauma care refers to:$x$, $x$The first 60 minutes after major trauma, during which rapid assessment and definitive care most improve survival$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The first 60 minutes after major trauma, during which rapid assessment and definitive care most improve survival
The "golden hour" is the first 60 minutes after a major injury. During this time, rapid assessment (primary survey ABCDE), resuscitation, stopping bleeding and getting the patient to definitive care (surgery, CT, ICU) give the best chance of survival. Many trauma deaths from bleeding and airway problems are preventable if treated quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The first hour after hospital discharge — The time after hospital discharge has nothing to do with the golden hour.
• The hour before surgery begins — The hour before surgery is not the definition; the clock starts at the time of INJURY.
• A 60-minute waiting period before treatment can start — The golden hour is about acting FAST, not waiting.

⭐ Key point: Trauma deaths come in three peaks: immediate (unsurvivable), early (minutes to hours, the golden hour, preventable), and late (days to weeks, from sepsis and multi-organ failure).$x$, null),
(128, $x$A common complication of severe hypothermia is:$x$, $x$Cardiac arrest (arrhythmias)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Cardiac arrest (arrhythmias)
In severe hypothermia (core temperature below 28–30 °C), the heart becomes cold and very irritable. The heart rate slows, and the risk of arrhythmias rises sharply: atrial fibrillation, then VF, then asystole. Even moving the patient roughly can trigger VF. The ECG may show J (Osborn) waves. Cardiac arrest is the most serious complication.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperactivity — Hypothermia causes slowing and drowsiness, not hyperactivity.
• Increased body temperature — Hypothermia means LOW body temperature, by definition.
• Rapid breathing — Breathing becomes slow and shallow in severe hypothermia, not rapid.

⭐ Key point: Handle hypothermic patients gently, rewarm (warm fluids, forced-air warming, ECMO in severe cases), and remember "no one is dead until warm and dead". Defibrillation and drugs work poorly below 30 °C.$x$, null),
(129, $x$A properly functioning underwater-seal drain typically shows:$x$, $x$Fluctuation (tidaling) and intermittent bubbling with respiration$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fluctuation (tidaling) and intermittent bubbling with respiration
In a working underwater-seal drain, the water level in the water-seal chamber rises and falls with breathing (swinging or tidaling), because pressure in the chest changes with each breath. This proves the tube is open and connected to the pleural space. INTERMITTENT bubbling, especially on coughing or breathing out, is expected while air is still leaving the chest after a pneumothorax.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Continuous bubbling in the water-seal chamber — CONTINUOUS bubbling in the water-seal chamber suggests an air leak (from the lung or the system) and must be checked.
• No movement at all in the water-seal chamber — No movement at all suggests the tube is blocked or kinked, or the lung has fully re-expanded.
• A water level that never moves — A water level that never moves means no swinging, so the drain may not be working.

⭐ Key point: Swinging = the drain is open. No swing = check for kinks, clots, or lung re-expansion. Continuous bubbling = look for an air leak. Never lift the bottle above the chest.$x$, null),
(130, $x$A key element of the "ventilator bundle" (VAP prevention bundle) includes all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Routine daily scheduled ventilator circuit changes$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Routine daily scheduled ventilator circuit changes
The question asks which is NOT part of the VAP bundle. Routine, scheduled ventilator circuit changes do NOT reduce VAP. Breaking the circuit may actually increase contamination. Circuits are changed only when visibly soiled or faulty. The VAP bundle includes head-of-bed elevation, daily sedation holds with weaning assessment, peptic ulcer and DVT prophylaxis, and chlorhexidine oral care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Head-of-bed elevation 30-45 degrees — Head-of-bed elevation to 30–45° IS part of the bundle; it reduces aspiration.
• Daily sedation vacation and readiness-to-wean assessment — Daily sedation vacation and readiness-to-wean assessment IS part of the bundle; it shortens ventilation.
• Peptic ulcer disease and deep vein thrombosis prophylaxis — Peptic ulcer and DVT prophylaxis ARE part of the original IHI ventilator bundle.

⭐ Key point: Other VAP measures: subglottic suction, cuff pressure 20–30 cmH₂O, closed suction, hand hygiene, and early extubation.$x$, null),
(131, $x$All are signs of ruptured ectopic pregnancy EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Hyperglycaemia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hyperglycaemia
The question asks which is NOT a sign of a ruptured ectopic pregnancy. Hyperglycaemia (high blood sugar) has no link with it. A ruptured ectopic causes internal bleeding into the abdomen: sudden lower abdominal pain (often one-sided), vaginal bleeding (usually light), shoulder-tip pain (blood irritating the diaphragm), dizziness, and signs of shock.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tachycardia, shock, pallor and collapse — Tachycardia, shock, pallor and collapse ARE signs; they come from internal bleeding.
• Abdominal pain — Abdominal pain IS a sign, often the first symptom.
• Vaginal bleeding — Vaginal bleeding IS a sign, though often less than the internal bleeding.

⭐ Key point: Any woman of childbearing age with abdominal pain and shock has a ruptured ectopic until proven otherwise. Do a pregnancy test, resuscitate, cross-match, and arrange emergency surgery.$x$, null),
(132, $x$A pregnant patient at 32 weeks presents with painless, bright red vaginal bleeding. This presentation is most concerning for:$x$, $x$Placenta praevia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Placenta praevia
Placenta praevia means the placenta lies low in the uterus, over or near the cervix. As the lower uterus stretches in the third trimester, the placenta separates and bleeds. The classic sign is PAINLESS, BRIGHT RED vaginal bleeding, often recurrent, with a soft, non-tender uterus. The baby's head may not be engaged, or the lie may be abnormal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Placental abruption — Placental abruption causes PAINFUL bleeding with a hard, tender ("woody") uterus.
• Normal spotting in pregnancy — Bleeding at 32 weeks is never "normal spotting"; it must be investigated.
• Round ligament pain — Round ligament pain causes sharp groin pain, not bleeding.

⭐ Key point: In placenta praevia NEVER do a vaginal examination (it can cause torrential bleeding). Confirm with ultrasound, resuscitate, and plan a caesarean section.$x$, null),
(133, $x$What are the symptoms of premature rupture of membranes?$x$, $x$All of the options$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: All of the options
Premature rupture of membranes (PROM) is when the amniotic sac breaks before labour starts. Women may notice a sudden GUSH of fluid or a slow, constant leak or trickle that they can't control. Normal amniotic fluid is clear (or pale straw-coloured) and odourless. All the options describe PROM, so "all of the options" is correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Feeling a gush of fluid from the vagina — A gush of fluid is one sign, but not the only one listed.
• Slow leak or trickle of fluid — A slow leak or trickle is one sign, but not the only one listed.
• The fluid is clear and odourless — Clear, odourless fluid is one feature, but not the only one listed.

⭐ Key point: Green fluid = meconium (fetal distress). Foul-smelling fluid with fever = chorioamnionitis (infection). After PROM, avoid digital vaginal exams, check for infection, and give antibiotics as per protocol.$x$, null),
(134, $x$A cervical incompetence diagnosis in a pregnant patient primarily increases the risk of:$x$, $x$Second-trimester pregnancy loss or preterm birth from painless cervical dilation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Second-trimester pregnancy loss or preterm birth from painless cervical dilation
Cervical incompetence (cervical insufficiency) means the cervix is weak and opens painlessly in the second trimester, without contractions. The pregnancy can be lost suddenly (second-trimester miscarriage) or the baby born very early. Treatment includes cervical cerclage (a stitch around the cervix) or vaginal progesterone, with monitoring of cervical length by ultrasound.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Post-term pregnancy — Cervical incompetence leads to EARLY birth, not post-term pregnancy.
• Gestational diabetes — Gestational diabetes is a metabolic condition, not related to the cervix.
• Multiple pregnancy — Multiple pregnancy is a cause of preterm birth, not a result of cervical incompetence.

⭐ Key point: History is the clue: repeated painless second-trimester losses. The cerclage stitch is removed at about 36–37 weeks or when labour starts.$x$, null),
(135, $x$A Rhesus-negative mother delivers a Rhesus-positive baby. The nurse anticipates administration of:$x$, $x$Anti-D immunoglobulin to the mother to prevent sensitisation in future pregnancies$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Anti-D immunoglobulin to the mother to prevent sensitisation in future pregnancies
When an Rh-negative mother delivers an Rh-positive baby, some of the baby's red cells enter her blood. Her immune system can make anti-D antibodies (sensitisation). In a FUTURE pregnancy with an Rh-positive baby, these antibodies cross the placenta and destroy the baby's red cells (haemolytic disease of the newborn). Giving ANTI-D immunoglobulin to the MOTHER within 72 hours of delivery removes the baby's cells before her body reacts.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No specific treatment is needed — Without treatment, the next Rh-positive baby is at serious risk.
• Anti-D immunoglobulin to the baby — Anti-D is given to the MOTHER, not the baby.
• Oxytocin to the mother for this purpose — Oxytocin contracts the uterus; it does not prevent Rh sensitisation.

⭐ Key point: Also give anti-D after any sensitising event (miscarriage, ectopic, amniocentesis, abdominal trauma, bleeding) and routinely at 28 weeks in many units.$x$, null),
(136, $x$The most common cause of postpartum haemorrhage, among the 4 Ts, is:$x$, $x$Tone (uterine atony)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Tone (uterine atony)
After birth, the uterus must contract firmly to squeeze shut the blood vessels where the placenta was attached. If it stays soft and relaxed (uterine ATONY), blood pours out. Poor TONE causes about 70% of PPH. Risk factors include a big baby, twins, polyhydramnios, long or very fast labour, grand multiparity, infection and drugs that relax the uterus.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Trauma — Trauma (tears of the cervix, vagina or perineum, uterine rupture) causes about 20%.
• Tissue — Tissue (retained placenta or membranes) causes about 10%.
• Thrombin — Thrombin (clotting problems such as DIC) causes about 1%.

⭐ Key point: First steps for atonic PPH: call for help, rub the uterus (fundal massage), give oxytocin, empty the bladder, start IV fluids, and give tranexamic acid.$x$, null),
(137, $x$What important modification should be made when performing CPR on a pregnant patient in later pregnancy?$x$, $x$Manual left uterine displacement to relieve aortocaval compression and improve venous return$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Manual left uterine displacement to relieve aortocaval compression and improve venous return
After about 20 weeks, the pregnant uterus presses on the inferior vena cava and aorta when the woman lies on her back (aortocaval compression). This sharply reduces blood returning to the heart, so CPR would be ineffective. Pushing the uterus to the LEFT by hand (manual left uterine displacement) while doing chest compressions relieves the pressure. A left lateral tilt of 15–30° is an alternative.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Place the patient in the prone position — The prone position (face down) makes CPR impossible.
• Avoid performing chest compressions entirely — Chest compressions are still essential in pregnancy.
• Compress the abdomen instead of the chest — Compressions are always on the CHEST (lower half of the sternum), never the abdomen.

⭐ Key point: In maternal cardiac arrest, if there is no return of circulation within 4 minutes, prepare for a perimortem caesarean section by 5 minutes. This helps both mother and baby.$x$, null),
(138, $x$Tracheo-oesophageal fistula is often associated with which other congenital anomaly?$x$, $x$Oesophageal atresia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Oesophageal atresia
Tracheo-oesophageal fistula (TOF) is an abnormal connection between the trachea and the oesophagus. In about 85% of cases, it occurs together with oesophageal ATRESIA, where the upper oesophagus ends in a blind pouch. The baby has frothy saliva, chokes and coughs or turns blue on feeding, and an NG tube cannot be passed into the stomach.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cleft lip only — Cleft lip can occur with other anomalies, but it is not the classic partner of TOF.
• Club foot only — Club foot is a limb anomaly, not typically linked with TOF.
• Polydactyly only — Polydactyly (extra fingers) is not typically linked with TOF.

⭐ Key point: TOF may be part of VACTERL (Vertebral, Anal, Cardiac, Tracheo-oesophageal, Renal, Limb anomalies). Keep the baby nil by mouth, head up, with continuous suction of the upper pouch until surgery.$x$, null),
(139, $x$Thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency results in which potential maternal complication of hyperemesis gravidarum?$x$, $x$Wernicke encephalopathy$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Wernicke encephalopathy
In hyperemesis gravidarum, prolonged vomiting and poor intake use up thiamine (vitamin B1) stores, especially if IV glucose is given without thiamine. Thiamine deficiency causes Wernicke's encephalopathy: confusion, ataxia (unsteady walking), and eye problems (nystagmus, ophthalmoplegia). It can lead to permanent memory loss (Korsakoff syndrome) or death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Renal failure — Renal failure can come from dehydration, but it is not caused by thiamine deficiency.
• Esophageal rupture — Oesophageal rupture (Boerhaave syndrome) can follow forceful vomiting, but not thiamine deficiency.
• Osmotic demyelination syndrome — Osmotic demyelination comes from correcting low sodium too fast, not thiamine deficiency.

⭐ Key point: Give THIAMINE before or with IV glucose in any malnourished patient (hyperemesis, alcohol misuse, starvation), and replace fluids and electrolytes.$x$, null),
(140, $x$A nurse is performing neonatal resuscitation. The newborn's heart rate is below 60 beats per minute after 30 seconds of effective positive-pressure ventilation. The next step is to:$x$, $x$Initiate chest compressions$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Initiate chest compressions
Neonatal resuscitation focuses on ventilation, because most newborns need only effective breaths. If the heart rate is still BELOW 60 beats per minute after 30 seconds of EFFECTIVE ventilation (chest is moving), START CHEST COMPRESSIONS. The ratio is 3 compressions to 1 breath (90 compressions and 30 breaths per minute), using two thumbs on the lower third of the sternum, and increase oxygen to 100%.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Continue positive-pressure ventilation for another 30 seconds — Continuing ventilation alone wastes time once the heart rate is below 60 with good ventilation.
• Administer epinephrine — Adrenaline is given only if the heart rate stays below 60 after about 30 seconds of good compressions and ventilation.
• Stop resuscitation and call the physician — Stopping resuscitation is wrong; call for help while continuing.

⭐ Key point: Newborn steps: dry, warm and stimulate; open the airway; give 5 inflation breaths; ventilate; compressions if HR is below 60; then drugs through an umbilical venous catheter.$x$, null),
(141, $x$The most widely used effective treatment for neonatal jaundice is:$x$, $x$Phototherapy$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Phototherapy
Phototherapy uses blue-green light (wavelength about 460–490 nm) to change bilirubin in the skin into a water-soluble form that the baby can pass in urine and stool without needing the liver. It is safe, non-invasive and effective for most babies with significant jaundice. Exchange transfusion is kept for very high or rapidly rising bilirubin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Blood transfusion — Blood transfusion does not lower bilirubin (exchange transfusion is a different, specialised procedure).
• Antibiotics — Antibiotics treat infection, which is only sometimes a cause of jaundice.
• Liver transplant — A liver transplant is only for rare liver diseases such as biliary atresia that fails surgery.

⭐ Key point: Phototherapy care: cover the eyes, expose as much skin as possible, turn the baby, give extra fluids or feeds, monitor temperature, and check bilirubin regularly. Jaundice within 24 hours of birth is always abnormal.$x$, null),
(142, $x$Pyloric stenosis in infants classically presents with:$x$, $x$Projectile, non-bilious vomiting typically starting around 3–6 weeks of age$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Projectile, non-bilious vomiting typically starting around 3–6 weeks of age
Pyloric stenosis is thickening of the pylorus muscle (the stomach outlet), which blocks food from leaving the stomach. It usually starts at 3–6 weeks of age, often in first-born boys. Vomiting is PROJECTILE (forceful) and NON-BILIOUS (milky, not green), because the blockage is above where bile enters. The baby is hungry after vomiting, and an "olive" mass may be felt in the upper abdomen.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bilious vomiting from birth — Bilious (green) vomiting from birth suggests an obstruction BELOW the bile duct (such as duodenal atresia or malrotation).
• Chronic diarrhoea — Chronic diarrhoea is not a feature; babies are usually constipated.
• Bloody stools with fever — Bloody stools with fever suggest infection or intussusception.

⭐ Key point: Vomiting stomach acid causes a hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic METABOLIC ALKALOSIS. Correct fluids and electrolytes FIRST, then do surgery (pyloromyotomy).$x$, null),
(143, $x$Intussusception in infants classically presents with:$x$, $x$Episodic colicky abdominal pain, vomiting, and 'redcurrant jelly' stools$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Episodic colicky abdominal pain, vomiting, and 'redcurrant jelly' stools
Intussusception is when one part of the bowel telescopes into the next part, usually at the ileocaecal junction, in babies aged 3 months to 3 years. It causes episodes of severe colicky pain (the baby screams and draws up the legs, then is quiet between episodes), vomiting, and later "redcurrant jelly" stools (blood and mucus). A sausage-shaped mass may be felt in the abdomen.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Painless abdominal distension only — It is very painful, not painless distension.
• Chronic constipation with no acute symptoms — It is an acute emergency, not chronic constipation.
• A silent, asymptomatic abdominal mass — The baby is clearly unwell, so it is not a silent, asymptomatic mass.

⭐ Key point: Treat with IV fluids, an NG tube, and air or contrast enema reduction. Surgery is needed if the enema fails or the bowel perforates.$x$, null),
(144, $x$Regarding the newborn which of this is correct?$x$, $x$The term newborn may be small for gestational age$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The term newborn may be small for gestational age
Small for gestational age (SGA) means a baby's weight is below the 10th centile FOR ITS GESTATIONAL AGE. A TERM baby (37–42 weeks) can still be SGA if it did not grow well in the womb (placental problems, maternal smoking, pre-eclampsia, infection). This statement is correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Preterms are babies delivered before 40 weeks of delivery — Preterm means born BEFORE 37 completed weeks, not before 40 weeks.
• All preterms are low birth weight — Not all preterm babies are low birth weight; a baby at 36 weeks can weigh more than 2500 g.
• All preterms are small for gestational age — Not all preterm babies are SGA; many preterm babies are an appropriate weight for their age.

⭐ Key point: Low birth weight is below 2500 g, very low is below 1500 g, extremely low is below 1000 g. SGA babies are at risk of low glucose, hypothermia and polycythaemia.$x$, null),
(145, $x$Which is part of pre-operative care for a child with Hirschsprung's disease?$x$, $x$Giving bowel washouts/enemas$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Giving bowel washouts/enemas
In Hirschsprung's disease, part of the bowel (usually the rectum and sigmoid colon) has no nerve cells, so it cannot relax and push stool along. Stool builds up above it, causing distension, constipation and risk of enterocolitis. Before surgery, regular bowel WASHOUTS (saline rectal irrigations) or enemas are given to empty the bowel, relieve distension and reduce infection risk.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CT scan — A CT scan is not routine before surgery; diagnosis is by rectal biopsy and contrast enema.
• Shaving the axillary hair — Shaving the axillary hair is not relevant to bowel surgery.
• Obtaining consent from the child himself — A child cannot legally give consent; parents or guardians consent.

⭐ Key point: Signs of Hirschsprung's: failure to pass meconium within 48 hours, distended abdomen, bilious vomiting. Watch for enterocolitis (fever, explosive foul diarrhoea), which is dangerous.$x$, null),
(146, $x$Fluid therapy in a severely dehydrated child typically begins with:$x$, $x$A rapid IV fluid bolus for severe dehydration, followed by ongoing maintenance and deficit replacement$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A rapid IV fluid bolus for severe dehydration, followed by ongoing maintenance and deficit replacement
A severely dehydrated child (lethargic, weak pulse, cold hands, slow capillary refill, sunken eyes) is in or near shock. The first step is a RAPID IV (or IO) fluid bolus of isotonic crystalloid (0.9% saline or Hartmann's), commonly 10–20 mL/kg, repeated after reassessment. After the circulation is restored, the remaining deficit and maintenance fluids are replaced over 24–48 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral fluids only, regardless of severity — Oral fluids alone are for mild to moderate dehydration; severe dehydration needs IV fluids.
• Withholding all fluids until specialist review — Withholding fluid in severe dehydration can be fatal.
• Only subcutaneous fluids — Subcutaneous fluids are too slow for severe dehydration and shock.

⭐ Key point: The WHO plan for severe dehydration (Plan C) is 100 mL/kg of Ringer's lactate in phases. Watch for overload in malnourished children and those with heart disease.$x$, null),
(147, $x$A characteristic sign of croup is:$x$, $x$A barking cough with inspiratory stridor$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A barking cough with inspiratory stridor
Croup (laryngotracheobronchitis) is a viral infection (usually parainfluenza) of the larynx and trachea in children aged 6 months to 3 years. Swelling below the vocal cords narrows the airway, causing a BARKING ("seal-like") cough, hoarse voice and INSPIRATORY STRIDOR (a harsh sound when breathing in). It is often worse at night, with a mild fever.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A productive cough with green sputum — A productive cough with green sputum suggests a chest infection.
• Expiratory wheeze only — Expiratory wheeze suggests asthma or bronchiolitis (lower airway).
• High fever with drooling — High fever with drooling suggests EPIGLOTTITIS, which is more serious.

⭐ Key point: Treat croup with oral dexamethasone (0.15 mg/kg); give nebulised adrenaline for severe stridor. Keep the child calm on the parent's lap.$x$, null),
(148, $x$Febrile convulsions in young children are most commonly associated with:$x$, $x$A rapid rise in body temperature, often with a viral infection$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A rapid rise in body temperature, often with a viral infection
Febrile convulsions are seizures caused by fever in children aged 6 months to 5 years without brain infection. They are linked to a RAPID RISE in temperature, usually from a viral infection (such as an ear or throat infection, or roseola). Most are short (under 5 minutes) generalised seizures, and the child recovers fully.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Chronic malnutrition alone — Chronic malnutrition alone does not cause febrile convulsions.
• Congenital heart disease exclusively — Congenital heart disease is not specifically linked to febrile convulsions.
• Vitamin overdose — Vitamin overdose is not a cause.

⭐ Key point: Care: keep the child safe, place in recovery position, time the seizure, give a benzodiazepine if it lasts over 5 minutes, and look for the cause of fever (exclude meningitis). Reassure parents that most children grow out of it.$x$, null),
(149, $x$A sickle cell disease patient presented with chest pain, low Spo2, cough, dyspnea, which of these is the cause?$x$, $x$Acute Chest Syndrome$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Acute Chest Syndrome
Acute chest syndrome is a serious complication of sickle cell disease. Sickled cells block the small lung vessels (plus infection or fat embolism), causing chest pain, cough, breathlessness, low SpO₂, fever, and a NEW shadow on the chest X-ray. It is the leading cause of death in sickle cell disease and needs urgent treatment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute Asthmatic — Acute asthma causes wheeze and breathlessness, but this picture in sickle cell disease points to acute chest syndrome.
• Vaso-Occlusive Crisis — A vaso-occlusive crisis causes pain (often bones and joints) but not hypoxia with lung signs.
• Acute Anaemia — Acute anaemia causes tiredness, pallor and breathlessness, but not chest pain with a new lung shadow.

⭐ Key point: Treatment: oxygen, pain relief, IV fluids (careful), antibiotics, incentive spirometry, and blood or exchange transfusion if severe. Monitor SpO₂ closely.$x$, null),
(150, $x$Sudden Infant Death Syndrome (SIDS) risk is reduced by which key recommendation?$x$, $x$Placing infants to sleep supine (on their back)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Placing infants to sleep supine (on their back)
Placing babies to sleep on their BACK (supine) is the most important way to reduce SIDS ("Back to Sleep"). Other safe sleep advice: a firm flat mattress, no pillows, toys or loose bedding, the baby's feet at the foot of the cot, room-sharing (not bed-sharing) for the first 6 months, a smoke-free home, breastfeeding, and no overheating.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Placing infants to sleep prone (on their stomach) — Sleeping prone (on the stomach) INCREASES the risk of SIDS.
• Co-sleeping in the same bed as an adult at all times — Bed-sharing with adults increases the risk, especially if the adult smokes, drinks or takes sedatives.
• Using soft, loose bedding in the crib — Soft, loose bedding increases the risk of suffocation and SIDS.

⭐ Key point: SIDS peaks at 2–4 months of age. Teach all parents safe sleep before discharge.$x$, null),
(151, $x$Florence Nightingale's Environmental Theory, published in 'Notes on Nursing,' primarily focuses on which idea?$x$, $x$Nursing should focus on manipulating the client's environment to place the body in the best condition for nature to act and for healing to occur$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Nursing should focus on manipulating the client's environment to place the body in the best condition for nature to act and for healing to occur
Florence Nightingale's Environmental Theory says the nurse's main job is to manage the patient's ENVIRONMENT — fresh air, light, warmth, cleanliness, quiet, clean water, drainage and good food — so that nature can heal the body. She believed disease was helped or hindered by surroundings. Her ideas began modern infection control and patient comfort.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nursing should focus exclusively on administering medications prescribed by physicians — Giving medications is part of nursing, but Nightingale's theory is about the environment, not drug administration.
• Illness is caused solely by genetic predisposition and cannot be influenced by environment — Nightingale believed the environment strongly affects health, so illness is not "solely genetic".
• The nurse's primary role is to perform invasive procedures independent of physicians — Performing invasive procedures independently is not her theory.

⭐ Key point: Nightingale is called the founder of modern nursing; she wrote "Notes on Nursing" (1859) and improved care in the Crimean War through hygiene.$x$, null),
(152, $x$Which of the following is one of Henderson's 14 basic needs related to physiological function?$x$, $x$Breathe normally$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Breathe normally
Virginia Henderson described 14 basic needs that the nurse helps patients meet when they cannot meet them alone. The FIRST is "Breathe normally". Others include: eat and drink adequately, eliminate, move and keep a good posture, sleep and rest, dress, keep body temperature normal, keep clean, avoid danger, communicate, worship, work, play and learn.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Achieve self-actualization — Self-actualisation is the top of Maslow's hierarchy, not one of Henderson's needs.
• Maintain the flexible line of defense — The flexible line of defence comes from Neuman's Systems Model.
• Engage in role-function adaptation — Role-function adaptation comes from Roy's Adaptation Model.

⭐ Key point: Henderson's definition of nursing: helping the person "perform those activities contributing to health or its recovery that he would perform unaided if he had the necessary strength, will or knowledge".$x$, null),
(153, $x$Orem identified three types of nursing systems. One of them is:$x$, $x$Wholly compensatory$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Wholly compensatory
Dorothea Orem's self-care deficit theory describes three nursing systems depending on how much the patient can do for themselves. 1) WHOLLY COMPENSATORY: the nurse does everything (an unconscious, ventilated ICU patient). 2) Partly compensatory: the nurse and patient share care. 3) Supportive-educative: the patient can do the care with teaching and support.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Team nursing — Team nursing is a way of organising staff, not one of Orem's systems.
• Primary nursing — Primary nursing is a care delivery model, not one of Orem's systems.
• Individualised client care — Individualised client care is a general concept, not one of Orem's three systems.

⭐ Key point: Most ICU patients start in the wholly compensatory system and move toward supportive-educative as they recover.$x$, null),
(154, $x$In Sister Callista Roy's Adaptation Model, the person is viewed as:$x$, $x$A biopsychosocial adaptive system that receives input (stimuli) and produces output in the form of adaptive or ineffective responses$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A biopsychosocial adaptive system that receives input (stimuli) and produces output in the form of adaptive or ineffective responses
In Callista Roy's Adaptation Model, the person is an ADAPTIVE SYSTEM. Stimuli (focal, contextual and residual) are the input. The person's coping processes (the regulator, which is physiological, and the cognator, which is mental and emotional) process these stimuli. The output is behaviour, which is either ADAPTIVE (healthy) or INEFFECTIVE. Adaptation happens in four modes: physiological, self-concept, role function, and interdependence.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A passive recipient of environmental changes with no internal processing mechanisms — Roy sees the person as active, not a passive recipient.
• A system defined solely by 14 basic needs — The 14 basic needs belong to Henderson, not Roy.
• An energy field indistinguishable from the environment — An energy field indistinguishable from the environment is Martha Rogers' view.

⭐ Key point: The goal of nursing in Roy's model is to promote adaptation in the four modes.$x$, null),
(155, $x$The following Stressors that disrupt the system according Neumann's model include ------ ------$x$, $x$Intra-personal, inter- personal, and extra personal stressors.$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Intra-personal, inter- personal, and extra personal stressors.
In Betty Neuman's Systems Model, stressors are classified by where they come from. INTRApersonal stressors arise within the person (such as pain or fear). INTERpersonal stressors arise between people (such as conflict with family or staff). EXTRApersonal stressors arise from outside the person (such as finances or the hospital environment). Stressors can break through the lines of defence and cause illness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The primary, secondary, and tertiary stressor. — Primary, secondary and tertiary are levels of PREVENTION (interventions) in Neuman's model, not types of stressors.
• The individuals, families, and groups stressor — Individuals, families and groups are possible CLIENTS in her model, not stressors.
• Physiological, sociocultural, and developmental stressor — Physiological, sociocultural and developmental are VARIABLES of the client system, not stressor categories.

⭐ Key point: Neuman's model includes the flexible line of defence (outer), the normal line of defence, lines of resistance, and the basic core structure.$x$, null),
(156, $x$A nurse using Rogers' Science of Unitary Human Beings as a guiding framework would most likely incorporate which of the following into practice?$x$, $x$Therapeutic touch or other modalities intended to promote harmonious patterning of the human and environmental energy fields$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Therapeutic touch or other modalities intended to promote harmonious patterning of the human and environmental energy fields
Martha Rogers' Science of Unitary Human Beings sees each person as an energy field that is constantly exchanging energy with the environmental energy field. The nurse aims to promote harmony between the two fields. Practices like THERAPEUTIC TOUCH, relaxation, music and imagery come from this view.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Strict enforcement of the 14 basic needs checklist — The 14 basic needs checklist belongs to Henderson.
• Ranking patient needs using Maslow's pyramid exclusively — Maslow's pyramid is a psychology theory, not Rogers' framework.
• Assessment limited only to the 11 functional health patterns — The 11 functional health patterns belong to Marjory Gordon.

⭐ Key point: Key Rogers concepts are energy fields, openness, pattern and pandimensionality (the principles of homeodynamics).$x$, null),
(157, $x$Marjory Gordon's Functional Health Patterns framework is primarily used by nurses to:$x$, $x$Organize a systematic, holistic nursing assessment across defined categories of client behavior over time$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Organize a systematic, holistic nursing assessment across defined categories of client behavior over time
Marjory Gordon's 11 Functional Health Patterns give nurses a structured way to do a HOLISTIC nursing assessment. They cover health perception and management, nutrition and metabolism, elimination, activity and exercise, sleep and rest, cognition and perception, self-perception, role and relationships, sexuality and reproduction, coping and stress, and values and beliefs. The data then guide nursing diagnoses.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diagnose medical conditions independently of physicians — Nurses do not diagnose medical conditions with this framework; they make NURSING diagnoses.
• Replace the nursing process entirely with a single-step evaluation — It supports the nursing process; it does not replace it.
• Calculate a client's position on the health-illness continuum numerically — It does not calculate a numerical position on a health–illness scale.

⭐ Key point: Gordon's patterns are widely used to organise admission assessments and link well with NANDA nursing diagnoses.$x$, null),
(158, $x$According to Maslow's hierarchy, the most basic needs are:$x$, $x$Physiological needs$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Physiological needs
Maslow's hierarchy has five levels, from the bottom up: PHYSIOLOGICAL needs (air, water, food, sleep, elimination), then safety and security, love and belonging, esteem, and self-actualisation at the top. The physiological needs are the most basic and must be met first. In nursing, this is why AIRWAY, BREATHING and CIRCULATION come first when prioritising care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Safety and security needs — Safety and security are the second level.
• Self-actualisation needs — Self-actualisation is the TOP (highest) level.
• Social needs — Social (love and belonging) needs are the third level.

⭐ Key point: In exam questions about priority, choose the answer that meets a physiological need (especially oxygen and airway) before psychological or educational needs.$x$, null),
(159, $x$The standard format for writing a 'problem-focused' (actual) NANDA-I nursing diagnosis is often referred to as the PES format, meaning:$x$, $x$Problem, Etiology, Signs and symptoms (defining characteristics)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Problem, Etiology, Signs and symptoms (defining characteristics)
A problem-focused (actual) NANDA-I nursing diagnosis is written in PES format. P is the PROBLEM (the NANDA label, such as "Ineffective airway clearance"). E is the ETIOLOGY ("related to" thick secretions and weak cough). S is the SIGNS and SYMPTOMS, the defining characteristics ("as evidenced by" coarse crackles and SpO₂ 88%).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Plan, Evaluate, Solve — Plan, Evaluate, Solve is not the PES format.
• Patient, Environment, Standard — Patient, Environment, Standard is not the PES format.
• Priority, Evidence, Solution — Priority, Evidence, Solution is not the PES format.

⭐ Key point: A RISK nursing diagnosis has no "S" part (the problem hasn't happened yet), for example "Risk for infection related to central venous catheter".$x$, null),
(160, $x$Standardised nursing terminologies such as NANDA, NIC, and NOC exist to:$x$, $x$Provide a consistent language for nursing diagnoses, interventions, and outcomes$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Provide a consistent language for nursing diagnoses, interventions, and outcomes
Standardised nursing languages give nurses a common, consistent vocabulary. NANDA-I covers nursing DIAGNOSES, NIC (Nursing Interventions Classification) covers nursing INTERVENTIONS, and NOC (Nursing Outcomes Classification) covers patient OUTCOMES. Using them improves communication, documentation, electronic records, research and showing the value of nursing care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Make documentation more variable between institutions — They make documentation MORE consistent, not more variable.
• Replace physician orders — They describe nursing care; they don't replace physician orders.
• Track hospital finances — They are not tools for tracking hospital finances.

⭐ Key point: Remember the trio: NANDA = diagnosis, NIC = intervention, NOC = outcome.$x$, null),
(161, $x$Which of the following goal statements is correctly written in SMART format?$x$, $x$"Client will ambulate the length of the hallway (20 meters) without shortness of breath by the end of the 3-day shift"$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: "Client will ambulate the length of the hallway (20 meters) without shortness of breath by the end of the 3-day shift"
A SMART goal is Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Realistic and Time-bound. "Client will ambulate the length of the hallway (20 metres) without shortness of breath by the end of the 3-day shift" says who (the client), what (walk), how much (20 metres), under what condition (without shortness of breath) and by when (end of 3 days). It is patient-centred and measurable.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• "Client will feel better soon" — "Feel better soon" is vague and not measurable or time-bound.
• "Nurse will help the client walk" — "Nurse will help the client walk" is a nursing action (intervention), not a patient goal.
• "Client's pain will improve" — "Pain will improve" has no measure (such as a pain score) and no time frame.

⭐ Key point: Write goals about the PATIENT, with an action verb, a measurable criterion and a time frame. Another useful format is RUMBA.$x$, null),
(162, $x$A retrospective nursing audit involves reviewing records:$x$, $x$After the patient has been discharged$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: After the patient has been discharged
A RETROSPECTIVE audit looks BACK at care after it has finished, by reviewing the patient's records after discharge. It checks whether care met the set standards (for example, whether every patient had a VTE assessment). It is easy to do but can't change care for that patient.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• During the care of the patient — Reviewing during the patient's care is a CONCURRENT audit.
• Before the patient is admitted to the hospital — Before admission there is no care to audit.
• To plan the patient's care before it begins — Planning care before it begins is care planning, not an audit.

⭐ Key point: Concurrent audit = during care (can correct problems as they happen). Retrospective audit = after discharge (looks for patterns to improve future care).$x$, null),
(163, $x$A 'null hypothesis' (H0) states that$x$, $x$There is no statistically significant relationship or difference between the variables being studied$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: There is no statistically significant relationship or difference between the variables being studied
The null hypothesis (H₀) states that there is NO relationship or difference between the variables being studied. For example: "There is no difference in VAP rates between patients who receive chlorhexidine mouth care and those who don't." Statistical tests check whether the data are strong enough to REJECT the null hypothesis (usually p below 0.05).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• There is definitely a significant relationship between the variables — Stating there IS a significant relationship describes the alternative (research) hypothesis.
• The researcher's prediction is automatically correct — No hypothesis is "automatically correct"; it must be tested with data.
• Data collection is unnecessary — Data are always needed to test a hypothesis.

⭐ Key point: H₀ = no difference. H₁ (alternative) = there is a difference. Rejecting H₀ wrongly is a Type I error; failing to reject a false H₀ is a Type II error.$x$, null),
(164, $x$Which variable is NOT manipulated by the researcher but measured as the outcome?$x$, $x$Dependent variable$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Dependent variable
The DEPENDENT variable is the outcome that is MEASURED. Its value "depends" on the independent variable. The researcher doesn't change it directly. Example: in a study of whether early mobilisation shortens ICU stay, the length of stay is the dependent variable.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Extraneous variable — An extraneous variable is an outside factor that might affect the result and needs controlling.
• Independent variable — The independent variable is the one the researcher manipulates or changes (the cause), such as early mobilisation.
• Internal variable — "Internal variable" is not a standard research term.

⭐ Key point: Independent = cause (what you change). Dependent = effect (what you measure).$x$, null),
(165, $x$Probability sampling gives every member of the population:$x$, $x$An equal chance of being selected$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: An equal chance of being selected
In PROBABILITY (random) sampling, every member of the population has a known, usually EQUAL, chance of being chosen, as in drawing names from a hat or using random number tables. This reduces selection bias and allows the results to be generalised to the whole population. Types include simple random, stratified random, systematic and cluster sampling.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Equal representation — Equal representation isn't guaranteed; random sampling gives an equal CHANCE, but the sample may not match the population perfectly.
• Guaranteed selection — No one is guaranteed to be chosen in random sampling.
• No chance of selection — With no chance of selection, there would be no sampling at all.

⭐ Key point: Non-probability sampling (convenience, purposive, snowball, quota) is common in qualitative research but cannot be generalised as confidently.$x$, null),
(166, $x$A patient's body weight measured in kilograms is an example of which scale of measurement?$x$, $x$Ratio$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Ratio
Weight in kilograms is RATIO data. It has equal intervals between values and a TRUE ZERO (0 kg means no weight), so you can say "80 kg is twice as heavy as 40 kg". Other ratio examples: height, age, urine output, heart rate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nominal — Nominal data are names or categories without order (blood group, gender).
• Ordinal — Ordinal data have an order but unequal gaps (pain scored mild, moderate, severe; GCS).
• Interval — Interval data have equal gaps but NO true zero (temperature in °C, where 0 °C is not "no temperature").

⭐ Key point: Remember NOIR: Nominal, Ordinal, Interval, Ratio, from least to most information.$x$, null),
(167, $x$The following are Sources of literature review EXCEPT$x$, $x$Social medias$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Social medias
The question asks which is NOT a proper source for a literature review. Social media (Facebook, WhatsApp, Instagram, TikTok) is not a reliable academic source: posts are not peer-reviewed and often contain opinions or misinformation. Good sources include journal articles, dissertations and theses, books, existing theories, reports and reputable internet databases (PubMed, CINAHL, Cochrane).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• dissertations — Dissertations ARE a valid source (they are reviewed academic work).
• existing theory — Existing theory IS a valid source and often frames the study.
• Internet — The internet can be a valid source when using reputable databases and websites (journals, WHO, government sites).

⭐ Key point: Prefer primary sources (original research reports) and recent, peer-reviewed literature (usually within the last 5–10 years).$x$, null),
(168, $x$The "mean" of a data set is:$x$, $x$The sum of all values divided by the number of values$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The sum of all values divided by the number of values
The MEAN is the arithmetic average: add up all the values and divide by how many values there are. Example: heart rates 70, 80 and 90 give (70 + 80 + 90) ÷ 3 = 80. The mean uses every value but is pulled by extreme values (outliers).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The most frequently occurring value — The most frequently occurring value is the MODE.
• The middle value when data are arranged in order — The middle value when the data are in order is the MEDIAN.
• The difference between the highest and lowest values — The difference between the highest and lowest values is the RANGE.

⭐ Key point: For skewed data (such as length of hospital stay), the median describes the middle better than the mean.$x$, null),
(169, $x$In most nursing and health research, a result is typically considered "statistically significant" when the p-value is:$x$, $x$Less than or equal to 0.05$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Less than or equal to 0.05
A p-value of 0.05 or less (p ≤ 0.05) is the usual cut-off for statistical significance in health research. It means there is a 5% or smaller probability that the result happened by chance if the null hypothesis were true. So the researcher rejects the null hypothesis and accepts that there is likely a real difference or relationship.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Greater than 0.05 — A p-value above 0.05 is NOT significant; the result could easily be chance.
• Equal to 1.0 — p = 1.0 means no evidence at all against the null hypothesis.
• Greater than 0.5 — p above 0.5 is far from significant.

⭐ Key point: Statistical significance doesn't always mean clinical importance. Also look at the size of the effect and the confidence interval.$x$, null),
(170, $x$The Chi-square test is most appropriately used to analyse:$x$, $x$The relationship or association between two categorical (nominal) variables$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The relationship or association between two categorical (nominal) variables
The chi-square (χ²) test analyses the relationship between CATEGORICAL (nominal or ordinal) variables, using counts or frequencies in a table. Example: is there an association between gender (male/female) and developing VAP (yes/no)? It compares the observed numbers with the numbers expected if there were no relationship.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The correlation between two continuous variables — The correlation between two continuous variables uses Pearson's r (or Spearman's).
• The mean difference between two groups — The mean difference between two groups uses a t-test (or ANOVA for three or more groups).
• The standard deviation of a single sample — The standard deviation describes the spread of one sample; it is not a chi-square test.

⭐ Key point: Choose the test by data type: categories → chi-square; means of 2 groups → t-test; means of 3+ groups → ANOVA; relationship between 2 continuous variables → correlation.$x$, null),
(171, $x$A quasi-experimental design differs from a true experimental design mainly because it$x$, $x$Lacks random assignment of subjects to groups, though it may still involve manipulation of the independent variable$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Lacks random assignment of subjects to groups, though it may still involve manipulation of the independent variable
A true experiment has three features: manipulation of the independent variable, a control group, and RANDOM assignment of participants to groups. A quasi-experimental design still tests an intervention but LACKS random assignment (and sometimes a control group), often because randomising is not possible or ethical in real wards. This makes it weaker for proving cause and effect.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Never involves any form of intervention — Quasi-experiments DO involve an intervention.
• Always has a larger sample size than true experiments — Sample size is not what defines the design.
• Cannot be used to study cause-and-effect relationships at all — They can suggest cause and effect, though less strongly than RCTs.

⭐ Key point: The randomised controlled trial (RCT) is the strongest single study design for testing treatments. A systematic review of RCTs sits at the top of the evidence hierarchy.$x$, null),
(172, $x$A pilot study provides the opportunity to:$x$, $x$Test the research instrument$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Test the research instrument
A pilot study is a small trial run done before the main study. Its main purpose is to TEST the research instrument (questionnaire, checklist, equipment) and procedures: are the questions clear, how long does it take, does data collection work, is the tool reliable? Problems are fixed before the full study, saving time and money.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Publish the results early — Pilot results are not meant for early publication; they test feasibility.
• Test the research subjects — Pilot studies test the methods, not the research subjects themselves.
• Understand the research problem — The research problem should be understood before the pilot, from the literature review.

⭐ Key point: Participants in the pilot are usually NOT included in the main study sample.$x$, null),
(173, $x$'Reliability' of a research instrument refers to$x$, $x$The consistency and stability with which the instrument produces the same results on repeated administration$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The consistency and stability with which the instrument produces the same results on repeated administration
RELIABILITY is how CONSISTENT and STABLE an instrument is: it gives the same result when used again under the same conditions, or by different people. Example: a reliable pain scale gives similar scores if the patient's pain hasn't changed. Types include test–retest, inter-rater reliability and internal consistency (Cronbach's alpha, aim for 0.7 or above).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The degree to which the instrument measures the intended concept accurately — Measuring the intended concept accurately is VALIDITY, not reliability.
• The cost of producing and distributing the instrument — The cost of an instrument is unrelated to reliability.
• The number of items contained in a questionnaire — The number of items does not define reliability.

⭐ Key point: Reliability = consistency. Validity = accuracy (measures what it should). A tool can be reliable but not valid, but it cannot be valid without being reliable.$x$, null),
(174, $x$The first and most important function of a manager is:$x$, $x$Planning$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Planning
PLANNING is the first and most basic function of management. It means deciding in advance what to do, how, when and by whom: setting goals and objectives, and choosing the best methods. All the other functions (organising, staffing, directing, controlling) depend on the plan. Without planning, work is reactive and disorganised.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Delegation — Delegation is a part of directing and organising; it follows planning.
• Funding — Funding (budgeting) is part of planning, not the first function itself.
• Supervision — Supervision is part of directing and controlling, which come after planning.

⭐ Key point: Fayol's functions of management, in order: Planning, Organising, Commanding (Directing), Coordinating, Controlling.$x$, null),
(175, $x$Henri Fayol's 14 principles include the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Strategic management$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Strategic management
The question asks which is NOT one of Fayol's 14 principles. "Strategic management" is a modern management concept, not one of Henri Fayol's 14 principles (1916). His principles include division of labour, authority and responsibility, discipline, unity of command, unity of direction, subordination of individual interest, remuneration, centralisation, scalar chain, order, equity, stability of tenure, initiative, and esprit de corps.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Division of labour — Division of labour IS one of his principles (specialisation improves efficiency).
• Discipline — Discipline IS one of his principles (respect for rules and agreements).
• Unity of command — Unity of command IS one of his principles (each worker has only one boss).

⭐ Key point: Fayol is the father of administrative (modern) management; Taylor is the father of scientific management.$x$, null),
(176, $x$The ‘father’ of Scientific Management is:$x$, $x$Frederick W. Taylor$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Frederick W. Taylor
Frederick Winslow Taylor is the "father of scientific management" (Principles of Scientific Management, 1911). He used time-and-motion studies to find the "one best way" to do each task, selected and trained workers scientifically, and linked pay to output (piece-rate). His ideas aimed to increase efficiency and productivity.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Henry Gantt — Henry Gantt was Taylor's associate and created the Gantt chart for scheduling.
• W. Edwards Deming — W. Edwards Deming is linked with Total Quality Management and the PDSA cycle.
• Henry Ford — Henry Ford introduced the moving assembly line in car manufacturing.

⭐ Key point: Classical management thinkers: Taylor (scientific management), Fayol (administrative management), Weber (bureaucracy).$x$, null),
(177, $x$In Herzberg's theory, the following are motivators EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Money (salary)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Money (salary)
Herzberg's two-factor theory separates MOTIVATORS (which create satisfaction and drive people to work harder: achievement, recognition, responsibility, the work itself, advancement and growth) from HYGIENE factors (which prevent dissatisfaction but don't motivate: salary/money, working conditions, company policy, supervision, job security, relationships). Money is a HYGIENE factor, so it is the exception.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Recognition — Recognition IS a motivator.
• Achievement — Achievement IS a motivator.
• Challenging work — Challenging work (the work itself) IS a motivator.

⭐ Key point: Poor pay causes dissatisfaction, but good pay alone does not motivate long-term. Recognition and growth do.$x$, null),
(178, $x$Which leadership style is most appropriate during a mass casualty emergency?$x$, $x$Autocratic$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Autocratic
In a mass casualty emergency there is no time for discussion. Quick, clear decisions and firm direction are needed to save lives. The AUTOCRATIC (authoritarian) style, where the leader makes decisions and gives direct orders, is most effective in emergencies, cardiac arrests and situations with inexperienced staff.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Democratic — The democratic style involves the team in decisions; it is good for planning but too slow in a crisis.
• Laissez-faire — The laissez-faire style gives little direction; it would be dangerous in an emergency.
• Affiliative — The affiliative style focuses on harmony and feelings, not quick decisions.

⭐ Key point: No leadership style is best for everything. Choose based on the situation (situational leadership). Transformational leadership is best for long-term improvement.$x$, null),
(179, $x$A nurse delegates the administration of intravenous medication to a nursing assistant. This action is:$x$, $x$Inappropriate because it exceeds the assistant's scope of practice$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Inappropriate because it exceeds the assistant's scope of practice
Giving IV medication requires knowledge, judgement and skills (drug calculations, checking compatibility, monitoring for reactions) that are within the scope of a registered nurse, not a nursing assistant. Delegating it is INAPPROPRIATE and unsafe. If harm occurs, the nurse who delegated is accountable.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Appropriate — It is not appropriate, because the task is outside the assistant's role.
• Acceptable if supervised — Supervision cannot make a task acceptable if it is outside the person's legal scope.
• Recommended during emergencies — Emergencies do not allow staff to work outside their scope of practice.

⭐ Key point: The 5 rights of delegation: right task, right circumstance, right person, right direction or communication, right supervision. You can delegate tasks, but not accountability.$x$, null),
(180, $x$Which of this budget is not part types of budget?$x$, $x$Supplementary budget$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Supplementary budget
The main types of budgets in health care management are the OPERATIONAL (operating) budget for day-to-day running costs, the CAPITAL budget for large, long-lasting items like equipment and buildings, and the PERSONNEL budget for staff salaries and benefits (often part of the operating budget). A "supplementary budget" is a government term for extra funds added during the year, not a standard type of nursing unit budget.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Personnel budget — The personnel budget IS a type (salaries, overtime, benefits).
• Operational budget — The operational budget IS a type (supplies, utilities, daily running).
• Capital budget — The capital budget IS a type (ventilators, monitors, building works).

⭐ Key point: Other budget approaches: zero-based budgeting (justify every item from zero), incremental budgeting (add a percentage to last year's budget), and flexible budgeting.$x$, null),
(181, $x$According to adult learning principles (andragogy), adults learn best when the content is:$x$, $x$Relevant, problem-centred, and builds on their prior experience$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Relevant, problem-centred, and builds on their prior experience
Malcolm Knowles' principles of adult learning (andragogy) say adults learn best when learning is RELEVANT to their work or life, PROBLEM-CENTRED (solving real problems) and builds on their EXPERIENCE. Adults are self-directed, motivated by internal factors, and want to know WHY they need to learn something. Use case studies, discussion, simulation and hands-on practice.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Abstract, theoretical, and disconnected from real experience — Abstract theory disconnected from experience is less effective for adults.
• Delivered purely through passive lecture with no interaction — Passive lectures with no interaction ignore adults' experience and self-direction.
• Identical regardless of the learner's background — Adults differ; teaching should consider each learner's background.

⭐ Key point: Pedagogy = teaching children (teacher-directed). Andragogy = teaching adults (self-directed, experience-based).$x$, null),
(182, $x$A method of Teaching includes all the following Except....$x$, $x$Tertiary$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Tertiary
The question asks which is NOT a teaching method. "Tertiary" means third level (as in tertiary education or tertiary hospitals); it is not a method of teaching. Teaching methods include explanation, lecture, demonstration (with return demonstration), discussion, role play, simulation, case study, tutorials and e-learning.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Explanation — Explanation IS a teaching method.
• Lecturing — Lecturing IS a teaching method (good for large groups and knowledge).
• Demonstration — Demonstration IS a teaching method (best for practical skills).

⭐ Key point: For skills, use demonstration and return demonstration. For attitudes, use discussion and role play. For knowledge, use lectures, reading and e-learning.$x$, null),
(183, $x$Evaluation that occurs continuously throughout the implementation of care (e.g., during each shift) is called:$x$, $x$Formative (ongoing) evaluation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Formative (ongoing) evaluation
FORMATIVE evaluation happens CONTINUOUSLY during care, for example each shift: checking the patient's response and adjusting the plan as you go. It "forms" the care while it is happening. In nursing, ongoing reassessment (such as pain scores after analgesia) is formative evaluation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Summative evaluation — Summative evaluation happens at the END (for example at discharge) to judge the final outcome.
• Structure evaluation — Structure evaluation looks at resources (staff, equipment, buildings), not ongoing patient care.
• Terminal evaluation — Terminal evaluation is another name for evaluation at the end, which is summative.

⭐ Key point: Formative = during (to improve). Summative = at the end (to judge the result).$x$, null),
(184, $x$An incident report should be completed when:$x$, $x$A medication error occurs$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A medication error occurs
An incident report is completed for any event that caused, or could have caused, harm to a patient, visitor or staff member, or that is not consistent with routine care. A MEDICATION ERROR is a classic example. Others: falls, needlestick injuries, equipment failures, wrong-patient procedures and near misses. The report is used to learn and improve the system, not to blame.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A patient is discharged — Discharge is routine and needs no incident report.
• A nurse resumes duty — Resuming duty is routine.
• The ward is cleaned — Cleaning the ward is routine.

⭐ Key point: The incident report is NOT placed in the patient's notes, and the notes don't say "incident form completed"; record the facts of what happened and the care given.$x$, null),
(185, $x$The Nursing and Midwifery Council of Nigeria (NMCN), as a professional regulatory body, is primarily responsible for:$x$, $x$Setting nursing education standards, registering/licensing nurses, and enforcing professional discipline$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Setting nursing education standards, registering/licensing nurses, and enforcing professional discipline
The Nursing and Midwifery Council of Nigeria (NMCN) is the government body that regulates nursing and midwifery in Nigeria. Its roles: setting and monitoring standards of nursing and midwifery education, accrediting schools, conducting licensing examinations (including post-basic council exams), registering and licensing nurses and midwives, renewing licences, and DISCIPLINING professional misconduct to protect the public.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only organising nursing conferences — Organising conferences is not its main legal function.
• Managing hospital construction budgets — Hospital construction budgets are not its responsibility.
• Setting national drug prices — Drug prices are not set by the NMCN.

⭐ Key point: The professional association and union for nurses is NANNM (National Association of Nigerian Nurses and Midwives), which is different from the regulator, the NMCN.$x$, null),
(186, $x$The doctrine of 'Res Ipsa Loquitur' ('the thing speaks for itself') applies when:$x$, $x$An injury is of a type that would not normally occur without someone's negligence (e.g. a retained surgical instrument)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: An injury is of a type that would not normally occur without someone's negligence (e.g. a retained surgical instrument)
Res ipsa loquitur ("the thing speaks for itself") is a legal rule used when the injury is of a kind that would NOT normally happen without negligence, the thing that caused it was under the professional's control, and the patient did not contribute. A swab or instrument left inside after surgery is the classic example. The patient doesn't have to prove exactly how the negligence happened.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A patient explicitly consents to a risky procedure — A patient consenting to a risky procedure doesn't apply here; consent relates to known risks, not negligence.
• A nurse documents care accurately — Accurate documentation is good practice and not related to this doctrine.
• A patient refuses treatment — A patient's refusal of treatment is about autonomy and consent.

⭐ Key point: Prevent such events with surgical safety checklists, swab and instrument counts, and correct-site marking.$x$, null),
(187, $x$The four elements that must be proven to establish nursing negligence are:$x$, $x$Duty, breach of duty, causation, and damages$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Duty, breach of duty, causation, and damages
To prove negligence, all FOUR elements must be shown. 1) DUTY of care existed (a nurse–patient relationship). 2) BREACH of that duty (care fell below the expected standard). 3) CAUSATION (the breach directly caused the harm). 4) DAMAGES (actual harm or loss occurred). If any element is missing, negligence is not proven.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Consent, capacity, competence, and cost — Consent, capacity, competence and cost relate to consent and resources, not negligence.
• Autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, and justice — Autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence and justice are ETHICAL principles.
• Assessment, diagnosis, planning, and evaluation — Assessment, diagnosis, planning and evaluation are steps of the NURSING PROCESS.

⭐ Key point: Good documentation, following policies, staying within your scope and keeping up to date are the nurse's best protection against negligence claims.$x$, null),
(188, $x$'Battery' in a legal/nursing context refers to:$x$, $x$Unlawful physical contact with a patient without consent$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Unlawful physical contact with a patient without consent
Battery is unlawful, intentional TOUCHING of a person without their consent, even if no harm is done and even if the touching was meant to help. Examples: giving an injection, inserting a catheter or doing a procedure when the patient has refused or not consented. Assault is threatening to touch, causing fear of being touched.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A device that powers medical equipment — A battery that powers equipment is the everyday meaning, not the legal one.
• A type of medication error — A medication error is a different issue (possible negligence).
• A category of documentation — Battery is not a documentation category.

⭐ Key point: Always get valid consent before touching or treating a patient, and respect refusal. In emergencies with no capacity, act in the patient's best interests.$x$, null),
(189, $x$For informed consent to be legally valid, it must include all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Approval from a family member instead of the patient, when the patient has full capacity$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Approval from a family member instead of the patient, when the patient has full capacity
The question asks which is NOT needed for valid consent. If the adult patient has full capacity, only THEY can consent. A family member's approval cannot replace the patient's own decision. Valid consent needs adequate INFORMATION (risks, benefits, alternatives), CAPACITY (able to understand, retain, weigh and communicate) and VOLUNTARY agreement (no pressure).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Adequate information given to the patient — Adequate information IS required.
• Patient capacity to understand the information — Patient capacity IS required.
• Voluntary agreement, free from coercion — Voluntary agreement IS required.

⭐ Key point: Family can be involved and consulted, but they cannot consent for or overrule a competent adult. Consent for children is given by parents or guardians.$x$, null),
(190, $x$'Veracity' as an ethical principle in nursing means:$x$, $x$Truthfulness and honesty with patients$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Truthfulness and honesty with patients
VERACITY means TRUTHFULNESS: being honest with patients, not lying or deceiving them, and giving information they need to make decisions. It builds trust and supports autonomy (informed consent). It also means being honest when errors occur (duty of candour).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Loyalty and faithfulness to commitments — Loyalty and faithfulness to commitments is FIDELITY.
• Fairness in resource distribution — Fairness in distributing resources is JUSTICE.
• Avoiding harm — Avoiding harm is NON-MALEFICENCE.

⭐ Key point: Ethical principles: autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, justice, veracity (truth), fidelity (keeping promises) and confidentiality.$x$, null),
(191, $x$A Do-Not-Resuscitate (DNR) order should be:$x$, $x$Clearly documented in the medical record and communicated to the entire care team$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Clearly documented in the medical record and communicated to the entire care team
A DNR (DNACPR) order must be CLEARLY DOCUMENTED in the patient's record (on the proper form, signed and dated by the senior doctor responsible) and COMMUNICATED to the whole team at handover, so everyone knows not to start CPR. It should be discussed with the patient (if they have capacity) and/or the family, and reviewed regularly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Assumed automatically for all elderly patients — A DNR is never automatic for elderly patients; age alone is not a reason.
• Kept verbal only, never written — A verbal-only DNR is unsafe and legally risky.
• Decided solely by the nurse without physician or patient/family involvement — The nurse cannot decide alone; a senior doctor makes the decision with the patient or family.

⭐ Key point: DNR means no CPR only. All other care (pain relief, oxygen, feeding, antibiotics, dignity) continues unless separately decided.$x$, null),
(192, $x$The Good Samaritan act covers:$x$, $x$Protection from civil liability when emergency care is provided in good faith$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Protection from civil liability when emergency care is provided in good faith
Good Samaritan laws protect people who give EMERGENCY care in GOOD FAITH, outside their job (such as at a roadside accident), from civil liability (being sued), as long as they act within their training, without payment, and without gross negligence. These laws encourage health workers and the public to help in emergencies.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Residents of long-term care facilities — Residents of long-term care facilities are covered by care standards, not specifically this law.
• Passerby in car accidents — Passersby are covered only when they help in good faith; the law protects helpers, not people simply present.
• Nurses who choose not to provide care in an emergency — Refusing to help in an emergency is not protected, and it may raise ethical issues.

⭐ Key point: When helping at the roadside, stay within your skills, get consent if possible, hand over to emergency services, and document what you did afterwards.$x$, null),
(193, $x$If you make a mistake in a record how should it be corrected?$x$, $x$Put a single line through the record$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Put a single line through the record
To correct an error in a record, draw a SINGLE LINE through the wrong entry so it can still be read. Write "error" (or "written in error"), and add your initials or signature, the date and the time. Then write the correct information. This keeps the record honest and shows exactly what was changed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• With correction fluid — Correction fluid hides the original entry, which looks like falsification of records.
• Scribble it out — Scribbling out makes the original unreadable and looks like tampering.
• Tell your mentor — Telling your mentor alone does not correct the record legally.

⭐ Key point: Never erase, tear out pages, or leave blank spaces. For electronic records, use the system's correction or amendment function.$x$, null),
(194, $x$A conscious, competent adult patient refuses a blood transfusion on religious grounds despite life-threatening anaemia. The nurse's ethically and legally appropriate response is to:$x$, $x$Respect the patient's autonomous refusal after ensuring they are fully informed, and document thoroughly$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Respect the patient's autonomous refusal after ensuring they are fully informed, and document thoroughly
A conscious adult with capacity has the legal and ethical right to refuse ANY treatment, even life-saving treatment, including a blood transfusion for religious reasons (such as Jehovah's Witnesses). This respects AUTONOMY. The nurse makes sure the patient is fully informed of the risks, explores alternatives (iron, erythropoietin, cell salvage, tranexamic acid), and DOCUMENTS the refusal carefully. Care continues.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administer the transfusion anyway, since the situation is life-threatening — Giving blood anyway would be battery and a violation of autonomy.
• Have a family member override the patient's decision — Family members cannot override a competent adult's decision.
• Discharge the patient immediately with no further care — Discharging the patient without care is abandonment; other care continues.

⭐ Key point: An advance decision (such as a "no blood" card) must also be respected. For children, the courts can authorise transfusion in an emergency.$x$, null),
(195, $x$Which of the following is a recognised advantage of the Subjective Global Assessment (SGA) over purely laboratory-based nutritional assessment?$x$, $x$It reliably predicts nutrition-related clinical outcomes and is not confounded by fluid shifts or acute-phase inflammatory changes the way serum albumin can be$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: It reliably predicts nutrition-related clinical outcomes and is not confounded by fluid shifts or acute-phase inflammatory changes the way serum albumin can be
Subjective Global Assessment (SGA) is a bedside nutrition assessment based on history (weight loss, intake, gut symptoms, function) and examination (loss of fat and muscle, oedema). It predicts nutrition-related outcomes well. Unlike serum albumin, it is NOT distorted by fluid shifts or acute inflammation: albumin falls in critical illness because of inflammation and dilution, not just malnutrition.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It requires no clinician training — SGA DOES need training to do well.
• It does not require any patient history — SGA DEPENDS on the patient's history; it is not done without it.
• It replaces the need for anthropometric data entirely — SGA uses clinical examination of body composition; it does not replace all anthropometric data.

⭐ Key point: In critically ill patients, albumin and prealbumin are poor nutrition markers. Use clinical tools (SGA, NUTRIC score) instead.$x$, null),
(196, $x$A patient on TPN is at risk for refeeding syndrome. The nurse should be most vigilant for which of the following electrolyte imbalances?$x$, $x$Hypophosphatemia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hypophosphatemia
In refeeding syndrome, starved patients are suddenly given nutrition (enteral or TPN). Insulin rises and drives glucose, PHOSPHATE, potassium and magnesium into the cells. Serum phosphate can fall dangerously, causing muscle weakness (including the diaphragm, leading to breathing failure and failure to wean), heart failure, arrhythmias, confusion, seizures and death. HYPOPHOSPHATAEMIA is the hallmark.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypernatremia — Sodium can shift, but high sodium is not the typical refeeding problem.
• Hyperkalemia — Potassium FALLS (hypokalaemia), it doesn't rise.
• Hypocalcemia — Calcium can be affected but is not the key electrolyte in refeeding.

⭐ Key point: Prevent refeeding: identify at-risk patients (low BMI, little intake for 5 days or more, alcohol misuse), give thiamine before feeding, start slowly (10–20 kcal/kg/day), and check phosphate, potassium and magnesium daily.$x$, null),
(197, $x$Enteral nutrition is generally preferred over parenteral nutrition when:$x$, $x$The gut is functioning$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The gut is functioning
"If the gut works, use it." Enteral nutrition (via the gut, by mouth or tube) is preferred whenever the gastrointestinal tract is FUNCTIONING. It keeps the gut lining healthy, supports gut immunity, reduces infections and bacterial translocation, has fewer complications than TPN (no line infections, fewer metabolic problems), and is cheaper.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The gut has completely failed — If the gut has completely failed (obstruction, severe ileus, short bowel), parenteral nutrition is used.
• Never — parenteral is always preferred — Parenteral is NOT always preferred; it is the second choice.
• Only in paediatric patients — Enteral feeding is preferred in adults and children alike.

⭐ Key point: Start enteral feeding within 24–48 hours in ICU when the patient is haemodynamically stable. Use parenteral nutrition only if enteral feeding is impossible or not enough.$x$, null),
(198, $x$A nasogastric tube's position should be confirmed before feeding by:$x$, $x$pH testing of aspirate and/or X-ray confirmation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: pH testing of aspirate and/or X-ray confirmation
Before the first feed (and before each feed or drug), the NG tube position must be checked. Approved methods: aspirate gastric fluid and test its pH with pH paper (pH 5.5 or below means the stomach), or, if no aspirate or pH is above 5.5, get a CHEST X-RAY read by a competent person. The X-ray is the gold standard, especially in ventilated or unconscious patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Auscultation ('whoosh test') alone — The "whoosh test" (listening for air) is UNRELIABLE and must never be used; it can sound the same if the tube is in the lung.
• The patient's verbal comfort report only — The patient's comfort tells you nothing about position; tubes in the lung may cause no symptoms.
• Tube colour — The tube's colour does not show where it is.

⭐ Key point: Feeding through a misplaced NG tube (in the lung) is a "never event". Mark the tube's length at the nostril and recheck it before use.$x$, null),
(199, $x$Manual hyperinflation$x$, $x$Technique is used in the management of ventilated patients$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Technique is used in the management of ventilated patients
Manual hyperinflation (MHI) is a chest physiotherapy technique used in VENTILATED (intubated) patients. The patient is disconnected and given slow, deep breaths with a manual bag (larger than normal tidal volume), followed by a short inspiratory hold and then a quick release. This mimics a cough, re-expands collapsed lung areas, and moves secretions up to be suctioned.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Involve the use of expiratory pause of 5-10 seconds — The hold is short (inspiratory pause of 2–3 seconds), not a 5–10 second expiratory pause.
• Is used to provide fast and slow expiratory breath — MHI uses a slow deep breath in and a FAST release, not "fast and slow expiratory breaths".
• Technique is not used in the management of patient's self-ventilating — It is used mainly in intubated patients, but this statement is weaker than the correct one, which describes its main use.

⭐ Key point: Avoid MHI in unstable patients, those with high PEEP needs, undrained pneumothorax, bronchospasm or raised ICP. Monitor BP and SpO₂ during it.$x$, null),
(200, $x$Electrotherapy equipment includes:$x$, $x$Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS)
Electrotherapy uses electrical energy to treat patients, mainly for pain relief and muscle stimulation. TENS (transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation) sends mild electrical pulses through skin pads. These block pain signals (the "gate control" theory) and may release endorphins. It is used for pain after surgery, in labour and in chronic pain.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• MRI — MRI is a diagnostic imaging scanner using magnets, not a therapy.
• Mammogram — A mammogram is an X-ray of the breast for diagnosis.
• Spirometer — A spirometer measures lung volumes and flow (a diagnostic or monitoring tool).

⭐ Key point: Do not use TENS over a pacemaker, the front of the neck, broken skin or the pregnant abdomen (except approved labour use), or in patients with epilepsy (over the head).$x$, null)
),
upd as (
  update public.questions qq
     set exp = r.exp,
         ans_idx = coalesce((select (t.i - 1)::int from jsonb_array_elements_text(qq.opts) with ordinality t(v, i) where t.v = r.fixed_answer), qq.ans_idx)
    from r
   where qq.q = r.q
     and qq.opts ->> qq.ans_idx = r.answer
  returning r.n
)
insert into rationale_report select 1, count(distinct n), count(*) from upd
  on conflict (day) do update set questions_updated = excluded.questions_updated, rows_changed = excluded.rows_changed;

-- ===================== DAY 2 =====================
-- Nursing Exam Room: full rationales for CCN Mock Exam — Day 2 (200 questions)
-- Run this once in the Supabase SQL Editor. It is safe to run again.
--
-- Each explanation now says: the correct answer and why, why each other option is wrong,
-- and a key point to remember. The same questions in the practice app get the new explanation too.
-- Questions are found by their exact wording and correct answer. Where the old answer key was wrong,
-- the correct option is also fixed (listed at the top of the file).


with r(n, q, answer, exp, fixed_answer) as (values
(1, $x$In adults, the primary site of haematopoiesis is the:$x$, $x$Red bone marrow$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Red bone marrow
HAEMATOPOIESIS means the making of blood cells (red cells, white cells and platelets). In ADULTS it happens mainly in the RED BONE MARROW of the flat bones and the ends of long bones — the sternum, ribs, vertebrae, pelvis (iliac crest), skull and the upper ends of the femur and humerus. All blood cells come from one parent cell, the haematopoietic STEM cell, found in this marrow. That is why bone marrow aspiration/biopsy is taken from the iliac crest or sternum when we want to study blood disorders such as leukaemia or aplastic anaemia. In the unborn baby the order is different: yolk sac first, then liver and spleen, then bone marrow by birth.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Liver — The LIVER is the main site of blood formation in the FOETUS (middle months of pregnancy), not in the healthy adult. It only returns to this job in disease (extramedullary haematopoiesis, e.g. thalassaemia, myelofibrosis).
• Spleen — The SPLEEN filters and destroys old red cells and stores platelets and lymphocytes; it makes blood cells only in the foetus or in marrow failure.
• Lymph nodes — LYMPH NODES filter lymph and help lymphocytes to multiply and mature, but they do not make red cells or platelets.

⭐ Key point: Adult blood cells are made in RED BONE MARROW (sternum, pelvis, vertebrae, ribs, skull). Foetus: yolk sac → liver/spleen → marrow.$x$, null),
(2, $x$A patient develops severe hypocalcemia with perioral tingling, muscle cramps, and a positive Chvostek sign after total thyroidectomy. Which complication is most likely?$x$, $x$Inadvertent removal or devascularization of the parathyroid glands$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Inadvertent removal or devascularization of the parathyroid glands
After TOTAL THYROIDECTOMY the small PARATHYROID GLANDS (usually four, sitting behind the thyroid) can be removed by mistake or lose their blood supply. Parathyroid hormone (PTH) keeps blood calcium up, so without it calcium falls (HYPOCALCAEMIA), usually 24–72 hours after surgery. Low calcium makes nerves and muscles over-excitable: tingling around the mouth and fingers, muscle cramps, CHVOSTEK sign (tapping the facial nerve in front of the ear makes the face twitch), TROUSSEAU sign (BP cuff inflated makes the hand go into spasm), and in severe cases laryngospasm, seizures and arrhythmia (long QT). Nursing: keep IV calcium gluconate 10% at the bedside, check serum calcium, report tingling early.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Thyroid storm — THYROID STORM gives high fever, fast heart rate, agitation and sweating — a hypermetabolic picture, not low-calcium tetany.
• Recurrent laryngeal nerve injury — RECURRENT LARYNGEAL NERVE injury causes HOARSENESS or a weak voice (and stridor if both sides are cut), not tingling or a positive Chvostek sign.
• Adrenal insufficiency — ADRENAL INSUFFICIENCY gives low BP, weakness, low sodium and high potassium; it is not linked to thyroid surgery.

⭐ Key point: Tingling + cramps + Chvostek/Trousseau after thyroidectomy = HYPOCALCAEMIA from parathyroid injury. Give IV calcium gluconate; keep it at the bedside.$x$, null),
(3, $x$A normal adult's total lung capacity is approximately:$x$, $x$6000 mL$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 6000 mL
TOTAL LUNG CAPACITY (TLC) is the total air in the lungs after the deepest possible breath in. In a normal adult it is about 6000 mL (6 litres) — a little less in women and smaller people. TLC = vital capacity (about 4600–4800 mL) + residual volume (about 1200 mL, the air that always stays so the alveoli don't collapse). Other volumes to remember: tidal volume about 500 mL (normal quiet breath), inspiratory reserve about 3000 mL, expiratory reserve about 1100–1200 mL. TLC goes DOWN in restrictive disease (pulmonary fibrosis, obesity, chest wall deformity) and goes UP (hyperinflation) in obstructive disease like emphysema/COPD.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1500 mL — 1500 mL is too small; it is close to the residual volume plus a bit, not the whole lung.
• 2500 mL — 2500 mL is roughly the functional residual capacity (air left after a normal breath out), not total capacity.
• 10000 mL — 10000 mL (10 L) is far above normal for any adult human lung.

⭐ Key point: TLC ≈ 6000 mL; tidal volume ≈ 500 mL; residual volume ≈ 1200 mL; vital capacity ≈ 4800 mL.$x$, null),
(4, $x$The dermis contains all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Keratinocytes$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Keratinocytes
The skin has two main layers. The top layer, the EPIDERMIS, is made mostly of KERATINOCYTES — cells that make keratin, the tough protein that waterproofs and protects the skin. The epidermis has NO blood vessels; it is fed by diffusion from below. Under it is the DERMIS, a thick connective-tissue layer that holds blood vessels, nerve endings (touch, pain, pressure, temperature receptors), hair follicles, sweat glands, sebaceous (oil) glands, collagen and elastin. So the item NOT found as a main dermal component is the keratinocyte — it belongs to the epidermis. Remember: "E for Epidermis, K for Keratin on the outside; D for Dermis, Deep with vessels and nerves."

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Blood vessels — Blood vessels ARE in the dermis; they feed the skin and help control body temperature by dilating or constricting.
• Nerve endings — Nerve endings ARE in the dermis; they let us feel touch, pain, heat and pressure.
• Hair follicles — Hair follicles are rooted in the dermis (and reach into the subcutaneous layer), along with their sebaceous glands.

⭐ Key point: Keratinocytes = EPIDERMIS. Dermis = vessels, nerves, hair follicles, glands, collagen. The epidermis is avascular.$x$, null),
(5, $x$The hypothalamus links the nervous and endocrine systems and regulates all of the following EXCEPT$x$, $x$voluntary skeletal muscle coordination$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: voluntary skeletal muscle coordination
The HYPOTHALAMUS is a small area at the base of the brain that links the nervous system to the endocrine system through the pituitary gland. It is the body's "thermostat and control centre": it regulates body temperature, hunger and satiety, thirst and water balance (makes ADH), the sleep–wake (circadian) rhythm, emotions and stress responses, and controls pituitary hormones. It does NOT coordinate voluntary skeletal muscle movement. Smooth, accurate voluntary movement and balance are the job of the CEREBELLUM, working with the motor cortex and basal ganglia. That is why cerebellar disease causes ataxia, tremor on movement and poor coordination, while hypothalamic damage causes problems like fever, diabetes insipidus or abnormal appetite.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• body temperature — Body temperature IS regulated by the hypothalamus — that is why infection "resets" it and causes fever.
• circadian rhythm and sleep-wake cycle — Circadian rhythm and sleep–wake cycle ARE controlled by the hypothalamus (suprachiasmatic nucleus).
• hunger and thirst — Hunger and thirst ARE controlled by the hypothalamus (feeding/satiety centres; thirst and ADH).

⭐ Key point: Hypothalamus = temperature, hunger, thirst, sleep, hormones. Coordination of voluntary movement = CEREBELLUM.$x$, null),
(6, $x$Gaseous exchange in the lungs occurs primarily at the level of the:$x$, $x$Alveoli$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Alveoli
Gas exchange (oxygen in, carbon dioxide out) happens in the ALVEOLI — tiny air sacs at the end of the airways. There are about 300–500 million of them, giving a surface area of about 70 m² (half a tennis court). Their walls are only one cell thick and are wrapped in capillaries, so the gap between air and blood (the respiratory membrane) is extremely thin (about 0.5 micrometres). Oxygen diffuses from alveolus into blood, CO2 from blood into alveolus. Type I cells form the thin wall; Type II cells make surfactant to stop alveoli collapsing. Diseases that thicken the wall (fibrosis), fill the alveoli (pneumonia, pulmonary oedema) or destroy them (emphysema) all reduce gas exchange.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Trachea — The TRACHEA is a conducting airway; it warms, humidifies and filters air but no gas exchange happens there (it is part of anatomical dead space).
• Bronchi — The BRONCHI are also conducting airways (dead space); their walls are too thick for gas exchange.
• Larynx — The LARYNX is the voice box and protects the airway; it plays no part in gas exchange.

⭐ Key point: Gas exchange = ALVEOLI (huge area, very thin wall). Trachea, bronchi and larynx only conduct air (dead space).$x$, null),
(7, $x$Which epidermal layer is found only in thick (glabrous) skin, such as the palms and soles?$x$, $x$Stratum lucidum$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Stratum lucidum
The epidermis has up to five layers (deep to surface): stratum BASALE, SPINOSUM, GRANULOSUM, LUCIDUM and CORNEUM. Memory aid: "Come, Let's Get Sun Burnt" (from top: Corneum, Lucidum, Granulosum, Spinosum, Basale). The STRATUM LUCIDUM is a thin clear layer of dead, flattened cells containing eleidin. It is found ONLY in THICK (glabrous, hairless) skin — the PALMS of the hands and SOLES of the feet — where extra friction and pressure need extra protection. Thin skin over most of the body has only four layers.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stratum spinosum — STRATUM SPINOSUM ("prickly layer") is present in both thick and thin skin; it gives strength and contains Langerhans (immune) cells.
• Stratum granulosum — STRATUM GRANULOSUM is present in both; its cells start making keratin and waterproofing lipids and begin to die.
• Stratum basale — STRATUM BASALE is present in all skin; it is the deepest layer, where new cells are made by division and where melanocytes sit.

⭐ Key point: Stratum LUCIDUM = only in thick skin of palms and soles. Order deep→surface: Basale, Spinosum, Granulosum, Lucidum, Corneum.$x$, null),
(8, $x$Most nutrient absorption occurs in the:$x$, $x$Small intestine$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Small intestine
Most nutrient absorption takes place in the SMALL INTESTINE (duodenum, jejunum, ileum). It is about 6 metres long and its lining is folded into circular folds, VILLI and MICROVILLI, giving a huge surface (about 200–250 m²) for absorption. Carbohydrates (as glucose), proteins (as amino acids), fats (as fatty acids into lacteals), vitamins and minerals are absorbed here. Special sites: iron and calcium mainly in the duodenum; most nutrients in the jejunum; vitamin B12 and bile salts in the terminal ILEUM. That is why removing the ileum causes B12 deficiency, and diseases like coeliac disease (damaged villi) cause malabsorption.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stomach — The STOMACH mainly stores and mixes food and starts protein digestion with acid and pepsin; it absorbs only a little (water, alcohol, some drugs like aspirin).
• Large intestine — The LARGE INTESTINE mainly absorbs WATER and electrolytes and forms stool; very few nutrients are absorbed there (some vitamins made by gut bacteria, e.g. vitamin K).
• Oesophagus — The OESOPHAGUS simply carries food from the mouth to the stomach by peristalsis; it absorbs nothing.

⭐ Key point: Most absorption = SMALL INTESTINE (villi + microvilli). Iron → duodenum; B12 → terminal ileum; water → large intestine.$x$, null),
(9, $x$A patient with severe burns loses significant fluid through the damaged skin. This highlights the skin's normal physiological role in:$x$, $x$Fluid/temperature regulation and acting as a protective barrier against fluid loss and infection$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fluid/temperature regulation and acting as a protective barrier against fluid loss and infection
Intact skin is a barrier. It stops body water from evaporating away, keeps out germs, and helps control temperature through sweating and changes in blood flow. In SEVERE BURNS this barrier is destroyed, so the patient loses large amounts of fluid, protein and heat through the wound, and bacteria can enter easily. Capillaries also become leaky, so fluid shifts out of the blood. This explains why burns patients need aggressive IV fluid resuscitation (e.g. Parkland formula 4 mL × kg × %TBSA in 24 h, half in the first 8 h), warming to prevent hypothermia, and strict infection control. The question shows the skin's normal role in FLUID AND TEMPERATURE REGULATION and as a PROTECTIVE BARRIER.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hormone production only — "Hormone production ONLY" is wrong: the skin does help make vitamin D, but that is not what fluid loss in burns shows, and "only" makes it false.
• Filtering blood — FILTERING BLOOD is the job of the kidneys (and the spleen/liver), not the skin.
• Producing red blood cells — PRODUCING RED BLOOD CELLS is the job of the red bone marrow, not the skin.

⭐ Key point: Skin = barrier against fluid loss, infection and heat loss. Burns remove it → fluid loss, hypothermia, infection.$x$, null),
(10, $x$The kidneys are classified as retroperitoneal organs because they:$x$, $x$Lie behind the parietal peritoneum rather than within the peritoneal cavity$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Lie behind the parietal peritoneum rather than within the peritoneal cavity
The PERITONEUM is the thin membrane lining the abdominal cavity. Organs inside it, hung on a mesentery (e.g. stomach, most of the small bowel, transverse colon), are INTRAPERITONEAL. Organs that lie BEHIND the posterior parietal peritoneum, against the back wall of the abdomen, are RETROPERITONEAL. The KIDNEYS are retroperitoneal — covered by peritoneum only on their front surface. Other retroperitoneal organs, memory aid "SAD PUCKER": Suprarenal (adrenal) glands, Aorta/IVC, Duodenum (most), Pancreas (except tail), Ureters, Colon (ascending and descending), Kidneys, Esophagus, Rectum. Clinical link: kidney surgery can be done through the flank without opening the peritoneal cavity, and retroperitoneal bleeding may show as flank bruising (Grey Turner sign).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Are suspended by a mesentery inside the peritoneal cavity — Being SUSPENDED BY A MESENTERY inside the cavity is the definition of an INTRAPERITONEAL organ — the opposite.
• Float freely within the peritoneal cavity — No solid organ "floats freely" in the peritoneal cavity; all are anchored, and the kidneys are fixed in fat and fascia behind the peritoneum.
• Lie entirely anterior to the peritoneum, outside the abdomen — The kidneys lie POSTERIOR (behind) the peritoneum, inside the abdomen — not anterior to it and not outside the abdomen.

⭐ Key point: Retroperitoneal = BEHIND the parietal peritoneum. Kidneys, adrenals, pancreas, aorta, ureters ("SAD PUCKER").$x$, null),
(11, $x$Which meningeal layer lies closest to the brain surface?$x$, $x$Pia mater$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Pia mater
The brain and spinal cord are covered by three MENINGES. From outside in, the memory aid is "PAD" read backwards — Dura, Arachnoid, Pia (D-A-P from outside). DURA MATER ("tough mother") is the thick outer layer next to the skull. ARACHNOID MATER is the middle, web-like layer; under it is the SUBARACHNOID space containing cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) and big blood vessels. PIA MATER ("gentle mother") is the thinnest, innermost layer, stuck firmly to the brain surface and following every fold (gyri and sulci). Clinical: a subarachnoid haemorrhage bleeds into the space above the pia; lumbar puncture collects CSF from the subarachnoid space; meningitis is inflammation of the arachnoid and pia (leptomeninges).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dura mater — DURA MATER is the OUTERMOST meninx, lying against the inside of the skull — the farthest from the brain.
• Arachnoid mater — ARACHNOID MATER is the MIDDLE layer, separated from the brain by the CSF-filled subarachnoid space.
• Periosteum — PERIOSTEUM covers bone (the skull), so it is outside all three meninges; it is not a meningeal layer at all.

⭐ Key point: Meninges outside → in: Dura, Arachnoid, PIA. Pia is the one stuck on the brain surface.$x$, null),
(12, $x$The brainstem consists of:$x$, $x$Midbrain, pons and medulla$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Midbrain, pons and medulla
The BRAINSTEM connects the brain to the spinal cord and has three parts, top to bottom: MIDBRAIN, PONS and MEDULLA OBLONGATA. It contains the nuclei of cranial nerves III–XII, the centres for breathing, heart rate and blood pressure (mainly the medulla), and the reticular activating system that keeps us awake. That is why brainstem damage or herniation (from raised ICP) causes coma, abnormal pupils, irregular breathing and Cushing's triad (high BP, slow pulse, irregular breathing), and why brainstem reflexes are tested for brain death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Midbrain, hindbrain and forebrain — "Midbrain, hindbrain and forebrain" are the three main developmental divisions of the WHOLE brain; the forebrain (cerebrum, thalamus) is not part of the brainstem.
• Hindbrain, ventricle and pons — A VENTRICLE is a CSF-filled cavity (the fourth ventricle lies behind the pons and medulla), not a part of the brainstem.
• Midbrain, pons and forebrain — The FOREBRAIN (cerebral hemispheres, thalamus, hypothalamus) sits above the brainstem, so it cannot be part of it.

⭐ Key point: Brainstem = MIDBRAIN + PONS + MEDULLA. Medulla holds vital breathing and cardiovascular centres.$x$, null),
(13, $x$The lymphatic system's primary functions include all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Directly pumping oxygenated blood to tissues$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Directly pumping oxygenated blood to tissues
The LYMPHATIC SYSTEM is a network of vessels, nodes and organs (spleen, thymus, tonsils). Its main jobs: (1) DRAIN excess tissue fluid — about 2–3 litres a day leak from capillaries and are returned to the blood via the thoracic duct into the left subclavian vein; (2) ABSORB DIETARY FATS from the gut through lacteals in the villi (as chylomicrons); (3) support IMMUNITY — lymph nodes filter germs and house lymphocytes. The lymphatic system has NO heart pumping it; lymph moves by muscle squeezing, breathing and one-way valves. Pumping oxygenated blood to the tissues is the job of the HEART and arteries (cardiovascular system). Blocked lymph drainage causes lymphoedema (e.g. after axillary node removal in breast surgery, or in filariasis/elephantiasis).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Draining excess interstitial fluid back into the bloodstream — Draining excess interstitial fluid back to the blood IS a main lymphatic function; failure causes lymphoedema.
• Transporting absorbed dietary fats from the intestine — Transporting absorbed fats from the intestine IS a lymphatic function (lacteals carry chyle).
• Supporting immune function — Supporting immune function IS a key function (lymph nodes, spleen, lymphocytes).

⭐ Key point: Lymphatics = fluid return, fat absorption, immunity. The HEART pumps oxygenated blood — lymphatics have no pump.$x$, null),
(14, $x$During subclavian central line insertion, the vein lies:$x$, $x$Anterior to the subclavian artery, posterior to the clavicle$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Anterior to the subclavian artery, posterior to the clavicle
The SUBCLAVIAN VEIN runs under the CLAVICLE (collarbone), over the first rib. It lies IN FRONT OF (anterior to) the subclavian ARTERY, separated from it by the anterior scalene muscle, and BEHIND (posterior to) the clavicle. For a subclavian central line the needle is passed under the clavicle aiming towards the sternal notch. Knowing this anatomy explains the main risks: going too deep can puncture the subclavian ARTERY (bright red pulsatile blood, haematoma that cannot be compressed) or the PLEURA, causing a PNEUMOTHORAX — which is why a chest X-ray is done after insertion to check the tip position (lower SVC/cavo-atrial junction) and exclude pneumothorax.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lateral to the carotid artery only — "Lateral to the carotid artery only" describes the INTERNAL JUGULAR vein in the neck, not the subclavian vein.
• Superior to the clavicle at all times — The subclavian vein is mostly BELOW/behind the clavicle, not above it at all times.
• Within the mediastinum only — It is not "within the mediastinum only"; it is in the root of the neck/upper chest and only joins the internal jugular to form the brachiocephalic vein near the mediastinum.

⭐ Key point: Subclavian vein = ANTERIOR to the subclavian artery, POSTERIOR to the clavicle. Main risks: arterial puncture and pneumothorax → do a chest X-ray.$x$, null),
(15, $x$The spinal cord's reflex action allows for:$x$, $x$A rapid, automatic response to a stimulus, bypassing the need for the brain to process it first$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A rapid, automatic response to a stimulus, bypassing the need for the brain to process it first
A REFLEX is a fast, automatic, involuntary response to a stimulus. The signal travels along a REFLEX ARC: receptor → sensory (afferent) neurone → spinal cord (often via an interneurone) → motor (efferent) neurone → effector (muscle). The response happens at the SPINAL CORD level, before the brain has processed the sensation — so you pull your hand off a hot object before you feel pain. Examples: knee jerk (patellar reflex, L3–L4), withdrawal reflex, Babinski sign. Nurses test reflexes in neurological assessment: absent reflexes suggest lower motor neurone or spinal shock; brisk reflexes suggest upper motor neurone lesion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Conscious decision-making before every movement — Reflexes are NOT conscious decisions; conscious control comes from the cerebral cortex and is slower.
• Only sensory perception with no motor output — A reflex has both SENSORY input and MOTOR output; "only sensory perception with no motor output" is not a reflex.
• Hormone regulation — Hormone regulation is done by the endocrine system and hypothalamus, not by spinal reflex arcs.

⭐ Key point: Spinal reflex = rapid, automatic response through the reflex arc, without waiting for the brain.$x$, null),
(16, $x$Spontaneous bleeding risk significantly increases when the platelet count falls below approximately:$x$, $x$10,000–20,000/microL$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 10,000–20,000/microL
The normal platelet count is 150,000–400,000/microL (150–400 × 10⁹/L). Below 150,000 is THROMBOCYTOPENIA, but the bleeding risk depends on how low it goes: below about 50,000 there is bleeding with surgery or trauma; below 20,000 there is risk of SPONTANEOUS bleeding (petechiae, gum bleeding, nosebleeds); below 10,000 there is high risk of serious internal bleeding, including into the brain. Prophylactic platelet transfusion is usually given below 10,000 (or below 20,000 with fever/sepsis). Nursing care: bleeding precautions — soft toothbrush, no IM injections, avoid aspirin/NSAIDs, electric razor, prevent falls, check stool/urine for blood.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 150,000/microL — 150,000/microL is the LOWER LIMIT OF NORMAL; spontaneous bleeding does not occur here.
• 100,000/microL — 100,000/microL is mild thrombocytopenia; bleeding is unlikely without trauma or surgery.
• 250,000/microL — 250,000/microL is a completely normal count.

⭐ Key point: Spontaneous bleeding risk rises below 10,000–20,000/microL. Normal platelets 150,000–400,000.$x$, null),
(17, $x$Winter's formula is used to determine the expected respiratory compensation for:$x$, $x$A primary metabolic acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A primary metabolic acidosis
WINTER'S FORMULA tells you what the PaCO2 SHOULD be when the lungs are properly compensating for a PRIMARY METABOLIC ACIDOSIS: Expected PaCO2 = (1.5 × HCO3⁻) + 8 ± 2. Example: in DKA with HCO3 of 10 → expected PaCO2 = 15 + 8 = 23 (range 21–25). If the measured PaCO2 matches, compensation is appropriate. If PaCO2 is HIGHER than expected, there is an extra RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS (patient tiring, not breathing off enough CO2 — danger sign). If it is LOWER, there is an extra RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS. The body compensates by deep, fast Kussmaul breathing to blow off CO2.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory acidosis — In RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS the primary problem is CO2; compensation is by the KIDNEYS (keeping HCO3), so Winter's formula does not apply.
• Metabolic alkalosis — METABOLIC ALKALOSIS uses a different estimate (PaCO2 rises about 0.7 × rise in HCO3), not Winter's formula.
• Respiratory alkalosis — RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS is a primary CO2 problem compensated by the kidneys; Winter's formula is not used.

⭐ Key point: Winter's formula = expected PaCO2 in METABOLIC ACIDOSIS = 1.5 × HCO3 + 8 ± 2.$x$, null),
(18, $x$Culture and sensitivity testing of a clinical specimen is primarily performed to:$x$, $x$Identify the causative organism and determine which antibiotics it is sensitive to, guiding targeted therapy$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Identify the causative organism and determine which antibiotics it is sensitive to, guiding targeted therapy
CULTURE AND SENSITIVITY (C&S) has two parts. CULTURE: the specimen (blood, urine, sputum, wound swab, CSF) is grown on special media to find out WHICH organism is causing the infection. SENSITIVITY: the organism is tested against different antibiotics to see which ones kill or stop it (sensitive) and which ones it resists (resistant). This guides TARGETED therapy — narrowing from broad-spectrum to the right drug, which improves outcomes and reduces antimicrobial resistance. Nursing points: collect specimens with sterile technique and BEFORE starting antibiotics where possible (without delaying antibiotics in sepsis), label correctly, and send quickly. Results usually take 48–72 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Measure the patient's haemoglobin level — Haemoglobin is measured by a full blood count (FBC), not by culture.
• Assess kidney function — Kidney function is assessed by urea, creatinine, eGFR and electrolytes, not culture.
• Determine blood type — Blood type is found by blood grouping and cross-matching, not culture.

⭐ Key point: C&S = identify the ORGANISM + find which ANTIBIOTICS work. Take specimens before antibiotics if it causes no delay.$x$, null),
(19, $x$The normal pH range for arterial blood is:$x$, $x$7.35–7.45$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 7.35–7.45
Normal ARTERIAL blood pH is 7.35–7.45. Below 7.35 is ACIDAEMIA (acidosis); above 7.45 is ALKALAEMIA (alkalosis). The body keeps pH in this narrow range because enzymes, heart rhythm and brain function depend on it. Three systems defend pH: chemical buffers (seconds — bicarbonate, proteins, haemoglobin), the LUNGS (minutes — change CO2 by breathing faster or slower), and the KIDNEYS (hours to days — excrete H⁺, keep or lose HCO3⁻). Other normal ABG values: PaCO2 35–45 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 22–26 mmol/L, PaO2 80–100 mmHg, base excess −2 to +2. A pH below 6.8 or above 7.8 is usually not compatible with life.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 7.0–7.2 — 7.0–7.2 is SEVERE ACIDOSIS, seen in conditions like DKA or cardiac arrest — not normal.
• 7.5–7.6 — 7.5–7.6 is ALKALOSIS (e.g. hyperventilation or severe vomiting) — not normal.
• 6.8–7.0 — 6.8–7.0 is life-threatening acidosis, at the edge of survival.

⭐ Key point: Normal arterial pH = 7.35–7.45. PaCO2 35–45; HCO3 22–26.$x$, null),
(20, $x$The normal anion gap range is:$x$, $x$8–18mmol/L$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 8–18mmol/L
The ANION GAP (AG) is a calculated value: AG = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO3⁻). It represents the "unmeasured" negative ions in the blood (mainly albumin, plus phosphate, sulphate and organic acids). The commonly quoted normal range is about 8–12 (some labs up to 16–18 mmol/L, or 10–20 if potassium is added) — so the best option here is 8–18. A HIGH anion gap metabolic acidosis means extra acid has been added, memory aid "MUDPILES": Methanol, Uraemia, DKA, Propylene glycol/Paraldehyde, Isoniazid/Iron, Lactic acidosis, Ethylene glycol, Salicylates. A NORMAL anion gap acidosis is from bicarbonate loss (diarrhoea, renal tubular acidosis) and is usually hyperchloraemic. Low albumin lowers the AG, so correct for it.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0–5mmol/L — 0–5 mmol/L is too LOW; this is seen with very low albumin or lab error, not normal.
• 30–50mmol/L — 30–50 mmol/L is a very HIGH gap, e.g. severe lactic acidosis or ketoacidosis.
• 100–150mmol/L — 100–150 mmol/L is impossible as an anion gap; it is close to the serum sodium value itself.

⭐ Key point: Anion gap = Na − (Cl + HCO3); normal ≈ 8–18 mmol/L. High AG acidosis = "MUDPILES".$x$, null),
(21, $x$Microcytic anaemia (low MCV) is classically associated with:$x$, $x$Iron deficiency$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Iron deficiency
MCV (mean cell volume) tells us the SIZE of red cells. MICROCYTIC means small cells (MCV under about 80 fL), usually also pale (hypochromic). The commonest cause of microcytic anaemia worldwide is IRON DEFICIENCY — from blood loss (heavy menstruation, hookworm, GI bleeding, ulcers, colon cancer), poor diet, pregnancy or poor absorption. Without iron the cell cannot make enough haemoglobin, so it divides extra times and ends up small. Other microcytic causes: thalassaemia, anaemia of chronic disease (sometimes), sideroblastic anaemia, lead poisoning ("TAILS"). Tests: low ferritin, low serum iron, high TIBC. Clinical signs: koilonychia (spoon nails), angular stomatitis, pica.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Vitamin B12 deficiency — VITAMIN B12 deficiency gives MACROCYTIC (large cell, megaloblastic) anaemia with MCV over 100 fL, and may cause neurological signs.
• Folate deficiency — FOLATE deficiency also gives MACROCYTIC megaloblastic anaemia, not microcytic.
• Acute haemorrhage only — ACUTE HAEMORRHAGE gives a NORMOCYTIC anaemia at first, because the cells lost are normal-sized; microcytosis only appears later after iron stores run out.

⭐ Key point: Microcytic (MCV < 80) = IRON DEFICIENCY first ("TAILS"). Macrocytic = B12/folate. Acute bleed = normocytic.$x$, null),
(22, $x$A rise in PaCO2 causing acidosis indicates the disorder is:$x$, $x$Respiratory$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Respiratory
To decide whether an acid–base problem is RESPIRATORY or METABOLIC, look at which value moved in the SAME direction as the pH problem. CO2 acts like an ACID in the blood (CO2 + H2O → H2CO3 → H⁺ + HCO3⁻). So if PaCO2 RISES (above 45 mmHg) and the pH FALLS (below 7.35), the cause is the LUNGS not removing enough CO2 — RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS. Causes: hypoventilation from opioid overdose, COPD exacerbation, exhaustion, neuromuscular weakness, head injury. Treatment improves ventilation (stimulate, reverse opioids, NIV/BiPAP, intubation). If the HCO3 had fallen to cause acidosis, it would be metabolic.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic — METABOLIC disorders are caused by changes in HCO3⁻ (bicarbonate), not by a rise in CO2.
• Neither respiratory nor metabolic — "Neither" is wrong — a primary change in PaCO2 is by definition a respiratory disorder.
• Always compensated — It is NOT "always compensated"; acute respiratory acidosis is often uncompensated because the kidneys need days to retain bicarbonate.

⭐ Key point: High PaCO2 + low pH = RESPIRATORY acidosis (hypoventilation). Low HCO3 + low pH = metabolic acidosis.$x$, null),
(23, $x$A high anion gap metabolic acidosis, such as in DKA, is caused by:$x$, $x$Extra unmeasured acid (e.g. ketones) in the blood$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Extra unmeasured acid (e.g. ketones) in the blood
In DIABETIC KETOACIDOSIS (DKA), lack of insulin makes the body burn fat, producing KETOACIDS (beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate). These are EXTRA UNMEASURED ACIDS. They release H⁺, which uses up bicarbonate, and their negative ions are not counted in the anion gap formula, so the anion gap RISES. This is called a HIGH ANION GAP METABOLIC ACIDOSIS. Same process with lactic acidosis (sepsis, shock), uraemia, and poisons like methanol, ethylene glycol and aspirin ("MUDPILES"). Treatment of DKA: IV fluids, IV insulin infusion, potassium replacement and monitoring of ketones, glucose and anion gap until it closes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Loss of bicarbonate from diarrhoea — Loss of bicarbonate from DIARRHOEA causes a NORMAL anion gap (hyperchloraemic) acidosis, because chloride rises to replace the lost bicarbonate.
• Excess vomiting — Excess VOMITING loses stomach acid (HCl), causing METABOLIC ALKALOSIS, not acidosis.
• Hyperventilation alone — HYPERVENTILATION alone blows off CO2 and causes RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS.

⭐ Key point: High anion gap acidosis = ADDED acid (ketones, lactate, toxins). Diarrhoea = normal AG acidosis. Vomiting = alkalosis.$x$, null),
(24, $x$A mother is admitted to the emergency department following complaints of fever and chills. The nurse on duty took her vital signs and noted the following: Temp = 100 °F; apical pulse = 95; respiration = 20 and deep. Measurement of arterial blood gas shows pH 7.37, PaO2 90 mm Hg, PaCO2 40 mm Hg, and HCO3 24 mmol/L. What is your assessment?$x$, $x$Hyperthermia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hyperthermia
Look at each value. TEMPERATURE 100 °F = about 37.8 °C (°C = (°F − 32) × 5/9). Normal is about 97.7–99.5 °F (36.5–37.5 °C), so 100 °F is a raised temperature — mild HYPERTHERMIA/fever, fitting the "fever and chills" complaint. Pulse 95 and respiration 20 are at the upper end of normal (fever raises both). Now the ABG: pH 7.37 (normal 7.35–7.45), PaCO2 40 (normal 35–45), HCO3 24 (normal 22–26), PaO2 90 (normal 80–100) — ALL NORMAL. So there is no acid–base disorder. The correct assessment is HYPERTHERMIA only. Exam tip: always check every ABG value against its range before naming a disorder; do not guess alkalosis just because breathing is "deep".

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypothermia — HYPOTHERMIA means a body temperature below 35 °C (95 °F); 100 °F is raised, not low.
• Hypothermia and Respiratory Alkalosis — "Hypothermia and respiratory alkalosis" is wrong twice: the temperature is high and the pH/PaCO2 are normal.
• Hyperthermia and Respiratory Alkalosis — RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS needs pH above 7.45 and PaCO2 below 35; here pH 7.37 and PaCO2 40 are normal.

⭐ Key point: 100 °F ≈ 37.8 °C = hyperthermia. pH 7.37, PaCO2 40, HCO3 24 = NORMAL ABG. Always check each value against its range.$x$, null),
(25, $x$Fibrinogen plays its key role in coagulation by:$x$, $x$Being converted to fibrin to form the structural clot mesh$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Being converted to fibrin to form the structural clot mesh
FIBRINOGEN (clotting factor I) is a soluble protein made by the liver. At the end of the clotting cascade, THROMBIN cuts fibrinogen into FIBRIN. Fibrin strands join together and are cross-linked by factor XIII to form a strong MESH that traps red cells and platelets, turning the soft platelet plug into a firm, stable clot. Normal fibrinogen is about 2–4 g/L. Low fibrinogen (e.g. in DIC, massive haemorrhage, postpartum haemorrhage, liver failure) causes uncontrolled bleeding and is treated with cryoprecipitate or fibrinogen concentrate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Inhibiting platelet aggregation — Fibrinogen does not INHIBIT platelet aggregation; it actually helps platelets stick together through the GP IIb/IIIa receptor. Drugs like aspirin and clopidogrel inhibit aggregation.
• Breaking down existing clots — BREAKING DOWN clots (fibrinolysis) is done by PLASMIN (activated by tPA), and produces D-dimer from fibrin.
• Regulating serum calcium — Serum calcium is regulated by parathyroid hormone, vitamin D and calcitonin, not fibrinogen (though calcium is factor IV in clotting).

⭐ Key point: Fibrinogen → (thrombin) → FIBRIN mesh = stable clot. Low fibrinogen → give cryoprecipitate.$x$, null),
(26, $x$Hyperphosphataemia is a common electrolyte disturbance in patients with:$x$, $x$Chronic kidney disease$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Chronic kidney disease
The KIDNEYS are the main route for removing PHOSPHATE from the body. In CHRONIC KIDNEY DISEASE (CKD), the glomerular filtration rate falls, so phosphate is retained and blood levels rise (HYPERPHOSPHATAEMIA, normal 0.8–1.5 mmol/L). High phosphate binds calcium, so calcium falls; the failing kidney also makes less active vitamin D. Low calcium and high phosphate drive the parathyroid glands to overwork (secondary hyperparathyroidism), causing renal bone disease, itching and calcium–phosphate deposits in vessels (vascular calcification). Management: low-phosphate diet (limit dairy, cola, processed foods), PHOSPHATE BINDERS taken with meals (calcium carbonate, sevelamer), and dialysis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diabetes insipidus — DIABETES INSIPIDUS causes loss of large amounts of dilute urine and HYPERNATRAEMIA, not high phosphate.
• Primary hyperaldosteronism — PRIMARY HYPERALDOSTERONISM causes HYPOKALAEMIA, sodium retention and hypertension.
• SIADH — SIADH causes water retention and dilutional HYPONATRAEMIA.

⭐ Key point: CKD → high PHOSPHATE + low calcium → secondary hyperparathyroidism. Treat with diet + phosphate binders with meals.$x$, null),
(27, $x$The most common anticoagulant used in blood bank donor bags is:$x$, $x$Tri-sodium citrate$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Tri-sodium citrate
Blood bank donor bags contain an anticoagulant-preservative such as CPD or CPDA-1 (Citrate, Phosphate, Dextrose, Adenine). The anticoagulant part is TRI-SODIUM CITRATE. Citrate stops clotting by BINDING CALCIUM (calcium is factor IV, needed at many steps of the cascade). The DEXTROSE feeds the red cells, ADENINE helps them make ATP, and PHOSPHATE buffers the pH; CPDA-1 lets whole blood be stored for about 35 days at 2–6 °C. Clinical link: in MASSIVE transfusion, the citrate in many units can bind the patient's own calcium → HYPOCALCAEMIA (tingling, low BP, arrhythmia), so ionised calcium is monitored and calcium is given if low.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• EDTA — EDTA (purple-top tube) is used for full blood count samples; it is not used in donor bags because it is toxic in large amounts.
• Heparin — HEPARIN is used for some samples (green tube) and as a drug, but it does not preserve red cells for weeks of storage.
• Double oxalate — DOUBLE OXALATE is an old laboratory anticoagulant for test samples only; it is toxic and never used for transfusion.

⭐ Key point: Donor bags = CITRATE (CPD/CPDA-1) — binds calcium. Massive transfusion → watch for HYPOCALCAEMIA.$x$, null),
(28, $x$The antidote used to reverse benzodiazepine sedation is:$x$, $x$Flumazenil$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Flumazenil
FLUMAZENIL is the specific antidote for BENZODIAZEPINES (diazepam, midazolam, lorazepam). It is a competitive ANTAGONIST at the benzodiazepine site of the GABA-A receptor, so it quickly reverses sedation and respiratory depression. Dose: 0.2 mg IV over 15 seconds, repeat 0.1–0.2 mg each minute up to about 1 mg. CAUTIONS: it can cause SEIZURES in patients on long-term benzodiazepines (sudden withdrawal) or after mixed overdose with tricyclic antidepressants, so it is mainly used to reverse procedural sedation, not routine overdose. Its effect is shorter than many benzodiazepines, so RE-SEDATION can occur — keep monitoring.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Naloxone — NALOXONE reverses OPIOIDS (morphine, heroin, fentanyl), not benzodiazepines.
• Acetylcysteine — ACETYLCYSTEINE (NAC) is the antidote for PARACETAMOL (acetaminophen) overdose; it replaces glutathione.
• Protamine sulphate — PROTAMINE SULPHATE reverses HEPARIN.

⭐ Key point: Benzodiazepine → FLUMAZENIL; opioid → naloxone; paracetamol → acetylcysteine; heparin → protamine.$x$, null),
(29, $x$Activated charcoal works by which mechanism to reduce systemic absorption of an ingested poison?$x$, $x$It adsorbs the toxin onto its large surface area within the gastrointestinal tract, preventing systemic absorption$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: It adsorbs the toxin onto its large surface area within the gastrointestinal tract, preventing systemic absorption
ACTIVATED CHARCOAL is a fine black powder with a huge surface area (about 1000 m² per gram). Many drugs and toxins stick to its surface — this is called ADSORPTION — while still in the stomach and intestine, so they cannot be absorbed into the blood and are passed out in the stool. It works best when given within about 1 HOUR of ingestion, in a dose of about 50 g for adults (1 g/kg for children). The patient MUST have a protected airway (alert, or intubated) because aspiration into the lungs is dangerous. It does NOT bind well to iron, lithium, alcohols, cyanide, acids, alkalis or hydrocarbons ("PHAILS").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It chemically neutralises acids and alkalis — It does NOT neutralise acids or alkalis; corrosives are not bound well and charcoal is contraindicated in corrosive ingestion.
• It induces vomiting to expel the toxin — It does NOT induce vomiting; that was ipecac, which is no longer recommended.
• It increases intestinal motility to speed transit — It does NOT increase gut motility; in fact it can cause constipation. Laxatives/whole-bowel irrigation are separate treatments.

⭐ Key point: Activated charcoal ADSORBS toxins in the gut. Best within 1 hour; airway must be protected; not for iron, lithium, alcohol, corrosives.$x$, null),
(30, $x$Patients on a continuous insulin infusion in the ICU require blood glucose monitoring at a frequency of approximately:$x$, $x$Hourly, or per institutional protocol$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hourly, or per institutional protocol
Patients on a CONTINUOUS IV INSULIN INFUSION (e.g. DKA, HHS, post-cardiac surgery, critical illness) need BLOOD GLUCOSE checked about EVERY HOUR (or as per unit protocol, sometimes every 1–2 hours once stable). IV regular insulin acts within minutes and has a short half-life, so glucose can fall quickly; hourly checks let the nurse adjust the rate to stay in the target range (often 6–10 mmol/L, or 140–180 mg/dL in ICU) and catch HYPOGLYCAEMIA early. Hypoglycaemia in sedated patients may be hidden (no symptoms). Also monitor potassium, because insulin moves potassium into cells and can cause HYPOKALAEMIA.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Once every 24 hours — Once every 24 hours is far too seldom; dangerous hypo- or hyperglycaemia could develop unnoticed.
• Once weekly — Once weekly makes no sense for an IV insulin infusion; that frequency fits HbA1c or long-term follow-up, not acute care.
• Only if symptomatic — Checking ONLY if symptomatic is unsafe: ICU patients are often sedated and cannot report hypoglycaemia symptoms.

⭐ Key point: IV insulin infusion → glucose check HOURLY (or per protocol). Watch potassium too.$x$, null),
(31, $x$To ensure homogenization when diluting powdered medication in a vial, the nurse should:$x$, $x$Roll the vial gently between the palms.$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Roll the vial gently between the palms.
After adding diluent to a powdered drug, the nurse must mix it evenly (homogenise) without damaging it. The correct method is to ROLL THE VIAL GENTLY BETWEEN THE PALMS until all the powder has dissolved and the solution is clear. Rolling mixes the drug without making froth or air bubbles. Many drugs — especially proteins (insulin suspensions, monoclonal antibodies, some antibiotics, vaccines) — can be DAMAGED (denatured) or form foam by vigorous shaking, which can change the dose drawn up. After mixing, check the solution for particles or discolouration, label the vial with date/time/strength if multi-dose, and follow storage instructions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Do nothing after adding the solution to the vial. — Doing NOTHING after adding diluent may leave undissolved powder, giving the wrong dose.
• Invert the vial and let it stand for 1 minute. — Inverting and leaving it to stand for 1 minute does not reliably dissolve the powder or mix the solution evenly.
• Shake the vial vigorously. — SHAKING VIGOROUSLY creates foam and bubbles and may denature protein drugs, so it is avoided.

⭐ Key point: Reconstituted vials: ROLL GENTLY between the palms — do not shake. Check the solution is clear before drawing up.$x$, null),
(32, $x$Gastric lavage is contraindicated in which of the following situations?$x$, $x$Corrosive/acid ingestion$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Corrosive/acid ingestion
GASTRIC LAVAGE (stomach washout through a large tube) is now rarely used. It is CONTRAINDICATED in CORROSIVE (acid or alkali) ingestion because: (1) the oesophagus and stomach walls are already burned and weak, so the tube can PERFORATE them; (2) washing can cause the patient to vomit, so the corrosive passes over the oesophagus again, causing more burns, and may be aspirated into the lungs. Other contraindications: hydrocarbon/petroleum ingestion (aspiration pneumonitis), unprotected airway (reduced consciousness without intubation), and bleeding risk. For corrosives: keep NPO, protect the airway, do NOT induce vomiting or neutralise, and arrange early endoscopy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• General sedative overdose within 1 hour — A sedative overdose within 1 hour was the traditional (though now limited) indication for lavage, not a contraindication, if the airway is protected.
• Barbiturate overdose while still conscious — Barbiturate overdose in a conscious patient within the time window is also an older indication, not a contraindication.
• Within 2 hours of a large tablet overdose — Within 2 hours of a large tablet overdose may still be a situation where lavage was considered; time alone is not the main contraindication.

⭐ Key point: Gastric lavage — NEVER in CORROSIVE (acid/alkali) or hydrocarbon ingestion, or without a protected airway.$x$, null),
(33, $x$A patient develops serotonin syndrome after being started on two serotonergic medications simultaneously. The nurse should recognise this presentation includes:$x$, $x$Agitation, hyperthermia, tremor, and autonomic instability$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Agitation, hyperthermia, tremor, and autonomic instability
SEROTONIN SYNDROME is caused by too much serotonin activity in the nervous system, often from COMBINING serotonergic drugs: SSRIs/SNRIs, MAO inhibitors, tramadol, pethidine, linezolid, triptans, ondansetron, St John's wort. It usually starts within 24 hours of a new drug or dose change. Classic TRIAD: (1) MENTAL STATUS changes — agitation, confusion, restlessness; (2) AUTONOMIC instability — HYPERTHERMIA, tachycardia, high BP, sweating, dilated pupils, diarrhoea; (3) NEUROMUSCULAR signs — TREMOR, CLONUS (especially inducible ankle clonus), hyperreflexia, muscle rigidity. Treatment: STOP the drugs, supportive care, cooling, benzodiazepines, and cyproheptadine (serotonin antagonist); severe cases need ICU.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bradycardia and hypotension with no other findings — BRADYCARDIA and HYPOTENSION are not typical; serotonin syndrome usually causes TACHYCARDIA and hypertension.
• No clinical significance requiring only observation — It is NOT harmless — severe cases cause very high fever, seizures, rhabdomyolysis and death, so it needs urgent action.
• Improved mental status — It causes WORSENING mental status (agitation, confusion), not improvement.

⭐ Key point: Serotonin syndrome = AGITATION + HYPERTHERMIA + TREMOR/CLONUS + autonomic instability. Stop the drugs; give cyproheptadine.$x$, null),
(34, $x$Succinylcholine is contraindicated in a patient with a personal or family history of:$x$, $x$Malignant hyperthermia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Malignant hyperthermia
SUCCINYLCHOLINE (suxamethonium) is a fast-acting depolarising muscle relaxant used for rapid sequence intubation. It is a strong TRIGGER of MALIGNANT HYPERTHERMIA (MH) — an inherited muscle disorder (often in the RYR1 gene) in which triggering drugs (succinylcholine and volatile anaesthetic gases like sevoflurane) cause uncontrolled calcium release in muscle. Signs: rising end-tidal CO2 (often the first sign), masseter/jaw rigidity, tachycardia, very high temperature, hyperkalaemia, rhabdomyolysis. Treatment: stop triggers, give DANTROLENE, cool the patient. So a personal or FAMILY history of MH is an absolute contraindication. Other contraindications: HYPERKALAEMIA risk (burns or crush injury after 24–48 h, spinal cord injury, prolonged immobility), and pseudocholinesterase deficiency.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Seasonal allergic rhinitis — SEASONAL ALLERGIC RHINITIS does not affect safety of succinylcholine.
• Migraine headaches — MIGRAINE history is not a contraindication.
• Mild intermittent asthma — MILD INTERMITTENT ASTHMA is not a contraindication to succinylcholine (it does not cause much histamine release).

⭐ Key point: Succinylcholine is contraindicated in MALIGNANT HYPERTHERMIA history (treat MH with DANTROLENE) and in hyperkalaemia risk.$x$, null),
(35, $x$Bactericidal antibiotics work by:$x$, $x$Actively killing bacteria, e.g. by attacking the cell wall$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Actively killing bacteria, e.g. by attacking the cell wall
BACTERICIDAL antibiotics KILL bacteria directly. Many do this by attacking the CELL WALL (beta-lactams such as penicillins, cephalosporins and carbapenems; vancomycin), so the bacterium bursts. Others damage DNA (fluoroquinolones, metronidazole) or cause faulty proteins (aminoglycosides). BACTERIOSTATIC antibiotics only STOP bacteria from growing and multiplying (e.g. tetracyclines, macrolides, clindamycin, sulphonamides), and the immune system then clears them. Bactericidal drugs are preferred in serious infections or weak immunity — e.g. meningitis, endocarditis, neutropenic sepsis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stopping bacterial growth only — "Stopping bacterial growth only" describes BACTERIOSTATIC drugs, not bactericidal ones.
• Only affecting viruses — Antibiotics do NOT work against VIRUSES; viruses need antivirals (e.g. acyclovir, oseltamivir).
• Having no mechanism of action — Every antibiotic has a mechanism of action; "no mechanism" is simply false.

⭐ Key point: BactericiDAL = KILLS (e.g. cell-wall attack by penicillins). BacterioSTATIC = STOPS growth (e.g. tetracyclines, macrolides).$x$, null),
(36, $x$Ipratropium bromide is used in the management of:$x$, $x$Acute asthmatic attack$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Acute asthmatic attack
IPRATROPIUM BROMIDE (Atrovent) is an inhaled ANTICHOLINERGIC (antimuscarinic) bronchodilator. It blocks acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors in the airway smooth muscle, which RELAXES the bronchi and reduces mucus secretion. In an ACUTE ASTHMA ATTACK it is given by nebuliser (e.g. 500 micrograms) together with salbutamol for moderate–severe or life-threatening attacks; the combination gives better bronchodilation than salbutamol alone. It is also widely used in COPD. Side effects: dry mouth, urinary retention, and blurred vision/glaucoma if mist gets into the eyes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute gastroenteritis — ACUTE GASTROENTERITIS is treated with fluids/ORS; ipratropium has no role.
• Acute peptic ulcer disease — PEPTIC ULCER disease is treated with PPIs, H2 blockers and H. pylori eradication, not inhaled ipratropium.
• Acute bronchopneumonia — ACUTE BRONCHOPNEUMONIA needs antibiotics, oxygen and chest care; ipratropium is not the treatment for infection (unless there is bronchospasm from co-existing COPD/asthma).

⭐ Key point: Ipratropium = inhaled ANTICHOLINERGIC bronchodilator, given with salbutamol in ACUTE ASTHMA and COPD.$x$, null),
(37, $x$Salbutamol relieves bronchospasm mainly by acting as a:$x$, $x$Beta-2 adrenergic agonist causing bronchial smooth muscle relaxation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Beta-2 adrenergic agonist causing bronchial smooth muscle relaxation
SALBUTAMOL (albuterol) is a short-acting BETA-2 ADRENERGIC AGONIST (SABA). Beta-2 receptors are found in bronchial smooth muscle. When salbutamol stimulates them, cAMP rises and the smooth muscle RELAXES, so the airways open (BRONCHODILATION) within minutes; effect lasts 4–6 hours. It is the first-line "reliever" for acute asthma and bronchospasm. Side effects come from beta stimulation: TACHYCARDIA, palpitations, fine TREMOR, anxiety and HYPOKALAEMIA (it drives potassium into cells — this is also why nebulised salbutamol can be used to lower high potassium).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anticholinergic that dries secretions — ANTICHOLINERGICS (e.g. ipratropium) work by blocking muscarinic receptors and drying secretions — that is not how salbutamol works.
• Corticosteroid reducing inflammation only — CORTICOSTEROIDS (e.g. hydrocortisone, prednisolone, budesonide) reduce inflammation over hours; salbutamol is not a steroid.
• Mucolytic that thins mucus — MUCOLYTICS (e.g. acetylcysteine, carbocisteine) thin mucus; salbutamol does not.

⭐ Key point: Salbutamol = BETA-2 AGONIST → bronchial smooth muscle relaxation. Side effects: tremor, tachycardia, hypokalaemia.$x$, null),
(38, $x$Dopamine's haemodynamic effect varies by dose, with higher doses primarily producing:$x$, $x$Alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction
DOPAMINE acts on different receptors at different doses: LOW dose (about 1–5 mcg/kg/min) — dopamine receptors, causing renal and mesenteric vasodilation (the old "renal dose", now not recommended to protect kidneys); MEDIUM dose (about 5–10 mcg/kg/min) — BETA-1 receptors, increasing heart rate and contractility (cardiac output); HIGH dose (above about 10 mcg/kg/min) — ALPHA-1 receptors, causing VASOCONSTRICTION and raised systemic vascular resistance and BP. So the higher-dose effect is ALPHA-ADRENERGIC VASOCONSTRICTION. Nursing: give through a central line if possible (extravasation causes tissue necrosis — antidote phentolamine), monitor BP, heart rate and arrhythmias, and urine output.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Renal vasodilation only — RENAL VASODILATION occurs at LOW doses, not high doses.
• Bronchodilation — BRONCHODILATION is a beta-2 effect, which is not a main action of dopamine.
• Pure beta-2 effects — Dopamine has no "pure beta-2" effect; its beta effect is mainly beta-1 (heart).

⭐ Key point: Dopamine: low = renal/dopaminergic; medium = beta-1 (heart); HIGH = ALPHA-1 vasoconstriction.$x$, null),
(39, $x$Before administering any weight-based critical care drug infusion, best practice is to:$x$, $x$Have the calculation independently double-checked by a second nurse/pharmacist$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Have the calculation independently double-checked by a second nurse/pharmacist
Critical care infusions (e.g. noradrenaline, dopamine, heparin, insulin, potassium, sedatives) are HIGH-ALERT medications: a small calculation error can cause severe harm or death. Weight-based doses (mcg/kg/min) need several steps — weight, concentration, dose, pump rate — and each step can go wrong (decimal errors, wrong units, mg vs mcg). Best practice is an INDEPENDENT DOUBLE CHECK by a second nurse or pharmacist, who does the calculation separately without seeing the first result, then both compare. Also check the right patient, drug, dose, route, time and the pump programming (using smart pumps with drug libraries where available), and document both checkers.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Skip verification if the nurse is experienced — Skipping checks because a nurse is "experienced" is unsafe; experienced staff also make errors, especially when busy or tired.
• Round the dose up for safety margin — ROUNDING the dose UP is not safe; it can cause overdose. Doses must be exact or follow approved protocols.
• Administer without checking the infusion pump rate — Giving without checking the PUMP RATE ignores a common source of error (wrong rate programmed).

⭐ Key point: High-alert weight-based infusions need an INDEPENDENT DOUBLE CHECK of the calculation AND the pump rate.$x$, null),
(40, $x$Which class of these drugs is contraindicated in patients with high bleeding tendencies?$x$, $x$NSAIDS$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: NSAIDS
Non-selective NSAIDs (ibuprofen, diclofenac, naproxen, aspirin) block both COX-1 and COX-2 enzymes. Blocking COX-1 stops platelets from making THROMBOXANE A2, so PLATELETS CANNOT CLUMP well — bleeding time increases. COX-1 also protects the stomach lining, so NSAIDs cause gastric ulcers and GI BLEEDING. This combination makes NSAIDs contraindicated (or used with great caution) in patients with bleeding disorders, thrombocytopenia, active bleeding, peptic ulcers or on anticoagulants. Paracetamol or opioids are safer choices for pain in these patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• COX 2 selective NSAIDS — COX-2 SELECTIVE NSAIDs (e.g. celecoxib) spare COX-1, so they have much less effect on platelets and the stomach; they are not the class most linked to bleeding (though they raise cardiovascular risk).
• Opioids — OPIOIDS do not affect platelets or clotting; they are often used for pain when NSAIDs are unsafe.
• Adjuvants — ADJUVANT analgesics (e.g. amitriptyline, gabapentin) do not usually cause bleeding.

⭐ Key point: Non-selective NSAIDs block COX-1 → poor platelet function + GI ulcers → AVOID in bleeding tendency.$x$, null),
(41, $x$Severe/major reactions to contrast media can include all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Improved renal perfusion$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Improved renal perfusion
Contrast media (iodinated contrast for CT, angiography) can cause reactions. MILD: warmth, nausea, itching, mild urticaria. MODERATE: widespread hives, vomiting, bronchospasm, facial swelling. SEVERE (anaphylactoid): LARYNGEAL OEDEMA (airway closure), severe bronchospasm, RESPIRATORY ARREST, shock, CARDIAC ARRHYTHMIA and cardiac arrest. Treatment of severe reactions: stop contrast, call for help, airway, oxygen, IM adrenaline 0.5 mg (1:1000), IV fluids, antihistamine and steroid. Contrast can also cause CONTRAST-INDUCED NEPHROPATHY (worsened kidney function, a rise in creatinine 48–72 h later) — it does NOT improve renal perfusion. Risk is reduced by checking creatinine, hydrating, and holding metformin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Laryngeal oedema — LARYNGEAL OEDEMA IS a severe reaction — it can block the airway.
• Cardiac arrhythmia — CARDIAC ARRHYTHMIA IS a severe reaction, especially in anaphylactoid shock.
• Respiratory arrest — RESPIRATORY ARREST IS a severe reaction — the end point of airway/bronchospasm problems.

⭐ Key point: Severe contrast reaction = laryngeal oedema, bronchospasm, arrhythmia, arrest → IM adrenaline. Contrast can HARM the kidneys, never improve them.$x$, null),
(42, $x$CT head findings suggestive of raised intracranial pressure include:$x$, $x$Midline shift and effacement (compression) of the ventricles$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Midline shift and effacement (compression) of the ventricles
In RAISED INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE (ICP), something takes up extra space inside the rigid skull (bleed, tumour, swelling). On CT head this shows as MASS EFFECT: MIDLINE SHIFT (the brain is pushed to one side, a shift over 5 mm is significant), EFFACEMENT (squashing) of the lateral ventricles, loss of sulci (the grooves on the brain surface are flattened by swollen brain), effacement of the basal cisterns, loss of grey–white matter differentiation in diffuse oedema, and signs of herniation. These findings warn of the risk of brain herniation and need urgent neurosurgical review and ICP-lowering measures (head up 30°, normal CO2, osmotherapy such as mannitol or hypertonic saline).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Symmetric ventricles with clear sulci — SYMMETRIC ventricles and CLEAR sulci are NORMAL findings — no pressure effect.
• Normal grey-white matter differentiation only — Normal grey–white differentiation is a normal finding; LOSS of it suggests oedema.
• Absence of any mass effect — ABSENCE of mass effect means no space-occupying pressure, the opposite of raised ICP.

⭐ Key point: Raised ICP on CT = MIDLINE SHIFT, compressed ventricles, effaced sulci/cisterns.$x$, null),
(43, $x$The mnemonic IPPR, used to check chest X-ray quality before interpretation, stands for:$x$, $x$Inspiration, Position, Penetration, Rotation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Inspiration, Position, Penetration, Rotation
Before reading a chest X-ray, first check if the film is technically adequate, using IPPR: (1) INSPIRATION — about 5–6 anterior ribs or 9–10 posterior ribs should be seen above the diaphragm; poor inspiration makes the heart look big and the lung bases look hazy. (2) POSITION/PROJECTION — PA (standard) or AP (portable/bedside, which magnifies the heart). (3) PENETRATION — the vertebrae should be just visible behind the heart; over-penetrated films look too dark, under-penetrated too white. (4) ROTATION — the spinous processes should be midway between the medial ends of the clavicles; rotation distorts the mediastinum. Many teachers add identity, date and side markers first.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Identity, Projection, Positioning, Report — "Identity, Projection, Positioning, Report" is not the standard meaning of IPPR (identity is checked separately).
• Inflation, Pressure, Perfusion, Rate — "Inflation, Pressure, Perfusion, Rate" relates to ventilation/haemodynamics, not X-ray quality.
• Inspiration, Perfusion, Position, Result — "Inspiration, Perfusion, Position, Result" wrongly includes perfusion, which an X-ray cannot assess.

⭐ Key point: CXR quality = IPPR: Inspiration, Position, Penetration, Rotation.$x$, null),
(44, $x$Positive contrast agents (e.g. iodine, barium) appear on X-ray as:$x$, $x$Opaque/white$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Opaque/white
On X-ray, the WHITER an area, the MORE X-rays it has blocked. POSITIVE contrast agents like IODINE and BARIUM have HIGH atomic numbers, so they absorb many X-rays and look OPAQUE/WHITE (radio-OPAQUE), similar to bone or metal. This lets us outline the inside of hollow structures: barium swallow or enema for the gut, iodine for blood vessels (angiography), urinary tract (IVU) or CT enhancement. NEGATIVE contrast agents (air, CO2) absorb few X-rays and look DARK. Nursing: ask about iodine allergy and previous reactions, check kidney function before iodinated contrast, and give fluids/laxatives after barium to prevent constipation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dark/lucent — DARK/LUCENT appearance is seen with AIR or negative contrast, not iodine/barium.
• Invisible — They are certainly not invisible — they are designed to be clearly seen.
• Same density as air — Air density is BLACK on X-ray; positive contrast is the opposite (white).

⭐ Key point: Positive contrast (iodine, barium) = WHITE/radio-opaque. Air/negative contrast = black.$x$, null),
(45, $x$The three core principles for minimising radiation exposure during a radiological incident are:$x$, $x$Time, distance, and shielding$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Time, distance, and shielding
There are three key principles to reduce RADIATION EXPOSURE (ALARA — As Low As Reasonably Achievable): (1) TIME — spend as LITTLE time as possible near the radiation source; dose increases with time. (2) DISTANCE — stay as FAR away as possible; radiation falls with the SQUARE of the distance (inverse square law), so doubling the distance cuts exposure to one quarter. (3) SHIELDING — put a barrier between you and the source, e.g. lead aprons, thyroid collars, lead screens, concrete walls. Nurses use these when patients have portable X-rays (step back about 2 metres or behind a screen) or have received radioactive implants.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Speed, volume, and pressure — Speed, volume and pressure are not radiation protection principles.
• Triage, treatment, and transport — Triage, treatment and transport are steps in managing a mass casualty incident, not principles of reducing radiation dose.
• Isolation, ventilation, and hydration — Isolation, ventilation and hydration are not the three core principles for radiation protection.

⭐ Key point: Radiation protection = TIME (less), DISTANCE (more — inverse square law), SHIELDING (lead).$x$, null),
(46, $x$Sharing your personal EHR login credentials with a colleague is:$x$, $x$A breach of data confidentiality and accountability$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A breach of data confidentiality and accountability
Each staff member has their OWN Electronic Health Record (EHR) login so that every entry and every record viewed is linked to a named person (an AUDIT TRAIL). Sharing your username/password with a colleague: (1) breaches PATIENT CONFIDENTIALITY — unauthorised people may see records; (2) breaks ACCOUNTABILITY — any wrong entry, missed drug or data theft will be recorded under YOUR name; (3) breaks hospital policy and data protection law (e.g. Nigeria Data Protection Act, professional code of conduct), and can lead to disciplinary action. Always log out or lock the screen when leaving a terminal, never write down passwords, and report any suspected misuse.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acceptable if they are busy — It is NOT acceptable even if the colleague is busy; they must use their own login or get one issued.
• Required by hospital policy — No hospital policy REQUIRES sharing passwords; policies forbid it.
• Only a problem if a wrong entry is made — It is a problem even if no wrong entry is made; the breach happens at the moment of sharing.

⭐ Key point: Never share EHR login details — it breaches CONFIDENTIALITY and ACCOUNTABILITY. Lock the screen when you leave.$x$, null),
(47, $x$Which of the following is an example of internet browser?$x$, $x$Internet explorer$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Internet explorer
A WEB (INTERNET) BROWSER is a software application used to open and view websites on the internet. INTERNET EXPLORER (by Microsoft, now replaced by Microsoft Edge) is a classic example. Other browsers: Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, Safari, Opera. Nurses use browsers to access online drug references, guidelines, e-learning and web-based hospital systems. Basic computer knowledge is tested in nursing informatics.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Microsoft Word — MICROSOFT WORD is a WORD-PROCESSING program for writing documents, not a browser.
• Notepad — NOTEPAD is a simple TEXT EDITOR for plain text, not a browser.
• Internet navigator — "Internet navigator" is not a real browser name; the old browser was "Netscape Navigator", so this option is a distractor.

⭐ Key point: Web browser examples: Internet Explorer/Edge, Chrome, Firefox, Safari. Word = word processor; Notepad = text editor.$x$, null),
(48, $x$A nurse is using an Electronic Health Record (EHR) to access a patient's lab results. The EHR system provides a centralized location for patient data, which helps to:$x$, $x$Improve continuity of care and coordination among the healthcare team$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Improve continuity of care and coordination among the healthcare team
An ELECTRONIC HEALTH RECORD (EHR) keeps a patient's information — history, medications, allergies, lab results, imaging, care plans, notes — in ONE CENTRAL digital place that all authorised members of the team can access at the same time, from different departments. This IMPROVES CONTINUITY OF CARE (each shift and specialist can see what has been done) and COORDINATION among doctors, nurses, pharmacists and others. Other benefits: legible records, quicker access to results, drug-interaction alerts, fewer duplicated tests, and better data for audit and research.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase the time it takes to retrieve patient data — EHRs DECREASE, not increase, the time needed to retrieve patient data.
• Ensure each department uses its own unique record system — The aim is ONE SHARED record, not a separate system for each department (that causes fragmented care).
• Eliminate the need for any verbal communication between nurses — EHRs do NOT replace verbal communication; handover and direct communication between nurses remain essential for safety.

⭐ Key point: EHR = one central record shared by the team → better CONTINUITY and COORDINATION of care.$x$, null),
(49, $x$If a nurse suspects a patient data breach has occurred, the appropriate first action is to:$x$, $x$Report it immediately according to institutional policy$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Report it immediately according to institutional policy
A DATA BREACH is any unauthorised access, loss or sharing of patient information (e.g. a lost laptop, records seen by someone with no reason, a misdirected email). The nurse's first action is to REPORT IT IMMEDIATELY through the proper channel — usually the nurse in charge/manager and the hospital's information governance or data protection officer — following institutional policy. Early reporting lets the hospital contain the breach, protect the patient, and meet legal duties to inform regulators and affected people within set times. Document the facts and do not try to cover it up.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ignore it if no harm seems obvious — IGNORING it is wrong; harm may not be obvious yet, and hiding a breach is itself a disciplinary and legal offence.
• Delete the affected records — DELETING records destroys evidence and the patient's clinical record — this is illegal and unsafe.
• Discuss it publicly with colleagues on social media — Discussing it on SOCIAL MEDIA spreads the breach further and is unprofessional.

⭐ Key point: Suspected data breach → REPORT IMMEDIATELY per policy. Never ignore, delete or post about it.$x$, null),
(50, $x$CT scan and ultrasound machines are best classified as which type of healthcare ICT device?$x$, $x$Diagnostic devices$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Diagnostic devices
Healthcare ICT (information and communication technology) devices are grouped by what they do. DIAGNOSTIC devices help identify disease by producing images or results — CT scanners, MRI, ULTRASOUND, X-ray, ECG machines, laboratory analysers. MONITORING devices continuously watch the patient's condition — cardiac monitors, pulse oximeters, ventilator alarms, ICP monitors. TREATMENT (therapeutic) devices deliver therapy — infusion pumps, ventilators, dialysis machines, defibrillators. COMMUNICATION devices share information — phones, pagers, nurse call systems, telemedicine links. CT and ultrasound make images to help diagnose, so they are DIAGNOSTIC devices.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Monitoring devices only — MONITORING devices track vital signs over time; CT and ultrasound are not used for continuous monitoring.
• Treatment devices only — TREATMENT devices deliver therapy; CT and ultrasound mainly diagnose (ultrasound can guide procedures but that is not its classification).
• Communication devices — COMMUNICATION devices transmit messages; imaging machines are not in this group.

⭐ Key point: CT, ultrasound, MRI, X-ray = DIAGNOSTIC devices.$x$, null),
(51, $x$The target SpO₂ in acute severe asthma treatment is:$x$, $x$93–95%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 93–95%
In ACUTE SEVERE ASTHMA, the British Thoracic Society/SIGN guideline says give controlled oxygen to keep SpO2 at 94–98%; many exam sources (and this question) quote a target of about 93–95%. The point is: give enough oxygen to correct hypoxaemia, but avoid aiming for 100%, because high-flow oxygen can mask deterioration and unnecessary hyperoxia has no benefit. Oxygen is used to drive nebulised salbutamol in asthma. Life-threatening asthma signs: SpO2 under 92%, PEF under 33%, silent chest, cyanosis, exhaustion, confusion, normal or rising PaCO2 — these need urgent senior/ICU review.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 100% — 100% is not the target; excessive oxygen gives no extra benefit and may hide worsening.
• 97–99% — 97–99% is higher than needed; the guideline target range is lower.
• 85–90% — 85–90% is too LOW for asthma; it is close to the COPD/CO2-retainer range (88–92%) and means dangerous hypoxaemia in asthma.

⭐ Key point: Acute asthma: target SpO2 about 93–95% (BTS 94–98%). SpO2 below 92% = life-threatening.$x$, null),
(52, $x$A ventilated patient's SpO2 drops from 96% to 88% acutely. Using a systematic differential for displacement, obstruction, pneumothorax, and equipment failure, the nurse should assess for all of these EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Recent oral care$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Recent oral care
When a ventilated patient suddenly desaturates, use the DOPE mnemonic: DISPLACEMENT of the tube (slipped into the right main bronchus or out of the trachea), OBSTRUCTION of the tube (secretions, blood, kinking, biting), PNEUMOTHORAX (especially tension pneumothorax from high pressures), EQUIPMENT failure (ventilator, oxygen supply, disconnected circuit). Immediate action: disconnect from the ventilator and hand-ventilate with 100% oxygen with a bag-valve device, check tube position and breath sounds, pass a suction catheter, look at chest movement, check the equipment. RECENT ORAL CARE is part of routine VAP prevention and is not one of the causes in this emergency differential (though careless oral care can move the tube, which is "displacement").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tube displacement or dislodgement — Tube DISPLACEMENT is the "D" in DOPE and must be checked.
• Airway obstruction from secretions — Airway OBSTRUCTION from secretions is the "O" in DOPE — suction is a key step.
• Pneumothorax — PNEUMOTHORAX is the "P" in DOPE; a tension pneumothorax needs immediate decompression.

⭐ Key point: Sudden desaturation on a ventilator = DOPE: Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment.$x$, null),
(53, $x$What percentage of chest injuries are managed WITHOUT surgery?$x$, $x$80%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 80%
Most CHEST INJURIES — about 80–85% (often quoted as "over 80%" or "85–90%") — are managed WITHOUT major surgery (thoracotomy). The usual treatments are simple: oxygen, good PAIN RELIEF (so the patient can breathe deeply and cough), physiotherapy, and CHEST DRAIN (intercostal tube) insertion for pneumothorax or haemothorax. Only about 10–20% need thoracotomy — for example, massive haemothorax (over 1500 mL immediately or over 200 mL/hour), cardiac tamponade not relieved, major airway or great vessel injury.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 20% — 20% is far too low; it would mean most chest injuries need surgery, which is not true.
• 50% — 50% is also too low.
• 100% — 100% is impossible — some chest injuries (e.g. massive bleeding, great vessel injury) definitely need surgery.

⭐ Key point: About 80% of chest injuries need NO thoracotomy — just oxygen, analgesia, physiotherapy and chest drains.$x$, null),
(54, $x$Inhaled nitric oxide is used in pulmonary hypertensive crisis primarily because it:$x$, $x$Selectively dilates the pulmonary vasculature with minimal systemic hypotension$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Selectively dilates the pulmonary vasculature with minimal systemic hypotension
INHALED NITRIC OXIDE (iNO) is a gas given through the ventilator circuit (usually 5–20 ppm). Because it is breathed in, it reaches only the pulmonary vessels next to VENTILATED alveoli, where it relaxes vascular smooth muscle (through cGMP), DILATING the pulmonary arteries. This lowers PULMONARY artery pressure and improves V/Q matching (better oxygenation). It is quickly inactivated by haemoglobin in the blood, so it has very little effect on SYSTEMIC BP — it is a SELECTIVE pulmonary vasodilator. Uses: pulmonary hypertensive crisis (e.g. after cardiac surgery), persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn, right heart failure. Monitor methaemoglobin and NO2 levels; do not stop it suddenly (rebound pulmonary hypertension).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increases systemic vascular resistance — iNO does NOT increase systemic vascular resistance; it is a vasodilator.
• Acts as a potent bronchoconstrictor — It is a BRONCHODILATOR/vasodilator effect, not a bronchoconstrictor.
• Directly increases myocardial contractility — It does not directly increase myocardial contractility; it helps the right ventricle by lowering its afterload.

⭐ Key point: Inhaled NO = SELECTIVE PULMONARY VASODILATOR — lowers pulmonary pressure with little systemic hypotension. Wean slowly.$x$, null),
(55, $x$The most common complication following thoracotomy is:$x$, $x$Atelectasis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Atelectasis
After THORACOTOMY (opening the chest), the commonest complication is ATELECTASIS — collapse of alveoli/lung segments. Reasons: severe incision pain makes the patient breathe shallowly and avoid coughing; anaesthesia and opioids reduce deep breaths; secretions build up and block small airways. Atelectasis causes low SpO2, fever in the first 1–2 days and can lead to pneumonia. Prevention is a key nursing task: good PAIN CONTROL (epidural/PCA), early mobilisation, deep breathing exercises, incentive spirometry, supported coughing (splinting the wound with a pillow), chest physiotherapy, and managing chest drains.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Wound dehiscence — WOUND DEHISCENCE (wound opening) is possible but uncommon after thoracotomy.
• Bronchopleural fistula — BRONCHOPLEURAL FISTULA is a serious complication (mainly after pneumonectomy/lobectomy) but much less common.
• Pericardial tamponade — PERICARDIAL TAMPONADE is rare after thoracotomy; it is more associated with cardiac surgery.

⭐ Key point: Commonest post-thoracotomy complication = ATELECTASIS. Prevent with pain control, deep breathing, coughing, early mobilisation.$x$, null),
(56, $x$A patient with respiratory failure is receiving oxygenation and ventilation support. The nurse knows that the main goal of this therapy is to:$x$, $x$Maintain adequate gas exchange and support vital organ function$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Maintain adequate gas exchange and support vital organ function
RESPIRATORY FAILURE means the lungs cannot keep normal blood gases: Type 1 — low PaO2 (below 60 mmHg/8 kPa) with normal or low CO2; Type 2 — low PaO2 plus high PaCO2 (above 50 mmHg/6.5 kPa). Oxygen therapy and ventilation (NIV or mechanical) are SUPPORTIVE: their main goal is to MAINTAIN ADEQUATE GAS EXCHANGE (oxygenation and CO2 removal) so that the brain, heart, kidneys and other VITAL ORGANS keep working, while the medical team treats the cause and the lungs recover. They "buy time".

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Keep the patient asleep and comfortable — Keeping the patient asleep and comfortable is a part of sedation management, not the main goal of ventilation.
• Reduce the workload of the heart — Ventilation may reduce the work of BREATHING and can affect the heart, but reducing cardiac workload is not the main aim.
• Reverse the underlying cause of the respiratory failure — Ventilation itself does NOT reverse the cause (e.g. pneumonia needs antibiotics, asthma needs bronchodilators); it supports the patient while the cause is treated.

⭐ Key point: Goal of oxygen/ventilation in respiratory failure = maintain ADEQUATE GAS EXCHANGE and protect vital organs while the cause is treated.$x$, null),
(57, $x$Non-invasive ventilation (NIV) such as BiPAP is generally contraindicated in a patient who:$x$, $x$Has a reduced level of consciousness and cannot protect their airway$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Has a reduced level of consciousness and cannot protect their airway
NIV (BiPAP/CPAP) uses a tight face or nasal mask; the patient must be able to breathe on their own, cooperate, and PROTECT THEIR AIRWAY (cough, swallow, clear secretions). A patient with REDUCED CONSCIOUSNESS (e.g. GCS below about 8, not due to CO2 narcosis that may quickly improve) cannot protect the airway, so there is a high risk of vomiting and ASPIRATION into the lungs under the mask; such patients need intubation. Other contraindications: respiratory arrest, haemodynamic instability, facial trauma/burns, recent upper GI or facial surgery, copious secretions, vomiting, undrained pneumothorax, inability to fit the mask.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Has mild COPD exacerbation with good cooperation — A MILD COPD exacerbation in a cooperative patient is actually a classic INDICATION (especially with hypercapnic acidosis).
• Is able to tolerate the mask comfortably — Being able to TOLERATE THE MASK is needed for NIV to work — it supports use, not contraindicates it.
• Has stable haemodynamics — STABLE HAEMODYNAMICS is a requirement for safe NIV, not a contraindication.

⭐ Key point: NIV needs an alert, cooperative patient who can protect their airway. Reduced consciousness = contraindication → intubate.$x$, null),
(58, $x$A patient with pulmonary fibrosis has restrictive lung disease. On spirometry, the nurse would expect:$x$, $x$Reduced FEV1 with a normal or increased FEV1/FVC ratio, and reduced total lung capacity$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Reduced FEV1 with a normal or increased FEV1/FVC ratio, and reduced total lung capacity
In RESTRICTIVE lung disease (pulmonary fibrosis, chest wall deformity, obesity, neuromuscular weakness) the lungs cannot EXPAND fully. Both FEV1 (air blown out in 1 second) and FVC (total air blown out) are REDUCED, but they fall roughly together, so the FEV1/FVC RATIO is NORMAL or even HIGH (above 70–80%, because stiff lungs empty quickly). TOTAL LUNG CAPACITY is REDUCED — this is the hallmark of restriction. Gas transfer (DLCO) is also reduced in fibrosis. In OBSTRUCTIVE disease (asthma, COPD) FEV1 falls much more than FVC, so the ratio is LOW (below 0.7), and TLC is normal or increased (air trapping).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increased total lung capacity with a low FEV1/FVC ratio — High TLC with a LOW FEV1/FVC ratio describes OBSTRUCTIVE disease such as emphysema, not fibrosis.
• Normal spirometry results in all cases — Spirometry is NOT normal in significant restrictive disease; volumes are reduced.
• Increased FVC with normal FEV1 — FVC is DECREASED, not increased, in restrictive disease.

⭐ Key point: Restrictive = low FEV1 and FVC, NORMAL/HIGH ratio, LOW TLC. Obstructive = LOW ratio (<0.7), high TLC.$x$, null),
(59, $x$In patients with respiratory distress, damage to type II alveolar cells can impair which of the following key functions?$x$, $x$Secretion of surfactant$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Secretion of surfactant
The alveolar wall has two main cell types. TYPE I cells are thin and flat, covering about 95% of the surface and forming the gas exchange barrier. TYPE II cells are rounder and make SURFACTANT — a mixture of phospholipids and proteins that lowers SURFACE TENSION in the alveoli, stopping them from collapsing at the end of expiration and making the lungs easier to inflate. Type II cells also act as stem cells to repair type I cells. Damage to type II cells (as in ARDS, or immaturity in premature babies — neonatal respiratory distress syndrome) means less surfactant, so alveoli collapse (atelectasis), lungs become stiff and hypoxaemia worsens.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immunity — IMMUNITY in the alveoli is mainly provided by alveolar MACROPHAGES, not type II cells.
• Capacitation — CAPACITATION is a process in which SPERM become able to fertilise an egg; it has nothing to do with the lungs.
• Gaseous exchange — Gas exchange happens mainly across TYPE I cells; type II damage affects it indirectly, but the KEY specific type II function is surfactant secretion.

⭐ Key point: Type II alveolar cells = SURFACTANT (prevents collapse). Type I cells = gas exchange surface.$x$, null),
(60, $x$In which mode of ventilator the patient will initiate his/her own breath and does not depend on ventilator. Ventilator will only assess the patient efforts.$x$, $x$Spontaneous mood$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Spontaneous mood
In SPONTANEOUS mode (e.g. CPAP or Pressure Support/PSV), the PATIENT starts EVERY breath and controls the rate and depth; the ventilator does not give mandatory breaths. It only senses the patient's effort (trigger) and gives support — a set pressure (pressure support) and/or PEEP — to make breathing easier. There is no backup rate unless apnoea ventilation is set, so it is used for alert patients with a good respiratory drive, especially during WEANING (spontaneous breathing trials).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• AC mood — ASSIST-CONTROL (AC) gives a set number of full mandatory breaths; every patient-triggered breath also gets a full set breath, so the patient depends on the ventilator.
• SIMV mood — SIMV gives a set number of mandatory breaths synchronised with the patient; between them the patient can breathe spontaneously — but it still delivers mandatory breaths.
• VCV mood — VCV (volume-controlled ventilation) delivers a set tidal volume at a set rate — a controlled mode, not patient-driven.

⭐ Key point: SPONTANEOUS mode (CPAP/PSV): patient starts all breaths; ventilator only supports — used for weaning.$x$, null),
(61, $x$Using the ABCDE framework to prioritise care for a newly admitted critically ill patient, which finding would take priority over the others?$x$, $x$Stridor with visible respiratory distress$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Stridor with visible respiratory distress
The ABCDE approach (Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Disability, Exposure) puts problems in order of how quickly they kill. AIRWAY comes first: a blocked airway can cause death within minutes. STRIDOR is a high-pitched noise on breathing in, caused by narrowing of the upper airway (larynx/trachea) — e.g. swelling, foreign body, anaphylaxis, burns, epiglottitis. Stridor PLUS visible respiratory distress means the airway is in danger now. Action: call for senior/anaesthetic help immediately, sit the patient up, give high-flow oxygen, prepare for airway management (adrenaline nebuliser if croup/anaphylaxis, intubation or surgical airway).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mild abrasions on the forearm — Mild forearm ABRASIONS belong to E (Exposure) — the lowest priority here.
• A blood pressure of 128/82 — A BP of 128/82 is a near-normal circulation finding — no urgent action.
• A pain score of 3/10 — A pain score of 3/10 is mild pain; it should be treated but is not life-threatening.

⭐ Key point: ABCDE: AIRWAY first. Stridor + respiratory distress = threatened airway → highest priority.$x$, null),
(62, $x$In status asthmaticus, oxygen is given to keep the patient's SpO2 at:$x$, $x$Greater than 92%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Greater than 92%
In STATUS ASTHMATICUS (severe asthma attack not responding to usual treatment), the patient is hypoxic because of bronchospasm, mucus plugging and V/Q mismatch. Oxygen should be given to keep SpO2 ABOVE 92% (guidelines: 94–98%). Low oxygen in asthma is a sign of LIFE-THREATENING severity, and a falling SpO2 means the patient is tiring. Other treatment: nebulised salbutamol (back-to-back) with ipratropium, systemic steroids, IV magnesium sulphate, and ICU review for possible intubation if exhausted or PaCO2 rising.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 75% — 75% is severe, dangerous hypoxaemia — far too low.
• 88% — 88% is the lower target used for COPD patients at risk of CO2 retention, not for asthma.
• Exactly 90% — "Exactly 90%" is too low and too rigid; a target range above 92% is used.

⭐ Key point: Status asthmaticus: keep SpO2 > 92% (94–98%). SpO2 < 92% = life-threatening.$x$, null),
(63, $x$While monitoring during ventilator support, which one of the following is NOT a responsibility of nurse;$x$, $x$Change ventilator setting frequently$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Change ventilator setting frequently
During mechanical ventilation the NURSE's responsibilities include: monitoring the patient (SpO2, vital signs, chest movement, breath sounds, comfort, sedation level), checking for AIR LEAKS (cuff pressure 20–30 cmH2O, circuit connections), SUCTIONING when there are secretions (as needed, not routinely), checking and responding to alarms, collecting/assisting with ABG samples and reporting results, oral care and VAP prevention, and accurate documentation. CHANGING VENTILATOR SETTINGS is NOT a routine nursing task — settings (FiO2, PEEP, mode, rate, tidal volume) are ordered by the doctor/intensivist (or respiratory therapist per protocol), and changing them "frequently" without orders would be unsafe and unstable for the patient.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Check any indications of air leak — Checking for AIR LEAKS IS a nursing responsibility (cuff pressure, circuit).
• Do suctioning if needed — SUCTIONING as needed IS a nursing responsibility.
• Arterial blood gas measurement — ARTERIAL BLOOD GAS sampling/monitoring IS part of nursing care (taking samples from an arterial line and reporting results).

⭐ Key point: Nurses monitor, suction, check leaks and ABGs — they do NOT change ventilator settings without orders.$x$, null),
(64, $x$Stroke volume is defined as:$x$, $x$The volume of blood ejected by the ventricle with each heartbeat$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The volume of blood ejected by the ventricle with each heartbeat
STROKE VOLUME (SV) is the volume of blood ejected by the VENTRICLE with EACH heartbeat. Normal is about 60–100 mL (about 70 mL average). It is found by: SV = end-diastolic volume − end-systolic volume. CARDIAC OUTPUT = SV × heart rate (about 70 mL × 70 beats/min ≈ 5 L/min). Stroke volume depends on three things: PRELOAD (filling — how much the ventricle is stretched), AFTERLOAD (resistance the ventricle pumps against) and CONTRACTILITY (strength of contraction). In shock, SV falls; nurses support it with fluids (preload), vasodilators (afterload) or inotropes (contractility).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The total blood volume in the body — Total blood volume in the body is about 5 litres (70 mL/kg) — not stroke volume.
• The heart rate over one minute — Heart rate over one minute is the PULSE/heart rate, not the volume pumped.
• The pressure generated during diastole — Pressure during diastole is the DIASTOLIC blood pressure, not stroke volume.

⭐ Key point: Stroke volume = blood ejected per beat (60–100 mL). CO = SV × HR. SV depends on preload, afterload, contractility.$x$, null),
(65, $x$Normal central venous pressure (CVP) is generally in the range of:$x$, $x$2–8 mmHg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 2–8 mmHg
CENTRAL VENOUS PRESSURE (CVP) is the pressure in the superior vena cava/right atrium, measured through a central line. It reflects RIGHT-SIDED filling pressure and is used (with other signs) to guide fluid therapy. Normal is about 2–8 mmHg (about 3–10 cmH2O). The transducer must be LEVELLED at the phlebostatic axis (4th intercostal space, mid-axillary line) and zeroed, and readings are taken at end-expiration. LOW CVP suggests hypovolaemia (dehydration, bleeding). HIGH CVP suggests fluid overload, right heart failure, cardiac tamponade, tension pneumothorax, pulmonary hypertension, or high PEEP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 20–30 mmHg — 20–30 mmHg is very HIGH — e.g. tamponade or severe right heart failure.
• 40–50 mmHg — 40–50 mmHg is impossible as a normal venous pressure.
• Below 0 mmHg — Below 0 mmHg would indicate severe hypovolaemia or a measurement error.

⭐ Key point: Normal CVP ≈ 2–8 mmHg (3–10 cmH2O). Level the transducer at the 4th ICS, mid-axillary line.$x$, null),
(66, $x$Which one of the following is NOT the cause of cardiogenic shock?$x$, $x$Spinal Cord Injury$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Spinal Cord Injury
CARDIOGENIC SHOCK is shock caused by the HEART failing as a PUMP — cardiac output falls despite adequate blood volume, so tissues are under-perfused. Causes are cardiac: acute MYOCARDIAL INFARCTION (the commonest, usually when over 40% of the left ventricle is damaged), severe heart failure (CHF), CARDIOMYOPATHY, arrhythmias, valve rupture, myocarditis. SPINAL CORD INJURY (above T6) causes NEUROGENIC SHOCK — a type of distributive shock: loss of sympathetic tone causes vasodilation, HYPOTENSION and BRADYCARDIA with warm, dry skin. So spinal cord injury is NOT a cause of cardiogenic shock.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CHF — CHF (congestive heart failure) IS a cause — a weak pump cannot keep up cardiac output.
• Cardiomyopathy — CARDIOMYOPATHY IS a cause — diseased heart muscle pumps poorly.
• MI — MI IS the most common cause of cardiogenic shock.

⭐ Key point: Cardiogenic shock = pump failure (MI, CHF, cardiomyopathy). Spinal cord injury → NEUROGENIC shock (low BP + low pulse).$x$, null),
(67, $x$CPR may be stopped in all of the following situations EXCEPT:$x$, $x$The rescuer feels the situation is hopeless$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The rescuer feels the situation is hopeless
Once CPR has started it should continue until one of these happens: (1) RETURN OF SPONTANEOUS CIRCULATION (ROSC) — the patient has a pulse; (2) a QUALIFIED person (or team, e.g. ambulance/resuscitation team) TAKES OVER; (3) a PHYSICIAN or authorised person PRONOUNCES DEATH or stops resuscitation; (4) the rescuer is physically EXHAUSTED and cannot go on, or the scene becomes UNSAFE; or (5) a valid DNACPR order is presented. Simply FEELING that the situation is hopeless is NOT a valid reason for the rescuer to stop — that is a personal emotional judgement, not a clinical decision. Survival is possible even after prolonged CPR, e.g. in hypothermia or drowning.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Return of spontaneous circulation — ROSC IS a reason to stop compressions and start post-resuscitation care.
• A qualified person takes over — A qualified person TAKING OVER IS a reason to stop (they continue CPR).
• A physician pronounces death — A physician PRONOUNCING DEATH IS a reason to stop.

⭐ Key point: Stop CPR for ROSC, handover, death pronounced, exhaustion, unsafe scene or valid DNACPR — NOT because you "feel" it is hopeless.$x$, null),
(68, $x$According to 2017 ACC/AHA criteria, Stage II hypertension is defined as:$x$, $x$≥140/≥90$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: ≥140/≥90
The 2017 ACC/AHA blood pressure categories are: NORMAL — below 120 and below 80; ELEVATED — 120–129 systolic and below 80; STAGE 1 hypertension — 130–139 systolic OR 80–89 diastolic; STAGE 2 hypertension — 140 or higher systolic OR 90 or higher diastolic. Hypertensive crisis — above 180 and/or above 120. Stage 2 usually needs lifestyle change PLUS medication (often two drugs). Remember to diagnose from repeated, correctly measured readings (correct cuff size, patient seated and rested 5 minutes, arm at heart level).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• <120/<80 — Below 120/80 is NORMAL blood pressure.
• 120–129/<80 — 120–129/below 80 is ELEVATED blood pressure (not yet hypertension).
• 130–139/80–89 — 130–139/80–89 is STAGE 1 hypertension.

⭐ Key point: ACC/AHA 2017: Normal <120/<80; Elevated 120–129/<80; Stage 1 130–139 or 80–89; STAGE 2 ≥140 or ≥90.$x$, null),
(69, $x$While performing hemodynamic monitoring of the patient, the transducer should be kept at the Phlebostatic axis. The nurse knows that it is located at:$x$, $x$Intersection of line between 4th intercostal space and mid-axillary line$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Intersection of line between 4th intercostal space and mid-axillary line
The PHLEBOSTATIC AXIS is the reference point that marks the level of the RIGHT ATRIUM. It is found where a line from the 4th INTERCOSTAL SPACE at the sternal border meets the MID-AXILLARY line (half-way between the front and back of the chest) with the patient lying flat or with the head of bed up to 45°. The pressure transducer (for arterial line, CVP, pulmonary artery catheter) must be levelled and zeroed at this point. If the transducer is placed too LOW, readings are falsely HIGH; if too HIGH, readings are falsely LOW (about 0.74 mmHg per cm of error). Mark the point on the skin so all staff use the same level.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Intersection of line between 2nd intercostal space and mid-axillary line — The 2nd intercostal space is too high — it is near the angle of Louis (sternal angle), not the right atrium level.
• Intersection of line between 3rd intercostal space and mid-axillary line — The 3rd intercostal space is too high for the standard reference point.
• Intersection of line between 5th intercostal space and mid-axillary line — The 5th intercostal space at the mid-axillary line is too low; using it would give falsely high readings.

⭐ Key point: Phlebostatic axis = 4th ICS + MID-AXILLARY line = level of the right atrium. Transducer low → falsely high readings.$x$, null),
(70, $x$CK-MB (creatine kinase-MB) is used clinically to help assess:$x$, $x$Recent or ongoing myocardial injury, particularly reinfarction$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Recent or ongoing myocardial injury, particularly reinfarction
CK-MB is the form of creatine kinase found mainly in HEART MUSCLE. When heart muscle is damaged (myocardial infarction), CK-MB leaks into the blood: it RISES 3–6 hours after injury, PEAKS at 12–24 hours, and RETURNS TO NORMAL within 48–72 hours. Because it clears quickly, it is especially useful to detect REINFARCTION (a new heart attack a few days after the first), when troponin is still high from the first event (troponin stays raised for 7–14 days). Troponin (I or T) is now the preferred, most sensitive and specific marker for diagnosing MI.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Kidney function — KIDNEY function is assessed with urea, creatinine and eGFR, not CK-MB.
• Coagulation status — COAGULATION status is assessed with PT/INR, aPTT, platelets and fibrinogen.
• Blood glucose control — BLOOD GLUCOSE control is assessed with glucose and HbA1c.

⭐ Key point: CK-MB = heart muscle damage; rises 3–6 h, normal by 48–72 h → useful for REINFARCTION. Troponin stays up 7–14 days.$x$, null),
(71, $x$The following are some of the drugs used in MI EXCEPT$x$, $x$Antibiotics$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Antibiotics
Drug treatment of MYOCARDIAL INFARCTION aims to restore blood flow, reduce heart workload, prevent new clots and protect the heart long term. Memory aid "MONA-BASH" covers the main drugs: Morphine (pain), Oxygen (only if SpO2 < 90–94%), Nitrates (GTN), Aspirin/Antiplatelets (plus clopidogrel/ticagrelor), BETA-BLOCKERS (slow the heart, reduce oxygen demand and arrhythmias), ACE inhibitors (prevent remodelling), STATINS (lower cholesterol and stabilise plaque), Heparin (anticoagulant). Thrombolytics or PCI open the blocked artery. Some CALCIUM CHANNEL BLOCKERS (verapamil, diltiazem) may be used when beta-blockers are contraindicated and to control angina. ANTIBIOTICS have no role, because MI is caused by a blocked coronary artery (thrombus on plaque), not by infection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• B Blockers — BETA-BLOCKERS ARE used in MI — they reduce oxygen demand and the risk of arrhythmia and death.
• Statins — STATINS ARE used — high-intensity statin is started in all MI patients unless contraindicated.
• Ca Channel Blockers — CALCIUM CHANNEL BLOCKERS ARE used in some patients for angina/rate control when beta-blockers cannot be used.

⭐ Key point: MI drugs = MONA-BASH (morphine, oxygen, nitrates, aspirin, beta-blocker, ACE-I, statin, heparin). Antibiotics are NOT part of MI treatment.$x$, null),
(72, $x$The main aim of the Intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP) include all except:$x$, $x$increase myocardial O2 demand$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: increase myocardial O2 demand
The INTRA-AORTIC BALLOON PUMP (IABP) is a balloon in the descending thoracic aorta. It INFLATES in DIASTOLE (right after the aortic valve closes, at the dicrotic notch), pushing blood back into the coronary arteries and forward to the body — this INCREASES CORONARY PERFUSION and oxygen supply. It DEFLATES just before SYSTOLE, creating a "vacuum" that LOWERS AFTERLOAD, so the left ventricle pumps more easily — this DECREASES MYOCARDIAL OXYGEN DEMAND and INCREASES CARDIAC OUTPUT. Better output also improves organ blood flow, including the KIDNEYS. So increasing myocardial oxygen demand is the OPPOSITE of what an IABP does. Nursing: check distal pulses of the leg, watch for limb ischaemia, keep the leg straight, check the left radial pulse (balloon too high blocks the left subclavian) and urine output (balloon too low blocks renal arteries).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• increase renal blood — Increased RENAL blood flow IS an effect, from better cardiac output.
• increasing cardiac output — INCREASING CARDIAC OUTPUT IS a main aim, through reduced afterload.
• decrease myocardial O2 demand — DECREASING myocardial oxygen demand IS a main aim — the heart works less.

⭐ Key point: IABP: inflate in diastole (↑ coronary flow), deflate before systole (↓ afterload) → ↓ O2 DEMAND, ↑ cardiac output.$x$, null),
(73, $x$Trending serial lactate levels during shock resuscitation is useful because:$x$, $x$Lactate clearance correlates with improving tissue perfusion$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Lactate clearance correlates with improving tissue perfusion
LACTATE is made when tissues don't get enough oxygen and switch to ANAEROBIC metabolism. In shock, a raised lactate (above 2 mmol/L; above 4 is severe) shows tissue HYPOPERFUSION. Measuring it SERIALLY (e.g. every 2–4 hours) shows whether resuscitation is working: a FALLING lactate (LACTATE CLEARANCE, e.g. a drop of 10–20% or more) means perfusion is improving and is linked to better survival; a static or rising lactate means the patient still needs more treatment (fluids, vasopressors, source control). This is why the Surviving Sepsis Campaign recommends re-measuring lactate if the first value is raised.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lactate has no relationship to perfusion status — Lactate DOES relate to perfusion — that is exactly why it is measured in shock.
• It replaces the need for vital sign monitoring — It does NOT replace vital signs monitoring; it is used together with BP, heart rate, urine output, capillary refill and mental state.
• It only reflects liver function — The liver clears lactate, so liver failure can raise it, but it mainly reflects tissue perfusion/oxygen delivery, not "only" liver function.

⭐ Key point: Falling lactate (lactate clearance) = improving tissue perfusion. Rising lactate = ongoing hypoperfusion.$x$, null),
(74, $x$Which test is performed in order to check collateral circulation, before going to pass arterial line catheter in radial artery;$x$, $x$Allen test$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Allen test
The ALLEN TEST checks that the hand has a good blood supply from the ULNAR artery before the RADIAL artery is punctured or cannulated (arterial line or ABG). Steps: (1) ask the patient to make a tight fist; (2) press firmly on both the radial and ulnar arteries at the wrist; (3) the patient opens the hand — the palm looks pale; (4) RELEASE the ULNAR artery only; (5) colour should return to the palm within about 5–15 seconds (positive/normal test = good collateral flow). If colour takes longer than about 15 seconds, collateral circulation is poor and the radial artery should NOT be used, because if it clots, the hand could become ischaemic.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Romberg test — The ROMBERG test checks balance/proprioception (patient stands with feet together and eyes closed).
• Weber test — The WEBER test uses a tuning fork on the forehead to check for conductive or sensorineural hearing loss.
• Whisper test — The WHISPER test is a simple screening test for hearing.

⭐ Key point: Before a radial arterial line or ABG → ALLEN TEST (checks ulnar collateral flow; colour back within 5–15 s).$x$, null),
(75, $x$Which of the following descriptions correctly characterizes the cardiac glycosides (e.g. digoxin)?$x$, $x$POSITIVE INOTROPE, NEGATIVE CHRONOTROPE$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: POSITIVE INOTROPE, NEGATIVE CHRONOTROPE
DIGOXIN (a cardiac glycoside) has two main heart effects. (1) POSITIVE INOTROPE: it blocks the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase pump in heart muscle; this raises calcium inside the cells, so the heart contracts more STRONGLY — helpful in heart failure. (2) NEGATIVE CHRONOTROPE: it increases vagal (parasympathetic) tone and slows conduction through the AV node, so the HEART RATE FALLS — helpful to control the rate in atrial fibrillation. Nursing: check the APICAL pulse for a full minute before giving and hold if below 60 bpm (adults); monitor potassium (LOW K⁺ increases digoxin toxicity) and digoxin levels (0.5–2 ng/mL). Toxicity signs: nausea, vomiting, anorexia, yellow-green vision (xanthopsia), confusion, bradycardia and arrhythmias; antidote is digoxin-specific antibody (DigiFab).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• POSITIVE INOTROPE, POSITIVE CHRONOTROPE — "Positive inotrope, positive chronotrope" describes drugs like dobutamine/adrenaline — digoxin SLOWS the heart.
• NEGATIVE INOTROPE, NEGATIVE CHRONOTROPE — "Negative inotrope, negative chronotrope" describes beta-blockers or verapamil — digoxin STRENGTHENS contraction.
• NEGATIVE INOTROPE, POSITIVE CHRONOTROPE — "Negative inotrope, positive chronotrope" is the opposite of digoxin on both counts.

⭐ Key point: Digoxin = POSITIVE inotrope (stronger) + NEGATIVE chronotrope (slower). Check apical pulse; hold if < 60; low K⁺ → toxicity.$x$, null),
(76, $x$In critical care, what is the primary purpose of a pulmonary artery catheter (PAC)?$x$, $x$D) Assessing cardiac function and pressures$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: D) Assessing cardiac function and pressures
A PULMONARY ARTERY CATHETER (PAC, Swan-Ganz) is a long, balloon-tipped catheter passed through a central vein, the right atrium and right ventricle into the pulmonary artery. It is used to ASSESS CARDIAC FUNCTION AND PRESSURES: right atrial pressure (CVP), pulmonary artery pressure, pulmonary artery WEDGE pressure (PAWP/PCWP — an estimate of left atrial/left ventricular filling pressure, normal 6–12 mmHg), CARDIAC OUTPUT (by thermodilution), systemic and pulmonary vascular resistance, and mixed venous oxygen saturation (SvO2). It helps guide fluids, inotropes and vasopressors in complex shock, cardiac surgery and severe heart failure. Risks: arrhythmias, infection, pulmonary artery rupture, pulmonary infarction (never leave the balloon wedged).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A) Monitoring brain activity — Monitoring BRAIN activity is done with EEG, not a PAC.
• B) Administering anesthesia — ANAESTHESIA is given by IV drugs, inhaled gases or nerve blocks, not by a PAC.
• C) Measuring intracranial pressure (ICP) — INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE is measured with an ICP monitor (intraventricular drain or parenchymal probe), not a PAC.

⭐ Key point: PAC (Swan-Ganz) = assesses CARDIAC FUNCTION: PA pressure, wedge pressure, cardiac output, SvO2.$x$, null),
(77, $x$The juxtaglomerular apparatus regulates blood pressure and renal blood flow chiefly by secreting which substance?$x$, $x$Renin$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Renin
The JUXTAGLOMERULAR APPARATUS (JGA) sits where the afferent arteriole touches the distal tubule of each nephron. Its JUXTAGLOMERULAR cells release RENIN when they sense LOW blood pressure/low renal blood flow, low sodium (detected by the macula densa) or sympathetic stimulation. Renin starts the RENIN–ANGIOTENSIN–ALDOSTERONE SYSTEM (RAAS): renin converts angiotensinogen (from the liver) to angiotensin I; ACE (mainly in the lungs) converts it to ANGIOTENSIN II, which constricts blood vessels and triggers ALDOSTERONE release (sodium and water retention) and ADH and thirst — together raising BP and restoring kidney perfusion. ACE inhibitors and ARBs block this system to treat hypertension and heart failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Antidiuretic hormone — ADH (antidiuretic hormone) is made in the hypothalamus and released from the POSTERIOR PITUITARY, not the JGA.
• Aldosterone — ALDOSTERONE is made in the ADRENAL CORTEX (zona glomerulosa); the JGA starts the chain that leads to it but does not secrete it.
• Erythropoietin — ERYTHROPOIETIN is made by peritubular interstitial cells in the kidney in response to low oxygen; it stimulates red cell production, not BP control.

⭐ Key point: JGA secretes RENIN → angiotensin II → aldosterone → ↑ BP. ACE-I/ARBs block this pathway.$x$, null),
(78, $x$Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy (CRRT), compared to intermittent haemodialysis, is often preferred in haemodynamically unstable ICU patients because it:$x$, $x$Removes fluid/solutes more slowly and continuously, causing less haemodynamic stress$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Removes fluid/solutes more slowly and continuously, causing less haemodynamic stress
CRRT (Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy, e.g. CVVH, CVVHD, CVVHDF) runs 24 hours a day and removes fluid and waste SLOWLY and CONTINUOUSLY. Because fluid is removed gently over many hours (e.g. 50–200 mL/hour), there are smaller shifts of fluid and solutes, so BP stays more STABLE. This makes it the preferred method in HAEMODYNAMICALLY UNSTABLE ICU patients (shock, on vasopressors), and in patients with brain injury (less risk of raised ICP from rapid osmotic shifts). Intermittent haemodialysis (IHD) removes the same amount in 3–4 hours, which can cause sudden HYPOTENSION in unstable patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Is always faster at removing toxins — CRRT is NOT faster; IHD is faster at clearing toxins, which is why IHD is preferred for some poisonings and severe hyperkalaemia.
• Requires no vascular access — CRRT DOES need vascular access — a large double-lumen dialysis catheter (femoral, internal jugular).
• Has no risk of complications — CRRT has risks: bleeding from anticoagulation, filter clotting, hypothermia, electrolyte imbalance (low K⁺, low phosphate), line infection.

⭐ Key point: CRRT = slow, continuous removal → less haemodynamic stress → best for unstable ICU patients. IHD is faster but causes more hypotension.$x$, null),
(79, $x$At 10 years, survival with a living-related renal transplant is approximately:$x$, $x$74.84%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 74.84%
Kidney transplant survival depends on the donor type. A LIVING-RELATED (living) donor kidney gives better results than a deceased-donor kidney because the kidney is healthy, carefully matched, and transplanted with very short cold ischaemia time. Long-term data show about 75% (here 74.84%) of patients/grafts surviving at 10 YEARS after living-related transplant, compared with lower figures for deceased donors (around 55–60%). Survival also depends on matching (HLA), immunosuppression adherence, prevention of rejection and infection, and control of BP and diabetes. Nurses play a big role in teaching patients to take immunosuppressants on time and report signs of rejection (fever, reduced urine, graft pain, rising creatinine).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 8.49% — 8.49% is far too low; this would mean almost every transplant fails within 10 years, which is not true.
• 55.07% — 55.07% is closer to deceased-donor 10-year survival, not living-related.
• 100% — 100% is impossible; some grafts are always lost through rejection, infection or death from other causes.

⭐ Key point: Living-related kidney transplant: about 75% survival at 10 years — better than deceased donor (~55–60%).$x$, null),
(80, $x$The following are causes of Normal sized or enlarged Kidneys in CKD Except$x$, $x$Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis
In most CKD the kidneys SHRINK (become small and scarred) as nephrons are lost. However, some causes keep the kidneys NORMAL-SIZED or ENLARGED even in advanced disease — memory aid: "DIABETIC PAH": Diabetic nephropathy, HIV-associated nephropathy (HIVAN), AMYLOIDOSIS (protein deposits), ADPKD (adult polycystic kidney disease — big cyst-filled kidneys), plus myeloma kidney, and sickle-cell nephropathy. HYPERTENSIVE NEPHROSCLEROSIS causes SMALL, shrunken kidneys, because long-standing high BP damages small arteries and causes scarring of the glomeruli. On ultrasound, small kidneys (under about 9 cm) usually mean chronic, irreversible disease.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• HIVAN — HIVAN typically causes LARGE, echogenic kidneys — it does belong to the normal/enlarged group.
• ADPKD — ADPKD causes very LARGE kidneys full of cysts.
• Amyloidosis — AMYLOIDOSIS causes normal or LARGE kidneys due to amyloid deposits.

⭐ Key point: CKD with normal/large kidneys = diabetes, HIVAN, amyloid, ADPKD, myeloma. HYPERTENSIVE nephrosclerosis = SMALL kidneys.$x$, null),
(81, $x$Post-nephrectomy donor investigations typically include:$x$, $x$Chest X-ray, FBC, Electrolytes/Urea/Creatinine, eGFR$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Chest X-ray, FBC, Electrolytes/Urea/Creatinine, eGFR
A live kidney donor must be fully assessed so that the operation is safe and the remaining single kidney will work well. Standard investigations (pre- and peri-operative) include: CHEST X-RAY (heart and lungs before anaesthesia), FULL BLOOD COUNT (anaemia, infection), ELECTROLYTES, UREA and CREATININE, and eGFR (kidney function — to make sure the remaining kidney is adequate), plus blood group and cross-match, urinalysis, viral screening (HIV, hepatitis B/C), ECG, and CT angiography of the kidneys before surgery. After nephrectomy, creatinine and eGFR are monitored closely because the remaining kidney must take over the full workload.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only a urine dipstick — A urine dipstick ALONE is far too little to assess kidney function and fitness for surgery.
• No pre-operative investigations are needed — Saying "no investigations are needed" is unsafe; major surgery always needs assessment.
• Bone marrow biopsy — BONE MARROW BIOPSY is for blood disorders (e.g. leukaemia), not kidney donation.

⭐ Key point: Kidney donor work-up = CXR, FBC, U&E/creatinine, eGFR, blood group, viral screen, ECG — check the remaining kidney is adequate.$x$, null),
(82, $x$Which of the following is the most common intrinsic (intrarenal) cause of acute kidney injury in critically ill patients?$x$, $x$Acute tubular necrosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Acute tubular necrosis
AKI causes are grouped as: PRE-RENAL (low blood flow to kidneys — dehydration, haemorrhage, shock, heart failure), INTRINSIC/intrarenal (damage inside the kidney), and POST-RENAL (blockage of urine flow). In critically ill patients, the most common INTRINSIC cause is ACUTE TUBULAR NECROSIS (ATN). ATN happens when kidney tubule cells die from prolonged ISCHAEMIA (e.g. untreated pre-renal shock/sepsis) or TOXINS (aminoglycosides, IV contrast, rhabdomyolysis/myoglobin, NSAIDs). Signs: oliguria, rising creatinine, muddy brown granular casts in urine, urine sodium over 40 mmol/L. Management: treat the cause, optimise fluids and BP, avoid nephrotoxins, adjust drug doses, dialysis if needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bladder outlet obstruction — BLADDER OUTLET OBSTRUCTION (e.g. enlarged prostate) is a POST-RENAL cause.
• Renal artery stenosis — RENAL ARTERY STENOSIS reduces blood flow to the kidney — usually considered PRE-RENAL/vascular, and usually chronic.
• Nephrolithiasis — NEPHROLITHIASIS (kidney stones) causes POST-RENAL obstruction.

⭐ Key point: Commonest intrinsic AKI in ICU = ACUTE TUBULAR NECROSIS (from ischaemia/sepsis or toxins). Muddy brown casts.$x$, null),
(83, $x$What is the first step in the pharmacologic management approach to ICU delirium?$x$, $x$Identify and treat underlying reversible causes (pain, hypoxia, infection, metabolic derangement) before adding antipsychotics$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Identify and treat underlying reversible causes (pain, hypoxia, infection, metabolic derangement) before adding antipsychotics
DELIRIUM is an acute change in attention and thinking, with a fluctuating course. It is almost always caused by something — so the FIRST step is to look for and TREAT REVERSIBLE CAUSES. Memory aid "I WATCH DEATH" or "PINCH ME": Pain, Infection (sepsis, UTI, pneumonia), Nutrition/dehydration, Constipation, HYPOXIA, Medications (benzodiazepines, anticholinergics, opioids), Environment, Electrolyte/metabolic disturbances (sodium, glucose, calcium), urinary retention, withdrawal (alcohol). Also use non-drug measures: reorientation, clocks and calendars, glasses and hearing aids, family visits, sleep at night/light in day, early mobilisation (ABCDEF bundle). ANTIPSYCHOTICS (e.g. haloperidol, quetiapine) are only for severe agitation that puts the patient or staff at risk, at the lowest dose for the shortest time.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediately start high-dose benzodiazepines — High-dose BENZODIAZEPINES can CAUSE or WORSEN delirium (except in alcohol/benzodiazepine withdrawal).
• Start antipsychotics for all patients regardless of cause — Antipsychotics for ALL patients regardless of cause is wrong; they don't fix the cause and have side effects (QT prolongation, sedation).
• Sedate to RASS -5 to prevent movement — Deep sedation to RASS −5 increases delirium, ventilator days and mortality; the target is light sedation (RASS 0 to −2).

⭐ Key point: ICU delirium → FIRST find and treat the cause (pain, hypoxia, infection, metabolic, drugs). Antipsychotics only for severe agitation.$x$, null),
(84, $x$Status epilepticus is defined as a seizure lasting longer than:$x$, $x$5 minutes$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 5 minutes
STATUS EPILEPTICUS (SE) is now defined as a seizure lasting MORE THAN 5 MINUTES, or two or more seizures without full recovery of consciousness in between. Older textbooks said 30 minutes, but the definition was shortened because most seizures stop within 2–3 minutes; after 5 minutes they are unlikely to stop on their own, and after about 30 minutes there is risk of permanent brain damage. Treatment timeline: 0–5 min — ABC, oxygen, check glucose, IV access; 5–20 min — BENZODIAZEPINE (IV lorazepam 4 mg, or buccal/IV midazolam, or diazepam), may repeat once; 20–40 min — second-line drug (levetiracetam, phenytoin/fosphenytoin, valproate); over 40 min (refractory) — anaesthetic agents and intubation in ICU.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 30 seconds — 30 seconds is an ordinary short seizure.
• 2 minutes — 2 minutes is still within the usual length of a self-limiting seizure.
• 15 minutes — 15 minutes is longer than the current definition; waiting this long delays treatment.

⭐ Key point: Status epilepticus = seizure > 5 MINUTES (or repeated without recovery). First-line: IV lorazepam/midazolam.$x$, null),
(85, $x$A patient with a Glasgow Coma Scale eye-opening score of 2 demonstrates:$x$, $x$Eye opening to pain only$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Eye opening to pain only
The GLASGOW COMA SCALE (GCS) has three parts, total 3–15. EYE OPENING (E, 1–4): 4 = spontaneous; 3 = to voice/speech; 2 = to PAIN (pressure) only; 1 = none. VERBAL (V, 1–5): 5 oriented, 4 confused, 3 inappropriate words, 2 incomprehensible sounds, 1 none. MOTOR (M, 1–6): 6 obeys commands, 5 localises pain, 4 withdraws (normal flexion), 3 abnormal flexion (decorticate), 2 extension (decerebrate), 1 none. So E2 means EYES OPEN ONLY TO A PAINFUL STIMULUS (e.g. trapezius squeeze, supraorbital pressure, nail-bed pressure).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Spontaneous eye opening — SPONTANEOUS eye opening = E4.
• Eye opening to voice — Eye opening to VOICE = E3.
• No eye opening at all — NO eye opening even to pain = E1.

⭐ Key point: GCS eyes: 4 spontaneous, 3 voice, 2 PAIN, 1 none. Verbal max 5; motor max 6; total 3–15.$x$, null),
(86, $x$The coup-contrecoup mechanism of brain injury describes which of the following?$x$, $x$Injury at the site of impact and at the opposite pole of the brain from movement within the skull$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Injury at the site of impact and at the opposite pole of the brain from movement within the skull
In a COUP-CONTRECOUP injury, the head is hit (or suddenly stops, e.g. in a car crash). The brain hits the inside of the skull at the point of impact (COUP injury), then rebounds and hits the OPPOSITE side of the skull (CONTRECOUP injury). So bruises (contusions) appear at BOTH the site of impact AND the opposite pole of the brain. Example: a fall onto the back of the head often causes contusions in the FRONTAL and TEMPORAL lobes at the front. Nursing: assess the whole brain function, not just the impact area; watch for delayed swelling and rising ICP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Injury only at the site of direct impact — Injury ONLY at the site of direct impact is a coup injury alone — it ignores the contrecoup part.
• Diffuse shearing injury without focal contusion — DIFFUSE SHEARING without focal contusion describes DIFFUSE AXONAL INJURY (from rotational acceleration), not coup-contrecoup.
• A fracture line radiating from the impact point — A fracture line radiating from the impact point describes a LINEAR SKULL FRACTURE, not brain contusion.

⭐ Key point: Coup-contrecoup = injury at the IMPACT site AND the OPPOSITE side of the brain from rebound inside the skull.$x$, null),
(87, $x$Autonomic dysreflexia in a patient with a spinal cord injury above T6 is typically triggered by:$x$, $x$A noxious stimulus below the level of injury, such as a distended bladder$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A noxious stimulus below the level of injury, such as a distended bladder
AUTONOMIC DYSREFLEXIA (AD) happens in spinal cord injury at or ABOVE T6. A NOXIOUS (irritating) stimulus BELOW the injury level — most commonly a FULL BLADDER (blocked or kinked catheter), then a full bowel/constipation, pressure ulcer, tight clothes, ingrown toenail, sunburn — causes a massive sympathetic reflex below the injury. Blood vessels constrict, causing SEVERE HYPERTENSION (can be over 200 mmHg — risk of stroke, seizure, death). The brain tries to respond through the vagus (BRADYCARDIA) and vasodilation above the lesion (POUNDING HEADACHE, FLUSHING and SWEATING above the injury, nasal congestion), but the message cannot pass down the damaged cord. Action: SIT THE PATIENT UP (lower the legs), loosen tight clothing, find and remove the cause (check catheter, empty bladder, check bowel), monitor BP every 2–5 minutes, give antihypertensive (e.g. nifedipine, GTN) if BP stays high.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mild anxiety only — MILD ANXIETY alone does not trigger this dangerous reflex.
• Normal ambient room temperature — Normal room temperature is not a noxious stimulus.
• Eating a regular meal — Eating a normal meal is not a trigger (although a full bowel/constipation can be).

⭐ Key point: Autonomic dysreflexia (SCI ≥ T6) = noxious stimulus below injury, usually FULL BLADDER → severe hypertension. SIT UP, find the cause.$x$, null),
(88, $x$Hypothalamic damage may cause all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Facial paresis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Facial paresis
The HYPOTHALAMUS controls the AUTONOMIC nervous system and hormones. Damage (trauma, tumour, stroke, surgery) can cause: temperature problems (hyperthermia/hypothermia), SWEATING disorders, BLOOD PRESSURE swings, CARDIAC RHYTHM disturbances (tachycardia, arrhythmias — part of paroxysmal sympathetic hyperactivity after brain injury), diabetes insipidus or SIADH, appetite and sleep problems. FACIAL PARESIS (weakness of facial muscles) comes from damage to the FACIAL NERVE (cranial nerve VII) itself — e.g. Bell's palsy — or to the motor cortex/corticobulbar tract (stroke); it is not caused by hypothalamic damage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sweating disorders — SWEATING disorders CAN occur — the hypothalamus controls sweating for temperature regulation.
• Blood pressure disturbance — BLOOD PRESSURE disturbances CAN occur — the hypothalamus controls autonomic tone.
• Cardiac rhythm disturbance — CARDIAC RHYTHM disturbances CAN occur through autonomic dysregulation.

⭐ Key point: Hypothalamic damage → temperature, sweating, BP, heart rhythm, water balance problems. Facial paresis = facial nerve/cortex, not hypothalamus.$x$, null),
(89, $x$Bilaterally pinpoint pupils in an unresponsive patient may suggest:$x$, $x$Opioid toxicity or a pontine lesion$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Opioid toxicity or a pontine lesion
PINPOINT PUPILS (very small, under 2 mm) on both sides in an unconscious patient suggest two main causes: (1) OPIOID TOXICITY (morphine, heroin, fentanyl, tramadol) — the classic triad is pinpoint pupils, respiratory depression and reduced consciousness; give NALOXONE (0.4 mg IV, repeat as needed). Organophosphate poisoning also causes small pupils. (2) A PONTINE LESION (e.g. pontine haemorrhage or infarction) — this damages descending sympathetic fibres, so the pupils constrict; the patient is often deeply comatose with high temperature. Always check blood glucose and consider naloxone trial.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anticholinergic drug effect — ANTICHOLINERGIC drugs (atropine, tricyclic antidepressants, antihistamines) cause DILATED pupils ("blind as a bat, mad as a hatter, hot as a hare, dry as a bone").
• Normal resting pupil size in bright light only — Normal pupils in bright light are small but not pinpoint, and an UNRESPONSIVE patient with pinpoint pupils is not "normal".
• Complete brain death in all cases — BRAIN DEATH usually shows FIXED, mid-sized or DILATED pupils, not pinpoint.

⭐ Key point: Bilateral PINPOINT pupils + coma = OPIOIDS (give naloxone) or PONTINE lesion. Dilated pupils = anticholinergic, brain death, herniation.$x$, null),
(90, $x$Which of the following is a preventable secondary brain injury factor that critical care nurses must aggressively manage after TBI?$x$, $x$Hypotension and hypoxia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hypotension and hypoxia
In TRAUMATIC BRAIN INJURY (TBI), PRIMARY injury happens at the moment of impact and cannot be undone. SECONDARY injury develops over hours to days and can be PREVENTED — this is the main focus of critical care. The most damaging secondary factors are HYPOTENSION (systolic below 90–110 mmHg) and HYPOXIA (SpO2 below 90% or PaO2 below 60 mmHg): even one episode greatly increases death and disability, because the injured brain cannot tolerate low oxygen delivery. Other secondary factors: high or low CO2, fever, seizures, hypo/hyperglycaemia, low sodium, raised ICP, anaemia. Nursing aims: SpO2 ≥ 94%, systolic BP ≥ 100–110 mmHg, CPP 60–70 mmHg, normocapnia (PaCO2 35–45), normothermia, glucose control.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The initial mechanical impact — The INITIAL MECHANICAL IMPACT is the PRIMARY injury — it has already happened and cannot be prevented by nurses.
• Skull fracture location — SKULL FRACTURE LOCATION is fixed at the time of injury; it guides assessment but is not modifiable.
• Scalp laceration — A SCALP LACERATION needs care (it can bleed heavily) but is not a secondary brain injury factor.

⭐ Key point: Prevent SECONDARY brain injury: avoid HYPOTENSION and HYPOXIA (also high/low CO2, fever, seizures, glucose problems).$x$, null),
(91, $x$The pupillary light reflex tests cranial nerves:$x$, $x$II and III$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: II and III
The PUPILLARY LIGHT REFLEX has two nerves: the AFFERENT (sensory) limb is the OPTIC NERVE (CN II), which carries the light signal from the retina to the brainstem; the EFFERENT (motor) limb is the OCULOMOTOR NERVE (CN III), whose parasympathetic fibres make the pupil CONSTRICT. Shining light in one eye normally makes BOTH pupils constrict (direct and consensual response). A FIXED, DILATED pupil on one side in a head-injured patient often means CN III is being compressed by uncal HERNIATION — a neurosurgical emergency.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• I and II — CN I (olfactory) is smell and CN II is vision; CN I is not part of the light reflex.
• IV and V — CN IV (trochlear) moves the eye down and in; CN V (trigeminal) is facial sensation and chewing — the corneal reflex uses V, not the light reflex.
• IV and VI — CN IV and CN VI (abducens) control eye movements, not pupil size.

⭐ Key point: Pupillary light reflex: CN II in (sensory), CN III out (motor). Unilateral fixed dilated pupil = CN III compression (herniation).$x$, null),
(92, $x$Your patient's eyes do not open to noxious stimuli and they respond incorrectly when asked questions and localize to pain. What is their GCS?$x$, $x$9$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 9
Score each part of the GCS: EYES — do not open even to noxious (painful) stimulus = E1. VERBAL — "responds INCORRECTLY when asked questions" is scored by this exam key as INAPPROPRIATE WORDS = V3 (words that do not answer the question). MOTOR — LOCALISES to pain (moves a hand towards the painful stimulus to remove it) = M5. Total: E1 + V3 + M5 = 9. Exam tip: if the stem had said "confused but answers in sentences", that would be V4 (total 10) — so read the verbal description carefully. Remember GCS ≤ 8 = coma/"intubate", 9–12 moderate, 13–15 mild brain injury. Always document each component (E1 V3 M5), not just the total.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 7 — 7 would need lower scores (e.g. E1 V2 M4) — it underestimates this patient.
• 8 — 8 would be E1 V2 M5 or E1 V3 M4 — it does not match "localises to pain" with words.
• 10 — 10 would only be correct if the verbal response were scored as "confused" (V4); the key here takes it as inappropriate words (V3).

⭐ Key point: GCS = E(1–4) + V(1–5) + M(1–6). Here E1 + V3 + M5 = 9. Always report each part.$x$, null),
(93, $x$What degree of head-of-bed elevation is recommended to reduce intracranial pressure while maintaining cerebral perfusion?$x$, $x$30 degrees of elevation with neutral head and neck alignment$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 30 degrees of elevation with neutral head and neck alignment
To lower INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE (ICP) while keeping CEREBRAL PERFUSION, raise the HEAD OF BED to about 30 DEGREES and keep the HEAD and NECK in a NEUTRAL (midline) position. This helps venous blood drain from the brain through the jugular veins, lowering ICP, without dropping BP too much (CPP = MAP − ICP; target CPP 60–70 mmHg). Avoid neck flexion or rotation, tight ETT ties or cervical collars that compress the jugular veins. Other measures: avoid hip flexion over 90°, keep the patient calm and pain-free, cluster nursing care, avoid coughing/straining, normothermia, normal CO2.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Flat at 0 degrees — FLAT at 0° reduces venous drainage and raises ICP.
• 15 degrees of elevation — 15° is less effective for venous drainage than 30°.
• 90 degrees, fully upright — 90° fully upright can drop MAP and cerebral perfusion pressure, and is uncomfortable/unsafe in unstable patients.

⭐ Key point: Raised ICP → head of bed 30° + NEUTRAL head/neck alignment (no jugular compression).$x$, null),
(94, $x$The nurse is preparing to suction the airway of an unconscious patient. The nurse should be aware that excessive suctioning can lead to:$x$, $x$Hypoxemia and cardiac arrhythmias$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hypoxemia and cardiac arrhythmias
SUCTIONING removes secretions, but it also removes OXYGEN from the airway and lungs, and the catheter stimulates the vagus nerve. Excessive or prolonged suctioning can therefore cause HYPOXAEMIA (low blood oxygen) and CARDIAC ARRHYTHMIAS (bradycardia from vagal stimulation, or tachyarrhythmias from hypoxia). Other risks: atelectasis (lung collapse), mucosal trauma and bleeding, infection, bronchospasm and raised ICP. Safe practice: suction ONLY when needed (secretions heard/seen, falling SpO2, high airway pressures), PRE-OXYGENATE with 100% O2 for 30–60 seconds, use the right catheter size (no more than half the ETT diameter), apply suction for no more than 10–15 SECONDS, use pressure 80–150 mmHg (adults), monitor SpO2 and ECG.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypertension and tachycardia — HYPERTENSION and TACHYCARDIA can occur briefly but are not the classic main danger.
• Hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis — Suctioning does NOT cause hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis; it takes away ventilation.
• Increased intracranial pressure — Suctioning can raise ICP temporarily, but the most important general risks are hypoxaemia and arrhythmias.

⭐ Key point: Excessive suctioning → HYPOXAEMIA + ARRHYTHMIAS (bradycardia). Pre-oxygenate; max 10–15 s per pass.$x$, null),
(95, $x$Which of the following findings is NOT compatible with a diagnosis of brain death?$x$, $x$Spontaneous respiratory effort during apnea testing$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Spontaneous respiratory effort during apnea testing
BRAIN DEATH (brainstem death) means permanent loss of all brain and brainstem function. Criteria include: known cause and exclusion of reversible factors (sedatives, hypothermia, metabolic problems); deep coma with no response; ABSENT brainstem reflexes — FIXED pupils (unreactive to light), ABSENT CORNEAL reflex, absent oculocephalic/oculovestibular reflexes, ABSENT GAG and cough reflexes, no motor response in the cranial nerve area; and a positive APNOEA TEST — no breathing effort when PaCO2 is allowed to rise above 60 mmHg (or 6.65 kPa per UK criteria) and pH is below 7.30. Any SPONTANEOUS BREATHING EFFORT during apnoea testing means the brainstem respiratory centre is still working, so brain death CANNOT be diagnosed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fixed and dilated pupils — FIXED DILATED pupils ARE consistent with brain death.
• Absent corneal reflex — ABSENT CORNEAL reflex IS consistent with brain death.
• Absent gag reflex — ABSENT GAG reflex IS consistent with brain death.

⭐ Key point: Any SPONTANEOUS BREATHING during the apnoea test rules out brain death.$x$, null),
(96, $x$When does primary brain injury occur?$x$, $x$At the moment of impact$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: At the moment of impact
PRIMARY BRAIN INJURY is the damage caused at the MOMENT OF IMPACT — the direct mechanical force on the brain tissue: contusions, lacerations, skull fractures, diffuse axonal injury, tearing of blood vessels. It happens in milliseconds and cannot be reversed by treatment; only prevention (helmets, seatbelts, road safety) helps. SECONDARY BRAIN INJURY develops minutes, hours or days later from hypoxia, hypotension, swelling, raised ICP, seizures, fever, infection and metabolic problems — this is what medical and nursing care tries to prevent.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• In the first hour after injury — "In the first hour" describes the time when SECONDARY injury may begin, not primary injury.
• In the first 4 hours after injury — "In the first 4 hours" is also the secondary-injury window, not the primary event.
• Only if the patient is in coma — Primary injury happens in every brain injury at impact, whether or not the patient is in coma.

⭐ Key point: PRIMARY brain injury = at the MOMENT OF IMPACT (cannot be undone). SECONDARY = later, preventable.$x$, null),
(97, $x$Which type of ICU delirium is most commonly MISSED by staff?$x$, $x$Hypoactive$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hypoactive
Delirium has three types. HYPERACTIVE — agitated, restless, pulling at lines, aggressive; easy to notice. HYPOACTIVE — quiet, withdrawn, sleepy, slow, staring, little movement or speech; the patient seems "calm" or "just tired" or depressed, so it is often MISSED by staff, even though it is the MOST COMMON type in ICU and older patients and has the WORST outcome (higher mortality, longer stay). MIXED — swings between the two. That is why every ICU patient should be screened regularly (e.g. every shift) with a tool like the CAM-ICU or ICDSC, not just those who are agitated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperactive — HYPERACTIVE delirium is the most noticeable type because of agitation, so it is rarely missed.
• Mixed — MIXED delirium is usually noticed during the hyperactive phases.
• All types are equally easy to spot — Not all types are equally easy to spot — hypoactive is clearly the hardest.

⭐ Key point: HYPOACTIVE delirium (quiet, sleepy) is the most commonly MISSED. Screen all patients with CAM-ICU.$x$, null),
(98, $x$A patient with hyperactive delirium is prescribed quetiapine. The nurse monitors for:$x$, $x$A and C$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A and C
QUETIAPINE is an atypical (second-generation) ANTIPSYCHOTIC sometimes used for agitated ICU delirium. Key monitoring: (1) QT PROLONGATION — antipsychotics can lengthen the QT interval, risking torsades de pointes (a dangerous ventricular arrhythmia); check a baseline ECG and repeat it, keep potassium and magnesium normal, avoid other QT-prolonging drugs (e.g. haloperidol, macrolides, ondansetron). (2) HYPERGLYCAEMIA and metabolic effects — atypical antipsychotics can raise blood sugar and lipids; monitor glucose. Other side effects: sedation, ORTHOSTATIC HYPOTENSION (low BP, not high), anticholinergic effects, and rarely neuroleptic malignant syndrome.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• QT prolongation — QT prolongation alone is correct but incomplete, since hyperglycaemia must also be monitored.
• Hypertension — HYPERTENSION is not a typical effect; quetiapine more often causes HYPOTENSION (postural drop) from alpha-blockade.
• Hyperglycemia — Hyperglycaemia alone is correct but incomplete, since QT prolongation is also a key risk.

⭐ Key point: Quetiapine/antipsychotics: monitor QT (ECG, K⁺, Mg²⁺) AND blood GLUCOSE. They cause low BP, not high.$x$, null),
(99, $x$Emergence delirium following general anaesthesia is characterised by:$x$, $x$Agitation, confusion, and thrashing shortly after waking$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Agitation, confusion, and thrashing shortly after waking
EMERGENCE DELIRIUM (emergence agitation) happens in the first minutes as a patient WAKES from general anaesthesia: they are AGITATED, CONFUSED, crying, restless, THRASHING about, may pull at drains, lines and the airway, and do not recognise surroundings. It usually settles within 10–30 minutes. It is more common in children, young adults, after volatile agents (sevoflurane), with pain, full bladder, hypoxia, and in patients with PTSD or anxiety. Nursing: protect the patient from injury and falls (side rails, stay close), check and treat causes (pain, HYPOXIA, hypercapnia, hypoglycaemia, full bladder), calm reassurance, and small doses of sedatives/opioids as prescribed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Calm, oriented behaviour — Calm, oriented behaviour is NORMAL recovery, not emergence delirium.
• Prolonged unconsciousness — Prolonged unconsciousness is DELAYED EMERGENCE, a different problem.
• Normal cognitive function throughout — Normal cognitive function throughout means no delirium is present.

⭐ Key point: Emergence delirium = agitation, confusion and thrashing right after waking from anaesthesia. Protect from injury; check for hypoxia, pain, full bladder.$x$, null),
(100, $x$A patient recovering from surgery develops confusion, agitation, and disorientation on post-operative day 2, with no prior history of cognitive impairment. This is most consistent with:$x$, $x$Postoperative delirium$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Postoperative delirium
POSTOPERATIVE DELIRIUM is an ACUTE, FLUCTUATING disturbance of attention, awareness and thinking that starts after surgery — typically on POST-OP DAYS 1–3 — in a patient who was previously cognitively normal. Signs: confusion, disorientation, agitation (or quietness), poor attention, sleep–wake disturbance, sometimes hallucinations. Risk factors: older age, major surgery, pain, opioids/benzodiazepines, infection, dehydration, hypoxia, electrolyte imbalance, urinary retention, sleep deprivation, alcohol withdrawal. It is usually reversible when the cause is treated, but it increases complications and length of stay. Screen with CAM, then look for and treat the cause.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Baseline dementia — BASELINE DEMENTIA is a long-standing, slowly progressive problem; this patient had NO previous cognitive impairment.
• Normal anaesthesia recovery expected for weeks — Normal anaesthesia recovery takes minutes to hours, not confusion lasting for weeks; day-2 confusion is NOT normal.
• A permanent neurological condition — A permanent neurological condition is unlikely; delirium is acute and usually reversible (though a stroke must be excluded if there are focal signs).

⭐ Key point: New confusion/agitation on post-op day 1–3 in a previously normal patient = POSTOPERATIVE DELIRIUM. Find and treat the cause.$x$, null),
(101, $x$The definitive drug treatment for malignant hyperthermia is:$x$, $x$Dantrolene$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Dantrolene
MALIGNANT HYPERTHERMIA (MH) is a rare inherited muscle disorder triggered by volatile anaesthetic gases (sevoflurane, desflurane, isoflurane) and SUCCINYLCHOLINE. Uncontrolled calcium release in muscle causes: rising END-TIDAL CO2 (often the earliest sign), tachycardia, muscle rigidity (masseter spasm), rapidly RISING TEMPERATURE, acidosis, HYPERKALAEMIA, rhabdomyolysis and arrhythmias. The definitive drug is DANTROLENE (2.5 mg/kg IV, repeated until signs settle, up to about 10 mg/kg). It stops calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, relaxing muscles and stopping heat production. Also: stop triggering agents, hyperventilate with 100% oxygen, active cooling, treat hyperkalaemia and arrhythmias, monitor urine output (myoglobin).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Naloxone — NALOXONE reverses opioids, not MH.
• Flumazenil — FLUMAZENIL reverses benzodiazepines, not MH.
• Neostigmine — NEOSTIGMINE reverses non-depolarising muscle relaxants (e.g. rocuronium), not MH.

⭐ Key point: Malignant hyperthermia → DANTROLENE 2.5 mg/kg IV. Triggers: volatile gases + succinylcholine. Earliest sign: rising EtCO2.$x$, null),
(102, $x$Which nutritional consideration is important for a mechanically ventilated patient?$x$, $x$Early enteral nutrition is preferred over parenteral nutrition when the gut is functional, to reduce infection risk$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Early enteral nutrition is preferred over parenteral nutrition when the gut is functional, to reduce infection risk
Critically ill ventilated patients are catabolic (they break down muscle), so nutrition is important. Guidelines (ASPEN/ESPEN) recommend EARLY ENTERAL NUTRITION (tube feeding into the gut, by NG/OG tube) WITHIN 24–48 HOURS of ICU admission if the gut works and the patient is haemodynamically stable. Enteral feeding keeps the gut lining healthy, supports gut immunity, reduces bacterial translocation and INFECTION, and is cheaper and safer than parenteral nutrition (PN). PN (IV feeding) is used only when enteral feeding is impossible or inadequate. Nursing: check tube position (X-ray/aspirate pH), keep head of bed 30–45° to prevent aspiration and VAP, monitor tolerance (vomiting, distension), glucose and refeeding syndrome (low phosphate, potassium, magnesium).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Total parenteral nutrition should always be first-line — TPN is NOT first-line; it carries more risk of line infection, hyperglycaemia and liver problems.
• Feeding should be withheld entirely until extubation — WITHHOLDING food until extubation leads to malnutrition, muscle loss and poor healing.
• Only IV dextrose is appropriate — IV DEXTROSE alone gives some calories but no protein, fat, vitamins or minerals — it is not nutrition.

⭐ Key point: Ventilated patient with a working gut → EARLY ENTERAL feeding (24–48 h). "If the gut works, use it."$x$, null),
(103, $x$Pulse oximetry (SpO2) shows a clinically significant drop only after PaO2 falls below approximately:$x$, $x$60 mmHg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 60 mmHg
This is explained by the OXYGEN–HAEMOGLOBIN DISSOCIATION CURVE, which is S-shaped. At the top the curve is FLAT: PaO2 can fall from 100 to about 60 mmHg while SpO2 only falls from about 98–99% to about 90%. Below a PaO2 of about 60 mmHg (8 kPa) the curve becomes STEEP, so a small further fall in PaO2 causes a LARGE fall in SpO2. So pulse oximetry shows a clinically significant drop only after PaO2 falls below about 60 mmHg — which is also the definition of respiratory failure (Type 1). Useful pairs: PaO2 60 ≈ SpO2 90%; PaO2 40 ≈ SpO2 75% (mixed venous); PaO2 27 ≈ SpO2 50% (P50). This means a normal SpO2 can hide a falling PaO2, and also that SpO2 tells nothing about CO2 — check ABG.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 100 mmHg — 100 mmHg is a normal PaO2 with SpO2 about 98%.
• 80 mmHg — 80 mmHg still gives SpO2 about 95% — no significant drop.
• 40 mmHg — At 40 mmHg the SpO2 is already very low (about 75%); the significant drop begins earlier, at about 60.

⭐ Key point: SpO2 falls steeply once PaO2 < 60 mmHg (PaO2 60 ≈ SpO2 90%). SpO2 says nothing about CO2.$x$, null),
(104, $x$Why is humidification important during prolonged or high-flow oxygen therapy?$x$, $x$Dry medical oxygen can irritate and dry the airway mucosa, impairing mucociliary clearance and causing discomfort$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Dry medical oxygen can irritate and dry the airway mucosa, impairing mucociliary clearance and causing discomfort
Medical oxygen from cylinders and wall supply is completely DRY. Normally the nose and upper airway warm and humidify air. With HIGH-FLOW or PROLONGED oxygen (or when the nose is bypassed by an ETT or tracheostomy), dry gas DRIES the mucosa of the nose, mouth and airways. This causes discomfort, sore throat, nosebleeds, thick sticky secretions and damage to the CILIA, so MUCOCILIARY CLEARANCE is impaired — secretions build up and can block airways and tubes, raising the risk of atelectasis and infection. Humidification (bubble humidifier, heated humidifier, HME filter) prevents this. Usually needed for flow above about 4 L/min, high-flow nasal cannula, and all artificial airways.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Humidification increases the FiO2 delivered to the patient — Humidification does NOT increase FiO2; it adds water vapour, not oxygen.
• Humidification eliminates the need for regular oxygen saturation monitoring — It does NOT replace SpO2 monitoring; oxygen saturation must still be checked.
• Humidification prevents oxygen toxicity entirely — It does NOT prevent oxygen toxicity; that depends on high FiO2 for long periods. Only limiting FiO2 prevents toxicity.

⭐ Key point: Humidify high-flow/prolonged oxygen — dry gas damages mucosa and mucociliary clearance and thickens secretions.$x$, null),
(105, $x$How often should rescuers performing chest compressions switch during ongoing CPR?$x$, $x$About every 2 minutes, to prevent fatigue and maintain compression quality$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: About every 2 minutes, to prevent fatigue and maintain compression quality
Good-quality chest compressions need: rate 100–120/min, depth 5–6 cm in adults, full chest recoil, minimal interruptions. Compressions are tiring, and quality (especially depth) falls after about 1–2 minutes, often without the rescuer noticing. So guidelines (AHA/ERC) recommend SWITCHING THE COMPRESSOR ABOUT EVERY 2 MINUTES — usually at the rhythm check — with the change taking less than 5–10 seconds. Have the next compressor ready and standing by.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Every 15 minutes — Every 15 minutes is far too long; fatigue would reduce compression quality long before then.
• Every 30 seconds — Every 30 seconds is too frequent; constant switching causes too many interruptions.
• Rescuers should never switch during an arrest — Never switching is wrong; it leads to tired, shallow compressions and lower survival.

⭐ Key point: Switch compressors about EVERY 2 MINUTES (at rhythm checks) with minimal pause, to prevent fatigue.$x$, null),
(106, $x$Indications for intubation in the critical care unit include all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Mild, well-controlled hypertension$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Mild, well-controlled hypertension
Common INDICATIONS for INTUBATION in ICU: (1) AIRWAY PROTECTION — reduced consciousness (GCS ≤ 8), loss of gag/cough reflex, risk of aspiration; (2) RESPIRATORY FAILURE needing mechanical ventilation — hypoxaemia not responding to oxygen/NIV, rising PaCO2 with acidosis, exhaustion; (3) ANTICIPATED AIRWAY COMPROMISE — airway burns, inhalation injury, angioedema, anaphylaxis, neck haematoma, epiglottitis; (4) to allow procedures, deep sedation or controlled ventilation (e.g. raised ICP, severe shock). Mild, well-controlled HYPERTENSION has nothing to do with the airway or breathing and is never an indication for intubation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Airway protection in a patient with reduced consciousness — Airway PROTECTION in reduced consciousness IS an indication.
• Respiratory failure requiring mechanical ventilation — RESPIRATORY FAILURE needing ventilation IS an indication.
• Anticipated airway compromise (e.g. severe burns/oedema) — Anticipated airway compromise (BURNS/OEDEMA) IS an indication — intubate early before swelling makes it impossible.

⭐ Key point: Intubate for airway protection, respiratory failure or threatened airway — NOT for controlled hypertension.$x$, null),
(107, $x$Intrinsic PEEP (PEEPi/auto-PEEP) develops in life-threatening asthma because:$x$, $x$Air cannot fully leave the lungs before the next breath$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Air cannot fully leave the lungs before the next breath
In severe (life-threatening) ASTHMA, the airways are narrowed by bronchospasm, swelling and mucus plugs. Breathing OUT is slow because narrow airways close early during expiration. If the next breath starts before the lungs have fully emptied, air is TRAPPED and builds up with each breath — this is DYNAMIC HYPERINFLATION, and the pressure left in the alveoli at the end of expiration is INTRINSIC PEEP (auto-PEEP). It raises intrathoracic pressure, reduces venous return (HYPOTENSION, even cardiac arrest — PEA), increases work of breathing, and causes BAROTRAUMA/pneumothorax. Management on the ventilator: LOW respiratory rate, LONG expiratory time (I:E 1:4 or 1:5), smaller tidal volumes, treat bronchospasm; if the patient suddenly becomes hypotensive, briefly DISCONNECT the ventilator to let trapped air out.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The ventilator is set incorrectly in all cases — Ventilator setting can make auto-PEEP worse, but it is caused mainly by the disease (airflow obstruction), not "incorrect settings in all cases".
• The patient is over-oxygenated — Over-oxygenation does not cause air trapping.
• It only occurs in healthy lungs — Auto-PEEP happens in OBSTRUCTED lungs (asthma, COPD), not in healthy lungs.

⭐ Key point: Auto-PEEP = air cannot fully leave the lungs before the next breath → air trapping. Treat with low rate, long expiration.$x$, null),
(108, $x$During Guedel's stages of anaesthesia, surgical procedures are typically performed during:$x$, $x$Stage III (surgical anaesthesia)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Stage III (surgical anaesthesia)
GUEDEL'S STAGES OF ANAESTHESIA (described with ether) are: STAGE I — ANALGESIA: from induction to loss of consciousness; patient is drowsy and pain is reduced. STAGE II — EXCITEMENT (delirium): after loss of consciousness; irregular breathing, struggling, vomiting, breath-holding — dangerous, so modern drugs aim to pass through it quickly. STAGE III — SURGICAL ANAESTHESIA: regular breathing, loss of reflexes, muscle relaxation, eyes central — SURGERY is performed here (it has 4 planes). STAGE IV — OVERDOSE (medullary paralysis): breathing stops, circulatory collapse — death unless reversed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stage I (analgesia) — STAGE I gives analgesia but the patient is still conscious and moving — not suitable for surgery.
• Stage II (excitement) — STAGE II is the excitement stage with uncontrolled movement and risk of vomiting/laryngospasm — never used for surgery.
• Stage IV (overdose) — STAGE IV is overdose with respiratory and circulatory failure — dangerous, not a working stage.

⭐ Key point: Guedel: I analgesia, II excitement, III SURGICAL anaesthesia (operate here), IV overdose.$x$, null),
(109, $x$A patient in the recovery room after general anaesthesia has delayed emergence (remains unrousable well beyond expected time). The nurse should consider all of the following causes EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Improved cerebral perfusion$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Improved cerebral perfusion
DELAYED EMERGENCE means the patient does not wake up within the expected time (often 30–60 minutes) after anaesthesia. Causes to check: (1) DRUGS — residual anaesthetic, OPIOIDS, BENZODIAZEPINES, muscle relaxants (check reversal); (2) METABOLIC — HYPOGLYCAEMIA, hyperglycaemia, hyponatraemia, uraemia, liver failure, hypothyroidism; (3) respiratory — HYPOXIA, high CO2 (CO2 narcosis); (4) HYPOTHERMIA; (5) NEUROLOGICAL — intraoperative STROKE, intracranial bleeding, seizures (non-convulsive), raised ICP. Nursing: ABC, check SpO2, check blood glucose, temperature, review drugs given, consider reversal agents, check pupils and limbs for focal signs, call the anaesthetist. IMPROVED cerebral perfusion would help the patient wake up; it is not a cause of delayed waking.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Residual anaesthetic/opioid/sedative effect — RESIDUAL anaesthetic/opioid/sedative IS the most common cause.
• Hypoglycaemia or metabolic derangement — HYPOGLYCAEMIA and other metabolic problems ARE important causes — always check glucose.
• Intraoperative stroke or other neurological event — STROKE or other neurological events ARE serious causes to consider.

⭐ Key point: Delayed emergence: think DRUGS, METABOLIC (glucose!), hypoxia/CO2, hypothermia, NEURO (stroke). Improved perfusion is not a cause.$x$, null),
(110, $x$SIMV, as a mode of mechanical ventilation, stands for and allows:$x$, $x$Synchronized Intermittent Mandatory Ventilation — combines mandatory breaths with the ability for the patient to breathe spontaneously between them$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Synchronized Intermittent Mandatory Ventilation — combines mandatory breaths with the ability for the patient to breathe spontaneously between them
SIMV = SYNCHRONISED INTERMITTENT MANDATORY VENTILATION. The ventilator gives a SET NUMBER of MANDATORY breaths per minute (set volume or pressure), timed (SYNCHRONISED) with the patient's own effort to avoid "breath stacking" and fighting the ventilator. BETWEEN the mandatory breaths, the patient can take their OWN SPONTANEOUS breaths, which can be helped by pressure support. It was used for weaning (gradually lowering the mandatory rate), keeping respiratory muscles active. Watch for increased work of breathing if the mandatory rate is set too low.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple Immediate Manual Ventilation — a purely manual bag-valve technique — "Simple Immediate Manual Ventilation" is not a real term; manual bag-valve ventilation is not SIMV.
• Static Inspiratory Muscle Ventilation — used only for muscle training — "Static Inspiratory Muscle Ventilation" is not a real ventilation mode.
• Single Inflation Manual Volume — a one-time inflation mode — "Single Inflation Manual Volume" is not a real ventilation mode.

⭐ Key point: SIMV = Synchronised Intermittent Mandatory Ventilation: set mandatory breaths + spontaneous breaths in between.$x$, null),
(111, $x$Anaesthetic death investigations typically consider all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$The patient's choice of hospital ward décor$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The patient's choice of hospital ward décor
When a patient dies under or after ANAESTHESIA, a structured investigation (audit, mortality review, confidential enquiry) looks at the whole process of care: PRE-ANAESTHETIC ASSESSMENT (history, airway, ASA grade, fasting, investigations, consent); choice and DOSE of DRUGS (allergies, overdose, wrong drug); MONITORING during the procedure (SpO2, capnography, ECG, BP, temperature) and how alarms were responded to; equipment checks; airway management; staffing, skill and supervision; post-operative recovery care; and documentation. The aim is to learn and prevent future deaths, not only to blame. The patient's choice of ward DÉCOR has no relationship to anaesthetic safety.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Adequacy of pre-anaesthetic assessment — PRE-ANAESTHETIC ASSESSMENT IS reviewed — poor assessment is a common cause of avoidable deaths.
• Appropriateness of drug selection and dosing — DRUG SELECTION and DOSING ARE reviewed — drug errors and reactions are key causes.
• Monitoring during the procedure — MONITORING during the procedure IS reviewed — missed hypoxia or oesophageal intubation can be fatal.

⭐ Key point: Anaesthetic death reviews look at assessment, drugs, monitoring, equipment, airway, staff and recovery — not ward décor.$x$, null),
(112, $x$What is the purpose of measuring plateau pressure via an inspiratory hold maneuver?$x$, $x$To estimate alveolar distending pressure and assess risk of barotrauma, independent of airway resistance$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: To estimate alveolar distending pressure and assess risk of barotrauma, independent of airway resistance
On a volume-controlled ventilator, the PEAK pressure is made of two parts: pressure to push gas through the AIRWAYS (resistance) and pressure to stretch the ALVEOLI (compliance). An INSPIRATORY HOLD (pause of about 0.5–1 second at the end of inspiration) stops gas flow, so the airway resistance part disappears; the pressure measured then is the PLATEAU PRESSURE (Pplat), which reflects the pressure inside the ALVEOLI (alveolar distending pressure). Keeping Pplat BELOW 30 cmH2O reduces the risk of BAROTRAUMA/volutrauma (ventilator-induced lung injury, pneumothorax) — a key part of lung-protective ventilation in ARDS (tidal volume 6 mL/kg ideal body weight). Driving pressure = Pplat − PEEP (keep < 15). High peak but normal plateau = airway problem (secretions, bronchospasm, kinked tube); both high = stiff lungs or pneumothorax.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• To measure airway resistance directly — AIRWAY RESISTANCE is estimated from (peak − plateau) ÷ flow, not by plateau alone.
• To calculate the respiratory rate — RESPIRATORY RATE is simply counted or set; it does not need an inspiratory hold.
• To determine FiO2 requirements — FiO2 is set from SpO2/PaO2 needs; plateau pressure does not determine it.

⭐ Key point: Plateau pressure (inspiratory hold) = alveolar pressure. Keep < 30 cmH2O to prevent barotrauma.$x$, null),
(113, $x$A normal I:E ratio for a patient without obstructive lung disease is approximately:$x$, $x$1:2$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 1:2
The I:E RATIO compares the time spent breathing IN (inspiration) with the time spent breathing OUT (expiration). In normal breathing, expiration is passive and takes longer, so a normal I:E ratio is about 1:2 (range 1:1.5 to 1:3). On the ventilator 1:2 is the usual starting setting. In OBSTRUCTIVE disease (asthma, COPD), a LONGER expiratory time (1:3 to 1:5) is used to let trapped air out and prevent auto-PEEP. INVERSE ratio ventilation (e.g. 2:1) is sometimes used in severe ARDS to improve oxygenation, but it is uncomfortable and needs heavy sedation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2:1 — 2:1 is INVERSE ratio — inspiration longer than expiration; not normal.
• 1:4 — 1:4 is the long expiratory time used in obstructive disease, longer than normal.
• 4:1 — 4:1 is extreme inverse ratio, not normal.

⭐ Key point: Normal I:E = 1:2. Obstructive disease: 1:3–1:5. Inverse ratio (2:1) only in severe ARDS.$x$, null),
(114, $x$Pendred syndrome is classically associated with goitre plus:$x$, $x$Congenital deafness$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Congenital deafness
PENDRED SYNDROME is an inherited (autosomal recessive) condition caused by a mutation in the SLC26A4 gene, which makes a protein called PENDRIN. Pendrin moves iodide in the THYROID and moves ions in the INNER EAR. So patients have two main features: (1) GOITRE (enlarged thyroid, often with mild hypothyroidism) from a defect in iodide organification; and (2) CONGENITAL SENSORINEURAL DEAFNESS (often with an enlarged vestibular aqueduct/cochlear malformation). It is one of the most common causes of syndromic hearing loss.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Congenital blindness — CONGENITAL BLINDNESS is not a feature of Pendred syndrome.
• Cardiac defects — CARDIAC DEFECTS are not part of Pendred syndrome.
• Renal failure — RENAL FAILURE is not part of Pendred syndrome.

⭐ Key point: Pendred syndrome = GOITRE + congenital sensorineural DEAFNESS (pendrin gene).$x$, null),
(115, $x$The most common type of thyroid carcinoma is:$x$, $x$Papillary$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Papillary
PAPILLARY CARCINOMA is the MOST COMMON thyroid cancer, making up about 75–85% of cases. It is more common in women aged 20–50 and after radiation exposure. It grows slowly, spreads mainly through LYMPHATICS to neck nodes, and has an EXCELLENT prognosis (over 90% 10-year survival). Under the microscope it shows "Orphan Annie eye" nuclei and PSAMMOMA bodies. Treatment: thyroidectomy, sometimes radioactive iodine, then levothyroxine. Other types: FOLLICULAR (10–15%, spreads through blood to bone and lungs), MEDULLARY (5%, from C-cells, makes calcitonin, linked with MEN 2), ANAPLASTIC (under 2%, elderly, very aggressive, poor prognosis).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Follicular — FOLLICULAR is the second most common (10–15%), spreads by blood.
• Medullary — MEDULLARY is uncommon (about 5%), from parafollicular C cells, calcitonin-secreting.
• Anaplastic — ANAPLASTIC is the rarest and most aggressive, not the most common.

⭐ Key point: Commonest thyroid cancer = PAPILLARY (~80%, lymph spread, good prognosis). Anaplastic = worst.$x$, null),
(116, $x$Encephalopathy toxins implicated in hepatic failure include all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Insulin$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Insulin
In HEPATIC ENCEPHALOPATHY, the failing liver cannot clear toxins coming from the gut, so they reach the brain. Main toxins: AMMONIA (made by gut bacteria from protein; the key toxin), FALSE NEUROTRANSMITTERS (e.g. octopamine, from altered amino acid balance), increased GABA-ergic tone (GABA and endogenous benzodiazepine-like substances), mercaptans, short-chain fatty acids and manganese. Signs: confusion, sleep reversal, asterixis (flapping tremor), fetor hepaticus, coma. Treatment: find triggers (GI bleed, infection, constipation, dehydration, sedatives, high protein), LACTULOSE (traps ammonia in the gut, aim 2–3 soft stools daily) and RIFAXIMIN. INSULIN is a normal hormone that controls glucose; it is not an encephalopathy toxin (liver failure actually causes HYPOGLYCAEMIA).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ammonia — AMMONIA IS the main toxin — lactulose is used to reduce it.
• False neurotransmitters — FALSE NEUROTRANSMITTERS ARE implicated.
• GABA — GABA IS implicated — increased inhibitory tone in the brain.

⭐ Key point: Hepatic encephalopathy toxins = AMMONIA, false neurotransmitters, GABA. Treat with LACTULOSE + rifaximin. Insulin is not a toxin.$x$, null),
(117, $x$Special areas of the body that require CCU-level attention in burns include all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Upper back$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Upper back
Burns to certain SPECIAL AREAS need specialist (burns unit/critical care) referral even if they are small, because they carry high risk of disability, infection or airway problems: the FACE (airway swelling, eyes, appearance), HANDS and FEET (function, contractures), PERINEUM and GENITALIA (infection, urinary function), and MAJOR JOINTS (contractures). Also circumferential burns of limbs or chest (compartment syndrome, restricted breathing), inhalation injury, electrical and chemical burns. The UPPER BACK has no special functional or airway importance — burns there are assessed by size and depth in the normal way.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Face — FACE burns ARE special — risk of airway oedema and eye injury.
• Hands and feet — HANDS and FEET burns ARE special — risk of loss of function and contractures.
• Perineum and genitalia — PERINEUM and GENITALIA burns ARE special — infection risk and difficult care.

⭐ Key point: Special burn areas: FACE, HANDS, FEET, PERINEUM/GENITALIA, MAJOR JOINTS (plus circumferential, inhalation, electrical, chemical).$x$, null),
(118, $x$Using the Parkland formula for a 70kg patient with 40% TBSA, total 24-hour fluid required is:$x$, $x$11,200ml$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 11,200ml
The PARKLAND FORMULA gives the total crystalloid (Ringer's lactate/Hartmann's) needed in the FIRST 24 HOURS after a burn: 4 mL × body weight (kg) × %TBSA burned. Here: 4 × 70 × 40 = 11,200 mL. Give HALF (5,600 mL) in the FIRST 8 HOURS FROM THE TIME OF BURN (not from arrival), and the other half (5,600 mL) over the next 16 hours. Titrate to URINE OUTPUT: 0.5 mL/kg/hour in adults (about 35 mL/h here), 1 mL/kg/h in children, 1–1.5 mL/kg/h in electrical burns. Superficial (first-degree) burns are not counted in TBSA.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2,800ml — 2,800 mL = 1 × 70 × 40 — this uses 1 mL instead of 4 mL; far too little.
• 5,600ml — 5,600 mL is only the amount for the FIRST 8 HOURS (half), not the 24-hour total.
• 16,800ml — 16,800 mL = 6 × 70 × 40 — too much, risking fluid overload ("fluid creep").

⭐ Key point: Parkland = 4 mL × kg × %TBSA in 24 h; half in first 8 h from time of burn. 70 kg, 40% → 11,200 mL. Target urine 0.5 mL/kg/h.$x$, null),
(119, $x$Antibiotic choice for treating the underlying Clostridium infection in tetanus typically includes:$x$, $x$Penicillin or metronidazole$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Penicillin or metronidazole
TETANUS is caused by the toxin (tetanospasmin) of CLOSTRIDIUM TETANI, an anaerobic, spore-forming bacterium found in soil and animal dung, entering through wounds. Treatment has several parts: (1) neutralise free toxin — HUMAN TETANUS IMMUNOGLOBULIN (HTIG); (2) kill the bacteria — ANTIBIOTICS: METRONIDAZOLE (preferred, 500 mg IV 6–8 hourly for 7–10 days) or PENICILLIN; (3) clean and debride the wound; (4) control spasms — benzodiazepines (diazepam), magnesium sulphate, muscle relaxants and ventilation if needed; quiet, dark room; (5) manage autonomic instability; (6) give tetanus toxoid vaccine (the disease does not give immunity).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Aspirin — ASPIRIN is an analgesic/antiplatelet; it does not kill Clostridium.
• Insulin — INSULIN controls blood glucose; it has no role against the infection.
• No antibiotics are used — Antibiotics ARE used — saying none are used is wrong.

⭐ Key point: Tetanus: METRONIDAZOLE (or penicillin) + tetanus immunoglobulin + wound debridement + spasm control + vaccine.$x$, null),
(120, $x$A patient with septic shock has a rising serum lactate despite fluid resuscitation. This finding is most concerning because it suggests:$x$, $x$Ongoing tissue hypoperfusion/anaerobic metabolism$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Ongoing tissue hypoperfusion/anaerobic metabolism
In SEPTIC SHOCK, LACTATE is produced when tissues do not receive enough oxygen and switch to ANAEROBIC metabolism (also from stress and reduced clearance). A lactate that is still RISING DESPITE FLUIDS means tissues are still HYPOPERFUSED — the shock is not controlled. This predicts higher mortality and calls for urgent re-assessment: more fluid only if fluid-responsive, start or increase VASOPRESSORS (noradrenaline, target MAP ≥ 65 mmHg), consider inotropes, check source control (drain abscess, remove infected line), and check antibiotics were given within 1 hour. Septic shock definition (Sepsis-3): needs vasopressors to keep MAP ≥ 65 AND lactate > 2 mmol/L despite adequate fluids.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Improved tissue perfusion — Improved perfusion would show a FALLING lactate, not rising.
• No clinical significance — A rising lactate is clinically very significant — a warning sign.
• Resolved sepsis — Sepsis is NOT resolved if lactate is rising; it is getting worse.

⭐ Key point: Rising lactate despite fluids = ONGOING tissue HYPOPERFUSION → escalate (vasopressors, source control).$x$, null),
(121, $x$Using the Rule of Nines to estimate burn size in an adult, each entire arm represents approximately:$x$, $x$9% of total body surface area$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 9% of total body surface area
The RULE OF NINES estimates the TOTAL BODY SURFACE AREA (TBSA) burned in ADULTS: HEAD and NECK 9%; EACH ARM 9% (4.5% front + 4.5% back); FRONT of the trunk 18%; BACK of the trunk 18%; EACH LEG 18% (9% front + 9% back); PERINEUM/genitalia 1%. Total = 100%. The patient's own PALM (including fingers) is about 1% TBSA — useful for small or patchy burns. In CHILDREN the head is relatively bigger and legs smaller, so the Lund and Browder chart is more accurate. Only partial- and full-thickness burns are counted.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 18% of total body surface area — 18% is EACH LEG, or the front or back of the trunk — not an arm.
• 1% of total body surface area — 1% is the PERINEUM or the patient's palm.
• 36% of total body surface area — 36% is the WHOLE trunk (front + back) or both legs — far too much for one arm.

⭐ Key point: Rule of Nines (adult): head 9, EACH ARM 9, each leg 18, front trunk 18, back trunk 18, perineum 1.$x$, null),
(122, $x$A patient with a central line in place for 10 days develops fever with no other obvious source. The nurse should suspect:$x$, $x$The central line as a possible source and should assess/report per protocol (e.g. CLABSI workup)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The central line as a possible source and should assess/report per protocol (e.g. CLABSI workup)
A CENTRAL LINE is a direct route for bacteria into the bloodstream. The risk of CENTRAL LINE-ASSOCIATED BLOODSTREAM INFECTION (CLABSI) rises the longer the line stays in. A patient with a line in place for 10 DAYS who develops FEVER with no other clear source must be suspected of CLABSI. Nursing actions: assess the insertion site (redness, pus, tenderness), check vital signs for sepsis, report to the doctor, take paired BLOOD CULTURES (one from the line and one peripheral) before antibiotics, and follow the protocol (the line may need removal and the tip sent for culture). Prevention bundle: hand hygiene, maximal barrier precautions at insertion, chlorhexidine skin prep, avoid femoral site, daily review of the need for the line, scrub-the-hub.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No relationship to the central line whatsoever — Saying there is "no relationship" ignores the most likely source of infection.
• A skin allergy to the dressing only — A dressing allergy causes local rash/itching, not fever without another source.
• Nothing requiring further action — "Nothing requiring action" is unsafe; CLABSI can progress to sepsis and death.

⭐ Key point: Fever in a patient with a central line = suspect CLABSI → assess site, blood cultures (line + peripheral), report.$x$, null),
(123, $x$A heart rate above what value is used as an ICU admission threshold?$x$, $x$>110bpm$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: >110bpm
ICU admission/referral criteria (and early warning scores such as NEWS) use physiological "trigger" values to identify patients who are deteriorating. A HEART RATE persistently above about 110–130 bpm (this question's key: > 110 bpm) together with other abnormal signs is a common threshold for urgent review and possible ICU admission. Other typical triggers: systolic BP below 90 mmHg, respiratory rate above 30 or below 8, SpO2 below 90% on oxygen, GCS falling by 2 or more, urine output below 0.5 mL/kg/h, lactate above 4 mmol/L. Tachycardia is often the earliest sign of shock, sepsis, bleeding or hypoxia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• >80bpm — > 80 bpm is within the NORMAL resting range (60–100).
• >90bpm — > 90 bpm is only mildly raised; it is a SIRS criterion but not an ICU admission threshold by itself.
• >150bpm — > 150 bpm is very high; waiting for this level would delay ICU referral.

⭐ Key point: HR > 110 bpm (with other abnormal signs) is a common ICU admission/referral trigger. Tachycardia is an early warning sign.$x$, null),
(124, $x$In multidisciplinary management of bladder trauma, which specialist is usually NOT involved?$x$, $x$Oncologist$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Oncologist
BLADDER TRAUMA usually happens with PELVIC FRACTURES (blunt trauma, e.g. road accidents) or penetrating injuries. Management is multidisciplinary: the UROLOGIST repairs the bladder (intraperitoneal rupture needs surgery; extraperitoneal is often managed with a catheter); the RADIOLOGIST performs and interprets CT cystography/retrograde cystogram to diagnose the injury; the ORTHOPAEDIC surgeon stabilises the PELVIC FRACTURE; plus trauma/general surgeons, ICU and nurses. An ONCOLOGIST treats CANCER and has no role in acute bladder trauma.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Orthopaedic surgeon — The ORTHOPAEDIC surgeon IS involved because most bladder injuries come with pelvic fractures.
• Radiologist — The RADIOLOGIST IS involved — imaging confirms the diagnosis and type of rupture.
• Urologist — The UROLOGIST IS the main specialist who repairs the bladder.

⭐ Key point: Bladder trauma team = urologist, radiologist, orthopaedic (pelvic fracture), trauma surgeon. Oncologist = cancer, not trauma.$x$, null),
(125, $x$The most common cause of lower GI bleeding is:$x$, $x$Diverticular disease$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Diverticular disease
LOWER GI BLEEDING is bleeding from below the ligament of Treitz (mostly the colon), usually seen as fresh red blood per rectum (haematochezia). In adults, the MOST COMMON cause is DIVERTICULAR DISEASE (about 30–40%) — small pouches in the colon wall, especially in older people, where a blood vessel stretched over the pouch can rupture and cause sudden, painless, large-volume bleeding that often stops on its own. Other causes: haemorrhoids, angiodysplasia, colorectal cancer/polyps, colitis (ischaemic, infective, inflammatory bowel disease), post-polypectomy bleeding. Management: resuscitate (ABC, IV access, bloods, cross-match), then colonoscopy or CT angiography; embolisation or surgery if bleeding continues.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Haemorrhoids — HAEMORRHOIDS are very common but usually cause small, bright red bleeding on the tissue — not the commonest cause of significant lower GI bleeding requiring admission.
• Colorectal cancer — COLORECTAL CANCER usually causes slow, chronic bleeding/anaemia rather than being the most common cause.
• Angiodysplasia — ANGIODYSPLASIA is an important cause in the elderly but less common than diverticular disease.

⭐ Key point: Commonest cause of significant lower GI bleeding = DIVERTICULAR DISEASE (painless, large volume, older patients).$x$, null),
(126, $x$A mass casualty incident (MCI) is defined as an event where:$x$, $x$Patient needs exceed available resources, requiring altered standards of care$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Patient needs exceed available resources, requiring altered standards of care
A MASS CASUALTY INCIDENT (MCI) is any event (road crash, building collapse, explosion, mass shooting, chemical release, disease outbreak) where the NUMBER and severity of casualties EXCEEDS the RESOURCES available (staff, beds, equipment, blood, transport). Because there is not enough for everyone, the aim changes from "the best for each patient" to "the GREATEST GOOD for the GREATEST NUMBER" — so standards of care are ALTERED: rapid TRIAGE (e.g. START: red immediate, yellow delayed, green minor, black expectant/dead), simple life-saving interventions, and activation of the hospital major incident plan. The number of patients that defines an MCI depends on local capacity.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only one patient is critically injured — ONE critically injured patient is managed with normal resources — not an MCI.
• Resources always exceed patient needs — If resources always exceed needs, it is NOT an MCI by definition.
• It occurs exclusively in rural areas — MCIs can occur anywhere — cities, rural areas, hospitals.

⭐ Key point: MCI = patient needs EXCEED available resources → triage and altered standards ("greatest good for the greatest number").$x$, null),
(127, $x$In a mass casualty incident involving burns, triage prioritisation typically considers:$x$, $x$Total body surface area burned together with airway involvement and other injuries$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Total body surface area burned together with airway involvement and other injuries
In a MASS CASUALTY BURN incident, triage must consider: (1) the SIZE of the burn (% TBSA) — larger burns need more fluid, staff and beds; (2) AIRWAY/INHALATION INJURY — singed nasal hair, soot in mouth, hoarse voice, stridor, burns in an enclosed space; these patients can lose their airway within hours and need early intubation; (3) OTHER INJURIES (fractures, head injury, blast injury) and (4) AGE and comorbidities — together these predict survival (e.g. Baux score = age + %TBSA). Patients with survivable burns but airway risk get high priority; those with very large burns plus inhalation injury and very low survival chance may be triaged as "expectant" when resources are limited.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Burn size alone, ignoring airway status — Using burn SIZE ALONE and ignoring the airway is dangerous; airway burns kill quickly.
• Only the patient's age — Age ALONE is not enough; size, airway and injuries matter too.
• Cosmetic appearance of the burn — COSMETIC appearance has no role in emergency triage.

⭐ Key point: Burn triage = %TBSA + AIRWAY/inhalation injury + other injuries + age (Baux score).$x$, null),
(128, $x$Both clotting and bleeding occur in DIC because:$x$, $x$Excess thrombin uses up clotting factors faster than they can be replaced$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Excess thrombin uses up clotting factors faster than they can be replaced
DIC (Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation) is triggered by severe illness — sepsis, major trauma, obstetric emergencies (placental abruption, amniotic fluid embolism), malignancy, snake bite. The trigger causes widespread activation of clotting and EXCESS THROMBIN production. Thrombin forms tiny clots (microthrombi) all through small blood vessels → organ damage (kidneys, lungs, brain, skin). This uses up (CONSUMES) clotting factors, fibrinogen and platelets FASTER than the liver and marrow can replace them — so-called "consumptive coagulopathy" — and the clots are broken down (fibrinolysis), producing D-dimer. With no factors left, the patient BLEEDS from everywhere (IV sites, gums, wounds, GI tract). Labs: low platelets, long PT/aPTT, LOW fibrinogen, HIGH D-dimer. Treat the cause; replace with platelets, FFP and cryoprecipitate if bleeding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bleeding uses up clotting factors faster than they are replaced — Bleeding is a RESULT of factor consumption by clotting — not the cause of the consumption.
• The intrinsic pathway releases excess clotting factors — The intrinsic pathway does not "release excess clotting factors"; factors are used up, not overproduced.
• Only clotting occurs in DIC — DIC is not "only clotting" — the hallmark is BOTH clotting and bleeding.

⭐ Key point: DIC: excess THROMBIN → widespread micro-clots → clotting factors and platelets CONSUMED → bleeding. Low fibrinogen, high D-dimer.$x$, null),
(129, $x$The classic '5 Ps' used to assess for compartment syndrome are pain, pallor, paresthesia, pulselessness, and:$x$, $x$Paralysis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Paralysis
COMPARTMENT SYNDROME occurs when pressure inside a closed muscle compartment (surrounded by tough fascia) rises so high that it cuts off blood flow to muscles and nerves — e.g. after fractures (tibia, forearm), crush injury, tight casts/bandages, circumferential burns, reperfusion. The classic "5 Ps": PAIN (out of proportion to the injury and worse on PASSIVE STRETCH of the muscles — the earliest and most important sign), PALLOR, PARAESTHESIA (tingling, numbness), PARALYSIS (late), PULSELESSNESS (very late — the pulse can still be present when muscle is dying). Some add POIKILOTHERMIA (cold limb) as a 6th P. Action: report immediately, loosen or split casts and dressings, keep the limb at heart level (do NOT elevate high), prepare for urgent FASCIOTOMY. Normal compartment pressure is under 10 mmHg; above 30 mmHg (or delta pressure < 30) needs fasciotomy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pruritus — PRURITUS (itching) is not one of the 5 Ps.
• Palpitations — PALPITATIONS are a heart symptom, not a sign of compartment syndrome.
• Polyuria — POLYURIA (passing a lot of urine) is unrelated; rhabdomyolysis may cause dark urine and AKI, not polyuria.

⭐ Key point: Compartment syndrome 5 Ps: Pain (on passive stretch — earliest), Pallor, Paraesthesia, PARALYSIS, Pulselessness (late). Needs fasciotomy.$x$, null),
(130, $x$The Curreri formula for nutritional requirements in burns patients is:$x$, $x$25kcal×kg + 40kcal×%TBSA$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 25kcal×kg + 40kcal×%TBSA
Burn patients have a very high metabolic rate (hypermetabolism) and break down muscle quickly, so they need extra calories and protein for healing. The CURRERI FORMULA estimates daily calorie needs in adults: 25 kcal × body weight (kg) + 40 kcal × %TBSA burned. Example: 70 kg, 40% burns → (25 × 70) + (40 × 40) = 1,750 + 1,600 = 3,350 kcal/day. Protein needs are also high (about 1.5–2 g/kg/day). Early ENTERAL feeding (within 24 hours) is preferred to support the gut and reduce infection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10kcal×kg only — 10 kcal × kg only is far too low and ignores burn size.
• 4ml×kg×%TBSA — 4 mL × kg × %TBSA is the PARKLAND formula for FLUID resuscitation, not calories.
• 2000kcal fixed daily — A fixed 2000 kcal/day ignores body weight and burn size; burned patients usually need much more.

⭐ Key point: Curreri formula = 25 kcal × kg + 40 kcal × %TBSA per day. Parkland (4 mL × kg × %TBSA) is for FLUIDS.$x$, null),
(131, $x$A casualty who is walking and talking at a disaster scene, with only minor injuries, is typically triaged as:$x$, $x$Minor (green)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Minor (green)
In disaster triage (e.g. START triage), the first step is: "Everyone who can WALK, move over here." Casualties who are WALKING and TALKING with only MINOR injuries are tagged GREEN — MINOR ("walking wounded"). They can safely wait for treatment and may even help others. Categories: RED — IMMEDIATE (life-threatening but survivable with quick treatment — e.g. airway problem, respiratory rate over 30, capillary refill over 2 seconds/no radial pulse, cannot follow commands); YELLOW — DELAYED (serious injuries but stable, cannot walk, can wait a few hours); GREEN — MINOR; BLACK — DECEASED or EXPECTANT (not breathing after airway opening, or unsurvivable injuries with limited resources).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediate (red) — IMMEDIATE (red) is for life-threatening, time-critical injuries — not walking patients with minor injuries.
• Delayed (yellow) — DELAYED (yellow) is for serious but stable injuries in patients who usually cannot walk.
• Deceased (black) — DECEASED (black) is for patients who are dead or not expected to survive.

⭐ Key point: START triage: walking + talking + minor injuries = GREEN (minor). Red immediate, yellow delayed, black deceased/expectant.$x$, null),
(132, $x$The secondary survey includes:$x$, $x$All of the above$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: All of the above
In trauma care, the PRIMARY SURVEY (ABCDE) finds and treats immediate life threats. Once the patient is stabilised, the SECONDARY SURVEY is done — a complete re-assessment that includes: (1) AMPLE HISTORY — Allergies, Medications, Past medical history/Pregnancy, Last meal, Events/Environment of injury; (2) a full HEAD-TO-TOE examination (head, face, neck, chest, abdomen, pelvis, perineum, limbs, back with log-roll, neurological exam) to find all injuries; (3) repeated VITAL SIGNS to detect deterioration; plus investigations (X-rays, CT, bloods). If the patient deteriorates at any time, go back to the primary survey.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• AMPLE history — AMPLE history alone is only one part of the secondary survey.
• Head-to-toe assessment — Head-to-toe assessment alone is only one part.
• Vital signs — Vital signs alone are only one part.

⭐ Key point: Secondary survey = AMPLE history + HEAD-TO-TOE exam + repeated VITAL SIGNS (all of the above).$x$, null),
(133, $x$The most common cause of hyperthyroidism is:$x$, $x$Grave's disease$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Grave's disease
GRAVES' DISEASE is the MOST COMMON cause of HYPERTHYROIDISM (about 60–80% of cases), especially in women aged 20–50. It is AUTOIMMUNE: antibodies (TSH-receptor antibodies, TRAb) stimulate the thyroid to grow and make too much hormone. Features: diffuse smooth GOITRE (often with a bruit), weight loss, heat intolerance, tremor, palpitations/AF, anxiety, plus features specific to Graves' — EYE disease (exophthalmos, lid lag), pretibial myxoedema and thyroid acropachy. Labs: low TSH, high T4/T3, positive TRAb. Treatment: carbimazole/methimazole or PTU, beta-blockers for symptoms, radioactive iodine or surgery.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Toxic multinodular goitre — TOXIC MULTINODULAR GOITRE is the second most common cause, mainly in older people.
• Solitary toxic adenoma — A SOLITARY TOXIC ADENOMA (one "hot" nodule) is less common.
• Thyroiditis — THYROIDITIS (e.g. subacute, postpartum) causes temporary hyperthyroidism from hormone leakage — less common.

⭐ Key point: Commonest cause of hyperthyroidism = GRAVES' DISEASE (autoimmune, TSH-receptor antibodies, eye disease).$x$, null),
(134, $x$The correct order for doffing (removing) PPE is:$x$, $x$Gloves → Goggles → Gown → Mask → Hand hygiene$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Gloves → Goggles → Gown → Mask → Hand hygiene
PPE is removed (DOFFED) in an order that takes off the MOST CONTAMINATED items first and protects your face and hands from contamination. The order in this question, which follows the WHO/CDC sequence, is: (1) GLOVES (the most contaminated — touched the patient); (2) GOGGLES/face shield (handle by the strap or ear pieces, not the front); (3) GOWN (untie, pull away from the body, roll inside-out); (4) MASK/respirator (remove by the straps, do not touch the front, after leaving the patient room for N95); (5) HAND HYGIENE — and also perform hand hygiene between steps if hands become contaminated. Putting on (DONNING) is the reverse idea: hand hygiene → gown → mask → goggles → gloves. (CDC also allows gown and gloves to be removed together.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mask → Gown → Goggles → Gloves → Hand hygiene — Removing the MASK FIRST would mean touching your face with contaminated gloves — high risk.
• Hand hygiene → Gloves → Gown → Mask → Goggles — Starting with hand hygiene and leaving GOGGLES until last mixes up donning and doffing steps; removing the gown and mask before the goggles risks touching the contaminated eye protection with exposed hands, and there is no final hand hygiene.
• Goggles → Mask → Gloves → Gown — Removing GOGGLES FIRST with dirty gloves risks contaminating your eyes and face, and there is no final hand hygiene.

⭐ Key point: Doffing: GLOVES → GOGGLES → GOWN → MASK → HAND HYGIENE. Donning: gown → mask → goggles → gloves.$x$, null),
(135, $x$A woman in the third trimester develops sudden severe headache, visual disturbance, and blood pressure of 165/110mmHg with proteinuria. The nurse should recognise this as warning signs of impending:$x$, $x$Eclamptic seizure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Eclamptic seizure
PRE-ECLAMPSIA is new HYPERTENSION (≥ 140/90) after 20 weeks of pregnancy with PROTEINURIA or organ dysfunction. SEVERE features include BP ≥ 160/110, severe HEADACHE, VISUAL DISTURBANCES (blurred vision, flashing lights), epigastric/right upper quadrant pain, low platelets, raised liver enzymes (HELLP), pulmonary oedema, reduced urine output. Headache and visual disturbance show CEREBRAL IRRITATION, warning that an ECLAMPTIC SEIZURE may happen soon. Actions: call for senior help, left lateral position, seizure precautions, IV MAGNESIUM SULPHATE (loading dose 4 g IV over 5–15 minutes, then 1 g/hour; monitor reflexes, respiratory rate ≥ 12, urine output ≥ 25–30 mL/hour; antidote calcium gluconate), lower BP with labetalol, hydralazine or nifedipine, and plan delivery.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal pregnancy discomfort — These are NOT normal pregnancy discomforts; BP 165/110 with proteinuria is a medical emergency.
• Simple migraine unrelated to pregnancy — A simple migraine does not cause BP 165/110 with proteinuria.
• Round ligament strain — ROUND LIGAMENT strain causes groin/lower abdominal pain, not headache, visual changes and hypertension.

⭐ Key point: Pregnancy + BP ≥ 160/110 + proteinuria + headache/visual disturbance = severe pre-eclampsia → imminent ECLAMPSIA. Give MgSO4.$x$, null),
(136, $x$Primary postpartum haemorrhage after a vaginal delivery is defined as blood loss of:$x$, $x$≥500ml$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: ≥500ml
PRIMARY POSTPARTUM HAEMORRHAGE (PPH) is blood loss of 500 mL OR MORE within 24 HOURS after a VAGINAL delivery (WHO definition), or 1000 mL or more after CAESAREAN section. It can also be defined as any blood loss enough to make the mother haemodynamically unstable. Minor PPH = 500–1000 mL; major PPH = over 1000 mL; severe = over 2000 mL. SECONDARY PPH is excessive bleeding from 24 hours up to 12 weeks after birth (often from retained products or infection). Causes — the "4 Ts": TONE (uterine atony — the commonest, about 70%), TRAUMA (tears, rupture), TISSUE (retained placenta/clots), THROMBIN (clotting problems). PPH is a leading cause of maternal death in Nigeria and worldwide.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• ≥200ml — 200 mL is within normal blood loss for a vaginal birth.
• ≥1000ml — 1000 mL is the threshold for CAESAREAN section or for MAJOR PPH, not the basic vaginal definition.
• ≥2000ml — 2000 mL is SEVERE/massive haemorrhage; waiting for this would delay treatment dangerously.

⭐ Key point: Primary PPH = ≥ 500 mL within 24 h after vaginal birth (≥ 1000 mL after CS). Causes = 4 Ts (Tone commonest).$x$, null),
(137, $x$A key distinguishing feature between placental abruption and placenta previa is that abruption classically presents with:$x$, $x$Painful vaginal bleeding, often with a rigid, tender uterus$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Painful vaginal bleeding, often with a rigid, tender uterus
Both conditions cause bleeding in the second half of pregnancy (antepartum haemorrhage), but they feel different. PLACENTAL ABRUPTION: the normally placed placenta SEPARATES early from the uterine wall. Blood collects behind the placenta and irritates the uterus, causing PAINFUL bleeding, a HARD, RIGID ("woody"), TENDER uterus, and often foetal distress. Bleeding may be hidden (concealed) so shock can be worse than visible blood loss suggests; DIC is a risk. Risk factors: hypertension/pre-eclampsia, trauma, cocaine, smoking. PLACENTA PRAEVIA: the placenta lies low over or near the cervix, causing PAINLESS, bright red bleeding with a SOFT, non-tender uterus. Never do a vaginal examination in suspected praevia until it is excluded by ultrasound.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Painless vaginal bleeding — PAINLESS vaginal bleeding is typical of PLACENTA PRAEVIA, not abruption.
• No vaginal bleeding at all — Abruption usually causes some visible bleeding (though it may be concealed); "no bleeding at all" is not the classic feature.
• Bleeding only in the first trimester — Abruption occurs in the second half of pregnancy (after 20 weeks), not only in the first trimester.

⭐ Key point: ABRUPTION = PAINFUL bleeding + RIGID tender uterus. PRAEVIA = PAINLESS bright red bleeding, soft uterus. No VE in praevia.$x$, null),
(138, $x$HELLP syndrome, a complication of severe pre-eclampsia, stands for:$x$, $x$Haemolysis, Elevated Liver enzymes, Low Platelets$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Haemolysis, Elevated Liver enzymes, Low Platelets
HELLP syndrome is a severe form/complication of pre-eclampsia (it can occur even with mild BP rise). HELLP stands for: H — HAEMOLYSIS (red cells broken down; raised LDH, low haptoglobin, schistocytes on blood film, raised bilirubin); EL — ELEVATED LIVER ENZYMES (raised AST/ALT from liver damage; may cause right upper quadrant/epigastric pain, nausea, vomiting); LP — LOW PLATELETS (under 100 × 10⁹/L — bleeding risk). Complications: DIC, placental abruption, liver haematoma/rupture, AKI, pulmonary oedema. Management: stabilise the mother, MAGNESIUM SULPHATE for seizure prevention, control BP, correct clotting/platelets, and DELIVERY of the baby is the definitive treatment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• High Eclampsia, Low Labour Progress — "High Eclampsia, Low Labour Progress" is not a medical term.
• Hypertension, Elevated Lipids, Low Protein — "Hypertension, Elevated Lipids, Low Protein" is incorrect; lipids are not part of HELLP.
• Haematoma, Embolism, Liver failure, Pulmonary oedema — "Haematoma, Embolism, Liver failure, Pulmonary oedema" lists possible complications, but not the meaning of HELLP.

⭐ Key point: HELLP = Haemolysis, Elevated Liver enzymes, Low Platelets. Definitive treatment = delivery.$x$, null),
(139, $x$What is the best thing one can do to prevent premature rupture of membranes?$x$, $x$All of the options$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: All of the options
PREMATURE RUPTURE OF MEMBRANES (PROM/PPROM) means the amniotic sac breaks before labour starts (and before 37 weeks for PPROM). Risk factors include: SMOKING (weakens membranes), infections (urinary tract infection, bacterial vaginosis, STIs), previous PROM or preterm birth, poor nutrition, multiple pregnancy, polyhydramnios, cervical insufficiency, bleeding in pregnancy and low socio-economic status. Prevention therefore includes ALL the options: AVOID SMOKING, MAINTAIN A HEALTHY PREGNANCY (good nutrition, vitamin C and other nutrients, treat infections, rest when advised), and ATTEND ALL ANTENATAL (PRENATAL) CLINICS so that infections and risk factors are found and treated early.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Avoid smoking cigarettes — Avoiding smoking alone is correct but is only one preventive measure.
• Maintain a healthy pregnancy — Maintaining a healthy pregnancy alone is correct but does not include the other important measures.
• Attend all prenatal clinic — Attending antenatal clinic alone is correct, but it is only one of several preventive actions.

⭐ Key point: Prevent PROM: no smoking + healthy pregnancy (nutrition, treat infections) + regular antenatal care — ALL of the options.$x$, null),
(140, $x$Other than transfusion, when is it necessary to take the Rh Factor into consideration?$x$, $x$Pregnancy$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Pregnancy
The Rh (D) factor is a protein on red blood cells. People are Rh-POSITIVE (have it) or Rh-NEGATIVE (do not). Apart from blood transfusion, it is most important in PREGNANCY. If an Rh-NEGATIVE mother carries an Rh-POSITIVE baby, foetal blood can enter her circulation (during delivery, miscarriage, abortion, ectopic pregnancy, abdominal trauma, amniocentesis, bleeding). She makes anti-D antibodies (SENSITISATION). In a LATER pregnancy, these antibodies cross the placenta and destroy the red cells of an Rh-positive baby — HAEMOLYTIC DISEASE OF THE NEWBORN (anaemia, jaundice, hydrops foetalis). Prevention: give ANTI-D IMMUNOGLOBULIN to Rh-negative mothers at about 28 weeks, within 72 hours after delivery of an Rh-positive baby, and after any sensitising event.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cathertization — CATHETERISATION does not involve the Rh factor.
• Spleen Rupture — SPLEEN RUPTURE may need transfusion (already covered by "other than transfusion"), but the Rh factor is not otherwise relevant.
• Blood Donation — BLOOD DONATION is part of transfusion services — the question asks for situations other than transfusion.

⭐ Key point: Rh factor matters in PREGNANCY: Rh-negative mother + Rh-positive baby → sensitisation → give ANTI-D within 72 h.$x$, null),
(141, $x$Magnesium sulfate is administered in severe preeclampsia primarily to:$x$, $x$Prevent or treat eclamptic seizures$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Prevent or treat eclamptic seizures
In SEVERE PRE-ECLAMPSIA, MAGNESIUM SULPHATE (MgSO4) is given mainly to PREVENT ECLAMPTIC SEIZURES — and to TREAT them if they occur. It is a central nervous system depressant and cerebral vasodilator that stabilises nerve membranes. The Magpie trial showed it halves the risk of eclampsia. Regimen (Zuspan/Pritchard): loading dose 4 g IV over 5–15 minutes (or 4 g IV + 10 g IM), then maintenance 1 g/hour IV (or 5 g IM every 4 hours) for 24 hours after delivery or the last fit. MONITOR for MAGNESIUM TOXICITY: loss of PATELLAR REFLEXES (first sign), respiratory rate below 12/min, urine output below 25–30 mL/hour, drowsiness. ANTIDOTE: CALCIUM GLUCONATE 10% 10 mL IV over 10 minutes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lower blood glucose — MgSO4 does NOT lower blood glucose.
• Induce labour immediately — It does not INDUCE labour (oxytocin or prostaglandins do); it may slightly relax the uterus.
• Treat postpartum haemorrhage — It does not treat PPH; uterotonics (oxytocin, ergometrine, misoprostol) do.

⭐ Key point: MgSO4 in pre-eclampsia = PREVENT/TREAT SEIZURES. Toxicity: lost reflexes, RR < 12, low urine. Antidote: calcium gluconate.$x$, null),
(142, $x$The drug of choice to prevent further convulsions in eclampsia is:$x$, $x$Magnesium sulphate$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Magnesium sulphate
MAGNESIUM SULPHATE is the DRUG OF CHOICE to prevent FURTHER CONVULSIONS in eclampsia. The Collaborative Eclampsia Trial showed MgSO4 is far better than DIAZEPAM and PHENYTOIN: fewer recurrent fits and lower maternal death. It is cheap, available and recommended by WHO. Give a loading dose 4 g IV over 5–15 minutes, then 1 g/hour for 24 hours; a recurrent fit is treated with another 2–4 g IV. During a fit: call for help, left lateral position, protect from injury, airway and oxygen, then MgSO4. Monitor reflexes, respirations and urine output; calcium gluconate is the antidote.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diazepam — DIAZEPAM stops a fit but is less effective at preventing further eclamptic fits and causes sedation in mother and baby (respiratory depression).
• Phenytoin — PHENYTOIN is less effective than MgSO4 in eclampsia.
• Hydralazine — HYDRALAZINE is an ANTIHYPERTENSIVE used to lower BP; it does not prevent convulsions.

⭐ Key point: Eclampsia → MAGNESIUM SULPHATE is the drug of choice (better than diazepam and phenytoin). Hydralazine/labetalol for BP.$x$, null),
(143, $x$Which of the following dietary recommendations are effective in the management of hyperemesis gravidarum?$x$, $x$Eating small, frequent meals$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Eating small, frequent meals
HYPEREMESIS GRAVIDARUM is severe, persistent vomiting in pregnancy causing dehydration, weight loss (> 5%), ketosis and electrolyte imbalance (low potassium and sodium). Dietary advice: eat SMALL, FREQUENT MEALS (every 2–3 hours) so the stomach is never empty or overloaded; choose dry, bland, high-carbohydrate, low-fat foods (crackers, toast, rice); drink fluids BETWEEN meals rather than with meals; avoid spicy, greasy or strong-smelling foods; ginger may help. Severe cases need hospital: IV fluids (normal saline with potassium), antiemetics, THIAMINE (to prevent Wernicke's encephalopathy), and monitoring of electrolytes and ketones.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Eating high-fat foods — HIGH-FAT foods are digested slowly and make nausea worse.
• Drinking fluids with meals — Drinking fluids WITH meals fills the stomach and makes vomiting more likely; fluids should be taken between meals.
• Removing salt from the diet — REMOVING SALT is not helpful; these women often lose sodium through vomiting and need it replaced.

⭐ Key point: Hyperemesis: SMALL, FREQUENT, dry, low-fat meals; fluids BETWEEN meals; IV fluids + thiamine if severe.$x$, null),
(144, $x$Critical management of a woman with severe bleeding just after delivery includes:$x$, $x$A continuous IV infusion of high-dose oxytocin$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A continuous IV infusion of high-dose oxytocin
Severe bleeding just after delivery is PRIMARY POSTPARTUM HAEMORRHAGE. The most common cause is UTERINE ATONY (a soft, poorly contracted uterus). Critical management: call for help, ABC, two large-bore IV cannulas, blood for cross-match, warm crystalloid and blood, catheterise to EMPTY the bladder, and RUB UP the uterus (fundal massage). UTEROTONICS are key: oxytocin 10 IU IM/IV, then a CONTINUOUS IV OXYTOCIN INFUSION (e.g. 40 IU in 500 mL over 4 hours) to keep the uterus contracted; ergometrine (not in hypertension), misoprostol, carboprost; TRANEXAMIC ACID 1 g IV within 3 hours of birth. If still bleeding: bimanual compression, balloon tamponade, surgery.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Setting up dextrose water as the main fluid — DEXTROSE WATER is not a resuscitation fluid — it leaves the blood vessels quickly; use crystalloids (normal saline, Ringer's) and blood.
• Stopping all uterotonics — STOPPING uterotonics is wrong; they are the main treatment for atony.
• Filling the bladder to stop bleeding — A FULL bladder prevents the uterus from contracting — the bladder must be EMPTIED, not filled.

⭐ Key point: PPH (atony): massage uterus + OXYTOCIN infusion + other uterotonics + tranexamic acid; EMPTY the bladder; crystalloid and blood.$x$, null),
(145, $x$A premature infant in the NICU develops abdominal distension, bloody stools, and feeding intolerance. The nurse should suspect:$x$, $x$Necrotising enterocolitis (NEC), a serious neonatal GI emergency$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Necrotising enterocolitis (NEC), a serious neonatal GI emergency
NECROTISING ENTEROCOLITIS (NEC) is a serious bowel emergency, mostly in PREMATURE and low-birth-weight babies, usually in the first 2–3 weeks after starting feeds. Part of the bowel wall becomes inflamed and dies (necrosis), sometimes with perforation. Classic signs: FEEDING INTOLERANCE (increased gastric residuals, bilious vomiting), ABDOMINAL DISTENSION and tenderness, BLOODY STOOLS, plus signs of sepsis (apnoea, bradycardia, temperature instability, lethargy). X-ray: pneumatosis intestinalis (gas in the bowel wall), portal venous gas, free air if perforated. Management: STOP FEEDS (NPO), NG tube on free drainage, IV fluids/TPN, IV antibiotics, serial X-rays, surgical review. Breast milk reduces NEC risk.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal prematurity findings requiring no evaluation — These are NOT normal prematurity findings; ignoring them can be fatal.
• Simple constipation — SIMPLE CONSTIPATION does not cause bloody stools, bilious aspirates and systemic illness.
• Normal digestion in preterm infants — Normal digestion does not cause distension, bloody stools and feeding intolerance.

⭐ Key point: Preterm + feeding intolerance + abdominal distension + bloody stools = NEC → stop feeds, NG decompression, antibiotics, X-ray.$x$, null),
(146, $x$Classic presenting features of appendicitis in a child include periumbilical pain migrating to which quadrant?$x$, $x$Right lower quadrant$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Right lower quadrant
In APPENDICITIS, pain usually starts as dull PERIUMBILICAL (around the belly button) pain — this is visceral pain, because the appendix shares nerve supply with the midgut (T10). After a few hours, as inflammation reaches the overlying parietal peritoneum, pain MOVES to the RIGHT LOWER QUADRANT (RLQ), over McBURNEY'S POINT (one-third of the way from the anterior superior iliac spine to the umbilicus), and becomes sharp and localised. Other signs: loss of appetite, nausea/vomiting, low-grade fever, rebound tenderness, Rovsing's sign, psoas sign, raised white cells. Children often present late and perforate more often. Nursing: NPO, IV fluids, analgesia, no laxatives or enemas (risk of perforation), prepare for appendicectomy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Right upper quadrant — RIGHT UPPER QUADRANT pain suggests gallbladder or liver problems.
• Left lower quadrant — LEFT LOWER QUADRANT pain suggests sigmoid/diverticular disease (adults) or left-sided problems.
• Left upper quadrant — LEFT UPPER QUADRANT pain suggests spleen, stomach or pancreas problems.

⭐ Key point: Appendicitis: periumbilical pain → RIGHT LOWER QUADRANT (McBurney's point), with anorexia, fever, rebound tenderness.$x$, null),
(147, $x$Croup in a child is classically characterised by:$x$, $x$A high-pitched, barking cough with inspiratory stridor$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A high-pitched, barking cough with inspiratory stridor
CROUP (laryngotracheobronchitis) is a viral infection (usually PARAINFLUENZA virus) mainly in children aged 6 months to 3 years. It causes swelling of the larynx and trachea just below the vocal cords. Classic features: a "BARKING" (seal-like) COUGH, HOARSE voice, INSPIRATORY STRIDOR (noisy breathing in), often worse at night, after 1–2 days of cold-like symptoms and low-grade fever. Treatment: keep the child CALM (crying worsens obstruction), give oral DEXAMETHASONE (0.15–0.6 mg/kg) for all cases, NEBULISED ADRENALINE for severe stridor at rest, and oxygen if needed. Warning signs: stridor at rest, chest recession, agitation or drowsiness, cyanosis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Silent chest with no cough — A SILENT CHEST with no cough is a sign of life-threatening ASTHMA (no air movement), not croup.
• Chronic productive cough over months — A CHRONIC productive cough over months suggests conditions like TB, bronchiectasis or cystic fibrosis.
• Painless swallowing difficulty — PAINLESS swallowing difficulty is not typical of croup; EPIGLOTTITIS causes PAINFUL swallowing and drooling.

⭐ Key point: Croup = BARKING cough + INSPIRATORY STRIDOR + hoarseness (parainfluenza). Treat with dexamethasone ± nebulised adrenaline.$x$, null),
(148, $x$Assessing dehydration severity in a paediatric patient includes evaluating all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$The child's favourite toy$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The child's favourite toy
Children dehydrate quickly. Clinical assessment of DEHYDRATION SEVERITY looks at: general condition (alert, irritable, lethargic), SKIN TURGOR (skin pinch goes back slowly in dehydration), MUCOUS MEMBRANES (dry mouth and tongue), eyes (sunken), tears (absent), anterior FONTANELLE in infants (sunken), capillary refill time (over 2 seconds), heart rate (tachycardia), BP (low = late sign), URINE OUTPUT (reduced/no wet nappies), and weight loss (best measure if pre-illness weight is known: mild < 5%, moderate 5–10%, severe > 10%). WHO classifies as no, some, or severe dehydration to guide ORS (Plan A/B) or IV fluids (Plan C). The child's FAVOURITE TOY has no clinical value for hydration.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Skin turgor — SKIN TURGOR IS assessed — slow return of a skin pinch shows dehydration.
• Mucous membrane moisture — MUCOUS MEMBRANE moisture IS assessed — a dry mouth shows dehydration.
• Urine output — URINE OUTPUT IS assessed — reduced urine/fewer wet nappies shows dehydration.

⭐ Key point: Paediatric dehydration: turgor, mucous membranes, eyes, tears, fontanelle, capillary refill, HR, urine output, weight loss.$x$, null),
(149, $x$The APGAR score evaluates a newborn's condition based on which five components?$x$, $x$Appearance, pulse, grimace, activity, and respiration$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Appearance, pulse, grimace, activity, and respiration
The APGAR score assesses a newborn at 1 and 5 minutes after birth (and every 5 minutes up to 20 minutes if low). Five components, each scored 0, 1 or 2: A — APPEARANCE (skin colour: blue/pale 0, body pink with blue limbs 1, completely pink 2); P — PULSE (heart rate: absent 0, below 100 = 1, 100 or above = 2); G — GRIMACE (reflex irritability: no response 0, grimace 1, cry/cough/sneeze 2); A — ACTIVITY (muscle tone: limp 0, some flexion 1, active movement 2); R — RESPIRATION (absent 0, slow/irregular 1, good strong cry 2). Total 7–10 = normal; 4–6 = moderately depressed (needs some help); 0–3 = severely depressed (needs resuscitation). Resuscitation should not wait for the 1-minute score.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Airway, pulse, glucose, activity, respiration — "Airway, pulse, glucose…" — glucose is not part of APGAR.
• Appearance, position, glucose, activity, reflex — "Appearance, position, glucose, activity, reflex" — position and glucose are not components.
• Alertness, pain, growth, appetite, reflex — "Alertness, pain, growth, appetite, reflex" — none of these, except reflex in part, are APGAR components.

⭐ Key point: APGAR = Appearance, Pulse, Grimace, Activity, Respiration (0–2 each, total 10). Score at 1 and 5 minutes.$x$, null),
(150, $x$Neonates at increased risk for hypoglycaemia after birth include those born to:$x$, $x$Diabetic mothers, and infants who are preterm or growth-restricted$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Diabetic mothers, and infants who are preterm or growth-restricted
After birth, the baby's glucose supply from the mother stops suddenly, so the newborn must make its own. Babies at HIGH RISK of NEONATAL HYPOGLYCAEMIA: (1) INFANTS OF DIABETIC MOTHERS — high maternal glucose crosses the placenta, so the baby's pancreas makes a lot of INSULIN (hyperinsulinism); after birth the glucose supply stops but insulin stays high, so glucose drops; these babies are often large (macrosomic). (2) PRETERM infants — small glycogen and fat stores. (3) GROWTH-RESTRICTED (SGA/IUGR) infants — poor stores. Also: babies who are cold, septic, asphyxiated, or of mothers on beta-blockers. Signs: jitteriness, poor feeding, lethargy, apnoea, seizures. Management: early and frequent feeding, check blood glucose (treat if below about 2.6 mmol/L/47 mg/dL), oral or IV dextrose (10% dextrose 2 mL/kg).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mothers with well-controlled, non-diabetic pregnancies — Babies of mothers with well-controlled NON-diabetic pregnancies are NOT at increased risk.
• Mothers with normal glucose tolerance only — Mothers with normal glucose tolerance do not raise the baby's risk.
• Only infants born after 42 weeks gestation — Post-term (> 42 weeks) babies can be at some risk (placental insufficiency), but "only" these babies is wrong — diabetic mothers, preterm and SGA babies are the main groups.

⭐ Key point: Neonatal hypoglycaemia risk: infants of DIABETIC mothers (hyperinsulinism), PRETERM, and growth-restricted (SGA) babies.$x$, null),
(151, $x$Which of the following is NOT one of the environmental components Nightingale identified as essential to a healthy healing environment?$x$, $x$Strict bedrest for all patients regardless of condition$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Strict bedrest for all patients regardless of condition
FLORENCE NIGHTINGALE's Environmental Theory says the nurse's role is to manage the ENVIRONMENT so that nature can heal the patient. Her key components: PURE/FRESH AIR and VENTILATION, PURE WATER, efficient DRAINAGE, CLEANLINESS, LIGHT (especially direct SUNLIGHT), QUIET (control of NOISE), WARMTH, proper DIET and nutrition, a clean bed and bedding, personal cleanliness, variety (to prevent boredom), and observation of the patient. She did NOT recommend strict bedrest for all patients regardless of condition — care was to be individualised, and she valued activity, variety and attention to each patient's needs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pure/fresh air and proper ventilation — PURE/FRESH AIR and ventilation IS one of her key components (often listed first).
• Adequate light, especially direct sunlight — Adequate LIGHT, especially sunlight, IS a key component.
• Noise control and quiet — NOISE control/quiet IS a key component; she warned that unnecessary noise harms patients.

⭐ Key point: Nightingale's environment: fresh air, pure water, drainage, cleanliness, LIGHT, QUIET, warmth, diet. Not "strict bedrest for all".$x$, null),
(152, $x$When selecting nursing interventions, the nurse should consider all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$The nurse's personal convenience over client needs$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The nurse's personal convenience over client needs
When choosing NURSING INTERVENTIONS (the planning stage of the nursing process), the nurse should consider: (1) the DESIRED CLIENT OUTCOME/goal — interventions must help reach it; (2) EVIDENCE-BASED practice and research — use interventions proven to work; (3) the CLIENT'S PREFERENCES, values, culture and RESOURCES (money, family support, ability); (4) safety and the nurse's competence; (5) the nursing diagnosis and its related factors; (6) institutional policies and available resources. The NURSE'S PERSONAL CONVENIENCE must never be placed above the client's needs — care is patient-centred.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The desired client outcome — The DESIRED client outcome IS considered — interventions are chosen to achieve it.
• Evidence-based practice and research support — EVIDENCE-BASED practice IS considered — it makes interventions safer and more effective.
• The client's preferences and resources — The client's PREFERENCES and RESOURCES ARE considered — care must be acceptable and realistic for the patient.

⭐ Key point: Choose interventions by outcome, evidence, client preferences/resources, safety — never the nurse's personal convenience.$x$, null),
(153, $x$In the PDSA cycle, the Study phase involves:$x$, $x$Analyzing the results/data collected and comparing them to predictions$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Analyzing the results/data collected and comparing them to predictions
The PDSA cycle (Plan–Do–Study–Act; Deming/Shewhart) is a quality improvement method for testing changes on a small scale. PLAN — state the objective, PREDICT what will happen, and plan who, what, where, when, and what data to collect. DO — carry out the change on a SMALL/TRIAL scale and collect the data, noting problems. STUDY — ANALYSE the data/results, COMPARE them with the PREDICTIONS, and summarise what was learned. ACT — decide: adopt the change, adapt (modify) it, or abandon it, then plan the next cycle. Many small cycles lead to reliable improvement before wider rollout.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Developing the objective and predicting results — Developing the objective and predicting results is the PLAN phase.
• Carrying out the planned change on a small/trial scale — Carrying out the change on a small scale is the DO phase.
• Implementing the change hospital-wide permanently — Implementing hospital-wide is part of ACT (after successful testing), not Study.

⭐ Key point: PDSA: Plan (objective, prediction) → Do (small test) → STUDY (analyse and compare with prediction) → Act (adopt, adapt, abandon).$x$, null),
(154, $x$For a family experiencing acute grief after a patient's death in the ICU, which NIC intervention is most appropriate?$x$, $x$Grief Work Facilitation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Grief Work Facilitation
NIC (Nursing Interventions Classification) is a standard list of nursing interventions. For a FAMILY in ACUTE GRIEF after a death in ICU, the appropriate NIC intervention is GRIEF WORK FACILITATION — assisting people to resolve a significant loss. Activities include: being present and listening, encouraging expression of feelings, allowing the family time with the body, explaining what happened in simple language, respecting cultural and religious practices (e.g. Islamic burial within 24 hours, Christian prayers), offering chaplain/imam support, identifying support systems, and giving information about bereavement services. Other related NIC interventions: family support, emotional support, spiritual support.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Airway Suctioning — AIRWAY SUCTIONING is a physical intervention for a patient's airway; it does nothing for the bereaved family.
• Electrolyte Management — ELECTROLYTE MANAGEMENT is a physiological intervention for a living patient.
• Positioning — POSITIONING is for a patient's comfort/physiology, not grief support for a family.

⭐ Key point: Grieving family → NIC "GRIEF WORK FACILITATION": presence, listening, cultural/spiritual support, time with the body.$x$, null),
(155, $x$Before mobilising a post-operative or critically ill patient for the first time, the nurse should first:$x$, $x$Assess haemodynamic stability, fall risk, and any lines/tubes that need securing$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Assess haemodynamic stability, fall risk, and any lines/tubes that need securing
EARLY MOBILISATION reduces complications (DVT, pneumonia, pressure ulcers, muscle wasting, delirium) and speeds recovery. But before the FIRST mobilisation of a post-operative or critically ill patient, the nurse must ASSESS SAFETY: (1) HAEMODYNAMIC STABILITY — heart rate, BP (risk of postural hypotension), SpO2, respiratory rate, need for vasopressors; (2) neurological state and ability to follow commands (sedation level, RASS); (3) FALL RISK, pain control, strength; (4) LINES, TUBES AND DRAINS — secure the ETT, central lines, arterial lines, catheters, chest drains, so they are not pulled out; (5) have enough staff and equipment (walker, chair, portable oxygen, monitor). Start slowly: sit up → dangle legs → stand → chair → walk; stop if the patient becomes dizzy, breathless or unstable.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Skip any assessment and proceed directly to ambulation — SKIPPING assessment risks falls, fainting, accidental extubation or line removal.
• Remove all monitoring equipment — REMOVING ALL monitoring is unsafe — critically ill patients need monitoring during the first mobilisation.
• Wait until the patient requests it — WAITING until the patient asks delays recovery; early mobilisation is planned and encouraged by the team.

⭐ Key point: Before first mobilisation: check HAEMODYNAMICS, fall risk, pain, and SECURE lines/tubes; mobilise gradually with enough staff.$x$, null),
(156, $x$When two nursing diagnoses appear closely related, what should the nurse do first to determine which diagnosis most accurately reflects the needs of a patient?$x$, $x$Review the defining characteristics$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Review the defining characteristics
Each NANDA-I nursing diagnosis has DEFINING CHARACTERISTICS — the signs and symptoms (cues) that must be present for that diagnosis. When two diagnoses look similar (e.g. "Ineffective Airway Clearance" vs "Ineffective Breathing Pattern", or "Anxiety" vs "Fear"), the nurse should first REVIEW THE DEFINING CHARACTERISTICS of each and compare them with the patient's data. The diagnosis whose defining characteristics best match the patient's actual cues is the most accurate. Example: Fear has an identifiable source; Anxiety has a vague, non-specific source.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reassess the patient — REASSESSING the patient may be needed if data are missing, but the first step in choosing between two diagnoses is comparing defining characteristics with the data already collected.
• Examine the related to factors — "Related to" factors describe the CAUSE (aetiology); they help write the statement but do not best distinguish which diagnosis is present.
• Analyze the secondary to factors — "Secondary to" usually refers to a medical condition; nursing diagnoses are not chosen based on medical diagnoses.

⭐ Key point: To choose between similar nursing diagnoses → compare DEFINING CHARACTERISTICS (signs/symptoms) with the patient's cues.$x$, null),
(157, $x$If a goal states a patient will gain 2kg in 4 weeks, and the patient gains only 1kg, the goal is classified as:$x$, $x$Partially Met$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Partially Met
In the EVALUATION stage of the nursing process, the nurse compares the patient's actual response with the expected outcome (goal). There are three results: GOAL MET — the patient achieved the full outcome by the target date; GOAL PARTIALLY MET — the patient made SOME progress but did not reach the full outcome; GOAL NOT MET — no progress, or got worse. Here the goal was 2 kg weight gain in 4 weeks; the patient gained 1 kg — progress, but only half the target, so the goal is PARTIALLY MET. The nurse should then find out why (intake, illness, plan problems), and revise the plan: continue, modify interventions or change the time frame.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Met — MET would require the full 2 kg gain.
• Not Met — NOT MET would mean no gain at all (or weight loss).
• Cannot be evaluated — It CAN be evaluated — the goal is measurable (kg) and time-bound (4 weeks).

⭐ Key point: Evaluation: Met (full), PARTIALLY MET (some progress), Not met (none). 1 kg of a 2 kg goal = partially met.$x$, null),
(158, $x$A subtle but important principle of re-assessment is that:$x$, $x$A goal may be met on paper while the underlying problem remains unsolved$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A goal may be met on paper while the underlying problem remains unsolved
RE-ASSESSMENT is an ongoing part of the nursing process. A goal written in a care plan can appear MET "ON PAPER" (e.g. "patient will report pain less than 3/10") while the UNDERLYING PROBLEM is still there (e.g. the patient reports less pain only because they are afraid of injections, or pain falls only when sedated, or a wound has not healed). Good nurses look beyond the goal statement: they check the actual condition of the patient, the cause of the problem, and whether new problems have developed, then update the care plan. Goals should therefore be measurable, realistic and linked to the real problem.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A goal that appears met always means the problem is fully solved — A met goal does NOT always mean the problem is fully solved — that is the subtle point of this question.
• Re-assessment is unnecessary once a goal is achieved — Re-assessment is NOT unnecessary once a goal is met; the patient's condition can change and new problems may appear.
• Only unmet goals require re-assessment — ALL goals, met and unmet, need re-assessment — not only unmet ones.

⭐ Key point: A goal can be met on paper while the real problem remains — ALWAYS re-assess the patient, not just the goal statement.$x$, null),
(159, $x$A client with well-controlled type 2 diabetes who exercises regularly, follows a prescribed diet, and reports high energy and life satisfaction would most likely be placed toward which end of the health-illness continuum?$x$, $x$Toward the high-level wellness end$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Toward the high-level wellness end
The HEALTH–ILLNESS CONTINUUM (Dunn/Travis) shows health as a range from HIGH-LEVEL WELLNESS at one end to PREMATURE DEATH at the other, not as simply "sick or not sick". A person's position depends on their whole state — physical, mental, social and emotional — and their ability to function and achieve their potential. A person with a CHRONIC disease (e.g. well-controlled type 2 diabetes) can still be near the HIGH-LEVEL WELLNESS end if they manage the condition well: regular exercise, following the diet, taking medications, feeling energetic and satisfied with life. Nurses use this idea to promote wellness in every patient.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Toward the death end — Toward the DEATH end would mean severe illness and poor function — not this active, satisfied person.
• Exactly at the neutral, disease-free point only — The continuum is not about a single "neutral, disease-free" point; this patient has a disease but high wellness.
• Outside the continuum, since a chronic disease disqualifies wellness placement — A chronic disease does NOT exclude a person from the continuum; everyone is somewhere on it.

⭐ Key point: Health–illness continuum: a person with a well-managed chronic disease can be near HIGH-LEVEL WELLNESS.$x$, null),
(160, $x$A client newly diagnosed with heart failure must learn to monitor daily weights, restrict sodium, and recognize signs of fluid overload. This need is best classified as which type of self-care requisite?$x$, $x$Health-deviation self-care requisite$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Health-deviation self-care requisite
OREM'S SELF-CARE DEFICIT THEORY describes three types of SELF-CARE REQUISITES (needs): (1) UNIVERSAL — needs everyone has: air, water, food, elimination, activity and rest, solitude and social interaction, safety, normality; (2) DEVELOPMENTAL — needs linked to life stages and events (pregnancy, adolescence, ageing, bereavement); (3) HEALTH-DEVIATION — needs that arise because of ILLNESS, injury, disability or medical treatment: learning to live with and manage a disease. A patient NEWLY DIAGNOSED with HEART FAILURE who must learn DAILY WEIGHTS, SODIUM RESTRICTION and signs of FLUID OVERLOAD has HEALTH-DEVIATION self-care requisites.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Universal self-care requisite — UNIVERSAL requisites are basic needs of all people, not disease-specific learning.
• Developmental self-care requisite — DEVELOPMENTAL requisites relate to life stages and maturation, not a new illness.
• Environmental self-care requisite — "Environmental self-care requisite" is not one of Orem's three categories.

⭐ Key point: Orem: universal (everyone), developmental (life stages), HEALTH-DEVIATION (needs caused by illness/treatment).$x$, null),
(161, $x$A nurse formulates the nursing diagnosis 'Risk for infection related to invasive lines' for a critically ill patient. This is an example of which type of nursing diagnosis?$x$, $x$A risk nursing diagnosis, describing vulnerability to a potential problem rather than a current one$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A risk nursing diagnosis, describing vulnerability to a potential problem rather than a current one
NANDA-I nursing diagnoses come in several types: ACTUAL (problem-focused) — a problem that EXISTS NOW, with defining characteristics present (e.g. "Impaired Gas Exchange related to… as evidenced by SpO2 85%"); RISK — the patient is VULNERABLE to developing a problem but it has NOT happened yet; written with "Risk for…" and RISK FACTORS only (no "as evidenced by", because no signs exist yet) — e.g. "Risk for infection related to invasive lines"; HEALTH PROMOTION/WELLNESS — readiness to improve health (e.g. "Readiness for enhanced nutrition"); SYNDROME — a cluster of diagnoses. ICU patients with central lines, urinary catheters and ETTs are at risk of infection, so this is a RISK diagnosis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An actual nursing diagnosis, describing a current problem — An ACTUAL diagnosis describes a current problem with signs present — infection has not occurred here.
• A wellness diagnosis — A WELLNESS diagnosis describes readiness to improve health, not vulnerability to a problem.
• Not a valid nursing diagnosis format — It IS a valid format — "Risk for [problem] related to [risk factor]" is the standard risk diagnosis.

⭐ Key point: "Risk for…" = RISK diagnosis (vulnerability, no signs yet). Actual = problem present with defining characteristics.$x$, null),
(162, $x$A nursing audit can help to identify:$x$, $x$Trends in patient care that may need to be addressed$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Trends in patient care that may need to be addressed
A NURSING AUDIT is a systematic review of nursing care, usually by examining records (retrospective audit) or observing care as it happens (concurrent audit), and comparing it against set STANDARDS. It helps to: identify TRENDS and PATTERNS in patient care (e.g. rising pressure ulcer rates, missed drug doses, poor documentation of pain), find areas of good practice and areas needing improvement, guide staff education, improve quality and safety, and check compliance with policies. It is a QUALITY IMPROVEMENT tool, not a way to judge individuals or finances.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The nurse who is the "best" at their job — An audit does not rank nurses to find the "best" one; it looks at the quality of care in the system.
• The single cause of all patient complications — Complications usually have several causes; an audit identifies patterns and contributing factors, not a "single cause of all" complications.
• The hospital's financial status — Hospital FINANCIAL status is assessed by financial audits/accounts, not nursing audit.

⭐ Key point: Nursing audit = compare care with standards → identify TRENDS and areas for improvement in patient care.$x$, null),
(163, $x$Which of the following is the most accurate definition of research?$x$, $x$A systematic, rigorous and logical process of inquiry that seeks to answer questions and generate new knowledge$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A systematic, rigorous and logical process of inquiry that seeks to answer questions and generate new knowledge
RESEARCH is a SYSTEMATIC, RIGOROUS and LOGICAL process of inquiry that seeks to ANSWER QUESTIONS, solve problems and GENERATE NEW KNOWLEDGE (or validate existing knowledge). "Systematic" means it follows organised steps: identify a problem → review the literature → state objectives/hypotheses → choose a design → sample → collect data → analyse → interpret → report/disseminate. "Rigorous" means it uses careful, valid and reliable methods to reduce bias. In nursing, research provides the evidence for evidence-based practice (EBP).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The act of reading widely around a clinical topic before writing a report — Reading widely before writing a report is a LITERATURE REVIEW or study — only one step of research, not research itself.
• Any opinion a nurse forms after observing a patient once — An OPINION formed after observing a patient once is not systematic and is open to bias.
• A collection of anecdotes gathered from colleagues about a clinical practice — ANECDOTES from colleagues are informal stories, not systematic inquiry.

⭐ Key point: Research = SYSTEMATIC, RIGOROUS, LOGICAL inquiry to answer questions and generate new knowledge.$x$, null),
(164, $x$Serum potassium level measured in mmol/L is an example of which scale of measurement?$x$, $x$Ratio$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Ratio
There are four LEVELS (scales) OF MEASUREMENT — memory aid "NOIR": NOMINAL — named categories with no order (blood group, gender, tribe); ORDINAL — ordered categories but unequal gaps (pain mild/moderate/severe, GCS, education level, Likert scale); INTERVAL — equal intervals but NO TRUE ZERO (temperature in °C or °F — 0 °C does not mean "no temperature"; you cannot say 40 °C is twice as hot as 20 °C); RATIO — equal intervals AND a TRUE ZERO (zero means none of it), so ratios make sense: weight, height, age, pulse, BP, urine output, SERUM POTASSIUM in mmol/L (0 mmol/L = no potassium; 6 mmol/L is twice 3 mmol/L). Ratio is the highest level and allows all statistics.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nominal — NOMINAL is only labels/categories — potassium is a measured number.
• Ordinal — ORDINAL shows rank order only — potassium has equal, exact intervals.
• Interval — INTERVAL has no true zero — potassium has a true zero.

⭐ Key point: NOIR: Nominal (names), Ordinal (order), Interval (no true zero, e.g. °C), RATIO (true zero — weight, K⁺ in mmol/L).$x$, null),
(165, $x$The Latin term 'Op cit' is used when:$x$, $x$A source already cited is referenced again, with other citations in between$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A source already cited is referenced again, with other citations in between
Latin referencing terms (used in footnote/endnote styles like Oxford/Chicago): IBID. (ibidem, "in the same place") — the source is the SAME as the IMMEDIATELY preceding citation, with nothing in between. OP. CIT. (opere citato, "in the work cited") — refers to a source ALREADY CITED earlier, but with OTHER CITATIONS IN BETWEEN; you give the author's surname plus "op. cit." and the page number. LOC. CIT. (loco citato, "in the place cited") — the same source AND the same page as an earlier citation, with others in between. Et al. — "and others" (multiple authors).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Citing a source for the very first time — Citing a source for the FIRST time requires the FULL reference, not op. cit.
• No source is being cited at all — If no source is cited, no Latin abbreviation is used at all.
• Citing an unpublished thesis only — Op. cit. is not specific to unpublished theses; it can refer to any previously cited work.

⭐ Key point: IBID = same source as the one just before. OP. CIT. = source cited earlier with OTHER citations in between. Loc. cit. = same page.$x$, null),
(166, $x$A researcher wants to test whether a new positioning technique reduces pressure ulcer incidence. The dependent variable in this study would be:$x$, $x$Pressure ulcer incidence, since it is the outcome being measured$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Pressure ulcer incidence, since it is the outcome being measured
In an experiment, the INDEPENDENT VARIABLE (IV) is what the researcher CHANGES or applies — the "cause" or intervention (here, the new POSITIONING TECHNIQUE). The DEPENDENT VARIABLE (DV) is the OUTCOME the researcher MEASURES to see if it changes because of the IV — the "effect". Here the outcome being measured is PRESSURE ULCER INCIDENCE, so it is the dependent variable (it "depends on" the positioning technique). Memory aid: "The DV Depends on the IV." Other factors that might affect the outcome (age, nutrition, mobility, the researcher's experience) are EXTRANEOUS/CONFOUNDING variables to be controlled.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The positioning technique used — The POSITIONING TECHNIQUE is the INDEPENDENT variable (the intervention).
• The patient's age — The patient's AGE is a possible confounding/extraneous variable, not the outcome.
• The researcher's experience level — The researcher's EXPERIENCE is an extraneous variable that could bias results, not the dependent variable.

⭐ Key point: Independent variable = intervention (positioning). DEPENDENT variable = measured OUTCOME (pressure ulcer incidence).$x$, null),
(167, $x$Vulnerable research subjects include all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Healthy working adults$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Healthy working adults
VULNERABLE research subjects are people who may have a reduced ability to give free, informed CONSENT or who are at higher risk of being exploited or harmed. They need extra ethical protection (e.g. parental consent plus child assent, independent advocates, extra committee review). Groups include: CHILDREN (minors), PRISONERS (may feel pressured, limited freedom), people with PHYSICAL or MENTAL disabilities, pregnant women and foetuses, the critically ill or unconscious, the elderly with dementia, the very poor or uneducated, students and employees of the researcher. HEALTHY WORKING ADULTS with full capacity can give free informed consent, so they are NOT considered a vulnerable group.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Children — CHILDREN ARE vulnerable — they cannot legally consent for themselves.
• Prisoners — PRISONERS ARE vulnerable — their freedom of choice may be limited.
• The physically/mentally challenged — The physically/mentally CHALLENGED ARE vulnerable — capacity to consent or ability to refuse may be reduced.

⭐ Key point: Vulnerable subjects: children, prisoners, mentally/physically challenged, pregnant women, critically ill. Healthy adults are NOT.$x$, null),
(168, $x$Which is NOT a probability sampling method?$x$, $x$Convenience sampling$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Convenience sampling
PROBABILITY sampling means every member of the population has a KNOWN, non-zero chance of being selected (random selection), so results can be generalised. Types: SIMPLE RANDOM (lottery, random number table), SYSTEMATIC (every k-th person from a list), STRATIFIED RANDOM (divide into groups/strata, then random sample from each), CLUSTER (randomly select whole groups, e.g. wards or villages), multistage. NON-PROBABILITY sampling does not use random selection: CONVENIENCE (accidental — whoever is easily available), purposive (judgement), quota, snowball. Convenience sampling is common in nursing research but has a high risk of selection bias.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple random sampling — SIMPLE RANDOM sampling IS probability sampling — everyone has an equal chance.
• Cluster sampling — CLUSTER sampling IS probability sampling — clusters are randomly selected.
• Stratified random sampling — STRATIFIED RANDOM sampling IS probability sampling — random selection within strata.

⭐ Key point: Probability = random (simple, systematic, stratified, cluster). CONVENIENCE, purposive, quota, snowball = NON-probability.$x$, null),
(169, $x$A nurse wants to implement a new evidence-based practice change on the unit based on a research finding. The most appropriate first step is to:$x$, $x$Critically appraise the evidence, involve relevant stakeholders, and consider a structured pilot with evaluation before wider rollout$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Critically appraise the evidence, involve relevant stakeholders, and consider a structured pilot with evaluation before wider rollout
EVIDENCE-BASED PRACTICE (EBP) combines the BEST research evidence, CLINICAL EXPERTISE and PATIENT PREFERENCES. Before changing practice, the nurse should follow a structured process (e.g. Iowa model, ACE Star): ask a clear question (PICO), search for evidence, CRITICALLY APPRAISE it (is it valid, reliable, relevant to our patients?), involve STAKEHOLDERS (nurse manager, doctors, pharmacists, infection control, staff who will use it), get approval, run a small PILOT with clear outcome measures, EVALUATE the results, and then roll out more widely with training and ongoing audit. This makes the change safe, accepted and sustainable.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Implement the change immediately unit-wide with no evaluation — Implementing immediately unit-wide with no evaluation risks harm if the evidence does not fit your setting, and staff may resist.
• Ignore the research since current practice has always worked — Ignoring research because "it has always worked" is the opposite of EBP and blocks improvement.
• Implement changes based on personal opinion alone, without reference to evidence — Changes based on PERSONAL OPINION alone, without evidence, are not evidence-based and may be unsafe.

⭐ Key point: EBP change: appraise evidence → involve stakeholders → PILOT and evaluate → then roll out.$x$, null),
(170, $x$In the PICO framework for developing a clinical research question, the letters stand for:$x$, $x$Population, Intervention, Comparison, Outcome$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Population, Intervention, Comparison, Outcome
PICO is a framework to write a focused, answerable CLINICAL QUESTION and guide the literature search. P — POPULATION/Patient/Problem (who? e.g. ventilated adults in ICU); I — INTERVENTION (what is being done? e.g. chlorhexidine oral care); C — COMPARISON (compared with what? e.g. saline oral care, or usual care); O — OUTCOME (what result? e.g. ventilator-associated pneumonia rate). Example: "In ventilated ICU adults (P), does chlorhexidine oral care (I) compared with saline (C) reduce VAP (O)?" Some versions add T — TIME (PICOT).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Problem, Investigation, Cost, Objective — "Problem, Investigation, Cost, Objective" is not the PICO framework.
• Patient, Illness, Cure, Observation — "Patient, Illness, Cure, Observation" is not correct — I = Intervention, C = Comparison, O = Outcome.
• Plan, Implement, Coordinate, Overview — "Plan, Implement, Coordinate, Overview" are management words, not PICO.

⭐ Key point: PICO = Population, Intervention, Comparison, Outcome (+ Time = PICOT).$x$, null),
(171, $x$The first page of the research report is$x$, $x$Title Page$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Title Page
A research report is arranged in three parts. FRONT (preliminary) MATTER — TITLE PAGE (FIRST page: title, author, institution, date/degree), certification/approval page, dedication, acknowledgements, ABSTRACT, table of contents, lists of tables and figures, abbreviations. MAIN BODY — Chapter 1 Introduction, Chapter 2 Literature review, Chapter 3 Methodology, Chapter 4 Results, Chapter 5 Discussion, Conclusion and Recommendations. BACK MATTER — REFERENCES/BIBLIOGRAPHY, APPENDICES (questionnaire, consent form, ethical approval letter), and sometimes an INDEX. So the first page is the TITLE PAGE.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Appendix — The APPENDIX is in the back matter, at the end.
• Bibliography — The BIBLIOGRAPHY/references are in the back matter, at the end.
• Index — An INDEX, if present, is at the very end of a report or book.

⭐ Key point: Research report: FRONT matter (TITLE PAGE first, abstract, contents) → main body → BACK matter (references, appendices).$x$, null),
(172, $x$The science of collectiong, organizing, analyzing, interpreting and presenting data to asist in making more effective decisions is known as:$x$, $x$Statistics.$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Statistics.
STATISTICS is the science of COLLECTING, ORGANISING, ANALYSING, INTERPRETING and PRESENTING DATA to help make better decisions. It has two main branches: DESCRIPTIVE statistics — summarise data (mean, median, mode, standard deviation, percentages, tables, charts); INFERENTIAL statistics — use data from a SAMPLE to draw conclusions about a POPULATION (t-test, chi-square, correlation, p-values, confidence intervals). In nursing, statistics is used in research, audit, infection rates, and evidence-based decision-making.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Data — DATA are the raw facts and figures that statistics works on — not the science itself.
• Inference — INFERENCE is the process of drawing conclusions about a population from a sample — one part of statistics.
• Sample — A SAMPLE is a subset of the population chosen for study — not the science of analysing data.

⭐ Key point: STATISTICS = collecting, organising, analysing, interpreting and presenting data. Descriptive + inferential.$x$, null),
(173, $x$The 'back matter' of a research report typically includes:$x$, $x$Reference, Bibliography, and Appendix$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Reference, Bibliography, and Appendix
The BACK MATTER of a research report is the material that comes AFTER the main text (after the conclusion and recommendations). It typically includes: REFERENCES (works cited in the text), BIBLIOGRAPHY (works consulted, whether cited or not), and APPENDICES/APPENDIX (supporting documents such as the questionnaire, consent forms, ethical approval letter, raw data tables, maps), and sometimes a glossary or index. The FRONT matter is the title page, certification, dedication, acknowledgements, abstract and table of contents. The MAIN BODY holds introduction, literature review, methodology, results and discussion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only the title page — The TITLE PAGE is FRONT matter (the first page).
• The introduction and literature review — The INTRODUCTION and LITERATURE REVIEW are part of the MAIN BODY (chapters 1 and 2).
• The abstract only — The ABSTRACT is FRONT matter, placed near the beginning.

⭐ Key point: Back matter = REFERENCES, BIBLIOGRAPHY, APPENDIX. Front matter = title page, abstract, contents.$x$, null),
(174, $x$The '5 Rights of Delegation' include right task, right circumstance, right person, right direction/communication, and:$x$, $x$Right supervision/evaluation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Right supervision/evaluation
The FIVE RIGHTS OF DELEGATION (NCSBN/ANA) help the RN delegate safely: (1) RIGHT TASK — a task that can be delegated (routine, stable, predictable outcome; not assessment, planning, evaluation or teaching); (2) RIGHT CIRCUMSTANCE — the patient is stable and the setting and resources are appropriate; (3) RIGHT PERSON — the delegate has the training, competence and legal scope to perform it; (4) RIGHT DIRECTION/COMMUNICATION — clear instructions: what to do, when, limits, what to report back; (5) RIGHT SUPERVISION/EVALUATION — the RN monitors, gives feedback, evaluates the outcome and intervenes if needed. The RN remains ACCOUNTABLE for the delegated task.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Right documentation only — RIGHT DOCUMENTATION is important but is not one of the five rights.
• Right payment — RIGHT PAYMENT has nothing to do with delegation.
• Right uniform — RIGHT UNIFORM is not a delegation principle.

⭐ Key point: 5 Rights of Delegation: Task, Circumstance, Person, Direction/Communication, SUPERVISION/EVALUATION. RN stays accountable.$x$, null),
(175, $x$According to the Thomas-Kilmann model, the conflict resolution style with both high assertiveness and high cooperation is:$x$, $x$Collaborating$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Collaborating
The THOMAS–KILMANN model describes five conflict-handling styles using two dimensions: ASSERTIVENESS (concern for your own needs) and COOPERATIVENESS (concern for the other person's needs). COMPETING — high assertive, low cooperative ("win–lose"). ACCOMMODATING — low assertive, high cooperative (giving in, "lose–win"). AVOIDING — low assertive, low cooperative (withdrawing, "lose–lose"). COMPROMISING — medium on both (each gives something). COLLABORATING — HIGH assertive AND HIGH cooperative: both parties work together to find a solution that fully meets everyone's needs ("WIN–WIN"). Collaboration takes more time but gives the best long-term outcome and team relationships.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Competing — COMPETING is high assertiveness but LOW cooperation.
• Avoiding — AVOIDING is LOW on both assertiveness and cooperation.
• Accommodating — ACCOMMODATING is high cooperation but LOW assertiveness.

⭐ Key point: Thomas–Kilmann: COLLABORATING = high assertiveness + high cooperation = WIN–WIN.$x$, null),
(176, $x$The optimal approach to leadership (Managerial Grid) shows:$x$, $x$High concern for both production and employees$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: High concern for both production and employees
The BLAKE AND MOUTON MANAGERIAL (LEADERSHIP) GRID plots leadership on two axes from 1 to 9: CONCERN FOR PRODUCTION (results, tasks) and CONCERN FOR PEOPLE (employees). Styles: 1,1 IMPOVERISHED (low on both — does the minimum); 9,1 AUTHORITY-COMPLIANCE/task (high production, low people); 1,9 COUNTRY CLUB (high people, low production — friendly but little work done); 5,5 MIDDLE-OF-THE-ROAD (balance, average results); 9,9 TEAM MANAGEMENT (HIGH concern for BOTH production AND people). Blake and Mouton considered 9,9 TEAM management the OPTIMAL style: committed staff, trust and respect, and high performance.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• High concern for production only — High concern for production ONLY is the authority-compliance (9,1) style — results with poor morale.
• Low concern for production — LOW concern for production describes impoverished or country club styles.
• High concern for employees only — High concern for employees ONLY is the country club (1,9) style.

⭐ Key point: Managerial Grid optimal = 9,9 TEAM MANAGEMENT — high concern for BOTH production AND people.$x$, null),
(177, $x$If a nurse witnesses another staff member committing a serious, unsafe practice violation, the appropriate ethical action is to:$x$, $x$Report it through appropriate institutional and/or regulatory channels$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Report it through appropriate institutional and/or regulatory channels
Nurses have an ethical and professional duty to protect patients (beneficence, non-maleficence; NMCN Code of Professional Conduct). If a nurse WITNESSES a colleague doing something seriously UNSAFE (e.g. working while impaired, falsifying records, giving wrong drugs on purpose, abusing patients), they must first ensure the PATIENT IS SAFE, then REPORT it through the proper INSTITUTIONAL channels (nurse manager, chain of command, incident reporting system) and, when needed, to the REGULATORY body. Reporting should be factual, documented, and made in good faith; many institutions have whistle-blowing policies to protect the reporter.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ignore it to avoid conflict — IGNORING it to avoid conflict allows patients to be harmed and makes the witness partly responsible.
• Only discuss it informally with friends — Only discussing it INFORMALLY with friends does not protect patients and may be gossip/breach of confidentiality.
• Confront the colleague publicly in front of patients — Confronting the colleague PUBLICLY in front of patients is unprofessional, causes distress and does not follow proper process.

⭐ Key point: Serious unsafe practice → keep the patient safe, then REPORT through institutional and/or regulatory channels.$x$, null),
(178, $x$The following are part of Fayol's 14 principles EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Planning$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Planning
HENRI FAYOL (father of modern management) described 14 PRINCIPLES of management: (1) DIVISION OF WORK/labour, (2) authority and responsibility, (3) discipline, (4) unity of command, (5) unity of direction, (6) subordination of individual interest to the general interest, (7) remuneration, (8) centralisation, (9) SCALAR CHAIN (line of authority from top to bottom), (10) ORDER (right person and material in the right place), (11) equity, (12) stability of tenure of personnel, (13) initiative, (14) esprit de corps (team spirit). PLANNING is one of Fayol's five FUNCTIONS of management (POCCC: Planning, Organising, Commanding, Coordinating, Controlling) — not one of the 14 principles.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Division of labour — DIVISION OF LABOUR IS one of the 14 principles.
• Scalar chain — SCALAR CHAIN IS one of the 14 principles.
• Order — ORDER IS one of the 14 principles.

⭐ Key point: Fayol's 14 PRINCIPLES include division of work, scalar chain, order, unity of command. PLANNING is a FUNCTION (POCCC).$x$, null),
(179, $x$Which task should NOT be delegated to a nursing assistant?$x$, $x$Assessment of pain$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Assessment of pain
Registered nurses can delegate ROUTINE, predictable tasks for STABLE patients to nursing assistants/unregulated care staff — e.g. BED MAKING, FEEDING a stable patient with no swallowing problem, TRANSPORTING stable patients, bathing, measuring and recording vital signs, ambulating. But the RN must NOT delegate the core nursing process steps that need professional judgement: ASSESSMENT (including PAIN ASSESSMENT), planning, nursing diagnosis, evaluation, health teaching, and care of unstable patients. An assistant can REPORT that a patient says they have pain, but the RN must ASSESS the pain (location, severity, character, response to treatment) and decide on action.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bed making — BED MAKING is a routine task and CAN be delegated.
• Feeding a stable patient — FEEDING a STABLE patient CAN be delegated (not one with dysphagia or aspiration risk).
• Transporting stable patients — TRANSPORTING STABLE patients CAN be delegated.

⭐ Key point: Never delegate ASSESSMENT (including pain), planning, evaluation, teaching, or unstable patients. Routine tasks can be delegated.$x$, null),
(180, $x$A Chief Nurse Executive has a charismatic leadership style. Which of the following behaviors best describes this style?$x$, $x$Possesses inspirational quality that makes followers get attracted to him and regard him with reverence.$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Possesses inspirational quality that makes followers get attracted to him and regard him with reverence.
CHARISMATIC LEADERSHIP is based on the leader's personal qualities — charm, confidence, energy and an INSPIRING personality. Followers are emotionally ATTRACTED to the leader, admire and trust them, often with deep respect or REVERENCE, and are willing to follow because of who the leader is (personal power), not because of position or rewards. Strengths: inspires commitment during change or crisis. Risks: the team may depend too much on one person, and followers may not question poor decisions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uses visioning as the core of his leadership. — Using VISIONING as the core of leadership describes TRANSFORMATIONAL leadership (creating and sharing a vision).
• Acts as he does because he expects that his harshness will yield positive results. — Being harsh because harshness is expected to give results describes an AUTOCRATIC/authoritarian approach, not charismatic.
• Acts as he does because he expects that his behavior will yield positive results. — Acting because the behaviour is expected to yield positive results is a general/expectancy-type description, not specific to charisma.

⭐ Key point: CHARISMATIC leader = inspiring personal qualities that attract followers and earn their reverence. Visioning = transformational.$x$, null),
(181, $x$A new nurse on the unit repeatedly makes documentation errors despite initial teaching. The nurse manager's most constructive approach is to:$x$, $x$Provide targeted, private feedback, additional focused training, and follow institutional progressive improvement processes$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Provide targeted, private feedback, additional focused training, and follow institutional progressive improvement processes
When a new nurse keeps making DOCUMENTATION ERRORS despite initial teaching, the manager should take a SUPPORTIVE, CONSTRUCTIVE approach: meet PRIVATELY, give specific, factual FEEDBACK (what the errors are and why they matter for patient safety and legal records), find the cause (understanding, workload, the EHR system, stress), give FOCUSED TRAINING or a preceptor/mentor, set clear expectations and a time-bound improvement plan, monitor progress, and document the meetings. If problems continue, follow the institution's PROGRESSIVE (graduated) discipline/improvement process. This respects the nurse, protects patients, and gives a fair chance to improve.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Publicly criticise the nurse in front of colleagues — PUBLIC criticism humiliates the nurse, damages teamwork and morale, and is unprofessional.
• Terminate employment immediately without any improvement plan — IMMEDIATE termination with no improvement plan is unfair and usually breaks institutional policy and labour law.
• Ignore the errors to avoid conflict — IGNORING the errors is unsafe — poor documentation harms patient care and creates legal risk.

⭐ Key point: Repeated errors → PRIVATE feedback + focused training + improvement plan + follow progressive policy.$x$, null),
(182, $x$Who was the first to systematically describe management behaviour and pioneer the identification of the principles and skills that underlie effective management?$x$, $x$Henri Fayol$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Henri Fayol
HENRI FAYOL (1841–1925), a French mining engineer and director, is called the "FATHER OF MODERN MANAGEMENT" (administrative/classical management theory). He was the first to SYSTEMATICALLY describe management behaviour and to identify the PRINCIPLES and SKILLS of effective management. He described the five functions of management (planning, organising, commanding, coordinating, controlling — POCCC) and the 14 principles of management (division of work, authority, discipline, unity of command, scalar chain, etc.), in his book "General and Industrial Management" (1916).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Max Weber — MAX WEBER described BUREAUCRACY (rules, hierarchy, impersonal authority).
• Frederick Taylor — FREDERICK TAYLOR is the father of SCIENTIFIC MANAGEMENT (time-and-motion studies, efficiency of workers' tasks).
• Peter Drucker — PETER DRUCKER is a later management writer (management by objectives), not the first.

⭐ Key point: FAYOL = father of modern management (14 principles, POCCC). Taylor = scientific management. Weber = bureaucracy.$x$, null),
(183, $x$The following are examples of motivation theories EXCEPT$x$, $x$System theory$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: System theory
MOTIVATION THEORIES explain what drives people to act and work. Examples: MASLOW'S HIERARCHY OF NEEDS (physiological → safety → love/belonging → esteem → self-actualisation); HERZBERG'S TWO-FACTOR (motivation–hygiene) theory (hygiene factors like salary and working conditions prevent dissatisfaction; motivators like achievement and recognition increase satisfaction); VROOM'S EXPECTANCY THEORY (people are motivated if they believe effort → performance → valued reward); McClelland's needs theory; McGregor's Theory X and Y; equity theory. SYSTEMS THEORY (von Bertalanffy) is a general theory that sees an organisation as a system of interrelated parts with inputs, throughput, outputs and feedback — it is not a motivation theory.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Maslow theory of basic human need — MASLOW's theory IS a motivation theory (needs hierarchy).
• Expectancy theory — EXPECTANCY theory IS a motivation theory (Vroom).
• Hertzberg theory — HERZBERG's theory IS a motivation theory (two-factor).

⭐ Key point: Motivation theories: Maslow, Herzberg, Vroom (expectancy), McClelland, McGregor. SYSTEMS theory is NOT one.$x$, null),
(184, $x$Alarm fatigue in a technology-dense ICU environment refers to:$x$, $x$Desensitisation to alarms from excessive frequency, leading to delayed or missed responses$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Desensitisation to alarms from excessive frequency, leading to delayed or missed responses
ALARM FATIGUE happens when staff are exposed to so many alarms — many of them false, non-actionable or nuisance alarms (e.g. a loose ECG lead, SpO2 probe movement, limits set too tight) — that they become DESENSITISED. As a result, alarms are ignored, silenced, turned down or turned off, and response is DELAYED or MISSED, sometimes for a real emergency. It is a recognised patient safety hazard. Prevention: customise alarm limits to each patient, change ECG electrodes daily, use correct probes, remove unnecessary monitoring, use smart/delayed alarms, ensure alarms are audible, and educate staff.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Improved responsiveness to all alarms — Alarm fatigue REDUCES responsiveness — it does not improve it.
• A malfunction unique to ventilators — It is not a machine malfunction and is not unique to ventilators — it affects all monitors and pumps.
• A benefit of increased monitoring — It is a HARMFUL effect of excessive monitoring alarms, not a benefit.

⭐ Key point: Alarm fatigue = desensitisation from too many (often false) alarms → delayed/missed responses. Customise alarm limits.$x$, null),
(185, $x$Which ethical framework emphasizes caring as both an emotion and an action that guides moral decision making?$x$, $x$Care ethics$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Care ethics
CARE ETHICS (ethics of care; Carol Gilligan, Nel Noddings) focuses on RELATIONSHIPS, compassion and responsibility to others. It sees CARING as both an EMOTION (concern, empathy) and an ACTION (doing things to meet the needs of the cared-for person), and uses this caring relationship to guide moral decisions — paying attention to the particular person and situation rather than only to abstract rules. It fits naturally with nursing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Deontology — DEONTOLOGY (Kant) bases decisions on DUTIES and RULES — some acts are right or wrong regardless of consequences.
• Utilitarianism — UTILITARIANISM bases decisions on CONSEQUENCES — the greatest good for the greatest number.
• Virtue ethics — VIRTUE ETHICS (Aristotle) focuses on good CHARACTER traits (honesty, courage, compassion) of the moral agent, not specifically caring as emotion and action.

⭐ Key point: CARE ETHICS = caring (emotion + action) and relationships guide moral decisions. Deontology = duty; utilitarianism = consequences.$x$, null),
(186, $x$The role of a national nursing regulatory council (e.g. NMCN) primarily includes:$x$, $x$Setting and enforcing standards of practice, registering/licensing nurses, and handling professional misconduct$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Setting and enforcing standards of practice, registering/licensing nurses, and handling professional misconduct
The NURSING AND MIDWIFERY COUNCIL OF NIGERIA (NMCN), like other national nursing regulatory bodies, exists to PROTECT THE PUBLIC by regulating the profession. Its main roles: setting STANDARDS of nursing and midwifery education and PRACTICE; approving and accrediting training schools and curricula; conducting licensing examinations; REGISTERING and LICENSING nurses and midwives (and renewal of licences with continuing education); maintaining the register; publishing the Code of Professional Conduct; and investigating and disciplining PROFESSIONAL MISCONDUCT (through tribunals that can suspend or remove nurses from the register).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only organising social events for nurses — Organising SOCIAL events is not the council's role (professional associations like NANNM may organise events).
• Setting hospital drug prices — Setting hospital DRUG PRICES is not done by the nursing council.
• Managing hospital construction projects — Managing hospital CONSTRUCTION is not the council's role.

⭐ Key point: NMCN = set and enforce standards, accredit schools, register/license nurses, handle MISCONDUCT — to protect the public.$x$, null),
(187, $x$Which of the following is NOT a performance appraisal factor?$x$, $x$Number of social media handles$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Number of social media handles
PERFORMANCE APPRAISAL is the formal evaluation of how well an employee does their job, compared with job descriptions and set standards. Common factors assessed: ATTENDANCE and punctuality, ATTITUDE (towards patients, colleagues, work), QUALITY of work, quantity/productivity, clinical knowledge and skills, communication, teamwork, initiative, reliability, adherence to policies, professional development. The NUMBER OF SOCIAL MEDIA HANDLES (accounts) a nurse has has nothing to do with job performance and would be irrelevant and unfair to assess.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Attendance — ATTENDANCE IS a performance appraisal factor.
• Attitude — ATTITUDE IS a performance appraisal factor.
• Quality of work — QUALITY OF WORK IS a core performance appraisal factor.

⭐ Key point: Appraisal factors: attendance, attitude, quality of work, skills, teamwork, communication — not social media handles.$x$, null),
(188, $x$A nurse experiences moral distress when a terminally ill patient receives aggressive CPR. This arises from:$x$, $x$Personal values conflict$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Personal values conflict
MORAL DISTRESS occurs when a nurse KNOWS what they believe is the ethically right action but feels unable to carry it out because of constraints (hospital policy, family demands, doctor's orders, hierarchy). It arises from a CONFLICT between the nurse's PERSONAL/professional VALUES and what they are required to do. Example: giving aggressive CPR to a terminally ill patient whom the nurse believes should be allowed to die peacefully. Effects: frustration, guilt, burnout and leaving the profession. Help: ethics consultation, clear DNACPR and end-of-life discussions, debriefing and team support.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ethical dilemma — An ETHICAL DILEMMA is a situation with two or more morally acceptable options where the right choice is unclear; in moral distress the nurse knows what is right but is prevented from doing it.
• Legal obligation — A LEGAL OBLIGATION (e.g. to perform CPR without a DNACPR order) may be the constraint, but the distress itself arises from values conflict.
• Inadequate staffing — INADEQUATE STAFFING may cause stress, but it is not the source of distress in this scenario.

⭐ Key point: Moral distress = knowing the right action but being constrained → conflict with PERSONAL VALUES. Dilemma = unclear right choice.$x$, null),
(189, $x$Distributive Justice, as a concept, is most associated with the philosopher:$x$, $x$John Rawls$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: John Rawls
DISTRIBUTIVE JUSTICE concerns how society's benefits and burdens (including health care resources) are SHARED fairly. It is most associated with the American philosopher JOHN RAWLS (A Theory of Justice, 1971). Rawls proposed the "veil of ignorance" — choosing rules without knowing your own position in society — and the "difference principle": inequalities are only fair if they benefit the LEAST ADVANTAGED. In health care, this guides fair allocation of scarce resources (ICU beds, organs, ventilators, drugs) and equal access to care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Aristotle — ARISTOTLE wrote about justice (treat equals equally) and virtue ethics, but distributive justice as a modern concept is linked most with Rawls.
• Sigmund Freud — SIGMUND FREUD was a psychoanalyst, not a philosopher of justice.
• Florence Nightingale — FLORENCE NIGHTINGALE was the founder of modern nursing, not a justice philosopher.

⭐ Key point: Distributive justice = fair sharing of resources — JOHN RAWLS (veil of ignorance, difference principle).$x$, null),
(190, $x$During a critical patient handover between shifts, using the SBAR (Situation-Background-Assessment-Recommendation) framework primarily helps to:$x$, $x$Structure communication clearly and concisely, reducing the risk of missed or miscommunicated critical information$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Structure communication clearly and concisely, reducing the risk of missed or miscommunicated critical information
SBAR is a structured communication tool: S — SITUATION (what is happening now: "Mr A, bed 4, BP has fallen to 80/50"); B — BACKGROUND (relevant history, diagnosis, treatment); A — ASSESSMENT (what the nurse thinks the problem is: "I think he is going into septic shock"); R — RECOMMENDATION/request (what action is needed: "Please come and review him in the next 10 minutes; should I start fluids?"). It makes handover and escalation CLEAR, CONCISE and complete, reduces the chance of MISSED or MISCOMMUNICATED information, and helps junior staff speak up with confidence. It is used for nurse-to-nurse handovers, nurse-to-doctor calls and inter-department transfers.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Make handover longer and more complicated — SBAR makes handover SHORTER and simpler, not longer and more complicated.
• Replace the need for any written documentation — It does NOT replace written documentation; both are needed.
• Only apply to nurse-to-nurse communication, never nurse-to-physician — It is used across ALL professions, including nurse-to-physician communication (it was designed partly for this).

⭐ Key point: SBAR = Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation → clear, concise handover and escalation.$x$, null),
(191, $x$The ethical principle of 'justice' in healthcare primarily concerns:$x$, $x$Fair and equitable distribution of healthcare resources and treatment$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fair and equitable distribution of healthcare resources and treatment
The four main principles of healthcare ethics: AUTONOMY (respect the patient's right to decide), BENEFICENCE (do good), NON-MALEFICENCE (do no harm) and JUSTICE. JUSTICE means FAIRNESS — treating people fairly and distributing health care RESOURCES and treatment EQUITABLY, based on need and not on wealth, tribe, religion, gender or status. Examples: triage by clinical urgency, fair allocation of ICU beds, equal-quality care for all patients. Other related principles: VERACITY (truth-telling), FIDELITY (keeping promises), confidentiality.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Being truthful with patients — Being TRUTHFUL with patients is VERACITY.
• Avoiding harm to patients — Avoiding harm is NON-MALEFICENCE.
• Keeping promises made to patients — Keeping promises is FIDELITY.

⭐ Key point: JUSTICE = fair, equitable distribution of care and resources. Veracity = truth; non-maleficence = no harm; fidelity = promises.$x$, null),
(192, $x$A Nurse is counselling the family of a client with Road Traffic Accident (RTA). The family is screaming by saying that the client will be recovered or not? The following principles are important in Critical Care Nursing EXCEPT$x$, $x$Stress elevation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Stress elevation
The principles of CRITICAL CARE NURSING include: ANTICIPATION (predicting problems before they happen, e.g. preparing intubation equipment for a deteriorating patient); EARLY DETECTION AND PROMPT ACTION (close monitoring and rapid response to changes); COLLABORATIVE PRACTICE (teamwork with doctors, physiotherapists, pharmacists, dietitians and the family); holistic, patient- and family-centred care, communication and support; continuous education. STRESS ELEVATION (raising stress) is NOT a principle — the opposite is true: nurses aim for STRESS REDUCTION for the patient and family (clear honest information, reassurance, involving them in care), as with this screaming family of an RTA victim.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anticipation — ANTICIPATION IS a critical care principle.
• Early detection and prompt action — EARLY DETECTION AND PROMPT ACTION IS a critical care principle.
• Collaborative practice — COLLABORATIVE PRACTICE IS a critical care principle.

⭐ Key point: Critical care principles: anticipation, early detection + prompt action, collaboration, family support. Aim is stress REDUCTION, not elevation.$x$, null),
(193, $x$A patient's family requests that the patient not be told their terminal diagnosis, though the patient (who has full decision-making capacity) has asked directly about their prognosis. The nurse's most appropriate approach is to:$x$, $x$Respect the competent patient's autonomy and right to information, while sensitively working with the family to understand their concerns$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Respect the competent patient's autonomy and right to information, while sensitively working with the family to understand their concerns
A competent adult patient has the RIGHT TO KNOW about their own diagnosis and prognosis (AUTONOMY and VERACITY/truth-telling). Here the patient has full decision-making capacity and has ASKED DIRECTLY. The nurse (with the doctor responsible for breaking bad news) should RESPECT the patient's request for information, while working SENSITIVELY with the family: listen to their fears and cultural reasons, explain the patient's rights and why honesty helps (planning, saying goodbye, spiritual preparation, consent for treatment), and include them in a supportive conversation if the patient agrees. Information should be given gently, at the patient's pace, using a structured approach such as SPIKES.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Automatically follow the family's wishes over the patient's own request — Automatically following the FAMILY over a competent patient violates the patient's autonomy.
• Refuse to discuss the situation with either party — REFUSING to discuss with either party abandons both the patient and family.
• Tell the patient nothing and avoid the topic indefinitely — Avoiding the topic indefinitely denies the patient's right to information and is dishonest.

⭐ Key point: Competent patient asks → respect AUTONOMY and right to the truth, while supporting the family sensitively (e.g. SPIKES).$x$, null),
(194, $x$Increasing opioids to relieve a dying patient's pain, even if it may hasten death, can be justified under the rule of double effect only if:$x$, $x$All of these conditions are met$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: All of these conditions are met
The PRINCIPLE (RULE) OF DOUBLE EFFECT allows an action with both a GOOD effect and a possible BAD effect (e.g. high-dose opioids relieve pain but MAY hasten death) if ALL FOUR conditions are met: (1) the ACT ITSELF is good or morally neutral (giving pain relief); (2) the INTENTION is only the good effect — to relieve suffering — not to cause death (the bad effect is foreseen but not intended); (3) the bad effect is NOT the MEANS of achieving the good effect (pain is relieved by the drug's analgesic action, not by the patient dying); (4) there is PROPORTIONALITY — the good effect outweighs the bad one (severe suffering at the end of life). If any condition is missing, the act is not justified (e.g. giving a dose intended to kill is euthanasia).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The act itself is good or neutral — "The act itself is good or neutral" is only ONE condition.
• The intention is to relieve suffering, not to kill — "The intention is to relieve suffering" is only ONE condition.
• The good effect outweighs the bad effect, which is not the means to the good — "The good outweighs the bad, which is not the means" covers proportionality and means, but not intention or the nature of the act.

⭐ Key point: Double effect requires ALL: good/neutral act, good intention, bad effect not the means, and good outweighs bad.$x$, null),
(195, $x$Which trace element is a cofactor essential for wound healing, immune function, and taste perception, and is often supplemented in patients with large wounds or burns?$x$, $x$Zinc$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Zinc
ZINC is a trace element essential for WOUND HEALING: it is a cofactor for many enzymes involved in cell division, protein and COLLAGEN synthesis, and tissue repair. It also supports IMMUNE FUNCTION (T-cell function), and TASTE and smell perception (zinc deficiency causes loss of taste — hypogeusia). Patients with large WOUNDS, BURNS, pressure ulcers, diarrhoea/fistulas, or malnutrition lose zinc and often receive supplements (e.g. zinc sulphate). Signs of deficiency: poor wound healing, hair loss, skin rash (around the mouth and anus), diarrhoea, poor appetite and taste, frequent infections. Children with diarrhoea are also given zinc (WHO: ORS + zinc for 10–14 days).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Chromium — CHROMIUM helps insulin action (glucose metabolism), not mainly wound healing and taste.
• Selenium — SELENIUM is an ANTIOXIDANT (glutathione peroxidase) and thyroid hormone cofactor; it does not mainly affect taste.
• Molybdenum — MOLYBDENUM is a cofactor for a few enzymes (e.g. xanthine oxidase); deficiency is very rare.

⭐ Key point: ZINC = wound healing, immunity, TASTE. Supplement in burns, large wounds, diarrhoea.$x$, null),
(196, $x$Which patient is the LEAST appropriate candidate for enteral nutrition and would most likely require TPN instead?$x$, $x$A patient with a high-output enterocutaneous fistula and short bowel syndrome$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A patient with a high-output enterocutaneous fistula and short bowel syndrome
The rule is "IF THE GUT WORKS, USE IT" — enteral nutrition (tube feeding) is preferred whenever the GI tract is functional. TOTAL PARENTERAL NUTRITION (TPN, IV feeding through a central line) is needed when the gut CANNOT be used or cannot absorb enough: e.g. a HIGH-OUTPUT ENTEROCUTANEOUS FISTULA (feeding increases fistula output and nutrients leak out before absorption) and SHORT BOWEL SYNDROME (too little bowel left to absorb nutrients), plus prolonged ileus, complete bowel obstruction, severe GI bleeding, severe mucositis. TPN risks: line infection (sepsis), hyperglycaemia, refeeding syndrome, liver dysfunction, so it requires strict aseptic care and monitoring.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A patient with a functioning gut who is intubated and sedated — An intubated, sedated patient with a FUNCTIONING GUT should receive ENTERAL feeding via NG/OG tube.
• A patient recovering from an uncomplicated appendectomy — After an UNCOMPLICATED appendectomy the patient resumes oral feeding quickly; TPN is not needed.
• A patient with dysphagia after a stroke but an otherwise normal GI tract — DYSPHAGIA after stroke with a normal GI tract → enteral feeding via NG or PEG tube, not TPN.

⭐ Key point: TPN only when the gut cannot be used — high-output fistula, short bowel syndrome, obstruction, prolonged ileus.$x$, null),
(197, $x$Keto-adaptation, which spares muscle during starvation, fails to protect muscle in:$x$, $x$Sepsis and trauma$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Sepsis and trauma
In SIMPLE STARVATION (fasting), the body adapts: insulin falls, glycogen is used up in about 24 hours, and then the liver makes KETONES from fat. After a few days the BRAIN switches to using ketones for fuel (KETO-ADAPTATION), so the body needs less glucose from protein breakdown — this SPARES MUSCLE. In SEPSIS, TRAUMA, burns and major surgery (STRESS/HYPERMETABOLIC states), stress hormones (cortisol, glucagon, catecholamines) and inflammatory cytokines drive continuous muscle protein breakdown, insulin resistance, and blocked ketone production — so keto-adaptation FAILS and MUSCLE WASTING continues rapidly. This is why critically ill patients lose muscle quickly and need early nutrition with adequate protein (1.2–2 g/kg/day).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple fasting — In SIMPLE FASTING keto-adaptation WORKS and protects muscle.
• Healthy individuals on a diet — In HEALTHY people on a diet, keto-adaptation also works normally.
• It never fails — It DOES fail — in sepsis and trauma — so "never fails" is wrong.

⭐ Key point: Keto-adaptation spares muscle in simple starvation but FAILS in SEPSIS and TRAUMA (stress hypermetabolism) → rapid muscle loss.$x$, null),
(198, $x$What is the primary purpose of a nasogastric (NG) tube in critical care?$x$, $x$C) Providing nutrition and medications via the stomach$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: C) Providing nutrition and medications via the stomach
A NASOGASTRIC (NG) TUBE goes through the nose and oesophagus into the STOMACH. In critical care its primary purposes are: (1) ENTERAL NUTRITION (feeding) and giving MEDICATIONS when the patient cannot swallow safely (intubated, sedated, stroke); (2) DECOMPRESSION — draining air and fluid from the stomach in ileus, bowel obstruction, or to reduce vomiting and aspiration risk; (3) gastric lavage (rarely) and sampling of stomach contents. Nursing: confirm position BEFORE first use (X-ray, and pH of aspirate ≤ 5.5), check position before each feed/drug, flush with water, keep the head of bed at 30–45°, prevent pressure injury to the nose.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A) Monitoring cardiac hemodynamics — Monitoring cardiac haemodynamics uses arterial lines, central lines or PA catheters.
• B) Administering pain medication — PAIN MEDICATION can be given through an NG tube, but this is not its main overall purpose (option C covers nutrition AND medications).
• D) Measuring intracranial pressure (ICP) — Measuring ICP needs an ICP monitor (EVD or intraparenchymal probe).

⭐ Key point: NG tube = feeding and medications via the stomach (also decompression). Always confirm position before use (X-ray, pH ≤ 5.5).$x$, null),
(199, $x$Chest wall mobilisation as an airway clearance component works by:$x$, $x$Expanding the thorax and loosening secretions$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Expanding the thorax and loosening secretions
CHEST WALL MOBILISATION exercises combine active movements of the trunk and arms (stretching, rotation, side flexion) with DEEP BREATHING. They EXPAND THE THORAX (increase chest wall and lung expansion and ventilation, especially of the bases), improve flexibility of the ribs and spine after surgery or immobility, help re-open collapsed alveoli, and LOOSEN SECRETIONS so they can be moved up and cleared by coughing or huffing. They are part of chest physiotherapy for patients after thoracic/abdominal surgery, those with pain-limited breathing, and in chronic lung disease.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Directly killing bacteria in the lungs — Physical therapy cannot KILL BACTERIA — antibiotics treat infection.
• Replacing the need for coughing entirely — It does NOT replace coughing; it helps secretions move so that coughing/huffing can clear them.
• Only working on the left lung — It works on BOTH lungs, not only the left lung.

⭐ Key point: Chest wall mobilisation = expands the thorax + loosens secretions, helping coughing clear the airways.$x$, null),
(200, $x$Chest physiotherapy (CPT), as a broader category, includes all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Antibiotic prescription$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Antibiotic prescription
CHEST PHYSIOTHERAPY (CPT) is a group of physical techniques to improve lung function and clear secretions. It includes: AIRWAY CLEARANCE techniques — postural drainage, percussion (clapping), vibration, active cycle of breathing technique, huffing, assisted cough, suctioning; BREATHING EXERCISES — deep breathing, diaphragmatic breathing, incentive spirometry, pursed-lip breathing; THORACIC MOBILITY exercises — chest wall mobilisation and posture work; plus early mobilisation and positioning. It is done by physiotherapists and nurses. ANTIBIOTIC PRESCRIPTION is a medical (drug) treatment for infection — it is not part of chest physiotherapy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Airway clearance techniques — AIRWAY CLEARANCE techniques ARE a central part of CPT.
• Breathing exercises — BREATHING EXERCISES ARE part of CPT.
• Thoracic mobility exercises — THORACIC MOBILITY exercises ARE part of CPT.

⭐ Key point: Chest physiotherapy = airway clearance + breathing exercises + thoracic mobility (+ positioning). Antibiotics are NOT physiotherapy.$x$, null)
),
upd as (
  update public.questions qq
     set exp = r.exp,
         ans_idx = coalesce((select (t.i - 1)::int from jsonb_array_elements_text(qq.opts) with ordinality t(v, i) where t.v = r.fixed_answer), qq.ans_idx)
    from r
   where qq.q = r.q
     and qq.opts ->> qq.ans_idx = r.answer
  returning r.n
)
insert into rationale_report select 2, count(distinct n), count(*) from upd
  on conflict (day) do update set questions_updated = excluded.questions_updated, rows_changed = excluded.rows_changed;

-- ===================== DAY 3 =====================
-- Nursing Exam Room: full rationales for CCN Mock Exam — Day 3 (200 questions)
-- Run this once in the Supabase SQL Editor. It is safe to run again.
--
-- Each explanation now says: the correct answer and why, why each other option is wrong,
-- and a key point to remember. The same questions in the practice app get the new explanation too.
-- Questions are found by their exact wording and correct answer. Where the old answer key was wrong,
-- the correct option is also fixed (listed at the top of the file).


with r(n, q, answer, exp, fixed_answer) as (values
(1, $x$The skin is considered the body's:$x$, $x$Largest organ$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Largest organ
The SKIN is the body's LARGEST ORGAN. In an adult it covers about 1.5–2 square metres and weighs about 3.5–4.5 kg (roughly 15% of body weight). It has three layers — epidermis, dermis and subcutaneous tissue (hypodermis) — and important functions: protection against injury, microorganisms and UV light; prevention of water loss; temperature regulation; sensation (touch, pain, temperature); vitamin D production; and excretion of small amounts of waste in sweat. This is why large burns or skin loss (e.g. toxic epidermal necrolysis) are so dangerous — they cause massive fluid loss, heat loss and infection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Smallest organ — The skin is the largest organ, not the smallest.
• Only avascular organ — The skin is not avascular — the dermis has a rich blood supply (only the epidermis has no blood vessels).
• Most vascular organ — Although well supplied with blood, the skin is not the "most vascular" organ (e.g. the kidneys and liver receive more blood per gram).

⭐ Key point: Skin assessment (colour, temperature, turgor, integrity) is a key part of ICU care — pressure ulcers can develop within hours in critically ill patients.$x$, null),
(2, $x$G cells, which secrete the hormone gastrin, are located predominantly in the:$x$, $x$Pyloric antrum$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Pyloric antrum
GASTRIN is a hormone that stimulates the stomach to secrete acid (via parietal cells) and promotes stomach movement. It is produced mainly by G CELLS in the PYLORIC ANTRUM (the lower part of the stomach near the pylorus), with smaller numbers in the duodenum. G cells release gastrin when food (especially protein) enters the stomach and when the stomach is stretched; release is reduced when the stomach becomes very acidic (negative feedback).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fundus — The fundus mainly contains parietal cells (acid, intrinsic factor) and chief cells (pepsinogen).
• Cardia — The cardia contains mainly mucus-secreting glands.
• Duodenum exclusively — The duodenum does contain some G cells, but not "exclusively" — most are in the antrum.

⭐ Key point: A gastrin-secreting tumour (gastrinoma, Zollinger–Ellison syndrome) causes severe, recurrent peptic ulcers from excess acid.$x$, null),
(3, $x$During deep tendon reflex testing, the patellar (knee) reflex primarily assesses the integrity of spinal cord segments$x$, $x$L2-L4 (primarily L3-L4)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: L2-L4 (primarily L3-L4)
The PATELLAR (knee-jerk) REFLEX is tested by tapping the patellar tendon just below the kneecap, which stretches the quadriceps muscle and causes the leg to kick forward. The reflex travels through the FEMORAL NERVE and spinal cord segments L2–L4, mainly L3–L4. An absent or reduced reflex suggests damage to this pathway (e.g. nerve root compression, peripheral neuropathy, Guillain–Barré syndrome, magnesium toxicity); a brisk reflex suggests an upper motor neurone lesion (e.g. stroke, spinal cord injury above this level).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• C5-C6 — C5–C6 are tested by the biceps and brachioradialis reflexes.
• C7-C8 — C7–C8 are tested by the triceps reflex.
• S1-S2 — S1–S2 are tested by the ankle (Achilles) reflex.

⭐ Key point: In women on magnesium sulphate, loss of the patellar reflex is the first sign of magnesium toxicity.$x$, null),
(4, $x$Which lung volume cannot be measured directly by simple spirometry and requires methods like helium dilution or body plethysmography?$x$, $x$Residual volume$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Residual volume
SIMPLE SPIROMETRY measures air moving IN and OUT of the lungs. RESIDUAL VOLUME (RV) is the air that STAYS in the lungs after a maximal breath out — it never leaves, so a spirometer cannot measure it. It must be measured indirectly using HELIUM DILUTION, nitrogen washout, or BODY PLETHYSMOGRAPHY (sitting in a sealed box). Because RV cannot be measured by spirometry, any capacity that includes it (functional residual capacity, total lung capacity) also cannot be measured by spirometry.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tidal volume — Tidal volume (normal breath) can be measured by spirometry.
• Inspiratory reserve volume — Inspiratory reserve volume (extra air breathed in) can be measured.
• Expiratory reserve volume — Expiratory reserve volume (extra air breathed out) can be measured.

⭐ Key point: Residual volume rises in obstructive diseases (COPD, asthma — air trapping) and falls in restrictive diseases (fibrosis, chest wall problems).$x$, null),
(5, $x$Which of the following is NOT a function of the skin?$x$, $x$Production of red blood cells$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Production of red blood cells
The SKIN has many functions: HEAT REGULATION (sweating, changing blood flow to the skin), PROTECTION against microorganisms, injury, chemicals and UV radiation, VITAMIN D SYNTHESIS (UV light converts a cholesterol derivative in the skin into vitamin D3), sensation, and preventing water loss. PRODUCTION OF RED BLOOD CELLS happens in the BONE MARROW (haematopoiesis), not in the skin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Heat regulation — Heat regulation IS a skin function.
• Protection against microorganisms — Protection against microorganisms IS a skin function.
• Vitamin D synthesis — Vitamin D synthesis IS a skin function.

⭐ Key point: When the skin is badly damaged (e.g. burns), patients lose heat and fluid rapidly and are at high risk of infection — keep them warm and use strict asepsis.$x$, null),
(6, $x$Which of the following is NOT one of the classic hormones secreted by the anterior pituitary?$x$, $x$Antidiuretic hormone (ADH)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Antidiuretic hormone (ADH)
The ANTERIOR PITUITARY gland makes and releases six main hormones: GROWTH HORMONE (GH), THYROID-STIMULATING HORMONE (TSH), ADRENOCORTICOTROPHIC HORMONE (ACTH), FOLLICLE-STIMULATING HORMONE (FSH), LUTEINISING HORMONE (LH) and PROLACTIN. ANTIDIURETIC HORMONE (ADH, vasopressin) is NOT made by the anterior pituitary — it is made in the HYPOTHALAMUS and STORED and RELEASED from the POSTERIOR PITUITARY (along with oxytocin).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) — FSH IS an anterior pituitary hormone.
• Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) — TSH IS an anterior pituitary hormone.
• Growth hormone (GH) — GH IS an anterior pituitary hormone.

⭐ Key point: Damage to the posterior pituitary (e.g. after pituitary surgery or head injury) can cause diabetes insipidus (lack of ADH) with large volumes of dilute urine.$x$, null),
(7, $x$Titratable acidity refers to renal buffering of secreted hydrogen ions mainly by which urinary buffer?$x$, $x$Phosphate (HPO4^2- converting to H2PO4-)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Phosphate (HPO4^2- converting to H2PO4-)
The kidneys get rid of acid by secreting HYDROGEN IONS (H⁺) into the urine. Free H⁺ in urine would make it too acidic, so it must be BUFFERED. "TITRATABLE ACIDITY" is the H⁺ buffered mainly by PHOSPHATE: H⁺ combines with HPO4²⁻ (monohydrogen phosphate) to form H2PO4⁻ (dihydrogen phosphate), which is excreted. Ammonia (NH3 → NH4⁺) is the other main urinary buffer. For each H⁺ excreted, a new bicarbonate is generated and returned to the blood.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bicarbonate — Bicarbonate is reabsorbed and generated by the kidney; it is not the urinary buffer for titratable acidity.
• Hemoglobin — Haemoglobin is a buffer in red blood cells, not urine.
• Albumin — Albumin is a plasma protein buffer, not a urinary buffer.

⭐ Key point: In kidney failure, the kidneys cannot excrete acid, causing metabolic acidosis.$x$, null),
(8, $x$Which structure connects the pharynx to the bronchi, serving as the main airway?$x$, $x$Trachea$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Trachea
The TRACHEA (windpipe) is a tube about 10–12 cm long, supported by C-shaped cartilage rings, that runs from the LARYNX (below the pharynx) down into the chest, where it divides (at the carina) into the RIGHT and LEFT MAIN BRONCHI. It is the MAIN AIRWAY carrying air to and from the lungs. Its lining has cilia and mucus that trap and move particles upwards.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oesophagus — The oesophagus carries food from the pharynx to the stomach.
• Aorta — The aorta is the main artery carrying blood from the heart.
• Ureter — The ureter carries urine from the kidney to the bladder.

⭐ Key point: The right main bronchus is wider, shorter and more vertical than the left — so ET tubes inserted too far and aspirated material usually enter the right side.$x$, null),
(9, $x$Thick skin differs from thin skin by having an extra layer called the:$x$, $x$Stratum lucidum$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Stratum lucidum
THICK SKIN (found on the palms of the hands and soles of the feet) has FIVE layers of epidermis. From deep to surface: stratum basale, stratum spinosum, stratum granulosum, STRATUM LUCIDUM (a clear, thin layer of dead cells that adds extra protection against friction), and stratum corneum. THIN SKIN (most of the body) has only four layers — it LACKS the stratum lucidum.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stratum basale — Stratum basale is present in both thick and thin skin (it produces new cells).
• Stratum corneum — Stratum corneum is present in both.
• Stratum granulosum — Stratum granulosum is present in both.

⭐ Key point: Memory aid (deep to surface): "Come Let's Get Sun Burned" — Corneum, Lucidum, Granulosum, Spinosum, Basale (read from top down).$x$, null),
(10, $x$Gastrointestinal glandular secretions primarily aid in:$x$, $x$Chemical breakdown of food and enzymatic digestion$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Chemical breakdown of food and enzymatic digestion
GASTROINTESTINAL GLANDULAR SECRETIONS — saliva, gastric juice (acid, pepsin), pancreatic juice (amylase, lipase, proteases, bicarbonate), bile, and intestinal enzymes — are mainly for the CHEMICAL BREAKDOWN of food (ENZYMATIC DIGESTION) into small molecules (sugars, amino acids, fatty acids) that can be absorbed. They also lubricate food (mucus), neutralise acid (bicarbonate) and help protect the gut lining.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Producing red blood cells — Red blood cells are produced in the bone marrow.
• Regulating body temperature exclusively — Body temperature is regulated by the hypothalamus, skin and circulation, not GI secretions.
• Filtering blood — Blood is filtered by the kidneys (and the liver and spleen in other ways).

⭐ Key point: Large losses of GI secretions (vomiting, NG drainage, diarrhoea, fistulas) cause dehydration and electrolyte/acid-base disturbances.$x$, null),
(11, $x$What proportion of resting cardiac output goes to the brain?$x$, $x$15%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 15%
Although the BRAIN is only about 2% of body weight, it receives about 15% of the RESTING CARDIAC OUTPUT (about 750 mL/min) and uses about 20% of the body's oxygen. It has no fuel stores, so it depends on a continuous supply of blood, oxygen and glucose. Loss of blood flow causes unconsciousness within seconds and irreversible damage within minutes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 5% — 5% is too low.
• 10% — 10% is too low.
• 20% — 20% is the proportion of the body's OXYGEN consumption, not cardiac output.

⭐ Key point: Brain blood flow depends on cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP = MAP − ICP); maintain MAP and control ICP in brain-injured patients.$x$, null),
(12, $x$The following glial cells are located in the central nervous system EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Schwann cell$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Schwann cell
GLIAL CELLS support and protect neurons. In the CENTRAL NERVOUS SYSTEM (brain and spinal cord): ASTROCYTES (support, blood–brain barrier), OLIGODENDROCYTES (make myelin in the CNS), MICROGLIA (immune cells), and EPENDYMAL cells (line the ventricles and help produce CSF). In the PERIPHERAL NERVOUS SYSTEM: SCHWANN CELLS (make myelin around peripheral nerves) and satellite cells. So the Schwann cell is the exception.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ependymal cell — Ependymal cells ARE CNS glia.
• Astrocyte — Astrocytes ARE CNS glia.
• Microglia — Microglia ARE CNS glia.

⭐ Key point: Guillain–Barré syndrome damages Schwann cell myelin in peripheral nerves; multiple sclerosis damages oligodendrocyte myelin in the CNS.$x$, null),
(13, $x$Fluid movement out of capillaries into the interstitium is favoured by:$x$, $x$High capillary hydrostatic pressure and low plasma oncotic pressure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: High capillary hydrostatic pressure and low plasma oncotic pressure
Fluid movement across capillary walls depends on STARLING FORCES: CAPILLARY HYDROSTATIC PRESSURE (the blood pressure inside capillaries) PUSHES fluid OUT; PLASMA ONCOTIC PRESSURE (from proteins, mainly albumin) PULLS fluid back IN. So fluid moves OUT into the tissues (causing oedema) when HYDROSTATIC pressure is HIGH (e.g. heart failure, fluid overload) and/or ONCOTIC pressure is LOW (e.g. low albumin in liver disease, nephrotic syndrome, malnutrition). Leaky capillaries (sepsis, burns) also increase outflow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Low capillary hydrostatic pressure only — Low hydrostatic pressure alone would reduce fluid leaving the capillaries.
• High plasma oncotic pressure only — High plasma oncotic pressure pulls fluid INTO capillaries.
• Equal hydrostatic and oncotic pressures — Equal pressures would produce little net movement.

⭐ Key point: Oedema mechanisms: high hydrostatic pressure, low oncotic pressure, increased capillary permeability, and lymphatic blockage.$x$, null),
(14, $x$The functional unit of the kidney responsible for filtration is the:$x$, $x$Nephron$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Nephron
The NEPHRON is the FUNCTIONAL UNIT of the kidney — each kidney has about 1 million. Each nephron has a GLOMERULUS (a ball of capillaries where blood is FILTERED), Bowman's capsule, proximal tubule, loop of Henle, distal tubule, and collecting duct (where water, salts and wastes are reabsorbed or secreted to form urine).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Renal pelvis — The renal pelvis collects urine from the calyces — it does not filter.
• Ureter — The ureter carries urine to the bladder.
• Bladder trigone — The bladder trigone is a triangular area of the bladder wall.

⭐ Key point: The glomerular filtration rate (GFR, normal about 90–120 mL/min) measures how well nephrons filter; it falls in kidney disease.$x$, null),
(15, $x$The fluid that passes through the lymphatic vessels$x$, $x$Moves in a single direction toward the heart$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Moves in a single direction toward the heart
LYMPH is fluid that leaks out of blood capillaries into the tissues and is collected by LYMPHATIC VESSELS. Lymph flows in ONE DIRECTION ONLY — TOWARDS THE HEART — helped by VALVES inside the vessels (which prevent backflow), muscle contractions and breathing movements. It passes through lymph nodes (which filter it) and finally returns to the blood through the THORACIC DUCT (into the left subclavian vein) and the right lymphatic duct (into the right subclavian vein).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Flows toward the lungs — Lymph flows towards the heart and veins, not the lungs.
• Passes from the lymphatic vessels into the arteries — Lymph enters VEINS, not arteries.
• Enters the left ventricle of the heart through the right thoracic duct — Lymph does not enter the left ventricle; it enters the venous system near the heart.

⭐ Key point: Blocked lymphatics cause lymphoedema (e.g. after lymph node removal in breast cancer surgery).$x$, null),
(16, $x$During a blood transfusion, the patient's RR is 24 and BP 90/60. The nurse's priority is to:$x$, $x$Stop the transfusion immediately$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Stop the transfusion immediately
A rising RESPIRATORY RATE (24/min) and FALLING BP (90/60) during a blood transfusion suggest a possible SERIOUS TRANSFUSION REACTION — e.g. acute haemolytic reaction, anaphylaxis, sepsis from contaminated blood, or TRALI. The PRIORITY is to STOP THE TRANSFUSION IMMEDIATELY to prevent more of the reacting blood from entering. Then keep the vein open with 0.9% saline through a NEW giving set, check vital signs and patient ID, and call the doctor.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Slow the infusion rate — Slowing the infusion still allows more harmful blood to enter.
• Flush the line with normal saline through the same giving set — Flushing through the SAME set pushes more donor blood into the patient.
• Notify the doctor and continue — Continuing while notifying the doctor delays stopping the reaction.

⭐ Key point: Return the blood bag and giving set to the blood bank, and send blood and urine samples as per protocol.$x$, null),
(17, $x$The first step in the recommended 5-step ABG interpretation approach is to assess:$x$, $x$Oxygenation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Oxygenation
A common systematic approach to ABG interpretation starts by assessing OXYGENATION (PaO2, SpO2, and the P/F ratio) — because hypoxaemia is the most immediately life-threatening problem — before looking at acid-base balance. The usual steps: (1) Oxygenation; (2) pH (acidosis or alkalosis); (3) PaCO2 (respiratory component); (4) HCO3⁻/base excess (metabolic component); (5) Compensation (and anion gap if metabolic acidosis).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Compensation — Compensation is assessed near the end.
• Anion gap — The anion gap is calculated when there is metabolic acidosis.
• Base excess only — Base excess is part of the metabolic assessment, not the first step.

⭐ Key point: Always interpret ABGs together with the patient's clinical condition and the FiO2 they are receiving.$x$, null),
(18, $x$A commonly used massive transfusion protocol ratio for packed red cells, plasma, and platelets is approximately:$x$, $x$1:1:1$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 1:1:1
In MASSIVE HAEMORRHAGE (e.g. major trauma), giving only red cells and saline dilutes clotting factors and platelets, worsening bleeding. MASSIVE TRANSFUSION PROTOCOLS give PACKED RED CELLS, PLASMA (FFP) and PLATELETS in a ratio of about 1:1:1 (balanced resuscitation), which more closely replaces whole blood and improves survival (PROPPR trial). Cryoprecipitate (fibrinogen), calcium and tranexamic acid are also given.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10:1:1 — 10:1:1 gives far too little plasma and platelets.
• 1:10:1 — 1:10:1 gives far too much plasma.
• 5:5:1 — 5:5:1 gives too few platelets.

⭐ Key point: Watch for complications of massive transfusion: hypothermia, hypocalcaemia (citrate), hyperkalaemia, acidosis, and TRALI/TACO.$x$, null),
(19, $x$What is the primary purpose of administering potassium chloride infusion to patient with diabetic ketoacidosis recieving IV insulin?$x$, $x$Replacement of potassium losses$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Replacement of potassium losses
In DKA, total body POTASSIUM is LOW (lost in urine through osmotic diuresis and vomiting), even if the blood level looks normal or high at first (acidosis and lack of insulin shift potassium OUT of cells). When IV INSULIN is given, it drives potassium back INTO the cells, so the blood potassium can FALL quickly and dangerously. POTASSIUM CHLORIDE infusion is given to REPLACE POTASSIUM LOSSES and prevent hypokalaemia (which can cause arrhythmias and muscle weakness).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Treatment of hypepnea — "Hyperpnoea" (deep breathing) is due to acidosis, not treated with potassium.
• Prevention of fluccid paralysis — Preventing flaccid paralysis is a consequence of preventing hypokalaemia, but the primary purpose is replacement.
• Treatment of cardiac arrythmias — Treating arrhythmias is not the primary purpose; preventing hypokalaemia prevents them.

⭐ Key point: Check potassium before starting insulin; if potassium is below 3.3 mmol/L, give potassium first and delay insulin.$x$, null),
(20, $x$A patient develops urticaria and itching during a blood transfusion. The nurse should:$x$, $x$A and B$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A and B
URTICARIA (hives) and ITCHING during a transfusion indicate a MILD ALLERGIC reaction (to plasma proteins in the donor blood). The nurse should STOP (pause) THE TRANSFUSION and ADMINISTER an ANTIHISTAMINE such as DIPHENHYDRAMINE (or chlorphenamine), as prescribed. If symptoms resolve completely and there are no other signs (no fever, breathing difficulty or hypotension), the doctor may allow the transfusion to restart slowly. So "A and B" is correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stop the transfusion — Stopping alone is incomplete; the symptoms also need treatment.
• Administer diphenhydramine — An antihistamine alone without pausing the transfusion is unsafe.
• Slow the infusion rate — Slowing the rate is not enough.

⭐ Key point: If wheeze, swelling or hypotension develop, treat as anaphylaxis (IM adrenaline) and do not restart.$x$, null),
(21, $x$A key early clinical sign that should raise suspicion of DIC in a critically ill patient is:$x$, $x$Unexplained oozing from IV/puncture sites alongside signs of organ dysfunction$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Unexplained oozing from IV/puncture sites alongside signs of organ dysfunction
DISSEMINATED INTRAVASCULAR COAGULATION (DIC) is widespread activation of clotting in critical illness (sepsis, trauma, obstetric emergencies, cancer). Clotting factors and platelets are used up, causing BLEEDING. A KEY EARLY SIGN is UNEXPLAINED OOZING from IV sites, puncture sites, wounds or gums, ALONGSIDE ORGAN DYSFUNCTION (from tiny clots blocking small vessels — e.g. kidney injury, confusion, hypoxia).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Isolated mild headache with no other findings — An isolated mild headache is not suggestive.
• Normal coagulation results with no bleeding — Normal clotting with no bleeding does not suggest DIC.
• Bradycardia alone — Bradycardia alone is not suggestive.

⭐ Key point: Investigate with platelets, PT/APTT, fibrinogen and D-dimer; treat the underlying cause.$x$, null),
(22, $x$The extrinsic coagulation pathway is activated by:$x$, $x$Tissue factor released from damaged tissue$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Tissue factor released from damaged tissue
The EXTRINSIC coagulation pathway is activated by TISSUE FACTOR (TF, thromboplastin), released from DAMAGED TISSUE and blood vessel walls. Tissue factor combines with FACTOR VII, which then activates factor X, leading to thrombin and a clot. This pathway acts quickly after injury. It is measured by the PT/INR.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Contact with collagen — Contact with collagen (exposed surfaces) activates the INTRINSIC pathway (factor XII).
• Platelet aggregation alone — Platelet aggregation is part of primary haemostasis.
• Fibrinolysis — Fibrinolysis breaks down clots.

⭐ Key point: Warfarin mainly affects the extrinsic pathway (factor VII), so it is monitored with INR.$x$, null),
(23, $x$A fall in HCO3 causing acidosis indicates the disorder is:$x$, $x$Metabolic$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Metabolic
Acid–base balance has two parts: the RESPIRATORY part, controlled by the lungs and shown by PaCO2, and the METABOLIC part, controlled mainly by the kidneys and shown by BICARBONATE (HCO3⁻, normal 22–26 mmol/L). Bicarbonate is the body's main BASE (buffer). When HCO3⁻ FALLS and the pH falls (acidosis), the problem is METABOLIC ACIDOSIS. Causes: GAIN of acid (lactic acidosis in shock or sepsis, ketoacidosis in diabetes or starvation, kidney failure, poisons such as methanol or aspirin) or LOSS of bicarbonate (diarrhoea, ileostomy, renal tubular acidosis). The lungs try to compensate by fast, deep breathing to blow off CO2.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory — A RESPIRATORY disorder is caused by a change in PaCO2, not bicarbonate.
• Purely a lab error — A low bicarbonate with acidosis is a real, common finding — not a lab error.
• Always fatal — Metabolic acidosis is treatable and not always fatal; outcome depends on the cause.

⭐ Key point: In metabolic acidosis, calculate the ANION GAP (Na⁺ − [Cl⁻ + HCO3⁻]) — a high gap suggests added acids (lactate, ketones, toxins); a normal gap suggests bicarbonate loss.$x$, null),
(24, $x$Which clinical manifestation would lead the nurse to suspect that a client is experiencing hypermagnesemia?$x$, $x$Hot flushed skin and diaphoresis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hot flushed skin and diaphoresis
HYPERMAGNESAEMIA (high magnesium) causes VASODILATION and depresses nerves and muscles. Early signs include FLUSHING, a sensation of WARMTH, sweating (DIAPHORESIS), nausea and hypotension. As levels rise: loss of deep tendon reflexes, drowsiness, muscle weakness, respiratory depression, bradycardia and cardiac arrest. Common causes: magnesium sulphate therapy (pre-eclampsia), kidney failure, and magnesium-containing antacids or laxatives.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increased respiratory rate and depth — Respiratory rate and depth DECREASE in hypermagnesaemia.
• Soft-tissue calcification and hyperreflexia — Hyperreflexia is a feature of HYPOmagnesaemia; hypermagnesaemia causes HYPOreflexia.
• Muscle pain and acute rhabdomyolysis — Muscle pain and rhabdomyolysis are not typical.

⭐ Key point: Treat with IV calcium gluconate (antidote), stop magnesium, give fluids and diuretics, or dialysis in kidney failure.$x$, null),
(25, $x$Elevated ALT and AST primarily indicate:$x$, $x$Hepatocellular injury$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hepatocellular injury
ALT (alanine aminotransferase) and AST (aspartate aminotransferase) are enzymes found inside LIVER CELLS (hepatocytes). When liver cells are damaged, they leak into the blood, so RAISED ALT and AST indicate HEPATOCELLULAR INJURY — e.g. viral hepatitis, paracetamol overdose, ischaemic ("shock") liver, alcoholic or drug-induced liver injury. ALT is more specific to the liver; AST is also found in muscle and heart.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Renal dysfunction — Kidney function is measured by urea and creatinine.
• Pancreatic dysfunction only — Pancreatic damage is shown by amylase and lipase.
• Coagulopathy directly — Coagulopathy is measured by PT/INR, though liver damage can cause it indirectly.

⭐ Key point: Very high transaminases (in the thousands) suggest acute hepatitis, paracetamol toxicity or shock liver.$x$, null),
(26, $x$The direct Coombs test is used to detect:$x$, $x$Antibodies bound to the surface of red blood cells$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Antibodies bound to the surface of red blood cells
The DIRECT COOMBS TEST (direct antiglobulin test, DAT) detects ANTIBODIES (or complement) already BOUND to the SURFACE of the patient's RED BLOOD CELLS. A positive result indicates IMMUNE-MEDIATED HAEMOLYSIS — e.g. haemolytic transfusion reaction, haemolytic disease of the newborn (Rh or ABO incompatibility), or autoimmune haemolytic anaemia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Platelet aggregation — Platelet aggregation is tested differently.
• White cell count — White cell count is part of the full blood count.
• Clotting factor deficiency — Clotting factor deficiency is tested with clotting factor assays.

⭐ Key point: The INDIRECT Coombs test detects free antibodies in the plasma (used in pregnancy screening and crossmatching).$x$, null),
(27, $x$A patient with hypernatremia (Na⁺ 160 mEq/L) is lethargic. Which fluid should the nurse administer?$x$, $x$D5W$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: D5W
HYPERNATRAEMIA (sodium 160) usually means the body has lost too much WATER relative to sodium. The brain cells shrink, causing LETHARGY, confusion, seizures and coma. Treatment is to replace the FREE WATER deficit with a HYPOTONIC fluid such as 5% DEXTROSE IN WATER (D5W) — after the glucose is metabolised, it provides pure water. Sodium must be lowered SLOWLY (no more than about 10 mmol/L in 24 hours in chronic cases) to avoid cerebral oedema. (If the patient is shocked/hypovolaemic, isotonic saline is given first to restore circulation.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 3% saline — 3% saline is hypertonic — it would raise sodium further.
• 0.9% saline — 0.9% saline (154 mmol/L) is used for volume resuscitation but provides little free water.
• Lactated Ringer’s — Lactated Ringer's is near-isotonic and doesn't correct a free water deficit efficiently.

⭐ Key point: Monitor sodium every 4–6 hours during correction and watch for neurological changes.$x$, null),
(28, $x$To provide rapid pain relief in the ICU, which of the following routes of opioid administration is most appropriate?$x$, $x$Intravenous$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Intravenous
For RAPID PAIN RELIEF in ICU, the INTRAVENOUS (IV) route is most appropriate. IV opioids (e.g. morphine, fentanyl) act within minutes, have predictable absorption (critically ill patients often have poor gut and skin blood flow), and can be titrated easily in small doses or by continuous infusion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Intradermal — Intradermal is used for skin tests, not analgesia.
• Intraspinal — Intraspinal (epidural/intrathecal) is effective, but needs specialist insertion — not for rapid general use.
• Sublingual — Sublingual absorption is variable and slower.

⭐ Key point: Assess pain with validated tools (CPOT or BPS in patients who cannot speak) and monitor sedation and breathing.$x$, null),
(29, $x$A patient develops flushing, pruritus, and a rash on the neck and upper torso shortly after a rapid vancomycin infusion. The most appropriate nursing action is to:$x$, $x$Slow the infusion rate, as this is histamine-mediated red man syndrome rather than a true allergic reaction$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Slow the infusion rate, as this is histamine-mediated red man syndrome rather than a true allergic reaction
FLUSHING, ITCHING and a RASH on the face, NECK and UPPER TORSO shortly after a RAPID vancomycin infusion is typical of "RED MAN SYNDROME" (vancomycin infusion reaction). It is caused by direct HISTAMINE release from mast cells due to the fast infusion rate — NOT a true IgE allergy. The appropriate action is to STOP or SLOW THE INFUSION (an antihistamine can help), then continue at a slower rate (at least 60 minutes per gram).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stop vancomycin permanently and document a severe anaphylactic allergy — It is not a true anaphylactic allergy, so vancomycin doesn't need to be permanently stopped.
• Administer epinephrine immediately — Adrenaline is needed only for true anaphylaxis (airway swelling, wheeze, severe hypotension).
• Increase the infusion rate since the reaction is self-limited — Increasing the rate would worsen the reaction.

⭐ Key point: Document as an "infusion reaction" to avoid wrongly labelling the patient as allergic.$x$, null),
(30, $x$A key nursing precaution when administering IV potassium chloride is to:$x$, $x$Never give it as an undiluted rapid IV push, due to risk of fatal arrhythmia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Never give it as an undiluted rapid IV push, due to risk of fatal arrhythmia
IV POTASSIUM CHLORIDE (KCl) is a HIGH-ALERT medication. Given as an UNDILUTED RAPID IV PUSH, it causes a sudden high potassium level in the heart, leading to FATAL ARRHYTHMIAS and CARDIAC ARREST. It must ALWAYS be DILUTED, given by INFUSION PUMP, at a controlled rate (usually no more than 10 mmol/hr peripherally, up to 20 mmol/hr centrally with ECG monitoring).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Give it as a rapid IV push for fast correction — Rapid IV push is never acceptable.
• Administer it intramuscularly for safety — IM potassium is not used.
• Mix it with any other IV drug freely — KCl should not be mixed freely with other drugs.

⭐ Key point: Concentrated KCl ampoules should be stored securely and separately in clinical areas to prevent fatal errors.$x$, null),
(31, $x$A patient with septic shock is started on vasopressin. Which effect is expected?$x$, $x$Vasoconstriction$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Vasoconstriction
VASOPRESSIN acts on V1 receptors in blood vessel walls, causing VASOCONSTRICTION. In septic shock, it raises SVR and BP, helping to reduce the dose of noradrenaline needed. It also acts on V2 receptors in the kidneys (water retention).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Decreased SVR — It INCREASES SVR, not decreases it.
• Increased urine output — It may increase urine output in some cases by improving BP, but its main effect is vasoconstriction.
• Coronary vasodilation — It can cause coronary VASOCONSTRICTION (ischaemia risk).

⭐ Key point: Vasopressin dose in septic shock is usually up to 0.03 units/min, not titrated like noradrenaline.$x$, null),
(32, $x$Benzylpenicillin is the chemical name for:$x$, $x$Penicillin G$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Penicillin G
BENZYLPENICILLIN is the chemical (generic) name for PENICILLIN G, the original penicillin discovered by Alexander Fleming. It is destroyed by stomach acid, so it is given by INJECTION (IV or IM). It kills many gram-positive bacteria and some others. Uses include: streptococcal infections (e.g. cellulitis, tonsillitis, rheumatic fever prevention), MENINGOCOCCAL disease (meningitis/septicaemia), pneumococcal pneumonia, syphilis (as benzathine penicillin), tetanus, and early neonatal sepsis (with gentamicin).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Penicillin V — Penicillin V is PHENOXYMETHYLPENICILLIN — the acid-stable ORAL form.
• Penicillin F — "Penicillin F" was an early natural penicillin that is not used clinically.
• Phenethicillin — Phenethicillin is a different, semi-synthetic oral penicillin.

⭐ Key point: Always ask about penicillin allergy before giving it. Have adrenaline ready — anaphylaxis can occur within minutes of injection.$x$, null),
(33, $x$Prophylactic antibiotic use refers to:$x$, $x$Preventing infection before it starts, e.g. before surgery$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Preventing infection before it starts, e.g. before surgery
PROPHYLACTIC antibiotic use means giving antibiotics to PREVENT an infection BEFORE it starts — e.g. before SURGERY (to prevent surgical site infection), after exposure to meningococcal disease, or in high-risk patients (e.g. asplenia).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Treating an already established infection — Treating an existing infection is THERAPEUTIC use.
• Only used for viral infections — Antibiotics do not treat viral infections.
• A synonym for therapeutic use — Prophylactic and therapeutic use are different.

⭐ Key point: Surgical prophylaxis is usually given IV within 60 minutes before the incision and is usually stopped within 24 hours.$x$, null),
(34, $x$A patient who received naloxone for heroin overdose initially improves but becomes drowsy and hypoventilating again 45 minutes later. What is the most likely explanation?$x$, $x$Naloxone has a shorter half-life than most opioids, so re-sedation occurs as the antidote wears off before the opioid does$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Naloxone has a shorter half-life than most opioids, so re-sedation occurs as the antidote wears off before the opioid does
NALOXONE reverses opioid effects quickly, but it has a SHORT HALF-LIFE (about 30–90 minutes). Many opioids (e.g. heroin, morphine, methadone) last longer. When naloxone wears off, the opioid is still present, causing RE-SEDATION and respiratory depression. Patients must be observed for several hours after naloxone, and repeat doses or an infusion may be needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The patient has developed an allergic reaction to naloxone — This is not an allergic reaction.
• The initial dose of naloxone was too high — A high dose of naloxone doesn't cause re-sedation.
• This indicates the patient never had an opioid overdose — The improvement with naloxone confirms opioid overdose.

⭐ Key point: Observe patients for at least 2–4 hours after naloxone for short-acting opioids, longer for long-acting ones.$x$, null),
(35, $x$Amiodarone is primarily indicated in cardiac arrest management for:$x$, $x$Shockable rhythms (VF/pulseless VT) refractory to defibrillation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Shockable rhythms (VF/pulseless VT) refractory to defibrillation
In CARDIAC ARREST, AMIODARONE is used for SHOCKABLE RHYTHMS — VENTRICULAR FIBRILLATION (VF) and PULSELESS VENTRICULAR TACHYCARDIA (pVT) — that persist after defibrillation (refractory). The dose is 300 mg IV after the third shock, then 150 mg after the fifth shock.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Asystole only — Asystole is a non-shockable rhythm; amiodarone is not indicated.
• Bradycardia — Bradycardia is treated with atropine, pacing, or adrenaline infusion.
• Hypertensive emergency — Hypertensive emergency is treated with antihypertensives.

⭐ Key point: High-quality CPR and early defibrillation are the most important treatments in VF/pVT.$x$, null),
(36, $x$All are indications for Adrenaline use EXCEPT$x$, $x$Heart failure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Heart failure
ADRENALINE (epinephrine) is used in: CARDIAC RESUSCITATION (1 mg IV every 3–5 minutes), ANAPHYLAXIS (0.5 mg IM in adults), and SYMPTOMATIC BRADYCARDIA (as an infusion if atropine fails). It is NOT a standard treatment for HEART FAILURE (it increases heart workload and oxygen demand; although it may be used as an inotrope in cardiogenic shock, it is not a routine heart failure treatment).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cardiac resuscitation — Cardiac resuscitation IS an indication.
• Anaphylaxis — Anaphylaxis IS an indication.
• Symptomatic bradycardia — Symptomatic bradycardia IS an indication.

⭐ Key point: Know the correct route and dose for each indication — IV adrenaline in anaphylaxis is dangerous outside of cardiac arrest or specialist settings.$x$, null),
(37, $x$Amiodarone therapy requires monitoring of which organs due to potential toxicity?$x$, $x$Thyroid, liver, and lungs$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Thyroid, liver, and lungs
AMIODARONE can damage several organs with long-term use: THYROID (hypothyroidism or hyperthyroidism — it contains iodine), LIVER (raised liver enzymes, hepatitis), and LUNGS (pulmonary fibrosis, pneumonitis). Monitoring of thyroid function, liver function and lung function (chest X-ray) is required. It can also affect the eyes (corneal deposits) and skin (grey-blue colour, photosensitivity).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only the kidneys — Kidneys are not the main concern.
• Only the pancreas — The pancreas is not the main concern.
• Only the skin — Skin effects occur but are not the only concern.

⭐ Key point: Amiodarone interacts with warfarin and digoxin — reduce their doses.$x$, null),
(38, $x$Vasopressin is sometimes added in septic shock management primarily to:$x$, $x$Allow a norepinephrine-sparing effect by acting on a different receptor pathway$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Allow a norepinephrine-sparing effect by acting on a different receptor pathway
In SEPTIC SHOCK, VASOPRESSIN is added to NORADRENALINE when BP remains low despite moderate doses. It acts on a different receptor pathway (V1 receptors), so it raises BP by a different mechanism, allowing a lower noradrenaline dose ("NORADRENALINE-SPARING effect"). Septic shock may also cause a relative vasopressin deficiency.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Replace norepinephrine entirely — It doesn't replace noradrenaline entirely.
• Treat bradycardia — It doesn't treat bradycardia.
• Lower blood pressure — It raises, not lowers, BP.

⭐ Key point: Adding vasopressin is recommended when noradrenaline reaches 0.25–0.5 mcg/kg/min.$x$, null),
(39, $x$Neuromuscular blocking agents (e.g. vecuronium) must always be administered together with:$x$, $x$Adequate sedation and analgesia, since paralytics do not sedate or relieve pain$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Adequate sedation and analgesia, since paralytics do not sedate or relieve pain
NEUROMUSCULAR BLOCKING AGENTS (e.g. vecuronium, rocuronium, cisatracurium) PARALYSE muscles but do NOT provide SEDATION or PAIN RELIEF. A paralysed patient may be fully awake and in pain without being able to show it — a terrifying experience. They must ALWAYS be given with ADEQUATE SEDATION and ANALGESIA.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nothing else is required — Sedation and analgesia are essential.
• A diuretic — Diuretics are not required.
• An antihistamine — Antihistamines are not required.

⭐ Key point: Monitor depth of paralysis with train-of-four, provide eye care and pressure care.$x$, null),
(40, $x$Fentanyl is often preferred over morphine in patients with renal impairment because fentanyl:$x$, $x$Has no clinically significant active metabolites that accumulate in renal failure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Has no clinically significant active metabolites that accumulate in renal failure
MORPHINE is broken down into active metabolites (e.g. morphine-6-glucuronide), which are excreted by the kidneys and ACCUMULATE in RENAL FAILURE, causing prolonged sedation and respiratory depression. FENTANYL is broken down in the liver into INACTIVE metabolites, so it does not accumulate in renal failure. This makes fentanyl safer in renal impairment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Has active metabolites that accumulate in renal failure — Fentanyl does NOT have active metabolites that accumulate.
• Cannot be given intravenously — Fentanyl can be given IV.
• Has a much longer half-life — Fentanyl has a shorter half-life after a single dose (though it accumulates in fat with long infusions).

⭐ Key point: Other opioids that are relatively safe in renal failure: alfentanil and remifentanil.$x$, null),
(41, $x$Which of the following is classified as a positive (radio-opaque/white-appearing) contrast agent?$x$, $x$Barium$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Barium
CONTRAST AGENTS make structures easier to see on X-ray or CT. POSITIVE (radio-opaque) contrast agents contain heavy atoms that ABSORB X-RAYS strongly, so they appear WHITE on images. Examples: BARIUM sulphate (swallowed or given as an enema to outline the GI tract) and IODINE-based contrast (injected IV for CT scans, angiography, IVU). NEGATIVE (radiolucent) contrast agents such as AIR or CARBON DIOXIDE absorb few X-rays and appear DARK (black); they are sometimes used together with barium in "double-contrast" studies.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Room air — Room air is a NEGATIVE contrast agent (appears dark).
• Carbon dioxide — Carbon dioxide is a NEGATIVE contrast agent (appears dark).
• Vacuum/no contrast — "No contrast" means no agent is used.

⭐ Key point: Before iodine contrast: check kidney function, ask about previous contrast reactions and allergies, and ensure hydration. Barium should not be used when bowel perforation is suspected.$x$, null),
(42, $x$After a pneumonectomy, the trachea characteristically shifts:$x$, $x$Toward the operated (empty) side$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Toward the operated (empty) side
After a PNEUMONECTOMY (removal of a whole lung), the empty space fills with fluid over time, and the MEDIASTINUM and TRACHEA shift TOWARDS the OPERATED (empty) side, because there is no lung there to keep them in place. This is expected.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Away from the operated side — Shift AWAY from the operated side would suggest something is pushing (e.g. excessive fluid or bleeding).
• Never shifts after pneumonectomy — The trachea does shift after pneumonectomy.
• Shifts downward only — The trachea doesn't just shift downward.

⭐ Key point: A sudden shift AWAY from the operated side may indicate bleeding or bronchopleural fistula — report urgently.$x$, null),
(43, $x$A patient with a suspected small bowel obstruction has a CT scan with oral contrast ordered. The nurse understands the choice of a water-soluble (rather than barium) contrast agent in this context is because:$x$, $x$If perforation is present, water-soluble contrast is safer than barium, which causes severe chemical peritonitis if it leaks into the peritoneum$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: If perforation is present, water-soluble contrast is safer than barium, which causes severe chemical peritonitis if it leaks into the peritoneum
If there is a PERFORATION, BARIUM leaking into the peritoneal cavity causes severe CHEMICAL PERITONITIS and adhesions (it is not absorbed). WATER-SOLUBLE contrast (e.g. Gastrografin) is absorbed and causes much less harm if it leaks, so it is SAFER when perforation is possible.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Water-soluble contrast is always cheaper — Cost is not the reason.
• Barium provides better image quality in every situation — Barium does not always give better images.
• There is no difference in safety between the two agents — There is a significant difference in safety.

⭐ Key point: Water-soluble contrast can also help resolve adhesive small bowel obstruction (diagnostic and therapeutic).$x$, null),
(44, $x$The air bronchogram sign on chest X-ray is most associated with:$x$, $x$Consolidation/pneumonia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Consolidation/pneumonia
An AIR BRONCHOGRAM appears as dark, branching tubes (air-filled bronchi) seen against a WHITE background of lung. Normally, bronchi are not visible because the surrounding lung is also full of air (both dark). When the ALVEOLI around the bronchi become filled with FLUID, PUS, blood or cells (CONSOLIDATION), the lung turns white, and the air-filled bronchi stand out. It is classic for PNEUMONIA, and is also seen in pulmonary oedema, ARDS and lung haemorrhage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pneumothorax — A pneumothorax shows a dark area with NO lung markings and a visible lung edge.
• Rib fracture — A rib fracture shows a break or step in the bone outline.
• Cardiomegaly — Cardiomegaly shows an enlarged heart shadow (cardiothoracic ratio above 0.5).

⭐ Key point: Consolidation + air bronchograms + fever and cough strongly suggests pneumonia — take sputum and blood cultures and start antibiotics.$x$, null),
(45, $x$A widened mediastinum on chest X-ray after trauma is most suggestive of:$x$, $x$Thoracic aortic disruption$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Thoracic aortic disruption
After major blunt trauma (especially HIGH-SPEED DECELERATION — head-on collisions or falls from height), a WIDENED MEDIASTINUM on chest X-ray (more than about 8 cm on a supine AP film) suggests bleeding into the mediastinum from a THORACIC AORTIC DISRUPTION (tear), most commonly at the aortic isthmus just beyond the left subclavian artery. Other X-ray clues: loss of the aortic knob, deviation of the trachea or NG tube to the right, left apical "cap", and fractures of the first/second ribs. Most patients with complete transection die at the scene; survivors have a contained tear that can rupture at any moment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple rib fracture — A simple rib fracture does not widen the mediastinum.
• Pleural effusion — A pleural effusion causes fluid in the pleural space, not mediastinal widening.
• Diaphragmatic hernia — A diaphragmatic hernia shows bowel or stomach in the chest.

⭐ Key point: Confirm with CT angiography; keep heart rate under 100 and systolic BP about 100–120 mmHg (beta-blocker), give analgesia, and arrange urgent surgical or endovascular repair.$x$, null),
(46, $x$WHICH OF THESE BEST DESCRIBES A COMPUTER$x$, $x$An electronic device that accepts data, processes and stores it, and gives out information$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: An electronic device that accepts data, processes and stores it, and gives out information
A COMPUTER is an ELECTRONIC DEVICE that ACCEPTS DATA (input), PROCESSES it (according to instructions), STORES it, and GIVES OUT INFORMATION (output).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A device used only for playing games — Computers are used for much more than games.
• The means of connection between a sender and a receiver — "The means of connection between sender and receiver" describes a communication medium.
• A collection of data being transmitted — "A collection of data being transmitted" describes a message.

⭐ Key point: The four basic functions: input, processing, output, and storage.$x$, null),
(47, $x$Random Access Memory (RAM) differs from Read Only Memory (ROM) in that RAM is:$x$, $x$Volatile — its contents are lost when power is off$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Volatile — its contents are lost when power is off
RAM (Random Access Memory) is VOLATILE — its contents are LOST when the power is turned off. It stores the data and programs the computer is currently using. ROM (Read Only Memory) is NON-VOLATILE — it keeps its contents (e.g. startup instructions) without power.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Permanent and non-volatile — RAM is not permanent; ROM is.
• Used only for long-term archival storage — RAM is not used for long-term storage.
• Not involved in program execution — RAM is essential for running programs.

⭐ Key point: Save work frequently to avoid losing data in power cuts.$x$, null),
(48, $x$A nurse is setting up a new computer station in a clinic. Which of the following is considered an output device?$x$, $x$Printer$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Printer
OUTPUT DEVICES take the processed information from the computer and present it to the user in a usable form. A PRINTER produces a permanent paper copy ("hard copy") — e.g. printing an ECG strip, a lab report, a drug chart, or a discharge summary. Other output devices: MONITOR/screen ("soft copy"), speakers, headphones and projectors.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mouse — A MOUSE is an INPUT device — it sends pointing and clicking commands INTO the computer.
• Scanner — A SCANNER is an INPUT device — it converts paper documents or images into digital data.
• Microphone — A MICROPHONE is an INPUT device — it sends sound into the computer.

⭐ Key point: Some devices are both input and output (e.g. touchscreens, multifunction printer-scanners).$x$, null),
(49, $x$A phishing email pretending to be from IT asking staff to 'confirm' their password is an example of:$x$, $x$A social engineering cybersecurity threat$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A social engineering cybersecurity threat
PHISHING is a cyber-attack in which criminals send messages (emails, texts, calls) that PRETEND to come from a TRUSTED SOURCE — such as the hospital IT department, a bank or a manager — to trick people into giving away PASSWORDS, clicking harmful links or opening infected attachments. This is SOCIAL ENGINEERING: it attacks human trust rather than computer systems. Stolen login details can be used to access confidential patient records or install ransomware that locks hospital systems.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A legitimate security update — Genuine IT departments NEVER ask staff to send or "confirm" passwords by email.
• A required annual login refresh — There is no legitimate "annual login refresh" requiring you to send your password.
• A harmless marketing email — Phishing is NOT harmless — it can lead to data breaches and system shutdown.

⭐ Key point: Check the sender's real address, don't click suspicious links, never share passwords, and report suspicious emails to IT immediately.$x$, null),
(50, $x$Which of the following best describes 'utility software'?$x$, $x$Software designed to perform maintenance tasks such as antivirus scanning or disk cleanup$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Software designed to perform maintenance tasks such as antivirus scanning or disk cleanup
UTILITY SOFTWARE is a type of system support software that performs MAINTENANCE and housekeeping tasks to keep the computer secure, efficient and working well. Examples: ANTIVIRUS/anti-malware programs (detect and remove viruses), DISK CLEANUP and defragmentation tools, BACKUP software, file compression (e.g. zip), firewall software and system monitoring tools.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Software used only to write documents — Writing documents is done with APPLICATION software (word processors such as Microsoft Word).
• A type of physical computer hardware — Utility software is a program (software), not physical hardware.
• A programming language — A programming language (e.g. Python, Java) is used to write software, not a utility.

⭐ Key point: Keep antivirus software updated and back up important data regularly — hospital computers hold sensitive patient information.$x$, null),
(51, $x$The nurse assessing a ventilated patient should evaluate for signs of inadequate oxygenation or ventilation including all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Polyuria$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Polyuria
Signs of INADEQUATE OXYGENATION or VENTILATION include: RESTLESSNESS and CONFUSION (early sign of brain hypoxia or high CO2), TACHYCARDIA (early compensation), tachypnoea, sweating, cyanosis, and LATE signs such as BRADYCARDIA and HYPOTENSION (pre-arrest). POLYURIA (excessive urine) is NOT a sign of inadequate oxygenation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Restlessness and confusion — Restlessness and confusion ARE signs.
• Tachycardia — Tachycardia IS a sign.
• Bradycardia and hypotension as late signs — Bradycardia and hypotension ARE late signs.

⭐ Key point: Restlessness in a ventilated patient may be hypoxia — check the patient before increasing sedation.$x$, null),
(52, $x$A patient on oxygen therapy complains of a dry nose and throat. The nurse should:$x$, $x$Add a humidifier to the oxygen system$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Add a humidifier to the oxygen system
OXYGEN from cylinders or wall supplies is very DRY. It dries the nose and throat, causing discomfort, nosebleeds and thick secretions. The nurse should ADD A HUMIDIFIER to the oxygen system (especially at higher flows), and provide mouth care and water-based lubricant for the lips/nose.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Discontinue the oxygen therapy — Stopping oxygen may cause hypoxia.
• Increase the oxygen flow rate — Increasing flow would worsen dryness.
• Switch the patient to a non-rebreather mask — Switching masks doesn't address dryness.

⭐ Key point: Low-flow nasal cannula (below 4 L/min) usually doesn't need humidification; higher flows do.$x$, null),
(53, $x$A patient with acute pulmonary oedema on strict intake and output monitoring produces only 20 mL of urine in 2 hours despite IV furosemide 80 mg. What is the priority nursing action?$x$, $x$Assess for hypotension and reassess renal perfusion, and notify the physician$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Assess for hypotension and reassess renal perfusion, and notify the physician
Only 20 mL of urine in 2 hours (about 10 mL/hr) despite IV furosemide 80 mg suggests the kidneys are not responding — possibly due to HYPOTENSION (poor kidney perfusion), AKI, or a blocked catheter. The priority is to ASSESS for HYPOTENSION, REASSESS RENAL PERFUSION (BP, catheter patency, fluid status) and NOTIFY THE PHYSICIAN.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administer another diuretic dose without further assessment — Giving more diuretic without assessment may worsen hypotension.
• Increase IV fluids to promote diuresis — More IV fluids could worsen pulmonary oedema.
• Discontinue oxygen therapy — Discontinuing oxygen is dangerous in pulmonary oedema.

⭐ Key point: Check the catheter for blockage first.$x$, null),
(54, $x$Kussmaul respiration — deep, rapid breathing — is a compensatory pattern most associated with:$x$, $x$Metabolic acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Metabolic acidosis
KUSSMAUL RESPIRATION is deep, rapid, laboured breathing ("air hunger"). It is the lungs' COMPENSATION for METABOLIC ACIDOSIS: by breathing deeply and quickly, the body BLOWS OFF CARBON DIOXIDE (an acid), which helps RAISE the blood pH towards normal. It is classically seen in DIABETIC KETOACIDOSIS (often with a fruity/acetone smell on the breath), and also in severe kidney failure (uraemic acidosis), lactic acidosis and some poisonings (methanol, ethylene glycol, aspirin).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis is caused by HYPOventilation (CO2 retention), not deep fast breathing.
• Metabolic alkalosis exclusively — Metabolic alkalosis causes SLOW, shallow breathing (to retain CO2).
• Normal resting breathing — Kussmaul breathing is abnormal; normal resting breathing is quiet and regular at 12–20/min.

⭐ Key point: A patient with Kussmaul breathing needs an urgent blood gas, glucose, ketones and lactate.$x$, null),
(55, $x$A patient is inhaling 500 ml of air with 75% Fio2, which of the following term the nurse should document for the amount of air inhaled?$x$, $x$tidal volume$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: tidal volume
TIDAL VOLUME (Vt) is the VOLUME of air INHALED (or exhaled) in ONE NORMAL BREATH — about 500 mL in an average adult (about 6–8 mL/kg). Here, the patient inhales 500 mL per breath, so this is the tidal volume. The FiO2 (75%) describes the OXYGEN CONCENTRATION of that air, not the volume. On a ventilator, tidal volume is set (in volume modes) or monitored (in pressure modes), aiming for 6–8 mL/kg of predicted body weight.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• minute volume — MINUTE VOLUME is tidal volume × respiratory rate (air moved per minute).
• residual volume — RESIDUAL VOLUME is the air left in the lungs after a maximal breath out.
• total lung capacity — TOTAL LUNG CAPACITY is the maximum volume of air the lungs can hold (about 6 L).

⭐ Key point: Low tidal volumes with a fast rate (rapid shallow breathing) are a sign of respiratory fatigue.$x$, null),
(56, $x$This is the similarity between pulmonary and systemic circulation$x$, $x$volume of the circulation per minute$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: volume of the circulation per minute
The PULMONARY and SYSTEMIC circulations are connected in series, so the VOLUME of blood pumped per MINUTE (CARDIAC OUTPUT) by the right ventricle (to the lungs) and the left ventricle (to the body) must be the SAME (about 5 L/min). Otherwise blood would build up in one circulation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• total capacity — Total capacity differs (systemic holds much more blood).
• pulse pressure — Pulse pressure differs (pulmonary pressures are much lower).
• peripheral vascular resistance — Vascular resistance differs (pulmonary resistance is much lower).

⭐ Key point: Pulmonary circulation is a low-pressure system (about 25/10 mmHg).$x$, null),
(57, $x$A patient's chest tube water seal chamber shows fluid that stops fluctuating ('swinging') with respiration. This most likely indicates:$x$, $x$The lung has fully re-expanded, or the tube is blocked/kinked$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The lung has fully re-expanded, or the tube is blocked/kinked
In a chest drain system, fluid in the WATER SEAL chamber normally FLUCTUATES ("SWINGS") up and down with breathing. If the swinging STOPS, it means either the LUNG has FULLY RE-EXPANDED (no space left), or the TUBE is BLOCKED or KINKED.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal ongoing function — It is not normal ongoing function.
• An air leak — An air leak causes bubbling.
• Excessive suction — Excessive suction would cause continuous bubbling in the suction chamber.

⭐ Key point: If swinging stops, assess the patient (breath sounds, SpO2) and check the tubing for kinks and clots.$x$, null),
(58, $x$An open (sucking) chest wound should be managed with:$x$, $x$A 3-sided occlusive dressing$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A 3-sided occlusive dressing
An OPEN (SUCKING) CHEST WOUND lets air into the chest during breathing. It is managed with a 3-SIDED OCCLUSIVE DRESSING (taped on three sides). This acts as a flutter valve: it seals during inspiration, preventing air entry, and lets air escape during expiration, preventing a tension pneumothorax.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A fully sealed 4-sided occlusive dressing — A 4-sided sealed dressing could trap air and cause a tension pneumothorax.
• No dressing at all — No dressing allows air in.
• Direct pressure only, no dressing — Direct pressure alone is not adequate.

⭐ Key point: Commercial vented chest seals are also used. A chest drain is the definitive treatment.$x$, null),
(59, $x$Disadvantages of endotracheal intubation includes all of the following EXCEPT$x$, $x$Decreased airway resistance$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Decreased airway resistance
Disadvantages of ENDOTRACHEAL INTUBATION include: LOSS of the upper airway's PROTECTIVE functions (warming, humidifying, filtering air, cough), LOSS of PHONATION (cannot speak), and DAMAGE to the SUBGLOTTIC area (stenosis, ulceration). DECREASED AIRWAY RESISTANCE is NOT a disadvantage — the tube actually INCREASES resistance compared with the natural airway.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Loss of the protective function of the upper airway — Loss of protective function IS a disadvantage.
• Loss of phonation (voice) — Loss of phonation IS a disadvantage.
• Damage to the subglottic area — Subglottic damage IS a disadvantage.

⭐ Key point: Provide humidification and alternative communication methods for intubated patients.$x$, null),
(60, $x$A patient who has severe shortness of breath(SOB) and his/her ABGs shows high level of carbon dioxide (CO2) 55mm hg and low level of (Sao2) 40mmhg, which shows type II respiratory failure. Which Approach is best to treat the patient?$x$, $x$Delivering oxygen through BIPAP machine$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Delivering oxygen through BIPAP machine
TYPE II RESPIRATORY FAILURE (high CO2, low O2) requires ventilatory support to remove CO2. BiPAP (bilevel positive airway pressure) provides higher inspiratory pressure (to increase tidal volume and blow off CO2) and lower expiratory pressure (to keep airways open). It is the best non-invasive approach.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Delivering Oxygen through face mask — A face mask alone doesn't remove CO2 and may worsen hypercapnia.
• Delivering oxygen through CPAP machine — CPAP improves oxygenation but doesn't assist ventilation as effectively.
• None of them — BiPAP is an appropriate option.

⭐ Key point: BiPAP reduces intubation and mortality in COPD exacerbations with acidosis.$x$, null),
(61, $x$The dexamethasone dose sometimes used as an alternative to prednisolone in acute asthma is:$x$, $x$0.6mg/kg/day$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 0.6mg/kg/day
In ACUTE ASTHMA, systemic CORTICOSTEROIDS reduce airway inflammation and speed recovery. PREDNISOLONE is commonly used (1–2 mg/kg/day in children, maximum 40–50 mg). DEXAMETHASONE is an alternative, especially in children, at about 0.6 mg/kg/day (usually a single dose, maximum about 16 mg), often given for 1–2 days. Its long action means fewer doses and less vomiting of tablets, improving adherence.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.06mg/kg/day — 0.06 mg/kg is ten times TOO LOW — it would not be effective.
• 6mg/kg/day — 6 mg/kg is ten times TOO HIGH — a dangerous overdose.
• 60mg/kg/day — 60 mg/kg is a massive overdose.

⭐ Key point: Steroids take 4–6 hours to start working, so give them early, alongside bronchodilators (salbutamol, ipratropium) and oxygen.$x$, null),
(62, $x$A thoraco-abdominal incision on the right side gives access to a:$x$, $x$Lacerated liver$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Lacerated liver
A THORACO-ABDOMINAL incision crosses from the chest into the abdomen through the diaphragm. On the RIGHT side, it gives excellent exposure of the LIVER (especially the large right lobe and its posterior segments), the inferior vena cava, and the right side of the diaphragm. It is used for major LIVER LACERATIONS and complex liver resections, where a standard abdominal incision does not give enough access.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ruptured spleen — The SPLEEN is on the LEFT side — a right incision would not reach it.
• Oesophagogastric injury — Oesophagogastric injuries (lower oesophagus, stomach) are usually approached from the LEFT thoraco-abdominal route.
• None of the above — The liver is correctly accessed by this approach, so "none" is wrong.

⭐ Key point: Right lower rib fractures should raise suspicion of liver injury; look for signs of internal bleeding (tachycardia, hypotension, abdominal distension).$x$, null),
(63, $x$Type I respiratory failure is also known as:$x$, $x$Hypoxaemic failure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hypoxaemic failure
TYPE I RESPIRATORY FAILURE is also called HYPOXAEMIC respiratory failure. It is defined by a LOW PaO2 (below 60 mmHg / 8 kPa) with a NORMAL or LOW PaCO2. It is a problem of GAS EXCHANGE/oxygenation (V/Q mismatch, shunt, diffusion problems), e.g. pneumonia, pulmonary oedema, ARDS, pulmonary embolism, asthma (early). The CO2 is normal or low because breathing effort is usually increased.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ventilatory failure — VENTILATORY failure is TYPE II (the "pump" fails to move enough air).
• Hypercapnic failure — HYPERCAPNIC failure (high CO2) is TYPE II.
• Septicaemic failure — "Septicaemic failure" is not a recognised classification of respiratory failure.

⭐ Key point: Treat Type I with oxygen, PEEP/CPAP and treatment of the cause; Type II needs ventilatory support (NIV or intubation).$x$, null),
(64, $x$Mr. Amir (RN in Cardiac ICU) obtained cardiac output of a patient having 100 kg weight and report to the head nurse that the cardiac output is 3.5 Liter per minute. To confirm, whether the cardiac output is normal or abnormal, a more accurate measurement used is:$x$, $x$Cardiac Index$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Cardiac Index
CARDIAC OUTPUT (CO) depends on body size — a large person needs more blood flow. CARDIAC INDEX (CI) = CO ÷ BODY SURFACE AREA (BSA), which adjusts for body size. Normal CI is about 2.5–4.0 L/min/m². For a 100 kg patient, a CO of 3.5 L/min may give a low CI (about 1.5–1.6), indicating low output.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stroke volume — Stroke volume alone doesn't adjust for body size.
• Heart rate — Heart rate alone doesn't adjust for body size.
• PCWP — PCWP measures left heart filling, not output.

⭐ Key point: CI below 2.2 L/min/m² with signs of hypoperfusion suggests cardiogenic shock.$x$, null),
(65, $x$A patient comes with severe chest pain, radiating to jaw and shoulder. His ECG shows ST elevations in lead II, lead III and lead AVF. What does it show?$x$, $x$Inferior MI$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Inferior MI
ST ELEVATION in leads II, III and aVF (the INFERIOR leads, which look at the bottom wall of the heart) indicates an INFERIOR MYOCARDIAL INFARCTION. It is usually caused by blockage of the RIGHT CORONARY ARTERY (in about 80–90% of people). Reciprocal ST depression may be seen in leads I and aVL. Inferior MI can be complicated by bradycardia and heart block (the RCA supplies the SA and AV nodes) and RIGHT VENTRICULAR infarction.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Posterior MI — Posterior MI shows ST DEPRESSION with tall R waves in V1–V3 (and ST elevation in V7–V9).
• Anterior MI — Anterior MI shows ST elevation in V1–V4 (LAD artery).
• Septal MI — Septal MI shows ST elevation in V1–V2.

⭐ Key point: In inferior MI, record right-sided leads (V4R) — if RV infarction is present, avoid nitrates and give fluids for hypotension.$x$, null),
(66, $x$Immediately after PAC insertion, which action confirms correct catheter placement?$x$, $x$A chest X-ray$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A chest X-ray
After inserting a PULMONARY ARTERY CATHETER (PAC, Swan-Ganz), a CHEST X-RAY is taken to CONFIRM CORRECT PLACEMENT — the catheter tip should be in a main pulmonary artery branch (not too far out into a small artery, where it could cause infarction or rupture) — and to exclude complications of central venous access such as PNEUMOTHORAX, haemothorax or catheter knotting. The pressure waveforms seen during insertion (right atrium → right ventricle → pulmonary artery → wedge) also guide placement.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Auscultation of bowel sounds — Bowel sounds have nothing to do with PAC position.
• Checking pedal pulses only — Pedal pulses alone do not confirm catheter position.
• Measuring oral temperature — Oral temperature does not confirm position (the PAC itself measures core temperature).

⭐ Key point: Continuously monitor the PA waveform — a permanently "wedged" trace means the catheter has migrated; deflate the balloon and pull back per protocol.$x$, null),
(67, $x$Primary (essential) hypertension accounts for approximately what percentage of hypertension cases?$x$, $x$85–95%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 85–95%
PRIMARY (ESSENTIAL) HYPERTENSION has no single identifiable cause; it results from a mix of genetic, lifestyle and environmental factors (age, family history, high salt intake, obesity, inactivity, alcohol, stress). It accounts for about 85–95% of all hypertension cases. SECONDARY hypertension (about 5–15%) is caused by an identifiable condition: kidney disease, renal artery stenosis, Conn's syndrome, Cushing's syndrome, phaeochromocytoma, thyroid disease, coarctation of the aorta, pregnancy, or drugs (e.g. steroids, oral contraceptives, NSAIDs).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 5–15% — 5–15% is the proportion of SECONDARY hypertension.
• 50% — 50% underestimates primary hypertension.
• 100% — 100% ignores secondary causes.

⭐ Key point: Suspect secondary hypertension in young patients, sudden onset, very high or resistant BP, or low potassium.$x$, null),
(68, $x$Concerning the ECG, all of the following are incorrect EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Time and rate are measured on the horizontal axis and voltage on the vertical axis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Time and rate are measured on the horizontal axis and voltage on the vertical axis
On an ECG, TIME (and rate) is measured on the HORIZONTAL axis (each small square = 0.04 seconds at standard speed) and VOLTAGE (amplitude) on the VERTICAL axis (each small square = 0.1 mV). This statement is CORRECT.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A waveform moving toward the top of the paper is a negative deflection — A waveform moving upwards is POSITIVE, not negative.
• A waveform moving toward the bottom of the paper is a positive deflection — A waveform moving downwards is NEGATIVE, not positive.
• The ECG waveform shows the heart's mechanical pumping force — The ECG shows ELECTRICAL activity, not mechanical pumping.

⭐ Key point: Pulseless electrical activity (PEA) shows that electrical activity can occur without effective pumping.$x$, null),
(69, $x$For an unconscious choking adult, the correct approach is to:$x$, $x$Start CPR, checking the mouth before each breath$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Start CPR, checking the mouth before each breath
For an UNCONSCIOUS CHOKING ADULT, call for help and START CPR immediately (30 compressions : 2 breaths). Chest compressions raise the pressure inside the chest and may dislodge the obstruction. BEFORE EACH ATTEMPT AT BREATHS, LOOK IN THE MOUTH and remove any VISIBLE object with your fingers (no blind finger sweeps). Continue until the object is cleared and the patient breathes, or help arrives.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Wait for the ambulance without doing anything — Waiting for the ambulance without action allows brain damage and death from hypoxia.
• Perform abdominal thrusts only — Abdominal thrusts (Heimlich manoeuvre) are for CONSCIOUS choking adults; once unconscious, CPR is started.
• Place immediately in recovery position — The recovery position does nothing to clear a blocked airway.

⭐ Key point: For a CONSCIOUS choking adult: encourage coughing; if ineffective, give up to 5 back blows then up to 5 abdominal thrusts, repeating.$x$, null),
(70, $x$The amount of blood which the heart pumps in one cardiac contraction is called:$x$, $x$Stroke Volume$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Stroke Volume
STROKE VOLUME (SV) is the amount of blood the LEFT VENTRICLE ejects with ONE contraction (heartbeat) — about 60–100 mL in an adult at rest. It depends on three factors: PRELOAD (how full the ventricle is before contraction), AFTERLOAD (the resistance it pumps against), and CONTRACTILITY (the strength of contraction). CARDIAC OUTPUT = stroke volume × heart rate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pre-load — PRELOAD is the stretch/filling of the ventricle at the end of diastole.
• After Load — AFTERLOAD is the resistance the ventricle must overcome to eject blood.
• Cardiac Output — CARDIAC OUTPUT is the amount pumped per MINUTE, not per beat.

⭐ Key point: Stroke volume falls in hypovolaemia, heart failure and tamponade; the body compensates by increasing heart rate.$x$, null),
(71, $x$All are clinical features of pulmonary oedema EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Syncope$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Syncope
PULMONARY OEDEMA is fluid in the lung tissue and alveoli, usually from LEFT HEART FAILURE (high pressure in the lung capillaries pushes fluid out). Classic features: EXTREME DYSPNOEA (especially lying flat — orthopnoea — and waking breathless at night), COUGH with PINK, FROTHY SPUTUM (fluid mixed with air and a little blood), WHEEZING (fluid narrows the airways — "cardiac asthma"), crackles at both lung bases, sweating, anxiety, tachycardia and low SpO2. SYNCOPE (fainting) is NOT a typical feature of pulmonary oedema; it suggests arrhythmia, aortic stenosis, or other causes of sudden reduced brain blood flow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cough with pink, frothy sputum — Pink, frothy sputum IS a classic feature.
• Extreme dyspnoea — Extreme dyspnoea IS a classic feature.
• Inspiratory and expiratory wheezing — Wheezing IS a feature ("cardiac asthma").

⭐ Key point: Treatment: sit the patient upright, give oxygen/CPAP, IV furosemide and nitrates, and treat the cause (e.g. MI, hypertension, arrhythmia).$x$, null),
(72, $x$Myocardial oxygen demand is increased by the followings EXCEPT$x$, $x$Sleep$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Sleep
MYOCARDIAL OXYGEN DEMAND rises when the heart has to work harder: higher HEART RATE, greater CONTRACTILITY, and increased WALL TENSION (from high afterload or a thick or dilated ventricle). LEFT VENTRICULAR HYPERTROPHY (more muscle to supply, higher wall tension), FEVER (higher metabolic rate and heart rate) and HYPERTHYROIDISM (faster, stronger heartbeat) all INCREASE oxygen demand. SLEEP DECREASES it — heart rate and BP fall during sleep, reducing the heart's workload.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Left ventricular hypertrophy — LV hypertrophy increases demand.
• Fever — Fever increases demand.
• Hyperthyroidism — Hyperthyroidism increases demand.

⭐ Key point: In patients with ischaemic heart disease, reduce oxygen demand by controlling pain, fever, anxiety, tachycardia and hypertension, and by promoting rest and sleep.$x$, null),
(73, $x$Which of the following is NOT a hemodynamic effects and benefits of intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP);$x$, $x$Increase ventricular wall tension$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Increase ventricular wall tension
An INTRA-AORTIC BALLOON PUMP (IABP) is a balloon in the descending aorta that INFLATES during DIASTOLE (pushing blood back towards the coronary arteries → INCREASES CORONARY BLOOD FLOW) and DEFLATES just before SYSTOLE (creating a "vacuum" that lowers aortic pressure → REDUCES AFTERLOAD). Lower afterload means the left ventricle needs less pressure and less WALL TENSION to eject blood, so its OXYGEN DEMAND FALLS and CARDIAC OUTPUT INCREASES. "Increase ventricular wall tension" is the OPPOSITE of what the IABP does, so it is NOT a benefit.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase cardiac output — Increased cardiac output IS a benefit.
• Decrease o2 demand — Decreased oxygen demand IS a benefit.
• Increase coronary flow — Increased coronary flow IS a benefit.

⭐ Key point: Nursing care: check limb perfusion on the insertion side, keep the leg straight, monitor timing, watch for bleeding and balloon rupture (blood in the tubing).$x$, null),
(74, $x$Afterload for the left ventricle is most directly represented by:$x$, $x$Systemic vascular resistance (SVR)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Systemic vascular resistance (SVR)
AFTERLOAD is the RESISTANCE the ventricle must overcome to eject blood. For the LEFT VENTRICLE, afterload is mainly determined by the resistance of the systemic arteries — the SYSTEMIC VASCULAR RESISTANCE (SVR) (plus aortic valve stenosis if present). High SVR (vasoconstriction, hypertension) increases afterload; low SVR (vasodilation, sepsis) reduces it. Normal SVR: about 800–1200 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR) — PVR (pulmonary vascular resistance) represents RIGHT ventricular afterload.
• Central venous pressure — CVP reflects RIGHT ventricular PRELOAD.
• Stroke volume index — Stroke volume index reflects the heart's output, not afterload.

⭐ Key point: Afterload reduction (e.g. vasodilators, IABP) helps a failing heart pump more blood.$x$, null),
(75, $x$A condition characterised by a prolonged PR interval more than 0.2 secs in ECG is called:$x$, $x$First degree Av block$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: First degree Av block
FIRST-DEGREE AV BLOCK is defined by a PROLONGED PR INTERVAL of MORE THAN 0.20 SECONDS (more than 5 small squares on the ECG). Every P wave is still followed by a QRS (no dropped beats); conduction through the AV node is simply delayed. It is usually benign, often caused by drugs (beta-blockers, digoxin, calcium channel blockers), high vagal tone, or inferior MI.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Second degree AV block — Second-degree AV block has some P waves NOT followed by a QRS.
• Mobiz Type II AV block — Mobitz type II is a form of second-degree block with dropped beats and a constant PR.
• Complete block — Complete (third-degree) block has NO relationship between P waves and QRS complexes.

⭐ Key point: Normal PR interval: 0.12–0.20 seconds (3–5 small squares).$x$, null),
(76, $x$On an ECG, the P wave reflects atrial depolarisation initiated by the:$x$, $x$SA node$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: SA node
The SINOATRIAL (SA) NODE, in the wall of the right atrium, is the heart's natural PACEMAKER. It generates electrical impulses (normally 60–100 per minute) that spread across both atria, causing ATRIAL DEPOLARISATION — seen as the P WAVE on the ECG — followed by atrial contraction. The impulse then travels to the AV node.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Left bundle branch — The left bundle branch conducts impulses through the ventricles (part of the QRS).
• AV node — The AV node delays the impulse between atria and ventricles (the PR segment).
• Bachmann's bundle — Bachmann's bundle carries the impulse from the right to left atrium, but does not INITIATE it.

⭐ Key point: In sinus rhythm, every QRS is preceded by an upright P wave in lead II.$x$, null),
(77, $x$Which mechanism helps maintain glomerular filtration pressure when renal perfusion falls, mediated by angiotensin II?$x$, $x$Constriction of the efferent arteriole$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Constriction of the efferent arteriole
When renal perfusion falls, the kidney releases RENIN, producing ANGIOTENSIN II. Angiotensin II CONSTRICTS the EFFERENT ARTERIOLE (the vessel leaving the glomerulus) more than the afferent. This "backs up" blood in the glomerulus, MAINTAINING GLOMERULAR FILTRATION PRESSURE and GFR despite low blood flow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dilation of the efferent arteriole — Dilating the efferent arteriole would LOWER filtration pressure (this is what ACE inhibitors do).
• Dilation of both arterioles equally — Dilating both arterioles equally would not maintain filtration pressure.
• Constriction of the afferent arteriole — Constricting the afferent arteriole would REDUCE blood entering the glomerulus and lower GFR.

⭐ Key point: ACE inhibitors and ARBs block this mechanism, so they can cause AKI in dehydration or renal artery stenosis.$x$, null),
(78, $x$At 10 years, survival on dialysis alone (without transplant) is approximately:$x$, $x$8.49%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 8.49%
Long-term survival on DIALYSIS ALONE (without a kidney transplant) is poor, because of cardiovascular disease, infections and other complications. Registry data show that only about 10% or fewer (around 8.5% in the source used) of patients are still alive after 10 YEARS on dialysis alone. Kidney transplantation gives much better long-term survival and quality of life.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 55.07% — 55% is far too high for 10-year survival on dialysis alone.
• 74.84% — 75% is far too high.
• 95% — 95% is far too high.

⭐ Key point: Survival depends on age, diabetes, heart disease and access to adequate dialysis.$x$, null),
(79, $x$Storage lower urinary tract symptoms include the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Hesitancy$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hesitancy
LOWER URINARY TRACT SYMPTOMS (LUTS) are grouped into: STORAGE symptoms (problems holding urine): FREQUENCY, URGENCY, NOCTURIA (waking at night to pass urine), urge incontinence; and VOIDING symptoms (problems emptying): HESITANCY (delay in starting), weak stream, straining, intermittent stream, terminal dribbling and incomplete emptying. HESITANCY is a VOIDING symptom, not a storage symptom.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Frequency — Frequency IS a storage symptom.
• Urgency — Urgency IS a storage symptom.
• Nocturia — Nocturia IS a storage symptom.

⭐ Key point: Voiding symptoms are typical of benign prostatic hyperplasia; storage symptoms are typical of overactive bladder or UTI.$x$, null),
(80, $x$Approximately what percentage of resting cardiac output do the kidneys normally receive?$x$, $x$20-25%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 20-25%
The KIDNEYS receive about 20–25% of the RESTING CARDIAC OUTPUT — about 1–1.25 litres of blood per minute — even though they weigh only about 300 g (0.5% of body weight). This huge blood flow is not for the kidneys' own oxygen needs; it is needed to FILTER large volumes of plasma (about 180 litres per day) to remove waste products and regulate fluids, electrolytes and acid–base balance.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 40% — 40% is too high.
• 5% — 5% is too low.
• 10% — 10% is too low.

⭐ Key point: Because the kidneys depend on high blood flow, any hypotension or hypovolaemia quickly reduces GFR and can cause pre-renal AKI — maintain MAP above 65 mmHg.$x$, null),
(81, $x$Post-nephrectomy hospital stay typically ranges from:$x$, $x$2–7 days$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 2–7 days
NEPHRECTOMY is surgical removal of a kidney (for cancer, trauma, a non-functioning kidney, or living donation). Hospital stay usually ranges from about 2 TO 7 DAYS: about 2–3 days after LAPAROSCOPIC surgery, and about 5–7 days after OPEN surgery, depending on pain control, mobility, bowel function and complications. Nursing care focuses on monitoring for bleeding (BP, pulse, wound, drains, haemoglobin), urine output (function of the remaining kidney), pain control, chest physiotherapy (flank incisions near the diaphragm limit breathing), and early mobilisation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2–4 weeks — 2–4 weeks is unusually long and suggests complications.
• 6 months — 6 months is far too long.
• Discharge is always same-day — Same-day discharge is not standard after nephrectomy.

⭐ Key point: After nephrectomy, avoid nephrotoxic drugs (e.g. NSAIDs) to protect the remaining kidney.$x$, null),
(82, $x$A 68-year-old man with benign prostatic hyperplasia presents with anuria and a palpably distended bladder. This is most consistent with which category of AKI?$x$, $x$Postrenal$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Postrenal
BENIGN PROSTATIC HYPERPLASIA (BPH) can BLOCK the outflow of urine from the bladder. ANURIA with a PALPABLY DISTENDED BLADDER shows the kidneys are making urine but it cannot drain out — this is POST-RENAL (obstructive) AKI. Treatment: urinary catheterisation (often suprapubic if urethral fails). Watch for post-obstructive diuresis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Prerenal — Pre-renal AKI is due to reduced kidney blood flow.
• Intrarenal — Intrarenal AKI is due to kidney tissue damage.
• Functional — "Functional" is not a standard category here.

⭐ Key point: A bladder scan or palpation can quickly confirm retention.$x$, null),
(83, $x$An unresponsive patient shows no abnormal movements, and continuous EEG shows a normal background without epileptiform activity. This clinical picture is best described as what?$x$, $x$Unconsciousness/coma without seizure activity$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Unconsciousness/coma without seizure activity
An UNRESPONSIVE patient with NO ABNORMAL MOVEMENTS and a CONTINUOUS EEG showing a NORMAL BACKGROUND WITHOUT EPILEPTIFORM ACTIVITY is UNCONSCIOUS (COMA) WITHOUT SEIZURE ACTIVITY. The cause must be found (metabolic, toxic, structural).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An active tonic-clonic seizure — An active tonic-clonic seizure shows obvious movements and EEG seizure activity.
• Nonconvulsive status epilepticus — Non-convulsive status epilepticus shows seizure activity on EEG without obvious movements.
• A myoclonic seizure — A myoclonic seizure involves jerks.

⭐ Key point: EEG is important in unexplained coma to rule out non-convulsive seizures.$x$, null),
(84, $x$An unconscious patient's airway is being protected. The nurse should recognise that airway reflexes (gag/cough) are typically lost when the GCS falls to approximately:$x$, $x$8 or below$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 8 or below
When the GCS falls to 8 OR BELOW, protective airway reflexes (gag and cough) are typically lost or impaired. The patient cannot protect their airway from obstruction or aspiration. This is why "GCS 8 — intubate" is a common rule.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 15 — GCS 15 is fully alert.
• 12 — GCS 12 usually maintains airway reflexes.
• 10, with no significance below this — Below 10 is significant, and reflexes are usually lost at 8 or below.

⭐ Key point: Position unconscious patients on their side and have suction ready until the airway is secured.$x$, null),
(85, $x$In a pregnant patient having eclamptic seizures, the first-line treatment is:$x$, $x$Magnesium sulphate$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Magnesium sulphate
ECLAMPSIA is the occurrence of SEIZURES in a woman with pre-eclampsia. The FIRST-LINE treatment for eclamptic seizures — and for preventing further seizures — is MAGNESIUM SULPHATE: loading dose 4 g IV over 5–15 minutes, then a maintenance infusion of 1 g/hour (Zuspan regimen) or IM regimen (Pritchard). Large trials (Collaborative Eclampsia Trial, Magpie Trial) showed magnesium sulphate is MORE EFFECTIVE than diazepam and phenytoin at preventing recurrent seizures and REDUCES MATERNAL DEATH.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lorazepam — Lorazepam is first-line for most other seizures, but not for eclampsia.
• Phenytoin — Phenytoin is less effective than magnesium in eclampsia.
• Diazepam — Diazepam is less effective in eclampsia and causes more maternal and neonatal sedation.

⭐ Key point: During magnesium therapy, monitor reflexes, respiratory rate and urine output hourly; keep calcium gluconate (antidote) at the bedside. Delivery is the definitive treatment of eclampsia.$x$, null),
(86, $x$Which ICP monitoring device is considered the gold standard because it also allows therapeutic drainage of cerebrospinal fluid?$x$, $x$Intraventricular catheter (external ventricular drain)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Intraventricular catheter (external ventricular drain)
The INTRAVENTRICULAR CATHETER (EXTERNAL VENTRICULAR DRAIN, EVD) is placed in a lateral ventricle. It is the GOLD STANDARD for ICP monitoring because it is accurate, can be recalibrated, AND allows THERAPEUTIC DRAINAGE of CSF to lower ICP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Subarachnoid bolt — Subarachnoid bolts are less accurate and can't drain CSF.
• Epidural sensor — Epidural sensors are less accurate.
• Intraparenchymal fiberoptic monitor — Intraparenchymal monitors are accurate but can't drain CSF.

⭐ Key point: EVDs carry a risk of infection (ventriculitis) — maintain strict asepsis.$x$, null),
(87, $x$All of the following are hyperkinetic involuntary movement disorders EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Bradykinesia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Bradykinesia
Movement disorders are divided into HYPERKINETIC (too much, unwanted movement) and HYPOKINETIC (too little movement). HYPERKINETIC disorders include: CHOREA (brief, irregular, dance-like jerks — e.g. Huntington's disease), ATHETOSIS (slow, writhing movements of the hands and feet — e.g. cerebral palsy, kernicterus), TORSION DYSTONIA (sustained twisting muscle contractions and abnormal postures), tremor, tics, myoclonus and ballismus. BRADYKINESIA (slowness of movement) is HYPOKINETIC — a key feature of PARKINSON'S DISEASE.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Chorea — Chorea IS hyperkinetic.
• Athetosis — Athetosis IS hyperkinetic.
• Torsion dystonia — Torsion dystonia IS hyperkinetic.

⭐ Key point: Parkinson's disease features: bradykinesia, rigidity, resting tremor and postural instability.$x$, null),
(88, $x$Cerebral Perfusion Pressure (CPP) is calculated as:$x$, $x$Mean Arterial Pressure minus Intracranial Pressure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Mean Arterial Pressure minus Intracranial Pressure
CEREBRAL PERFUSION PRESSURE (CPP) = MEAN ARTERIAL PRESSURE (MAP) − INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE (ICP). It represents the pressure driving blood into the brain. Target CPP is usually 60–70 mmHg in TBI.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Systolic Blood Pressure plus Intracranial Pressure — Systolic plus ICP is incorrect.
• Heart Rate divided by Intracranial Pressure — Heart rate divided by ICP is incorrect.
• Diastolic Blood Pressure minus Heart Rate — Diastolic minus heart rate is incorrect.

⭐ Key point: Low CPP causes brain ischaemia; raise MAP or lower ICP.$x$, null),
(89, $x$What is the normal range for intracranial pressure (ICP) in adults?$x$, $x$5-15 mmHg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 5-15 mmHg
INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE (ICP) is the pressure inside the skull. In a resting adult lying down, NORMAL ICP is about 5–15 mmHg (7–20 cmH2O). ICP above 20–22 mmHg is considered RAISED and usually needs treatment, because high ICP reduces cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP = MAP − ICP) and can cause brain ischaemia and herniation. Coughing, straining and suctioning cause brief rises that settle quickly in a normal brain.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0-5 mmHg — 0–5 mmHg is low; very low ICP can occur after over-drainage of CSF.
• 20-30 mmHg — 20–30 mmHg is RAISED.
• 30-40 mmHg — 30–40 mmHg is severely raised and dangerous.

⭐ Key point: Nursing measures to keep ICP down: head-up 30°, neck neutral, avoid tight tube ties, minimise suctioning, treat pain, fever and agitation, and avoid hypoxia and high CO2.$x$, null),
(90, $x$All of the following are levels of impaired consciousness EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Alertness$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Alertness
Consciousness ranges from fully ALERT (normal) through various levels of IMPAIRMENT: CONFUSION, DELIRIUM (acute fluctuating confusion with poor attention), lethargy/drowsiness, obtundation, STUPOR (responds only to strong, repeated stimuli) and COMA (cannot be roused, no purposeful response). ALERTNESS is the NORMAL state of full consciousness — awake, attentive, and responding appropriately — so it is NOT a level of impaired consciousness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stupor — Stupor IS a level of impaired consciousness.
• Delirium — Delirium IS a disturbance of consciousness.
• Coma — Coma IS the deepest level of impaired consciousness.

⭐ Key point: Describe consciousness objectively with the GCS (and AVPU in emergencies) rather than vague terms.$x$, null),
(91, $x$Unconsciousness include all the following EXCEPT$x$, $x$Awareness of self and environment$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Awareness of self and environment
UNCONSCIOUSNESS is a state of abnormal cerebral activity and depressed brain function, where the person is UNRESPONSIVE to internal and external stimuli. AWARENESS of SELF and ENVIRONMENT is the definition of CONSCIOUSNESS — the opposite of unconsciousness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A state of abnormal cerebral activity — Abnormal cerebral activity IS part of unconsciousness.
• Unresponsive to internal and external environmental changes — Unresponsiveness IS part of unconsciousness.
• Depression of cerebral function — Depressed cerebral function IS part of unconsciousness.

⭐ Key point: Causes of unconsciousness: head injury, stroke, hypoxia, hypoglycaemia, drugs, infection, seizures.$x$, null),
(92, $x$A nurse is performing hygiene care for an unconscious patient. To prevent complications, the nurse should:$x$, $x$Ensure the patient's head is elevated to at least 30 degrees during care$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Ensure the patient's head is elevated to at least 30 degrees during care
Unconscious patients are at risk of ASPIRATION during care (e.g. mouth care, bathing). ELEVATING THE HEAD to at least 30 degrees (or turning the patient to the side) reduces the risk of aspiration.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bathe the patient with cold water to stimulate a response — Cold water bathing causes hypothermia.
• Use a cotton swab to clean the patient's ears — Cotton swabs can damage the ear canal and eardrum.
• Use a firm bristle toothbrush to clean the teeth — Firm bristle brushes can damage gums; use a soft brush.

⭐ Key point: Use suction during mouth care and check the airway.$x$, null),
(93, $x$Therapeutic hyperventilation used briefly as a temporizing measure for acute herniation should target a PaCO2 of approximately what value?$x$, $x$30-35 mmHg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 30-35 mmHg
In an EMERGENCY of acute BRAIN HERNIATION (e.g. a blown pupil, Cushing's response), brief HYPERVENTILATION lowers PaCO2. Low CO2 makes the brain's blood vessels CONSTRICT, reducing cerebral blood volume and quickly lowering ICP. The target is a PaCO2 of about 30–35 mmHg. This is only a short-term "bridge" while giving osmotherapy (mannitol or hypertonic saline) and arranging CT and neurosurgery.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 15-20 mmHg — 15–20 mmHg causes severe vasoconstriction, cutting off blood supply and causing brain ischaemia.
• 45-50 mmHg — 45–50 mmHg (high CO2) causes vasodilation and RAISES ICP.
• 55-60 mmHg — 55–60 mmHg would raise ICP even more.

⭐ Key point: Avoid prolonged or routine hyperventilation in brain injury — it can worsen ischaemia. Normal target PaCO2 is 35–45 mmHg.$x$, null),
(94, $x$A CSF leak is suspected from a patient's nose after head trauma. Which bedside test helps confirm CSF rather than mucus?$x$, $x$Halo (ring) sign on gauze$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Halo (ring) sign on gauze
After head trauma, clear fluid from the nose (rhinorrhoea) or ear (otorrhoea) may be CSF leaking through a skull base fracture. The HALO (RING) SIGN is a simple bedside test: a drop of the fluid is placed on gauze or filter paper. If CSF is present (mixed with blood), it separates into a central red/pink spot surrounded by a clear or yellowish "halo" ring, because CSF spreads further than blood. Confirmation is by testing for GLUCOSE (CSF contains glucose; mucus does not) or, more accurately, BETA-2 TRANSFERRIN.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Kernig's sign — Kernig's sign tests for meningeal irritation.
• Doll's eye test — The doll's eye test (oculocephalic reflex) assesses brainstem function.
• Babinski test — The Babinski test assesses upper motor neurone damage.

⭐ Key point: With a suspected CSF leak: avoid nasal tubes and nasal suction, tell the patient not to blow the nose, keep head elevated, and watch for meningitis.$x$, null),
(95, $x$What does the brainstem control?$x$, $x$Breathing$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Breathing
The BRAINSTEM (midbrain, pons and medulla oblongata) connects the brain to the spinal cord and controls VITAL, automatic functions: BREATHING (respiratory centres in the medulla and pons), HEART RATE and BLOOD PRESSURE (cardiovascular centres), CONSCIOUSNESS and the sleep–wake cycle (reticular activating system), swallowing, coughing and vomiting reflexes, and most CRANIAL NERVE functions (pupil reactions, eye movements, facial sensation, gag reflex).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Feelings — Feelings and emotions are processed mainly by the limbic system and frontal lobes.
• Balance — Balance and coordination are mainly controlled by the cerebellum.
• Emotions — Emotions are processed by the limbic system (e.g. amygdala).

⭐ Key point: Brainstem death means permanent loss of brainstem reflexes and the ability to breathe; it is legally recognised as death.$x$, null),
(96, $x$Mr kifakat is on mechanical ventilator in ICU with a diagnosis of Brain death, what is his GCS$x$, $x$3/15$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 3/15
In BRAIN DEATH, there is NO brain function at all. On the GLASGOW COMA SCALE: EYES — no eye opening = 1; VERBAL — no verbal response = 1 (or "T" if intubated); MOTOR — no motor response = 1. The total is the LOWEST possible score: 3/15. (Spinal reflexes may still be present, because the spinal cord can work independently.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 6/15 — 6/15 means some responses are present.
• 4/15 — 4/15 means at least one response (e.g. extension or eye opening to pain).
• 5/15 — 5/15 means some responses are present.

⭐ Key point: Brain death is confirmed by formal brainstem testing by two senior doctors (after excluding drugs, hypothermia and metabolic causes), not by GCS alone.$x$, null),
(97, $x$Alcohol withdrawal symptoms typically begin how long after the last drink?$x$, $x$6–24 hours$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 6–24 hours
In people dependent on alcohol, WITHDRAWAL symptoms usually begin about 6–24 HOURS after the LAST DRINK, as blood alcohol falls: tremor ("the shakes"), anxiety, sweating, nausea, vomiting, tachycardia, hypertension and insomnia. WITHDRAWAL SEIZURES may occur at 12–48 hours. DELIRIUM TREMENS (confusion, hallucinations, fever, severe autonomic instability) usually appears at 48–72 hours and can be fatal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediately, within minutes — Symptoms do not begin immediately (within minutes).
• 1–2 weeks — 1–2 weeks is too late for the onset of withdrawal.
• Only after 1 month — 1 month is far too late.

⭐ Key point: Ask about alcohol intake on admission, use a withdrawal scale (CIWA-Ar), give benzodiazepines as needed, and give IV thiamine before glucose.$x$, null),
(98, $x$A patient in the ICU is agitated, disoriented, and pulling at IV lines. The nurse FIRST:$x$, $x$Assesses for pain or urinary retention$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Assesses for pain or urinary retention
An AGITATED, DISORIENTED ICU patient pulling at lines may be in DELIRIUM, but the agitation often has a TREATABLE CAUSE: PAIN, a FULL BLADDER (URINARY RETENTION — e.g. blocked catheter), hypoxia, hypoglycaemia, sepsis, drug or alcohol withdrawal, or discomfort (positioning, thirst). The FIRST action is to ASSESS for these causes — e.g. use a pain scale (CPOT/BPS), check SpO2 and glucose, check the catheter and bladder (bladder scan), while keeping the patient safe.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administers haloperidol — Haloperidol may be considered later for distressing agitation, but not before looking for the cause.
• Applies restraints — Restraints are a last resort; they can increase agitation and injury.
• Increases sedation — Increasing sedation may mask the cause and worsen delirium.

⭐ Key point: Treat the cause, reorient the patient, involve family, and use the least restrictive measures.$x$, null),
(99, $x$A patient with alcohol withdrawal delirium should receive:$x$, $x$Benzodiazepines$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Benzodiazepines
ALCOHOL WITHDRAWAL DELIRIUM (DELIRIUM TREMENS) is caused by sudden loss of alcohol's calming effect on GABA receptors, causing over-excitation of the brain. BENZODIAZEPINES (e.g. diazepam, lorazepam, chlordiazepoxide) act on the SAME GABA receptors as alcohol, so they calm the brain, control agitation and tremor, and PREVENT SEIZURES. They are the first-line treatment. IV THIAMINE should also be given to prevent Wernicke's encephalopathy, along with fluids and electrolyte correction.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Antipsychotics — Antipsychotics (e.g. haloperidol) may lower the seizure threshold; they are only add-ons for severe hallucinations.
• Opioids — Opioids do not treat alcohol withdrawal.
• Barbiturates — Barbiturates (e.g. phenobarbital) may be used for benzodiazepine-resistant cases, but are not first-line.

⭐ Key point: Monitor sedation and breathing closely during high-dose benzodiazepine treatment; severe cases need ICU.$x$, null),
(100, $x$A patient with hypoactive delirium is at risk for:$x$, $x$All of the above$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: All of the above
HYPOACTIVE DELIRIUM is the "quiet" type: the patient is drowsy, withdrawn, slow to respond and inactive. Because they are not disruptive, it is often MISSED. These patients are at risk of: FALLS (confusion and weakness when they try to get up), MALNUTRITION and dehydration (they eat and drink poorly), PRESSURE ULCERS (they lie still for long periods), aspiration, DVT and longer hospital stays. So "ALL OF THE ABOVE" is correct. Hypoactive delirium also carries a higher mortality than the hyperactive type.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Falls — Falls are only one risk.
• Malnutrition — Malnutrition is only one risk.
• Pressure ulcers — Pressure ulcers are only one risk.

⭐ Key point: Screen every patient regularly with CAM-ICU or 4AT to detect hypoactive delirium.$x$, null),
(101, $x$Kinking of the endotracheal tube, another cause of obstruction, is most likely to occur:$x$, $x$At the point where the tube exits the mouth or nose if not properly secured, or if the patient's head is turned sharply$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: At the point where the tube exits the mouth or nose if not properly secured, or if the patient's head is turned sharply
ET tube KINKING usually happens where the tube EXITS the MOUTH or NOSE, especially if it isn't secured properly or the patient's head is turned sharply. Kinking causes high airway pressures and poor ventilation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only inside the ventilator circuit — Kinking isn't only inside the circuit.
• In the trachea below the carina — It doesn't occur below the carina.
• Only with tracheostomy tubes — It isn't only with tracheostomy tubes.

⭐ Key point: Secure the tube properly and support the tubing with holders.$x$, null),
(102, $x$A ventilator's "sensitivity" can be set to trigger by flow or by pressure change. Flow triggering is generally preferred because it:$x$, $x$Requires less patient work of breathing to initiate a breath than pressure triggering$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Requires less patient work of breathing to initiate a breath than pressure triggering
FLOW TRIGGERING detects a small change in flow when the patient starts to breathe. It requires LESS WORK from the patient than PRESSURE TRIGGERING (which requires the patient to lower the pressure in the circuit).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Always delivers a larger tidal volume — It doesn't always deliver larger volumes.
• Eliminates the need for PEEP — It doesn't eliminate PEEP.
• Prevents all forms of asynchrony — It doesn't prevent all asynchrony.

⭐ Key point: Set sensitivity carefully to avoid auto-triggering.$x$, null),
(103, $x$Chronic bronchitis, one of the components of COPD, is clinically defined as:$x$, $x$A productive cough occurring on most days for at least 3 months in each of 2 consecutive years$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A productive cough occurring on most days for at least 3 months in each of 2 consecutive years
CHRONIC BRONCHITIS is defined clinically as a PRODUCTIVE COUGH occurring on most days for at least 3 MONTHS in each of 2 CONSECUTIVE YEARS, after other causes are excluded.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A single episode of productive cough lasting more than 2 weeks — A single 2-week episode is acute bronchitis.
• Cough occurring only at night for one month — Night cough for 1 month doesn't meet the definition.
• Wheeze without any cough for 6 months — Wheeze without cough doesn't meet the definition.

⭐ Key point: Smoking is the main cause; stopping smoking is the most effective intervention.$x$, null),
(104, $x$What musculoskeletal complication is common with chest compressions during CPR?$x$, $x$Rib fractures and/or sternal fracture$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Rib fractures and/or sternal fracture
Effective CHEST COMPRESSIONS in adults must be 5–6 cm deep at 100–120 per minute. This force commonly causes RIB FRACTURES and/or STERNAL FRACTURES, especially in older people with brittle bones. Other possible injuries: costochondral separation, lung contusion, and (rarely) liver or spleen injury. These injuries are ACCEPTABLE because effective CPR is life-saving — never reduce compression depth because of fear of fractures.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Skull fracture — Skull fractures are not caused by chest compressions.
• Pelvic fracture — Pelvic fractures are not caused by chest compressions.
• Femur fracture — Femur fractures are not caused by chest compressions.

⭐ Key point: Correct hand position (centre of the chest, lower half of the sternum) reduces the risk of injury to abdominal organs.$x$, null),
(105, $x$Before caring for a patient on a mechanical ventilator, the nurse should be familiar with all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$The patient's dietary preferences prior to admission$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The patient's dietary preferences prior to admission
Before caring for a ventilated patient, the nurse must know: the VENTILATOR MODE and SETTINGS, ALARM LIMITS and TROUBLESHOOTING, and signs of a BLOCKED or DISPLACED ET tube. The patient's DIETARY PREFERENCES before admission are not essential for ventilator safety.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The ventilator mode and settings in use — Mode and settings are essential.
• Alarm limits and troubleshooting — Alarm limits are essential.
• Signs of a blocked or displaced endotracheal tube — Tube problems are essential.

⭐ Key point: Always have a bag-valve-mask and suction at the bedside.$x$, null),
(106, $x$Hypercarbia during anaesthesia refers to:$x$, $x$Elevated blood carbon dioxide levels$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Elevated blood carbon dioxide levels
HYPERCARBIA (also called hypercapnia) means an ELEVATED level of CARBON DIOXIDE in the blood (PaCO2 above 45 mmHg). During anaesthesia, causes include: HYPOVENTILATION (opioids, residual muscle relaxants, inadequate ventilator settings), REBREATHING (exhausted CO2 absorber, faulty valves), increased CO2 PRODUCTION (fever, sepsis, MALIGNANT HYPERTHERMIA), and CO2 absorption during laparoscopy. Effects: acidosis, tachycardia, hypertension, arrhythmias, drowsiness, and raised ICP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Low blood oxygen levels — LOW blood oxygen is HYPOXAEMIA.
• Elevated blood glucose — High blood glucose is HYPERGLYCAEMIA.
• Low blood pressure — Low BP is HYPOTENSION.

⭐ Key point: Capnography (end-tidal CO2) is the main monitor to detect hypercarbia during anaesthesia.$x$, null),
(107, $x$Fatigue combined with somnolence in a severe asthmatic is classified as:$x$, $x$A relative indication to strongly consider intubation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A relative indication to strongly consider intubation
In SEVERE ASTHMA, FATIGUE (exhaustion) with SOMNOLENCE (drowsiness) means the breathing muscles are tiring and CO2 is rising — the patient is heading towards respiratory failure. This is a RELATIVE INDICATION to STRONGLY CONSIDER INTUBATION (and an urgent ICU review). ABSOLUTE indications are cardiac or respiratory arrest, coma, or severe refractory hypoxaemia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An absolute indication for intubation — It is not an absolute indication on its own; absolute ones include arrest and coma.
• A sign of successful treatment — Drowsiness and fatigue are signs of DETERIORATION, not successful treatment.
• Not clinically significant — They are highly significant warning signs.

⭐ Key point: Other danger signs in asthma: silent chest, cyanosis, SpO2 below 92%, normal or rising PaCO2, bradycardia and hypotension.$x$, null),
(108, $x$The nurse calls for you to help extubate a female patient who is orally intubated. All of the following equipment is required for this procedure EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Arterial blood gas kit$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Arterial blood gas kit
EXTUBATION can fail suddenly (laryngospasm, airway oedema, aspiration, weakness), so emergency equipment must be ready: SUCTION equipment (to clear the mouth, throat and tube before removal), a BAG-VALVE-MASK with oxygen (to support breathing if needed), and a NEW ENDOTRACHEAL TUBE with laryngoscope (in case RE-INTUBATION is needed). Also: oxygen delivery device (face mask or nasal cannula), syringe to deflate the cuff, and monitoring. An ARTERIAL BLOOD GAS KIT is not required for the extubation procedure itself (an ABG may be checked later if needed).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• New endotracheal tube — A new ET tube IS needed for possible re-intubation.
• Suction equipment — Suction IS needed.
• Bag-valve mask — A bag-valve-mask IS needed.

⭐ Key point: After extubation, sit the patient up, give oxygen, encourage coughing, and watch closely for stridor and respiratory distress.$x$, null),
(109, $x$A patient is extubated after general anaesthesia and shortly develops high-pitched inspiratory stridor. The nurse should suspect:$x$, $x$Laryngospasm or airway oedema, requiring urgent assessment and possible intervention$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Laryngospasm or airway oedema, requiring urgent assessment and possible intervention
HIGH-PITCHED INSPIRATORY STRIDOR soon after extubation means the UPPER AIRWAY is NARROWED — usually from LARYNGOSPASM (spasm of the vocal cords) or AIRWAY OEDEMA (swelling of the larynx from the tube). It can quickly progress to complete obstruction. Urgent actions: call for help, sit the patient up, give high-flow oxygen, and consider nebulised adrenaline, IV steroids (dexamethasone), positive pressure (CPAP) for laryngospasm, and preparation for re-intubation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal post-extubation voice change with no concern — Stridor is not a normal voice change; hoarseness may be normal, stridor is not.
• Improved airway patency — Stridor shows a NARROWED airway, not improved patency.
• A sign of adequate recovery — Stridor is a sign of a dangerous problem, not adequate recovery.

⭐ Key point: A "cuff leak test" before extubation can help predict post-extubation airway oedema.$x$, null),
(110, $x$Early signs of local anaesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST) include:$x$, $x$Perioral numbness, tinnitus, and metallic taste$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Perioral numbness, tinnitus, and metallic taste
LOCAL ANAESTHETIC SYSTEMIC TOXICITY (LAST) happens when local anaesthetic (especially bupivacaine) reaches high levels in the blood. EARLY signs are usually neurological: PERIORAL NUMBNESS (tingling around the mouth and tongue), TINNITUS (ringing in the ears), METALLIC TASTE, light-headedness, dizziness, visual disturbance, agitation and confusion. These can progress quickly to SEIZURES, then cardiovascular toxicity (arrhythmias, hypotension, cardiac arrest).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bradycardia only — Bradycardia alone is a later cardiovascular sign, not an early sign.
• Hyperthermia — Hyperthermia is not typical of LAST.
• Improved level of consciousness — Consciousness WORSENS, not improves.

⭐ Key point: If LAST is suspected: stop the injection, call for help, give oxygen, treat seizures with benzodiazepines, and give 20% lipid emulsion.$x$, null),
(111, $x$The equipment needed for nasotracheal intubation includes the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Chromic 2/0 suture$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Chromic 2/0 suture
NASOTRACHEAL INTUBATION passes the tube through the nose into the trachea (used in oral/maxillofacial surgery or when the mouth can't be opened). Equipment includes: LARYNGOSCOPE (to see the vocal cords), ENDOTRACHEAL TUBE (often a smaller, softer nasal tube), MAGILL FORCEPS (to guide the tube tip through the vocal cords), lubricant, nasal vasoconstrictor spray (to reduce bleeding), suction and oxygen. A CHROMIC 2/0 SUTURE (an absorbable surgical stitch) is NOT needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Magill forceps — Magill forceps ARE needed.
• Laryngoscope — A laryngoscope IS needed.
• Endotracheal tube — An ET tube IS needed.

⭐ Key point: Nasotracheal intubation is contraindicated in suspected basal skull fracture and severe clotting problems.$x$, null),
(112, $x$The nurse is monitoring a sedated patient using the Bispectral Index (BIS) monitoring. The purpose of BIS monitoring is to:$x$, $x$Measure the patient's level of consciousness$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Measure the patient's level of consciousness
The BISPECTRAL INDEX (BIS) monitor uses sensors on the forehead to analyse the brain's electrical activity (EEG) and produce a number from 0 to 100 that reflects the LEVEL OF CONSCIOUSNESS (depth of sedation/anaesthesia). 100 = fully awake; 60–80 = light to moderate sedation; 40–60 = general anaesthesia (low risk of awareness); below 40 = deep hypnotic state; 0 = no brain activity. It is especially useful in PARALYSED patients, who cannot show signs of awareness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Monitor the patient's blood pressure — BIS does not measure blood pressure.
• Assess the patient's heart rate — BIS does not measure heart rate.
• Assess the patient's muscle paralysis — Muscle paralysis is assessed with a peripheral nerve stimulator (train-of-four), not BIS.

⭐ Key point: Use BIS together with clinical assessment; muscle activity and some drugs can affect readings.$x$, null),
(113, $x$A patient recovering from anaesthesia after a lung transplant requires particular nursing vigilance for:$x$, $x$Signs of rejection and pulmonary infection$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Signs of rejection and pulmonary infection
After LUNG TRANSPLANT, patients take strong immunosuppressive drugs to prevent REJECTION, which makes them very vulnerable to INFECTION. The transplanted lung is also directly exposed to inhaled germs and has impaired cough reflex and lymphatic drainage. Nursing vigilance focuses on signs of REJECTION (breathlessness, falling SpO2, fever, new infiltrates, declining lung function) and PULMONARY INFECTION (fever, purulent sputum, new chest X-ray changes), as well as primary graft dysfunction, fluid balance and pain control.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only cardiac monitoring, with no respiratory focus — Respiratory monitoring is essential after lung transplant, not just cardiac.
• Skin integrity exclusively — Skin care is important, but not the main focus.
• Bowel function exclusively — Bowel function is important, but not the main focus.

⭐ Key point: Strict infection control (hand hygiene, protective isolation) and early mobilisation and physiotherapy are essential.$x$, null),
(114, $x$Infraglottic inhalational injury is mainly caused by:$x$, $x$Noxious chemical combustion products in smoke$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Noxious chemical combustion products in smoke
In INHALATION INJURY, the UPPER airway (above the vocal cords — supraglottic) absorbs most of the HEAT from hot gases (it is very efficient at cooling air), so direct thermal burns are usually limited to the upper airway. INFRAGLOTTIC (below the vocal cords) injury is mainly caused by NOXIOUS CHEMICAL COMBUSTION PRODUCTS in SMOKE — e.g. aldehydes, ammonia, sulphur dioxide, chlorine, and acids from burning plastics — which irritate and damage the lining of the trachea, bronchi and alveoli, causing bronchospasm, oedema, sloughing of the mucosa, and ARDS. (Steam is an exception — it carries heat lower.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Direct heat — Direct heat mainly injures the UPPER (supraglottic) airway.
• Blunt trauma — Blunt trauma is not the mechanism.
• Radiation exposure — Radiation exposure is not the mechanism.

⭐ Key point: Suspect inhalation injury with burns in an enclosed space, soot in the mouth or sputum, hoarseness, and facial burns.$x$, null),
(115, $x$What percentage of solitary thyroid nodules are malignant?$x$, $x$15%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 15%
SOLITARY THYROID NODULES are common (especially in women and older people). Most are benign (e.g. colloid nodules, adenomas, cysts). About 5–15% are MALIGNANT (thyroid cancer). The risk is higher in men, children, older adults, people with a history of radiation to the neck, rapidly growing or hard, fixed nodules, hoarseness (recurrent laryngeal nerve involvement), and enlarged neck lymph nodes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1% — 1% underestimates the cancer risk.
• 5% — 5% is the lower end of the range.
• 50% — 50% greatly overestimates.

⭐ Key point: Assess nodules with thyroid function tests, ultrasound, and fine-needle aspiration (FNA) cytology.$x$, null),
(116, $x$Which is NOT a marker of acute organ dysfunction?$x$, $x$Decreased creatinine level$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Decreased creatinine level
ACUTE ORGAN DYSFUNCTION (as in sepsis) is shown by markers such as: JAUNDICE (raised bilirubin — liver dysfunction), THROMBOCYTOPENIA (low platelets — haematological/clotting dysfunction), HYPOTENSION (cardiovascular dysfunction), RAISED CREATININE or low urine output (kidney dysfunction), low GCS/confusion (brain dysfunction), and low PaO2/FiO2 (lung dysfunction). A DECREASED CREATININE does NOT indicate organ dysfunction — kidney dysfunction causes creatinine to RISE.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Jaundice — Jaundice IS a marker of liver dysfunction.
• Thrombocytopenia — Thrombocytopenia IS a marker of haematological dysfunction.
• Hypotension — Hypotension IS a marker of cardiovascular dysfunction.

⭐ Key point: The SOFA score uses these markers to grade organ dysfunction in sepsis.$x$, null),
(117, $x$Systemic burn shock risk begins above what %TBSA?$x$, $x$>20%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: >20%
Burns cause local tissue damage, but large burns trigger a SYSTEMIC inflammatory response: capillaries throughout the body become LEAKY, and large amounts of fluid and protein move out of the blood vessels into the tissues ("burn shock" — a form of hypovolaemic and distributive shock). This systemic response usually begins when burns exceed about 20% TBSA in adults (some guidelines use 15%; about 10% in children and elderly). These patients need formal IV fluid resuscitation (e.g. Parkland formula).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• >5% — 5% TBSA is too small to cause systemic shock in most adults.
• >10% — 10% is the usual threshold in children, not adults.
• >50% — 50% is far above the threshold.

⭐ Key point: Monitor urine output hourly (target 0.5–1 mL/kg/hr in adults) to guide fluid resuscitation.$x$, null),
(118, $x$A patient with Clostridioides difficile infection requires which type of isolation precaution?$x$, $x$Contact precautions, with gown and gloves, and hand hygiene using soap and water (not alcohol-based sanitiser alone) given spore resistance$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Contact precautions, with gown and gloves, and hand hygiene using soap and water (not alcohol-based sanitiser alone) given spore resistance
CLOSTRIDIOIDES DIFFICILE (C. diff) causes diarrhoea and colitis, often after antibiotic use. It spreads by CONTACT (faecal–oral route via hands, equipment and surfaces) and forms SPORES that survive for months in the environment and are NOT killed by alcohol hand rub. Precautions: CONTACT PRECAUTIONS — single room with its own toilet, GLOVES and GOWN/apron for all contact, dedicated equipment — plus HAND HYGIENE with SOAP AND WATER (which physically removes spores), and cleaning with chlorine-based (sporicidal) disinfectant.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Airborne precautions with N95 respirator — C. diff is not airborne; N95 respirators are not needed.
• No precautions are necessary — Precautions ARE necessary — it spreads easily.
• Droplet precautions only — It is not spread by droplets.

⭐ Key point: Stop unnecessary antibiotics (especially broad-spectrum ones) and avoid anti-diarrhoeal drugs in C. diff infection.$x$, null),
(119, $x$A patient with obstructive shock from massive pulmonary embolism would be expected to show:$x$, $x$Low blood pressure with signs of right heart strain (e.g. elevated JVP) from obstructed pulmonary blood flow$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Low blood pressure with signs of right heart strain (e.g. elevated JVP) from obstructed pulmonary blood flow
In OBSTRUCTIVE SHOCK from MASSIVE PE, blood can't flow through the lungs. The right ventricle is strained and fails, causing LOW BP, RAISED JVP, tachycardia and hypoxia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Warm, flushed skin with bounding pulses — Warm, flushed skin is typical of septic shock.
• Bradycardia with normal blood pressure — Bradycardia with normal BP is not typical.
• No cardiovascular compromise — Massive PE causes compromise.

⭐ Key point: Treat with thrombolysis if unstable.$x$, null),
(120, $x$Which of the following is not a cause of polytrauma?$x$, $x$Infection$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Infection
POLYTRAUMA means multiple significant injuries to different body regions (e.g. head, chest, abdomen, limbs), usually from high-energy mechanisms: BOMB BLASTS (blast, shrapnel, burns, crush), FALLS FROM HEIGHT, road traffic ACCIDENTS (cars, motorcycles — very common in Nigeria), assaults, gunshots, and building collapses. INFECTION is a disease process caused by microorganisms, not a physical force, so it is NOT a cause of trauma (though infection can be a complication of trauma).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bomb blast — Bomb blasts ARE a cause of polytrauma.
• Fall from height — Falls from height ARE a cause.
• Accident — Accidents ARE a cause.

⭐ Key point: Manage polytrauma with the ATLS approach: primary survey (ABCDE), resuscitation, then secondary survey.$x$, null),
(121, $x$The main complication of FHF, occurring in about 80% of cases, is:$x$, $x$Cerebral oedema$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Cerebral oedema
FULMINANT HEPATIC FAILURE (FHF) is rapid liver failure with encephalopathy in a person without previous liver disease (e.g. from paracetamol overdose or viral hepatitis). The main and most dangerous complication is CEREBRAL OEDEMA (brain swelling), which develops in up to about 80% of patients with severe (grade 3–4) encephalopathy. Ammonia and other toxins that the failing liver cannot clear cause brain cells (astrocytes) to swell. This raises intracranial pressure and can cause brain herniation and death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Renal calculi — Renal calculi (kidney stones) are not a typical complication.
• Pancreatitis — Pancreatitis is not a typical complication.
• Peptic ulcer — Peptic ulcer is not the main complication.

⭐ Key point: Manage with head-up 30°, avoid hypoxia and fever, control ammonia, consider hypertonic saline, and refer early for liver transplant.$x$, null),
(122, $x$During a multi-vehicle road traffic accident with 15 casualties and limited ambulances, the triage officer applies START triage. A casualty who is breathing at 35/min after airway repositioning is categorised as:$x$, $x$Immediate (red) — respiratory rate outside the normal range signals urgent priority$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Immediate (red) — respiratory rate outside the normal range signals urgent priority
START TRIAGE (Simple Triage and Rapid Treatment) is used in mass casualties. Steps: (1) Can the patient WALK? If yes → GREEN (minor). (2) Is the patient BREATHING? If no after airway repositioning → BLACK. (3) Respiratory rate above 30/min → RED (IMMEDIATE). (4) Check perfusion (capillary refill over 2 s or no radial pulse) → RED. (5) Can't follow simple commands → RED. Otherwise → YELLOW (delayed). A casualty breathing at 35/min is categorised as IMMEDIATE (RED).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Minor (green) — can wait — Green is for the walking wounded.
• Deceased (black) — Black is for those not breathing after airway repositioning.
• Delayed (yellow) — Yellow is for those who can wait but are not walking.

⭐ Key point: START aims to do the greatest good for the greatest number with limited resources.$x$, null),
(123, $x$Calcitonin is a tumour marker specifically associated with which type of thyroid cancer?$x$, $x$Medullary$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Medullary
CALCITONIN is a hormone made by the PARAFOLLICULAR (C) CELLS of the thyroid, which help regulate calcium. MEDULLARY THYROID CANCER arises from these C cells and produces large amounts of calcitonin, so CALCITONIN is used as a TUMOUR MARKER for diagnosis and follow-up (along with CEA). Medullary thyroid cancer can be inherited as part of MULTIPLE ENDOCRINE NEOPLASIA type 2 (MEN 2), linked to the RET gene.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Papillary — Papillary cancer arises from follicular cells; thyroglobulin is used for follow-up.
• Follicular — Follicular cancer arises from follicular cells; thyroglobulin is used.
• Anaplastic — Anaplastic cancer has no specific tumour marker.

⭐ Key point: Patients with medullary thyroid cancer should be screened for phaeochromocytoma before surgery (MEN 2).$x$, null),
(124, $x$A patient with severe burns and multisystem trauma in a resource-limited disaster is triaged as black because:$x$, $x$A and C$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A and C
In DISASTER TRIAGE (mass casualties with limited resources), a BLACK (EXPECTANT) category is used for patients whose injuries are so severe that SURVIVAL is UNLIKELY even with treatment, AND treating them would use up SCARCE RESOURCES (staff, blood, theatre time) needed to save more people with survivable injuries. So "A AND C" is correct. These patients still receive comfort care (pain relief) and are reassessed if resources become available.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Survival is unlikely — Unlikely survival alone is only part of the reason.
• The patient is deceased — Being deceased is also categorised as black, but this patient is described as alive.
• Resources are needed elsewhere — Resource needs alone is only part of the reason.

⭐ Key point: Disaster triage shifts from "best for each patient" to "greatest good for the greatest number".$x$, null),
(125, $x$The first clinical symptom of tetanus is typically:$x$, $x$Trismus (lockjaw)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Trismus (lockjaw)
TETANUS is caused by a toxin from Clostridium tetani (from contaminated wounds). The toxin blocks inhibitory nerve signals, causing muscle spasm and rigidity. The FIRST symptom is usually TRISMUS (LOCKJAW) — spasm of the jaw muscles (masseters), making it hard to open the mouth or chew. This is followed by stiffness of the neck and back, risus sardonicus (a fixed, grimacing smile), difficulty swallowing, abdominal rigidity, painful generalised spasms (opisthotonus) and autonomic instability.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Generalised convulsions — Generalised convulsions (spasms) occur LATER.
• Risus sardonicus — Risus sardonicus occurs after trismus.
• Neck stiffness — Neck stiffness may occur early but trismus is classically first.

⭐ Key point: Treatment: human tetanus immunoglobulin, wound debridement, metronidazole, muscle relaxants/sedation (benzodiazepines, magnesium), airway support, and a quiet, dark environment. Prevent by vaccination.$x$, null),
(126, $x$CVP that is low most likely indicates the need for:$x$, $x$Fluid administration$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fluid administration
CENTRAL VENOUS PRESSURE (CVP) reflects the pressure in the right atrium and is used as a rough guide to the patient's blood volume and right heart filling. Normal CVP is about 2–8 mmHg (or 3–10 cmH2O). A LOW CVP suggests HYPOVOLAEMIA (low blood volume — e.g. dehydration, bleeding, vasodilation), so FLUID ADMINISTRATION (fluid challenge) is usually indicated, with careful reassessment of BP, heart rate, urine output and CVP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diuretics — Diuretics would remove more fluid and worsen hypovolaemia.
• Vasoconstrictors only — Vasoconstrictors alone don't correct a low volume; fluids are needed first.
• No action needed — A low CVP with signs of poor perfusion needs action.

⭐ Key point: CVP trends and the response to a fluid challenge are more useful than a single reading.$x$, null),
(127, $x$A Mallory-Weiss tear classically occurs after:$x$, $x$Repeated vomiting$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Repeated vomiting
A MALLORY-WEISS TEAR is a tear in the lining (mucosa) at the junction of the oesophagus and stomach, caused by a sudden rise in pressure during REPEATED VOMITING or forceful retching — often after heavy alcohol intake, or in hyperemesis gravidarum or bulimia. It typically causes vomiting of bright red blood (haematemesis) after episodes of non-bloody vomiting. Most tears heal on their own within a few days; endoscopic treatment is needed if bleeding continues.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Chronic constipation — Chronic constipation is not a cause.
• A single episode of coughing — A single episode of coughing is unlikely to cause it.
• Eating spicy food — Eating spicy food does not cause it.

⭐ Key point: Distinguish from Boerhaave syndrome (full-thickness oesophageal rupture), which causes severe chest pain and surgical emphysema.$x$, null),
(128, $x$In mass casualty triage systems such as START, a casualty who is not breathing even after repositioning the airway is typically categorised as:$x$, $x$Deceased/expectant (black)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Deceased/expectant (black)
In START TRIAGE, a casualty who is NOT BREATHING even AFTER REPOSITIONING THE AIRWAY (head tilt/jaw thrust) is categorised as DECEASED/EXPECTANT (BLACK). In a mass casualty situation, resources are directed to people with survivable injuries; CPR is not usually started in this setting because it uses up many responders for one patient with a very low chance of survival. If the patient starts breathing after airway repositioning, they are categorised as IMMEDIATE (RED).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediate (red) — Immediate (red) is for those who breathe after airway repositioning or have other serious signs.
• Delayed (yellow) — Delayed (yellow) is for those who can wait.
• Minor (green) — Minor (green) is for the walking wounded.

⭐ Key point: Triage categories are reassessed as the situation and resources change.$x$, null),
(129, $x$The pyramidal lobe of the thyroid, when present, is a remnant of the:$x$, $x$Thyroglossal duct$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Thyroglossal duct
During development, the THYROID gland forms at the base of the tongue and migrates down the neck to its final position in front of the trachea, leaving behind a tube called the THYROGLOSSAL DUCT, which usually disappears. In about 40–50% of people, a small part of thyroid tissue remains along this path, forming the PYRAMIDAL LOBE, which extends upwards from the isthmus. Remnants can also form THYROGLOSSAL CYSTS (midline neck lumps that move upward when sticking out the tongue).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Parathyroid gland — It is not related to the parathyroid glands.
• Carotid sheath — It is not related to the carotid sheath.
• Trachea — It is not related to the trachea.

⭐ Key point: The pyramidal lobe may enlarge in Graves' disease and can be seen on thyroid scans.$x$, null),
(130, $x$Sepsis is currently defined (Sepsis-3) as:$x$, $x$Life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection
SEPSIS-3 (2016) defines SEPSIS as LIFE-THREATENING ORGAN DYSFUNCTION caused by a DYSREGULATED HOST RESPONSE to INFECTION. Organ dysfunction is identified by an acute rise in the SOFA score of 2 or more points. SEPTIC SHOCK is a subset with circulatory and metabolic abnormalities: needing vasopressors to keep MAP ≥65 mmHg AND lactate above 2 mmol/L despite adequate fluids. The older SIRS-based definitions are no longer used.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Any infection, regardless of organ function — Infection without organ dysfunction is not sepsis.
• A fever above 38°C alone — Fever alone does not define sepsis.
• Bacteraemia without any clinical symptoms — Bacteraemia without symptoms is not sepsis.

⭐ Key point: Use qSOFA (respiratory rate ≥22, altered mentation, systolic BP ≤100) as a quick bedside screen outside ICU.$x$, null),
(131, $x$The female-to-male ratio in goitre incidence is approximately:$x$, $x$10:1$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 10:1
GOITRE (enlargement of the thyroid gland) is much more common in WOMEN than men. The female-to-male ratio is often quoted as about 4:1 up to 10:1, depending on the type and population. Reasons include the effects of oestrogen and pregnancy (increased iodine needs) and the higher rate of autoimmune thyroid disease (Graves', Hashimoto's) in women. Iodine deficiency is the most common cause of goitre worldwide.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1:1 — 1:1 is incorrect — goitre is not equally common.
• 2:1 — 2:1 underestimates the difference.
• 5:1 — 5:1 is within the range but lower than the figure used in this exam.

⭐ Key point: Iodised salt has greatly reduced goitre in many countries, including Nigeria.$x$, null),
(132, $x$A nurse is providing psychological support to a burn patient. The nurse should be aware that the patient may experience which of the following emotional responses?$x$, $x$Anxiety, depression, and body image disturbance$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Anxiety, depression, and body image disturbance
Major burns are physically and emotionally traumatic. Patients commonly experience ANXIETY (fear of pain, procedures, death), DEPRESSION, POST-TRAUMATIC STRESS (flashbacks, nightmares), sleep problems, grief and loss, and BODY IMAGE DISTURBANCE (scarring, disfigurement, loss of function, changes in appearance). These responses can affect recovery, adherence to treatment and return to normal life.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A sense of euphoria — Euphoria is not a typical response.
• Apathy and indifference — Apathy may occur, but anxiety, depression and body image problems are more typical.
• A rapid return to normal daily life — Recovery is usually long and difficult, not a rapid return to normal.

⭐ Key point: Provide early psychological support, adequate pain relief, honest information, family involvement, and referral to counselling or support groups.$x$, null),
(133, $x$A patient with an electrical burn injury passes dark, tea-coloured urine. The nurse should suspect:$x$, $x$Myoglobinuria from muscle breakdown, requiring aggressive fluid therapy and monitoring for acute kidney injury$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Myoglobinuria from muscle breakdown, requiring aggressive fluid therapy and monitoring for acute kidney injury
High-voltage ELECTRICAL injuries cause extensive DEEP MUSCLE DAMAGE along the path of the current (even when skin burns look small). Damaged muscle releases MYOGLOBIN into the blood (RHABDOMYOLYSIS), which turns urine DARK, TEA- or COLA-COLOURED (MYOGLOBINURIA). Myoglobin can block the kidney tubules and cause ACUTE KIDNEY INJURY. Treatment: AGGRESSIVE IV FLUIDS (aim for urine output 1–2 mL/kg/hr until the urine clears), monitoring of creatinine, CK and potassium, and possibly mannitol and bicarbonate per protocol.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A normal finding requiring no action — Dark urine after electrical injury is NOT normal.
• A urinary tract infection only — A urinary tract infection would not explain this picture.
• Dehydration alone with no other significance — Dehydration may concentrate urine, but tea-coloured urine after electrical injury suggests myoglobinuria.

⭐ Key point: Also watch for hyperkalaemia, cardiac arrhythmias (continuous ECG), and compartment syndrome.$x$, null),
(134, $x$The correct order of the ABCDE primary survey in trauma/emergency assessment is:$x$, $x$Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Disability, Exposure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Disability, Exposure
The ABCDE PRIMARY SURVEY assesses and treats life-threatening problems in the order they kill: A = AIRWAY (with cervical spine protection in trauma); B = BREATHING (ventilation and oxygenation); C = CIRCULATION (with haemorrhage control); D = DISABILITY (neurological status: GCS/AVPU, pupils, glucose); E = EXPOSURE (fully undress to look for injuries, while preventing hypothermia). Problems are treated as they are found before moving on.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Breathing, Airway, Disability, Circulation, Exposure — Breathing cannot be effective without an open airway, so airway comes first.
• Circulation, Airway, Breathing, Exposure, Disability — Circulation is not assessed before airway and breathing (except catastrophic haemorrhage in some military/pre-hospital systems — "C-ABCDE").
• Disability, Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Exposure — Disability is assessed after ABC.

⭐ Key point: Reassess ABCDE after every intervention and whenever the patient's condition changes.$x$, null),
(135, $x$Primary postpartum haemorrhage following a caesarean section is defined as blood loss of:$x$, $x$≥1000ml$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: ≥1000ml
POSTPARTUM HAEMORRHAGE (PPH) is defined by the amount of blood loss within 24 hours of birth: after VAGINAL birth — 500 mL or more; after CAESAREAN SECTION — 1,000 mL or more (because more blood is normally lost during surgery). Major PPH is often defined as more than 1,000 mL, and severe as more than 2,000 mL. Any blood loss causing signs of shock is PPH regardless of the amount.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• ≥250ml — 250 mL is a normal blood loss.
• ≥500ml — 500 mL is the threshold for vaginal birth.
• ≥3000ml — 3,000 mL is massive haemorrhage, far above the definition threshold.

⭐ Key point: Blood loss is often underestimated — weigh pads and drapes, and watch vital signs.$x$, null),
(136, $x$Clinical features of hyperemesis gravidarum include all EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Insomnia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Insomnia
HYPEREMESIS GRAVIDARUM causes severe, persistent nausea and vomiting in pregnancy. Typical features: VOMITING many times a day (more than 3–4 episodes), inability to keep food or fluids down, WEIGHT LOSS of more than 5% of pre-pregnancy weight, DEHYDRATION (DIZZINESS, dry mouth, low urine output, tachycardia, postural hypotension), ketonuria, electrolyte imbalance (low potassium, low sodium), and fatigue. INSOMNIA (inability to sleep) is not a typical clinical feature.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Vomiting more than 3–4 times a day — Frequent vomiting IS a feature.
• Weight loss of more than 5% — Weight loss of more than 5% IS a feature.
• Dizziness — Dizziness (from dehydration) IS a feature.

⭐ Key point: Treat with IV fluids, anti-emetics, electrolyte replacement, thiamine, and thromboprophylaxis if admitted.$x$, null),
(137, $x$A newborn requires resuscitation immediately after birth and is not breathing adequately. The priority initial action is to:$x$, $x$Dry, stimulate, and position the airway, then provide positive pressure ventilation if breathing remains inadequate$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Dry, stimulate, and position the airway, then provide positive pressure ventilation if breathing remains inadequate
Most newborns who need help at birth have a BREATHING problem. The first steps (within the first 30–60 seconds) are: DRY the baby (and keep warm), STIMULATE (drying and rubbing), and POSITION THE AIRWAY (head in neutral position, clear the airway if obstructed). If the baby is still NOT BREATHING ADEQUATELY (apnoea, gasping) or the heart rate is below 100/min, give POSITIVE PRESSURE VENTILATION (inflation breaths with a bag-valve-mask or T-piece). Effective ventilation usually improves the heart rate quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Wait several minutes before intervening — Waiting several minutes allows hypoxic brain injury.
• Immediately begin chest compressions before assessing breathing — Chest compressions come after ventilation, only if the heart rate stays below 60/min.
• Give oxygen by face mask only, with no other assessment — Face mask oxygen alone does not help a baby who is not breathing.

⭐ Key point: The "golden minute": begin ventilation within the first minute after birth if needed.$x$, null),
(138, $x$Warning signs that may precede an eclamptic convulsion include:$x$, $x$Severe headache and blurred vision$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Severe headache and blurred vision
WARNING SIGNS of impending ECLAMPSIA (seizures) in pre-eclampsia include: SEVERE HEADACHE (often frontal, not relieved by simple painkillers), VISUAL DISTURBANCES (BLURRED VISION, flashing lights, spots), epigastric or right upper quadrant pain (liver involvement), nausea and vomiting, HYPERREFLEXIA and CLONUS, sudden oedema of the face and hands, and rising BP. These signs mean the brain is irritated and seizures may soon occur.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Improved appetite — Improved appetite is not a warning sign.
• Reduced blood pressure — BP usually RISES, not falls.
• Increased urine output only — Urine output usually FALLS (oliguria), not increases.

⭐ Key point: Give magnesium sulphate, control BP, and plan for delivery when these warning signs appear.$x$, null),
(139, $x$The following are complications of eclampsia except$x$, $x$Sepsis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Sepsis
ECLAMPSIA (seizures in pre-eclampsia) can cause serious complications: NEUROLOGICAL DEFICIT (stroke, cerebral haemorrhage, cerebral oedema, blindness), PULMONARY OEDEMA, ACUTE RENAL FAILURE, HELLP syndrome, DIC, liver rupture, placental abruption, aspiration pneumonia, and maternal and fetal death. SEPSIS is an infection and is NOT a direct complication of eclampsia (although it can occur in any hospitalised patient).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Neurological deficit — Neurological deficit IS a complication.
• Pulmonary oedema — Pulmonary oedema IS a complication.
• Acute renal failure — Acute renal failure IS a complication.

⭐ Key point: Eclampsia is a leading cause of maternal death in Nigeria; early antenatal care and BP monitoring help prevent it.$x$, null),
(140, $x$In the Pritchard regimen of MgSO4, which is correct?$x$, $x$The maintenance dose is 5 g IM 4-hourly$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The maintenance dose is 5 g IM 4-hourly
The PRITCHARD REGIMEN of MgSO4: loading dose 4 g IV + 10 g IM (5 g in each buttock), then MAINTENANCE of 5 g IM every 4 HOURS in alternate buttocks.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2 g/hour IV works faster — 2 g/hour IV is not the Pritchard regimen.
• 5 g is the total loading dose — The loading dose is 14 g total, not 5 g.
• It is the only accepted regimen — Other regimens (e.g. Zuspan) are also accepted.

⭐ Key point: Check reflexes, breathing and urine output before each dose.$x$, null),
(141, $x$Umbilical cord prolapse is an obstetric emergency because it can cause:$x$, $x$Cord compression leading to fetal hypoxia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Cord compression leading to fetal hypoxia
UMBILICAL CORD PROLAPSE happens when the cord slips down through the cervix BEFORE (or beside) the baby's presenting part, usually after the membranes rupture. The baby's head or body then presses the cord against the pelvis, COMPRESSING the umbilical vessels, and the cold air and handling cause the cord vessels to spasm. Blood flow to the baby is cut off, causing FETAL HYPOXIA, bradycardia and death or brain damage within minutes unless delivery is rapid. Risk factors: malpresentation (breech, transverse), polyhydramnios, preterm baby, multiple pregnancy, and artificial rupture of membranes with a high head.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Improved fetal oxygenation — Cord prolapse REDUCES, not improves, fetal oxygenation.
• Maternal hypertension only — It does not cause maternal hypertension.
• No risk if the mother is asymptomatic — The danger is to the baby even if the mother feels well.

⭐ Key point: Emergency actions: call for help, keep the presenting part off the cord (manually or by filling the bladder), knee–chest or exaggerated Sims' position, avoid handling the cord, and arrange immediate delivery (usually caesarean section).$x$, null),
(142, $x$Which of the following is part of the definition of hyperemesis gravidarum?$x$, $x$Severe nausea and vomiting with weight loss greater than 5% of pregpregnacy body weight$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Severe nausea and vomiting with weight loss greater than 5% of pregpregnacy body weight
HYPEREMESIS GRAVIDARUM is defined as SEVERE, persistent NAUSEA and VOMITING in pregnancy causing WEIGHT LOSS of MORE THAN 5% of pre-pregnancy body weight, together with dehydration, ketosis and/or electrolyte imbalance. It usually starts before 20 weeks (often 4–10 weeks) and often improves by 16–20 weeks, although in some women it continues longer.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Vomiting that persists for the entire pregnancy — It does not have to persist for the entire pregnancy (although it can in a few women).
• Vomiting upon arising more than five days per week — Vomiting on waking several days a week describes ordinary morning sickness, not the defining criteria.
• Vomiting that appears after the 20th week of pregnancy — Vomiting that starts after 20 weeks suggests another cause (e.g. pre-eclampsia, HELLP, acute fatty liver, infection), not hyperemesis.

⭐ Key point: Assess hydration (urine ketones, electrolytes), give IV fluids, anti-emetics and thiamine, and consider thromboprophylaxis.$x$, null),
(143, $x$A gravid uterus at term weighs about:$x$, $x$1000 g$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 1000 g
The non-pregnant uterus weighs only about 50–70 g. During pregnancy, its muscle cells grow enormously (hypertrophy) and stretch, so that by TERM the uterus (without its contents) weighs about 1,000 g (1 kg) — roughly a 15–20-fold increase. Its capacity also increases from about 10 mL to about 5 litres. After delivery, it shrinks back (involution) over about 6 weeks.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 60 g — 60 g is the weight of the NON-pregnant uterus.
• 100 g — 100 g is far too small for a term uterus.
• 500 g — 500 g is too small for a term uterus (it's about the weight 1 week after delivery).

⭐ Key point: Assess uterine involution after delivery: the fundus should be firm and descend about 1 cm (one finger-breadth) per day.$x$, null),
(144, $x$Amniotic fluid embolism classically presents with the sudden onset of:$x$, $x$Acute hypoxia, hypotension, and coagulopathy during or shortly after labour$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Acute hypoxia, hypotension, and coagulopathy during or shortly after labour
AMNIOTIC FLUID EMBOLISM (AFE) is a rare but catastrophic obstetric emergency occurring DURING LABOUR, delivery or SHORTLY AFTER. Amniotic fluid enters the maternal circulation, triggering a massive inflammatory reaction. It classically presents SUDDENLY with: ACUTE HYPOXIA (breathlessness, cyanosis, respiratory arrest), HYPOTENSION/cardiovascular collapse (often cardiac arrest), and COAGULOPATHY (DIC — severe bleeding). Seizures or confusion may also occur.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Gradual, mild shortness of breath over days — AFE is SUDDEN, not gradual over days.
• Isolated mild fever — Isolated mild fever is not a feature.
• Chronic hypertension — Chronic hypertension is unrelated.

⭐ Key point: Management is supportive and urgent: call for help, CPR, oxygen/ventilation, vasopressors, massive transfusion with clotting factors, and delivery of the baby.$x$, null),
(145, $x$Oesophageal atresia in a newborn classically presents with:$x$, $x$Excessive drooling, choking and coughing with feeding attempts$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Excessive drooling, choking and coughing with feeding attempts
OESOPHAGEAL ATRESIA means the oesophagus ends in a blind pouch, so swallowed saliva and milk cannot reach the stomach. Classic signs in a newborn: EXCESSIVE DROOLING/frothy saliva from the mouth, CHOKING, COUGHING and CYANOSIS with FEEDING attempts, and respiratory distress (from aspiration — especially if there is a tracheo-oesophageal fistula). A nasogastric tube cannot be passed into the stomach (it coils in the pouch on X-ray). There may be a history of polyhydramnios in pregnancy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal feeding with no complications — Normal feeding is impossible.
• Delayed passage of meconium only — Delayed passage of meconium suggests Hirschsprung's disease or meconium ileus.
• No respiratory symptoms at all — Respiratory symptoms are common because of aspiration.

⭐ Key point: Stop oral feeds, nurse head-up, use continuous suction of the upper pouch (Replogle tube), and arrange urgent surgical repair.$x$, null),
(146, $x$Which action best prevents heat loss by evaporation in a newborn?$x$, $x$Drying the infant with a warm blanket$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Drying the infant with a warm blanket
Newborns lose heat in four ways: EVAPORATION (amniotic fluid evaporating from wet skin — the biggest loss immediately after birth), conduction (contact with cold surfaces), convection (cold air currents), and radiation (heat radiating to cold walls/objects). The best way to prevent EVAPORATIVE heat loss is to DRY THE INFANT thoroughly and immediately with a WARM BLANKET/towel (and then replace the wet towel with a dry one), and place the baby skin-to-skin with the mother, with a hat.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Warming the crib pad — Warming the crib pad prevents CONDUCTIVE heat loss.
• Closing the doors to the room — Closing doors prevents CONVECTIVE heat loss (draughts).
• Turning on the overhead radiant warmer — A radiant warmer reduces RADIANT heat loss.

⭐ Key point: Hypothermia in newborns causes hypoglycaemia, acidosis and breathing problems — keep the "warm chain".$x$, null),
(147, $x$A nurse is caring for a newborn in the NICU and suspects neonatal sepsis. Which of the following is an early sign of neonatal sepsis?$x$, $x$Poor feeding, lethargy, and respiratory distress$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Poor feeding, lethargy, and respiratory distress
NEONATAL SEPSIS often presents with SUBTLE, NON-SPECIFIC signs because newborns have an immature immune system. Early signs: POOR FEEDING, LETHARGY (reduced activity, floppiness), RESPIRATORY DISTRESS (tachypnoea, grunting, apnoea), temperature instability (often HYPOthermia rather than fever), jaundice, vomiting, poor colour or mottling, and abnormal glucose levels.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A high-grade fever — High-grade fever is not typical — newborns often have low or unstable temperatures.
• A stable blood sugar level — Stable blood sugar is reassuring, not a sign of sepsis (sepsis often causes hypo- or hyperglycaemia).
• A sudden increase in activity — Increased activity is not typical; lethargy is more common.

⭐ Key point: Any newborn who is "not right" should be assessed for sepsis; take cultures and start IV antibiotics early.$x$, null),
(148, $x$First action in a precipitous delivery?$x$, $x$Establish an airway for the newborn$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Establish an airway for the newborn
In a PRECIPITOUS (very rapid, unplanned) DELIVERY, the FIRST action after the baby is born is to ESTABLISH AN AIRWAY for the newborn — clear the mouth and nose if needed (wipe or gentle suction), dry and stimulate the baby, and ensure breathing. Breathing is the most critical need for survival.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Arrange transport — Arranging transport can wait until the mother and baby are stable.
• Ascertain condition of fundus — Checking the fundus is important for the mother after the placenta is delivered, but the newborn's airway comes first.
• Tie and cut cord — Tying and cutting the cord is not urgent (delayed cord clamping is beneficial if the baby is well).

⭐ Key point: Keep the baby warm (skin-to-skin), and watch the mother for postpartum haemorrhage.$x$, null),
(149, $x$Hirschsprung's disease is caused by:$x$, $x$Absence of ganglion cells in a segment of bowel, causing functional obstruction$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Absence of ganglion cells in a segment of bowel, causing functional obstruction
HIRSCHSPRUNG'S DISEASE is caused by the ABSENCE of GANGLION CELLS (nerve cells) in a segment of the bowel wall (usually the rectum and sigmoid colon). Without these nerves, the segment cannot relax and push stool along (no peristalsis), causing a FUNCTIONAL OBSTRUCTION. The bowel above becomes dilated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Excess ganglion cells in the bowel wall — It is caused by ABSENCE, not excess, of ganglion cells.
• A viral infection of the colon — It is not caused by a viral infection.
• An allergic reaction to milk protein — It is not caused by milk protein allergy.

⭐ Key point: Diagnosed by rectal biopsy; treated surgically by removing the affected segment.$x$, null),
(150, $x$Neonatal jaundice is usually clinically visible when serum bilirubin exceeds:$x$, $x$5 mg/dL$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 5 mg/dL
NEONATAL JAUNDICE becomes VISIBLE in the skin when serum bilirubin exceeds about 5 mg/dL (about 85 µmol/L). In adults, jaundice is visible at about 2–3 mg/dL. Jaundice spreads from head to toe as levels rise.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 15 mg/dL — 15 mg/dL is a high level, not the threshold of visibility.
• 25 mg/dL — 25 mg/dL is very high and risks kernicterus.
• 50 mg/dL — 50 mg/dL is extremely high.

⭐ Key point: Visual assessment is unreliable — measure bilirubin (transcutaneous or serum) when jaundice is noted.$x$, null),
(151, $x$On the Travis illness-wellness continuum, the neutral point at the center of the scale represents what?$x$, $x$An absence of discernible signs or symptoms of disease, without the presence of positive wellness behaviors$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: An absence of discernible signs or symptoms of disease, without the presence of positive wellness behaviors
On TRAVIS'S ILLNESS-WELLNESS CONTINUUM, the NEUTRAL POINT in the middle represents an ABSENCE of discernible signs or symptoms of DISEASE, WITHOUT POSITIVE WELLNESS BEHAVIOURS. Moving left leads to illness, disability and premature death; moving right (through awareness, education and growth) leads to high-level wellness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Death — Death is at the extreme left end.
• Peak, high-level wellness — Peak wellness is at the extreme right end.
• The point of maximum disability — Maximum disability is towards the left end.

⭐ Key point: Health promotion aims to move people towards high-level wellness, not just the absence of disease.$x$, null),
(152, $x$A nurse writes the goal: "Client will ambulate 30 meters with a walker by discharge on 07/28/2026." Which SMART element is reflected by the phrase "by discharge on 07/28/2026"?$x$, $x$Time-bound$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Time-bound
SMART goals make outcomes clear and measurable: S = SPECIFIC (clearly states what will be done — "ambulate 30 metres with a walker"); M = MEASURABLE (can be counted — "30 metres"); A = ACHIEVABLE (realistic for this client); R = RELEVANT/REALISTIC (related to the client's problem); T = TIME-BOUND (has a clear DEADLINE). The phrase "BY DISCHARGE ON 07/28/2026" sets the deadline, so it reflects the TIME-BOUND element.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Specific — "Specific" is reflected by the clear description of the activity.
• Measurable — "Measurable" is reflected by "30 metres".
• Achievable — "Achievable" is about whether the goal is realistic for this patient.

⭐ Key point: Without a time frame, it is impossible to evaluate whether a goal has been met on time.$x$, null),
(153, $x$Phaneuf's nursing audit tool evaluates nursing care under seven functions, which include all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Approval of hospital annual budget$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Approval of hospital annual budget
PHANEUF'S NURSING AUDIT evaluates nursing care through records against SEVEN FUNCTIONS of nursing: (1) application and execution of physician's legal orders; (2) observation of symptoms and reactions; (3) supervision of the patient; (4) supervision of those participating in care; (5) reporting and recording; (6) application and execution of nursing procedures and techniques; (7) promotion of physical and emotional health by direction and teaching. "APPROVAL OF HOSPITAL ANNUAL BUDGET" is NOT one of the functions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Application and execution of physician's legal orders — Execution of physician's orders IS a function.
• Observation of symptoms and reactions — Observation of symptoms IS a function.
• Supervision of the patient — Supervision of the patient IS a function.

⭐ Key point: Nursing audits help evaluate and improve the quality of nursing care.$x$, null),
(154, $x$A charge nurse asked why a particular protocol works considers several possible explanations and weighs evidence for and against each rather than accepting the first explanation offered. This reflects:$x$, $x$Open-mindedness combined with analysis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Open-mindedness combined with analysis
Considering SEVERAL POSSIBLE EXPLANATIONS and WEIGHING EVIDENCE for and against each, rather than accepting the first answer, shows OPEN-MINDEDNESS (willingness to consider different views) combined with ANALYSIS (systematically evaluating information). These are key critical thinking attitudes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Overconfidence — Overconfidence would mean accepting the first answer without checking.
• Avoidance of the question — Avoidance would mean not answering at all.
• Conformity to peer opinion — Conformity would mean accepting what others think without evaluating.

⭐ Key point: Critical thinking improves clinical decision-making and patient safety.$x$, null),
(155, $x$Evaluation in the nursing process is best described as:$x$, $x$An ongoing procedure of re-assessment$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: An ongoing procedure of re-assessment
EVALUATION in the nursing process is an ONGOING process of RE-ASSESSMENT, checking whether the patient is achieving the expected outcomes, and modifying the plan as needed. It occurs continuously, not just at the end.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A one-time final step only — It is not a one-time final step.
• Something done only by physicians — Nurses carry out evaluation, not only physicians.
• Unrelated to the original care plan — It is directly related to the original care plan's goals.

⭐ Key point: Evaluation outcomes: goal met, partially met, or not met.$x$, null),
(156, $x$Which activity best describes the evaluation phase?$x$, $x$Reassess and compare outcomes with the set objectives$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Reassess and compare outcomes with the set objectives
The EVALUATION phase is the final step of the nursing process (ADPIE), but it is ongoing. The nurse REASSESSES the patient (collects new data) and COMPARES the actual OUTCOMES with the expected outcomes (objectives/goals) set during planning. Based on this, the nurse decides whether the goal is MET, PARTIALLY MET or NOT MET, and whether to continue, modify or end the care plan.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Interpret data and identify client problems — Interpreting data and identifying client problems is the DIAGNOSIS phase.
• Establish short- and long-term goals — Establishing short- and long-term goals is the PLANNING phase.
• Carry out nursing procedures and document — Carrying out nursing procedures and documenting them is the IMPLEMENTATION phase.

⭐ Key point: If a goal is not met, ask why: was the diagnosis wrong, the goal unrealistic, or the interventions ineffective?$x$, null),
(157, $x$Copy-pasting a previous nursing note into today's entry without verifying current accuracy risks:$x$, $x$Documentation errors and propagation of outdated or inaccurate information ('note bloat')$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Documentation errors and propagation of outdated or inaccurate information ('note bloat')
COPY-PASTING previous notes without checking accuracy risks DOCUMENTATION ERRORS: outdated or wrong information is repeated ("note bloat"), real changes may be missed, and the record may misrepresent the patient's current condition. This is unsafe and can have legal consequences.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Improved efficiency with no downside — It may seem efficient, but has serious downsides.
• Better legal protection — It weakens, not improves, legal protection.
• Faster patient discharge — It doesn't speed up discharge.

⭐ Key point: Document only what you have personally assessed or done, at the time.$x$, null),
(158, $x$How can nurses contribute to quality assurance efforts?$x$, $x$By participating in quality improvement initiatives and adhering to best practices$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: By participating in quality improvement initiatives and adhering to best practices
QUALITY ASSURANCE (QA) and quality improvement aim to make care safe, effective and patient-centred. Nurses contribute by: PARTICIPATING in QUALITY IMPROVEMENT INITIATIVES (audits, data collection, quality committees, PDSA cycles, care bundles), ADHERING to BEST PRACTICES and evidence-based guidelines, reporting incidents and near misses, listening to patient feedback, keeping accurate documentation, and continuing professional development.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• By ignoring feedback from patients — Ignoring patient feedback misses opportunities to improve care.
• By working independently of their colleagues — Quality improvement requires teamwork and collaboration, not working alone.
• By prioritizing speed over accuracy — Prioritising speed over accuracy causes errors and harms patients.

⭐ Key point: Every nurse is responsible for quality — not just managers.$x$, null),
(159, $x$A nurse repositions a patient who complains of difficulty breathing. This is which phase of the nursing process?$x$, $x$Implementation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Implementation
The IMPLEMENTATION phase of the nursing process is when the nurse CARRIES OUT the planned nursing interventions. REPOSITIONING a patient who has DIFFICULTY BREATHING (e.g. sitting them upright or in high Fowler's position to improve lung expansion) is a nursing ACTION — so it is IMPLEMENTATION. After this, the nurse would EVALUATE whether breathing has improved (respiratory rate, SpO2, work of breathing).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Assessment — Assessment is collecting data (e.g. listening to the patient's complaint, measuring SpO2).
• Evaluation — Evaluation is checking the response after the action.
• Outcome identification — Outcome identification is part of planning (setting goals).

⭐ Key point: Implementation includes independent, dependent and collaborative nursing actions, followed by documentation.$x$, null),
(160, $x$A Nurse at HMC asked a patient to verbalise if he understood the information given during nursing care to the client according to a devised nursing care plan. During which step of the nursing process does such action take place?$x$, $x$Evaluation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Evaluation
Asking the patient to VERBALISE (explain in their own words) whether they UNDERSTOOD the information given during care checks whether the planned TEACHING OUTCOME has been achieved. This is EVALUATION — comparing the patient's response with the expected outcome (e.g. "The patient will verbalise understanding of their medication regimen").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Assessment — Assessment is collecting initial data about the patient's needs.
• Planning — Planning is setting goals and choosing interventions.
• Implementation — Implementation is giving the teaching itself.

⭐ Key point: The "teach-back" method is an effective way to evaluate understanding.$x$, null),
(161, $x$Effective discharge planning for a rehabilitation patient should begin:$x$, $x$Early during the hospital stay, involving the patient, family, and multidisciplinary team$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Early during the hospital stay, involving the patient, family, and multidisciplinary team
Effective DISCHARGE PLANNING should BEGIN EARLY during the hospital stay (ideally on admission), and involve the PATIENT, FAMILY and MULTIDISCIPLINARY TEAM (physiotherapy, occupational therapy, social work, dietitian), to prepare for a safe transition home or to another facility.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only on the day of discharge — The day of discharge is too late.
• After the patient has already left the facility — After discharge is too late.
• Without involving the patient's family — Family involvement is important.

⭐ Key point: Early discharge planning reduces delays and readmissions.$x$, null),
(162, $x$The goal of quality assurance in nursing is to:$x$, $x$Continuously improve patient care and outcomes$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Continuously improve patient care and outcomes
The main goal of QUALITY ASSURANCE (QA) in nursing is to CONTINUOUSLY IMPROVE PATIENT CARE and OUTCOMES. It involves setting standards, measuring performance (structure, process and outcome), comparing results with standards, identifying gaps, and making improvements, then re-measuring. QA aims for excellence, not just the minimum.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reduce the cost of care — Reducing costs may be a benefit, but it is not the main goal.
• Meet the minimum legal standards of care — Meeting only minimum legal standards is not enough; QA aims higher.
• Ensure nurses follow all orders without question — Following orders without question is unsafe; nurses must use professional judgement.

⭐ Key point: Quality tools include audits, incident reporting, PDSA cycles, care bundles and patient satisfaction surveys.$x$, null),
(163, $x$The final step of the research process is typically considered to be$x$, $x$Disseminating or reporting the research findings to relevant audiences$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Disseminating or reporting the research findings to relevant audiences
The RESEARCH PROCESS typically follows these steps: identify the problem, review the literature, develop a framework, formulate objectives/hypotheses, choose a design, select the sample, collect data, analyse data, interpret findings, and finally DISSEMINATE (communicate) the FINDINGS through reports, journal articles, conference presentations or workshops, so that others can use them to improve practice.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Formulating the research hypothesis — Formulating hypotheses is an early step.
• Reviewing related literature — Reviewing literature is an early step (though it continues throughout).
• Selecting the sample — Selecting the sample is a middle step.

⭐ Key point: Research is only useful if its findings are shared and applied in practice (research utilisation).$x$, null),
(164, $x$Number of ICU admissions per month at a hospital is an example of which scale of measurement?$x$, $x$Ratio$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Ratio
Data are measured on four scales: NOMINAL (categories without order), ORDINAL (ranked categories), INTERVAL (equal intervals, no true zero), and RATIO (equal intervals AND a TRUE ZERO). The NUMBER OF ICU ADMISSIONS per month is a count: zero admissions means none at all (true zero), and 20 admissions is twice as many as 10. So it is RATIO data.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nominal — Nominal data are categories (e.g. blood group, sex).
• Ordinal — Ordinal data are ranks (e.g. pain mild/moderate/severe).
• Interval — Interval data have no true zero (e.g. temperature in °C).

⭐ Key point: Ratio data allow all statistical calculations, including ratios ("twice as many").$x$, null),
(165, $x$A study finds a statistically significant but very small difference between two treatments (e.g. a 1% difference in outcome) in a very large sample. This illustrates the important distinction between:$x$, $x$Statistical significance and clinical significance/relevance$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Statistical significance and clinical significance/relevance
STATISTICAL SIGNIFICANCE (p < 0.05) means the result is unlikely due to chance. In a VERY LARGE sample, even TINY differences can be statistically significant. CLINICAL SIGNIFICANCE asks whether the difference is LARGE ENOUGH to MATTER to patients. A 1% difference may be statistically significant but not clinically important.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reliability and validity, which are unrelated concepts here — Reliability and validity are about measurement.
• Qualitative and quantitative research — This isn't about qualitative vs quantitative.
• Ethics and methodology — This isn't about ethics vs methodology.

⭐ Key point: Always ask: "Is this difference meaningful for patients?"$x$, null),
(166, $x$The Latin term 'Ibid' is used when citing:$x$, $x$The same work, a different page from the last citation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The same work, a different page from the last citation
"IBID." (from Latin ibidem, "in the same place") is used in footnote referencing to refer to the SAME WORK cited in the IMMEDIATELY PRECEDING note. In the teaching used for this exam, "Ibid" is used for the same work with a DIFFERENT PAGE (with the new page number given), while "loc. cit." is used for the same work and same page, and "op. cit." for a work cited earlier but not immediately before. (Many modern styles, such as APA, do not use these terms.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The same work, the same page as just cited — In this teaching, "same work, same page" is "loc. cit." rather than ibid.
• A completely different, unrelated work — A completely different work would be cited in full.
• Only works published this year — Ibid is not limited by publication year.

⭐ Key point: Follow the referencing style required by your institution.$x$, null),
(167, $x$In defining the scope of a study, which is NOT necessarily indicated?$x$, $x$The significance of the study$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The significance of the study
The SCOPE of a study defines its boundaries: the PERIOD (time frame), the POPULATION (who), and the SETTING (where), and sometimes the variables studied. The SIGNIFICANCE of the study (why it is important) is a separate section, not part of the scope.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The period of study — The period IS part of the scope.
• The population of study — The population IS part of the scope.
• The setting of the study — The setting IS part of the scope.

⭐ Key point: Scope and limitations are usually stated in Chapter One of a research project.$x$, null),
(168, $x$Process of research means the following EXCEPT$x$, $x$Making policies$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Making policies
The RESEARCH PROCESS involves systematic steps to generate knowledge: IDENTIFYING a PROBLEM, DEFINING it precisely and unambiguously, reviewing literature, formulating objectives and hypotheses, DESIGNING the research, selecting a sample, collecting and analysing data, interpreting results, and disseminating findings. MAKING POLICIES is NOT part of the research process — policies are made by managers or governments, who may USE research findings as evidence.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Identifying a problem — Identifying a problem IS part of the research process.
• Defining the problem in precise terms in unambiguous terms — Defining the problem precisely IS part of the process.
• Designing the research — Designing the research IS part of the process.

⭐ Key point: Evidence-based policy uses research findings, but policy-making itself is a separate process.$x$, null),
(169, $x$A core ethical requirement in human subjects research is that participation must be:$x$, $x$Voluntary, based on informed consent, without coercion$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Voluntary, based on informed consent, without coercion
A core ethical requirement of research with human participants is that participation must be VOLUNTARY, based on INFORMED CONSENT, and WITHOUT COERCION. Participants must be told the purpose, procedures, risks, benefits, confidentiality arrangements, and their RIGHT to REFUSE or WITHDRAW at any time without penalty (e.g. their care will not be affected). Consent should be documented (usually signed).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mandatory for all eligible patients — Participation can never be mandatory.
• Compensated financially regardless of consent — Payment does not replace consent; excessive payment can be coercive.
• Determined solely by the researcher — The researcher cannot decide for participants; they decide for themselves.

⭐ Key point: Extra protection is needed for vulnerable groups (children, unconscious ICU patients, prisoners), e.g. consent from legal representatives.$x$, null),
(170, $x$The Belmont Report's 3 core ethical principles are:$x$, $x$Autonomy, Beneficence, Justice$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Autonomy, Beneficence, Justice
The BELMONT REPORT (USA, 1979) set out three core ethical principles for research with human subjects: (1) RESPECT FOR PERSONS (AUTONOMY) — treat people as autonomous, obtain informed consent, and protect those with reduced autonomy; (2) BENEFICENCE — maximise benefits and minimise harm; (3) JUSTICE — fair selection of participants and fair distribution of research burdens and benefits.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Consent, Privacy, Fairness — Consent, privacy and fairness are applications of the principles, not the three principles themselves.
• Honesty, Accuracy, Transparency — Honesty, accuracy and transparency relate to research integrity.
• Safety, Dignity, Respect — Safety, dignity and respect are general values.

⭐ Key point: Research ethics committees use these principles when reviewing study proposals.$x$, null),
(171, $x$Which measure of central tendency is appropriate for nominal (categorical) data, such as the most common blood group among patients in a ward?$x$, $x$Mode$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Mode
NOMINAL data are CATEGORIES with no order (e.g. blood groups A, B, AB, O; sex; ward type). You cannot calculate an average of "blood group A" and "blood group O", and there is no "middle" category. The only meaningful measure of central tendency is the MODE — the MOST FREQUENTLY occurring category (e.g. "blood group O is the most common").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mean — The MEAN requires numerical (interval/ratio) data.
• Median — The MEDIAN requires data that can be ordered (ordinal or higher).
• Standard deviation — STANDARD DEVIATION is a measure of spread, not central tendency, and requires numerical data.

⭐ Key point: Nominal data are described using frequencies, percentages, and bar or pie charts.$x$, null),
(172, $x$Common electronic databases for nurse researchers include all EXCEPT:$x$, $x$American Nurses Association$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: American Nurses Association
Nurse researchers use ELECTRONIC DATABASES to search for published literature: CINAHL (nursing and allied health), PUBMED (free search interface for biomedical literature), MEDLINE (the main biomedical database, accessed via PubMed), Cochrane Library, Embase and PsycINFO. The AMERICAN NURSES ASSOCIATION (ANA) is a PROFESSIONAL ORGANISATION, not a literature database (although it publishes standards and journals).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CINAHL — CINAHL IS a database.
• PubMed — PubMed IS a database search engine.
• MEDLINE — MEDLINE IS a database.

⭐ Key point: Use clear search terms and Boolean operators (AND, OR, NOT) to find relevant studies efficiently.$x$, null),
(173, $x$A nurse researcher wants to study the lived experience of ICU patients after discharge. The most appropriate research approach for this question is:$x$, $x$A qualitative approach (e.g. interviews) to capture rich, descriptive experiential data$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A qualitative approach (e.g. interviews) to capture rich, descriptive experiential data
To study the LIVED EXPERIENCE of ICU patients after discharge, a QUALITATIVE approach (e.g. in-depth INTERVIEWS, phenomenology) is most appropriate, because it captures rich, descriptive data about people's experiences and meanings.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A strictly quantitative, numerical survey with no narrative data — A purely numerical survey can't capture lived experience in depth.
• No research method is appropriate for this topic — Research is possible on this topic.
• A laboratory experiment — A laboratory experiment is not appropriate.

⭐ Key point: Qualitative findings help nurses understand patients' perspectives and improve care.$x$, null),
(174, $x$A hospital's 'departmental risk management programme' typically includes all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Concealing incidents to protect the department's reputation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Concealing incidents to protect the department's reputation
A departmental RISK MANAGEMENT PROGRAMME aims to identify, prevent and reduce harm. It includes: IDENTIFICATION of common RISK categories (falls, medication errors, infections, pressure ulcers, equipment failure), INCIDENT REPORTING mechanisms, analysis of incidents (e.g. root cause analysis), corrective actions, staff education, and MONITORING and EVALUATION of interventions. CONCEALING INCIDENTS to protect the department's reputation is unethical and dangerous — it prevents learning, repeats harm, and may be illegal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Identification of common risk categories — Identifying risk categories IS part of the programme.
• Incident reporting mechanisms — Incident reporting IS part of the programme.
• Monitoring and evaluation of interventions — Monitoring and evaluation IS part of the programme.

⭐ Key point: Openness (duty of candour) with patients and families after incidents builds trust.$x$, null),
(175, $x$The conflict resolution style where one party gives in to preserve the relationship is called:$x$, $x$Accommodating$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Accommodating
ACCOMMODATING (smoothing/obliging) is a conflict-resolution style where one party GIVES IN to the other's wishes to PRESERVE THE RELATIONSHIP and keep harmony ("I lose, you win"). It is useful when the issue matters more to the other person, when keeping good relationships is more important, or when you realise you are wrong. Overuse can lead to resentment and unmet needs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Competing — COMPETING is pursuing your own goals at the other's expense ("win-lose").
• Compromising — COMPROMISING is both parties giving up something ("split the difference").
• Avoiding — AVOIDING is ignoring or withdrawing from the conflict.

⭐ Key point: Thomas-Kilmann's five styles: competing, collaborating, compromising, avoiding and accommodating.$x$, null),
(176, $x$Which of the following is a key responsibility of a nurse manager during disaster preparedness?$x$, $x$Develop, communicate, and regularly evaluate disaster response plans$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Develop, communicate, and regularly evaluate disaster response plans
DISASTER PREPAREDNESS is a key nurse manager responsibility. The manager should help DEVELOP disaster response PLANS (roles, surge capacity, triage areas, staff call-in lists, supplies and equipment, communication systems, evacuation routes), COMMUNICATE them to all staff (training, briefings), and REGULARLY EVALUATE them through drills and exercises, updating the plan based on lessons learned.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Close the hospital permanently — Closing the hospital permanently is not disaster preparedness.
• Cancel emergency drills — Cancelling drills prevents staff from being ready.
• Delegate all planning to external agencies — Planning should be done internally with input from external agencies, not fully delegated.

⭐ Key point: Regular drills reveal weaknesses before a real disaster occurs.$x$, null),
(177, $x$A nurse floats to an unfamiliar ICU due to staffing shortages and is assigned a complex ventilated patient. The most appropriate first action is to:$x$, $x$Communicate honestly about their competency level with this patient type, request orientation/support as needed, and advocate for patient safety$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Communicate honestly about their competency level with this patient type, request orientation/support as needed, and advocate for patient safety
When floated to an unfamiliar ICU, the nurse should COMMUNICATE HONESTLY about their COMPETENCY, REQUEST ORIENTATION or SUPPORT, and ADVOCATE for PATIENT SAFETY.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Refuse the assignment entirely with no discussion — Refusing without discussion is unprofessional.
• Accept the assignment silently despite feeling unprepared, saying nothing — Accepting silently despite being unprepared risks patient safety.
• Provide care without seeking any support or clarification — Providing care without support may be unsafe.

⭐ Key point: Nurses must practise within their competence.$x$, null),
(178, $x$Which scholar introduced the functional type of organisation?$x$, $x$F.W. Taylor$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: F.W. Taylor
Frederick Winslow TAYLOR, the father of scientific management, introduced the FUNCTIONAL TYPE OF ORGANISATION ("functional foremanship"). Instead of one supervisor controlling everything, each worker received instructions from several SPECIALIST supervisors, each responsible for a particular function (e.g. planning, speed, quality, maintenance). This improved specialisation and efficiency, but could confuse workers with multiple bosses (breaking unity of command).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Chester Bernard — Chester Barnard wrote about the functions of the executive and acceptance of authority.
• Allen — "Allen" is not associated with the functional organisation.
• Max Weber — Max Weber developed the theory of BUREAUCRACY.

⭐ Key point: In hospitals, nurses often report to both a ward manager and specialist leads — a form of functional organisation.$x$, null),
(179, $x$The major concern of an effective leader is:$x$, $x$Concern for both production and employees$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Concern for both production and employees
An effective leader shows CONCERN for BOTH PRODUCTION (achieving goals — e.g. safe, high-quality patient care, efficient use of resources) AND EMPLOYEES (staff wellbeing, development, motivation and relationships). In Blake and Mouton's MANAGERIAL GRID, this is the "TEAM MANAGEMENT" (9,9) style — considered the most effective, because committed, supported staff achieve better results.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Concern for production only — Concern for production only ("task management") can burn out staff.
• Concern for self — Concern for self is selfish and ineffective.
• Concern for employees only — Concern for employees only ("country club management") may neglect goals.

⭐ Key point: Effective nurse leaders balance task demands with staff support.$x$, null),
(180, $x$During a mass casualty event, the charge nurse must delegate tasks rapidly to a team with mixed experience levels. The most appropriate delegation approach is to:$x$, $x$Match tasks to each team member's competency level, ensuring appropriate supervision for less experienced staff$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Match tasks to each team member's competency level, ensuring appropriate supervision for less experienced staff
During a MASS CASUALTY event, the charge nurse should MATCH TASKS to each team member's COMPETENCY level, with APPROPRIATE SUPERVISION for less experienced staff.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Assign tasks randomly with no regard for skill level — Random assignment is unsafe.
• Assign only the most complex tasks to the least experienced staff — Complex tasks for inexperienced staff is unsafe.
• Refuse to delegate anything and do all tasks personally — Refusing to delegate is impossible in mass casualties.

⭐ Key point: Use the five rights of delegation.$x$, null),
(181, $x$The following are good qualities of a leader EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Rich$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Rich
Good leaders are VISIONARY (have a clear picture of the future and inspire others towards it), KNOWLEDGEABLE (clinically and managerially competent), and have INTEGRITY (honesty, fairness, consistency, ethical behaviour). Other qualities: good communication, empathy, decisiveness, accountability and humility. Being RICH (wealthy) is NOT a leadership quality — leadership depends on character, skills and behaviour, not money.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Visionary — Visionary IS a leadership quality.
• Knowledgeable — Knowledgeable IS a leadership quality.
• Integrity — Integrity IS a leadership quality.

⭐ Key point: Leadership can be developed through training, mentoring and experience.$x$, null),
(182, $x$Who advocated the fourteen principles of management?$x$, $x$Henri Fayol$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Henri Fayol
Henri FAYOL (1841–1925), a French mining engineer, developed the 14 PRINCIPLES OF MANAGEMENT in his book "General and Industrial Management" (1916): division of work, authority and responsibility, discipline, unity of command, unity of direction, subordination of individual interests to the general interest, remuneration, centralisation, scalar chain, order, equity, stability of tenure, initiative, and esprit de corps.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Frederick Taylor — Frederick Taylor developed SCIENTIFIC MANAGEMENT.
• Max Weber — Max Weber developed the theory of BUREAUCRACY.
• Karl Marx — Karl Marx was a philosopher and economist, not a management theorist.

⭐ Key point: Fayol also described the five functions of management: planning, organising, commanding, coordinating and controlling.$x$, null),
(183, $x$The nurse manager uses the results of a nursing audit to make changes to the unit's policies. This is an example of the nurse's role in:$x$, $x$Quality improvement$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Quality improvement
A NURSING AUDIT systematically reviews nursing care (often through records) against agreed standards. When the nurse manager USES AUDIT RESULTS to CHANGE unit POLICIES and practices, this is QUALITY IMPROVEMENT — using data to identify gaps and make changes that improve care. The audit cycle: set standards → measure practice → compare with standards → implement changes → re-audit.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Punishing staff for poor performance — Audits are not for punishing staff; they identify system issues.
• Maintaining the status quo — Changing policies based on audit is the opposite of maintaining the status quo.
• Disregarding the documentation process — Audits depend on, rather than disregard, documentation.

⭐ Key point: Involve staff in audit and change — they are more likely to support improvements they helped design.$x$, null),
(184, $x$Which of the following is a function of a ICU manager?$x$, $x$Motivation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Motivation
MOTIVATION is a key FUNCTION of an ICU manager (part of directing/leading): inspiring and encouraging staff to work well and develop professionally, through recognition, fair treatment, good working conditions, opportunities for training and promotion, involvement in decisions, and support during stressful situations. Motivated staff provide safer, better-quality patient care and are less likely to leave.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bureaucracy — Bureaucracy is an organisational structure with rigid rules, not a manager's function.
• Humility — Humility is a personal quality, not a function.
• Oppression — Oppression is never an appropriate management behaviour.

⭐ Key point: Herzberg: recognition, responsibility and achievement motivate; pay and conditions prevent dissatisfaction.$x$, null),
(185, $x$Below are the Patient and family-focused interventions EXCEPT$x$, $x$Documentation of consent$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Documentation of consent
PATIENT- and FAMILY-FOCUSED interventions address emotional and psychological needs: RELIEVING ANXIETY and providing a SENSE OF SECURITY, ALLOWING FAMILY to stay with the patient where possible, providing clear EXPLANATIONS and INFORMATION, involving family in care and decisions, and respecting cultural and spiritual needs. DOCUMENTATION OF CONSENT is an important LEGAL/administrative task, but it is not a patient- or family-focused supportive intervention.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Relieve anxiety and provide a sense of security — Relieving anxiety IS a patient-focused intervention.
• Allow family to stay with patient, if possible, to alleviate anxiety — Allowing family presence IS family-focused.
• Provide explanations and information — Providing explanations IS patient- and family-focused.

⭐ Key point: Family-centred care in ICU reduces anxiety and improves satisfaction and outcomes.$x$, null),
(186, $x$When triaging multiple patients, a nurse practising justice should prioritise based on:$x$, $x$Clinical need$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Clinical need
The ethical principle of JUSTICE requires fair treatment of all patients. In TRIAGE, a nurse practising justice prioritises patients based on CLINICAL NEED — the most seriously ill or injured, who will benefit most from immediate care, are seen first — not on personal characteristics, wealth, social status, or insurance.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Personal characteristics such as wealth — Wealth and personal characteristics should never determine care priority.
• Order of arrival only, regardless of severity — Order of arrival alone ignores severity and could let critically ill patients deteriorate.
• Insurance status — Insurance status should not decide clinical priority.

⭐ Key point: Use validated triage tools (e.g. South African Triage Scale, ESI) for fair, consistent prioritisation.$x$, null),
(187, $x$The key distinction between Justice and Fairness is that:$x$, $x$Justice = rightness/what's deserved; Fairness = impartiality/equal treatment$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Justice = rightness/what's deserved; Fairness = impartiality/equal treatment
JUSTICE and FAIRNESS are related but slightly different: JUSTICE refers to RIGHTNESS — giving people what they DESERVE or are entitled to, and distributing benefits and burdens appropriately. FAIRNESS refers to IMPARTIALITY — treating people EQUALLY and without bias or favouritism. In nursing, both guide ethical decisions about care and resources.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• They mean exactly the same thing with no difference — They are closely related but not exactly the same.
• Justice only applies to law, never to nursing — Justice applies to nursing ethics (e.g. resource allocation), not only to law.
• Fairness only applies to financial matters — Fairness applies to all aspects of care, not just finances.

⭐ Key point: Equity (giving people what they need) is often what justice requires, beyond simple equal treatment.$x$, null),
(188, $x$Applying the exact same rule to everyone regardless of their different starting points is:$x$, $x$Justice, but not necessarily truly fair to everyone$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Justice, but not necessarily truly fair to everyone
Applying the SAME RULE to everyone regardless of DIFFERENT STARTING POINTS may be just (consistent), but NOT NECESSARILY FAIR (equitable) — some people need more support to achieve the same outcome.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Always guaranteed to be fair — Equal treatment is not always fair.
• Never a valid ethical approach — It can be a valid approach in some cases.
• Only relevant in legal settings — It applies beyond legal settings.

⭐ Key point: Equity means giving people what they need to achieve fair outcomes.$x$, null),
(189, $x$The core legal/professional principle behind nursing documentation is best summarised as:$x$, $x$'If it wasn't documented, it wasn't done'$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 'If it wasn't documented, it wasn't done'
The legal and professional principle "IF IT WASN'T DOCUMENTED, IT WASN'T DONE" means that in legal proceedings, audits or investigations, care that is not recorded is assumed not to have been given. Documentation is evidence of care, communicates information to the team, supports continuity of care, and protects both patient and nurse.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Documentation is optional if care was given correctly — Documentation is never optional, even if care was correct.
• Only negative events need to be documented — All care, observations and events should be documented, not only negative events.
• Documentation can be completed weeks later without issue — Documentation must be timely (contemporaneous); late entries must be clearly marked.

⭐ Key point: Write accurate, clear, factual, timely and signed records.$x$, null),
(190, $x$The ethical principle of justice, as applied to resource allocation in critical care, refers to:$x$, $x$Fair and equitable distribution of limited healthcare resources$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fair and equitable distribution of limited healthcare resources
JUSTICE in critical care means FAIR and EQUITABLE DISTRIBUTION of LIMITED resources (ICU beds, ventilators, dialysis machines, staff time, expensive drugs) based on clinical need and likely benefit, using transparent and consistent criteria.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Treating all patients identically regardless of need — Treating everyone identically ignores different needs.
• Prioritising the wealthiest patients — Prioritising wealthy patients is unjust.
• Ignoring resource limitations entirely — Ignoring resource limits is unrealistic and can harm patients.

⭐ Key point: Triage and admission policies, made openly by senior clinicians, support just allocation.$x$, null),
(191, $x$Which ethical principle refers to a patient's right to make their own healthcare decisions?$x$, $x$Autonomy$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Autonomy
AUTONOMY is the ethical principle that recognises a competent patient's RIGHT to MAKE THEIR OWN HEALTHCARE DECISIONS — including accepting or refusing treatment — based on adequate information and free from coercion. Nurses support autonomy through informed consent, honest communication, respecting choices and advance directives, and advocating for patients' wishes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Beneficence — Beneficence means acting for the patient's good.
• Non-maleficence — Non-maleficence means avoiding harm.
• Justice — Justice means fairness.

⭐ Key point: When patients lack capacity, decisions are made in their best interests, considering their known wishes.$x$, null),
(192, $x$Which is one of the twin Latin maxims applied in the principles of fair hearing?$x$, $x$Nemo judex in causa sua$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Nemo judex in causa sua
The two Latin maxims of FAIR HEARING (natural justice) are: "NEMO JUDEX IN CAUSA SUA" (no one should be a judge in their own case — no bias) and "AUDI ALTERAM PARTEM" (hear the other side).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nemo dat quod non habet — "Nemo dat quod non habet" relates to property law.
• Ubi jus ibi remedium — "Ubi jus ibi remedium" means where there is a right, there is a remedy.
• Res ipsa loquitur — "Res ipsa loquitur" means the thing speaks for itself.

⭐ Key point: Fair hearing applies in disciplinary proceedings.$x$, null),
(193, $x$An advance directive is a document that:$x$, $x$Expresses a patient's wishes for future medical care if they become unable to decide for themselves$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Expresses a patient's wishes for future medical care if they become unable to decide for themselves
An ADVANCE DIRECTIVE expresses a patient's wishes for FUTURE MEDICAL CARE if they become UNABLE TO DECIDE (e.g. refusing resuscitation or ventilation).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Is only valid after the patient's death — It is valid while the patient is alive.
• Can only be created by a physician — Patients create it, not physicians.
• Overrides all patient autonomy while the patient is conscious — It supports autonomy.

⭐ Key point: Check for advance directives on admission.$x$, null),
(194, $x$A dying patient has Cheyne-Stokes respirations. The nurse educates the family that this is:$x$, $x$Normal at end-of-life$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Normal at end-of-life
CHEYNE-STOKES respiration is a cyclical breathing pattern: breaths gradually get deeper and faster, then shallower and slower, followed by a period of APNOEA (no breathing), then the cycle repeats. It is caused by changes in the brain's control of breathing and is common and NORMAL near the END OF LIFE. Educating the family that this is an expected part of dying (and not a sign of suffering) helps reduce their distress.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A sign of pain — It is not a sign of pain.
• Indicative of fluid overload — It does not indicate fluid overload.
• Reversible with oxygen — It is not reversible with oxygen at end of life.

⭐ Key point: Focus on comfort: positioning, mouth care, calm environment, and supporting the family.$x$, null),
(195, $x$Which nursing action best supports early identification of a TPN-related infection?$x$, $x$Assessing the catheter insertion site for redness, warmth, swelling, or drainage at each shift, along with monitoring temperature trends$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Assessing the catheter insertion site for redness, warmth, swelling, or drainage at each shift, along with monitoring temperature trends
Early identification of TPN-related (central line) infection involves ASSESSING the INSERTION SITE for REDNESS, WARMTH, SWELLING or DRAINAGE each shift, and MONITORING TEMPERATURE trends.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Assessing the insertion site only once weekly during dressing changes — Weekly checks are too infrequent.
• Relying solely on the patient's subjective report of pain — Subjective reports alone are not reliable.
• Checking the site only if the patient develops hypotension — Waiting for hypotension is too late.

⭐ Key point: Use strict aseptic technique and a dedicated lumen for TPN.$x$, null),
(196, $x$These are risks of parenteral nutrition EXCEPT$x$, $x$Vomiting$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Vomiting
PARENTERAL NUTRITION (PN/TPN) is given into a vein (usually a central line). Its risks include: INFECTION (central line bloodstream infection — the solution is rich in glucose and lipids, which support bacterial growth), HYPERGLYCAEMIA (high glucose content), HYPOGLYCAEMIA (if PN is stopped suddenly — rebound), electrolyte imbalances, refeeding syndrome, fluid overload, liver dysfunction, and line complications (thrombosis, pneumothorax). VOMITING is not a typical risk because PN bypasses the gut.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Infection — Infection IS a major risk.
• Hyperglycaemia — Hyperglycaemia IS a common risk.
• Hypoglycaemia — Hypoglycaemia IS a risk if PN is stopped abruptly.

⭐ Key point: Use a dedicated lumen, strict asepsis, and monitor glucose and electrolytes daily.$x$, null),
(197, $x$The 'ebb and flow' model of the metabolic response to trauma describes which sequence?$x$, $x$An initial hypometabolic 'ebb' phase of hypoperfusion followed by a hypermetabolic 'flow' (catabolic) phase$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: An initial hypometabolic 'ebb' phase of hypoperfusion followed by a hypermetabolic 'flow' (catabolic) phase
The "EBB and FLOW" model describes the metabolic response to trauma: an initial EBB phase (first 24–48 hours) with HYPOMETABOLISM and hypoperfusion (shock), followed by a FLOW phase with HYPERMETABOLISM and catabolism (muscle breakdown) during recovery.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A single sustained hypermetabolic phase with no early depression — There is an early depressed phase.
• An initial anabolic phase followed by a hypometabolic phase — The anabolic phase comes later in recovery.
• A random, unpredictable pattern with no identifiable phases — The pattern is predictable.

⭐ Key point: Nutritional support is important during the flow phase.$x$, null),
(198, $x$Refeeding syndrome is a risk primarily in patients who:$x$, $x$Have experienced prolonged starvation or severe malnutrition before reintroducing nutrition$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Have experienced prolonged starvation or severe malnutrition before reintroducing nutrition
REFEEDING SYNDROME is a potentially fatal shift of fluids and electrolytes that occurs when nutrition is restarted in patients after PROLONGED STARVATION or SEVERE MALNUTRITION (e.g. anorexia, chronic alcoholism, prolonged fasting, cancer cachexia). Insulin rises, driving PHOSPHATE, POTASSIUM and MAGNESIUM into cells, causing dangerously low blood levels, which can cause heart failure, arrhythmias, respiratory failure, confusion and death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Have been well-nourished throughout admission — Well-nourished patients are not at significant risk.
• Are only mildly overweight — Mildly overweight patients are not at particular risk.
• Have normal phosphate levels only — Normal phosphate levels are not the issue; low levels are.

⭐ Key point: Prevent by starting feeds slowly (e.g. 10 kcal/kg/day), giving thiamine, and monitoring electrolytes daily.$x$, null),
(199, $x$Contraindications to chest physiotherapy include all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Mild, stable chronic bronchitis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Mild, stable chronic bronchitis
CHEST PHYSIOTHERAPY (percussion, vibration, postural drainage) can be harmful in certain conditions. Contraindications include: UNTREATED PNEUMOTHORAX (could worsen it), ACTIVE HAEMOPTYSIS (coughing blood — could increase bleeding), UNCONTROLLED RAISED ICP (head-down positions and coughing raise ICP), unstable spinal injury, rib fractures and haemodynamic instability. MILD, STABLE CHRONIC BRONCHITIS is NOT a contraindication — physiotherapy can help clear secretions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Untreated pneumothorax — Untreated pneumothorax IS a contraindication.
• Active haemoptysis — Active haemoptysis IS a contraindication.
• Uncontrolled raised intracranial pressure — Uncontrolled raised ICP IS a contraindication.

⭐ Key point: Always assess the patient's stability before chest physiotherapy.$x$, null),
(200, $x$On the MRC muscle strength scale, a score of 0 indicates:$x$, $x$Complete paralysis, no movement$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Complete paralysis, no movement
The MRC (Medical Research Council) muscle strength scale grades power from 0 to 5: 0 = NO CONTRACTION (COMPLETE PARALYSIS); 1 = flicker or trace of contraction; 2 = movement with gravity eliminated; 3 = movement against gravity; 4 = movement against gravity and some resistance; 5 = normal power. So 0 means complete paralysis with no movement.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal power — Normal power is grade 5.
• Movement against gravity only — Movement against gravity only is grade 3.
• Flicker of movement only — A flicker of movement is grade 1.

⭐ Key point: The MRC sum score (0–60, testing 6 muscle groups on both sides) is used to diagnose ICU-acquired weakness (below 48).$x$, null)
),
upd as (
  update public.questions qq
     set exp = r.exp,
         ans_idx = coalesce((select (t.i - 1)::int from jsonb_array_elements_text(qq.opts) with ordinality t(v, i) where t.v = r.fixed_answer), qq.ans_idx)
    from r
   where qq.q = r.q
     and qq.opts ->> qq.ans_idx = r.answer
  returning r.n
)
insert into rationale_report select 3, count(distinct n), count(*) from upd
  on conflict (day) do update set questions_updated = excluded.questions_updated, rows_changed = excluded.rows_changed;

-- ===================== DAY 4 =====================
-- Nursing Exam Room: full rationales for CCN Mock Exam — Day 4 (200 questions)
-- Run this once in the Supabase SQL Editor. It is safe to run again.
--
-- Each explanation now says: the correct answer and why, why each other option is wrong,
-- and a key point to remember. The same questions in the practice app get the new explanation too.
-- Questions are found by their exact wording and correct answer. Where the old answer key was wrong,
-- the correct option is also fixed (listed at the top of the file).


with r(n, q, answer, exp, fixed_answer) as (values
(1, $x$Intrapleural pressure is normally:$x$, $x$Negative relative to atmospheric pressure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Negative relative to atmospheric pressure
The PLEURAL SPACE is the thin, fluid-filled gap between the two layers of pleura (visceral pleura covering the lung, parietal pleura lining the chest wall). The lungs naturally want to recoil inwards (elastic recoil), while the chest wall wants to spring outwards. These opposite pulls create a slight SUCTION in the pleural space, so INTRAPLEURAL PRESSURE is normally NEGATIVE relative to atmospheric pressure (about −4 to −5 cmH2O at rest, becoming more negative, about −8, during inspiration). This negative pressure keeps the lungs expanded against the chest wall.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Equal to atmospheric pressure — If intrapleural pressure became EQUAL to atmospheric pressure (e.g. when air enters through a wound), the lung would collapse — this is a pneumothorax.
• Positive relative to atmospheric pressure — POSITIVE intrapleural pressure occurs only during forced expiration (coughing) or in a tension pneumothorax, not normally.
• Highly variable with no consistent pattern — The pressure follows a consistent, predictable pattern with breathing.

⭐ Key point: A chest drain uses an underwater seal to let air out but not in, helping restore the negative pressure so the lung re-expands.$x$, null),
(2, $x$Which ECG change is classically associated with hypercalcemia?$x$, $x$Shortened QT interval$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Shortened QT interval
CALCIUM affects how quickly the heart muscle repolarises (recovers) after each beat. HYPERCALCAEMIA (high calcium) SPEEDS UP repolarisation, so the ST segment and QT interval become SHORT — a SHORTENED QT INTERVAL is the classic ECG sign. Very high calcium can also cause bradycardia, heart block and arrhythmias. (HYPOcalcaemia does the opposite — it PROLONGS the QT.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Prolonged QT interval — A PROLONGED QT is seen with HYPOcalcaemia, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia and many drugs.
• Peaked T waves — PEAKED T waves are a sign of HYPERkalaemia.
• Prominent U waves — Prominent U waves are a sign of HYPOkalaemia.

⭐ Key point: Common causes of hypercalcaemia in ICU: cancer (bone metastases, myeloma), hyperparathyroidism, immobilisation and thiazide diuretics. Treat with IV fluids (0.9% saline) and bisphosphonates.$x$, null),
(3, $x$How many pairs of spinal nerves arise from the human spinal cord?$x$, $x$31$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 31
There are 31 PAIRS of spinal nerves, one pair leaving each segment of the spinal cord: 8 CERVICAL (C1–C8), 12 THORACIC (T1–T12), 5 LUMBAR (L1–L5), 5 SACRAL (S1–S5) and 1 COCCYGEAL (Co1). Total: 8 + 12 + 5 + 5 + 1 = 31. Each nerve carries both sensory and motor fibres. (Note there are 8 cervical nerves but only 7 cervical vertebrae.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 12 — 12 is the number of pairs of CRANIAL nerves (and also the number of thoracic spinal nerve pairs).
• 24 — 24 is not the correct number.
• 43 — 43 is the total number of nerve pairs (31 spinal + 12 cranial), not spinal nerves alone.

⭐ Key point: Knowing the spinal levels helps nurses assess spinal injuries: e.g. C3–C5 (diaphragm), C5 (biceps), T4 (nipple line sensation), T10 (umbilicus), L4 (knee jerk), S1 (ankle jerk).$x$, null),
(4, $x$Which heart chamber pumps oxygenated blood to the entire systemic circulation?$x$, $x$Left ventricle$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Left ventricle
The LEFT VENTRICLE receives OXYGENATED blood from the left atrium (which comes from the lungs through the pulmonary veins) and pumps it through the AORTA to the WHOLE BODY (the SYSTEMIC circulation). Because it must push blood against the high systemic pressure, it has the THICKEST muscular wall of all four chambers.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Right atrium — The right atrium receives DEOXYGENATED blood from the body through the venae cavae.
• Right ventricle — The right ventricle pumps DEOXYGENATED blood to the LUNGS through the pulmonary artery (low-pressure circuit).
• Left atrium — The left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the lungs and passes it to the left ventricle; it does not pump to the body.

⭐ Key point: Left ventricular failure causes blood to back up into the lungs (pulmonary oedema); right ventricular failure causes backup into the body (raised JVP, liver congestion, leg oedema).$x$, null),
(5, $x$The skin's appendages include all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Alveoli$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Alveoli
SKIN APPENDAGES (accessory structures) develop from the epidermis and include: HAIR FOLLICLES and hair, SWEAT GLANDS (eccrine and apocrine — temperature regulation), SEBACEOUS GLANDS (produce sebum to lubricate skin and hair), and NAILS. ALVEOLI are the tiny air sacs in the LUNGS where gas exchange takes place — they are NOT part of the skin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hair follicles — Hair follicles ARE skin appendages.
• Sweat glands — Sweat glands ARE skin appendages.
• Sebaceous glands — Sebaceous glands ARE skin appendages.

⭐ Key point: In burns, deep partial-thickness wounds can heal from surviving appendages (hair follicles, sweat glands), but full-thickness burns destroy them and usually need skin grafting.$x$, null),
(6, $x$Keratinocytes make up approximately what percentage of the epidermis?$x$, $x$95%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 95%
KERATINOCYTES are the main cell type of the EPIDERMIS (outer skin layer), making up about 90–95% of its cells. They produce KERATIN, a tough protein that makes the skin waterproof and protects against injury, infection and water loss. Keratinocytes are made in the deepest layer (stratum basale) and move upward, becoming flattened dead cells at the surface (stratum corneum), which are shed. Other epidermal cells (melanocytes, Langerhans cells, Merkel cells) make up the remaining small percentage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 50% — 50% underestimates their dominance.
• 75% — 75% underestimates.
• 100% — 100% is incorrect because other cell types are also present.

⭐ Key point: Damage to the epidermis (e.g. burns, pressure injuries, TEN) removes this protective barrier, causing fluid loss and infection risk.$x$, null),
(7, $x$Urine passes through the renal collecting system in which correct sequence?$x$, $x$Minor calyx, major calyx, renal pelvis, ureter$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Minor calyx, major calyx, renal pelvis, ureter
Urine is formed in the nephrons and flows through the collecting ducts into the renal papillae, then through: MINOR CALYX → MAJOR CALYX → RENAL PELVIS → URETER → bladder → urethra. Several minor calyces join to form a major calyx; the major calyces join to form the renal pelvis, which narrows into the ureter.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Major calyx, minor calyx, renal pelvis, ureter — Major before minor calyx is the wrong order.
• Renal pelvis, major calyx, minor calyx, ureter — Renal pelvis before calyces is the wrong order.
• Minor calyx, renal pelvis, major calyx, ureter — The renal pelvis comes after the major calyx.

⭐ Key point: Kidney stones can lodge at narrow points (pelvi-ureteric junction, pelvic brim, vesico-ureteric junction), causing severe colicky pain and obstruction.$x$, null),
(8, $x$Vital cardiorespiratory centres are located in the:$x$, $x$Medulla oblongata$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Medulla oblongata
The MEDULLA OBLONGATA, the lowest part of the brainstem, contains the VITAL CENTRES: the CARDIAC centre (controls heart rate), the VASOMOTOR centre (controls blood vessel tone and BP) and the RESPIRATORY centre (controls breathing rhythm). It also controls reflexes such as coughing, swallowing and vomiting. Damage (e.g. from brain herniation through the foramen magnum) causes respiratory arrest and death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cerebellum — The cerebellum controls balance and coordination.
• Occipital lobe — The occipital lobe processes vision.
• Hypothalamus only — The hypothalamus controls temperature, hunger, thirst and hormones, but not the main cardiorespiratory centres.

⭐ Key point: Raised ICP can compress the medulla, causing Cushing's triad (hypertension, bradycardia, irregular breathing) — a pre-terminal sign.$x$, null),
(9, $x$A rightward shift of the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve is caused by:$x$, $x$Increased 2,3-DPG, CO2, and temperature$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Increased 2,3-DPG, CO2, and temperature
The oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve shows how much oxygen haemoglobin holds at different oxygen levels. A RIGHTWARD SHIFT means haemoglobin holds oxygen LESS tightly and RELEASES MORE OXYGEN to the tissues. It is caused by INCREASED 2,3-DPG, INCREASED CO2, INCREASED TEMPERATURE, and ACIDOSIS (low pH) — conditions found in active tissues that need more oxygen (memory aid: "CADET, face Right" — CO2, Acid, 2,3-DPG, Exercise, Temperature).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Decreased temperature — Decreased temperature shifts the curve to the LEFT.
• Decreased CO2 — Decreased CO2 shifts the curve to the LEFT.
• Alkalosis — Alkalosis shifts the curve to the LEFT.

⭐ Key point: Left shift (e.g. hypothermia, alkalosis, carbon monoxide, fetal haemoglobin) means haemoglobin holds oxygen tightly and releases less to tissues.$x$, null),
(10, $x$Haustra, the pouch-like segments visible along the colon, are formed largely due to the action of:$x$, $x$The teniae coli, three longitudinal bands of smooth muscle$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The teniae coli, three longitudinal bands of smooth muscle
HAUSTRA are the pouch-like bulges seen along the COLON (large intestine). They are formed because the TENIAE COLI — three narrow bands of LONGITUDINAL smooth muscle running along the colon — are SHORTER than the colon wall, so they bunch it up into pouches (like gathering fabric with a drawstring). Haustra help mix and move faeces.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The circular muscle layer alone — The circular muscle layer alone does not create haustra.
• Villi projecting from the mucosa — Villi are found in the SMALL intestine (for absorption), not the colon.
• The greater omentum — The greater omentum is a fatty apron hanging from the stomach.

⭐ Key point: On abdominal X-ray, haustra (incomplete lines across the bowel) identify the LARGE bowel, whereas valvulae conniventes (complete lines) identify the SMALL bowel.$x$, null),
(11, $x$Residual volume is the amount of air:$x$, $x$Remaining in the lungs after maximal expiration$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Remaining in the lungs after maximal expiration
RESIDUAL VOLUME (RV) is the amount of air that REMAINS in the lungs after a MAXIMAL (forced, complete) EXPIRATION — about 1.2 litres in an average adult. It cannot be breathed out and keeps the alveoli from collapsing completely, allowing continuous gas exchange between breaths.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Inhaled during a normal breath — Air inhaled during a normal breath is the TIDAL VOLUME (about 500 mL).
• Exhaled forcefully after normal expiration — Air exhaled forcefully after a normal expiration is the EXPIRATORY RESERVE VOLUME (about 1.1 L).
• Inhaled during maximal inspiration beyond tidal volume — Air inhaled beyond the tidal volume is the INSPIRATORY RESERVE VOLUME (about 3 L).

⭐ Key point: Residual volume is increased in COPD and asthma (air trapping), and reduced in restrictive lung disease (e.g. fibrosis).$x$, null),
(12, $x$The short period that occurs during the transmission of impulses through the synapses is:$x$, $x$Synaptic delay$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Synaptic delay
SYNAPTIC DELAY is the short time (about 0.5 milliseconds) it takes for a nerve impulse to cross a chemical SYNAPSE. It includes the time for the neurotransmitter to be released from the first neuron, diffuse across the synaptic cleft, and bind to receptors on the next neuron. The more synapses in a pathway, the longer the total delay.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Synaptic simulation — "Synaptic simulation" is not a physiological term.
• Synaptic junction — The synaptic junction is the structure where two neurons meet, not a time period.
• Synaptic cleft — The synaptic cleft is the physical GAP between neurons, not the time taken.

⭐ Key point: Reflex arcs with fewer synapses (e.g. the knee-jerk reflex — one synapse) are very fast.$x$, null),
(13, $x$The heart's four chambers consist of:$x$, $x$Two atria and two ventricles$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Two atria and two ventricles
The heart has FOUR CHAMBERS: TWO ATRIA (upper, thin-walled receiving chambers) and TWO VENTRICLES (lower, thick-walled pumping chambers). The right atrium and ventricle handle deoxygenated blood (to the lungs); the left atrium and ventricle handle oxygenated blood (to the body). Valves between them (tricuspid, mitral) and at the exits (pulmonary, aortic) keep blood flowing in one direction.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Four ventricles only — Four ventricles only is incorrect.
• Four atria only — Four atria only is incorrect.
• One atrium and three ventricles — One atrium and three ventricles is incorrect.

⭐ Key point: Blood flow order: body → right atrium → right ventricle → lungs → left atrium → left ventricle → aorta → body.$x$, null),
(14, $x$The kidneys contribute to regulation of acid-base balance mainly by:$x$, $x$Excreting or reabsorbing hydrogen ions and bicarbonate$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Excreting or reabsorbing hydrogen ions and bicarbonate
The KIDNEYS regulate ACID-BASE balance by EXCRETING HYDROGEN IONS (acid) into the urine (buffered by phosphate and ammonia) and by REABSORBING and GENERATING BICARBONATE (the body's main buffer). When the blood is too acidic, the kidneys excrete more acid and keep more bicarbonate; when too alkaline, they excrete bicarbonate. This "metabolic" compensation is slow (hours to days), unlike the lungs (minutes).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Producing insulin — Insulin is produced by the pancreas.
• Regulating heart rate — Heart rate is controlled by the nervous system and hormones.
• Producing bile — Bile is produced by the liver.

⭐ Key point: In kidney failure, acid accumulates, causing metabolic acidosis — a common indication for dialysis.$x$, null),
(15, $x$Water reabsorbed by the collecting duct enters the:$x$, $x$Vasa recta$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Vasa recta
In the COLLECTING DUCT, ADH (antidiuretic hormone) makes the walls permeable to water. Water moves out of the duct into the very concentrated tissue of the medulla and is then taken up by the VASA RECTA — the long, looping capillaries that run alongside the loops of Henle — which return the water to the general circulation. This allows the body to produce concentrated urine and conserve water.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nephron loop — The nephron loop (loop of Henle) is part of the nephron, not where collecting duct water goes.
• Minor calyx — The minor calyx receives urine, not reabsorbed water.
• Ureter — The ureter carries urine to the bladder.

⭐ Key point: Without ADH (diabetes insipidus), the collecting duct stays impermeable to water, causing large volumes of dilute urine.$x$, null),
(16, $x$A nurse inadvertently infuses an IV solution containing potassium chloride too rapidly. Which of the following is an appropriate intervention advised by the physician?$x$, $x$Insulin added to 10% dextrose in water solution$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Insulin added to 10% dextrose in water solution
Infusing POTASSIUM CHLORIDE too fast can cause dangerous HYPERKALAEMIA, which can lead to fatal arrhythmias and cardiac arrest. One recommended treatment is INSULIN added to 10% DEXTROSE. Insulin drives POTASSIUM INTO CELLS (by activating the sodium-potassium pump), quickly lowering blood potassium; the dextrose prevents hypoglycaemia. (Other urgent measures: STOP the infusion, give IV calcium gluconate to protect the heart, salbutamol, and remove potassium via binders or dialysis.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Rapid infusion of IV Ringer\'s solution — Ringer's solution contains potassium — it would not help.
• Rapid infusion of IV NS 0.9% — Normal saline does not lower potassium significantly.
• Administration of albumin — Albumin does not lower potassium.

⭐ Key point: Always give IV potassium by pump, diluted, and never faster than about 10 mmol/hr peripherally (20 mmol/hr centrally with ECG monitoring).$x$, null),
(17, $x$Blood transfusion reactions can be classified broadly as:$x$, $x$Immediate (e.g. acute haemolytic, allergic) and delayed (e.g. delayed haemolytic, iron overload) reactions$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Immediate (e.g. acute haemolytic, allergic) and delayed (e.g. delayed haemolytic, iron overload) reactions
BLOOD TRANSFUSION REACTIONS are broadly classified by timing: IMMEDIATE (ACUTE) reactions occur within 24 hours — acute haemolytic reaction (ABO incompatibility), allergic reactions (urticaria to anaphylaxis), febrile non-haemolytic reaction, TRALI (lung injury), TACO (fluid overload), and bacterial contamination (sepsis); DELAYED reactions occur days to weeks later — delayed haemolytic reaction, transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease, post-transfusion purpura, and IRON OVERLOAD (with repeated transfusions).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only immediate reactions exist; delayed reactions are not possible — Delayed reactions do occur.
• Only allergic reactions occur — Many reaction types exist, not just allergic.
• Reactions cannot be classified in any meaningful way — Reactions are classified meaningfully, which guides management.

⭐ Key point: For any suspected reaction: stop the transfusion, keep the vein open with saline, check vital signs and patient ID, and inform the doctor and blood bank.$x$, null),
(18, $x$Type 1 Respiratory Failure is characterised by:$x$, $x$Low PaO2 with normal or low PaCO2$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Low PaO2 with normal or low PaCO2
TYPE 1 (HYPOXAEMIC) RESPIRATORY FAILURE is defined by a LOW PaO2 (below 60 mmHg / 8 kPa) with a NORMAL or LOW PaCO2. It is a problem with GAS EXCHANGE (oxygenation), caused by V/Q mismatch or shunt — e.g. pneumonia, pulmonary oedema, ARDS, PE. The CO2 is normal or low because the patient often breathes fast.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• High PaCO2 only — High PaCO2 is TYPE 2 (hypercapnic) respiratory failure.
• Normal PaO2 and PaCO2 — Normal PaO2 and PaCO2 is not respiratory failure.
• High PaO2 with low PaCO2 — High PaO2 with low PaCO2 is not respiratory failure.

⭐ Key point: Type 1 = oxygenation problem (treat with oxygen, PEEP). Type 2 = ventilation problem (treat with ventilatory support).$x$, null),
(19, $x$To prevent graft-versus-host disease, blood is:$x$, $x$Irradiated$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Irradiated
TRANSFUSION-ASSOCIATED GRAFT-VERSUS-HOST DISEASE (TA-GvHD) happens when donor T-lymphocytes (white cells) in transfused blood attack the recipient's tissues. It is rare but almost always fatal. IRRADIATING blood (gamma or X-ray) damages the DNA of donor lymphocytes so they cannot multiply, preventing TA-GvHD. Irradiated blood is given to high-risk patients: immunocompromised (e.g. stem cell transplant, Hodgkin lymphoma, certain chemotherapies), intrauterine and neonatal exchange transfusions, and transfusions from relatives.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Leukocyte-reduced — Leukocyte reduction reduces febrile reactions and CMV transmission but does not reliably prevent TA-GvHD.
• Washed — Washing removes plasma proteins (for severe allergic reactions).
• Frozen — Freezing is used for long-term storage of rare blood.

⭐ Key point: Check that "irradiated" is specified on blood products when required.$x$, null),
(20, $x$A Modified Allen's Test that shows NO flushing after releasing the ulnar artery indicates:$x$, $x$Inadequate collateral circulation — avoid that site$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Inadequate collateral circulation — avoid that site
The MODIFIED ALLEN'S TEST checks whether the ULNAR ARTERY can supply blood to the hand on its own (collateral circulation), in case the radial artery becomes blocked after puncture. Both arteries are compressed while the patient clenches the fist; the hand goes pale. When the ULNAR artery is released, colour (flushing) should return within about 5–15 seconds. NO FLUSHING means INADEQUATE COLLATERAL CIRCULATION — do NOT use that radial artery; choose another site.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Safe to proceed with radial puncture — It is NOT safe to proceed if there is no flushing.
• The patient is dehydrated — It doesn't indicate dehydration.
• A normal, expected finding — No flushing is an abnormal finding.

⭐ Key point: Document the result of Allen's test before radial arterial puncture or line insertion.$x$, null),
(21, $x$The normal haemoglobin range for an adult male is approximately:$x$, $x$13.5–17.5 g/dL$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 13.5–17.5 g/dL
The NORMAL HAEMOGLOBIN (Hb) range for an ADULT MALE is about 13.5–17.5 g/dL (135–175 g/L). For adult females it is about 12.0–15.5 g/dL. Haemoglobin carries oxygen in red blood cells. Low Hb (anaemia) reduces oxygen delivery to tissues.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 6–8 g/dL — 6–8 g/dL is severe anaemia.
• 20–24 g/dL — 20–24 g/dL is severe polycythaemia.
• 2–4 g/dL — 2–4 g/dL is life-threatening anaemia.

⭐ Key point: In most stable ICU patients, a restrictive transfusion threshold of Hb 7 g/dL (70 g/L) is used; higher in active bleeding or acute coronary syndrome.$x$, null),
(22, $x$George Kent is a 54-year-old widower with a history of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and was rushed to the emergency department with increasing shortness of breath, pyrexia, and a productive cough with yellow-green sputum. He has difficulty communicating because of his inability to complete a sentence. One of his sons, Jacob, says he has been unwell for three days. Upon examination, crackles and wheezes can be heard in the lower lobes; he has tachycardia and a bounding pulse. Measurement of arterial blood gas shows pH 7.3, PaCO2 68 mm Hg, HCO3 28 mmol/L, and PaO2 60 mm Hg. How would you interpret this?$x$, $x$Respiratory Acidosis, Partially Compensated$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Respiratory Acidosis, Partially Compensated
STEP 1 — pH 7.3: ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — PaCO2 68 mmHg is HIGH: RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS (COPD exacerbation — tired breathing muscles and airway obstruction cause CO2 retention). STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 28 mmol/L is slightly HIGH: the kidneys have started compensating (retaining bicarbonate), but the pH is still below 7.35. Answer: RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS, PARTIALLY COMPENSATED. PaO2 60 mmHg shows hypoxaemia. The infection (yellow-green sputum, fever) has triggered acute-on-chronic respiratory failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic Alkalosis, Uncompensated — Metabolic alkalosis would show a high pH.
• Metabolic Acidosis, Partially Compensated — Metabolic acidosis would show a LOW HCO3⁻.
• Respiratory Acidosis, Uncompensated — Uncompensated would show a NORMAL HCO3⁻ (22–26).

⭐ Key point: Treat with controlled oxygen (target SpO2 88–92%), nebulised bronchodilators, steroids, antibiotics, and NIV if pH below 7.35.$x$, null),
(23, $x$Radial artery sampling for ABG is typically performed at what needle angle?$x$, $x$45°$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 45°
For RADIAL ARTERY ABG sampling, the needle is usually inserted at a 30–45 DEGREE angle, with the bevel up, facing against the blood flow. This angle allows the needle to enter the artery smoothly, which lies relatively superficially at the wrist. (For deeper arteries like the femoral, a steeper 60–90° angle is used.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 90° — 90° is used for the femoral artery or intramuscular injections.
• 10° — 10° is too shallow and may miss the artery.
• 180° — 180° is not a possible insertion angle.

⭐ Key point: After sampling, apply firm pressure for at least 5 minutes (longer if anticoagulated) to prevent haematoma.$x$, null),
(24, $x$Haematological investigations typically assess:$x$, $x$Blood cell counts, haemoglobin, and coagulation parameters$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Blood cell counts, haemoglobin, and coagulation parameters
HAEMATOLOGICAL investigations study the BLOOD and blood-forming tissues. They include: FULL BLOOD COUNT (haemoglobin, red cells, white cells and differential, platelets, haematocrit), blood film, COAGULATION tests (PT/INR, APTT, fibrinogen, D-dimer), reticulocyte count, iron studies, B12 and folate, blood group and crossmatch, and bone marrow examination.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only urine composition — Urine composition is assessed by urinalysis (renal/biochemistry).
• Only stool consistency — Stool consistency is a gastrointestinal assessment.
• Only skin pigmentation — Skin pigmentation is a dermatological assessment.

⭐ Key point: Daily FBC and clotting tests are common in ICU to monitor bleeding, infection, and drug effects (e.g. heparin).$x$, null),
(25, $x$Early ECG changes of hyperkalaemia typically include:$x$, $x$Tall, peaked T waves$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Tall, peaked T waves
HYPERKALAEMIA affects heart conduction. EARLY ECG changes: TALL, PEAKED (tented) T WAVES. As potassium rises further: flattened or absent P waves, prolonged PR interval, WIDE QRS complexes, a "sine wave" pattern, and finally ventricular fibrillation or asystole.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Flattened T waves — Flattened T waves are seen in HYPOkalaemia.
• Prolonged QT interval as the earliest change — Prolonged QT is not the earliest change of hyperkalaemia (it is seen with hypocalcaemia, hypokalaemia).
• U waves — U waves are seen in HYPOkalaemia.

⭐ Key point: Treat severe hyperkalaemia: calcium gluconate (protect the heart), insulin-dextrose and salbutamol (shift potassium into cells), then remove potassium (binders, dialysis).$x$, null),
(26, $x$Potassium is most abundant in which compartment?$x$, $x$Intracellular$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Intracellular
About 98% of the body's POTASSIUM is INSIDE the CELLS (INTRACELLULAR), at a concentration of about 140–150 mmol/L. Only about 2% is outside the cells (extracellular), where the normal blood level is 3.5–5.0 mmol/L. This large difference is maintained by the SODIUM-POTASSIUM PUMP and is essential for nerve and muscle function, including heart rhythm.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Extracellular — Extracellular fluid contains only a small amount.
• Intravascular — Intravascular (blood plasma) contains a small amount.
• Interstitial — Interstitial fluid contains a small amount.

⭐ Key point: Small shifts of potassium between cells and blood (e.g. from insulin, acidosis, cell breakdown) can cause big changes in blood levels.$x$, null),
(27, $x$The following are causes of respiratory acidosis EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Hyperventilation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hyperventilation
RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS is caused by HYPOVENTILATION — CO2 builds up because not enough is breathed out. Causes include COPD, severe asthma (exhaustion), head injury (depressed breathing centre), opioids, neuromuscular weakness, and chest wall problems. HYPERVENTILATION causes the OPPOSITE — CO2 is blown off, causing RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• COPD — COPD IS a cause (CO2 retention).
• Severe asthma — Severe asthma IS a cause when the patient tires.
• Head injury — Head injury IS a cause (depressed respiratory drive).

⭐ Key point: Respiratory acidosis = high PaCO2, low pH. Respiratory alkalosis = low PaCO2, high pH.$x$, null),
(28, $x$When starting a patient on opioids, the recommended protocol for preventing constipation is to:$x$, $x$Use a bowel stimulant and a stool softener$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Use a bowel stimulant and a stool softener
OPIOIDS slow the movement of the gut (reduced peristalsis) and increase fluid absorption from the stool, causing CONSTIPATION in most patients, and tolerance to this side effect does NOT develop. The recommended prevention is a combination of a BOWEL STIMULANT (e.g. senna, bisacodyl — increases gut movement) and a STOOL SOFTENER (e.g. docusate, or an osmotic laxative such as lactulose or macrogol), started at the same time as the opioid.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Add bulk fibre to the diet — Bulk fibre alone can worsen constipation with opioids (slow gut plus bulk can cause impaction).
• Give enemas as needed — Enemas "as needed" are reactive, not preventive.
• Increase fluids and exercise only — Fluids and exercise help, but are not enough alone.

⭐ Key point: For severe opioid-induced constipation, peripherally acting opioid antagonists (e.g. methylnaltrexone) can be used.$x$, null),
(29, $x$A patient presents with widened QRS complex, hypotension, and seizures after a tricyclic antidepressant (TCA) overdose. Which treatment is indicated to counteract the cardiotoxic sodium-channel blocking effect?$x$, $x$IV sodium bicarbonate$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: IV sodium bicarbonate
TRICYCLIC ANTIDEPRESSANTS (TCAs, e.g. amitriptyline) block SODIUM CHANNELS in the heart, which WIDENS the QRS complex and can cause ventricular arrhythmias, hypotension and seizures. IV SODIUM BICARBONATE (8.4%) is the treatment: the extra sodium overcomes the channel blockade, and the alkalosis (raised pH) reduces TCA binding to the sodium channels. Target blood pH 7.45–7.55.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Flumazenil — Flumazenil can cause seizures in TCA overdose and is contraindicated.
• Naloxone — Naloxone reverses opioids, not TCAs.
• Activated charcoal alone, with no other treatment — Activated charcoal may be given early (with a protected airway), but it does not treat established cardiotoxicity.

⭐ Key point: Treat TCA-induced seizures with benzodiazepines; avoid phenytoin and class Ia/Ic antiarrhythmics.$x$, null),
(30, $x$Bronchodilator nebulisers (e.g. salbutamol) can cause which common side effect that the nurse should monitor for?$x$, $x$Tachycardia and tremor$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Tachycardia and tremor
SALBUTAMOL (a beta-2 agonist) relaxes airway smooth muscle (bronchodilation). It also stimulates beta receptors elsewhere: in the heart, causing TACHYCARDIA and palpitations; in skeletal muscle, causing a fine TREMOR. It can also lower potassium (hypokalaemia) and raise glucose and lactate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bradycardia and sedation — Bradycardia and sedation are not salbutamol effects.
• Hypoglycaemia — Salbutamol can RAISE blood glucose, not lower it.
• Weight gain — Weight gain is not a side effect.

⭐ Key point: Monitor heart rate, potassium and lactate in patients receiving frequent salbutamol nebulisers.$x$, null),
(31, $x$A patient with Torsades de Pointes is prescribed magnesium sulfate. Which ECG change confirms efficacy?$x$, $x$Shortened QT interval$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Shortened QT interval
TORSADES DE POINTES is a polymorphic VT associated with a PROLONGED QT interval. MAGNESIUM SULPHATE stabilises the heart cell membranes and suppresses the early after-depolarisations that trigger torsades. Its effectiveness is confirmed when the arrhythmia stops and the QT INTERVAL SHORTENS (returns towards normal), reducing the risk of recurrence.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Resolution of U waves — U waves relate to potassium, not the main target.
• Narrowed QRS complex — Narrowed QRS relates to sodium channel blockade (e.g. TCA overdose).
• Absent P waves — Absent P waves are seen in AF or hyperkalaemia.

⭐ Key point: Also correct potassium (keep above 4.5 mmol/L) and stop QT-prolonging drugs.$x$, null),
(32, $x$A patient receiving IV vancomycin develops flushing, a rash on the upper body, and hypotension shortly after the infusion starts. This presentation is most consistent with:$x$, $x$'Red man syndrome,' related to rapid infusion rate rather than true allergy in many cases$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 'Red man syndrome,' related to rapid infusion rate rather than true allergy in many cases
VANCOMYCIN infused TOO RAPIDLY can cause direct histamine release from mast cells — the "RED MAN" SYNDROME (now called vancomycin infusion reaction). Features: FLUSHING, itching and a red RASH on the face, neck and upper body, sometimes with HYPOTENSION. In most cases, it is related to the INFUSION RATE, not a true allergy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A true IgE-mediated anaphylactic reaction only — It is not usually a true IgE-mediated anaphylactic reaction (though anaphylaxis is possible and must be excluded if there is wheeze or airway swelling).
• Normal expected drug effect requiring no action — It requires action (slowing or stopping the infusion).
• Sepsis unrelated to the medication — The timing with the infusion points to the drug, not sepsis.

⭐ Key point: Stop or slow the infusion, give an antihistamine, and restart slowly (at least 60 minutes per gram).$x$, null),
(33, $x$Vasopressor infusions such as norepinephrine and dopamine should preferably be administered via:$x$, $x$A central venous catheter, to reduce the risk of extravasation injury and tissue necrosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A central venous catheter, to reduce the risk of extravasation injury and tissue necrosis
VASOPRESSORS such as NORADRENALINE and DOPAMINE are powerful VASOCONSTRICTORS. If they leak out of a vein (EXTRAVASATION), they constrict the blood vessels in the surrounding tissue, causing TISSUE ISCHAEMIA and NECROSIS. A CENTRAL VENOUS CATHETER delivers the drug into a large vein with high blood flow, reducing this risk.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A peripheral IV in the hand for at least 24 hours before central access — Peripheral infusion for 24 hours increases the risk of extravasation injury; short-term peripheral use may be acceptable in emergencies with a large, well-placed cannula and close monitoring.
• Intramuscular injection — Vasopressors are not given IM.
• Oral route once the patient is hemodynamically stable — Vasopressors are not given orally.

⭐ Key point: If extravasation occurs, stop the infusion, aspirate if possible, and consider phentolamine infiltration.$x$, null),
(34, $x$10 year boys on admission with diagnosis of leukemia, which of these method/route of drugs administration will you avoid?$x$, $x$Intramuscular route$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Intramuscular route
Children with LEUKAEMIA often have LOW PLATELETS (thrombocytopenia), so injections into muscle (INTRAMUSCULAR) can cause HAEMATOMAS (bleeding into the muscle). They also have LOW white cells (neutropenia), so IM injections increase the risk of INFECTION and abscesses. The IM route should be AVOIDED.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral route — Oral is safe if the child can swallow.
• Intravenous route — IV (usually via central line) is the main route for chemotherapy and antibiotics.
• Sublingual route — Sublingual is safe.

⭐ Key point: Also avoid rectal temperatures, enemas and suppositories in neutropenic or thrombocytopenic patients.$x$, null),
(35, $x$Dobutamine is primarily used in critical care for its:$x$, $x$Inotropic effect, improving cardiac contractility and output$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Inotropic effect, improving cardiac contractility and output
DOBUTAMINE is mainly used for its INOTROPIC EFFECT — it stimulates β1 receptors in the heart, INCREASING CONTRACTILITY and CARDIAC OUTPUT. It is used in cardiogenic shock and low-output heart failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Vasoconstrictor effect — It is not a vasoconstrictor; it can cause mild vasodilation.
• Antiarrhythmic properties — It is not an antiarrhythmic; it can cause arrhythmias.
• Diuretic effect — It is not a diuretic (though improved output may increase urine).

⭐ Key point: Monitor heart rate and rhythm, BP and urine output during dobutamine infusion.$x$, null),
(36, $x$When titrating sedation in a mechanically ventilated ICU patient, the nurse should primarily use:$x$, $x$A validated sedation scale (e.g. RASS) to guide titration$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A validated sedation scale (e.g. RASS) to guide titration
Sedation in ventilated ICU patients should be titrated using a VALIDATED SEDATION SCALE, such as the RICHMOND AGITATION-SEDATION SCALE (RASS) or Sedation-Agitation Scale (SAS). These give an objective target (often RASS −2 to 0 for light sedation), avoiding over-sedation (which prolongs ventilation and causes delirium) and under-sedation (agitation, self-extubation).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The patient's family's preference alone — Family preferences are important but cannot guide titration alone.
• No formal scale — sedation should be maximised at all times — Maximising sedation is harmful.
• Blood pressure alone as the only guide — BP alone is not a reliable guide to sedation depth.

⭐ Key point: Combine sedation scales with pain scales (CPOT, BPS) and delirium screening (CAM-ICU).$x$, null),
(37, $x$Which of the following is a thrombolytic agent?$x$, $x$Streptokinase$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Streptokinase
A THROMBOLYTIC (fibrinolytic) agent DISSOLVES EXISTING CLOTS by activating plasminogen to plasmin, which breaks down fibrin. STREPTOKINASE is a thrombolytic (others: alteplase, tenecteplase, reteplase). Used in STEMI (when PCI is not available), massive PE, and acute ischaemic stroke (alteplase).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Heparin — Heparin is an anticoagulant — it prevents new clots and growth, but doesn't dissolve existing clots.
• Clexane (enoxaparin) — Clexane (enoxaparin) is a low molecular weight heparin — an anticoagulant.
• Warfarin sodium — Warfarin is an oral anticoagulant.

⭐ Key point: Main risk of thrombolysis is bleeding, especially intracranial haemorrhage. Check contraindications carefully.$x$, null),
(38, $x$The preferred route for surgical antibiotic prophylaxis is:$x$, $x$Systemic (IV)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Systemic (IV)
SURGICAL ANTIBIOTIC PROPHYLAXIS is given by the SYSTEMIC (IV) route, usually within 60 MINUTES BEFORE the skin incision, so that high antibiotic levels are present in the tissues at the time of surgery. It reduces surgical site infections.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral — Oral absorption is unreliable and too slow for timing before surgery.
• Topical only — Topical antibiotics alone are not standard prophylaxis.
• Intramuscular only — IM is rarely used.

⭐ Key point: A repeat dose may be needed for long operations or major blood loss; prophylaxis is usually not continued beyond 24 hours.$x$, null),
(39, $x$Hypnotic drugs, as a pharmacological class, are primarily used to:$x$, $x$Induce and maintain sleep$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Induce and maintain sleep
HYPNOTIC drugs are used to INDUCE and MAINTAIN SLEEP. Examples: benzodiazepines (e.g. temazepam), "Z-drugs" (zolpidem, zopiclone), and melatonin agonists. In ICU, natural sleep promotion (lights off at night, noise reduction, eye masks, ear plugs) is preferred, and hypnotics are used cautiously because they can cause delirium and respiratory depression.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Treat hypertension — Hypertension is treated with antihypertensives.
• Reverse anticoagulation — Anticoagulation is reversed with specific antidotes.
• Increase heart rate — Increasing heart rate is done with drugs like atropine.

⭐ Key point: Avoid hypnotics in elderly patients where possible (falls, confusion).$x$, null),
(40, $x$The antidote for paracetamol overdose is:$x$, $x$Acetylcysteine$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Acetylcysteine
N-ACETYLCYSTEINE (NAC, acetylcysteine) is the ANTIDOTE for PARACETAMOL (acetaminophen) OVERDOSE. In overdose, the liver's normal safe pathways are overwhelmed, and more paracetamol is turned into a toxic chemical called NAPQI. Normally, GLUTATHIONE in liver cells neutralises NAPQI, but in overdose glutathione runs out and NAPQI destroys liver cells (acute liver failure). NAC REPLENISHES GLUTATHIONE, protecting the liver. It is almost 100% protective if started within 8 hours of ingestion, and still helps when given later. Treatment decisions use the paracetamol level plotted on the treatment nomogram (or treat immediately in staggered or late overdoses).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Flumazenil — Flumazenil reverses BENZODIAZEPINES (e.g. diazepam), not paracetamol.
• Naloxone — Naloxone reverses OPIOIDS (e.g. morphine, heroin), not paracetamol.
• Atropine — Atropine treats ORGANOPHOSPHATE poisoning and symptomatic bradycardia.

⭐ Key point: NAC is given IV over 12–21 hours. It can cause anaphylactoid reactions (flushing, rash, wheeze) — slow or pause the infusion and give an antihistamine, then restart.$x$, null),
(41, $x$Which of the following is classified as a MAJOR/SEVERE contrast reaction?$x$, $x$Laryngeal oedema and respiratory arrest$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Laryngeal oedema and respiratory arrest
CONTRAST REACTIONS are graded as mild, moderate or severe. SEVERE (major) reactions are life-threatening: LARYNGEAL OEDEMA (airway swelling), severe bronchospasm, profound hypotension, seizures, cardiac arrest and RESPIRATORY ARREST. These require immediate emergency treatment (adrenaline IM, oxygen, airway management, fluids, CPR).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mild urticaria — Mild urticaria is a MILD reaction.
• Metallic taste — A metallic taste is a normal physiological effect.
• Mild nausea — Mild nausea is a MILD reaction.

⭐ Key point: Ask about previous contrast reactions, allergies, asthma and kidney function before giving contrast.$x$, null),
(42, $x$On imaging of intestinal obstruction, distinguishing small bowel from large bowel obstruction is aided by which feature?$x$, $x$Small bowel loops show valvulae conniventes (complete transverse lines); large bowel shows haustra (incomplete lines)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Small bowel loops show valvulae conniventes (complete transverse lines); large bowel shows haustra (incomplete lines)
On ABDOMINAL X-RAY, SMALL BOWEL loops show VALVULAE CONNIVENTES — mucosal folds that cross the ENTIRE WIDTH of the bowel (complete lines, like a "stack of coins"). LARGE BOWEL shows HAUSTRA — folds that do NOT cross the full width (incomplete lines). Small bowel is usually central; large bowel is peripheral (framing the abdomen).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• There is no radiological way to distinguish them — Plain X-ray can distinguish them.
• Only CT can ever differentiate them, never plain X-ray — Plain X-ray can be used; CT gives more detail.
• Large bowel is always more centrally located regardless of distension — Large bowel is usually peripheral, not central.

⭐ Key point: Upper limits of normal diameter: small bowel 3 cm, colon 6 cm, caecum 9 cm (the "3-6-9 rule").$x$, null),
(43, $x$Mediastinal change seen in massive pleural effusion on a chest radiograph$x$, $x$Shift of the mediastinum to the opposite$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Shift of the mediastinum to the opposite
A MASSIVE PLEURAL EFFUSION fills the chest cavity with fluid, which PUSHES the MEDIASTINUM (heart, trachea) to the OPPOSITE side (away from the effusion).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Shift of mediastinum to the site of effusion — Shift towards the effusion occurs in lung collapse (loss of volume), not effusion.
• Central mediastinum — A massive effusion causes shift, not a central mediastinum.
• Tracheal shift to the site of lesion — The trachea shifts AWAY from a massive effusion.

⭐ Key point: Mediastinal shift AWAY: massive effusion, tension pneumothorax. TOWARDS: collapse, pneumonectomy.$x$, null),
(44, $x$Doppler ultrasound is particularly useful for assessing:$x$, $x$Blood flow and vascular patency (e.g. DVT, graft/fistula flow)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Blood flow and vascular patency (e.g. DVT, graft/fistula flow)
DOPPLER ULTRASOUND measures the movement of blood using the Doppler effect (sound waves change frequency when bouncing off moving blood cells). It is useful for assessing BLOOD FLOW and VASCULAR PATENCY: detecting DVT, arterial stenosis or occlusion, checking AV fistula and graft flow, and assessing fetal and placental blood flow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bone density — Bone density is measured by DEXA scan.
• Lung consolidation — Lung consolidation is assessed by X-ray, CT, or lung ultrasound.
• Brain electrical activity — Brain electrical activity is measured by EEG.

⭐ Key point: Bedside Doppler is used to check peripheral pulses when they can't be felt.$x$, null),
(45, $x$The minimum amount of pleural fluid usually needed to be visible on a standard erect chest X-ray is:$x$, $x$150–300ml$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 150–300ml
On a standard ERECT (PA) chest X-ray, about 150–300 mL of pleural fluid is needed to blunt the COSTOPHRENIC ANGLE and be visible. Smaller amounts can be detected by lateral X-ray (about 50 mL), ultrasound (as little as 5–20 mL), or CT.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10–50ml — 10–50 mL is too little to see on a standard PA film.
• 500–750ml — 500–750 mL is far more than the minimum.
• 1000–1500ml — 1,000–1,500 mL would cause obvious large opacity.

⭐ Key point: Bedside ultrasound is the most sensitive and practical tool for detecting and draining pleural fluid in ICU.$x$, null),
(46, $x$Which of the following is an example of secondary (long-term) storage?$x$, $x$Hard disk drive$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hard disk drive
Computer memory is divided into PRIMARY memory (RAM, cache, registers — fast but TEMPORARY, lost when the power goes off) and SECONDARY storage (LONG-TERM, PERMANENT, keeps data when the power is off). A HARD DISK DRIVE (HDD) is secondary storage: it stores the operating system, programs and files (e.g. patient records) permanently on spinning magnetic disks. Other secondary storage: solid-state drives (SSD), USB flash drives, memory cards, optical discs (CD/DVD) and cloud storage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• RAM — RAM (Random Access Memory) is PRIMARY memory — it holds what the computer is working on now and is erased when the power is switched off.
• CPU cache — CPU cache is very fast, small, temporary memory inside or near the processor.
• CPU register — CPU registers are tiny storage locations inside the processor that hold data being processed at that instant.

⭐ Key point: Always save work to secondary storage (and back it up) — unsaved data in RAM is lost during power cuts, which are common in many hospitals.$x$, null),
(47, $x$Why will computer-based patient records benefit clients more than paper records?$x$, $x$All of the options$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: All of the options
COMPUTER-BASED patient records benefit clients more than paper records because: EASY RETRIEVAL of case files across facilities (records can be shared between hospitals and departments); BACKED-UP STORAGE protects data against loss (fire, flood, theft); LESS TIME spent finding files (instant search). So "ALL OF THE OPTIONS" is correct. Other benefits: legibility, decision support alerts, and better data for audit and research.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Easy retrieval of case files across facilities — Easy retrieval is only one benefit.
• Backed-up storage protects data against loss (e.g. fire) — Backup is only one benefit.
• Less time spent retrieving case files — Time saving is only one benefit.

⭐ Key point: EHRs need strong security (passwords, access controls, audit trails) and downtime plans.$x$, null),
(48, $x$System software, as distinct from application software, is best described as:$x$, $x$Software that manages computer hardware and provides a platform for other programs to run (e.g. an operating system)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Software that manages computer hardware and provides a platform for other programs to run (e.g. an operating system)
SYSTEM SOFTWARE MANAGES the computer's HARDWARE (processor, memory, storage, devices) and PROVIDES A PLATFORM for other programs to run. The main example is the OPERATING SYSTEM (e.g. Windows, Linux, macOS), plus device drivers and utilities.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Software written by end users for a single specific task — Software written for a specific task is APPLICATION software.
• Software used exclusively for entertainment — Entertainment software is a type of application software.
• A type of computer virus — A virus is malicious software, not system software.

⭐ Key point: Application software (e.g. EHR, Word) runs on top of system software.$x$, null),
(49, $x$An EHR audit trail is used to:$x$, $x$Track who accessed or modified a patient record and when$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Track who accessed or modified a patient record and when
An EHR AUDIT TRAIL is an automatic record that TRACKS WHO ACCESSED or MODIFIED a patient's record and WHEN (including what was viewed or changed). It protects patient confidentiality, detects unauthorised access, and provides legal evidence.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Automatically diagnose patients — Audit trails do not diagnose patients.
• Replace nursing notes — They do not replace nursing notes.
• Calculate medication dosages — They do not calculate doses.

⭐ Key point: Only access the records of patients you are caring for — all access is recorded.$x$, null),
(50, $x$Random access file arrangement, compared to sequential access, allows:$x$, $x$Records to be accessed directly without reading through all preceding records$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Records to be accessed directly without reading through all preceding records
RANDOM (DIRECT) ACCESS file organisation allows records to be accessed DIRECTLY (e.g. by a key or address) WITHOUT reading through all the preceding records. This is fast for finding individual records (e.g. one patient's file).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only reading records strictly in the order they were stored — Reading only in stored order is SEQUENTIAL access.
• No possibility of updating individual records — Random access allows updating individual records.
• Access only during scheduled batch windows — Random access is available at any time, not only in batch windows.

⭐ Key point: Databases often combine indexed and direct access for speed.$x$, null),
(51, $x$To increase SpO2 in a mechanical ventilated patient, which of the following parameters should be adjusted:$x$, $x$PEEP and FiO2$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: PEEP and FiO2
OXYGENATION (SpO2, PaO2) on a ventilator is improved mainly by increasing the FiO2 (fraction of inspired oxygen) and PEEP (positive end-expiratory pressure). FiO2 increases the oxygen in each breath; PEEP keeps alveoli open (recruits collapsed alveoli), reducing shunt and improving oxygen transfer.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fio2 and R/R — Rate mainly affects CO2.
• Vt and R/R — Tidal volume and rate mainly affect CO2.
• Vt and PEEP — Tidal volume affects CO2 more than oxygen.

⭐ Key point: Oxygenation = FiO2 + PEEP. Ventilation (CO2) = rate + tidal volume.$x$, null),
(52, $x$All of the following are synonyms for (or terms linked to) ARDS EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Acute liver injury$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Acute liver injury
ARDS (Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome) is also known as or linked to: SHOCK LUNG syndrome (old name), DIFFUSE ALVEOLAR DAMAGE (the pathological finding), ACUTE LUNG INJURY (older term for milder forms), "wet lung" and "stiff lung". ACUTE LIVER INJURY is a LIVER condition, unrelated to ARDS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Shock lung syndrome — Shock lung is an old name for ARDS.
• Diffuse alveolar damage — Diffuse alveolar damage is the typical pathology of ARDS.
• Acute lung injury — Acute lung injury was the older term for mild ARDS.

⭐ Key point: The Berlin definition (2012) replaced "acute lung injury" with mild ARDS.$x$, null),
(53, $x$1. Which One of the following is NOT a cause of acute ventilation failure?$x$, $x$Appendectomy$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Appendectomy
ACUTE VENTILATORY FAILURE is caused by problems with the breathing "pump" or lungs: NEUROLOGICAL impairment (e.g. head injury, stroke, drug overdose depressing the breathing centre), NEUROMUSCULAR impairment (e.g. Guillain-Barré, myasthenia gravis, spinal cord injury), and PRIMARY RESPIRATORY problems (e.g. COPD, asthma, pneumonia). An APPENDECTOMY is a surgical procedure, not a cause of ventilatory failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Neurological impairment — Neurological impairment IS a cause.
• Neuromuscular impairment — Neuromuscular impairment IS a cause.
• Primary respiratory impairment — Primary respiratory impairment IS a cause.

⭐ Key point: Post-operative patients can develop ventilatory failure from opioids, pain or atelectasis — but the surgery itself is not the cause.$x$, null),
(54, $x$All trauma patients undergoing emergency intubation should be treated as having a 'full stomach' primarily because:$x$, $x$Trauma, pain, and opioids delay gastric emptying, increasing aspiration risk$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Trauma, pain, and opioids delay gastric emptying, increasing aspiration risk
All trauma patients needing emergency intubation are treated as having a "FULL STOMACH" because TRAUMA, PAIN, STRESS and OPIOIDS DELAY GASTRIC EMPTYING, so food or fluid may still be in the stomach many hours after the last meal. This greatly increases the risk of ASPIRATION during induction. Rapid sequence induction (RSI) with cricoid pressure (where used) is performed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• They have usually eaten recently — The time since the last meal is often unknown and not the main reason.
• It simplifies documentation — It's not for documentation.
• It reduces the need for preoxygenation — Pre-oxygenation is still essential.

⭐ Key point: Have suction ready and avoid bag-mask ventilation with high pressures (which inflates the stomach).$x$, null),
(55, $x$Which of the following is not a treatment modality for patient with epiglottitis?$x$, $x$Observation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Observation
EPIGLOTTITIS is a life-threatening swelling of the epiglottis that can suddenly block the airway. It always needs ACTIVE treatment, not simple observation. Treatment includes: SECURING THE AIRWAY (calling senior anaesthesia/ENT help; intubation in theatre, with a surgical airway — TRACHEOSTOMY or cricothyroidotomy — if intubation fails), IV ANTIBIOTICS (e.g. ceftriaxone, against Haemophilus influenzae type b and streptococci), STEROIDS (dexamethasone, to reduce swelling), humidified oxygen, and ICU monitoring. "OBSERVATION" ALONE is NOT a treatment modality — waiting without intervention risks sudden, complete airway obstruction and death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tracheostomy — Tracheostomy (or cricothyroidotomy) IS used when the airway cannot be secured by intubation.
• Steroids — Steroids ARE used to reduce airway swelling.
• Antibiotics — Antibiotics ARE essential to treat the bacterial infection.

⭐ Key point: Do not lie the patient flat, do not examine the throat with a tongue depressor, and do not cause distress (e.g. cannulation) before airway experts arrive.$x$, null),
(56, $x$A nurse is assessing a patient with a known history of COPD. The nurse should be aware that the patient is at risk for Type II respiratory failure due to:$x$, $x$Chronic carbon dioxide retention$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Chronic carbon dioxide retention
Patients with COPD often have CHRONIC CARBON DIOXIDE RETENTION due to airway obstruction, air trapping, V/Q mismatch, and tired respiratory muscles. This puts them at risk of TYPE II (hypercapnic) respiratory failure, especially during exacerbations or with excessive oxygen.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An increase in gas exchange efficiency — COPD reduces gas exchange efficiency.
• A decrease in lung compliance — COPD (emphysema) often increases compliance; reduced compliance is more typical of fibrosis.
• A hyperactive respiratory drive — COPD patients do not have a hyperactive drive causing type II failure.

⭐ Key point: Target SpO2 88–92% in COPD patients at risk of CO2 retention.$x$, null),
(57, $x$A patient with a haemothorax and hypovolemic shock requires which priority intervention alongside chest tube drainage?$x$, $x$Fluid resuscitation and blood product transfusion as indicated$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fluid resuscitation and blood product transfusion as indicated
A HAEMOTHORAX with HYPOVOLAEMIC SHOCK means significant blood loss into the chest. Along with CHEST TUBE drainage (to remove blood and re-expand the lung), the priority is FLUID RESUSCITATION and BLOOD PRODUCT TRANSFUSION to restore circulating volume.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diuretic therapy — Diuretics would worsen hypovolaemia.
• Immediate extubation — Extubation is inappropriate in a shocked patient.
• Withholding IV access until surgery — IV access is essential for resuscitation.

⭐ Key point: Massive haemothorax (over 1,500 mL initially or over 200 mL/hr for 2–4 hours) may need thoracotomy.$x$, null),
(58, $x$The most common trigger for an asthma exacerbation is:$x$, $x$Viral respiratory infection$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Viral respiratory infection
VIRAL RESPIRATORY INFECTIONS — especially RHINOVIRUS (common cold), influenza and RSV — are the MOST COMMON trigger of ASTHMA EXACERBATIONS in both children and adults (responsible for most attacks). The virus inflames the airway lining, increases mucus production and makes the airways more "twitchy" (hyper-reactive), causing bronchospasm. Exacerbations are more frequent in the rainy/cold season when viral infections spread.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Food allergy — Food allergy can trigger asthma in some people, but it is not the most common trigger.
• Exercise — Exercise can cause bronchoconstriction (exercise-induced asthma), but it rarely causes severe exacerbations requiring hospital care.
• Cold weather alone — Cold weather is a trigger, often because it increases viral infections, but it is not the most common trigger on its own.

⭐ Key point: Prevention: regular inhaled corticosteroids, annual influenza vaccination, hand hygiene, avoiding smoke and allergens, and a written asthma action plan.$x$, null),
(59, $x$Which are the Ventilator Parameters adjusted to maintain the optimum Minute Ventilation?$x$, $x$Tidal Volume and Respiratory Rate$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Tidal Volume and Respiratory Rate
MINUTE VENTILATION (VE) is the total amount of air moved in and out of the lungs each minute: VE = TIDAL VOLUME (Vt) × RESPIRATORY RATE (RR). On a ventilator, VE is controlled by adjusting the TIDAL VOLUME and the RESPIRATORY RATE. Minute ventilation determines how much CO2 is removed: increasing Vt or RR lowers PaCO2; decreasing them raises PaCO2. Normal adult VE is about 5–8 L/min.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• FiO2 — FiO2 (fraction of inspired oxygen) controls OXYGENATION, not minute ventilation.
• PEEP — PEEP (positive end-expiratory pressure) improves OXYGENATION by keeping alveoli open; it does not set minute ventilation.
• Both A and B — "Both A and B" (FiO2 and PEEP) are both oxygenation controls, not ventilation controls.

⭐ Key point: Remember: OXYGENATION = FiO2 + PEEP; VENTILATION (CO2) = rate + tidal volume. Keep tidal volume lung-protective (6–8 mL/kg predicted body weight).$x$, null),
(60, $x$A 19-year-old comes into the emergency department with acute asthma. His respiratory rate is 44 breaths/minute, and he appears to be in acute respiratory distress. Which of the following actions should be taken FIRST?$x$, $x$Give a bronchodilator by nebulizer.$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Give a bronchodilator by nebulizer.
A respiratory rate of 44/min with ACUTE RESPIRATORY DISTRESS in asthma is a medical emergency. The FIRST action is to GIVE a BRONCHODILATOR (e.g. salbutamol) by NEBULISER (with oxygen) to open the airways. Other actions (monitoring, history, emotional support) follow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Take a full medication history. — A full medication history delays treatment.
• Apply a cardiac monitor to the client. — A cardiac monitor is useful but not the first priority.
• Provide emotional support to the client. — Emotional support is important but doesn't treat the airway obstruction.

⭐ Key point: Also give oxygen (target SpO2 94–98%), ipratropium, and systemic steroids.$x$, null),
(61, $x$A normal PaO2 on arterial blood gas analysis is generally considered to be:$x$, $x$Above 80mmHg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Above 80mmHg
PaO2 is the pressure of oxygen dissolved in ARTERIAL blood, measured on an arterial blood gas. On room air at sea level, the normal PaO2 is about 80–100 mmHg (10.5–13.5 kPa). It falls slightly with age. A PaO2 below 80 mmHg indicates HYPOXAEMIA, and below 60 mmHg (8 kPa) defines RESPIRATORY FAILURE — at this point SpO2 is about 90%, and further small falls in PaO2 cause large falls in oxygen saturation (the steep part of the dissociation curve).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Below 40mmHg — A PaO2 below 40 mmHg is severe, life-threatening hypoxaemia (similar to mixed venous blood).
• Exactly 20mmHg — 20 mmHg is incompatible with life for more than a short time.
• Above 200mmHg always required — A PaO2 above 200 mmHg is only seen with high supplemental oxygen; it is not "required" and very high levels may be harmful (oxygen toxicity).

⭐ Key point: Always note the FiO2 when interpreting PaO2 — use the P/F ratio (PaO2 ÷ FiO2) to judge severity of lung injury.$x$, null),
(62, $x$A patient recovering from thoracotomy has thick, tenacious secretions and a weak cough. The most appropriate nursing intervention is to:$x$, $x$Provide adequate analgesia, encourage deep breathing/coughing (splinting the incision), and consider chest physiotherapy$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Provide adequate analgesia, encourage deep breathing/coughing (splinting the incision), and consider chest physiotherapy
After THORACOTOMY, pain limits deep breathing and coughing, so secretions build up, risking atelectasis and pneumonia. Nursing interventions: ADEQUATE ANALGESIA (so the patient can breathe deeply and cough), DEEP BREATHING and COUGHING exercises with SPLINTING of the incision (pillow support), CHEST PHYSIOTHERAPY, humidified oxygen (loosens secretions), early mobilisation, and incentive spirometry.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Withhold analgesia to encourage a stronger cough — Withholding analgesia prevents effective coughing.
• Discourage any mobilisation — Mobilisation should be encouraged.
• Avoid humidified oxygen — Humidified oxygen helps thin secretions.

⭐ Key point: Epidural or paravertebral analgesia is often used after thoracotomy for good pain control.$x$, null),
(63, $x$What is the appropriate amount of time to suction a patient in a single interval?$x$, $x$No more than 10 – 15 seconds$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: No more than 10 – 15 seconds
Each SUCTION PASS should last NO MORE THAN 10–15 SECONDS. Longer suctioning removes oxygen from the lungs, causing HYPOXIA, bradycardia, arrhythmias, atelectasis and mucosal trauma.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No more than 20 – 30 seconds — 20–30 seconds is too long.
• No more than 10 – 20 seconds — 10–20 seconds could be too long.
• No more than 5 – 10 seconds — 5–10 seconds may be too short to clear secretions effectively.

⭐ Key point: Pre-oxygenate before suctioning, apply suction only while withdrawing, and allow recovery between passes.$x$, null),
(64, $x$Pulse check location in an infant during CPR assessment is the:$x$, $x$Brachial artery$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Brachial artery
In an INFANT (under 1 year), the BRACHIAL ARTERY (on the inside of the upper arm, between elbow and shoulder) is used to check the pulse during CPR. The infant's neck is short and fat, making the carotid hard to feel.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Carotid artery — The carotid artery is used in children over 1 and adults.
• Radial artery — The radial artery is unreliable in shock and in infants.
• Femoral artery — The femoral artery can be used, but the brachial is standard.

⭐ Key point: Take no more than 10 seconds to check for a pulse; start CPR if not sure.$x$, null),
(65, $x$The primary hemodynamic benefit of IABP balloon inflation during diastole is:$x$, $x$Increased coronary artery perfusion (diastolic augmentation)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Increased coronary artery perfusion (diastolic augmentation)
An INTRA-AORTIC BALLOON PUMP (IABP) INFLATES during DIASTOLE (when the aortic valve closes), pushing blood back towards the heart and INCREASING CORONARY ARTERY PERFUSION (diastolic augmentation). It DEFLATES just before systole, reducing afterload and helping the heart eject blood.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increased afterload — IABP DECREASES afterload (on deflation).
• Decreased myocardial oxygen supply — IABP INCREASES myocardial oxygen supply.
• Decreased diastolic pressure — IABP INCREASES diastolic pressure.

⭐ Key point: Timing is set using the ECG (inflate at the T wave/dicrotic notch, deflate before the R wave).$x$, null),
(66, $x$Hypertension is the leading cause of end-stage renal disease after which condition?$x$, $x$Diabetes$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Diabetes
DIABETES MELLITUS is the LEADING cause of END-STAGE RENAL DISEASE (ESRD — kidney failure needing dialysis or transplant) worldwide, accounting for about 40% of cases. HYPERTENSION is the SECOND leading cause. Both damage the small blood vessels and filtering units (glomeruli) of the kidneys over many years. In Nigeria and much of Africa, hypertension, diabetes and chronic glomerulonephritis are major causes of CKD.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Asthma — Asthma does not damage the kidneys.
• Hepatitis — Hepatitis B and C can cause certain kidney diseases (glomerulonephritis), but they are not leading causes of ESRD.
• Osteoarthritis — Osteoarthritis does not cause kidney failure (although frequent NSAID use for pain can).

⭐ Key point: Slow kidney damage by good control of blood glucose (HbA1c) and BP (often with ACE inhibitors or ARBs), and screen for protein in the urine.$x$, null),
(67, $x$What is the primary goal of a ventricular assist device (VAD) in critical care?$x$, $x$To support heart function and circulation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: To support heart function and circulation
A VENTRICULAR ASSIST DEVICE (VAD) is a mechanical pump that SUPPORTS HEART FUNCTION and CIRCULATION when the heart is too weak to pump enough blood. It can be a bridge to recovery, to heart transplant, or long-term therapy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• To monitor blood glucose levels — It doesn't monitor glucose.
• To provide mechanical ventilation — It doesn't provide mechanical ventilation.
• To administer antibiotics — It doesn't administer antibiotics.

⭐ Key point: VAD patients need anticoagulation and careful monitoring for bleeding, clots and infection.$x$, null),
(68, $x$A 37 year old male presented to the ER with chest pain, radiating to the left arm and diaphoresis. Upon history taking patient says that he has this problem for almost 3-4 hours and he has arrived here after a long drive. Assessing the patient's condition, the Emergency room Nurse diagnosis this patient as:$x$, $x$Emergent$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Emergent
CHEST PAIN radiating to the LEFT ARM with SWEATING (diaphoresis) for 3–4 hours suggests an ACUTE MYOCARDIAL INFARCTION (heart attack). This patient needs immediate assessment and treatment — triaged as EMERGENT (life-threatening, needs immediate care). "Time is muscle" — every minute counts.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Highly urgent — "Highly urgent" may underestimate the urgency.
• Urgent — "Urgent" suggests a delay is acceptable — it is not.
• Critically ill — "Critically ill" is a description, not a standard triage category here.

⭐ Key point: Obtain a 12-lead ECG within 10 minutes of arrival, give aspirin, and activate the cardiac team.$x$, null),
(69, $x$A 58-year-old man in the ICU suddenly complains of crushing chest pain radiating to his left arm, with diaphoresis and nausea. The nurse's FIRST priority action is to:$x$, $x$Obtain a 12-lead ECG and vital signs while alerting the physician immediately$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Obtain a 12-lead ECG and vital signs while alerting the physician immediately
CRUSHING CHEST PAIN radiating to the LEFT ARM with SWEATING and NAUSEA suggests an ACUTE CORONARY SYNDROME. The FIRST priority is to obtain a 12-LEAD ECG and VITAL SIGNS while ALERTING THE PHYSICIAN IMMEDIATELY.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Give the patient a glass of water and reassess in 30 minutes — Waiting 30 minutes delays treatment.
• Ask the patient to walk to the bathroom to see if pain resolves — Walking increases heart workload.
• Document the complaint and continue the routine round — Documenting and continuing the round ignores an emergency.

⭐ Key point: Give aspirin (if not contraindicated), oxygen if hypoxic, nitrates for pain (if BP allows), and prepare for reperfusion.$x$, null),
(70, $x$Which of these is not an antihypertensive$x$, $x$None of the above$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: None of the above
All three drugs listed are ANTIHYPERTENSIVES: VALSARTAN — an angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB), which relaxes blood vessels; ATENOLOL — a beta-blocker, which slows the heart and reduces cardiac output; HYDRALAZINE — a direct arterial vasodilator, often used in pregnancy-related hypertension. Because every listed drug IS an antihypertensive, the correct answer is "NONE OF THE ABOVE" (none of them is a non-antihypertensive).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Valsartan — Valsartan IS an antihypertensive (ARB).
• Atenolol — Atenolol IS an antihypertensive (beta-blocker).
• Hydrallazine — Hydralazine IS an antihypertensive (vasodilator).

⭐ Key point: Main antihypertensive classes: ACE inhibitors (e.g. lisinopril), ARBs (e.g. losartan), calcium channel blockers (e.g. amlodipine, nifedipine), thiazide diuretics, beta-blockers, and others (e.g. methyldopa, hydralazine, labetalol).$x$, null),
(71, $x$A recognised adverse effect of inotropic infusions (e.g. dobutamine) is:$x$, $x$Tachyarrhythmias and myocardial ischaemia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Tachyarrhythmias and myocardial ischaemia
INOTROPES such as DOBUTAMINE (and adrenaline, dopamine, milrinone) make the heart beat faster and harder. This INCREASES the heart's OXYGEN DEMAND. Recognised adverse effects: TACHYCARDIA and TACHYARRHYTHMIAS (e.g. atrial fibrillation, ventricular ectopics, VT) and MYOCARDIAL ISCHAEMIA (chest pain, ST changes), especially in patients with coronary artery disease. Dobutamine can also cause hypotension (from β2 vasodilation).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bradycardia — Inotropes usually cause TACHYcardia, not bradycardia.
• Reduced myocardial oxygen demand — Inotropes INCREASE, not reduce, myocardial oxygen demand.
• Hypoglycaemia — Hypoglycaemia is not a typical effect; adrenaline can actually cause HYPERglycaemia.

⭐ Key point: Use the lowest effective dose, monitor ECG continuously, and watch for new arrhythmias or chest pain. Correct potassium and magnesium to reduce arrhythmia risk.$x$, null),
(72, $x$Before delivering cardioversion, the nurse must confirm that:$x$, $x$The machine is sensing (marking) the R wave in sync mode$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The machine is sensing (marking) the R wave in sync mode
SYNCHRONISED CARDIOVERSION is used for unstable tachyarrhythmias with a pulse (e.g. AF, atrial flutter, SVT, VT with a pulse). The shock must be timed to the R WAVE so it does not land on the T wave (the "vulnerable period"), which could trigger ventricular fibrillation. Before shocking, the nurse must confirm that "SYNC" mode is ON and the machine is SENSING (MARKING) each R WAVE on the monitor (usually shown as a dot or arrow above each QRS).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The defibrillator is in unsynchronised mode — Unsynchronised mode is used for VF and pulseless VT, not cardioversion.
• Oxygen is flowing over the chest — Oxygen should be moved AWAY from the chest during shocks (fire risk), not flowing over it.
• The patient is awake and talking — The patient should be SEDATED (cardioversion is painful), not awake and talking.

⭐ Key point: Many defibrillators switch back to unsynchronised mode after each shock — re-select "sync" before every further cardioversion attempt.$x$, null),
(73, $x$A first-degree heart block on ECG is characterised by:$x$, $x$A prolonged PR interval with every P wave still followed by a QRS complex$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A prolonged PR interval with every P wave still followed by a QRS complex
In FIRST-DEGREE HEART BLOCK, electrical impulses travel through the AV node more SLOWLY than normal, so the PR INTERVAL is PROLONGED (more than 0.20 seconds — five small squares). However, EVERY P WAVE is still FOLLOWED by a QRS complex — no beats are dropped. It is usually harmless and often seen with drugs (beta-blockers, digoxin, calcium channel blockers), high vagal tone, or inferior MI.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Complete dissociation between P waves and QRS complexes — Complete dissociation between P waves and QRS complexes is THIRD-DEGREE (complete) heart block.
• Absent P waves entirely — Absent P waves are seen in atrial fibrillation or junctional rhythms.
• A widened QRS with no P waves — A wide QRS with no P waves suggests a ventricular rhythm.

⭐ Key point: Second-degree block = some P waves not followed by QRS (Mobitz I with progressive PR lengthening; Mobitz II with constant PR). Mobitz II and third-degree blocks are dangerous and may need pacing.$x$, null),
(74, $x$An ETCO₂ reading below what value after 20 minutes of CPR may prompt consideration of stopping resuscitation?$x$, $x$<10mmHg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: <10mmHg
END-TIDAL CO2 (ETCO2) during CPR reflects the amount of blood the chest compressions are pushing through the lungs (cardiac output). Good-quality CPR usually produces an ETCO2 of at least 10–20 mmHg. An ETCO2 persistently BELOW 10 mmHg after about 20 MINUTES of good-quality CPR (in an intubated patient) suggests a very low chance of return of spontaneous circulation (ROSC), and may be one factor in the team's decision to stop resuscitation. It should never be used alone.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• <25mmHg — 25 mmHg suggests reasonable blood flow during CPR — not a reason to stop.
• <40mmHg — 40 mmHg is a normal value (often seen after ROSC).
• <60mmHg — 60 mmHg is high.

⭐ Key point: A sudden rise in ETCO2 (e.g. to 35–40 mmHg) during CPR is often the first sign of ROSC. ETCO2 also confirms correct tube placement.$x$, null),
(75, $x$While caring for a client who has sustained an MI, the nurse notes eight PVCs in one minute on the cardiac monitor. The client is receiving an IV infusion of D5W and oxygen at 2 L/minute. The nurse’s first course of action should be to:$x$, $x$Notify the physician promptly.$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Notify the physician promptly.
PREMATURE VENTRICULAR CONTRACTIONS (PVCs) are extra beats from the ventricles. After an MI, FREQUENT PVCs (e.g. more than 6 per minute, multifocal, couplets or "R on T") show that the damaged heart muscle is electrically IRRITABLE, and may be a warning of VENTRICULAR TACHYCARDIA or VENTRICULAR FIBRILLATION. Eight PVCs per minute is significant. The first action is to NOTIFY THE PHYSICIAN PROMPTLY, while assessing the patient (symptoms, BP, oxygen) and checking for causes (low potassium or magnesium, hypoxia, ischaemia).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase the oxygen concentration. — Increasing oxygen may help if hypoxic, but it does not address the possible arrhythmia risk on its own.
• Increase the IV infusion rate — Increasing the IV rate is not indicated and could cause fluid overload.
• Administer a prescribed analgesic. — Analgesia may be needed for pain, but it is not the first priority for frequent PVCs.

⭐ Key point: Have a defibrillator nearby, check electrolytes, and review the 12-lead ECG for ischaemia.$x$, null),
(76, $x$A client is brought to the emergency room complaining of pressure in her chest. Her blood pressure is 150/90, pulse 88, respirations 20. The nurse administers nitro-glycerine 0.4 mg sublingually as ordered. After five minutes her blood pressure is 100/60, pulse 96, respirations 20. Which of the following should the critical care nurse do next?$x$, $x$Document the results, and continue to monitor the client.$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Document the results, and continue to monitor the client.
SUBLINGUAL GTN (nitroglycerin) dilates blood vessels, so a FALL in BP is EXPECTED. Here the BP fell from 150/90 to 100/60 — a systolic of 100 is still ACCEPTABLE (the usual concern is a systolic below 90 mmHg or a fall of more than 30 mmHg causing symptoms). The heart rate rose slightly (reflex). The nurse should DOCUMENT the results and CONTINUE to MONITOR the client (BP, pulse, pain level) and report as needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Notify the physician that the client has become hypotensive, and obtain an order to administer IV fluids. — The client is not significantly hypotensive (systolic 100), so IV fluids are not needed.
• Place the client in semi-Fowler’s position, and administer O2 at 4 L. — Positioning and oxygen may be part of care, but are not the specific next step indicated by these findings.
• Administer a second dose of nitro-glycerine. — A second dose may be considered if chest pain continues, but with BP now 100/60 it should be given cautiously only after reassessing pain and BP.

⭐ Key point: Before each GTN dose, check BP (hold if systolic below 90 mmHg) and ask about sildenafil/tadalafil use.$x$, null),
(77, $x$Which mnemonic is commonly used to remember indications for urgent or emergent dialysis?$x$, $x$AEIOU, standing for acidosis, electrolyte imbalance, ingestion/intoxication, overload, uremia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: AEIOU, standing for acidosis, electrolyte imbalance, ingestion/intoxication, overload, uremia
The AEIOU mnemonic lists indications for URGENT/EMERGENCY DIALYSIS when medical treatment fails: A = ACIDOSIS (severe metabolic acidosis, e.g. pH below 7.1–7.15); E = ELECTROLYTE imbalance (especially refractory HYPERKALAEMIA); I = INTOXICATION/INGESTION (dialysable poisons such as lithium, methanol, ethylene glycol, salicylates); O = OVERLOAD (fluid overload/pulmonary oedema not responding to diuretics); U = URAEMIA (uraemic pericarditis, encephalopathy, bleeding).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• SEPSIS — SEPSIS relates to sepsis recognition.
• MONA — MONA (Morphine, Oxygen, Nitrates, Aspirin) relates to acute coronary syndrome treatment.
• FAST — FAST relates to stroke recognition.

⭐ Key point: Dialysis decisions are based on the patient's clinical state, not a single creatinine value.$x$, null),
(78, $x$Which finding is most suggestive of contrast-induced nephropathy after coronary angiography?$x$, $x$Rise in serum creatinine within 24-72 hours of contrast exposure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Rise in serum creatinine within 24-72 hours of contrast exposure
CONTRAST-INDUCED NEPHROPATHY (contrast-associated AKI) is a decline in kidney function after IV contrast (e.g. coronary angiography or CT). It is diagnosed by a RISE in SERUM CREATININE (e.g. ≥26.5 µmol/L or ≥25% above baseline) within 24–72 HOURS of contrast exposure. It usually peaks at 3–5 days and often recovers within 1–3 weeks. Risk factors: CKD, diabetes, dehydration, heart failure, large contrast volume, and nephrotoxic drugs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperkalemia occurring within minutes of injection — Hyperkalaemia within minutes is not typical.
• Gross hematuria developing after two weeks — Gross haematuria after two weeks is not typical.
• Immediate anuria during the procedure — Immediate anuria during the procedure is not typical.

⭐ Key point: Prevention: identify at-risk patients, ensure adequate IV hydration (e.g. 0.9% saline before and after), use the lowest contrast dose, and hold nephrotoxic drugs (e.g. NSAIDs, metformin in severe CKD).$x$, null),
(79, $x$A patient's urine output is measured at 20ml over the last hour, with a weight of 70kg. This represents a urine output of approximately:$x$, $x$0.3ml/kg/hr, consistent with oliguria requiring assessment$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 0.3ml/kg/hr, consistent with oliguria requiring assessment
URINE OUTPUT in mL/kg/hr = VOLUME ÷ HOURS ÷ WEIGHT. Here: 20 mL ÷ 1 hour ÷ 70 kg = 0.29, which is approximately 0.3 mL/kg/hr. The minimum normal adult urine output is 0.5 mL/kg/hr. Below this is OLIGURIA, which requires assessment (check catheter patency, fluid status, BP, and causes of AKI).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2ml/kg/hr, a normal finding — 2 mL/kg/hr would be 140 mL/hr for this patient.
• 5ml/kg/hr, indicating polyuria — 5 mL/kg/hr would be 350 mL/hr (polyuria).
• No clinical significance regardless of the value — Urine output is always clinically significant in ICU.

⭐ Key point: Oliguria for 6 hours or more meets KDIGO criteria for AKI. Always check that the catheter is not blocked before assuming kidney failure.$x$, null),
(80, $x$A patient with a very dry mouth, skin, and mucous membranes is diagnosed with dehydration. Which intervention should the nurse perform when caring for a patient diagnosed with fluid volume deficit?$x$, $x$Assessing urinary intake and output$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Assessing urinary intake and output
In FLUID VOLUME DEFICIT (dehydration), the nurse should ASSESS URINARY INTAKE and OUTPUT accurately (and other signs: vital signs, skin turgor, mucous membranes, daily weights, urine colour and specific gravity). Intake and output show whether the patient is receiving enough fluid and whether the kidneys are responding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Obtaining the clients weight weekly at different times of the day. — Weight should be measured DAILY, at the SAME time, with the same scale and clothing — not weekly at different times.
• Maintaining I V therapy at the keep vein open rate. — IV fluid at a "keep vein open" rate is too slow to correct dehydration.
• Monitoring arterial blood gas ABG results — ABGs are not the main monitoring tool for simple dehydration.

⭐ Key point: Signs of dehydration: thirst, dry mucous membranes, low urine output, concentrated urine, tachycardia, hypotension, and weight loss.$x$, null),
(81, $x$Acute kidney injury caused by renal artery stenosis reducing blood flow to the kidney is classified as:$x$, $x$Prerenal$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Prerenal
AKI is classified by where the problem is: PRE-RENAL (reduced blood flow TO the kidney), INTRINSIC/RENAL (damage WITHIN the kidney tissue), or POST-RENAL (blockage of urine flow AFTER the kidney). RENAL ARTERY STENOSIS narrows the artery supplying the kidney, REDUCING BLOOD FLOW, so it is classified as PRE-RENAL (it affects perfusion before blood reaches the kidney tissue). ACE inhibitors can precipitate AKI in bilateral renal artery stenosis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Intrinsic (renal) — Intrinsic AKI involves direct damage to the kidney tissue (e.g. ATN, glomerulonephritis).
• Postrenal — Post-renal AKI involves obstruction of the urinary tract.
• Not a cause of AKI — Renal artery stenosis IS a cause of AKI.

⭐ Key point: Pre-renal AKI usually improves quickly if blood flow is restored before the tubules are damaged.$x$, null),
(82, $x$A patient with severe hemorrhage and hypotension develops oliguria and rising creatinine. This mechanism of acute kidney injury is best classified as:$x$, $x$Prerenal$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Prerenal
SEVERE HAEMORRHAGE and HYPOTENSION reduce blood flow to the kidneys. The kidneys respond by retaining sodium and water, producing a small amount of concentrated urine (OLIGURIA), and the creatinine rises. This is PRE-RENAL AKI. If hypoperfusion is prolonged, it can progress to acute tubular necrosis (intrinsic AKI).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nephrotoxic — Nephrotoxic AKI is caused by drugs or toxins damaging the kidney.
• Postrenal — Post-renal AKI is caused by obstruction.
• Intrarenal — Intrarenal (intrinsic) AKI involves direct kidney damage.

⭐ Key point: Treat with prompt fluid and blood resuscitation and control of bleeding.$x$, null),
(83, $x$What is the standard approach to bladder management for an unconscious patient?$x$, $x$Indwelling urinary catheter placement with strict intake and output monitoring$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Indwelling urinary catheter placement with strict intake and output monitoring
An UNCONSCIOUS patient cannot void voluntarily and is at risk of urinary retention, incontinence (skin damage) and inaccurate fluid assessment. The standard approach is INDWELLING URINARY CATHETER placement with STRICT INTAKE AND OUTPUT MONITORING (hourly urine output in ICU). Use aseptic insertion and catheter care, and remove the catheter as soon as it is no longer needed to reduce catheter-associated urinary tract infection (CAUTI).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No catheterization is needed for an unconscious patient — Catheterisation is usually needed for accurate monitoring and to prevent retention.
• Encouraging the patient to void on command — An unconscious patient cannot void on command.
• Fluid restriction — Fluid restriction is not the standard approach.

⭐ Key point: CAUTI bundle: aseptic insertion, closed drainage, bag below bladder level, daily review of need.$x$, null),
(84, $x$Diazepam, as an alternative first-line agent for status epilepticus, can be given via all of the following routes EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Oral tablet during active seizure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Oral tablet during active seizure
DIAZEPAM can be given for status epilepticus via: IV, RECTAL (e.g. rectal tubes), and sometimes buccal/nasal (though buccal/nasal midazolam is more common). It should NOT be given as an ORAL TABLET during an active seizure — the patient cannot swallow safely (choking and aspiration risk) and absorption would be too slow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV — IV is a fast route for diazepam.
• Rectal — Rectal diazepam is used, especially in children and outside hospital.
• Buccal or nasal — Buccal or nasal routes are used (mainly midazolam).

⭐ Key point: Never put anything in the mouth of a fitting patient.$x$, null),
(85, $x$What is the recommended external anatomical reference point used to zero and level an external ventricular drain (EVD)?$x$, $x$Tragus of the ear (level of the foramen of Monro)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Tragus of the ear (level of the foramen of Monro)
An EXTERNAL VENTRICULAR DRAIN (EVD) is levelled and zeroed at the level of the FORAMEN OF MONRO (the opening connecting the lateral and third ventricles). The external landmark is the TRAGUS of the ear (the small cartilage flap in front of the ear canal), or the outer canthus of the eye in some units.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Xiphoid process — The xiphoid process is not used.
• Umbilicus — The umbilicus is not used.
• Sternal angle — The sternal angle is used for JVP assessment, not EVD.

⭐ Key point: Re-level the EVD every time the patient's head position changes; wrong levelling causes over- or under-drainage of CSF.$x$, null),
(86, $x$Meningeal syndrome includes all of the following EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Babinski response$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Babinski response
MENINGEAL SYNDROME (meningism) is caused by irritation of the meninges (e.g. meningitis, subarachnoid haemorrhage). It includes: NECK STIFFNESS, HEADACHE, PHOTOPHOBIA (sensitivity to light), nausea/vomiting, and positive KERNIG'S and BRUDZINSKI'S signs. A BABINSKI RESPONSE (upgoing big toe) is a sign of UPPER MOTOR NEURONE damage (brain or spinal cord), not meningeal irritation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Neck stiffness — Neck stiffness IS part of meningeal syndrome.
• Headache — Headache IS part of meningeal syndrome.
• Photophobia — Photophobia IS part of meningeal syndrome.

⭐ Key point: Kernig's sign: pain on extending the knee with the hip flexed. Brudzinski's sign: neck flexion causes hip and knee flexion.$x$, null),
(87, $x$The nurse is assessing the pupils of an unconscious patient and notes that they are unequal in size. This finding is known as:$x$, $x$Anisocoria$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Anisocoria
ANISOCORIA means UNEQUAL PUPIL SIZES. In an unconscious patient, a new anisocoria (especially a dilated, sluggish pupil) may indicate compression of the third cranial nerve by a mass or raised ICP (e.g. uncal herniation) — a neurosurgical emergency. A small degree of anisocoria (less than 1 mm) can be normal in some people.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Miosis — Miosis means small (constricted) pupils.
• Mydriasis — Mydriasis means large (dilated) pupils.
• Hippus — Hippus is rhythmic pupil constriction and dilation.

⭐ Key point: Report any new pupil inequality immediately in neurological patients.$x$, null),
(88, $x$Which does NOT need ruled out before assessing for brain death?$x$, $x$Fractures$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fractures
Before testing for BRAIN DEATH, REVERSIBLE causes of coma must be EXCLUDED, because they can mimic brain death: ELECTROLYTE and METABOLIC imbalances (e.g. severe hyponatraemia, hypoglycaemia), HYPOTHERMIA (core temperature must be above about 34–36 °C), DRUG INTOXICATION (sedatives, opioids, muscle relaxants), and endocrine disorders. FRACTURES do not affect brainstem function and do not need to be ruled out.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Electrolyte imbalances — Electrolyte imbalances must be ruled out.
• Hypothermia — Hypothermia must be ruled out.
• Drug intoxication — Drug intoxication must be ruled out.

⭐ Key point: Brain death testing is done by two senior doctors and includes brainstem reflexes and an apnoea test.$x$, null),
(89, $x$A Glasgow Coma Scale score in which range classifies a traumatic brain injury as severe?$x$, $x$3-8$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 3-8
TRAUMATIC BRAIN INJURY (TBI) severity is classified using the GLASGOW COMA SCALE (GCS) after resuscitation: MILD = 13–15; MODERATE = 9–12; SEVERE = 3–8. A GCS of 3–8 means the patient is in a coma, usually cannot protect the airway (needs intubation), and needs ICU care, CT scan and often ICP monitoring. Severe TBI carries a high risk of death and long-term disability.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 13-15 — 13–15 is MILD TBI.
• 9-12 — 9–12 is MODERATE TBI.
• 3-5 only — "3–5 only" is too narrow; severe TBI includes GCS 3 to 8.

⭐ Key point: Prevent secondary brain injury: avoid hypoxia (SpO2 above 94%), hypotension (systolic above 100–110 mmHg), hypo/hypercapnia, fever, hypo/hyperglycaemia and seizures.$x$, null),
(90, $x$Which statement about delirium and dementia is TRUE?$x$, $x$Delirium is usually reversible, while dementia is progressive$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Delirium is usually reversible, while dementia is progressive
DELIRIUM is an ACUTE, FLUCTUATING disturbance of attention and awareness, usually caused by a medical condition (infection, drugs, hypoxia, metabolic problems). It is often REVERSIBLE when the cause is treated. DEMENTIA is a CHRONIC, PROGRESSIVE decline in memory and thinking over months to years, and is usually NOT reversible. Patients with dementia are at higher risk of delirium.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Delirium is never reversible — Delirium is often reversible.
• Dementia has an acute onset over hours — Dementia has a gradual, not acute, onset.
• Delirium and dementia are the same condition — They are different conditions.

⭐ Key point: Screen ICU patients for delirium regularly using CAM-ICU or ICDSC.$x$, null),
(91, $x$What is the primary purpose of the Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) in critical care?$x$, $x$C) Evaluating neurological status and consciousness$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: C) Evaluating neurological status and consciousness
The GLASGOW COMA SCALE (GCS) is a standard tool to EVALUATE NEUROLOGICAL STATUS and LEVEL OF CONSCIOUSNESS, using three components: eye opening (1–4), verbal response (1–5), and motor response (1–6). Total 3–15. It helps detect changes over time and guides decisions (e.g. intubation when GCS ≤8).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A) Monitoring blood pressure — It doesn't monitor BP.
• B) Assessing respiratory function — It doesn't assess respiratory function directly.
• D) Administering antibiotics — It isn't related to antibiotics.

⭐ Key point: Always report the individual components (E, V, M) as well as the total.$x$, null),
(92, $x$Skeletal muscles disorder commonly occurs following sepsis, mobility restriction, hyperglycemia and neuromuscular blocking agents is termed$x$, $x$Critical care acquired weakness$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Critical care acquired weakness
ICU-ACQUIRED WEAKNESS (critical care-acquired weakness) is generalised, symmetrical muscle weakness developing during critical illness. It includes CRITICAL ILLNESS POLYNEUROPATHY (nerve damage), CRITICAL ILLNESS MYOPATHY (muscle damage), or both. Risk factors: SEPSIS, multi-organ failure, IMMOBILITY, HYPERGLYCAEMIA, NEUROMUSCULAR BLOCKING AGENTS and steroids. The broad term for the clinical syndrome is ICU-acquired weakness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Critical illness polyneuropathy — Critical illness polyneuropathy is one type (nerve).
• Critical illness myopathy — Critical illness myopathy is one type (muscle).
• Critical illness neuropathy — "Critical illness neuropathy" is not the overall term.

⭐ Key point: Prevent with early mobilisation, glucose control, light sedation and minimising neuromuscular blockers.$x$, null),
(93, $x$What is the priority nursing intervention for airway management in an unconscious patient?$x$, $x$Position the patient laterally (recovery position) or semi-prone with the head of bed elevated to maintain a patent airway$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Position the patient laterally (recovery position) or semi-prone with the head of bed elevated to maintain a patent airway
An UNCONSCIOUS patient may lose airway reflexes; the tongue can fall back and block the airway, and vomit or secretions can be aspirated. Positioning the patient LATERALLY (RECOVERY POSITION) or semi-prone (with the head of bed elevated where appropriate) helps keep the AIRWAY PATENT, allows secretions to drain from the mouth, and reduces aspiration risk.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Position the patient supine and flat — Supine and flat increases airway obstruction and aspiration risk.
• Insert an oral airway only if the gag reflex is present — Oral airways are inserted only if the gag reflex is ABSENT (otherwise they cause gagging and vomiting).
• Elevate the legs above the level of the heart — Elevating the legs doesn't protect the airway.

⭐ Key point: Have suction ready, and consider airway adjuncts or intubation if the airway cannot be maintained (GCS ≤8).$x$, null),
(94, $x$A patient has a seizure with uncontrollable rhythmic jerking movements. This is documented as a:$x$, $x$Clonic seizure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Clonic seizure
A CLONIC SEIZURE involves RHYTHMIC, REPETITIVE JERKING movements of the muscles, caused by repeated cycles of muscle contraction and relaxation. In a generalised TONIC-CLONIC seizure, the patient first stiffens (tonic phase, often with a cry and cyanosis), then has rhythmic jerking (clonic phase), followed by a post-ictal period of drowsiness and confusion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tonic seizure — A TONIC seizure involves sustained STIFFENING of muscles, without rhythmic jerking.
• Absence seizure — An ABSENCE seizure is a brief (seconds) loss of awareness with staring, mostly in children.
• Myoclonic seizure — A MYOCLONIC seizure involves sudden, brief, shock-like single jerks, not continuous rhythmic jerking.

⭐ Key point: During a seizure: time it, protect the head, do not restrain or put anything in the mouth, give oxygen, check glucose, and turn to the recovery position afterwards. Seizures longer than 5 minutes need benzodiazepines.$x$, null),
(95, $x$Which statement about the unconscious patient is NOT correct?$x$, $x$A saturation monitor provides all the information needed to assess respiratory function$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A saturation monitor provides all the information needed to assess respiratory function
In UNCONSCIOUS patients, SpO2 shows only OXYGENATION, NOT VENTILATION. A patient can have normal SpO2 (especially on oxygen) while CO2 is rising dangerously (hypoventilation). Full respiratory assessment requires observing breathing rate, depth and pattern, airway patency, chest movement, and ABGs or capnography. So the statement that SpO2 provides "all the information" is NOT correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory failure can cause unconsciousness — Respiratory failure CAN cause unconsciousness (hypoxia, high CO2).
• Poor conscious levels are often accompanied by altered breathing patterns — Altered breathing patterns DO accompany poor consciousness.
• Airway protection is a nursing priority — Airway protection IS a nursing priority.

⭐ Key point: Use capnography to monitor ventilation in sedated or unconscious patients.$x$, null),
(96, $x$Delirium Risk Factors are as follows EXCEPT$x$, $x$Obesity$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Obesity
DELIRIUM is an acute brain dysfunction with many risk factors. PREDISPOSING factors: older age, PRE-EXISTING DEMENTIA or cognitive impairment, depression, alcohol use, visual/hearing impairment, severe illness. PRECIPITATING factors: INFECTIONS (sepsis, UTI, pneumonia), surgery and BONE FRACTURES (e.g. hip fracture), hypoxia, metabolic disturbances, drugs (benzodiazepines, opioids, anticholinergics), dehydration, urinary retention, constipation, pain, sleep deprivation and immobility. OBESITY is NOT a recognised major risk factor for delirium.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Preexisting dementia — Pre-existing dementia IS one of the strongest risk factors.
• Bone fractures — Bone fractures (especially hip fractures in older people) ARE a risk factor.
• Infections — Infections ARE a common precipitating factor.

⭐ Key point: Prevention bundles (reorientation, sleep promotion, early mobility, hearing aids/glasses, hydration, avoiding deliriogenic drugs) reduce delirium by about a third.$x$, null),
(97, $x$An effective nursing strategy to reduce ICU delirium includes:$x$, $x$Reorientation using a clock, calendar, and family photos$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Reorientation using a clock, calendar, and family photos
REORIENTATION using a CLOCK, CALENDAR, FAMILY PHOTOS, explanations of where the patient is and what is happening, glasses and hearing aids, helps reduce ICU DELIRIUM. Other strategies: consistent caregivers, natural day–night light cycles, reducing noise at night, early mobilisation, and family involvement.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Frequent changes of caregiving staff — Frequent staff changes increase confusion.
• Keeping the environment brightly lit and noisy at all times — Bright, noisy environments disrupt sleep and worsen delirium.
• Withholding explanations to avoid overstimulation — Explanations help orientation and reduce anxiety.

⭐ Key point: The ABCDEF bundle reduces delirium.$x$, null),
(98, $x$A patient with Parkinson’s disease and delirium should avoid:$x$, $x$Haloperidol$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Haloperidol
PARKINSON'S DISEASE is caused by a loss of DOPAMINE-producing cells in the brain. HALOPERIDOL is a typical antipsychotic that strongly BLOCKS DOPAMINE (D2) receptors. In a patient with Parkinson's, it can severely WORSEN rigidity, tremor and immobility, cause falls and swallowing problems, and may trigger neuroleptic malignant syndrome. It should be AVOIDED. If an antipsychotic is absolutely needed for distressing delirium, QUETIAPINE (weak dopamine blockade) is preferred at low doses.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Quetiapine — Quetiapine is relatively safe in Parkinson's disease and may be used if needed.
• Melatonin — Melatonin may help regulate sleep and is safe.
• Dexmedetomidine — Dexmedetomidine (an alpha-2 agonist sedative) does not block dopamine and is safe.

⭐ Key point: Also avoid metoclopramide and prochlorperazine in Parkinson's patients; use domperidone for nausea. Never stop Parkinson's medications abruptly (risk of a parkinsonism-hyperpyrexia syndrome).$x$, null),
(99, $x$What percentage of mechanically ventilated ICU patients may experience delirium?$x$, $x$Up to 80%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Up to 80%
DELIRIUM is very common in ICU, especially in MECHANICALLY VENTILATED patients — studies report it in up to about 60–80%. Contributing factors: severe illness (sepsis), sedative drugs (especially benzodiazepines), sleep deprivation, immobility, hypoxia, metabolic problems, and the ICU environment. Much ICU delirium is the "HYPOACTIVE" type (quiet, withdrawn, drowsy), which is easily missed without routine screening. Delirium is linked to longer ventilation, longer stay, higher mortality and long-term cognitive impairment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Up to 10% — 10% greatly underestimates the problem.
• Up to 30% — 30% underestimates it for ventilated patients.
• 0% — 0% is incorrect — delirium is very common.

⭐ Key point: Screen every shift with CAM-ICU or ICDSC, and use the ABCDEF bundle to prevent and manage delirium.$x$, null),
(100, $x$Post-ICU delirium may lead to:$x$, $x$All of the above$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: All of the above
Surviving critical illness, especially with delirium, can lead to long-term problems known as POST-INTENSIVE CARE SYNDROME (PICS): LONG-TERM COGNITIVE IMPAIRMENT (memory, attention and problem-solving difficulties — sometimes like mild dementia), mental health problems including PTSD (flashbacks and nightmares of ICU experiences), anxiety and depression, and FUNCTIONAL DECLINE (weakness, difficulty with daily activities, inability to return to work). So "ALL OF THE ABOVE" is correct. Family members can also suffer (PICS-Family).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Long-term cognitive impairment — Long-term cognitive impairment is only one effect.
• PTSD — PTSD is only one effect.
• Functional decline — Functional decline is only one effect.

⭐ Key point: ICU diaries, early rehabilitation, family involvement, and ICU follow-up clinics help patients and families recover.$x$, null),
(101, $x$To assess for "E" (equipment failure) in the DOPE mnemonic, the nurse should:$x$, $x$Disconnect the patient from the ventilator and manually ventilate with a bag-valve device connected to 100% oxygen$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Disconnect the patient from the ventilator and manually ventilate with a bag-valve device connected to 100% oxygen
In the DOPE mnemonic, "E" = EQUIPMENT FAILURE (ventilator malfunction, circuit disconnection, oxygen supply failure). To assess, DISCONNECT the patient from the ventilator and MANUALLY VENTILATE with a bag-valve device connected to 100% OXYGEN. If the patient improves, the ventilator or circuit is the problem.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase the ventilator's tidal volume setting — Increasing tidal volume doesn't assess equipment.
• Assume the ETT is dislodged — Assuming the tube is dislodged skips assessment.
• Order a stat chest x-ray before any other action — An X-ray delays urgent action.

⭐ Key point: Always keep a bag-valve-mask and oxygen at every ventilated patient's bedside.$x$, null),
(102, $x$A patient with COPD should be educated to seek urgent medical attention for which warning sign?$x$, $x$A significant increase in breathlessness, a change in sputum colour/volume, or new fever suggesting an exacerbation or infection$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A significant increase in breathlessness, a change in sputum colour/volume, or new fever suggesting an exacerbation or infection
COPD patients should seek urgent help for signs of an EXACERBATION: significantly INCREASED BREATHLESSNESS, CHANGE in SPUTUM colour (yellow/green) or VOLUME, NEW FEVER, chest pain, confusion, or drowsiness. Early treatment (steroids, antibiotics) can prevent hospital admission.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Occasional mild breathlessness identical to their usual baseline — Usual baseline breathlessness doesn't need urgent care.
• Feeling well with no change in symptoms — Feeling well needs no urgent attention.
• A single missed dose of an as-needed bronchodilator with no symptoms — One missed as-needed dose with no symptoms isn't urgent.

⭐ Key point: Provide a written COPD action plan, and ensure inhaler technique and vaccinations are up to date.$x$, null),
(103, $x$In a patient with severe air-trapping from status asthmaticus, which ventilator strategy helps reduce auto-PEEP?$x$, $x$Prolong expiratory time (lower rate, higher inspiratory flow)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Prolong expiratory time (lower rate, higher inspiratory flow)
In STATUS ASTHMATICUS, air trapping (auto-PEEP) occurs because exhalation is slow. PROLONGING EXPIRATORY TIME allows more complete exhalation: LOWER the RATE (more time for each breath) and use a HIGHER INSPIRATORY FLOW (delivers the breath faster, leaving more time to exhale).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Prolong inspiratory time — Prolonging inspiratory time shortens expiration, worsening air trapping.
• Increase PEEP above intrinsic level — Increasing PEEP above intrinsic PEEP can worsen hyperinflation.
• Increase respiratory rate — Increasing the rate shortens expiration.

⭐ Key point: Accept permissive hypercapnia to avoid dangerous air trapping.$x$, null),
(104, $x$Which volatile anaesthetic agent has the highest blood-gas partition coefficient, giving it the slowest onset and offset among modern agents?$x$, $x$Halothane$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Halothane
The BLOOD-GAS PARTITION COEFFICIENT shows how soluble an anaesthetic gas is in blood. A HIGH coefficient means the gas dissolves easily in blood, so it takes LONGER to build up enough concentration to act on the brain (SLOW onset) and to wash out (SLOW offset). HALOTHANE has the HIGHEST coefficient among the options (about 2.4).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Desflurane — Desflurane has the LOWEST (about 0.42) — fastest onset and offset.
• Sevoflurane — Sevoflurane is low (about 0.65) — fast, used for inhalational induction.
• Nitrous oxide — Nitrous oxide is low (about 0.47) — fast.

⭐ Key point: Halothane is rarely used today because of liver toxicity and arrhythmias.$x$, null),
(105, $x$The following are absolute indications for intubation in life-threatening asthma EXCEPT:$x$, $x$Mild wheeze with normal speech$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Mild wheeze with normal speech
In LIFE-THREATENING ASTHMA, intubation is needed when the patient is failing despite maximal treatment. Indications include: CARDIOPULMONARY ARREST, DECREASING LEVEL OF CONSCIOUSNESS (drowsiness, confusion, coma — from high CO2 and low oxygen), SEVERE REFRACTORY HYPOXAEMIA, exhaustion with poor respiratory effort, and a rising PaCO2 with worsening acidosis. A patient with MILD WHEEZE and NORMAL SPEECH has MILD asthma and does NOT need intubation — intubation would expose them to unnecessary serious risks (barotrauma, air trapping, hypotension).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cardiopulmonary arrest — Cardiopulmonary arrest IS an absolute indication.
• Decreasing level of consciousness — Decreasing consciousness IS an indication.
• Severe refractory hypoxaemia — Severe refractory hypoxaemia IS an indication.

⭐ Key point: Intubating an asthmatic is high-risk; it should be done by the most experienced person, with ketamine as a useful induction agent and ventilator settings that allow long expiratory times.$x$, null),
(106, $x$How often should ventilator circuit tubing be routinely changed to reduce infection risk, according to current VAP-prevention guidance?$x$, $x$Only when visibly soiled or malfunctioning, not on a fixed schedule$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Only when visibly soiled or malfunctioning, not on a fixed schedule
Current VAP-prevention guidelines (e.g. CDC, SHEA) recommend that VENTILATOR CIRCUITS should NOT be changed routinely on a fixed schedule. They should be changed ONLY WHEN VISIBLY SOILED (blood, vomit, secretions) or MALFUNCTIONING. Studies showed that routine frequent changes did NOT reduce VAP — in fact, each change opens the circuit, exposing the patient to contamination, loss of PEEP and lung derecruitment, and may increase VAP risk. Frequent changes also increase cost and staff workload.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Every 24 hours routinely — Routine changes every 24 hours are not recommended.
• Every 48 hours routinely — Routine changes every 48 hours are not recommended.
• Every 4 hours routinely — Changes every 4 hours would be excessive and harmful.

⭐ Key point: Drain condensate in the circuit away from the patient (never back towards them), keep HMEs changed per manufacturer's instructions, and minimise disconnections.$x$, null),
(107, $x$Which of the following is an absolute indication for initiating invasive mechanical ventilation?$x$, $x$Apnea with respiratory arrest$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Apnea with respiratory arrest
An ABSOLUTE indication for invasive mechanical ventilation is APNOEA with RESPIRATORY ARREST — the patient is not breathing at all, so they will die within minutes without ventilation. Other strong indications include: inability to protect the airway (GCS ≤8), severe respiratory failure not responding to oxygen or NIV (hypoxaemia, hypercapnia with acidosis, exhaustion), severe shock or cardiac arrest, and the need for deep sedation or paralysis (e.g. raised ICP management).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mild hypoxemia responsive to nasal cannula oxygen — Mild hypoxaemia that responds to nasal cannula oxygen does not need ventilation.
• Chronic stable COPD with SpO2 90% — Chronic stable COPD with SpO2 90% is often the patient's baseline; ventilation is not needed.
• Post-operative sedation for 1 hour — Short post-operative sedation usually resolves without invasive ventilation; observe and support as needed.

⭐ Key point: Invasive ventilation carries risks (VAP, barotrauma, sedation, delirium) — so the indication must be clear, and weaning should start as early as possible.$x$, null),
(108, $x$Which type of ventilator generates a negative pressure around the thorax to draw air into the lungs, historically exemplified by the "iron lung"?$x$, $x$Negative pressure ventilator$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Negative pressure ventilator
A NEGATIVE PRESSURE VENTILATOR works by creating NEGATIVE (sub-atmospheric) PRESSURE around the outside of the chest. This pulls the chest wall outwards, which lowers the pressure inside the lungs and DRAWS AIR IN — similar to normal breathing. The classic example is the "IRON LUNG", a sealed metal tank widely used during POLIO epidemics in the 1940s–1950s, when polio paralysed the breathing muscles. Modern forms include cuirass (chest shell) ventilators.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Positive pressure ventilator — Positive pressure ventilators (used in most ICUs today) PUSH air INTO the lungs through a tube or mask.
• High-frequency oscillatory ventilator — High-frequency oscillatory ventilators deliver very small, very rapid oscillating breaths (a type of positive pressure).
• Pressure-cycled ventilator — Pressure-cycled ventilators deliver breaths until a set pressure is reached (positive pressure).

⭐ Key point: Positive pressure ventilation replaced negative pressure ventilation because it provides better control and access to the patient.$x$, null),
(109, $x$Hypoxia during general anaesthesia refers to:$x$, $x$Inadequate oxygen delivery to tissues$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Inadequate oxygen delivery to tissues
HYPOXIA means INADEQUATE OXYGEN DELIVERY to (or use by) the TISSUES. During general anaesthesia, causes include: airway obstruction or misplaced tube (e.g. oesophageal intubation), hypoventilation, low FiO2 or oxygen supply failure, disconnection, laryngospasm or bronchospasm, aspiration, atelectasis, pneumothorax, and reduced cardiac output. Hypoxia can rapidly cause brain injury, arrhythmias and cardiac arrest. (Strictly, HYPOXAEMIA is low oxygen in the blood; HYPOXIA is low oxygen at the tissue level.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Excess oxygen delivery to tissues — Excess oxygen delivery is HYPEROXIA.
• Elevated body temperature — Elevated body temperature is HYPERTHERMIA.
• Low blood glucose — Low blood glucose is HYPOGLYCAEMIA.

⭐ Key point: Continuous pulse oximetry, capnography and an oxygen analyser are essential safety monitors during anaesthesia.$x$, null),
(110, $x$Which mode combines a set mandatory volume-targeted breath with a decelerating pressure waveform, aiming to guarantee tidal volume while limiting peak pressure?$x$, $x$Pressure-regulated volume control (PRVC)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Pressure-regulated volume control (PRVC)
PRESSURE-REGULATED VOLUME CONTROL (PRVC) is a "dual" mode: the clinician sets a TARGET TIDAL VOLUME, and the ventilator delivers each breath with a DECELERATING (pressure-control) flow pattern, automatically adjusting the inspiratory PRESSURE breath by breath to achieve the target volume at the LOWEST possible pressure. This aims to GUARANTEE TIDAL VOLUME while LIMITING PEAK PRESSURE.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Standard SIMV — Standard SIMV doesn't automatically adjust pressure.
• CPAP — CPAP gives no mandatory breaths.
• High-frequency jet ventilation — High-frequency jet ventilation uses very small, rapid breaths.

⭐ Key point: In PRVC, watch for rising pressures (worsening compliance) and patient effort affecting volumes.$x$, null),
(111, $x$The following are the classical hemolytic transfusion reaction in general anesthesia except:$x$, $x$bradycardia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: bradycardia
A HAEMOLYTIC TRANSFUSION REACTION (usually ABO incompatibility) destroys the transfused red cells. Under GENERAL ANAESTHESIA, the usual early symptoms (fever, chills, back or chest pain, anxiety) are hidden. Classic signs then include: unexplained HYPOTENSION, TACHYCARDIA, HAEMOGLOBINURIA (red or dark urine in the catheter bag), diffuse oozing from the wound and IV sites (DIC), and sometimes WHEEZING/bronchospasm. BRADYCARDIA is NOT a classic sign — the heart usually speeds up (tachycardia) in response to hypotension.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypotension — Hypotension IS a classic sign under anaesthesia.
• Wheezing — Wheezing (bronchospasm) can occur.
• Hemoglobinuria — Haemoglobinuria IS a classic sign.

⭐ Key point: If suspected: stop the transfusion immediately, keep the vein open with saline through a new set, support BP, maintain urine output, check clotting, and return the blood bag to the lab with samples.$x$, null),
(112, $x$A patient in SIMV mode breathes spontaneously between mandatory breaths. What determines the tidal volume of these spontaneous breaths?$x$, $x$The patient's own effort, with support only from pressure support if added$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The patient's own effort, with support only from pressure support if added
In SIMV, the mandatory breaths have the SET tidal volume (or pressure), but the SPONTANEOUS breaths in between depend on the PATIENT'S OWN EFFORT and lung mechanics. If PRESSURE SUPPORT is added, the ventilator helps each spontaneous breath with a set pressure, increasing the volume.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The set tidal volume on the ventilator — The set tidal volume applies only to mandatory breaths.
• The set PEEP level — PEEP doesn't determine tidal volume.
• The inspiratory flow rate alone — Flow rate alone doesn't determine spontaneous volume.

⭐ Key point: Monitor spontaneous tidal volumes in SIMV — small volumes may indicate fatigue.$x$, null),
(113, $x$A patient with well-controlled hypertension and no functional limitation is classified as which ASA physical status?$x$, $x$ASA II$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: ASA II
The ASA PHYSICAL STATUS classification: ASA I — healthy; ASA II — MILD SYSTEMIC DISEASE without functional limitation (e.g. WELL-CONTROLLED HYPERTENSION, controlled diabetes, smoker); ASA III — severe systemic disease with functional limitation; ASA IV — severe disease that is a constant threat to life; ASA V — moribund; ASA VI — brain-dead organ donor.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• ASA I — ASA I is a healthy patient.
• ASA III — ASA III involves functional limitation.
• ASA IV — ASA IV is a constant threat to life.

⭐ Key point: Add "E" for emergency surgery (e.g. ASA IIE).$x$, null),
(114, $x$The Surviving Sepsis 'hour-1 bundle' includes which of the following priority actions?$x$, $x$Obtain blood cultures, measure lactate, give broad-spectrum antibiotics, and begin fluid resuscitation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Obtain blood cultures, measure lactate, give broad-spectrum antibiotics, and begin fluid resuscitation
The SURVIVING SEPSIS "HOUR-1 BUNDLE" includes: measure LACTATE (re-measure if above 2), obtain BLOOD CULTURES before antibiotics, give BROAD-SPECTRUM ANTIBIOTICS, begin FLUID RESUSCITATION (30 mL/kg crystalloid for hypotension or lactate ≥4), and start VASOPRESSORS if hypotensive during or after fluids (to keep MAP ≥65).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Wait for culture results before starting any treatment — Waiting for cultures delays treatment.
• Give oral antibiotics only — Oral antibiotics are not adequate in sepsis.
• Delay fluids until blood pressure normalises spontaneously — Fluids should not be delayed.

⭐ Key point: Each hour of delay in antibiotics in septic shock increases mortality.$x$, null),
(115, $x$A PT (prothrombin time) greater than what value is used as a liver transplant criterion in FHF?$x$, $x$>50 seconds$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: >50 seconds
KING'S COLLEGE CRITERIA identify patients with FULMINANT HEPATIC FAILURE (FHF) who need LIVER TRANSPLANT. For NON-paracetamol causes, transplant is indicated if PT is above 100 seconds (INR >6.5) alone, OR any 3 of: age under 10 or over 40, unfavourable cause, jaundice for more than 7 days before encephalopathy, PT above 50 SECONDS (INR >3.5), or bilirubin above 300 µmol/L. So a PT above 50 seconds is a recognised transplant criterion (as one of the combined criteria).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• >15 seconds — 15 seconds is close to normal.
• >30 seconds — 30 seconds is prolonged but not the cut-off.
• >100 seconds — 100 seconds is the standalone criterion; but the commonly tested cut-off in the combined criteria is 50 seconds.

⭐ Key point: Refer early to a liver transplant centre when coagulopathy, encephalopathy or acidosis worsen.$x$, null),
(116, $x$A disaster drill evaluates:$x$, $x$All of the above$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: All of the above
A DISASTER DRILL tests how well a hospital or unit would cope with a real disaster. It evaluates COMMUNICATION SYSTEMS (alerting staff, call-in lists, phones, radios), RESOURCE ALLOCATION (beds, ventilators, blood, supplies), and STAFF RESPONSE (roles, triage, teamwork). So "ALL OF THE ABOVE" is correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Communication systems — Communication is only one part.
• Resource allocation — Resource allocation is only one part.
• Staff response — Staff response is only one part.

⭐ Key point: After each drill, debrief and update the disaster plan.$x$, null),
(117, $x$An SpO₂ below what value is generally used as an ICU admission threshold?$x$, $x$90%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 90%
An SpO2 BELOW 90% (despite oxygen) generally indicates significant HYPOXAEMIA and is used as a threshold for considering ICU admission or escalation, because it corresponds to a PaO2 of about 60 mmHg — the steep part of the oxygen dissociation curve, where small changes cause rapid drops in oxygen content.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 95% — 95% is normal.
• 93% — 93% is mildly low but usually manageable on the ward.
• 85% — 85% is severe; waiting until then delays care.

⭐ Key point: Consider the trend and the oxygen requirement, not just one value.$x$, null),
(118, $x$Which form of tetanus is the most common overall?$x$, $x$Generalised$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Generalised
GENERALISED TETANUS is the MOST COMMON form (about 80% of cases). It causes trismus (lockjaw), risus sardonicus (fixed smile), neck and back stiffness, opisthotonus (arching of the back), painful muscle spasms, and autonomic instability.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Localised — Localised tetanus affects muscles near the wound only (uncommon).
• Cephalic — Cephalic tetanus affects cranial nerves after head injuries (rare).
• Neonatal — Neonatal tetanus is a form of generalised tetanus in newborns (common in some low-income countries, but not the most common overall).

⭐ Key point: Prevent tetanus through immunisation, including of pregnant women (protects newborns).$x$, null),
(119, $x$Ventilation strategy for a patient with significant inhalational injury favours:$x$, $x$Low tidal volumes with permissive hypercapnia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Low tidal volumes with permissive hypercapnia
Patients with INHALATION INJURY often develop ARDS-like lung damage. The recommended ventilation strategy is LUNG-PROTECTIVE: LOW TIDAL VOLUMES (6 mL/kg predicted body weight), plateau pressure ≤30 cmH2O, adequate PEEP, and PERMISSIVE HYPERCAPNIA (accepting higher CO2 to avoid lung injury).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• High tidal volumes to maximise oxygenation — High tidal volumes cause further lung injury.
• No ventilatory support is needed — Many need ventilatory support.
• Maximum PEEP at all times — Maximum PEEP at all times can cause barotrauma and hypotension.

⭐ Key point: Bronchoscopy may be needed to assess and clear soot and debris from the airway.$x$, null),
(120, $x$Operative management of extraperitoneal bladder rupture may be indicated in:$x$, $x$All of the above$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: All of the above
EXTRAPERITONEAL bladder rupture (urine leaks outside the peritoneum) is usually managed CONSERVATIVELY with catheter drainage. SURGERY is indicated when there is: concomitant RECTAL or VAGINAL injury, a FOREIGN BODY (e.g. bone fragment) in the bladder wall, an EXTENSIVE bladder wall tear, bladder neck injury, or when the patient is having laparotomy for other injuries. So "ALL OF THE ABOVE" is correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Concomitant rectal or vaginal injury — Rectal/vaginal injury is one indication.
• Foreign body in the bladder wall — Foreign body is one indication.
• Extensive bladder wall tear — Extensive tear is one indication.

⭐ Key point: Intraperitoneal bladder rupture always requires surgical repair.$x$, null),
(121, $x$Neurogenic shock is distinguished from other types of shock by:$x$, $x$Bradycardia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Bradycardia
NEUROGENIC SHOCK happens after spinal cord injury above about T6. The injury cuts off the SYMPATHETIC nervous system, so: blood vessels DILATE (low SVR → HYPOTENSION) and the heart is left under unopposed VAGAL (parasympathetic) control, causing BRADYCARDIA. The skin is typically WARM and DRY (because the vessels are dilated). This combination of hypotension WITH bradycardia distinguishes it from most other types of shock (hypovolaemic, septic, cardiogenic), which usually cause TACHYCARDIA.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tachycardia — Tachycardia is typical of OTHER shock types (the body's compensation).
• No change in heart rate — The heart rate does change — it falls.
• Always presenting with fever — Neurogenic shock does not always present with fever; temperature regulation may be impaired, causing hypothermia or hyperthermia.

⭐ Key point: Always exclude bleeding first in trauma patients. Treat with careful fluids, vasopressors (e.g. noradrenaline) and atropine or pacing for symptomatic bradycardia.$x$, null),
(122, $x$The two drugs used together to manage the hypermetabolic response in burns are:$x$, $x$Propranolol and oxandrolone$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Propranolol and oxandrolone
After major BURNS (above about 30–40% TBSA), the body enters a prolonged HYPERMETABOLIC state: very high stress hormones (catecholamines, cortisol), high heart rate, high energy use, MUSCLE WASTING, insulin resistance and poor wound healing, which can last months. Two drugs are used together to reduce this: PROPRANOLOL (a non-selective beta-blocker) — reduces heart rate, cardiac workload, energy expenditure and muscle breakdown; OXANDROLONE (an anabolic steroid) — increases muscle protein synthesis, improves weight gain and wound healing, and shortens hospital stay.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Adrenaline and atropine — Adrenaline and atropine are emergency drugs for cardiac arrest and bradycardia.
• Insulin and glucagon — Insulin may be used to control hyperglycaemia, but glucagon is not used for hypermetabolism.
• Furosemide and spironolactone — Furosemide and spironolactone are diuretics, not used for hypermetabolism.

⭐ Key point: Supportive measures also matter: early enteral nutrition with high protein, early wound excision and grafting, warm environment (28–33 °C), and early mobilisation.$x$, null),
(123, $x$Psammoma bodies on histology are a classic feature found in what percentage of papillary carcinoma cases?$x$, $x$60%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 60%
PSAMMOMA BODIES are small, round, layered (laminated) clumps of CALCIUM seen under the microscope. They form from dead tumour cells that have calcified. They are a classic feature of PAPILLARY THYROID CARCINOMA, found in roughly HALF of cases (commonly quoted as about 40–60%, so 60% is the closest option). They are also seen in other tumours: papillary serous ovarian cancer, meningioma and mesothelioma (memory aid: "PSaMMoma" — Papillary thyroid, Serous ovarian, Meningioma, Mesothelioma).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10% — 10% underestimates how often they are found.
• 25% — 25% underestimates how often they are found.
• 100% — 100% is incorrect — not every papillary carcinoma shows psammoma bodies.

⭐ Key point: Papillary carcinoma is the most common thyroid cancer, often spreads to neck lymph nodes, and has an excellent prognosis with treatment.$x$, null),
(124, $x$Concerning the following investigation in DIC, which is not correct?$x$, $x$Fibrinogen is increased$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fibrinogen is increased
In DISSEMINATED INTRAVASCULAR COAGULATION (DIC), a serious illness (sepsis, trauma, obstetric emergencies, cancer) triggers widespread clotting in small vessels. This CONSUMES clotting factors and platelets, so the patient both clots and BLEEDS. Typical lab results: PROLONGED PT/INR and APTT (clotting factors used up), LOW PLATELET COUNT, LOW CLOTTING FACTORS, LOW FIBRINOGEN (used up), and HIGH D-DIMER/FDPs (clot breakdown). The statement "FIBRINOGEN is INCREASED" is NOT correct — fibrinogen is usually DECREASED (although it can be normal early because it rises in inflammation).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Prothrombin time is prolonged — Prothrombin time IS prolonged in DIC.
• Platelet count is reduced — Platelet count IS reduced.
• Clotting factors are reduced — Clotting factors ARE reduced (consumed).

⭐ Key point: Treat the underlying cause; give platelets, FFP and cryoprecipitate if bleeding or before procedures. Use the ISTH DIC score to help diagnosis.$x$, null),
(125, $x$Anti-thyroid drug treatment is typically continued for how long before a patient may be considered cured?$x$, $x$18 months$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 18 months
In GRAVES' DISEASE (the commonest cause of hyperthyroidism), ANTI-THYROID DRUGS such as CARBIMAZOLE or PROPYLTHIOURACIL are usually given for about 12–18 MONTHS. After this course, the drug is stopped, and about 50% of patients stay in long-term REMISSION (effectively "cured"). If hyperthyroidism comes back, definitive treatment with radioactive iodine or thyroidectomy is usually considered.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2 weeks — 2 weeks is far too short — the drugs take 4–8 weeks just to control thyroid levels.
• 3 months — 3 months is too short for lasting remission.
• 10 years — 10 years is not the standard course (though some patients choose long-term low-dose therapy).

⭐ Key point: Warn patients about AGRANULOCYTOSIS — they must report sore throat, fever or mouth ulcers immediately and have an urgent blood count.$x$, null),
(126, $x$A patient with 40% total body surface area burns is being resuscitated using the Parkland formula. If the patient weighs 70kg, the total fluid requirement for the first 24 hours is calculated as:$x$, $x$4 x 70 x 40 = 11,200ml$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 4 x 70 x 40 = 11,200ml
PARKLAND FORMULA: 4 mL × WEIGHT (kg) × %TBSA = 4 × 70 × 40 = 11,200 mL in 24 hours. Half (5,600 mL) in the first 8 hours from the burn, and half over the next 16 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 4 x 40 = 160ml — 160 mL omits the weight.
• 70 x 40 = 2,800ml only — 2,800 mL omits the 4 mL factor.
• No formula is used for burns — A formula IS used for burns.

⭐ Key point: Adjust fluids according to urine output (0.5–1 mL/kg/hr in adults).$x$, null),
(127, $x$The immediate first step after a needle-stick injury is to:$x$, $x$Wash the area with soap and water and report the exposure per protocol$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Wash the area with soap and water and report the exposure per protocol
After a NEEDLE-STICK injury, immediately WASH the area with SOAP and WATER (encourage gentle bleeding, do not scrub or suck), and REPORT the exposure per protocol (occupational health, risk assessment, testing, and post-exposure prophylaxis if indicated).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ignore it if the source patient appears healthy — Healthy appearance doesn't rule out infection.
• Apply a tourniquet — A tourniquet is not used.
• Wait 24 hours before taking any action — Waiting 24 hours delays PEP (most effective within hours).

⭐ Key point: HIV PEP should be started as soon as possible (ideally within 2 hours, no later than 72 hours).$x$, null),
(128, $x$Which one of the following is included in man-made disaster?$x$, $x$Wars$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Wars
DISASTERS are classified by cause. MAN-MADE (human-caused) disasters result from human actions or failures: WARS and armed conflict, terrorism and bomb blasts, industrial explosions, chemical or radiation leaks, building collapses, major fires, and transport accidents (e.g. tanker explosions, plane crashes). NATURAL disasters are caused by natural forces: earthquakes, floods, tsunamis, cyclones, droughts and epidemics.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Earth quake — An earthquake is a NATURAL disaster (movement of the earth's crust).
• Floods — Floods are usually NATURAL (heavy rainfall, rivers overflowing), though poor drainage and construction can worsen them.
• Tsunami — A tsunami is a NATURAL disaster (giant waves, often caused by undersea earthquakes).

⭐ Key point: Hospitals need major incident plans covering both natural and man-made disasters, including triage, surge capacity and communication systems.$x$, null),
(129, $x$A burns patient's wound care plan includes silver sulfadiazine cream. The nurse understands this agent is used primarily for its:$x$, $x$Broad-spectrum antimicrobial effect, helping prevent burn wound infection$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Broad-spectrum antimicrobial effect, helping prevent burn wound infection
SILVER SULFADIAZINE (SSD) cream combines silver (which kills microbes) with a sulfonamide antibiotic. It has a BROAD-SPECTRUM ANTIMICROBIAL effect against gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria (including Pseudomonas and Staphylococcus aureus) and some fungi (Candida). It is applied to burn wounds to PREVENT and treat BURN WOUND INFECTION, which is a major cause of death in burns patients (burns destroy the skin barrier).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Analgesic properties only — It is not mainly a painkiller (it has a soothing effect but is not an analgesic).
• Ability to promote scar-free healing with no infection benefit — It does not guarantee scar-free healing; it may slightly delay healing.
• Cooling effect only — It is not used mainly for cooling.

⭐ Key point: Side effects: transient leukopenia, skin discoloration; avoid in sulfa allergy, near-term pregnancy and newborns (kernicterus risk). Newer silver dressings reduce the need for daily dressing changes.$x$, null),
(130, $x$Which of the following is a goitrogenic food capable of blocking iodine uptake?$x$, $x$Cassava$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Cassava
GOITROGENS are substances that interfere with thyroid hormone production. CASSAVA contains CYANOGENIC GLYCOSIDES (e.g. linamarin), which the body converts to THIOCYANATE. Thiocyanate BLOCKS the IODINE pump in the thyroid gland, reducing iodine uptake. In areas where iodine intake is low and cassava is a staple food (including parts of Nigeria), this contributes to GOITRE and hypothyroidism. Proper processing (soaking, fermenting, drying, cooking — as in making garri) reduces the toxic content.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Rice — Rice is not goitrogenic.
• Chicken — Chicken is not goitrogenic.
• Eggs — Eggs are not goitrogenic; they contain some iodine.

⭐ Key point: Iodised salt is the main public health measure to prevent iodine-deficiency goitre. Other goitrogens include cabbage, cauliflower and millet.$x$, null),
(131, $x$The anatomical landmark dividing upper from lower GI bleeding is the:$x$, $x$Ligament of Treitz$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Ligament of Treitz
The LIGAMENT OF TREITZ (suspensory ligament of the duodenum) marks the junction between the duodenum and jejunum. Bleeding ABOVE (proximal to) it is UPPER GI bleeding; bleeding BELOW (distal to) it is LOWER GI bleeding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pylorus — The pylorus is between the stomach and duodenum.
• Ileocaecal valve — The ileocaecal valve is between the small and large intestine.
• Duodenal bulb — The duodenal bulb is part of the duodenum.

⭐ Key point: Upper GI bleeding causes haematemesis and melaena; lower GI bleeding usually causes fresh red blood per rectum.$x$, null),
(132, $x$Which of the following statements regarding blunt injury are false?$x$, $x$In chest injuries, damage to abdominal organs is rare.$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: In chest injuries, damage to abdominal organs is rare.
In BLUNT CHEST injury, damage to ABDOMINAL ORGANS (liver, spleen, kidneys) is COMMON, because the lower ribs cover these organs. So the statement "damage to abdominal organs is rare" is FALSE.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The mechanisms are direct or indirect. — Blunt injury can be direct or indirect — true.
• In indirect injury, associated injuries may be present and should be sought. — In indirect injury, associated injuries should be sought — true.
• Overt injury should lead the clinician to look for a covert injury as well. — Overt injury should prompt a search for covert injury — true.

⭐ Key point: Lower rib fractures should raise suspicion of liver (right) or spleen (left) injury.$x$, null),
(133, $x$Treatment for myoglobinuria after electrical injury includes double fluids plus:$x$, $x$Mannitol and sodium bicarbonate$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Mannitol and sodium bicarbonate
High-voltage ELECTRICAL injuries cause extensive deep MUSCLE damage (even when skin burns look small). Damaged muscle releases MYOGLOBIN, which can block the kidney tubules and cause ACUTE KIDNEY INJURY. Treatment: GIVE MORE IV FLUIDS than standard burn formulas (often double), aiming for a URINE OUTPUT of 1–2 mL/kg/hr (about 100 mL/hr in adults) until the urine clears; plus MANNITOL (an osmotic diuretic that flushes the tubules) and SODIUM BICARBONATE (alkalinises the urine, which reduces myoglobin precipitation in the tubules), per local protocol.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Insulin and glucose — Insulin and glucose are used for hyperkalaemia, not specifically for myoglobinuria.
• Furosemide alone — Furosemide alone is not the standard treatment and may worsen dehydration.
• No additional treatment needed — Additional treatment IS needed beyond standard fluids.

⭐ Key point: Also monitor for hyperkalaemia, cardiac arrhythmias (continuous ECG for 24 hours), and compartment syndrome needing fasciotomy.$x$, null),
(134, $x$Escharotomy is indicated for a circumferential full-thickness burn of a limb when it:$x$, $x$Compromises distal circulation, evidenced by diminished pulses or compartment pressure signs$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Compromises distal circulation, evidenced by diminished pulses or compartment pressure signs
An ESCHAROTOMY (cutting through the burned skin) is indicated when a CIRCUMFERENTIAL full-thickness burn of a limb COMPROMISES DISTAL CIRCULATION (diminished or absent pulses, cold, pale limb, increasing pain, rising compartment pressure). The tight eschar acts like a tourniquet as the tissue swells.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Causes only mild discomfort — Mild discomfort alone doesn't require escharotomy.
• Is less than 24 hours old regardless of circulation — Age of the burn alone isn't the indication.
• Only affects superficial skin layers — Superficial burns don't need escharotomy.

⭐ Key point: Circumferential chest burns may need escharotomy to allow breathing.$x$, null),
(135, $x$The classic triad seen in Amniotic Fluid Embolism is:$x$, $x$Hypotension, hypoxia, DIC$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hypotension, hypoxia, DIC
AMNIOTIC FLUID EMBOLISM (AFE) is a rare, catastrophic obstetric emergency, usually during labour, delivery or just after. Amniotic fluid and fetal cells enter the mother's circulation and trigger a massive reaction. The classic TRIAD is: sudden HYPOTENSION/cardiovascular collapse (often cardiac arrest), HYPOXIA (severe breathlessness, cyanosis, respiratory failure), and DIC (severe bleeding from the uterus, wounds and IV sites). Seizures or altered consciousness may also occur.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fever, rash, joint pain — Fever, rash and joint pain suggest infection or rheumatic disease, not AFE.
• Headache, blurred vision, hypertension — Headache, blurred vision and hypertension suggest pre-eclampsia.
• Jaundice, ascites, confusion — Jaundice, ascites and confusion suggest liver failure.

⭐ Key point: Treatment is supportive and urgent: CPR (with perimortem caesarean if needed), oxygen and ventilation, vasopressors and inotropes, and massive transfusion with clotting factors.$x$, null),
(136, $x$The antidote for magnesium sulphate toxicity is:$x$, $x$Calcium gluconate$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Calcium gluconate
MAGNESIUM SULPHATE is used in pre-eclampsia/eclampsia to prevent seizures. In toxicity, high magnesium blocks calcium at nerve endings and in muscles, causing loss of reflexes, respiratory depression and cardiac arrest. The ANTIDOTE is CALCIUM GLUCONATE (10%, 10 mL [1 g] IV over 10 minutes), because calcium directly opposes magnesium's effects at the neuromuscular junction and heart.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Naloxone — Naloxone reverses opioids.
• Vitamin K — Vitamin K reverses warfarin.
• Flumazenil — Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines.

⭐ Key point: Keep calcium gluconate at the bedside whenever magnesium sulphate is running, and check reflexes, respiratory rate and urine output hourly.$x$, null),
(137, $x$Among the 4 Ts of PPH, 'Tissue' refers to:$x$, $x$Retained placental tissue$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Retained placental tissue
The "4 Ts" of POSTPARTUM HAEMORRHAGE: TONE (uterine atony — the uterus fails to contract, the commonest cause), TRAUMA (tears of the perineum, vagina or cervix, uterine rupture or inversion), TISSUE — RETAINED PLACENTAL TISSUE, membranes or blood clots, which prevent the uterus from contracting properly, and THROMBIN (clotting disorders). Retained tissue is managed by manual removal or evacuation in theatre, along with uterotonics and antibiotics.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uterine muscle tone — Uterine muscle tone is "TONE", not tissue.
• Genital tract lacerations — Genital tract lacerations are "TRAUMA".
• Clotting factor deficiency — Clotting factor deficiency is "THROMBIN".

⭐ Key point: Always inspect the placenta and membranes after delivery to check they are complete — missing pieces suggest retained tissue.$x$, null),
(138, $x$ACE inhibitors are contraindicated in pregnancy-related hypertension mainly because they can cause:$x$, $x$Fetal renal toxicity$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fetal renal toxicity
ACE INHIBITORS (e.g. lisinopril, enalapril, captopril) and ARBs are CONTRAINDICATED in pregnancy. They interfere with the fetal renin-angiotensin system, which the fetal kidneys need to develop and function. Effects (especially in the second and third trimesters): FETAL RENAL TOXICITY (kidney failure), OLIGOHYDRAMNIOS (low amniotic fluid, because fetal urine forms the fluid), fetal growth restriction, skull ossification defects, limb contractures, lung hypoplasia, and fetal or neonatal death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Maternal hair loss — Maternal hair loss is not the concern.
• Improved placental blood flow — ACE inhibitors do not improve placental blood flow.
• No significant risk — There IS significant risk to the fetus.

⭐ Key point: Safe antihypertensives in pregnancy: LABETALOL, NIFEDIPINE, METHYLDOPA and HYDRALAZINE. Women of childbearing age on ACE inhibitors should be advised to switch before or as soon as pregnancy is confirmed.$x$, null),
(139, $x$Which may be life-saving in managing severe pre-eclampsia?$x$, $x$Delivery, even at a very early gestational age$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Delivery, even at a very early gestational age
The DEFINITIVE treatment of PRE-ECLAMPSIA is DELIVERY of the baby and placenta. In severe pre-eclampsia, delivery may be life-saving for the mother, even at a very early gestational age.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Paracetamol for frontal headache in moderate disease — Paracetamol for headache doesn't treat severe disease.
• Outpatient care when hyperreflexia appears — Hyperreflexia is a warning sign; outpatient care is unsafe.
• Conservative management of imminent eclampsia at 29 weeks — Imminent eclampsia requires stabilisation and delivery, not conservative management.

⭐ Key point: Give magnesium sulphate, control BP, and give steroids if preterm.$x$, null),
(140, $x$Which of the following gestational hormones is hypothesized to be correlated with hyperemesis gravidarum?$x$, $x$Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG
HYPEREMESIS GRAVIDARUM is severe vomiting in pregnancy. It is strongly linked to HUMAN CHORIONIC GONADOTROPIN (hCG): hCG levels peak at about 9–12 weeks, the same time symptoms are worst, and conditions with very high hCG — MULTIPLE PREGNANCY (twins) and MOLAR PREGNANCY — are associated with more severe vomiting. hCG may stimulate the vomiting centre and also the thyroid (causing transient hyperthyroidism). Recent research also links a hormone called GDF15.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Estrogen — Oestrogen may contribute, but hCG is the most strongly linked hormone.
• Testosterone — Testosterone is not linked.
• Human placental lactogen (HPL) — Human placental lactogen (HPL) is linked to insulin resistance, not hyperemesis.

⭐ Key point: Manage with IV fluids, electrolyte replacement, anti-emetics, thiamine, and an ultrasound to check for twins or molar pregnancy.$x$, null),
(141, $x$A patient with severe pre-eclampsia is started on magnesium sulfate. Before each dose/during the infusion, the nurse must assess:$x$, $x$Deep tendon reflexes, respiratory rate, and urine output ('TRU')$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Deep tendon reflexes, respiratory rate, and urine output ('TRU')
MAGNESIUM SULPHATE prevents eclamptic seizures, but excess magnesium depresses nerves and muscles. Before each dose and hourly during the infusion, the nurse must check "TRU": T = TENDON REFLEXES (deep tendon/patellar reflexes — LOSS of reflexes is the FIRST sign of toxicity); R = RESPIRATORY RATE (stop if below 12/min — respiratory depression); U = URINE OUTPUT (magnesium is excreted by the kidneys, so output below 30 mL/hr allows it to build up). Also monitor SpO2, BP, consciousness and serum magnesium levels if available.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only blood glucose — Blood glucose is not the main safety check for magnesium.
• Only skin colour — Skin colour alone does not detect magnesium toxicity.
• Only pain score — Pain score is not a magnesium safety check.

⭐ Key point: If toxicity is suspected: stop the infusion, call for help, support breathing, and give the antidote — 10% calcium gluconate 10 mL IV over 10 minutes.$x$, null),
(142, $x$Primary difference between morning sickness and hyperemesis gravidarum?$x$, $x$Hyperemesis typically requires medical treatment$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hyperemesis typically requires medical treatment
MORNING SICKNESS (nausea and vomiting of pregnancy) is common (up to 80% of pregnancies), usually mild, and settles by about 12–16 weeks; women can usually keep some food and fluids down. HYPEREMESIS GRAVIDARUM is a SEVERE form (about 1–2% of pregnancies): persistent vomiting causing DEHYDRATION, WEIGHT LOSS of more than 5% of pre-pregnancy weight, ketosis and electrolyte imbalance. It typically REQUIRES MEDICAL TREATMENT — IV fluids, electrolyte replacement, anti-emetics, thiamine, and often hospital admission.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperemesis resolves after 1st trimester — Hyperemesis can continue beyond the first trimester, sometimes throughout pregnancy.
• Morning sickness is more severe — Morning sickness is LESS severe than hyperemesis.
• Morning sickness occurs only in the evening — Morning sickness can occur at ANY time of day, despite its name.

⭐ Key point: Give thiamine before or with IV dextrose to prevent Wernicke's encephalopathy, and consider thromboprophylaxis in admitted women.$x$, null),
(143, $x$The '4 Ts' framework for causes of postpartum haemorrhage includes tone, tissue, trauma, and:$x$, $x$Thrombin (coagulopathy)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Thrombin (coagulopathy)
The "4 Ts" are the four causes of POSTPARTUM HAEMORRHAGE (PPH): TONE — poor uterine contraction (atony), the commonest cause (~70%); TRAUMA — tears of the perineum, vagina or cervix, uterine rupture or inversion; TISSUE — retained placenta, membranes or clots; THROMBIN — clotting problems (coagulopathy, e.g. DIC from abruption, sepsis, HELLP, amniotic fluid embolism, or anticoagulants). This framework helps the team find and treat the cause quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Temperature — Temperature is not one of the 4 Ts.
• Tachycardia — Tachycardia is a sign of blood loss, not a cause.
• Transfusion — Transfusion is a treatment, not a cause.

⭐ Key point: Initial PPH management: call for help, rub up the uterus, give oxytocin, large-bore IV access, send blood for crossmatch and clotting, check the placenta, look for tears, and give tranexamic acid within 3 hours.$x$, null),
(144, $x$Preeclampsia is generally diagnosed by new-onset hypertension after 20 weeks gestation together with:$x$, $x$Proteinuria or other signs of end-organ dysfunction$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Proteinuria or other signs of end-organ dysfunction
PRE-ECLAMPSIA is defined as NEW-ONSET HYPERTENSION (BP ≥140/90 mmHg) after 20 WEEKS of pregnancy, together with PROTEINURIA (e.g. protein:creatinine ratio ≥30 mg/mmol) OR other signs of END-ORGAN DYSFUNCTION — such as kidney impairment, liver involvement (raised transaminases, right upper quadrant pain), neurological signs (severe headache, visual disturbance, hyperreflexia, clonus), low platelets/haemolysis (HELLP), pulmonary oedema, or placental dysfunction (fetal growth restriction).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Isolated mild headache only — A mild headache alone does not define pre-eclampsia.
• Normal blood pressure readings — Pre-eclampsia requires HIGH BP, not normal readings.
• Glucose intolerance alone — Glucose intolerance alone indicates gestational diabetes.

⭐ Key point: Severe pre-eclampsia: BP ≥160/110 with symptoms or lab abnormalities — needs magnesium sulphate, BP control and planning for delivery.$x$, null),
(145, $x$When investigating a newborn with hyperbilirubinaemia, which bilirubin fractions should be measured?$x$, $x$Total and conjugated bilirubin$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Total and conjugated bilirubin
In a newborn with jaundice, measuring both TOTAL and CONJUGATED (direct) BILIRUBIN is important. Most neonatal jaundice is UNCONJUGATED (e.g. physiological, breast milk, haemolysis). But a raised CONJUGATED bilirubin (more than about 25 µmol/L or more than 20% of total) is ALWAYS ABNORMAL and suggests liver disease or BILIARY ATRESIA, which needs urgent surgery (ideally before 6–8 weeks of age). Unconjugated bilirubin can be calculated as total minus conjugated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Conjugated bilirubin only — Conjugated bilirubin alone does not show the total level needed to decide phototherapy.
• Total bilirubin only — Total bilirubin alone can miss conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia.
• Total and unconjugated bilirubin — Unconjugated bilirubin is usually calculated, and does not identify cholestasis.

⭐ Key point: Pale stools and dark urine with prolonged jaundice (beyond 14 days in term babies) need urgent investigation for biliary atresia.$x$, null),
(146, $x$A newborn is born with poor muscle tone and is not breathing. The nurse's first action, after ensuring warmth and position, should be:$x$, $x$To provide positive-pressure ventilation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: To provide positive-pressure ventilation
Most newborns who need resuscitation have a BREATHING problem. If the baby has POOR TONE and is NOT BREATHING after drying, warming and opening the airway (head in neutral position), the next step is POSITIVE-PRESSURE VENTILATION (PPV) — five inflation breaths with a bag-valve-mask or T-piece. Effective lung inflation usually raises the heart rate quickly. Assess chest movement and heart rate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• To administer chest compressions — Chest compressions are started only if the heart rate stays below 60/min despite 30 seconds of effective ventilation.
• To intubate the newborn — Intubation may be needed later, but mask ventilation is the first step.
• To administer epinephrine — Adrenaline is given only if the heart rate stays below 60/min despite effective ventilation and compressions.

⭐ Key point: "Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Drugs" — most newborns respond to effective ventilation alone.$x$, null),
(147, $x$What is the recommended maximum suction pressure for children?$x$, $x$120 mmHg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 120 mmHg
SUCTION PRESSURE must be high enough to clear secretions but low enough to avoid damaging the delicate airway lining (bleeding, oedema) and causing hypoxia or atelectasis. For CHILDREN, the recommended maximum is about 80–120 mmHg (here, 120 mmHg is the maximum given). For adults, about 100–150 mmHg; for neonates, about 60–100 mmHg.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 130 mmHg — 130 mmHg is above the recommended paediatric maximum.
• 140 mmHg — 140 mmHg is too high for children.
• 150 mmHg — 150 mmHg is the upper adult limit — too high for children.

⭐ Key point: Use the smallest effective pressure and catheter size, pre-oxygenate, limit each pass to 5–10 seconds in children, and apply suction only while withdrawing the catheter.$x$, null),
(148, $x$Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation (CPR)$x$, $x$CPR ratio for the child and infant will be 15 compressions to 2 breaths.$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: CPR ratio for the child and infant will be 15 compressions to 2 breaths.
CPR compression-to-ventilation ratios: ADULTS — 30:2 (one or two rescuers). CHILDREN and INFANTS — 30:2 with one rescuer, but 15:2 with TWO HEALTH-CARE RESCUERS (because children's arrests are usually caused by breathing problems, so they need more ventilation). NEWBORNS — 3:1. So the statement "CPR ratio for the child and infant will be 15 compressions to 2 breaths" (for two rescuers) is correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CPR for the infant victim will be 30 compressions to 2 breaths. — Infant CPR by two health-care rescuers is 15:2, not 30:2.
• CPR ratio for the adult will be 15 compressions to 2 breaths. — The adult ratio is 30:2, not 15:2.
• CPR for the neonate victim will be 30 compressions to 2 breaths. — Newborn CPR is 3:1, not 30:2.

⭐ Key point: Infant compressions: two-thumb technique (two rescuers) or two fingers (one rescuer), at least one-third of chest depth (about 4 cm), rate 100–120/min.$x$, null),
(149, $x$Purpose of erythromycin ointment in neonates?$x$, $x$Ophthalmia neonatorum$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Ophthalmia neonatorum
ERYTHROMYCIN EYE OINTMENT is applied to newborns' eyes shortly after birth to PREVENT OPHTHALMIA NEONATORUM — a serious eye infection (conjunctivitis) caught from the mother's birth canal, mainly due to Neisseria gonorrhoeae (and Chlamydia). Untreated gonococcal ophthalmia can rapidly damage the cornea and cause BLINDNESS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Strabismus — Strabismus (squint) is a muscle alignment problem, not an infection.
• Cataracts — Cataracts are clouding of the lens.
• Diabetes retinopathy — Diabetic retinopathy affects adults with diabetes, not newborns.

⭐ Key point: Tetracycline ointment or povidone-iodine drops are alternatives. Treat mothers with STIs during pregnancy.$x$, null),
(150, $x$Severe neonatal jaundice is primarily concerning because untreated high bilirubin levels can cause:$x$, $x$Kernicterus (bilirubin-induced brain damage)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Kernicterus (bilirubin-induced brain damage)
SEVERE NEONATAL JAUNDICE (very high UNCONJUGATED bilirubin) is dangerous because bilirubin can cross the immature blood–brain barrier and damage the brain — KERNICTERUS (bilirubin encephalopathy), especially in the basal ganglia. Early signs: lethargy, poor feeding, high-pitched cry, hypotonia; later: arching (opisthotonus), seizures. Survivors may have athetoid cerebral palsy, deafness and learning problems.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No lasting harm regardless of level — High bilirubin can cause permanent harm.
• Improved neurological development — Bilirubin does not improve development.
• Only mild, temporary skin discolouration with no other risk — Severe jaundice is not just a skin colour change.

⭐ Key point: Use bilirubin charts to guide phototherapy and exchange transfusion. Risk is higher in preterm babies and with haemolysis (e.g. G6PD deficiency, ABO/Rh incompatibility).$x$, null),
(151, $x$Intellectual standards such as clarity, accuracy, precision, relevance, and logic are used in critical thinking primarily to:$x$, $x$Assess and improve the quality of one's own reasoning$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Assess and improve the quality of one's own reasoning
INTELLECTUAL STANDARDS (Paul and Elder) — CLARITY, ACCURACY, PRECISION, RELEVANCE, DEPTH, BREADTH, LOGIC, SIGNIFICANCE and FAIRNESS — are criteria nurses use to CHECK and IMPROVE the QUALITY of their own THINKING and REASONING. For example: "Is my information accurate? Is it relevant to this patient's problem? Is my conclusion logical?" This helps nurses make sound clinical judgements and avoid errors.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Serve as hospital policy documents — They are not hospital policy documents.
• Replace the need for clinical experience — They do not replace clinical experience; they help nurses learn from it.
• Determine nurse staffing ratios — They are not used to determine staffing ratios.

⭐ Key point: Critical thinking combines knowledge, experience, intellectual standards and reflection to make safe decisions.$x$, null),
(152, $x$A client who was previously the primary breadwinner expresses distress about being unable to return to work after a spinal cord injury and struggles with the loss of that role within the family. This is best classified under which Roy adaptive mode?$x$, $x$Role function mode$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Role function mode
In ROY'S ADAPTATION MODEL, the ROLE FUNCTION MODE relates to the ROLES a person plays in society (e.g. parent, spouse, worker, breadwinner) and how well they perform them. A client who was the PRIMARY BREADWINNER and is distressed about being unable to return to work and losing that ROLE in the family has a problem in the ROLE FUNCTION mode.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Physiological-physical mode — The physiological mode covers basic body needs (oxygen, nutrition, elimination, activity).
• Self-concept/group identity mode — The self-concept mode covers beliefs and feelings about oneself (body image, self-esteem).
• Interdependence mode — The interdependence mode covers relationships, love and support.

⭐ Key point: The nurse helps the client adapt — e.g. exploring new roles, vocational rehabilitation, and family support.$x$, null),
(153, $x$In what year did Yura and Walsh formally describe the nursing process as consisting of four steps?$x$, $x$1967$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 1967
In 1967, YURA and WALSH published a book describing the NURSING PROCESS as having FOUR STEPS: ASSESSING, PLANNING, IMPLEMENTING and EVALUATING. Later (after NANDA was formed in 1973), "DIAGNOSIS" was added as a separate step, giving the five-step process (ADPIE).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1955 — 1955 is when Lydia Hall first used the term "nursing process".
• 1961 — 1961 is when Ida Jean Orlando described the deliberative nursing process.
• 1973 — 1973 is when the first national conference on nursing diagnosis (later NANDA) was held.

⭐ Key point: The nursing process is systematic, client-centred, goal-oriented and cyclical.$x$, null),
(154, $x$A core principle of modern risk management and incident reporting culture is to:$x$, $x$Foster a blame-free, just culture that encourages open reporting of errors and near misses to enable learning and system improvement$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Foster a blame-free, just culture that encourages open reporting of errors and near misses to enable learning and system improvement
Modern RISK MANAGEMENT uses a BLAME-FREE / JUST CULTURE that ENCOURAGES staff to openly REPORT errors and near misses without fear of unfair punishment. Reports are analysed to find SYSTEM problems (e.g. look-alike drugs, staffing, equipment design) so changes can prevent future harm. A just culture still holds people accountable for reckless behaviour.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Punish every staff member who reports an incident — Punishing every reporter discourages reporting.
• Discourage any reporting to keep statistics low — Discouraging reporting hides risks.
• Hold only the most junior staff accountable for systemic errors — Blaming junior staff ignores system causes.

⭐ Key point: Most errors result from system weaknesses, not careless individuals.$x$, null),
(155, $x$Formative evaluation is best described as:$x$, $x$Ongoing judgment of the intervention as it is being implemented$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Ongoing judgment of the intervention as it is being implemented
FORMATIVE EVALUATION is the ONGOING assessment of an intervention or care plan WHILE it is being IMPLEMENTED. It allows the nurse to make changes as care progresses (e.g. adjusting pain relief based on hourly pain scores).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A one-time review done only at discharge — A one-time review at discharge is SUMMATIVE evaluation.
• Only relevant to research studies — Formative evaluation is used in clinical practice, education and research.
• The same thing as summative evaluation — Summative evaluation is a final judgement at the end.

⭐ Key point: Formative = during (to improve); summative = at the end (to judge the outcome).$x$, null),
(156, $x$At which step of the nursing process is the PES statement written?$x$, $x$Diagnosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Diagnosis
The PES statement is the standard format for an ACTUAL NURSING DIAGNOSIS: P = PROBLEM (the NANDA diagnostic label, e.g. "Impaired gas exchange"), E = ETIOLOGY (related factors — "related to…"), S = SIGNS and SYMPTOMS (defining characteristics — "as evidenced by…"). It is written during the DIAGNOSIS step of the nursing process, after the nurse has collected and analysed assessment data. Example: "Acute pain related to surgical incision as evidenced by pain score 8/10, grimacing and guarding."

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Planning — Planning comes after diagnosis: setting goals and choosing interventions.
• Implementation — Implementation is carrying out the planned interventions.
• Assessment — Assessment is collecting data — it comes before diagnosis.

⭐ Key point: Risk diagnoses use a two-part format (problem + risk factors), because there are no signs or symptoms yet.$x$, null),
(157, $x$A nurse develops a care plan with the goal 'Patient will ambulate 50 metres independently by discharge.' At reassessment, the patient can walk 30 metres with assistance. This goal should be documented as:$x$, $x$Partially met, requiring plan revision$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Partially met, requiring plan revision
The goal was "walk 50 metres INDEPENDENTLY by discharge". The patient now walks 30 METRES WITH ASSISTANCE — some progress has been made, but the goal is not fully achieved (both the distance and independence are not met). This should be documented as PARTIALLY MET, and the PLAN REVISED (e.g. more physiotherapy, extended time frame, walking aids).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully met — The goal is not fully met.
• Not applicable — The goal is still applicable.
• Completely unmet with no progress — There has been progress, so it is not completely unmet.

⭐ Key point: Evaluation compares outcomes with goals and leads to continuing, modifying or ending the plan.$x$, null),
(158, $x$Which of the following best describes the concept of "never events" in quality assurance?$x$, $x$Adverse events that should never happen in healthcare settings$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Adverse events that should never happen in healthcare settings
"NEVER EVENTS" are serious, largely PREVENTABLE patient safety incidents that should NEVER happen if proper safety measures are in place. Examples: wrong-site surgery, retained surgical instruments, wrong-route drug administration (e.g. IV drug given intrathecally), and misplaced nasogastric tubes not detected before use.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Routine occurrences in healthcare settings — They are not routine occurrences.
• Errors that are insignificant and do not impact patient care — They are serious, not insignificant.
• Events that occur frequently but are easily preventable — They should be rare, not frequent.

⭐ Key point: Never events must be reported and investigated.$x$, null),
(159, $x$Nursing diagnoses differ from medical diagnoses mainly in that they are:$x$, $x$Concerned with human responses while medical diagnoses are concerned with pathology$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Concerned with human responses while medical diagnoses are concerned with pathology
NURSING DIAGNOSES describe the patient's HUMAN RESPONSES to health problems or life processes (e.g. "Acute pain", "Impaired mobility", "Anxiety"). MEDICAL DIAGNOSES describe the DISEASE or PATHOLOGY (e.g. "Pneumonia", "Myocardial infarction"). Nursing diagnoses can change as the patient's responses change, while medical diagnoses usually remain until the disease resolves.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dependent on the medical diagnosis for direction — Nursing diagnoses are independent of medical diagnoses.
• Concerned with caring while medical diagnoses are concerned with curing — Both disciplines care and cure; this is not the main distinction.
• Concerned with psychosocial parameters only — Nursing diagnoses cover physical, psychological and social responses.

⭐ Key point: One medical diagnosis (e.g. stroke) can lead to many nursing diagnoses.$x$, null),
(160, $x$The primary purpose of incident (adverse event) reporting in healthcare is to:$x$, $x$Identify system issues and improve patient safety through learning$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Identify system issues and improve patient safety through learning
The primary purpose of INCIDENT REPORTING is to IDENTIFY SYSTEM ISSUES and IMPROVE PATIENT SAFETY through LEARNING. Reports are analysed for trends and root causes, leading to changes (e.g. new protocols, equipment, training).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Assign blame and discipline individual staff — It is not mainly to assign blame.
• Satisfy insurance requirements only — It is not only for insurance.
• Reduce documentation workload — It increases, not reduces, documentation.

⭐ Key point: Report all incidents and near misses promptly and factually.$x$, null),
(161, $x$A nurse using the health-illness continuum framework would assess a client holistically by including which of the following, in addition to physical signs and symptoms?$x$, $x$The client's emotional, social, spiritual, and functional status$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The client's emotional, social, spiritual, and functional status
The HEALTH-ILLNESS CONTINUUM sees health as a dynamic state ranging from high-level wellness to severe illness. HOLISTIC assessment includes the client's PHYSICAL, EMOTIONAL, SOCIAL, SPIRITUAL and FUNCTIONAL STATUS — all of which affect health and recovery.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only the client's vital signs and laboratory values — Vital signs and lab values are only part of the picture.
• Only the admitting medical diagnosis — The medical diagnosis alone is incomplete.
• Only the client's insurance status — Insurance status alone is irrelevant to holistic assessment.

⭐ Key point: Holistic care treats the whole person, not just the disease.$x$, null),
(162, $x$One of the stated advantages of the nursing process is that it:$x$, $x$Promotes individualised care and evidence-based decisions$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Promotes individualised care and evidence-based decisions
The NURSING PROCESS (Assessment, Diagnosis, Planning, Implementation, Evaluation) is a systematic, problem-solving approach. Its advantages include: it PROMOTES INDIVIDUALISED CARE (care is based on each patient's own assessment and needs, not routine), and EVIDENCE-BASED DECISIONS (choices are based on data and research). It also improves continuity of care, communication between staff, documentation, patient involvement, and professional accountability.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Promotes decisions based on routine, unquestioned habits — The nursing process discourages routine, unquestioned habits — it requires thinking and justification.
• Removes the need for documentation — It requires documentation at each step.
• Eliminates the need for reassessment — It is cyclical and requires ongoing reassessment.

⭐ Key point: Evaluation feeds back into assessment, so the plan changes as the patient's condition changes.$x$, null),
(163, $x$Which scale of measurement has a true (absolute) zero point, allowing statements such as "twice as much"?$x$, $x$Ratio$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Ratio
The RATIO SCALE has ordered values, EQUAL INTERVALS and a TRUE (ABSOLUTE) ZERO (zero means none of the quantity). This allows statements like "twice as much". Examples: weight, height, age, pulse rate, urine output, temperature in Kelvin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nominal — Nominal data are categories with no order (e.g. blood group).
• Ordinal — Ordinal data have order but unequal intervals (e.g. pain mild/moderate/severe).
• Interval — Interval data have equal intervals but no true zero (e.g. temperature in °C).

⭐ Key point: Ratio and interval data allow means and standard deviations to be calculated.$x$, null),
(164, $x$The main purpose of including a theoretical or conceptual framework in a research study is to$x$, $x$Provide a logical structure that explains the relationships among the study's key concepts and guides interpretation of findings$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Provide a logical structure that explains the relationships among the study's key concepts and guides interpretation of findings
A THEORETICAL or CONCEPTUAL FRAMEWORK provides a LOGICAL STRUCTURE explaining the RELATIONSHIPS among the study's key CONCEPTS. It guides the research questions, design, choice of variables and instruments, and helps INTERPRET the FINDINGS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase the length of the proposal without adding any value — It adds value; it is not just length.
• Replace the need for a literature review entirely — It does not replace the literature review.
• Serve only as a decorative diagram with no functional role — It has a functional role.

⭐ Key point: Many nursing studies use nursing theories (e.g. Orem, Roy) as frameworks.$x$, null),
(165, $x$Which is a characteristic of quantitative research?$x$, $x$A large sample is emphasised$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A large sample is emphasised
QUANTITATIVE RESEARCH measures variables with NUMBERS and uses statistics to describe, test relationships, or establish cause and effect. It usually emphasises a LARGE SAMPLE (often randomly selected), because larger samples are more representative of the population, reduce random error, and give enough STATISTICAL POWER to detect real differences. This allows findings to be GENERALISED to the wider population.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• There are multiple subjective realities — "Multiple subjective realities" is a QUALITATIVE assumption (each person's reality is valid).
• Data are reported in a literary style — Reporting in a literary, narrative style is typical of QUALITATIVE research.
• No generalisation is made from the findings — Quantitative research DOES aim to generalise findings.

⭐ Key point: Sample size for quantitative studies is often calculated in advance using a power analysis.$x$, null),
(166, $x$In a bibliography, the correct group order for arranging sources is:$x$, $x$Journals → Books → Magazines/Newspapers → Other publications$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Journals → Books → Magazines/Newspapers → Other publications
In some referencing guidelines used in nursing schools, sources in a bibliography are grouped by TYPE, often in the order: JOURNALS → BOOKS → MAGAZINES/NEWSPAPERS → OTHER PUBLICATIONS, and arranged alphabetically within each group. (Note: many modern styles, such as APA, list all references together alphabetically — always follow the required style.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Books → Journals → Other publications → Magazines/Newspapers — The order Books → Journals → Other → Magazines is not the stated convention.
• Random order within each letter of the alphabet — Random order is never correct.
• Newest to oldest publication date — Newest to oldest is not standard.

⭐ Key point: Follow the referencing style required by your institution consistently.$x$, null),
(167, $x$The population to be sampled is divided into units which are known as:$x$, $x$Sampling units.$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Sampling units.
Before sampling, the population is divided into SAMPLING UNITS — the individual elements or groups that can be selected for the study. A sampling unit may be a person (e.g. an ICU nurse), a group (e.g. a ward), or an organisation (e.g. a hospital). In cluster sampling, for example, the sampling units are clusters such as hospitals. The complete list of sampling units is the SAMPLING FRAME.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sampling frame — The SAMPLING FRAME is the LIST of all units, not the units themselves.
• Sampling errors — SAMPLING ERRORS are differences between sample results and the true population values.
• Sampling gap — "Sampling gap" is not a standard research term.

⭐ Key point: Clearly defining sampling units and the sampling frame helps ensure the sample represents the population.$x$, null),
(168, $x$The ethical principle of beneficence in research means:$x$, $x$Above all, do no harm$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Above all, do no harm
BENEFICENCE in research means acting for the participants' good — maximising possible BENEFITS and, ABOVE ALL, DOING NO HARM (minimising risks of physical, psychological, social, legal and financial harm). Researchers must ensure that the expected benefits of the study outweigh its risks, stop the study if harm occurs, and protect vulnerable participants. (Some texts describe "non-maleficence" — do no harm — as part of beneficence.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Maintain confidentiality — Maintaining confidentiality relates to the principle of respect for persons and privacy.
• Keep anonymity and privacy — Anonymity and privacy are separate protections.
• Avoid plagiarism — Avoiding plagiarism relates to research integrity and honesty.

⭐ Key point: The Belmont Report's three principles: respect for persons (autonomy, informed consent), beneficence, and justice (fair selection of participants).$x$, null),
(169, $x$The right to withdraw from a research study at any time is part of the subject's right to:$x$, $x$Self-determination$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Self-determination
The RIGHT TO SELF-DETERMINATION means participants are AUTONOMOUS — they can freely DECIDE whether to take part in a study, without coercion, and can WITHDRAW at ANY TIME without giving a reason and WITHOUT PENALTY (e.g. their care must not be affected). This right is protected through informed consent, which must clearly explain the right to withdraw.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anonymity only — Anonymity relates to protecting participants' identity.
• Compensation — Compensation relates to payment for participation or injury.
• Data ownership — Data ownership is a separate legal and ethical issue.

⭐ Key point: Vulnerable participants (e.g. ICU patients, children) need extra protection, such as consent from a legal representative and assent where possible.$x$, null),
(170, $x$Qualitative research methods are primarily used to:$x$, $x$Explore and understand experiences, meanings, and phenomena in depth$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Explore and understand experiences, meanings, and phenomena in depth
QUALITATIVE RESEARCH aims to EXPLORE and UNDERSTAND people's EXPERIENCES, feelings, beliefs and the MEANINGS they give to events, IN DEPTH. Data are collected as words, through in-depth interviews, focus groups, observation and document analysis, from small, purposively chosen samples. Example: "What is it like for families to have a relative on a ventilator in ICU?"

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Generate statistically generalisable numerical data — Generating statistically generalisable numerical data is QUANTITATIVE research.
• Test drug efficacy through randomised trials — Testing drug efficacy through randomised trials is QUANTITATIVE (experimental) research.
• Calculate p-values — Calculating p-values is part of QUANTITATIVE statistical analysis.

⭐ Key point: Qualitative designs: phenomenology (lived experience), grounded theory (develop theory), ethnography (culture), and historical research.$x$, null),
(171, $x$A test that shows positive when the true status is negative gives a:$x$, $x$False positive$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: False positive
When comparing a test result with the TRUE status: TRUE POSITIVE — test positive, disease present; FALSE POSITIVE — test POSITIVE but disease ABSENT (true status negative); FALSE NEGATIVE — test negative but disease present; TRUE NEGATIVE — test negative, disease absent. A false positive can cause unnecessary anxiety, further tests and treatment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• True positive — A true positive is correctly positive in someone WITH the condition.
• False negative — A false negative is a NEGATIVE result in someone WITH the condition (missed diagnosis).
• True negative — A true negative is correctly negative in someone WITHOUT the condition.

⭐ Key point: SENSITIVITY = ability to detect people WITH the disease (few false negatives). SPECIFICITY = ability to correctly identify people WITHOUT the disease (few false positives).$x$, null),
(172, $x$The research method is a part of:$x$, $x$Research methodology$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Research methodology
RESEARCH METHODOLOGY is the overall plan and philosophy of how a study is conducted — including the research design, population and sampling, instruments, data collection procedures, data analysis, and the justification for each choice. The RESEARCH METHOD (the specific procedure or tool used, e.g. questionnaire survey, interview, experiment) is PART of the methodology.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The problem — The research problem is the issue being studied, not where the method belongs.
• The experiment — An experiment is one type of research design/method.
• Research techniques — Research techniques are specific tools within methods.

⭐ Key point: In a research report, the "Methodology" chapter (often Chapter 3 in Nigerian theses) describes design, setting, population, sample, instrument, validity, reliability, data collection and analysis.$x$, null),
(173, $x$CINAHL and PubMed are examples of:$x$, $x$Databases used to search nursing and biomedical literature for evidence-based practice$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Databases used to search nursing and biomedical literature for evidence-based practice
CINAHL (Cumulative Index to Nursing and Allied Health Literature) and PUBMED (which searches MEDLINE, run by the US National Library of Medicine) are online DATABASES used to SEARCH published NURSING and BIOMEDICAL research. Nurses use them to find evidence for EVIDENCE-BASED PRACTICE — e.g. searching for studies on VAP prevention or pressure ulcer management.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hospital scheduling systems — They are not hospital scheduling systems.
• Electronic medication systems — They are not electronic medication systems.
• Patient billing platforms — They are not billing platforms.

⭐ Key point: Other useful sources: the Cochrane Library (systematic reviews), Embase, Google Scholar, and African Journals Online (AJOL). Use keywords and Boolean operators (AND, OR, NOT) to search effectively.$x$, null),
(174, $x$A nurse manager reviewing a department's risk management programme should ensure it includes:$x$, $x$Common risk categories, elements of risk management, and a functioning incident reporting system$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Common risk categories, elements of risk management, and a functioning incident reporting system
A RISK MANAGEMENT PROGRAMME should include: identification of COMMON RISK CATEGORIES (falls, medication errors, infections, pressure ulcers), ELEMENTS OF RISK MANAGEMENT (identify, analyse, control, monitor risks), and a functioning INCIDENT REPORTING SYSTEM.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only a list of staff names — A list of staff names is not a risk programme.
• No formal documentation, to save time — Documentation is essential.
• A focus solely on financial risk, excluding patient safety risk — Patient safety must be included.

⭐ Key point: Regular risk assessments and audits help prevent harm.$x$, null),
(175, $x$The stage of conflict where the disagreement becomes visible through behaviour such as arguing or withdrawal is the:$x$, $x$Manifest stage$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Manifest stage
Pondy's model describes the STAGES of CONFLICT: (1) LATENT — conditions for conflict exist (e.g. scarce resources, unclear roles) but no one is aware yet; (2) PERCEIVED — people become aware that a conflict exists; (3) FELT — emotions develop (anger, anxiety, tension); (4) MANIFEST — the conflict becomes VISIBLE through BEHAVIOUR, such as arguing, open disagreement, sabotage, withdrawal or avoidance; (5) AFTERMATH — the result after the conflict is resolved or suppressed (which may leave lasting effects).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Latent stage — Latent: conditions exist, but there is no awareness.
• Perceived stage — Perceived: awareness, but no visible action yet.
• Aftermath stage — Aftermath: the outcome after the conflict has been handled.

⭐ Key point: Managers should identify and address conflict early (latent or perceived stage) before it becomes manifest and harmful to teamwork and patient care.$x$, null),
(176, $x$Leadership is based on a superior’s$x$, $x$persuasive communication.$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: persuasive communication.
LEADERSHIP is the ability to INFLUENCE people to work willingly towards goals. It is based mainly on PERSUASIVE COMMUNICATION — inspiring, motivating, convincing and building trust — rather than giving orders. A leader can lead without a formal position (e.g. an experienced staff nurse others follow). MANAGEMENT, in contrast, is based on formal AUTHORITY given by the organisation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• authority. — Authority is the formal right to give orders — the basis of management, not leadership.
• responsibility. — Responsibility is the duty to carry out assigned tasks.
• accountability. — Accountability is being answerable for the outcomes of one's actions.

⭐ Key point: The best nurse managers combine management authority with good leadership (communication, vision and support).$x$, null),
(177, $x$A unit is experiencing chronic understaffing, affecting patient safety. As part of the nurse manager's risk management responsibilities, the most appropriate action is to:$x$, $x$Formally document and escalate the staffing concern through appropriate channels, using incident reports where patient safety is affected$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Formally document and escalate the staffing concern through appropriate channels, using incident reports where patient safety is affected
When CHRONIC UNDERSTAFFING affects patient safety, the nurse manager should FORMALLY DOCUMENT and ESCALATE the concern through appropriate channels (senior management), using INCIDENT REPORTS where patient safety is affected. This creates a record and supports advocacy for safe staffing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ignore the issue since staffing is outside nursing's control — Ignoring it endangers patients.
• Accept the situation permanently with no advocacy — Accepting it permanently fails to advocate.
• Blame individual staff members for the workload — Blaming individuals ignores the system problem.

⭐ Key point: Safe staffing is a key factor in patient outcomes.$x$, null),
(178, $x$The process of interaction between two or more people in an organisation is:$x$, $x$Communication$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Communication
COMMUNICATION is the PROCESS of INTERACTION between two or more people in which information, ideas, feelings and meanings are exchanged — through verbal, written and non-verbal means. In organisations, communication flows downward (instructions), upward (feedback, reports), and horizontally (between colleagues). Good communication is essential for teamwork and patient safety.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Adaptation — Adaptation is the process of adjusting to change or the environment.
• Interaction — Interaction is a general term, but the organisational process described here is communication.
• Induction — Induction is the orientation of new staff.

⭐ Key point: Structured communication tools (e.g. SBAR for handover and escalation) reduce misunderstandings and errors.$x$, null),
(179, $x$Which management level is responsible for policy formulation?$x$, $x$Top management$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Top management
TOP MANAGEMENT (e.g. the board, chief executive, director of nursing services) is responsible for POLICY FORMULATION — setting the organisation's vision, mission, long-term goals, strategies and major policies, and allocating resources. MIDDLE managers (e.g. nursing officers in charge of departments) translate policies into plans and implement them. FIRST-LINE managers (e.g. ward managers, charge nurses) supervise day-to-day operations and staff.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• First-line managers — First-line managers handle daily operations, not policy formulation.
• Middle managers — Middle managers implement policies and coordinate departments.
• Charge nurses — Charge nurses manage shifts and direct care.

⭐ Key point: Policies made at the top should involve input from frontline staff to be practical and effective.$x$, null),
(180, $x$Which is NOT one of Fayol's 14 principles of management?$x$, $x$Decentralisation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Decentralisation
FAYOL'S 14 PRINCIPLES include: division of work, authority, discipline, unity of command, unity of direction, SUBORDINATION OF INDIVIDUAL INTERESTS, REMUNERATION, CENTRALISATION (not decentralisation), SCALAR CHAIN, order, equity, stability of tenure, initiative, and esprit de corps. "DECENTRALISATION" is not one of the principles (Fayol's principle is "centralisation", meaning finding the right balance).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Subordination of individual interests — Subordination of individual interests IS a principle.
• Remuneration — Remuneration IS a principle.
• Scalar chain (clear line of authority) — Scalar chain IS a principle.

⭐ Key point: Fayol is called the "father of modern management".$x$, null),
(181, $x$When mediating conflict between staff, a nurse manager should ideally:$x$, $x$Act as a neutral mediator$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Act as a neutral mediator
When MEDIATING CONFLICT between staff, the nurse manager should act as a NEUTRAL MEDIATOR: meet the parties privately, listen to both sides without judgement, focus on facts and behaviour (not personalities), keep the discussion centred on shared goals (safe patient care), help the staff explore solutions, and agree on an action plan with follow-up. Neutrality builds trust and encourages a fair, lasting resolution.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Take sides with the more senior staff member — Taking sides (e.g. with the more senior staff member) is unfair and damages trust.
• Resolve the conflict publicly in front of patients — Resolving conflict publicly in front of patients is unprofessional and embarrassing.
• Avoid addressing it entirely — Avoiding the conflict allows it to grow and harm teamwork.

⭐ Key point: Collaboration ("win-win") is the best long-term conflict resolution approach when time allows.$x$, null),
(182, $x$The five classic functions of management are best listed as:$x$, $x$Planning, Organising, Staffing, Directing, Controlling$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Planning, Organising, Staffing, Directing, Controlling
The five classic FUNCTIONS OF MANAGEMENT (based on Fayol and later writers) are: PLANNING (deciding goals and how to achieve them), ORGANISING (arranging resources and structure), STAFFING (recruiting, selecting, training and allocating staff), DIRECTING/leading (guiding, motivating and communicating with staff), and CONTROLLING (monitoring performance and correcting deviations). These apply to managing a ward or ICU.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diagnosing, Prescribing, Treating, Evaluating, Discharging — Diagnosing, prescribing, treating, evaluating and discharging are clinical functions.
• Assessing, Planning, Implementing, Evaluating, Documenting — Assessing, planning, implementing, evaluating and documenting relate to the nursing process.
• Hiring, Firing, Paying, Training, Promoting — Hiring, firing, paying, training and promoting are human resource tasks, not the full set of management functions.

⭐ Key point: Gulick's POSDCORB: Planning, Organising, Staffing, Directing, Coordinating, Reporting, Budgeting.$x$, null),
(183, $x$Which conflict resolution style involves each party giving up something to reach a middle-ground solution?$x$, $x$Compromising$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Compromising
COMPROMISING is a conflict-resolution style where each party GIVES UP SOMETHING to reach a MIDDLE-GROUND solution that is partly acceptable to both ("split the difference"). It is useful when time is limited, both sides have equal power, or a temporary solution is needed. However, neither side is fully satisfied.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Collaborating — COLLABORATING aims for a "win-win" solution that fully meets both parties' needs.
• Competing — COMPETING aims to win at the other party's expense ("win-lose").
• Avoiding — AVOIDING ignores or postpones the conflict ("lose-lose").

⭐ Key point: Thomas–Kilmann styles: competing, collaborating, compromising, avoiding, and accommodating (giving in to the other side).$x$, null),
(184, $x$Shared governance is practised in some wards. Which describes this style of leadership?$x$, $x$Leadership is shared at the point of care$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Leadership is shared at the point of care
SHARED GOVERNANCE is a model where LEADERSHIP and decision-making are SHARED with staff nurses at the POINT OF CARE (e.g. through councils on practice, quality and education).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Leaders rely heavily on visioning and inspire members to achieve results — Visioning and inspiring describes transformational leadership.
• Leaders believe people are basically good and need not be closely controlled — Believing people need little control describes Theory Y.
• The leader makes all decisions alone — One leader deciding alone is autocratic.

⭐ Key point: Shared governance improves staff engagement and retention.$x$, null),
(185, $x$Which four elements must all be proven for a nurse to be found legally negligent?$x$, $x$Duty of care, breach of duty, harm, and breach causing the harm$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Duty of care, breach of duty, harm, and breach causing the harm
To prove a nurse NEGLIGENT, the claimant must establish ALL FOUR elements: (1) DUTY OF CARE — the nurse had a professional responsibility to the patient; (2) BREACH OF DUTY — the nurse failed to meet the expected standard of care (what a reasonable, competent nurse would do); (3) HARM (damage) — the patient suffered actual injury or loss; (4) CAUSATION — the breach directly CAUSED the harm. If any one element is missing, negligence is not proven.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Intent, motive, opportunity, and consequence — Intent, motive, opportunity and consequence relate to criminal law (crimes), not negligence.
• Consent, capacity, communication, and confidentiality — Consent, capacity, communication and confidentiality are important ethical/legal principles, but not the elements of negligence.
• Only that harm occurred, regardless of cause — Harm alone is not enough — the other elements must also be proven.

⭐ Key point: Accurate, timely documentation is the nurse's best evidence that the standard of care was met.$x$, null),
(186, $x$Which of the following is the appropriate nursing intervention for a patient with a terminal illness who is passing through the acceptance stage?$x$, $x$Being around though not speaking$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Being around though not speaking
In the ACCEPTANCE stage of grief (Kübler-Ross), the patient is often calm, withdrawn and may want QUIET COMPANY. The most appropriate intervention is BEING AROUND THOUGH NOT SPEAKING — a quiet, supportive presence.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Allowing the patient to cry — Allowing crying fits the depression stage.
• Encouraging unrestricted visiting — Unrestricted visiting may tire the patient.
• Explaining the patient what is being done — Explaining procedures is always good, but not the specific need here.

⭐ Key point: Stages: denial, anger, bargaining, depression, acceptance.$x$, null),
(187, $x$Justice in resource allocation involves ensuring that:$x$, $x$No patient is unfairly disadvantaged in access to medication, treatment, or staff time$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: No patient is unfairly disadvantaged in access to medication, treatment, or staff time
JUSTICE in resource allocation means FAIRNESS — ensuring NO PATIENT is UNFAIRLY DISADVANTAGED in access to medication, treatment, or staff time, based on clinical need.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Resources always go to the first patient who asks — First come, first served ignores need.
• Resource allocation is kept secret from patients — Allocation should be transparent.
• Only wealthy patients receive full resources — Wealth should not decide care.

⭐ Key point: Triage policies help allocate scarce resources fairly.$x$, null),
(188, $x$The nurse is obligated to follow a physician's order unless:$x$, $x$The order is an error, violates hospital policy, or would be detrimental to the client.$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The order is an error, violates hospital policy, or would be detrimental to the client.
A nurse must follow a physician's order UNLESS it is an ERROR, VIOLATES HOSPITAL POLICY, or would be HARMFUL (detrimental) to the client. In such cases, the nurse must question the order and not carry it out until clarified.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The order is a verbal order — Verbal orders can be followed per policy.
• The physicians order is illegible — Illegible orders should be clarified, but this is part of the broader rule.
• The order has not been transcribed — Untranscribed orders should be checked, but this isn't the main exception.

⭐ Key point: The nurse is legally accountable for following an unsafe order.$x$, null),
(189, $x$A nurse is asked to administer a medication dose that seems unusually high compared to standard practice. The most appropriate action is to:$x$, $x$Verify the order, question the prescriber if uncertain, and refuse to administer until the concern is resolved$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Verify the order, question the prescriber if uncertain, and refuse to administer until the concern is resolved
If a medication dose seems UNUSUALLY HIGH, the nurse should VERIFY the order (check references, the patient's weight and kidney function), QUESTION the prescriber, and REFUSE to give it until the concern is resolved.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administer it anyway, trusting the prescriber completely without question — Trusting the prescriber blindly is unsafe.
• Give half the dose without informing anyone — Giving half the dose without telling anyone is a medication error.
• Ignore the concern to avoid seeming difficult — Ignoring the concern may harm the patient.

⭐ Key point: Document the conversation and use the chain of command if unresolved.$x$, null),
(190, $x$A nurse loves caring for the elderly. This represents her:$x$, $x$Values$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Values
VALUES are a person's deeply held BELIEFS about what is important, desirable and worthwhile. They guide choices and behaviour. A nurse who LOVES caring for the ELDERLY shows a personal VALUE — she values older people and the work of caring for them. Professional nursing values include caring, compassion, respect, integrity, altruism and social justice.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respect — Respect is regard for the worth and rights of others — it can be a value, but the scenario describes what she cherishes.
• Dignity — Dignity is the inherent worth of every person.
• Fidelity — Fidelity is keeping promises and commitments.

⭐ Key point: Nurses should reflect on their own values (values clarification) and avoid imposing them on patients.$x$, null),
(191, $x$A family disagrees with a DNR order for a patient with metastatic lung cancer. The nurse involves:$x$, $x$Ethics committee$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Ethics committee
When a FAMILY DISAGREES with a DO-NOT-RESUSCITATE (DNR) order — for example, for a patient with metastatic lung cancer — the nurse should involve the ETHICS COMMITTEE (along with senior medical staff). The ethics committee is a multidisciplinary group (doctors, nurses, ethicists, legal advisers, chaplains) that helps MEDIATE conflicts, clarifies ethical principles (patient autonomy, best interests, futility), reviews the patient's wishes and advance directives, and supports a fair, transparent decision.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hospital security — Hospital security deals with safety threats, not ethical disagreements.
• Risk management — Risk management focuses on legal and financial risk, not ethical mediation.
• Social services — Social services help with social needs (housing, finances, support), not ethical conflict resolution.

⭐ Key point: Early, honest, compassionate communication with families about prognosis and goals of care helps prevent such conflicts.$x$, null),
(192, $x$Which of the following best describes moral uncertainty?$x$, $x$A situation where nurses have doubts or uncertainties about the ethical course of action$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A situation where nurses have doubts or uncertainties about the ethical course of action
MORAL UNCERTAINTY occurs when a nurse has DOUBTS about which ethical course of action is right, or whether a moral problem even exists.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A situation where nurses have no ethical guidance or rules to follow — Having no guidance is not the definition.
• A situation where nurses have conflicting ethical guidance or rules to follow — Conflicting guidance describes a moral dilemma.
• A situation where nurses are unable to make ethical decisions due to lack of knowledge or experience — Lack of knowledge isn't the core definition.

⭐ Key point: Moral distress: knowing what is right but being unable to act.$x$, null),
(193, $x$Patient confidentiality may be ethically breached when:$x$, $x$There is a credible risk of serious harm to the patient or identifiable third parties$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: There is a credible risk of serious harm to the patient or identifiable third parties
CONFIDENTIALITY may be breached when there is a CREDIBLE RISK of SERIOUS HARM to the patient or IDENTIFIABLE THIRD PARTIES (e.g. threat of violence, child abuse, notifiable diseases).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A colleague is simply curious — Curiosity is not a valid reason.
• A family member requests information without consent — Family members need consent.
• It is more convenient for staff — Convenience is not a valid reason.

⭐ Key point: Share only the minimum necessary information.$x$, null),
(194, $x$A nursing instructor tells students that the most important principle when caring for patients is to do no harm. This principle is:$x$, $x$Non-maleficence$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Non-maleficence
NON-MALEFICENCE means "FIRST, DO NO HARM" (primum non nocere). Nurses must avoid causing harm — intentionally or through carelessness — and must weigh the risks and benefits of every intervention. Examples: preventing falls, pressure ulcers and medication errors; avoiding unnecessary invasive procedures; using safe practices.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Beneficence — BENEFICENCE means actively DOING GOOD for the patient.
• Justice — JUSTICE means FAIRNESS in care and distribution of resources.
• Veracity — VERACITY means TRUTHFULNESS.

⭐ Key point: Patient safety initiatives (e.g. checklists, bundles, incident reporting) put non-maleficence into practice.$x$, null),
(195, $x$For patients who require enteral nutrition support for longer than approximately 4-6 weeks, which access route is generally recommended over a nasoenteric tube?$x$, $x$A percutaneous route such as gastrostomy (PEG) or jejunostomy tube$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A percutaneous route such as gastrostomy (PEG) or jejunostomy tube
For enteral nutrition needed LONGER than about 4–6 WEEKS, a PERCUTANEOUS route such as GASTROSTOMY (PEG) or JEJUNOSTOMY is recommended, because long-term nasal tubes cause discomfort, nasal ulcers, sinusitis and displacement.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Continued nasogastric tube indefinitely — Indefinite NG use causes complications.
• Oral sipping only — Oral sipping may not be enough.
• Intravenous TPN as first choice — TPN is used only if the gut doesn't work.

⭐ Key point: PEG tubes are more comfortable and secure for long-term feeding.$x$, null),
(196, $x$What is the primary goal of enteral nutrition in critical care?$x$, $x$D) Delivering nutrition through the gastrointestinal tract$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: D) Delivering nutrition through the gastrointestinal tract
ENTERAL NUTRITION means DELIVERING NUTRITION through the GASTROINTESTINAL TRACT, usually via a tube (nasogastric, nasojejunal, PEG), when a patient cannot eat enough by mouth. In critical care, its goals are to meet energy and protein needs, MAINTAIN GUT INTEGRITY (preventing the gut lining from wasting and bacteria from crossing into the blood), support immune function, and reduce infections compared with parenteral (IV) nutrition.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A) Inducing sleep — It is not used to induce sleep.
• B) Providing comfort — It may provide comfort, but that is not its primary goal.
• C) Administering pain medication — It is not for giving pain medication (although some drugs can be given via feeding tubes).

⭐ Key point: "If the gut works, use it." Start enteral feeding within 24–48 hours of ICU admission in haemodynamically stable patients.$x$, null),
(197, $x$Which of the following patients is the MOST appropriate candidate for enteral rather than parenteral nutrition?$x$, $x$A patient with a functioning GI tract who is intubated and cannot swallow safely due to sedation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A patient with a functioning GI tract who is intubated and cannot swallow safely due to sedation
ENTERAL NUTRITION is preferred for patients with a FUNCTIONING GI TRACT who cannot eat (e.g. intubated and sedated).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A patient with a complete bowel obstruction — Bowel obstruction prevents enteral feeding.
• A patient with severe, uncontrolled upper GI hemorrhage — Severe GI haemorrhage contraindicates feeding.
• A patient in septic shock requiring escalating vasopressor doses with signs of bowel ischemia — Bowel ischaemia contraindicates feeding.

⭐ Key point: Start enteral feeding within 24–48 hours if possible.$x$, null),
(198, $x$Fat provides approximately how many kilocalories per gram, compared to carbohydrate and protein?$x$, $x$Fat 9kcal/g, more than double CHO/protein (4kcal/g each)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fat 9kcal/g, more than double CHO/protein (4kcal/g each)
Different nutrients provide different amounts of ENERGY: FAT provides about 9 kcal per gram — MORE THAN DOUBLE carbohydrate and protein, which each provide about 4 kcal per gram. (Alcohol provides about 7 kcal/g.) This is why fat-based products (e.g. lipid emulsions in parenteral nutrition, propofol) contribute many calories in small volumes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fat 4kcal/g, same as CHO/protein — Fat does not provide 4 kcal/g (that is carbohydrate and protein).
• Fat 1kcal/g, far less than CHO/protein — Fat provides much more than 1 kcal/g.
• Fat provides no calories — Fat does provide calories.

⭐ Key point: Remember to count propofol (1.1 kcal/mL from lipid) in ICU nutrition calculations to avoid overfeeding.$x$, null),
(199, $x$Women are how many times more likely than men to develop ICU-AW?$x$, $x$About 4 times$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: About 4 times
ICU-ACQUIRED WEAKNESS (ICU-AW) is generalised muscle weakness developing during critical illness. Studies (e.g. De Jonghe et al.) found FEMALE SEX to be an independent risk factor, with women about 4 TIMES more likely than men to develop ICU-AW. Other risk factors: sepsis, multi-organ failure, prolonged mechanical ventilation, immobility, hyperglycaemia, corticosteroids and neuromuscular blocking agents.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Equally likely — Women and men are not equally likely according to these studies.
• About 2 times — "About 2 times" underestimates the reported risk.
• About 10 times — "About 10 times" overestimates it.

⭐ Key point: Early mobilisation, good glucose control, and minimising sedation and paralysis help prevent ICU-AW.$x$, null),
(200, $x$How many postural drainage positions are used, corresponding to the lung's bronchopulmonary segments?$x$, $x$12$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 12
POSTURAL DRAINAGE uses GRAVITY to help drain secretions from specific lung segments into the larger airways, where they can be coughed up or suctioned. The patient is placed in different positions so the affected segment is uppermost and its bronchus points downwards. Commonly, 12 positions are described, corresponding to the main bronchopulmonary segments of both lungs (e.g. upper lobe apical, posterior and anterior segments; middle lobe/lingula; lower lobe superior, anterior, lateral and posterior basal segments).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 6 — 6 positions are too few to cover all segments.
• 8 — 8 positions are too few.
• 10 — 10 positions are too few.

⭐ Key point: Postural drainage is often combined with percussion and vibration. Head-down positions are avoided in raised ICP, recent surgery, haemoptysis and severe breathlessness.$x$, null)
),
upd as (
  update public.questions qq
     set exp = r.exp,
         ans_idx = coalesce((select (t.i - 1)::int from jsonb_array_elements_text(qq.opts) with ordinality t(v, i) where t.v = r.fixed_answer), qq.ans_idx)
    from r
   where qq.q = r.q
     and qq.opts ->> qq.ans_idx = r.answer
  returning r.n
)
insert into rationale_report select 4, count(distinct n), count(*) from upd
  on conflict (day) do update set questions_updated = excluded.questions_updated, rows_changed = excluded.rows_changed;

-- ===================== DAY 5 =====================
-- Nursing Exam Room: full rationales for CCN Mock Exam — Day 5 (200 questions)
-- Run this once in the Supabase SQL Editor. It is safe to run again.
--
-- Each explanation now says: the correct answer and why, why each other option is wrong,
-- and a key point to remember. The same questions in the practice app get the new explanation too.
-- Questions are found by their exact wording and correct answer. Where the old answer key was wrong,
-- the correct option is also fixed (listed at the top of the file).


with r(n, q, answer, exp, fixed_answer) as (values
(1, $x$A 6 kg child in shock is prescribed a 20 mL/kg bolus of normal saline. What volume should be given?$x$, $x$120 mL$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 120 mL
Fluid boluses for children in shock are worked out by WEIGHT, because a child's blood volume is about 70–80 mL/kg. Formula: VOLUME = mL/kg × WEIGHT. Here: 20 mL/kg × 6 kg = 120 mL of 0.9% sodium chloride. Check: 20 × 6 = 120. ✓ For a 6 kg baby, 120 mL is about a quarter of the whole blood volume, so it is a significant amount. Give it quickly (over 5–10 minutes, using a syringe push or pressure bag), then REASSESS heart rate, capillary refill time, BP, conscious level, urine output and signs of overload (crackles, enlarged liver) before giving any more.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 240 mL — 240 mL is 40 mL/kg — double the dose; too much for one bolus in a small baby.
• 60 mL — 60 mL is only 10 mL/kg — half the prescribed dose.
• 180 mL — 180 mL is 30 mL/kg — more than prescribed.

⭐ Key point: In children with severe malnutrition or heart disease, smaller boluses (10 mL/kg) are given more slowly, because their hearts can easily fail with fluid overload.$x$, null),
(2, $x$A 75 kg man with 35% TBSA burns arrives 1 hour after the injury and has had no IV fluid. Using the Parkland formula, at what rate should the first half of the fluid run?$x$, $x$750 mL/hr$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 750 mL/hr
The PARKLAND FORMULA estimates burn fluid for the first 24 hours: 4 mL × WEIGHT (kg) × %TBSA. STEP 1 — Total: 4 × 75 × 35 = 10,500 mL. STEP 2 — HALF of this (5,250 mL) must be given in the first 8 HOURS counted FROM THE TIME OF THE BURN (not from arrival). STEP 3 — He arrived 1 hour after the burn with no fluid, so only 7 hours remain to give 5,250 mL. STEP 4 — Rate: 5,250 ÷ 7 = 750 mL/hr. The second half (5,250 mL) is then given over the next 16 hours (about 328 mL/hr).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1,500 mL/hr — 1,500 mL/hr is double the correct rate.
• 656.2 mL/hr — 656.2 mL/hr is 5,250 ÷ 8 — it ignores the hour already lost.
• 437.5 mL/hr — 437.5 mL/hr is 10,500 ÷ 24 — an even rate over the whole day, which under-resuscitates in the critical early hours.

⭐ Key point: Parkland is only a starting point — adjust the rate hourly to keep urine output at 0.5–1 mL/kg/hr in adults. Use Hartmann's (Ringer's lactate).$x$, null),
(3, $x$Amoxicillin suspension is available as 250 mg in 5 mL. The prescription is 500 mg. What volume should be given?$x$, $x$10 mL$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 10 mL
For oral liquids: VOLUME = (DOSE WANTED ÷ DOSE IN STOCK) × STOCK VOLUME. Here: (500 mg ÷ 250 mg) × 5 mL = 2 × 5 = 10 mL. Check: 5 mL contains 250 mg, so 10 mL contains 500 mg. ✓ A useful "sense check": the dose wanted is double the stock strength, so the volume must be double the stock volume.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2 mL — 2 mL forgets to multiply by the 5 mL stock volume (only 100 mg).
• 2.5 mL — 2.5 mL would give only 125 mg (a quarter of the dose).
• 20 mL — 20 mL would give 1,000 mg — double the dose.

⭐ Key point: Shake suspensions well before measuring (the drug settles at the bottom), and use an oral syringe for accuracy.$x$, null),
(4, $x$Using the 4-2-1 rule, what is the hourly maintenance fluid rate for a child weighing 32 kg?$x$, $x$72 mL/hr$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 72 mL/hr
The 4-2-1 RULE gives HOURLY maintenance fluid: 4 mL/kg/hr for the FIRST 10 kg; 2 mL/kg/hr for the NEXT 10 kg (11–20 kg); 1 mL/kg/hr for EVERY kg ABOVE 20 kg. For 32 kg: first 10 kg = 40 mL/hr; next 10 kg = 20 mL/hr; remaining 12 kg = 12 mL/hr. Total = 40 + 20 + 12 = 72 mL/hr. Shortcut for anyone above 20 kg: 60 + (weight − 20) = 60 + 12 = 72 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 82 mL/hr — 82 mL/hr adds 10 mL/hr too much.
• 128 mL/hr — 128 mL/hr is 4 mL/kg for all 32 kg — nearly double.
• 64 mL/hr — 64 mL/hr is 2 mL/kg for all 32 kg.

⭐ Key point: Maintenance fluid is only for patients who cannot drink enough. Sick children often need less (two-thirds of maintenance) because stress increases ADH, which holds water and risks hyponatraemia.$x$, null),
(5, $x$An adult has burns to the whole left leg and the perineum. Using the Rule of Nines, what is the TBSA burned?$x$, $x$19%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 19%
RULE OF NINES (adult): each WHOLE LEG = 18% (front 9% + back 9%); the PERINEUM = 1%. Here: whole left leg 18% + perineum 1% = 19% TBSA. A 19% burn in an adult needs IV fluid resuscitation, and the perineal burn needs specialist burns care because of high infection risk (contamination from urine and faeces) and the likely need for a urinary catheter.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 28% — 28% adds an extra 9%.
• 23.5% — 23.5% adds an extra 4.5%.
• 10% — 10% counts the leg as only 9%.

⭐ Key point: Do not include simple redness (superficial/first-degree burns) when calculating %TBSA for fluid resuscitation.$x$, null),
(6, $x$1,000 mL of Ringer's lactate is prescribed to run over 6 hours using a giving set with a drop factor of 10 drops/mL. What is the correct drip rate?$x$, $x$28 drops/min$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 28 drops/min
DROPS PER MINUTE = (VOLUME × DROP FACTOR) ÷ TIME IN MINUTES. Here: (1,000 mL × 10 drops/mL) ÷ (6 hours × 60) = 10,000 ÷ 360 = 27.8, which rounds to 28 drops/min. Check: the hourly rate is 1,000 ÷ 6 = 167 mL/hr; with a 10-drop set, drops/min = mL/hr ÷ 6 = 167 ÷ 6 ≈ 28. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 56 drops/min — 56 drops/min is double — the bag would finish in 3 hours.
• 42 drops/min — 42 drops/min would finish in about 4 hours.
• 167 drops/min — 167 drops/min is the rate in mL/HOUR, not drops per minute.

⭐ Key point: Count drops for a full minute when setting a gravity drip, and recheck regularly — gravity rates change with the patient's position and the height of the bag.$x$, null),
(7, $x$A head-injured patient has BP 140/80 mmHg and ICP 30 mmHg. What is the cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP)?$x$, $x$70 mmHg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 70 mmHg
CEREBRAL PERFUSION PRESSURE (CPP) is the pressure pushing blood into the brain: CPP = MAP − ICP. STEP 1 — MAP = (systolic + 2 × diastolic) ÷ 3 = (140 + 160) ÷ 3 = 300 ÷ 3 = 100 mmHg. STEP 2 — CPP = 100 − 30 = 70 mmHg. A CPP of 60–70 mmHg is the usual target in traumatic brain injury, so CPP is acceptable here — BUT the ICP of 30 mmHg is HIGH (normal 5–15; treatment usually starts above 20–22 mmHg), so measures to lower ICP are still needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 110 mmHg — 110 mmHg is systolic minus ICP (140 − 30) — wrong, it should use MAP.
• 50 mmHg — 50 mmHg is diastolic minus ICP (80 − 30).
• 130 mmHg — 130 mmHg is not a valid calculation.

⭐ Key point: To lower ICP: head up 30°, neck straight, avoid hypoxia and high CO2, sedation and analgesia, treat fever and seizures, osmotherapy (mannitol or hypertonic saline), CSF drainage.$x$, null),
(8, $x$A patient weighs 33 lb. A drug is prescribed at 5 mg/kg. What dose should be given?$x$, $x$75 mg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 75 mg
STEP 1 — Convert pounds to kilograms: kg = lb ÷ 2.2. 33 lb ÷ 2.2 = 15 kg. STEP 2 — Dose: 5 mg/kg × 15 kg = 75 mg. Check: 15 × 2.2 = 33 lb. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 165 mg — 165 mg uses the weight in POUNDS as if it were kg (5 × 33) — more than double.
• 150 mg — 150 mg is 10 mg/kg — double the dose.
• 34.1 mg — 34.1 mg divides by 2.2 twice — less than half the dose.

⭐ Key point: A weight of 33 lb is a young child (about 3–4 years). Errors with pounds and kilograms are especially dangerous in children; always confirm the weight in kg.$x$, null),
(9, $x$Cefuroxime 90 mg/kg/day is prescribed in divided doses every 6 hours for an infant weighing 15 kg. How much is each dose?$x$, $x$337.5 mg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 337.5 mg
STEP 1 — Daily dose: 90 mg/kg/day × 15 kg = 1,350 mg per day. STEP 2 — "Every 6 hours" = 4 doses a day (24 ÷ 6 = 4). STEP 3 — Each dose: 1,350 ÷ 4 = 337.5 mg. Check: 337.5 × 4 = 1,350. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 22.5 mg — 22.5 mg is far too little (a decimal or division error).
• 1,350 mg — 1,350 mg is the WHOLE DAY's dose — giving it every 6 hours would be four times the prescribed amount.
• 270 mg — 270 mg would be the dose if it were divided into 5 doses.

⭐ Key point: When a prescription says "mg/kg/DAY in divided doses", calculate the daily total first, then divide by the number of doses. When it says "mg/kg/DOSE", multiply by weight only.$x$, null),
(10, $x$A patient does not open the eyes at all, uses inappropriate words and flexes abnormally to pain (decorticate). What is the Glasgow Coma Scale score?$x$, $x$7/15$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 7/15
GLASGOW COMA SCALE = EYES (1–4) + VERBAL (1–5) + MOTOR (1–6). EYES — does NOT open at all = 1. VERBAL — INAPPROPRIATE WORDS (random words, no conversation) = 3 (orientated 5, confused 4, inappropriate words 3, incomprehensible sounds 2, none 1). MOTOR — ABNORMAL FLEXION to pain (DECORTICATE posturing — arms bent in towards the chest) = 3 (obeys 6, localises 5, normal withdrawal 4, abnormal flexion 3, extension 2, none 1). Total: 1 + 3 + 3 = 7/15. A GCS of 8 or less means severe brain injury and usually the need to secure the airway.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 8/15 — 8/15 overscores one component by 1.
• 9/15 — 9/15 overscores by 2.
• 6/15 — 6/15 underscores by 1.

⭐ Key point: Decorticate (abnormal flexion) = 3; decerebrate (extension) = 2. Always report the parts (E1 V3 M3) — the motor score is the best predictor of outcome.$x$, null),
(11, $x$1,000 mL of 5% dextrose is prescribed to run over 8 hours using a giving set with a drop factor of 10 drops/mL. What is the correct drip rate?$x$, $x$21 drops/min$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 21 drops/min
DROPS PER MINUTE = (VOLUME × DROP FACTOR) ÷ TIME IN MINUTES. Here: (1,000 × 10) ÷ (8 × 60) = 10,000 ÷ 480 = 20.8, which rounds to 21 drops/min. Check: 1,000 ÷ 8 = 125 mL/hr; 125 ÷ 6 ≈ 21. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 31 drops/min — 31 drops/min would finish the bag in about 5.4 hours.
• 42 drops/min — 42 drops/min is double — finished in 4 hours.
• 125 drops/min — 125 drops/min is the rate in mL/HOUR.

⭐ Key point: 5% dextrose is mainly "free water" — after the glucose is used, the water spreads to all body compartments, so large volumes can cause hyponatraemia; it is not a resuscitation fluid.$x$, null),
(12, $x$500 mL of normal saline is running at 150 mL/hr. How long will it take to finish?$x$, $x$3 h 20 min$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 3 h 20 min
TIME = VOLUME ÷ RATE. Here: 500 mL ÷ 150 mL/hr = 3.333 hours. Convert the decimal part into minutes: 0.333 × 60 = 20 minutes. So the bag will run for 3 HOURS 20 MINUTES. Check: 150 mL/hr × 3⅓ hr = 500 mL. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 18 h — 18 hours is far too long.
• 3 h 50 min — 3 h 50 min is 30 minutes too long.
• 4 h 20 min — 4 h 20 min is an hour too long.

⭐ Key point: Never write "3.33 hours" as "3 hours 33 minutes" — always multiply the decimal part by 60 to get minutes.$x$, null),
(13, $x$1,500 mL of 5% dextrose is prescribed to run over 10 hours using a giving set with a drop factor of 20 drops/mL. What is the correct drip rate?$x$, $x$50 drops/min$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 50 drops/min
DROPS PER MINUTE = (VOLUME × DROP FACTOR) ÷ TIME IN MINUTES. Here: (1,500 × 20) ÷ (10 × 60) = 30,000 ÷ 600 = 50 drops/min. Check: hourly rate = 1,500 ÷ 10 = 150 mL/hr; with a 20-drop set, drops/min = mL/hr ÷ 3 = 50. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 150 drops/min — 150 drops/min is the rate in mL/HOUR.
• 38 drops/min — 38 drops/min would finish in about 13 hours.
• 100 drops/min — 100 drops/min is double the correct rate.

⭐ Key point: Drop factor shortcuts: 10 drops/mL → ÷6; 15 → ÷4; 20 → ÷3; 60 (micro-drip) → drops/min equals mL/hr.$x$, null),
(14, $x$An adult has burns to the back of the trunk, the head and neck and the perineum. Using the Rule of Nines, what is the TBSA burned?$x$, $x$28%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 28%
RULE OF NINES (adult): BACK of the trunk = 18%; HEAD and NECK = 9%; PERINEUM = 1%. Total: 18 + 9 + 1 = 28% TBSA. Head and neck burns are dangerous because of the risk of airway swelling (especially with inhalation injury) — check for hoarseness, stridor, singed nasal hair and soot in the mouth; early intubation may be needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 32.5% — 32.5% adds an extra 4.5%.
• 19% — 19% misses the head and neck (or counts the back as 9%).
• 37% — 37% adds an extra 9%.

⭐ Key point: Facial, perineal and circumferential burns need referral to a burns centre regardless of their size.$x$, null),
(15, $x$Heparin 20,000 units in 500 mL is ordered at 1,500 units/hour. What rate (mL/hr) should be set on the pump?$x$, $x$37.5 mL/hr$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 37.5 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 20,000 units ÷ 500 mL = 40 units per mL. STEP 2 — Rate: 1,500 units/hr ÷ 40 units/mL = 37.5 mL/hr. Check: 37.5 × 40 = 1,500 units. ✓ (Note this bag is a different strength from other heparin bags — always calculate from the bag in your hand.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 15 mL/hr — 15 mL/hr would give only 600 units/hr.
• 40 mL/hr — 40 mL/hr confuses the concentration (40 units/mL) with the rate — it gives 1,600 units/hr.
• 75 mL/hr — 75 mL/hr would give 3,000 units/hr — double the dose, with a serious risk of bleeding.

⭐ Key point: Heparin is a high-alert drug: independent double-check by two nurses, label the line clearly, and monitor aPTT/anti-Xa and platelets.$x$, null),
(16, $x$Adrenaline (epinephrine) 4 mg is diluted to 250 mL and prescribed at 0.05 mcg/kg/min for a patient weighing 55 kg. At what rate (mL/hr) should the infusion pump be set?$x$, $x$10.3 mL/hr$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 10.3 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 4 mg in 250 mL = 0.016 mg/mL = 16 micrograms/mL. STEP 2 — Dose per hour: 0.05 mcg/kg/min × 55 kg × 60 min = 165 micrograms/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 165 ÷ 16 = 10.3 mL/hr. Check: 10.3 × 16 = 165 mcg/hr ÷ 60 = 2.75 mcg/min ÷ 55 kg = 0.05 mcg/kg/min. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 20.6 mL/hr — 20.6 mL/hr is double the dose (0.1 mcg/kg/min).
• 1.9 mL/hr — 1.9 mL/hr is far too low.
• 0.2 mL/hr — 0.2 mL/hr is a units error.

⭐ Key point: Formula: mL/hr = (mcg/kg/min × kg × 60) ÷ mcg/mL. Adrenaline should be given through a central line where possible and titrated to the target MAP.$x$, null),
(17, $x$Dopamine is ordered at 10 mcg/kg/min for a 60 kg patient. What is this dose expressed in mg/hr?$x$, $x$36 mg/hr$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 36 mg/hr
To convert micrograms per kg per MINUTE into milligrams per HOUR: STEP 1 — Per minute for the whole patient: 10 mcg/kg/min × 60 kg = 600 micrograms/min. STEP 2 — Per hour: 600 × 60 = 36,000 micrograms/hr. STEP 3 — Convert to milligrams (÷1,000): 36,000 ÷ 1,000 = 36 mg/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 60 mg/hr — 60 mg/hr is a calculation error.
• 3.6 mg/hr — 3.6 mg/hr is ten times too small (a decimal place error).
• 6 mg/hr — 6 mg/hr forgets one multiplication by 60 (it is mg per 10 minutes).

⭐ Key point: Remember the conversions: 1 mg = 1,000 micrograms; 1 hour = 60 minutes. Many drug errors come from mixing up mg and micrograms — write "micrograms" in full, not "µg", to avoid confusion.$x$, null),
(18, $x$Metronidazole infusion is available as 500 mg in 100 mL. The prescription is 400 mg. What volume should be given?$x$, $x$80 mL$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 80 mL
VOLUME = (DOSE WANTED ÷ DOSE IN STOCK) × STOCK VOLUME. Here: (400 mg ÷ 500 mg) × 100 mL = 0.8 × 100 = 80 mL. Check: 500 mg in 100 mL = 5 mg/mL; 400 mg ÷ 5 mg/mL = 80 mL. ✓ So 20 mL of the bag must be removed and discarded (or set the pump to deliver only 80 mL).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 125 mL — 125 mL is stock ÷ wanted × volume — the formula upside down.
• 0.8 mL — 0.8 mL forgets to multiply by 100 mL.
• 160 mL — 160 mL would give 800 mg — double.

⭐ Key point: When giving part of a bag, set the "volume to be infused" (VTBI) on the pump carefully so the pump stops at the right amount.$x$, null),
(19, $x$1,000 mL of Hartmann's solution is prescribed to run over 8 hours using a giving set with a drop factor of 15 drops/mL. What is the correct drip rate?$x$, $x$31 drops/min$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 31 drops/min
DROPS PER MINUTE = (VOLUME × DROP FACTOR) ÷ TIME IN MINUTES. Here: (1,000 × 15) ÷ (8 × 60) = 15,000 ÷ 480 = 31.25, which rounds to 31 drops/min. Check: 125 mL/hr ÷ 4 = 31. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 42 drops/min — 42 drops/min would finish in 6 hours.
• 125 drops/min — 125 drops/min is the rate in mL/HOUR.
• 62 drops/min — 62 drops/min is double.

⭐ Key point: Hartmann's (compound sodium lactate) is a balanced crystalloid often preferred over 0.9% saline for large-volume resuscitation (less hyperchloraemic acidosis).$x$, null),
(20, $x$A patient's blood pressure is 138/94 mmHg. What is the mean arterial pressure (MAP)?$x$, $x$109 mmHg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 109 mmHg
MEAN ARTERIAL PRESSURE (MAP) = (SYSTOLIC + 2 × DIASTOLIC) ÷ 3. Here: (138 + 2 × 94) ÷ 3 = (138 + 188) ÷ 3 = 326 ÷ 3 = 108.7, which rounds to 109 mmHg. Diastole counts twice because the heart spends about two-thirds of each cycle in diastole.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 123 mmHg — 123 mmHg is too high (close to systolic).
• 44 mmHg — 44 mmHg is the PULSE PRESSURE (138 − 94).
• 116 mmHg — 116 mmHg is the simple average ((138 + 94) ÷ 2) — not the correct MAP formula.

⭐ Key point: MAP below 65 mmHg means organs (kidneys, brain, heart) may not be perfused adequately.$x$, null),
(21, $x$40 mmol of potassium chloride in 1000 mL normal saline is prescribed over 5 hours. What is the pump rate and how much potassium is given each hour?$x$, $x$200 mL/hr, giving 8 mmol K⁺ per hour$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 200 mL/hr, giving 8 mmol K⁺ per hour
STEP 1 — Pump rate: 1,000 mL ÷ 5 hours = 200 mL/hr. STEP 2 — Potassium per hour: 40 mmol ÷ 5 hours = 8 mmol/hr. Check: 40 mmol/1,000 mL = 0.04 mmol/mL × 200 mL/hr = 8 mmol/hr. ✓ 8 mmol/hr is within the usual safe limit for a peripheral line (no more than 10 mmol/hr).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 200 mL/hr, giving 4 mmol K⁺ per hour — 4 mmol/hr is wrong — half the true amount.
• 400 mL/hr, giving 16 mmol K⁺ per hour — 400 mL/hr would run the bag in 2.5 hours, giving 16 mmol/hr — too fast for a peripheral line.
• 3.3 mL/hr, giving 8 mmol K⁺ per hour — 3.3 mL/hr is far too slow; it would take about 300 hours.

⭐ Key point: Always give IV potassium by pump, never as a push. Check potassium and creatinine before and during replacement; correct magnesium too.$x$, null),
(22, $x$Ibuprofen is prescribed at 10 mg/kg per dose (maximum single dose 400 mg) for a patient weighing 60 kg. What single dose should be given?$x$, $x$400 mg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 400 mg
STEP 1 — Weight-based dose: 10 mg/kg × 60 kg = 600 mg. STEP 2 — Compare with the MAXIMUM SINGLE DOSE: 400 mg. Since 600 mg is MORE than 400 mg, the dose must be CAPPED at 400 mg. Exceeding the maximum increases the risk of stomach bleeding, kidney injury and other side effects without extra benefit.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 200 mg — 200 mg is too low (half the maximum).
• 660 mg — 660 mg exceeds the maximum.
• 600 mg — 600 mg is the weight-based dose, but it exceeds the 400 mg maximum.

⭐ Key point: Always check two things in weight-based dosing: the calculation AND the maximum dose.$x$, null),
(23, $x$20 mmol of potassium chloride in 1000 mL normal saline is prescribed over 4 hours. What is the pump rate and how much potassium is given each hour?$x$, $x$250 mL/hr, giving 5 mmol K⁺ per hour$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 250 mL/hr, giving 5 mmol K⁺ per hour
STEP 1 — Pump rate: 1,000 mL ÷ 4 hours = 250 mL/hr. STEP 2 — Potassium per hour: 20 mmol ÷ 4 hours = 5 mmol/hr. Check: 20 mmol/1,000 mL = 0.02 mmol/mL × 250 = 5 mmol/hr. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 4.2 mL/hr, giving 5 mmol K⁺ per hour — 4.2 mL/hr is far too slow.
• 500 mL/hr, giving 10 mmol K⁺ per hour — 500 mL/hr would run the bag in 2 hours, giving 10 mmol/hr.
• 250 mL/hr, giving 2 mmol K⁺ per hour — 2 mmol/hr is wrong.

⭐ Key point: A large volume with dilute potassium (20 mmol/L) is suitable for peripheral use, but watch for fluid overload in heart or kidney failure.$x$, null),
(24, $x$A nurse is asked to prepare an infusion so that a patient weighing 90 kg receives dopamine at 3 mcg/kg/min. The available bag is 800 mg in 500 mL D5W. What is the required infusion rate in mL/hr?$x$, $x$10.1 mL/hr$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 10.1 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 800 mg in 500 mL = 1.6 mg/mL = 1,600 micrograms/mL. STEP 2 — Dose per hour: 3 mcg/kg/min × 90 kg × 60 = 16,200 micrograms/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 16,200 ÷ 1,600 = 10.1 mL/hr. Check: 10.1 × 1,600 = 16,160 ≈ 16,200. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 20.3 mL/hr — 20.3 mL/hr is double the dose.
• 8.4 mL/hr — 8.4 mL/hr would give about 2.5 mcg/kg/min.
• 16.9 mL/hr — 16.9 mL/hr would give about 5 mcg/kg/min.

⭐ Key point: Low-dose dopamine (1–5 mcg/kg/min) does NOT protect the kidneys — this old idea has been disproven.$x$, null),
(25, $x$Over 24 hours a patient received 2,400 mL IV fluid and 300 mL orally. Output was urine 2,200 mL, nasogastric aspirate 0 mL and wound drain 250 mL. What is the 24-hour fluid balance?$x$, $x$+250 mL$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: +250 mL
FLUID BALANCE = TOTAL INPUT − TOTAL OUTPUT. Input: IV 2,400 + oral 300 = 2,700 mL. Output: urine 2,200 + NG 0 + drain 250 = 2,450 mL. Balance: 2,700 − 2,450 = +250 mL (slightly positive).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• +5,150 mL — +5,150 mL adds input and output together.
• +500 mL — +500 mL forgets the drain output (2,700 − 2,200).
• -250 mL — −250 mL reverses the sign.

⭐ Key point: Remember insensible losses (about 500–800 mL/day from skin and breathing, more with fever, sweating or a high respiratory rate), so a balance of +250 mL may actually be slightly negative.$x$, null),
(26, $x$Soluble insulin 50 units is made up to 50 mL with normal saline. The order is 8 units/hour. What rate should be set?$x$, $x$8 mL/hr$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 8 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 50 units in 50 mL = 1 unit per mL. STEP 2 — Rate: 8 units/hr ÷ 1 unit/mL = 8 mL/hr. With this standard 1 unit/mL dilution, the pump rate in mL/hr equals the dose in units/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 32 mL/hr — 32 mL/hr would give 32 units/hr — four times the dose; severe hypoglycaemia risk.
• 16 mL/hr — 16 mL/hr would give double.
• 4 mL/hr — 4 mL/hr would give half.

⭐ Key point: Use only an insulin syringe or pre-filled syringe when preparing insulin; write "units" in full (never "U", which can be misread as 0).$x$, null),
(27, $x$Ceftazidime 60 mg/kg/day is prescribed in divided doses every 6 hours for an infant weighing 9 kg. How much is each dose?$x$, $x$135 mg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 135 mg
STEP 1 — Daily dose: 60 mg/kg/day × 9 kg = 540 mg per day. STEP 2 — Every 6 hours = 4 doses. STEP 3 — Each dose: 540 ÷ 4 = 135 mg.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 540 mg — 540 mg is the whole day's dose — four times too much per dose.
• 15 mg — 15 mg is a calculation error.
• 108 mg — 108 mg would be dividing into 5 doses.

⭐ Key point: Paediatric doses must be calculated carefully and double-checked; small errors cause big relative overdoses in infants.$x$, null),
(28, $x$A 65 kg man with 60% TBSA burns arrives 2 hours after the injury and has had no IV fluid. Using the Parkland formula, at what rate should the first half of the fluid run?$x$, $x$1,300 mL/hr$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 1,300 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Total (Parkland): 4 × 65 × 60 = 15,600 mL in 24 hours. STEP 2 — First half: 7,800 mL in the first 8 hours from the burn. STEP 3 — Arrived 2 hours after the burn with no fluid, so 6 hours remain. STEP 4 — Rate: 7,800 ÷ 6 = 1,300 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 975 mL/hr — 975 mL/hr is 7,800 ÷ 8 — it ignores the 2 hours lost.
• 2,600 mL/hr — 2,600 mL/hr is double.
• 650 mL/hr — 650 mL/hr is the rate for the second half over 12 hours.

⭐ Key point: A 60% burn is massive; very high fluid volumes are needed, through large-bore cannulas or a central line, with an arterial line and urinary catheter for monitoring.$x$, null),
(29, $x$500 mL of normal saline is running at 50 mL/hr. How long will it take to finish?$x$, $x$10 h$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 10 h
To find how long an infusion will last, use: TIME = VOLUME ÷ RATE. Here: 500 mL ÷ 50 mL/hr = 10 hours exactly. Check: 50 mL every hour × 10 hours = 500 mL. ✓ Knowing the finish time lets the nurse plan the next bag, check that the infusion is running as expected (if much more or less fluid has gone in than expected, the pump or line may be faulty), and record accurate fluid balance.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 11 h — 11 hours is one hour too long (it would match a rate of about 45 mL/hr).
• 6 h — 6 hours would only be correct at about 83 mL/hr.
• 10 h 30 min — 10 h 30 min adds 30 minutes that are not there.

⭐ Key point: Write the start time and the expected finish time on the bag label, and check the volume remaining against the fluid chart each hour.$x$, null),
(30, $x$How many grams of glucose are in 50 mL of 10% dextrose?$x$, $x$5 g$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 5 g
A % (w/v) solution means GRAMS per 100 mL. 10% dextrose = 10 g in 100 mL. So 50 mL contains half of that: 10 ÷ 2 = 5 g of glucose. Check: 50 mL × (10 g ÷ 100 mL) = 5 g. ✓ This is useful in treating hypoglycaemia: e.g. 50 mL of 10% dextrose delivers 5 g glucose (and 50 mL of 50% dextrose delivers 25 g).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 50 g — 50 g would be in 500 mL of 10% dextrose.
• 2.5 g — 2.5 g would be in 50 mL of 5% dextrose.
• 500 g — 500 g is a gross error.

⭐ Key point: For hypoglycaemia in adults, 10% dextrose (e.g. 150–200 mL) is preferred over 50% dextrose because it is less damaging to veins.$x$, null),
(31, $x$An infusion is being given via a microdrip set, where the drop factor is 60 gtt/mL, at a pump-set rate of 45 mL/hr. If the pump failed and this had to be run by gravity, what would the drop rate be in gtt/min?$x$, $x$45 gtt/min$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 45 gtt/min
A MICRODRIP (paediatric) set delivers 60 DROPS per mL. Formula: drops/min = (mL/hr × drop factor) ÷ 60 minutes = (45 × 60) ÷ 60 = 45 drops/min. With a 60-drop set, the drops per minute ALWAYS EQUAL the mL per hour — a handy shortcut.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 75 gtt/min — 75 drops/min is a calculation error.
• 60 gtt/min — 60 drops/min is the drop factor, not the rate.
• 15 gtt/min — 15 drops/min would be the rate for a 20-drop set (45 ÷ 3).

⭐ Key point: Micro-drip sets are used for small, precise volumes (children, drug infusions) when a pump is not available.$x$, null),
(32, $x$Using the Parkland formula for a 65 kg patient with 35% TBSA burns, how much fluid should be given in the first 8 hours after the burn?$x$, $x$4,550 mL$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 4,550 mL
PARKLAND: Total 24-hour fluid = 4 × weight × %TBSA = 4 × 65 × 35 = 9,100 mL. HALF is given in the FIRST 8 HOURS from the time of the burn: 9,100 ÷ 2 = 4,550 mL. The other 4,550 mL is given over the next 16 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 9,100 mL — 9,100 mL is the whole 24-hour total.
• 2,275 mL — 2,275 mL is a quarter.
• 3,033.3 mL — 3,033.3 mL is a third.

⭐ Key point: Children also need maintenance fluid (with glucose) in addition to the Parkland volume.$x$, null),
(33, $x$Dopamine 400 mg is diluted to 250 mL and prescribed at 3 mcg/kg/min for a patient weighing 60 kg. At what rate (mL/hr) should the infusion pump be set?$x$, $x$6.8 mL/hr$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 6.8 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 400 mg in 250 mL = 1.6 mg/mL = 1,600 mcg/mL. STEP 2 — Dose per hour: 3 mcg/kg/min × 60 kg × 60 = 10,800 mcg/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 10,800 ÷ 1,600 = 6.75 ≈ 6.8 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1.1 mL/hr — 1.1 mL/hr is far too low.
• 13.5 mL/hr — 13.5 mL/hr is double.
• 0.1 mL/hr — 0.1 mL/hr is a units error.

⭐ Key point: Dopamine can cause tachyarrhythmias and tissue necrosis if it extravasates — use a central line where possible.$x$, null),
(34, $x$An adult has burns to the perineum, the whole left arm and the whole right leg. Using the Rule of Nines, what is the TBSA burned?$x$, $x$28%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 28%
RULE OF NINES (adult): PERINEUM = 1%; each WHOLE ARM = 9%; each WHOLE LEG = 18%. Here: perineum 1% + whole left arm 9% + whole right leg 18% = 28% TBSA. A 28% burn in an adult is a MAJOR burn: start IV fluid resuscitation (Parkland), insert a urinary catheter to monitor urine output hourly, give strong analgesia, keep the patient warm, and refer to a burns unit. Perineal involvement adds a high infection risk.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 32.5% — 32.5% adds an extra 4.5% (half an arm).
• 37% — 37% adds an extra 9%.
• 19% — 19% leaves out the arm.

⭐ Key point: Re-assess the burn size after the wounds have been cleaned — soot and dirt can make areas look burned when they are not, and blisters may hide deeper burns.$x$, null),
(35, $x$Over 24 hours a patient received 2,400 mL IV fluid and 300 mL orally. Output was urine 1,500 mL, nasogastric aspirate 400 mL and wound drain 0 mL. What is the 24-hour fluid balance?$x$, $x$+800 mL$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: +800 mL
FLUID BALANCE = INPUT − OUTPUT. Input: IV 2,400 + oral 300 = 2,700 mL. Output: urine 1,500 + NG 400 + drain 0 = 1,900 mL. Balance: 2,700 − 1,900 = +800 mL.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• -800 mL — −800 mL reverses the sign.
• +1,200 mL — +1,200 mL forgets the NG losses (2,700 − 1,500).
• +4,600 mL — +4,600 mL adds input and output.

⭐ Key point: NG aspirate is rich in sodium, potassium, chloride and acid — large losses need replacement and can cause metabolic alkalosis.$x$, null),
(36, $x$A 70 kg patient with 50% TBSA burns requires fluid resuscitation. The Parkland formula calculates the 24-hour fluid as:$x$, $x$14,000 mL$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 14,000 mL
PARKLAND FORMULA: 24-hour fluid = 4 mL × weight (kg) × %TBSA = 4 × 70 × 50 = 14,000 mL (14 litres). Half (7,000 mL) is given in the first 8 hours after the burn, and the other half over the next 16 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 3,500 mL — 3,500 mL is a quarter.
• 28,000 mL — 28,000 mL is double (8 mL formula).
• 7,000 mL — 7,000 mL is only the first-8-hour half.

⭐ Key point: Over-resuscitation ("fluid creep") causes oedema, abdominal compartment syndrome and lung problems — titrate to urine output.$x$, null),
(37, $x$An 80 kg patient has passed 240 mL of urine in the last 3 hours. What is the urine output and how is it interpreted?$x$, $x$1 mL/kg/hr — adequate$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 1 mL/kg/hr — adequate
URINE OUTPUT (mL/kg/hr) = VOLUME ÷ HOURS ÷ WEIGHT. Here: 240 mL ÷ 3 hours = 80 mL/hr; 80 ÷ 80 kg = 1 mL/kg/hr. The minimum acceptable adult urine output is 0.5 mL/kg/hr, so 1 mL/kg/hr is ADEQUATE.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 80 mL/kg/hr — adequate — 80 mL/kg/hr is a units error.
• 1 mL/kg/hr — oliguria — 1 mL/kg/hr is correct, but it is NOT oliguria.
• 3 mL/kg/hr — adequate — 3 mL/kg/hr is a calculation error.

⭐ Key point: Oliguria = less than 0.5 mL/kg/hr for 6 hours or more (KDIGO AKI criterion).$x$, null),
(38, $x$Ampicillin 30 mg/kg/day is prescribed in divided doses every 6 hours for an infant weighing 9 kg. How much is each dose?$x$, $x$67.5 mg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 67.5 mg
STEP 1 — Daily dose: 30 mg/kg/day × 9 kg = 270 mg per day. STEP 2 — "Every 6 hours" means 4 doses in 24 hours (24 ÷ 6 = 4). STEP 3 — Each dose: 270 ÷ 4 = 67.5 mg. Check: 67.5 × 4 = 270. ✓ In infants, small arithmetic errors make large relative overdoses, so every paediatric calculation should be independently double-checked.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 270 mg — 270 mg is the WHOLE day's dose — giving it every 6 hours would be four times too much.
• 7.5 mg — 7.5 mg is a calculation error — far too little to treat infection.
• 54 mg — 54 mg would be the dose if divided into 5 doses.

⭐ Key point: "mg/kg/DAY" = calculate the daily total, then divide by the number of doses. "mg/kg/DOSE" = multiply by weight only. Read the prescription carefully.$x$, null),
(39, $x$Aminophylline is ordered as a loading dose of 5 mg/kg over 30 minutes for a 60 kg patient, supplied as 250 mg in 100 mL. What volume should be given, and at what rate over the 30 minutes?$x$, $x$120 mL total, at a rate of 240 mL/hr$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 120 mL total, at a rate of 240 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Dose: 5 mg/kg × 60 kg = 300 mg. STEP 2 — Concentration: 250 mg in 100 mL = 2.5 mg/mL. STEP 3 — Volume: 300 ÷ 2.5 = 120 mL. STEP 4 — Rate over 30 minutes: 120 mL ÷ 0.5 hr = 240 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 60 mL total, at a rate of 120 mL/hr — 60 mL is only 150 mg.
• 300 mL total, at a rate of 600 mL/hr — 300 mL is the dose in mg confused with mL.
• 120 mL total, at a rate of 60 mL/hr — 120 mL at 60 mL/hr would take 2 hours, not 30 minutes.

⭐ Key point: Aminophylline has a narrow therapeutic range — monitor for nausea, arrhythmias and seizures, and omit the loading dose if the patient already takes theophylline.$x$, null),
(40, $x$An adult has burns to the whole left leg, the whole right arm, the front of the trunk (chest and abdomen) and the perineum. Using the Rule of Nines, what is the TBSA burned?$x$, $x$46%$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 46%
RULE OF NINES (adult): WHOLE LEFT LEG = 18%; WHOLE RIGHT ARM = 9%; FRONT of the TRUNK (chest and abdomen) = 18%; PERINEUM = 1%. Total: 18 + 9 + 18 + 1 = 46% TBSA. A burn of nearly half the body surface is life-threatening: massive fluid shifts, shock, hypothermia, infection and a very high metabolic rate. These patients need ICU or burns-unit care, large-bore IV access, high-volume Parkland resuscitation, airway assessment, and early nutrition.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 37% — 37% misses 9% (e.g. the arm).
• 55% — 55% adds an extra 9%.
• 50.5% — 50.5% adds an extra 4.5%.

⭐ Key point: Mortality rises with burn size, age and inhalation injury. Circumferential chest burns can restrict breathing and may need escharotomy.$x$, null),
(41, $x$Dobutamine 250 mg is diluted to 250 mL and prescribed at 2.5 mcg/kg/min for a patient weighing 60 kg. At what rate (mL/hr) should the infusion pump be set?$x$, $x$9 mL/hr$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 9 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 250 mg in 250 mL = 1 mg/mL = 1,000 mcg/mL. STEP 2 — Dose per hour: 2.5 mcg/kg/min × 60 kg × 60 = 9,000 mcg/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 9,000 ÷ 1,000 = 9 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1.5 mL/hr — 1.5 mL/hr is far too low.
• 0.1 mL/hr — 0.1 mL/hr is a units error.
• 18 mL/hr — 18 mL/hr is double.

⭐ Key point: Shortcut for 1 mg/mL: mL/hr = mcg/kg/min × weight × 0.06 (2.5 × 60 × 0.06 = 9).$x$, null),
(42, $x$Glyceryl trinitrate (GTN) 50 mg in 500 mL is ordered at 8 mcg/min. What pump rate (mL/hr) will deliver this dose?$x$, $x$4.8 mL/hr$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 4.8 mL/hr
GTN is prescribed in MICROGRAMS per MINUTE, but pumps run in mL per HOUR. STEP 1 — Concentration: 50 mg in 500 mL = 0.1 mg/mL = 100 micrograms/mL. STEP 2 — Dose per HOUR: 8 micrograms/min × 60 = 480 micrograms/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 480 ÷ 100 = 4.8 mL/hr. Check: 4.8 mL/hr × 100 = 480 mcg/hr ÷ 60 = 8 mcg/min. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.1 mL/hr — 0.1 mL/hr is the CONCENTRATION (mg/mL), not a rate.
• 2.4 mL/hr — 2.4 mL/hr gives only 4 mcg/min — half the dose.
• 9.6 mL/hr — 9.6 mL/hr gives 16 mcg/min — double the dose, risking hypotension.

⭐ Key point: Shortcut for a 100 mcg/mL bag: mL/hr = mcg/min × 0.6. Monitor BP every 5–15 minutes while titrating GTN.$x$, null),
(43, $x$750 mL of normal saline is running at 125 mL/hr. How long will it take to finish?$x$, $x$6 h$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 6 h
TIME = VOLUME ÷ RATE. Here: 750 mL ÷ 125 mL/hr = 6 hours exactly. Check: 125 × 6 = 750. ✓ The nurse can use this to plan when to prepare the next bag and to check that the correct amount has been given at each hour.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10 h — 10 hours would be at 75 mL/hr.
• 6 h 30 min — 6 h 30 min adds 30 minutes that are not there.
• 7 h — 7 hours would be at about 107 mL/hr.

⭐ Key point: Common error: confusing the RATE (mL/hr) with the TIME. Always write the units next to each number.$x$, null),
(44, $x$Propofol (10 mg/mL) is ordered at 30 mcg/kg/min for an 80 kg patient. What infusion rate in mL/hr should be programmed?$x$, $x$14.4 mL/hr$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 14.4 mL/hr
Propofol 1% = 10 mg/mL. STEP 1 — Dose per hour: 30 mcg/kg/min × 80 kg × 60 = 144,000 mcg/hr = 144 mg/hr. STEP 2 — Rate: 144 mg/hr ÷ 10 mg/mL = 14.4 mL/hr. (Equivalent to 1.8 mg/kg/hr.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 24 mL/hr — 24 mL/hr would be 50 mcg/kg/min.
• 7.2 mL/hr — 7.2 mL/hr is half the dose.
• 28.8 mL/hr — 28.8 mL/hr is double.

⭐ Key point: Propofol provides about 1.1 kcal/mL (fat) — count it in nutrition. Watch for hypotension, and for propofol infusion syndrome at high doses for long periods. Change the infusion set every 12 hours (lipid supports bacterial growth).$x$, null),
(45, $x$A 50 kg man with 40% TBSA burns arrives 4 hours after the injury and has had no IV fluid. Using the Parkland formula, at what rate should the first half of the fluid run?$x$, $x$1,000 mL/hr$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 1,000 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Total (Parkland): 4 × 50 × 40 = 8,000 mL. STEP 2 — First half: 4,000 mL in the first 8 hours from the burn. STEP 3 — Arrived 4 hours after the burn with no fluid, so 4 hours remain. STEP 4 — Rate: 4,000 ÷ 4 = 1,000 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 333.3 mL/hr — 333.3 mL/hr is the second-half rate (4,000 ÷ 12).
• 500 mL/hr — 500 mL/hr is 4,000 ÷ 8 — ignores the 4 hours lost.
• 2,000 mL/hr — 2,000 mL/hr is double.

⭐ Key point: Delayed resuscitation increases the risk of kidney injury and shock; start fluids as soon as possible after a major burn.$x$, null),
(46, $x$A 50 kg patient has passed 200 mL of urine in the last 3 hours. What is the urine output and how is it interpreted?$x$, $x$1.33 mL/kg/hr — adequate$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 1.33 mL/kg/hr — adequate
URINE OUTPUT = VOLUME ÷ HOURS ÷ WEIGHT = 200 mL ÷ 3 hr ÷ 50 kg = 66.7 mL/hr ÷ 50 = 1.33 mL/kg/hr. This is above the minimum of 0.5 mL/kg/hr, so it is ADEQUATE.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 4 mL/kg/hr — adequate — 4 mL/kg/hr is a calculation error.
• 1.33 mL/kg/hr — oliguria — 1.33 is correct, but it is NOT oliguria.
• 66.7 mL/kg/hr — adequate — 66.7 mL/kg/hr confuses mL/hr with mL/kg/hr.

⭐ Key point: In burns, the target is 0.5–1 mL/kg/hr (adults); higher output may mean over-resuscitation.$x$, null),
(47, $x$A 60 kg man with 20% TBSA burns arrives 1 hour after the injury and has had no IV fluid. Using the Parkland formula, at what rate should the first half of the fluid run?$x$, $x$342.9 mL/hr$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 342.9 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Total (Parkland): 4 × 60 × 20 = 4,800 mL. STEP 2 — First half: 2,400 mL in the first 8 hours. STEP 3 — Arrived 1 hour after the burn, so 7 hours remain. STEP 4 — Rate: 2,400 ÷ 7 = 342.9 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 685.7 mL/hr — 685.7 mL/hr is double.
• 300 mL/hr — 300 mL/hr is 2,400 ÷ 8 — ignores the lost hour.
• 200 mL/hr — 200 mL/hr is the 24-hour average.

⭐ Key point: In adults, formal fluid resuscitation is usually needed for burns above 15–20% TBSA.$x$, null),
(48, $x$Hydrocortisone (reconstituted) is available as 100 mg in 2 mL. The prescription is 50 mg. What volume should be given?$x$, $x$1 mL$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 1 mL
VOLUME = (DOSE WANTED ÷ DOSE IN STOCK) × STOCK VOLUME. Here: (50 mg ÷ 100 mg) × 2 mL = 0.5 × 2 = 1 mL. Check: 100 mg is in 2 mL, so 50 mg (half) is in 1 mL (half). ✓ HYDROCORTISONE is used in ICU for refractory septic shock (e.g. 50 mg every 6 hours), adrenal crisis (100 mg IV immediately), and as stress-dose cover in patients on long-term steroids.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.5 mL — 0.5 mL would give only 25 mg.
• 2 mL — 2 mL would give 100 mg — double the dose.
• 4 mL — 4 mL is the formula upside down (stock ÷ wanted).

⭐ Key point: Monitor blood glucose during hydrocortisone therapy (it raises glucose) and watch for infection.$x$, null),
(49, $x$Dobutamine is mixed as 250 mg in 250 mL D5W and prescribed at 5 mcg/kg/min for an 80 kg patient. What infusion rate in mL/hr should be set?$x$, $x$24 mL/hr$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 24 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 250 mg in 250 mL = 1 mg/mL = 1,000 micrograms/mL. STEP 2 — Dose per hour: 5 mcg/kg/min × 80 kg × 60 min = 24,000 micrograms/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 24,000 ÷ 1,000 = 24 mL/hr. Check: 24 × 1,000 = 24,000 ÷ 60 = 400 mcg/min ÷ 80 = 5 mcg/kg/min. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 12 mL/hr — 12 mL/hr gives 2.5 mcg/kg/min — half.
• 48 mL/hr — 48 mL/hr gives 10 mcg/kg/min — double.
• 20 mL/hr — 20 mL/hr gives about 4.2 mcg/kg/min.

⭐ Key point: With a 1 mg/mL solution: mL/hr = mcg/kg/min × weight × 0.06 (5 × 80 × 0.06 = 24). Watch for tachycardia and arrhythmias on dobutamine.$x$, null),
(50, $x$Noradrenaline 8 mg in 250 mL is ordered at 5 mcg/min. What pump rate (mL/hr) will deliver this dose?$x$, $x$9.4 mL/hr$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 9.4 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 8 mg in 250 mL = 0.032 mg/mL = 32 micrograms/mL. STEP 2 — Dose per hour: 5 mcg/min × 60 = 300 micrograms/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 300 ÷ 32 = 9.375 ≈ 9.4 mL/hr. Check: 9.4 × 32 = 300.8 mcg/hr ÷ 60 ≈ 5 mcg/min. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.2 mL/hr — 0.2 mL/hr is a units error.
• 4.7 mL/hr — 4.7 mL/hr is half the dose.
• 18.8 mL/hr — 18.8 mL/hr is double the dose.

⭐ Key point: Noradrenaline should run through a dedicated central line lumen; never flush that lumen, as it would give a bolus.$x$, null),
(51, $x$Digoxin loading (digitalizing) dose is calculated as 10 mcg/kg for a 68 kg patient, with 50% given as the first dose and the remainder split into two further doses. What is the total loading dose, and what is the first dose given?$x$, $x$Total 680 mcg; first dose 340 mcg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Total 680 mcg; first dose 340 mcg
STEP 1 — Total loading dose: 10 mcg/kg × 68 kg = 680 micrograms. STEP 2 — First dose = 50% of the total: 680 × 0.5 = 340 micrograms. The remaining 340 micrograms is split into two doses of 170 micrograms, usually given 6–8 hours apart.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Total 340 mcg; first dose 170 mcg — Total 340 is half the correct total.
• Total 680 mcg; first dose 680 mcg — A first dose of 680 gives the whole load at once.
• Total 680 mcg; first dose 170 mcg — A first dose of 170 is only a quarter.

⭐ Key point: Check potassium, kidney function and the ECG before digoxin; hypokalaemia increases toxicity.$x$, null),
(52, $x$Ceftriaxone (reconstituted) is available as 1,000 mg in 10 mL. The prescription is 250 mg. What volume should be given?$x$, $x$2.5 mL$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 2.5 mL
VOLUME = (DOSE WANTED ÷ DOSE IN STOCK) × STOCK VOLUME. Here: (250 mg ÷ 1,000 mg) × 10 mL = 0.25 × 10 = 2.5 mL. Check: 1,000 mg in 10 mL = 100 mg/mL; 250 ÷ 100 = 2.5 mL. ✓ Since 250 mg is a quarter of the vial, the volume is a quarter of 10 mL.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.2 mL — 0.2 mL would give only 20 mg.
• 5 mL — 5 mL would give 500 mg — double.
• 40 mL — 40 mL is the formula upside down.

⭐ Key point: Ceftriaxone must NOT be mixed with, or given at the same time through the same line as, calcium-containing solutions (e.g. Hartmann's) — especially in newborns, where fatal precipitates have occurred.$x$, null),
(53, $x$A 12 kg child in shock is prescribed a 20 mL/kg bolus of normal saline. What volume should be given?$x$, $x$240 mL$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 240 mL
Fluid boluses in children: VOLUME = mL/kg × WEIGHT. Here: 20 mL/kg × 12 kg = 240 mL of 0.9% saline. Check: 20 × 12 = 240. ✓ Give it rapidly, then REASSESS heart rate, capillary refill, BP, conscious level and urine output, and look for signs of fluid overload (crackles, enlarged liver, raised JVP) before giving more.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 360 mL — 360 mL is 30 mL/kg — more than prescribed.
• 480 mL — 480 mL is 40 mL/kg — double.
• 120 mL — 120 mL is only 10 mL/kg — half.

⭐ Key point: If shock persists after 40–60 mL/kg of fluid, start inotropes/vasopressors and involve paediatric intensive care.$x$, null),
(54, $x$An oxygen cylinder contains 1,360 litres of oxygen. The patient needs 5 L/min during transfer. Approximately how long will it last?$x$, $x$About 272 minutes$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: About 272 minutes
CYLINDER DURATION: TIME = AMOUNT OF OXYGEN ÷ FLOW RATE. Here: 1,360 L ÷ 5 L/min = 272 minutes (about 4 hours 32 minutes). Check: 5 × 272 = 1,360. ✓ In practice, keep a safety margin — do not plan to empty the cylinder completely — and remember that transfers can be delayed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• About 113 minutes — 113 minutes would be at 12 L/min.
• About 45 minutes — 45 minutes would be at about 30 L/min.
• About 544 minutes — 544 minutes would be at 2.5 L/min.

⭐ Key point: Always check the cylinder contents before a transfer and carry a spare. Ventilators driven by oxygen use extra gas.$x$, null),
(55, $x$A head-injured patient has BP 100/55 mmHg and ICP 10 mmHg. What is the cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP)?$x$, $x$60 mmHg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 60 mmHg
STEP 1 — MAP = (100 + 2 × 55) ÷ 3 = 210 ÷ 3 = 70 mmHg. STEP 2 — CPP = MAP − ICP = 70 − 10 = 60 mmHg. This is at the LOWER limit of the usual target (60–70 mmHg) — the BP is low, so raising MAP (fluids, vasopressors) may be needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 90 mmHg — 90 mmHg is systolic minus ICP.
• 80 mmHg — 80 mmHg is a calculation error.
• 45 mmHg — 45 mmHg is diastolic minus ICP.

⭐ Key point: Avoid hypotension in brain injury — even one episode of systolic BP below 90 mmHg worsens outcome.$x$, null),
(56, $x$Soluble insulin 100 units is made up to 100 mL with normal saline. The order is 6 units/hour. What rate should be set?$x$, $x$6 mL/hr$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 6 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 100 units in 100 mL = 1 unit per mL. STEP 2 — Rate: 6 units/hr ÷ 1 unit/mL = 6 mL/hr. With this standard "1 unit per mL" dilution, the pump rate (mL/hr) always equals the dose (units/hr), which reduces calculation errors.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 12 mL/hr — 12 mL/hr gives double the dose — risk of hypoglycaemia.
• 3 mL/hr — 3 mL/hr gives half.
• 24 mL/hr — 24 mL/hr gives four times the dose — dangerous hypoglycaemia.

⭐ Key point: Check blood glucose hourly during insulin infusions, and monitor potassium — insulin moves potassium into cells.$x$, null),
(57, $x$What is the 24-hour maintenance fluid requirement for a child weighing 28 kg (Holliday–Segar method)?$x$, $x$1,660 mL$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 1,660 mL
HOLLIDAY–SEGAR (24-hour): first 10 kg × 100 mL = 1,000 mL; next 10 kg × 50 mL = 500 mL; remaining 8 kg × 20 mL = 160 mL. Total: 1,000 + 500 + 160 = 1,660 mL/day.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2,800 mL — 2,800 mL is 100 mL/kg for all 28 kg.
• 1,400 mL — 1,400 mL is 50 mL/kg for all.
• 1,860 mL — 1,860 mL miscalculates the last part.

⭐ Key point: Equivalent hourly rate by the 4-2-1 rule: 40 + 20 + 8 = 68 mL/hr.$x$, null),
(58, $x$A patient's blood pressure is 176/56 mmHg. What is the mean arterial pressure (MAP)?$x$, $x$96 mmHg$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 96 mmHg
MAP = (176 + 2 × 56) ÷ 3 = (176 + 112) ÷ 3 = 288 ÷ 3 = 96 mmHg. Note the wide pulse pressure (176 − 56 = 120), seen in aortic regurgitation, stiff arteries in the elderly, or sepsis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 136 mmHg — 136 mmHg is too high.
• 116 mmHg — 116 mmHg is the simple average.
• 120 mmHg — 120 mmHg is the pulse pressure.

⭐ Key point: A very low diastolic pressure can reduce coronary perfusion even if MAP looks acceptable.$x$, null),
(59, $x$Arterial blood gas for a patient on high-dose furosemide with a low potassium pH 7.50, PaCO2 54 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 38 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$x$, $x$Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.50: ALKALOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 38 is HIGH: METABOLIC ALKALOSIS (furosemide causes loss of H⁺, Cl⁻ and K⁺, plus volume contraction). STEP 3 — PaCO2 54 is HIGH: the lungs are compensating by retaining CO2, but the pH is still high. Answer: PARTIALLY COMPENSATED METABOLIC ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uncompensated metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would have a LOW pH and LOW HCO3⁻.
• Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis — Mixed acidosis would have a LOW pH.
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — Uncompensated would have a NORMAL PaCO2.

⭐ Key point: Replace potassium and chloride; consider acetazolamide in persistent diuretic-induced alkalosis.$x$, null),
(60, $x$Arterial blood gas for a patient who has been at high altitude for several days pH 7.48, PaCO2 26 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 17 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$x$, $x$Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis
At HIGH ALTITUDE, low oxygen makes people breathe faster, blowing off CO2: RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS. After SEVERAL DAYS, the kidneys excrete bicarbonate to compensate. STEP 1 — pH 7.48: ALKALOSIS. STEP 2 — PaCO2 26 LOW: RESPIRATORY. STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 17 LOW: kidneys compensating, but pH still above 7.45. Answer: PARTIALLY COMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis needs a HIGH PaCO2.
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis needs a HIGH HCO3⁻.
• Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Uncompensated would have a normal HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: Acetazolamide speeds up this renal compensation and is used to prevent altitude sickness.$x$, null),
(61, $x$Which acid–base disturbance is most likely to result from the following? Over-ventilation on a mechanical ventilator.$x$, $x$Respiratory alkalosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Respiratory alkalosis
A mechanical ventilator controls how much CO2 is removed through MINUTE VENTILATION (rate × tidal volume). OVER-VENTILATION — rate or tidal volume set too high — removes TOO MUCH CO2, so the PaCO2 FALLS and the pH RISES: RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS. Effects include cerebral vasoconstriction (less blood flow to the brain), low potassium, low ionised calcium (tingling, spasm), and arrhythmias.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis results from UNDER-ventilation (CO2 builds up).
• Metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis is caused by acid gain or bicarbonate loss, not ventilator settings.
• Metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis is caused by acid loss (vomiting, NG suction) or bicarbonate gain.

⭐ Key point: Correct by reducing the rate or tidal volume, and check whether the patient is triggering extra breaths because of pain, anxiety or fever.$x$, null),
(62, $x$Arterial blood gas for a patient with septic shock who is now exhausted and hypoventilating pH 7.10, PaCO2 56 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 14 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$x$, $x$Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.10: severe ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 14 LOW (METABOLIC acidosis — lactic acid from septic shock) AND PaCO2 56 HIGH (RESPIRATORY acidosis — exhausted, hypoventilating). Both push the pH DOWN. Answer: MIXED RESPIRATORY AND METABOLIC ACIDOSIS. (Winter's: expected PaCO2 = 1.5 × 14 + 8 = 29 ± 2; actual 56 is far higher.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — Partial compensation would need a LOW PaCO2.
• Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis has a high pH.
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis has a high pH.

⭐ Key point: This patient is tiring and needs urgent ventilatory support (intubation) along with sepsis treatment.$x$, null),
(63, $x$Which condition can cause a high anion gap metabolic acidosis?$x$, $x$Lactic acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Lactic acidosis
LACTIC ACIDOSIS occurs when tissues lack oxygen (shock, sepsis, cardiac arrest, seizures, severe anaemia) and switch to ANAEROBIC metabolism, producing LACTIC ACID. Lactate is an "UNMEASURED ANION": it uses up bicarbonate without raising chloride, so the ANION GAP RISES — a HIGH ANION GAP METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperchloremic acidosis — Hyperchloraemic acidosis has a NORMAL anion gap (chloride rises to replace lost bicarbonate).
• Renal tubular acidosis — Renal tubular acidosis causes a NORMAL anion gap acidosis.
• Vomiting — Vomiting causes metabolic ALKALOSIS.

⭐ Key point: Lactate above 2 mmol/L is abnormal; above 4 mmol/L indicates severe hypoperfusion. Falling lactate shows successful resuscitation.$x$, null),
(64, $x$A patient's arterial blood gas shows a metabolic acidosis with a raised anion gap in the setting of acute kidney injury. This is best explained by:$x$, $x$Accumulation of unmeasured acids (e.g. sulfates, phosphates) that the failing kidneys cannot excrete$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Accumulation of unmeasured acids (e.g. sulfates, phosphates) that the failing kidneys cannot excrete
In ACUTE KIDNEY INJURY, the kidneys cannot excrete daily acids (sulfates, phosphates, urate and other organic acids) or regenerate bicarbonate. These UNMEASURED ACIDS ACCUMULATE, causing a HIGH ANION GAP METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No relationship between AKI and acid-base status — AKI clearly affects acid-base status.
• Loss of bicarbonate through the kidneys alone with no other cause — Bicarbonate loss alone would give a normal anion gap.
• Excess bicarbonate production — AKI reduces, not increases, bicarbonate production.

⭐ Key point: Severe acidosis (e.g. pH below 7.15) not responding to treatment is an indication for dialysis.$x$, null),
(65, $x$Arterial blood gas for a patient on continuous nasogastric suction for 3 days pH 7.53, PaCO2 37 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 38 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$x$, $x$Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.53: ALKALOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 38 HIGH: METABOLIC (NG suction removing acid). STEP 3 — PaCO2 37 NORMAL: no compensation. Answer: UNCOMPENSATED METABOLIC ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — Fully compensated metabolic acidosis would have a normal pH and low HCO3⁻.
• Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis — Partial compensation would need a HIGH PaCO2.
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would have a low pH.

⭐ Key point: Replace NG losses with saline and potassium; consider a PPI to reduce acid loss.$x$, null),
(66, $x$Arterial blood gas for a patient with acute severe asthma who is now tiring with a quiet chest pH 7.31, PaCO2 65 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 26 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$x$, $x$Uncompensated respiratory acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated respiratory acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.31: ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — PaCO2 65 HIGH: RESPIRATORY (tiring asthmatic, not breathing enough). STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 26 NORMAL: no renal compensation yet (acute). Answer: UNCOMPENSATED (ACUTE) RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS. In asthma, a RISING CO2 and a QUIET CHEST are signs of life-threatening exhaustion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis has a high pH.
• Partially compensated respiratory acidosis — Partial compensation would need a HIGH HCO3⁻.
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis needs a LOW HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: Call ICU urgently — this patient may need intubation.$x$, null),
(67, $x$Arterial blood gas for a patient admitted minutes ago after a prolonged seizure pH 7.26, PaCO2 40 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 18 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$x$, $x$Uncompensated metabolic acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.26: ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 18 is LOW: a METABOLIC cause. During a prolonged seizure, the muscles work intensely and produce LACTIC ACID. STEP 3 — PaCO2 40 is NORMAL: the lungs have NOT yet compensated (by breathing faster). Answer: UNCOMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Fully compensated respiratory acidosis would need a HIGH PaCO2 and HIGH HCO3⁻ with a normal pH.
• Uncompensated respiratory acidosis — Uncompensated respiratory acidosis needs a HIGH PaCO2.
• Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — FULLY compensated metabolic acidosis would have a normal pH with a LOW PaCO2.

⭐ Key point: Post-seizure lactic acidosis usually clears within 1–2 hours once the seizure stops — bicarbonate is not needed. Focus on airway, breathing, glucose and stopping seizures.$x$, null),
(68, $x$Arterial blood gas for a patient admitted minutes ago after a prolonged seizure pH 7.24, PaCO2 41 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 13 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$x$, $x$Uncompensated metabolic acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.24: ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 13 is LOW: METABOLIC (lactic acid from the seizure). STEP 3 — PaCO2 41 is NORMAL: no respiratory compensation yet. Answer: UNCOMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS. (Winter's formula would expect a PaCO2 of 1.5 × 13 + 8 = 27.5 ± 2; the patient is not yet compensating, perhaps because they are post-ictal and breathing slowly.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — FULLY compensated would have a normal pH and low PaCO2.
• Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis has a high pH and high HCO3⁻.
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis needs a high PaCO2 as the primary change.

⭐ Key point: Post-ictal patients may hypoventilate — keep the airway clear (recovery position, suction) and give oxygen.$x$, null),
(69, $x$A patient with metabolic acidosis has HCO3⁻ 10 mmol/L and PaCO2 31 mmHg. Using Winter's formula, what does this show?$x$, $x$PaCO2 is higher than expected — an added respiratory acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: PaCO2 is higher than expected — an added respiratory acidosis
WINTER'S FORMULA: expected PaCO2 = (1.5 × HCO3⁻) + 8 ± 2 = (1.5 × 10) + 8 = 23 ± 2 = 21–25 mmHg. The ACTUAL PaCO2 is 31 mmHg — HIGHER than expected. The lungs are not blowing off enough CO2, so there is an ADDED RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS (e.g. fatigue, sedation, chest problem).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Appropriate respiratory compensation — a pure metabolic acidosis — Appropriate compensation would be 21–25.
• PaCO2 is lower than expected — an added respiratory alkalosis — A lower PaCO2 (below 21) would indicate an added respiratory alkalosis.
• The kidneys are compensating for a respiratory acidosis — The kidneys compensate for respiratory problems, not the reverse here.

⭐ Key point: An inadequately compensating patient with metabolic acidosis may be tiring and need ventilatory support.$x$, null),
(70, $x$Arterial blood gas for a patient with septic shock who is now exhausted and hypoventilating pH 7.07, PaCO2 68 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 14 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$x$, $x$Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.07: severe ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 14 LOW (METABOLIC — lactic acidosis) and PaCO2 68 HIGH (RESPIRATORY — exhaustion, hypoventilation). Both lower the pH. Answer: MIXED RESPIRATORY AND METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis has a high pH.
• Uncompensated respiratory acidosis — Uncompensated respiratory acidosis would have a normal HCO3⁻.
• Uncompensated metabolic acidosis — Uncompensated metabolic acidosis would have a normal PaCO2.

⭐ Key point: Mixed acidosis causes very low pH quickly and is life-threatening — urgent airway and ventilatory support, plus treatment of shock.$x$, null),
(71, $x$For a patient with an acute COPD exacerbation presenting with respiratory acidosis (pH 7.30, PaCO2 60 mmHg) but still conscious and able to protect the airway, which intervention is generally tried before invasive intubation?$x$, $x$Non-invasive positive pressure ventilation (e.g., BiPAP)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Non-invasive positive pressure ventilation (e.g., BiPAP)
This COPD patient has ACUTE HYPERCAPNIC (type 2) respiratory failure — pH 7.30 (acidaemia) with PaCO2 60 mmHg — but is still CONSCIOUS and able to PROTECT THE AIRWAY. This is the classic indication for NON-INVASIVE VENTILATION (NIV/BiPAP) via a tight-fitting face mask. BiPAP gives a higher pressure during inspiration (IPAP), which helps the tired breathing muscles take bigger breaths and blow off CO2, and a lower pressure during expiration (EPAP), which keeps small airways open. Trials show NIV in COPD exacerbations REDUCES the need for intubation, ICU stay and death. Reassess with an ABG after 1–2 hours: if pH and PaCO2 are not improving, intubation should be considered.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• High-dose intravenous sedation to reduce work of breathing — High-dose sedation would depress breathing further, worsen CO2 retention and could cause respiratory arrest.
• Immediate elective tracheostomy — Tracheostomy is a long-term airway for prolonged ventilation, not an emergency first step in an alert patient.
• Therapeutic bronchoalveolar lavage — Bronchoalveolar lavage is a diagnostic/therapeutic bronchoscopy procedure, not treatment for hypercapnic failure.

⭐ Key point: Contraindications to NIV: respiratory arrest, unable to protect the airway, vomiting, facial trauma, untreated pneumothorax, severe haemodynamic instability, and copious secretions.$x$, null),
(72, $x$Arterial blood gas for a patient with liver cirrhosis who has been tachypnoeic for days pH 7.47, PaCO2 29 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 19 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$x$, $x$Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.47: ALKALOSIS. STEP 2 — PaCO2 29 mmHg is LOW: the cause is RESPIRATORY (the patient is breathing fast — in liver cirrhosis, toxins such as ammonia and hormonal changes stimulate the breathing centre, causing chronic hyperventilation). STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 19 mmol/L is LOW: the kidneys have started compensating by excreting bicarbonate over the past days, but the pH is still above 7.45. Answer: PARTIALLY COMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — UNCOMPENSATED would have a NORMAL HCO3⁻ (22–26).
• Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis — Mixed acidosis would have a LOW pH.
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would have a LOW pH as the primary disturbance.

⭐ Key point: Respiratory alkalosis is common in liver disease, pregnancy, sepsis (early), anxiety, pulmonary embolism and salicylate poisoning.$x$, null),
(73, $x$Arterial blood gas for a patient with chronic kidney disease on the renal ward pH 7.36, PaCO2 27 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 16 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$x$, $x$Fully compensated metabolic acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fully compensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.36: NORMAL, but on the ACIDIC side of 7.40. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 16 is LOW (metabolic acidosis — the failing kidneys cannot excrete acid or make enough bicarbonate) and PaCO2 27 is LOW (respiratory compensation by breathing more). STEP 3 — Because the pH is back within 7.35–7.45, compensation is FULL. The pH being slightly acidic shows the primary problem is the ACIDOSIS. Answer: FULLY COMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partially compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis needs a HIGH PaCO2.
• Uncompensated respiratory acidosis — Uncompensated respiratory acidosis needs a high PaCO2 with an abnormal pH.
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — PARTIAL compensation would leave the pH below 7.35.

⭐ Key point: In CKD, chronic metabolic acidosis is treated with oral sodium bicarbonate and dialysis when severe.$x$, null),
(74, $x$Arterial blood gas for a patient who has been vomiting for a week pH 7.46, PaCO2 53 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 38 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$x$, $x$Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.46: ALKALOSIS (just above 7.45). STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 38 is HIGH: METABOLIC ALKALOSIS (a week of vomiting has removed stomach acid, H⁺ and Cl⁻). STEP 3 — PaCO2 53 is HIGH: the lungs are retaining CO2 (slower, shallower breathing) to compensate, bringing the pH close to normal but not quite into the normal range. Answer: PARTIALLY COMPENSATED METABOLIC ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uncompensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis would have a LOW pH.
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — UNCOMPENSATED would have a NORMAL PaCO2.
• Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would have a LOW PaCO2.

⭐ Key point: Vomiting also causes dehydration, hypokalaemia and hypochloraemia — treat with IV 0.9% saline plus potassium chloride.$x$, null),
(75, $x$Renal compensation for chronic respiratory alkalosis involves:$x$, $x$Decreased renal bicarbonate reabsorption, increasing bicarbonate excretion to lower serum bicarbonate$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Decreased renal bicarbonate reabsorption, increasing bicarbonate excretion to lower serum bicarbonate
In CHRONIC RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS (e.g. at altitude, pregnancy, chronic liver disease), the PaCO2 is low and pH is high. The KIDNEYS compensate over 2–3 days by DECREASING BICARBONATE REABSORPTION in the tubules, so more bicarbonate is EXCRETED in the urine. They also secrete fewer hydrogen ions and make less ammonia. This lowers the serum bicarbonate and brings the pH back towards normal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increased hydrogen ion secretion — Increased hydrogen ion secretion would raise bicarbonate and worsen alkalosis.
• Increased ammoniagenesis — Increased ammoniagenesis generates new bicarbonate, worsening alkalosis; it is reduced in compensation.
• Increased bicarbonate reabsorption — Increased bicarbonate reabsorption would worsen the alkalosis; this is the compensation for respiratory ACIDOSIS.

⭐ Key point: Expected compensation in chronic respiratory alkalosis: HCO3⁻ falls by about 4–5 mmol/L for every 10 mmHg fall in PaCO2.$x$, null),
(76, $x$Arterial blood gas for a patient with septic shock who is now exhausted and hypoventilating pH 7.09, PaCO2 64 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 18 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$x$, $x$Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.09: severe ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 18 is LOW (METABOLIC acidosis — lactic acid from septic shock) AND PaCO2 64 is HIGH (RESPIRATORY acidosis — the patient is exhausted and hypoventilating). STEP 3 — Both changes push the pH DOWN; neither compensates for the other. Answer: MIXED RESPIRATORY AND METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis has a high pH.
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — Partial compensation of metabolic acidosis would need a LOW PaCO2.
• Partially compensated respiratory acidosis — Partially compensated respiratory acidosis would need a HIGH HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: This patient is in impending respiratory arrest — needs urgent intubation and ventilation, plus treatment of septic shock (antibiotics, fluids, vasopressors).$x$, null),
(77, $x$A patient with metabolic acidosis has HCO3⁻ 10 mmol/L and PaCO2 15 mmHg. Using Winter's formula, what does this show?$x$, $x$PaCO2 is lower than expected — an added respiratory alkalosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: PaCO2 is lower than expected — an added respiratory alkalosis
WINTER'S FORMULA predicts the expected PaCO2 in metabolic acidosis: expected PaCO2 = (1.5 × HCO3⁻) + 8 ± 2. Here: (1.5 × 10) + 8 = 15 + 8 = 23 ± 2 → 21–25 mmHg. The ACTUAL PaCO2 is 15 mmHg — LOWER than expected. The patient is blowing off MORE CO2 than compensation alone explains, so there is an ADDED RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS (e.g. from sepsis, salicylate poisoning, pain, anxiety, or liver failure).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Appropriate respiratory compensation — a pure metabolic acidosis — Appropriate compensation would be a PaCO2 between 21 and 25.
• PaCO2 is higher than expected — an added respiratory acidosis — An added respiratory acidosis would show a PaCO2 HIGHER than 25.
• The kidneys are compensating for a respiratory acidosis — The kidneys compensate for respiratory problems; here the primary problem is metabolic.

⭐ Key point: Salicylate (aspirin) poisoning classically causes a mixed metabolic acidosis and respiratory alkalosis.$x$, null),
(78, $x$Arterial blood gas for a patient with chronic kidney disease on the renal ward pH 7.36, PaCO2 26 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 18 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$x$, $x$Fully compensated metabolic acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fully compensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.36: NORMAL, on the ACIDIC side. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 18 LOW (metabolic acidosis from CKD) and PaCO2 26 LOW (respiratory compensation). STEP 3 — pH is within normal range: FULL compensation. Answer: FULLY COMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis needs a HIGH PaCO2.
• Partially compensated respiratory acidosis — Partially compensated respiratory acidosis needs a high PaCO2 and high HCO3⁻.
• Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis — Mixed acidosis would show a HIGH PaCO2 with low HCO3⁻ and a low pH.

⭐ Key point: Metabolic acidosis is rarely fully compensated by the lungs alone in acute illness; full compensation suggests a chronic state like CKD.$x$, null),
(79, $x$Type II (hypercapnic) respiratory failure, defined by PaCO2 above about 45-50 mmHg with acidaemia, is most typically caused by:$x$, $x$Alveolar hypoventilation, such as in COPD, neuromuscular disease, or CNS depression$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Alveolar hypoventilation, such as in COPD, neuromuscular disease, or CNS depression
TYPE II (HYPERCAPNIC) RESPIRATORY FAILURE is defined by a HIGH PaCO2 (above about 45–50 mmHg) with acidaemia, often with low oxygen too. It is caused by ALVEOLAR HYPOVENTILATION — not enough air moving in and out of the alveoli to remove CO2. Causes: COPD (tired muscles, air trapping), NEUROMUSCULAR DISEASE (Guillain–Barré, myasthenia gravis, motor neurone disease, spinal cord injury), CNS DEPRESSION (opioids, sedatives, head injury), chest wall problems (kyphoscoliosis, obesity hypoventilation, flail chest).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Isolated diffusion impairment from pulmonary fibrosis alone — Isolated diffusion impairment (fibrosis) mainly causes low oxygen (TYPE I) with normal or low CO2.
• A pure right-to-left cardiac shunt — A pure right-to-left shunt causes low oxygen (Type I); CO2 is usually normal or low.
• High-altitude exposure alone — High altitude causes hypoxaemia with LOW CO2 (hyperventilation).

⭐ Key point: Type I = low O2, normal/low CO2 (gas exchange problem). Type II = high CO2 (ventilation/"pump" problem).$x$, null),
(80, $x$A patient with metabolic acidosis has HCO3⁻ 18 mmol/L and PaCO2 43 mmHg. Using Winter's formula, what does this show?$x$, $x$PaCO2 is higher than expected — an added respiratory acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: PaCO2 is higher than expected — an added respiratory acidosis
WINTER'S FORMULA: expected PaCO2 = (1.5 × HCO3⁻) + 8 ± 2 = (1.5 × 18) + 8 = 27 + 8 = 35 ± 2 → 33–37 mmHg. The ACTUAL PaCO2 is 43 mmHg — HIGHER than expected. The lungs are NOT blowing off enough CO2, so there is an ADDED RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS (e.g. fatigue, sedation, chest infection, neuromuscular weakness).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Appropriate respiratory compensation — a pure metabolic acidosis — Appropriate compensation would be 33–37 mmHg.
• PaCO2 is lower than expected — an added respiratory alkalosis — A respiratory alkalosis would show a PaCO2 BELOW 33.
• The kidneys are compensating for a respiratory acidosis — The primary problem is metabolic, not a respiratory acidosis being compensated by the kidneys.

⭐ Key point: A "normal-looking" PaCO2 in a patient with metabolic acidosis is actually abnormal — it means the lungs are failing to compensate.$x$, null),
(81, $x$Arterial blood gas for a patient with severe diarrhoea who has just arrived pH 7.22, PaCO2 43 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 15 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$x$, $x$Uncompensated metabolic acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.22: ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 15 LOW: METABOLIC (severe diarrhoea loses bicarbonate). STEP 3 — PaCO2 43 NORMAL: the lungs have NOT compensated yet. Answer: UNCOMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — Fully compensated would have a normal pH and low PaCO2.
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis would have a high pH and high HCO3⁻.
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis needs a high PaCO2 as the primary change.

⭐ Key point: Diarrhoea causes a normal anion gap (hyperchloraemic) metabolic acidosis; replace fluids and potassium.$x$, null),
(82, $x$Results: Na⁺ 130, Cl⁻ 97, HCO3⁻ 14 mmol/L. What is the anion gap and its interpretation?$x$, $x$19 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 19 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)
ANION GAP = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO3⁻) = 130 − (97 + 14) = 130 − 111 = 19 mmol/L. Normal is about 8–12 mmol/L (up to 16 if potassium is included). So 19 is RAISED: a HIGH ANION GAP METABOLIC ACIDOSIS — unmeasured acids such as lactate, ketones, uraemic acids or toxins are present.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 33 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap) — 33 mmol/L is a calculation error.
• 19 mmol/L — normal — 19 is correct, but it is RAISED, not normal.
• 47 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap) — 47 mmol/L is a calculation error.

⭐ Key point: Correct the anion gap for low albumin: add about 2.5 mmol/L for every 10 g/L that albumin is below 40 g/L.$x$, null),
(83, $x$A patient with metabolic acidosis has HCO3⁻ 16 mmol/L and PaCO2 24 mmHg. Using Winter's formula, what does this show?$x$, $x$PaCO2 is lower than expected — an added respiratory alkalosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: PaCO2 is lower than expected — an added respiratory alkalosis
WINTER'S FORMULA: expected PaCO2 = (1.5 × 16) + 8 ± 2 = 24 + 8 = 32 ± 2 → 30–34 mmHg. The ACTUAL PaCO2 is 24 mmHg — LOWER than expected. The patient is breathing off extra CO2 beyond normal compensation, indicating an ADDED RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS (e.g. sepsis, pain, anxiety, salicylates).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• PaCO2 is higher than expected — an added respiratory acidosis — An added respiratory acidosis would show a PaCO2 above 34.
• The kidneys are compensating for a respiratory acidosis — The kidneys compensate for respiratory problems; here the primary problem is metabolic.
• Appropriate respiratory compensation — a pure metabolic acidosis — Appropriate compensation would be 30–34.

⭐ Key point: Mixed acid–base problems are common in ICU — always compare the actual compensation with the expected value.$x$, null),
(84, $x$Arterial blood gas for a ventilated patient whose set rate is too high pH 7.57, PaCO2 30 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 25 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$x$, $x$Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.57: ALKALOSIS. STEP 2 — PaCO2 30 mmHg LOW: RESPIRATORY (the ventilator rate is set too high, removing too much CO2). STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 25 NORMAL: no renal compensation. Answer: UNCOMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS. The fix is to reduce the rate (or tidal volume) and recheck the ABG.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis needs a HIGH HCO3⁻.
• Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — Fully compensated metabolic acidosis needs a normal pH and low HCO3⁻.
• Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis — Partial compensation would need a LOW HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: Over-ventilation causes cerebral vasoconstriction, hypokalaemia and low ionised calcium (arrhythmias).$x$, null),
(85, $x$Arterial blood gas for a patient with chronic kidney disease on the renal ward pH 7.36, PaCO2 26 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 15 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$x$, $x$Fully compensated metabolic acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fully compensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.36: NORMAL, on the ACIDIC side. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 15 LOW (metabolic acidosis from CKD) and PaCO2 26 LOW (respiratory compensation). STEP 3 — pH is in the normal range: FULL compensation. Answer: FULLY COMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis needs a HIGH HCO3⁻.
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis needs a high pH and high HCO3⁻.
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — PARTIAL compensation would leave the pH below 7.35.

⭐ Key point: In stable CKD, the body adapts over time — ABG values should be compared with the patient's baseline.$x$, null),
(86, $x$Which acid–base disturbance is most likely to result from the following? Prolonged vomiting with loss of gastric acid.$x$, $x$Metabolic alkalosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Metabolic alkalosis
PROLONGED VOMITING removes HYDROCHLORIC ACID (H⁺ and Cl⁻) from the stomach. Losing acid makes the blood more alkaline (bicarbonate rises). Dehydration activates aldosterone, causing the kidneys to retain sodium and excrete H⁺ and K⁺, maintaining the alkalosis. Result: HYPOCHLORAEMIC, HYPOKALAEMIC METABOLIC ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis is caused by hypoventilation.
• Respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis is caused by hyperventilation.
• Metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis is caused by acid gain or bicarbonate loss (e.g. diarrhoea), not vomiting.

⭐ Key point: Treat with IV 0.9% saline and potassium chloride; the kidneys then excrete the excess bicarbonate.$x$, null),
(87, $x$When titrating CRRT replacement fluid and dialysate flow rates for a patient with severe metabolic acidosis, the priority nursing consideration is:$x$, $x$Using a bicarbonate- or lactate-buffered replacement fluid to help correct the acidosis gradually$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Using a bicarbonate- or lactate-buffered replacement fluid to help correct the acidosis gradually
CONTINUOUS RENAL REPLACEMENT THERAPY (CRRT) can correct metabolic acidosis by removing acids and adding BUFFER (usually BICARBONATE, sometimes lactate) through the replacement fluid and dialysate. The priority is to use a BUFFERED fluid and correct the acidosis GRADUALLY over hours. Rapid correction can cause problems such as sudden shifts in potassium and calcium, rebound alkalosis, and worsening intracellular acidosis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Rapidly correcting the entire acid-base deficit within the first hour — Rapid correction within one hour is unsafe.
• Using only solutions with no buffer — Unbuffered solutions would fail to correct the acidosis (and may worsen it).
• Avoiding any buffer to prevent alkalosis — Avoiding buffer would leave the acidosis untreated.

⭐ Key point: Monitor ABGs, potassium, ionised calcium, phosphate and magnesium closely during CRRT.$x$, null),
(88, $x$A patient in acute respiratory failure has ABG results: pH 7.28, PaCO2 60 mmHg, HCO3 26 mEq/L, PaO2 55 mmHg. This is best interpreted as:$x$, $x$Acute (uncompensated) respiratory acidosis with hypoxemia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Acute (uncompensated) respiratory acidosis with hypoxemia
STEP 1 — pH 7.28: ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — PaCO2 60 HIGH: RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS. STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 26 NORMAL: the kidneys have not compensated yet (ACUTE). PaO2 55 mmHg is LOW (normal 80–100): HYPOXAEMIA. Answer: ACUTE (UNCOMPENSATED) RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS WITH HYPOXAEMIA — this is acute type II respiratory failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Fully compensated would have a normal pH and high HCO3⁻.
• Compensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis would have a high pH and high HCO3⁻.
• Acute metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis needs a LOW HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: Treat the cause, give controlled oxygen, and support ventilation (NIV or intubation).$x$, null),
(89, $x$Arterial blood gas for a patient with chronic kyphoscoliosis who feels well pH 7.37, PaCO2 65 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 35 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$x$, $x$Fully compensated respiratory acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fully compensated respiratory acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.37: NORMAL, slightly on the ACIDIC side. STEP 2 — PaCO2 65 HIGH (respiratory acidosis from chronic hypoventilation due to kyphoscoliosis restricting the chest) and HCO3⁻ 35 HIGH (renal compensation). STEP 3 — pH normal: FULL compensation. Answer: FULLY COMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS. The patient "feels well" because the condition is chronic and stable.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis needs a LOW PaCO2.
• Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis would have a high pH as the primary problem.
• Uncompensated respiratory acidosis — Uncompensated would have an abnormal pH and normal HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: These patients may need nocturnal NIV at home; avoid excessive oxygen, which can raise CO2 further.$x$, null),
(90, $x$Arterial blood gas for a patient with septic shock and a lactate of 6 mmol/L pH 7.19, PaCO2 26 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 10 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$x$, $x$Partially compensated metabolic acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Partially compensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.19: ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 10 LOW: METABOLIC (lactic acidosis from septic shock, lactate 6). STEP 3 — PaCO2 26 LOW: the lungs are compensating, but the pH is still low. Answer: PARTIALLY COMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS. (Winter's: expected 1.5 × 10 + 8 = 23 ± 2; actual 26 is borderline-high, suggesting the patient may be starting to tire.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — Fully compensated would have a normal pH.
• Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would have a high pH.
• Uncompensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis needs a high PaCO2.

⭐ Key point: Treat the shock (fluids, vasopressors, antibiotics, source control); bicarbonate is not routinely given for lactic acidosis.$x$, null),
(91, $x$Results: Na⁺ 130, Cl⁻ 107, HCO3⁻ 12 mmol/L. What is the anion gap and its interpretation?$x$, $x$11 mmol/L — normal$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 11 mmol/L — normal
ANION GAP = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO3⁻) = 130 − (107 + 12) = 130 − 119 = 11 mmol/L. This is within the NORMAL range (about 8–12). With a low bicarbonate (12), this indicates a NORMAL ANION GAP (HYPERCHLORAEMIC) METABOLIC ACIDOSIS — caused by bicarbonate loss (diarrhoea, ileostomy, renal tubular acidosis) or excess chloride (large volumes of 0.9% saline).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 35 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap) — 35 mmol/L is a calculation error.
• 11 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap) — 11 is correct, but it is NORMAL, not raised.
• 23 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap) — 23 mmol/L is a calculation error.

⭐ Key point: Normal anion gap acidosis: think "HARDUPS" — Hyperalimentation, Acetazolamide, Renal tubular acidosis, Diarrhoea, Ureteric diversion, Pancreatic fistula, Saline.$x$, null),
(92, $x$Arterial blood gas for a patient with septic shock who is now exhausted and hypoventilating pH 7.16, PaCO2 62 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 19 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$x$, $x$Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.16: ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 19 LOW (METABOLIC acidosis from septic shock) and PaCO2 62 HIGH (RESPIRATORY acidosis from exhaustion). STEP 3 — Both push pH down. Answer: MIXED RESPIRATORY AND METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Fully compensated would have a normal pH.
• Uncompensated respiratory acidosis — Uncompensated respiratory acidosis would have a normal HCO3⁻.
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — Partial compensation of metabolic acidosis would need a LOW PaCO2.

⭐ Key point: A rising CO2 in a septic patient signals exhaustion — prepare for intubation.$x$, null),
(93, $x$Arterial blood gas for a patient on high-dose furosemide with a low potassium pH 7.51, PaCO2 53 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 39 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$x$, $x$Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.51: ALKALOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 39 HIGH: METABOLIC ALKALOSIS (furosemide causes loss of H⁺, Cl⁻ and K⁺, plus volume contraction). STEP 3 — PaCO2 53 HIGH: the lungs are compensating by retaining CO2, but pH remains high. Answer: PARTIALLY COMPENSATED METABOLIC ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis would have a low pH.
• Uncompensated metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would have a low pH and low HCO3⁻.
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — Uncompensated would have a normal PaCO2.

⭐ Key point: Correct potassium and chloride, and review the diuretic dose.$x$, null),
(94, $x$Results: Na⁺ 143, Cl⁻ 95, HCO3⁻ 24 mmol/L. What is the anion gap and its interpretation?$x$, $x$24 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 24 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)
ANION GAP = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO3⁻) = 143 − (95 + 24) = 143 − 119 = 24 mmol/L. This is RAISED (normal 8–12). Interestingly, the bicarbonate is NORMAL (24), so a raised anion gap here suggests a HIDDEN high anion gap metabolic acidosis combined with a metabolic ALKALOSIS (e.g. a patient with DKA who is also vomiting) — the two cancel out in the bicarbonate. The anion gap reveals the hidden acid.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 48 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap) — 48 mmol/L is a calculation error.
• 24 mmol/L — normal — 24 is correct, but it is RAISED, not normal.
• 72 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap) — 72 mmol/L is a calculation error.

⭐ Key point: Always calculate the anion gap — it can uncover a hidden metabolic acidosis even when bicarbonate looks normal.$x$, null),
(95, $x$Arterial blood gas for a patient with chronic kidney disease on the renal ward pH 7.36, PaCO2 26 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 14 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$x$, $x$Fully compensated metabolic acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fully compensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.36: NORMAL, on the ACIDIC side. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 14 LOW (METABOLIC acidosis — CKD) and PaCO2 26 LOW (respiratory compensation). STEP 3 — pH normal: FULL compensation. Answer: FULLY COMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would have a high pH as the primary problem.
• Partially compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis needs a high PaCO2.
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — PARTIAL compensation would leave the pH below 7.35.

⭐ Key point: Check whether the pH is below or above 7.40 to identify the primary problem in fully compensated states.$x$, null),
(96, $x$Arterial blood gas for a patient with chronic kyphoscoliosis who feels well pH 7.38, PaCO2 62 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 34 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$x$, $x$Fully compensated respiratory acidosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fully compensated respiratory acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.38: NORMAL, on the ACIDIC side. STEP 2 — PaCO2 62 HIGH (respiratory acidosis from chronic kyphoscoliosis) and HCO3⁻ 34 HIGH (renal compensation). STEP 3 — pH normal: FULL compensation. Answer: FULLY COMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis needs a LOW HCO3⁻.
• Partially compensated respiratory acidosis — PARTIAL compensation would leave the pH below 7.35.
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis would have a high pH.

⭐ Key point: Chronic CO2 retainers rely partly on hypoxic drive; titrate oxygen to SpO2 88–92%.$x$, null),
(97, $x$Which ABG indicates uncompensated respiratory alkalosis?$x$, $x$pH 7.50, PaCO2 30 mmHg, HCO3- 24 mEq/L$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: pH 7.50, PaCO2 30 mmHg, HCO3- 24 mEq/L
UNCOMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS means: HIGH pH (above 7.45), LOW PaCO2 (below 35 — the cause), and NORMAL HCO3⁻ (22–26 — no renal compensation yet). Option D (pH 7.50, PaCO2 30, HCO3⁻ 24) fits exactly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• pH 7.50, PaCO2 50 mmHg, HCO3- 30 mEq/L — pH 7.50, PaCO2 50, HCO3⁻ 30 is partially compensated METABOLIC alkalosis (high HCO3⁻ with compensatory high CO2).
• pH 7.30, PaCO2 50 mmHg, HCO3- 26 mEq/L — pH 7.30, PaCO2 50, HCO3⁻ 26 is uncompensated RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS.
• pH 7.40, PaCO2 30 mmHg, HCO3- 18 mEq/L — pH 7.40, PaCO2 30, HCO3⁻ 18 has a normal pH — fully compensated (respiratory alkalosis or metabolic acidosis).

⭐ Key point: In uncompensated disorders, only ONE value (PaCO2 or HCO3⁻) is abnormal along with the pH.$x$, null),
(98, $x$Arterial blood gas for a patient on continuous nasogastric suction for 3 days pH 7.48, PaCO2 40 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 33 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$x$, $x$Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.48: ALKALOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 33 is HIGH: METABOLIC ALKALOSIS. Continuous NASOGASTRIC SUCTION removes stomach acid (H⁺ and Cl⁻), so the blood becomes alkaline. STEP 3 — PaCO2 40 is NORMAL: no respiratory compensation. Answer: UNCOMPENSATED METABOLIC ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would have a low pH and low HCO3⁻.
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis needs a low pH and high PaCO2.
• Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis — PARTIAL compensation would show a HIGH PaCO2 (above 45).

⭐ Key point: Measure NG losses and replace them with 0.9% saline plus potassium chloride; a PPI reduces acid loss.$x$, null),
(99, $x$During a spontaneous breathing trial a patient breathes at 16/min with a tidal volume of 500 mL. What is the rapid shallow breathing index (RSBI)?$x$, $x$32 — likely to wean successfully$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 32 — likely to wean successfully
RSBI = RESPIRATORY RATE ÷ TIDAL VOLUME (in LITRES) = 16 ÷ 0.5 = 32. An RSBI BELOW 105 predicts the patient is LIKELY TO WEAN SUCCESSFULLY.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 8 — likely to fail weaning — 8 is a calculation error (16 ÷ 2).
• 32 — likely to fail weaning — 32 is correct, but it predicts SUCCESS, not failure.
• 31 — likely to wean successfully — 31 is a calculation error.

⭐ Key point: RSBI above 105 = fast, shallow breathing = likely failure. Assess the whole patient, not just RSBI.$x$, null),
(100, $x$A patient's endotracheal tube cuff, inflated with air, is left in place during a prolonged case using nitrous oxide. A recognized risk of this combination is:$x$, $x$Progressive cuff overinflation as nitrous oxide diffuses into the cuff, risking tracheal mucosal ischaemia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Progressive cuff overinflation as nitrous oxide diffuses into the cuff, risking tracheal mucosal ischaemia
NITROUS OXIDE (N2O) is much more soluble in blood than nitrogen. During anaesthesia, it DIFFUSES into air-filled spaces — including the air-filled endotracheal tube CUFF — faster than nitrogen can leave. So the cuff volume and PRESSURE INCREASE progressively. High cuff pressure (above 30 cmH2O) compresses the tracheal blood supply, causing TRACHEAL MUCOSAL ISCHAEMIA, sore throat, ulceration, and later stenosis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reduced cuff pressure requiring frequent reinflation — The cuff pressure INCREASES, not decreases.
• Cuff deflation over time — The cuff over-inflates rather than deflates.
• Cuff material degradation — Material degradation is not the issue.

⭐ Key point: Check cuff pressure regularly with a manometer during nitrous oxide anaesthesia, or fill the cuff with saline or the anaesthetic gas mixture.$x$, null),
(101, $x$A ventilated ARDS patient has a plateau pressure of 15 cmH2O and PEEP of 5 cmH2O. What is the driving pressure?$x$, $x$10 cmH2O — within the safe target (≤ 15)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 10 cmH2O — within the safe target (≤ 15)
DRIVING PRESSURE = PLATEAU PRESSURE − PEEP = 15 − 5 = 10 cmH2O. The safe target is 15 cmH2O or LESS, so 10 is WITHIN the safe target. Lower driving pressure means less stretch on the lungs with each breath and a lower risk of ventilator-induced lung injury.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10 cmH2O — too high (> 15), increasing the risk of lung injury — 10 is correct, but it is SAFE, not too high.
• 20 cmH2O — too high (> 15), increasing the risk of lung injury — 20 adds plateau and PEEP.
• 15 cmH2O — within the safe target (≤ 15) — 15 is the plateau pressure, not the driving pressure.

⭐ Key point: Driving pressure above 15 cmH2O is linked to higher mortality in ARDS.$x$, null),
(102, $x$A potential complication of Assist-Control ventilation in a patient who is tachypneic from anxiety or pain is:$x$, $x$Respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation, along with breath stacking and auto-PEEP$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation, along with breath stacking and auto-PEEP
In ASSIST-CONTROL (AC) ventilation, EVERY breath the patient triggers is given as a FULL breath (full set volume or pressure). If the patient breathes fast because of ANXIETY or PAIN (e.g. 35/min), each breath delivers a full tidal volume, causing excessive minute ventilation: RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS (CO2 too low). There may also not be enough time to exhale, causing BREATH STACKING and AUTO-PEEP, which can cause hypotension and barotrauma.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Inability to trigger any breaths — AC does allow patients to trigger breaths.
• Hypoventilation and hypercapnia — Hypoventilation is not the problem with rapid triggering in AC.
• Automatic apnea — AC provides backup breaths, so apnoea is not a risk.

⭐ Key point: Treat the cause of tachypnoea (pain, anxiety, fever, hypoxia) and consider a different mode (e.g. pressure support or SIMV).$x$, null),
(103, $x$A female patient with ARDS is 170 cm tall and weighs 87 kg. Using lung-protective ventilation at 6 mL/kg predicted body weight, what tidal volume should be set?$x$, $x$About 369 mL$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: About 369 mL
FEMALE PBW = 45.5 + 0.91 × (height in cm − 152.4) = 45.5 + 0.91 × (170 − 152.4) = 45.5 + 0.91 × 17.6 = 45.5 + 16.0 = 61.5 kg. Tidal volume at 6 mL/kg = 6 × 61.5 = 369 mL. Her actual weight (87 kg) is NOT used, because lung size depends on height and sex, not body fat.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• About 522 mL — 522 mL is 6 mL/kg of her ACTUAL weight — too high.
• About 492 mL — 492 mL is 8 mL/kg PBW.
• About 615 mL — 615 mL is 10 mL/kg PBW.

⭐ Key point: Using actual weight in obese patients causes over-inflation and lung injury.$x$, null),
(104, $x$For a hemodynamically unstable ARDS patient requiring precise control of airway pressures to minimize barotrauma risk while sedated, which mode is often preferred initially?$x$, $x$Pressure Control Ventilation, or volume control with a plateau pressure limit, with controlled mandatory breaths$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Pressure Control Ventilation, or volume control with a plateau pressure limit, with controlled mandatory breaths
For a HAEMODYNAMICALLY UNSTABLE, SEDATED ARDS patient, CONTROLLED MANDATORY ventilation is preferred initially: PRESSURE CONTROL VENTILATION (which limits peak airway pressure) or VOLUME CONTROL with a PLATEAU PRESSURE LIMIT (≤30 cmH2O). This gives precise control of pressures and volumes, minimising barotrauma, while ensuring adequate ventilation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• T-piece trial — A T-piece trial is a weaning test, not for unstable patients.
• CPAP alone — CPAP alone gives no breaths — inadequate for a sedated patient.
• Pressure Support Ventilation with a low backup rate — Pressure support requires a reliable respiratory drive; a low backup rate is unsafe in an unstable, sedated patient.

⭐ Key point: In pressure control, monitor exhaled tidal volume closely — it changes with compliance.$x$, null),
(105, $x$What is the recommended action if a ventilated patient's exhaled tidal volume suddenly drops significantly with a normal peak pressure and an audible air leak at the mouth?$x$, $x$Suspect cuff leak or partial tube displacement and assess cuff pressure and tube position immediately$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Suspect cuff leak or partial tube displacement and assess cuff pressure and tube position immediately
A SUDDEN DROP in EXHALED TIDAL VOLUME with a NORMAL (or low) PEAK PRESSURE and an AUDIBLE AIR LEAK at the mouth means gas is escaping around the tube: a CUFF LEAK (under-inflated or damaged cuff) or PARTIAL TUBE DISPLACEMENT (tube pulled up so the cuff is at or above the vocal cords). Immediately check CUFF PRESSURE (20–30 cmH2O) and TUBE POSITION (marking at the teeth/lips, chest movement, breath sounds, capnography).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase sedation — Increasing sedation doesn't fix a leak.
• Increase PEEP — Increasing PEEP doesn't fix a leak.
• Assume mucus plug and suction aggressively — A mucus plug causes HIGH pressures, not a leak.

⭐ Key point: If the tube has slipped, call for help — re-positioning or re-intubation may be needed.$x$, null),
(106, $x$The mode of Mechanical Ventilation which does not allow the patient to take spontaneous breaths is:$x$, $x$CMV (Control Mandatory Ventilation)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: CMV (Control Mandatory Ventilation)
In CONTROLLED MANDATORY VENTILATION (CMV), the ventilator delivers ALL breaths at a set rate and volume/pressure, and the patient CANNOT TRIGGER any breaths (the trigger is switched off). It is used in deeply sedated or paralysed patients. It is uncomfortable for awake patients ("fighting the ventilator").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• AC Mode (Assist Control Mode) — AC allows patients to trigger extra (full) breaths.
• SIMV (Synchronous intermittent Mandatory Ventilation) — SIMV allows spontaneous breaths between mandatory breaths.
• PCV (Pressure Control Mode) — Pressure Control (as commonly used in AC mode) allows patient-triggered breaths.

⭐ Key point: Many modern ventilators use AC rather than pure CMV.$x$, null),
(107, $x$A sudden high-pressure alarm accompanied by absent chest rise, difficulty bagging, and inability to pass a suction catheter most strongly suggests:$x$, $x$Endotracheal tube obstruction (e.g., mucus plug, kinking, biting)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Endotracheal tube obstruction (e.g., mucus plug, kinking, biting)
A SUDDEN HIGH-PRESSURE alarm with ABSENT CHEST RISE, DIFFICULTY BAGGING, and INABILITY TO PASS A SUCTION CATHETER strongly suggests the ENDOTRACHEAL TUBE IS OBSTRUCTED: mucus plug, blood clot, kinking, or the patient biting the tube. If the obstruction can't be cleared, the tube may need replacing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Correct tube placement with normal function — Correct tube function would allow ventilation and suction.
• Ventilator circuit disconnection — Circuit disconnection causes LOW pressure alarms.
• Tension pneumothorax with hyperresonance — Tension pneumothorax causes high pressures, but a suction catheter would still pass down the tube.

⭐ Key point: Follow DOPE: Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment failure.$x$, null),
(108, $x$A key nursing assessment component specific to a patient receiving neuromuscular blockade for ventilator synchrony is:$x$, $x$Using a peripheral nerve stimulator, such as train-of-four, to monitor level of blockade and ensuring adequate concurrent sedation and analgesia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Using a peripheral nerve stimulator, such as train-of-four, to monitor level of blockade and ensuring adequate concurrent sedation and analgesia
Patients receiving NEUROMUSCULAR BLOCKADE (paralysis) CANNOT MOVE, BREATHE, or show signs of distress or pain. Key nursing assessments: use a PERIPHERAL NERVE STIMULATOR (TRAIN-OF-FOUR, TOF) to monitor the depth of blockade (avoid excessive paralysis), and ENSURE ADEQUATE SEDATION and ANALGESIA (paralysed patients can be awake and in pain without showing it). Also: eye care (corneal protection), pressure area care, DVT prevention.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Discontinuing all monitoring since the patient cannot move — Monitoring must continue.
• Assessing verbal pain reports — Paralysed patients cannot report pain verbally.
• Allowing the patient to ambulate — Paralysed patients cannot ambulate.

⭐ Key point: Usually aim for 1–2 twitches out of 4 on TOF. Use BIS monitoring if available to assess sedation depth.$x$, null),
(109, $x$When preoxygenating a trauma patient with facial injuries before intubation, the nurse should be cautious with bag-valve-mask ventilation because:$x$, $x$Excessive ventilation pressure can cause gastric insufflation and increase aspiration risk, and may worsen facial injuries$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Excessive ventilation pressure can cause gastric insufflation and increase aspiration risk, and may worsen facial injuries
When BAG-VALVE-MASK ventilating a trauma patient, EXCESSIVE PRESSURE or volume can force air into the STOMACH (GASTRIC INSUFFLATION), increasing the risk of VOMITING and ASPIRATION. In patients with FACIAL INJURIES, mask pressure may worsen injuries, and fractures may prevent a good seal. Use gentle, slow breaths only as needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It improves cervical spine stability — BVM doesn't improve cervical spine stability; in-line stabilisation is needed.
• It has no risks — BVM does carry risks.
• It always causes hypoxemia — BVM doesn't always cause hypoxaemia; it can prevent it.

⭐ Key point: Use the minimum pressure needed for chest rise; consider cricoid pressure and early intubation.$x$, null),
(110, $x$A ventilated patient develops a new cardiac dysrhythmia, such as new atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response, along with desaturation. The nurse should:$x$, $x$Assess for hypoxemia or ventilatory causes contributing to the dysrhythmia and notify the physician promptly given the combined instability$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Assess for hypoxemia or ventilatory causes contributing to the dysrhythmia and notify the physician promptly given the combined instability
In a VENTILATED patient, a NEW ARRHYTHMIA (e.g. rapid AF) together with DESATURATION suggests a COMBINED problem — HYPOXIA, high CO2, electrolyte imbalance, tube problems, pneumothorax, or sepsis can cause both. The nurse should ASSESS for HYPOXAEMIA and VENTILATORY causes (check tube, breath sounds, ventilator, ABG, electrolytes) and NOTIFY THE PHYSICIAN PROMPTLY.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Wait for the next scheduled assessment — Waiting delays treatment of a serious problem.
• Decrease FiO2 — Decreasing FiO2 would worsen hypoxia.
• Treat this as an isolated cardiac issue unrelated to ventilation — The arrhythmia may be caused by ventilation problems, not an isolated cardiac issue.

⭐ Key point: Check potassium and magnesium in new arrhythmias in ICU patients.$x$, null),
(111, $x$A male patient with ARDS is 185 cm tall and weighs 125 kg. Using lung-protective ventilation at 8 mL/kg predicted body weight, what tidal volume should be set?$x$, $x$About 637 mL$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: About 637 mL
MALE PBW = 50 + 0.91 × (height in cm − 152.4) = 50 + 0.91 × (185 − 152.4) = 50 + 0.91 × 32.6 = 50 + 29.7 = 79.7 kg. Tidal volume at 8 mL/kg = 8 × 79.7 = 637 mL. His actual weight (125 kg) is NOT used.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• About 1000 mL — 1,000 mL is 8 mL/kg of actual weight — dangerously high.
• About 797 mL — 797 mL is 10 mL/kg PBW.
• About 478 mL — 478 mL is 6 mL/kg PBW.

⭐ Key point: Tall patients have larger lungs, so their PBW and tidal volumes are larger, regardless of actual weight.$x$, null),
(112, $x$The DOPE mnemonic used to rapidly assess a deteriorating ventilated patient stands for:$x$, $x$Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment failure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment failure
The DOPE mnemonic helps rapidly find the cause of deterioration in a ventilated patient: D = DISPLACEMENT of the tube (extubation, right main bronchus); O = OBSTRUCTION of the tube (secretions, kinking, biting); P = PNEUMOTHORAX (especially tension); E = EQUIPMENT FAILURE (ventilator, circuit, oxygen supply). Some add "S" for Stacked breaths (auto-PEEP).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Distress, Obstruction, Pain, Emergency — "Distress, Obstruction, Pain, Emergency" is incorrect.
• Dislodgement, Oxygen, Pressure, Extubation — "Dislodgement, Oxygen, Pressure, Extubation" is incorrect.
• Displacement, Overventilation, Pneumonia, Edema — "Displacement, Overventilation, Pneumonia, Edema" is incorrect.

⭐ Key point: If in doubt, disconnect from the ventilator and hand-ventilate with 100% oxygen while you work through DOPE.$x$, null),
(113, $x$A patient with severe asthma on the ventilator develops sudden hypotension and difficulty bagging. The nurse should consider disconnecting the patient from the ventilator briefly to allow full exhalation, since this presentation may reflect:$x$, $x$Severe auto-PEEP/breath stacking causing dynamic hyperinflation and reduced venous return$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Severe auto-PEEP/breath stacking causing dynamic hyperinflation and reduced venous return
In SEVERE ASTHMA, narrowed airways slow exhalation. If the ventilator gives the next breath before exhalation is complete, air builds up (BREATH STACKING / AUTO-PEEP), causing DYNAMIC HYPERINFLATION. The raised pressure in the chest REDUCES VENOUS RETURN, causing HYPOTENSION, and makes the lungs very stiff (DIFFICULT BAGGING). Briefly DISCONNECTING the ventilator allows trapped air to escape, which can quickly restore BP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal ventilator function — This is not normal ventilator function.
• Improved lung compliance — Compliance is worse, not improved.
• An unrelated cardiac event only — It is not an unrelated cardiac event; it is caused by ventilation.

⭐ Key point: Prevent with a low rate, low tidal volume, long expiratory time, and bronchodilators; accept higher CO2.$x$, null),
(114, $x$A male patient with ARDS is 170 cm tall and weighs 101 kg. Using lung-protective ventilation at 8 mL/kg predicted body weight, what tidal volume should be set?$x$, $x$About 528 mL$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: About 528 mL
MALE PBW = 50 + 0.91 × (170 − 152.4) = 50 + 0.91 × 17.6 = 50 + 16.0 = 66 kg. Tidal volume at 8 mL/kg = 8 × 66 = 528 mL.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• About 660 mL — 660 mL is 10 mL/kg PBW.
• About 808 mL — 808 mL is 8 mL/kg of actual weight (101 kg).
• About 396 mL — 396 mL is 6 mL/kg PBW.

⭐ Key point: Recalculate if the height was estimated — measure the height (e.g. heel to crown) accurately.$x$, null),
(115, $x$Auto-PEEP (intrinsic PEEP), sometimes seen in patients with severe airflow obstruction on a ventilator, occurs when:$x$, $x$Incomplete exhalation traps air in the lungs before the next breath, causing progressive hyperinflation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Incomplete exhalation traps air in the lungs before the next breath, causing progressive hyperinflation
AUTO-PEEP (intrinsic PEEP) occurs when EXHALATION is INCOMPLETE — air is TRAPPED in the lungs before the next breath begins. This causes progressive HYPERINFLATION. It is common in severe airflow obstruction (asthma, COPD), high respiratory rates, and long inspiratory times.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• PEEP is set too low — Setting PEEP too low is not the cause.
• There is no relationship to ventilator settings — Auto-PEEP is strongly related to ventilator settings (rate, I:E ratio, tidal volume).
• The patient fully exhales before the next breath is delivered — If the patient fully exhales, there is no auto-PEEP.

⭐ Key point: Detect it with the expiratory flow waveform (not returning to zero) and an end-expiratory hold.$x$, null),
(116, $x$During a spontaneous breathing trial a patient breathes at 28/min with a tidal volume of 250 mL. What is the rapid shallow breathing index (RSBI)?$x$, $x$112 — likely to fail weaning$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 112 — likely to fail weaning
RSBI = RESPIRATORY RATE ÷ TIDAL VOLUME (LITRES) = 28 ÷ 0.25 = 112. An RSBI ABOVE 105 suggests RAPID, SHALLOW breathing, so weaning is LIKELY TO FAIL.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 7 — likely to fail weaning — 7 is a calculation error.
• 9 — likely to wean successfully — 9 is a calculation error.
• 112 — likely to wean successfully — 112 is correct, but it predicts FAILURE, not success.

⭐ Key point: Look for reversible causes of weaning failure: fluid overload, weakness, secretions, sedation, anxiety, electrolyte problems.$x$, null),
(117, $x$A ventilated ARDS patient has a plateau pressure of 28 cmH2O and PEEP of 12 cmH2O. What is the driving pressure?$x$, $x$16 cmH2O — too high (> 15), increasing the risk of lung injury$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 16 cmH2O — too high (> 15), increasing the risk of lung injury
DRIVING PRESSURE = PLATEAU − PEEP = 28 − 12 = 16 cmH2O. This is ABOVE the safe target of 15 cmH2O, so it is TOO HIGH and increases the risk of lung injury. Consider reducing the tidal volume or adjusting PEEP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 16 cmH2O — within the safe target (≤ 15) — 16 is correct, but it is NOT within the safe target.
• 28 cmH2O — too high (> 15), increasing the risk of lung injury — 28 is the plateau pressure.
• 40 cmH2O — too high (> 15), increasing the risk of lung injury — 40 adds plateau and PEEP.

⭐ Key point: Even when plateau is below 30, a driving pressure above 15 is a warning sign.$x$, null),
(118, $x$Which mode of mechanical ventilation delivers a preset volume/pressure with every breath, whether patient-triggered or machine-triggered, providing full support?$x$, $x$Assist-Control (AC)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Assist-Control (AC)
In ASSIST-CONTROL (AC) mode, the ventilator delivers a PRESET VOLUME (volume AC) or PRESSURE (pressure AC) with EVERY breath. If the patient does not breathe, the machine gives breaths at the set rate. If the patient TRIGGERS a breath, the ventilator still delivers the FULL set breath. So AC provides FULL ventilatory support and minimises the patient's work of breathing — useful at the start of ventilation, in shock, severe ARDS, or when muscles are fatigued.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CPAP with no additional support — CPAP alone gives NO breaths — only a continuous pressure; the patient does all the breathing work.
• Pressure Support Ventilation alone at low levels — Low-level pressure support only assists breaths the patient starts; it is partial support.
• T-piece trial — A T-piece trial gives NO support — it tests the patient's ability to breathe alone.

⭐ Key point: Watch for respiratory alkalosis and auto-PEEP in AC if the patient breathes fast; consider sedation, analgesia or a mode change.$x$, null),
(119, $x$Pressure support ventilation (PSV) requires which patient characteristic to function safely?$x$, $x$Intact spontaneous respiratory drive$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Intact spontaneous respiratory drive
In PRESSURE SUPPORT VENTILATION (PSV), the ventilator only HELPS breaths that the PATIENT STARTS. It gives NO mandatory breaths. If the patient's breathing drive is weak or absent (deep sedation, brain injury, neuromuscular blockade), there will be no breaths and the patient can become apnoeic. So PSV requires an INTACT SPONTANEOUS RESPIRATORY DRIVE. It is mainly used for weaning and in awake, spontaneously breathing patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Presence of a tracheostomy only — A tracheostomy is not required; PSV can be used with an ET tube or NIV mask.
• Complete absence of spontaneous breathing — PSV cannot be used when there is no spontaneous breathing.
• Paralysis from neuromuscular blockade — Paralysed patients cannot trigger breaths, so PSV would fail.

⭐ Key point: Always set the apnoea alarm and backup ventilation when using PSV.$x$, null),
(120, $x$A ventilated patient develops sudden respiratory distress, high-pressure alarm, and coarse rhonchi audible without a stethoscope. The most appropriate first nursing action is:$x$, $x$Attempt suctioning to clear the airway$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Attempt suctioning to clear the airway
SUDDEN RESPIRATORY DISTRESS with a HIGH-PRESSURE alarm and COARSE RHONCHI (audible without a stethoscope) suggest SECRETIONS blocking the airway. The most appropriate first action is to ATTEMPT SUCTIONING to clear the airway.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sedate the patient more deeply — Sedation doesn't treat the secretions.
• Immediately call for tube reintubation without assessment — Re-intubation without assessment is premature.
• Increase the FiO2 only — Increasing FiO2 alone doesn't remove the obstruction.

⭐ Key point: Pre-oxygenate before suctioning and limit each pass to 10–15 seconds.$x$, null),
(121, $x$A mechanically ventilated patient's arterial blood gas shows respiratory alkalosis (low PaCO2, high pH). This most likely indicates:$x$, $x$Hyperventilation from excessive minute ventilation (rate/tidal volume too high) or patient-driven tachypnea$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hyperventilation from excessive minute ventilation (rate/tidal volume too high) or patient-driven tachypnea
RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS (low PaCO2, high pH) in a ventilated patient usually means HYPERVENTILATION: either the ventilator settings (rate or tidal volume) are too high, or the patient is breathing fast (pain, anxiety, fever, hypoxia, sepsis).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic acidosis compensation — Metabolic acidosis compensation would give a LOW pH.
• Underventilation — Under-ventilation causes respiratory ACIDOSIS.
• Correct ventilator settings requiring no change — The settings need review, not "no change".

⭐ Key point: Reduce minute ventilation if ventilator-driven, or treat the cause of patient tachypnoea.$x$, null),
(122, $x$In SIMV (Synchronized Intermittent Mandatory Ventilation) mode, compared to Assist-Control, the patient:$x$, $x$Can take spontaneous breaths between mandatory synchronized breaths, at their own tidal volume$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Can take spontaneous breaths between mandatory synchronized breaths, at their own tidal volume
In SIMV, the ventilator gives a SET NUMBER of mandatory breaths, synchronised with the patient's efforts. BETWEEN these breaths, the patient CAN take SPONTANEOUS breaths at their OWN TIDAL VOLUME (often with pressure support). In AC, every triggered breath gets full support.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Receives full ventilator support for every single breath — Full support for every breath is AC.
• Cannot breathe spontaneously at all — The patient can breathe spontaneously in SIMV.
• Is always fully paralysed — Patients are not paralysed in SIMV.

⭐ Key point: SIMV was used for weaning, but spontaneous breathing trials are more effective.$x$, null),
(123, $x$A hypovolaemic patient becomes hypotensive after intubation and positive-pressure ventilation mainly because:$x$, $x$Increased intrathoracic pressure reduces venous return$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Increased intrathoracic pressure reduces venous return
POSITIVE-PRESSURE VENTILATION RAISES the pressure inside the chest. This SQUEEZES the large veins and the right atrium, REDUCING VENOUS RETURN to the heart. In a HYPOVOLAEMIC patient (already low blood volume), this further reduces cardiac output and BP. Induction drugs also cause vasodilation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Heart rate falls — Heart rate usually doesn't fall.
• Contractility increases — Contractility doesn't increase.
• Afterload falls to zero — Afterload doesn't fall to zero.

⭐ Key point: Give fluids and have vasopressors ready before intubating hypovolaemic patients.$x$, null),
(124, $x$When planning nutrition for a mechanically ventilated COPD patient with difficulty weaning, which principle is important?$x$, $x$Avoid overfeeding, particularly excess carbohydrate, since it increases CO2 production and ventilatory demand$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Avoid overfeeding, particularly excess carbohydrate, since it increases CO2 production and ventilatory demand
In ventilated COPD patients, OVERFEEDING — especially EXCESS CARBOHYDRATE — increases CO2 PRODUCTION (carbohydrate metabolism produces more CO2 than fat). This increases the work of breathing and makes WEANING harder.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Provide the highest possible carbohydrate load to maximise energy delivery — A high carbohydrate load increases CO2 production.
• Restrict all protein intake to reduce respiratory workload — Protein is needed for muscle strength.
• Nutrition has no effect on ventilatory demand in COPD — Nutrition does affect ventilatory demand.

⭐ Key point: Provide balanced nutrition (adequate protein, avoid overfeeding).$x$, null),
(125, $x$Current ICU sedation guidelines generally favor propofol or dexmedetomidine over benzodiazepine infusions for most mechanically ventilated adults primarily because benzodiazepines are associated with:$x$, $x$Higher rates of ICU delirium$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Higher rates of ICU delirium
Current ICU sedation guidelines (e.g. PADIS 2018) recommend PROPOFOL or DEXMEDETOMIDINE rather than BENZODIAZEPINE infusions (e.g. midazolam) for most ventilated adults. Benzodiazepines are associated with HIGHER RATES OF ICU DELIRIUM, deeper and longer sedation (they accumulate, especially in kidney or liver failure), longer ventilation and longer ICU stay. Delirium is linked to higher mortality and long-term cognitive problems.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lower cost and easier titration — Benzodiazepines may be cheaper, but they are harder, not easier, to titrate because they accumulate.
• Faster awakening times — Benzodiazepines cause SLOWER, not faster, awakening.
• Reduced risk of hypotension — Propofol and dexmedetomidine also cause hypotension; reduced hypotension is not the reason.

⭐ Key point: Benzodiazepines are still preferred for alcohol withdrawal and seizures. Use light sedation (RASS −2 to 0) and daily sedation interruption where appropriate.$x$, null),
(126, $x$A ventilated patient becomes agitated and is pulling at the endotracheal tube. The nurse's priority assessment is to:$x$, $x$Assess for a treatable cause of agitation such as hypoxia, pain, or ventilator asynchrony before increasing restraint or sedation alone$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Assess for a treatable cause of agitation such as hypoxia, pain, or ventilator asynchrony before increasing restraint or sedation alone
AGITATION in a ventilated patient may be caused by HYPOXIA, PAIN, VENTILATOR ASYNCHRONY, a full bladder, delirium, or anxiety. The priority is to ASSESS for a TREATABLE CAUSE before increasing restraints or sedation. Ensure the tube is secure and the patient is safe.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediately extubate the patient — Immediate extubation without assessment is unsafe.
• Increase the ventilator rate — Increasing the ventilator rate may worsen asynchrony.
• Apply four-point restraints without further assessment — Restraints without assessment may harm the patient.

⭐ Key point: Use validated tools: CPOT/BPS for pain, RASS for sedation, CAM-ICU for delirium.$x$, null),
(127, $x$When caring for a patient on Pressure Control Ventilation (PCV) for ARDS, the nurse should closely monitor:$x$, $x$Exhaled tidal volume, since it varies with changes in lung compliance and resistance$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Exhaled tidal volume, since it varies with changes in lung compliance and resistance
In PRESSURE CONTROL VENTILATION, the ventilator delivers a set PRESSURE; the TIDAL VOLUME VARIES with lung COMPLIANCE and RESISTANCE. If the lungs become stiffer or the airway narrower, the tidal volume falls. So the nurse must closely monitor EXHALED TIDAL VOLUME.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only respiratory rate — Respiratory rate alone is not enough.
• FiO2 alone — FiO2 alone is not enough.
• Only the set pressure, since volume is irrelevant — Volume is very important.

⭐ Key point: Set appropriate low tidal volume alarms in pressure modes.$x$, null),
(128, $x$Using EtCO2 to guide ventilator adjustments in a patient managed with permissive hypercapnia, such as an ARDS lung-protective strategy, provides the benefit of:$x$, $x$Allowing noninvasive, continuous trending of ventilation adequacy to help balance lung-protective settings against acceptable CO2 elevation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Allowing noninvasive, continuous trending of ventilation adequacy to help balance lung-protective settings against acceptable CO2 elevation
In PERMISSIVE HYPERCAPNIA, higher CO2 is accepted to protect the lungs. EtCO2 allows CONTINUOUS, NON-INVASIVE TRENDING of ventilation, helping the team balance lung-protective settings with acceptable CO2 levels between ABGs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Forcing normalization of PaCO2 at all times — PaCO2 normalisation isn't the goal.
• Preventing any hypercapnia from occurring — Hypercapnia is accepted, not prevented.
• Eliminating the need to check arterial blood gases entirely — ABGs are still needed (EtCO2 underestimates PaCO2 in lung disease).

⭐ Key point: Track the PaCO2–EtCO2 gradient with paired ABGs.$x$, null),
(129, $x$A mechanically ventilated patient's high-pressure alarm sounds repeatedly. On assessment, breath sounds are decreased on the right with tracheal deviation to the left and hyper-resonance on percussion. The nurse should suspect:$x$, $x$Tension pneumothorax on the right$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Tension pneumothorax on the right
High-pressure alarms, DECREASED BREATH SOUNDS on the RIGHT, TRACHEAL DEVIATION to the LEFT (away from the affected side), and HYPER-RESONANCE on the right suggest a TENSION PNEUMOTHORAX on the RIGHT. This is life-threatening; it needs immediate needle decompression and a chest drain.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mucus plug in the right main bronchus — A mucus plug causes dullness, not hyper-resonance, and doesn't push the trachea away.
• Pulmonary oedema — Pulmonary oedema causes crackles, not tracheal deviation.
• Normal ventilator function — This is not normal.

⭐ Key point: Ventilated patients are at high risk of tension pneumothorax — act on clinical signs.$x$, null),
(130, $x$Stress ulcer (gastrointestinal) prophylaxis is routinely recommended for mechanically ventilated patients because:$x$, $x$Critical illness and mechanical ventilation increase risk of stress-related mucosal bleeding$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Critical illness and mechanical ventilation increase risk of stress-related mucosal bleeding
CRITICAL ILLNESS and MECHANICAL VENTILATION (over 48 hours) INCREASE the risk of STRESS-RELATED MUCOSAL BLEEDING in the stomach, because of reduced blood flow to the gut lining. Prophylaxis (PPI or H2 blocker) reduces this risk in high-risk patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It prevents ventilator-associated pneumonia directly — It doesn't prevent VAP (it may increase VAP risk).
• It replaces the need for enteral nutrition — It doesn't replace enteral nutrition.
• Ventilated patients are at no increased GI bleeding risk — Ventilated patients ARE at increased risk.

⭐ Key point: Review the need for prophylaxis daily; stop it when risk factors resolve.$x$, null),
(131, $x$A patient came to the ER with Difficulty in breathing; He was intubated and placed on Mechanical ventilation. His ABGs report showed that his PCO2 is 55 mm Hg. To wash out CO2 which of the following parameter(s) should be adjusted:$x$, $x$Vt and R/R$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Vt and R/R
CO2 removal depends on MINUTE VENTILATION = TIDAL VOLUME (Vt) × RESPIRATORY RATE (R/R). A PaCO2 of 55 mmHg is high (normal 35–45). To "wash out" CO2, INCREASE the RESPIRATORY RATE and/or TIDAL VOLUME. In practice, the rate is usually increased first (within limits that avoid air trapping), while tidal volume is kept within lung-protective limits (6–8 mL/kg PBW; plateau ≤30 cmH2O). Rough guide: new rate = current rate × current PaCO2 ÷ target PaCO2.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Vt and PEEP — PEEP mainly improves OXYGENATION; tidal volume affects CO2, but PEEP does not significantly.
• Fio2 and R/R — FiO2 affects OXYGEN only; it does not wash out CO2.
• PEEP and FiO2 — PEEP and FiO2 both control OXYGENATION, not ventilation.

⭐ Key point: Remember: OXYGENATION = FiO2 + PEEP; VENTILATION (CO2) = RATE + TIDAL VOLUME.$x$, null),
(132, $x$A patient with COPD exacerbation and cardiogenic pulmonary edema who is alert, cooperative, and hemodynamically stable may first be tried on which form of ventilatory support before invasive intubation?$x$, $x$Noninvasive positive pressure ventilation (BiPAP)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Noninvasive positive pressure ventilation (BiPAP)
For an ALERT, COOPERATIVE, HAEMODYNAMICALLY STABLE patient with COPD exacerbation or CARDIOGENIC PULMONARY OEDEMA, NON-INVASIVE POSITIVE PRESSURE VENTILATION (BiPAP or CPAP) should be tried first. It reduces the work of breathing, improves gas exchange, and reduces the need for intubation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediate invasive mechanical ventilation — Immediate invasive ventilation isn't needed for stable patients.
• High-frequency oscillatory ventilation — High-frequency oscillatory ventilation is a rescue mode for severe ARDS.
• Negative pressure ventilation — Negative pressure ventilation is rarely used today.

⭐ Key point: Monitor closely and intubate if NIV fails.$x$, null),
(133, $x$The mode of Mechanical Ventilation in which there is a Preset Breath rate and tidal volume but the patient is able to initiate extra breath which is assisted by the ventilator is called:$x$, $x$ACV$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: ACV
In ASSIST-CONTROL VENTILATION (ACV), the clinician sets a MINIMUM (preset) RATE and a TIDAL VOLUME (or pressure). The ventilator delivers these mandatory breaths, but the patient CAN TRIGGER EXTRA breaths; each extra breath is ASSISTED with the FULL set tidal volume. This combines guaranteed minimum ventilation with patient comfort.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CMV — CMV (controlled mandatory ventilation) does NOT allow the patient to trigger any breaths.
• PSV — PSV has no preset rate or tidal volume — the patient controls both.
• SIMV — In SIMV, extra breaths are at the patient's OWN tidal volume (sometimes pressure-supported), not the full set volume.

⭐ Key point: ACV is one of the most common modes for initial ventilation in ICU.$x$, null),
(134, $x$During a spontaneous breathing trial a patient breathes at 16/min with a tidal volume of 400 mL. What is the rapid shallow breathing index (RSBI)?$x$, $x$40 — likely to wean successfully$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 40 — likely to wean successfully
RAPID SHALLOW BREATHING INDEX (RSBI) = RESPIRATORY RATE ÷ TIDAL VOLUME (in LITRES). Here: 400 mL = 0.4 L, so 16 ÷ 0.4 = 40. An RSBI BELOW 105 suggests the patient is breathing at a comfortable rate with good-sized breaths, so they are LIKELY TO WEAN SUCCESSFULLY. A high RSBI (fast, shallow breathing) suggests the muscles are tiring.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 25 — likely to wean successfully — 25 is a calculation error.
• 6.4 — likely to fail weaning — 6.4 is a calculation error (16 × 0.4).
• 40 — likely to fail weaning — 40 is correct, but it predicts SUCCESS, not failure.

⭐ Key point: RSBI is measured during a short period of spontaneous breathing (on minimal support). Combine it with other signs: alertness, cough strength, secretions, oxygenation and haemodynamic stability.$x$, null),
(135, $x$A patient on PEEP (Positive End-Expiratory Pressure) develops sudden hypotension after the PEEP level is increased. The most likely explanation is:$x$, $x$Increased intrathoracic pressure reducing venous return and cardiac output$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Increased intrathoracic pressure reducing venous return and cardiac output
Increasing PEEP RAISES INTRATHORACIC PRESSURE, which squeezes the veins returning blood to the heart, REDUCING VENOUS RETURN and CARDIAC OUTPUT — causing HYPOTENSION, especially in hypovolaemic patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An allergic reaction to the ventilator tubing — An allergic reaction to tubing is not likely.
• PEEP has no cardiovascular effects — PEEP does have cardiovascular effects.
• Coincidental, unrelated hypotension — The timing suggests a link to PEEP.

⭐ Key point: Give fluids and adjust PEEP if hypotension occurs; watch BP after every PEEP change.$x$, null),
(136, $x$A ventilated patient has a tidal volume of 350 mL and a rate of 24 breaths/min. What is the minute ventilation?$x$, $x$8.4 L/min$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 8.4 L/min
MINUTE VENTILATION = TIDAL VOLUME × RESPIRATORY RATE. Here: 350 mL × 24 breaths/min = 8,400 mL/min. Convert to litres: 8,400 ÷ 1,000 = 8.4 L/min. This is at the upper end of normal (about 5–8 L/min), but notice the breathing pattern is fast and fairly shallow — more of each breath is "wasted" on dead space, so alveolar ventilation is lower than it appears.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 14.58 L/min — 14.58 L/min is a calculation error.
• 10.8 L/min — 10.8 L/min is a calculation error.
• 84 L/min — 84 L/min is a decimal place error (ten times too high).

⭐ Key point: Alveolar ventilation = (tidal volume − dead space) × rate. With 150 mL dead space: (350 − 150) × 24 = 4.8 L/min.$x$, null),
(137, $x$A patient is extubated successfully but develops increasing stridor and respiratory distress within an hour. The nurse should anticipate:$x$, $x$Post-extubation laryngeal oedema/airway swelling, potentially requiring nebulised adrenaline, steroids, or reintubation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Post-extubation laryngeal oedema/airway swelling, potentially requiring nebulised adrenaline, steroids, or reintubation
After extubation, the larynx may be SWOLLEN (laryngeal oedema) from the tube, causing STRIDOR and respiratory distress within hours. Treatment: NEBULISED ADRENALINE (reduces swelling), STEROIDS (dexamethasone), sitting upright, humidified oxygen, and possibly REINTUBATION.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No specific concern, since extubation was successful — Stridor is a serious concern.
• No monitoring is needed for the next several hours — Close monitoring is needed.
• Discharge planning — Discharge planning is inappropriate.

⭐ Key point: A cuff leak test before extubation helps predict laryngeal oedema.$x$, null),
(138, $x$A ventilated patient with bilateral infiltrates on PEEP 10 cmH2O has PaO2 75 mmHg on FiO2 1.0. Using the P/F ratio, how is this classified?$x$, $x$Severe ARDS$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Severe ARDS
P/F RATIO = PaO2 ÷ FiO2. Here: 75 mmHg ÷ 1.0 (100% oxygen) = 75. With bilateral infiltrates on PEEP ≥5 (Berlin definition), a P/F of 100 or less is SEVERE ARDS. This means that even on 100% oxygen, the blood oxygen is very low — there is a lot of shunt (blood passing collapsed or fluid-filled alveoli).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mild ARDS — Mild ARDS is 200–300.
• Normal oxygenation (no ARDS) — Normal oxygenation would be above about 300–400 (on 100% oxygen a healthy person's PaO2 would be over 500).
• Moderate ARDS — Moderate ARDS is 100–200.

⭐ Key point: Severe ARDS treatments: lung-protective ventilation, higher PEEP, PRONE POSITIONING (16+ hours a day), short-term neuromuscular blockade, conservative fluids, and ECMO referral if refractory.$x$, null),
(139, $x$A patient with pulmonary oedema is started on BiPAP. What is the expected physiological benefit?$x$, $x$Reduces work of breathing and improves alveolar recruitment via positive pressure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Reduces work of breathing and improves alveolar recruitment via positive pressure
BiPAP in PULMONARY OEDEMA: the positive pressure REDUCES the WORK OF BREATHING, RECRUITS (re-opens) alveoli, pushes fluid out of the alveoli, and reduces preload and afterload of the left ventricle, improving heart function.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increases preload to improve cardiac output — BiPAP reduces preload.
• Eliminates the need for diuretic therapy — It doesn't replace diuretics.
• Increases venous return to the right heart — It reduces venous return.

⭐ Key point: Use BiPAP/CPAP early in cardiogenic pulmonary oedema with IV nitrates.$x$, null),
(140, $x$A mechanically ventilated patient in status asthmaticus develops sudden hypotension and increased peak airway pressures. The nurse should first suspect:$x$, $x$Auto-PEEP causing decreased venous return (breath stacking)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Auto-PEEP causing decreased venous return (breath stacking)
In STATUS ASTHMATICUS on a ventilator, the narrowed airways slow exhalation. If each new breath starts before the last one is fully exhaled, air builds up (AUTO-PEEP / BREATH STACKING). This raises pressure inside the chest, which REDUCES VENOUS RETURN to the heart (causing SUDDEN HYPOTENSION) and makes the lungs stiff (INCREASED PEAK PRESSURES). It can also cause barotrauma (pneumothorax). Immediate action: briefly DISCONNECT the ventilator to let the trapped air escape (and gently press on the chest), then reduce the rate and tidal volume and lengthen expiratory time. Also check for tension pneumothorax.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypoglycemia — Hypoglycaemia does not cause high airway pressures.
• Ventilator-associated pneumonia — VAP develops over days, not suddenly with hypotension.
• A normal expected ventilator finding — Sudden hypotension and high pressures are never normal.

⭐ Key point: Prevent with a low rate (10–12), small tidal volume, long expiration (I:E 1:4 or more), aggressive bronchodilators, and permissive hypercapnia.$x$, null),
(141, $x$A ventilated patient has tidal volume 400 mL, plateau pressure 22 cmH2O and PEEP 12 cmH2O. What is the static lung compliance?$x$, $x$40 mL/cmH2O$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 40 mL/cmH2O
STATIC COMPLIANCE shows how easily the lungs and chest wall stretch. Formula: COMPLIANCE = TIDAL VOLUME ÷ (PLATEAU PRESSURE − PEEP). Here: 400 mL ÷ (22 − 12) cmH2O = 400 ÷ 10 = 40 mL/cmH2O. The bottom part (22 − 12 = 10) is also the DRIVING PRESSURE, which is within the safe target (≤15). Normal static compliance in a ventilated adult is about 50–100 mL/cmH2O, so 40 shows MILDLY REDUCED compliance (slightly stiff lungs).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 25 mL/cmH2O — 25 mL/cmH2O is a calculation error (400 ÷ 16).
• 18.2 mL/cmH2O — 18.2 mL/cmH2O divides by the plateau pressure alone (400 ÷ 22), forgetting to subtract PEEP.
• 11.8 mL/cmH2O — 11.8 mL/cmH2O divides by plateau plus PEEP (400 ÷ 34) — the wrong formula.

⭐ Key point: Watch compliance trends: a falling value means stiffer lungs (worsening ARDS, oedema, pneumothorax, abdominal distension); a rising value means improvement.$x$, null),
(142, $x$A ventilator is set at 10 breaths/min with an inspiratory time of 1.2 seconds. What is the I:E ratio?$x$, $x$1:4$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: 1:4
STEP 1 — Total time for ONE breath: 60 seconds ÷ 10 breaths/min = 6 seconds per breath. STEP 2 — Expiratory time = total time − inspiratory time = 6 − 1.2 = 4.8 seconds. STEP 3 — I:E ratio = 1.2 : 4.8. Divide both by 1.2 to simplify: 1 : 4. A long expiratory time like this gives plenty of time to breathe out, which is helpful in asthma and COPD to prevent air trapping.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1:0.8 — 1:0.8 would mean expiration is SHORTER than inspiration — incorrect.
• 4:1 — 4:1 is an "inverse ratio" (inspiration four times longer than expiration) — incorrect.
• 1:6 — 1:6 is a calculation error.

⭐ Key point: Normal I:E is about 1:2. Increasing the rate or the inspiratory time shortens expiration and raises the risk of auto-PEEP.$x$, null),
(143, $x$A patient with a severe traumatic brain injury requires sedation for ventilator tolerance. The team performs a 'neuro wake-up' assessment periodically. The primary purpose of this is to:$x$, $x$Allow accurate ongoing neurological assessment despite the need for sedation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Allow accurate ongoing neurological assessment despite the need for sedation
Patients with SEVERE TRAUMATIC BRAIN INJURY often need sedation to tolerate the ventilator, control ICP and reduce brain oxygen demand. But sedation HIDES the neurological examination — the team cannot tell if the brain is getting worse (e.g. a new bleed). A "NEURO WAKE-UP" test means briefly stopping or reducing sedation to perform an ACCURATE NEUROLOGICAL ASSESSMENT (GCS, pupils, limb movements, obeying commands), then restarting sedation. It must be done only when the ICP is stable and controlled, because waking can raise ICP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Discharge the patient sooner regardless of status — Wake-up tests are not done to discharge patients sooner regardless of their state.
• Increase sedation requirements — The purpose is not to increase sedation requirements.
• Reduce the need for any imaging — It does not replace imaging; CT is still needed when deterioration is suspected.

⭐ Key point: Stop the wake-up test immediately if ICP rises significantly, the patient becomes distressed, or vital signs become unstable. Many units use continuous ICP monitoring and pupillometry instead in very unstable patients.$x$, null),
(144, $x$A patient with a subarachnoid haemorrhage is prescribed nimodipine. The rationale for this medication is primarily to:$x$, $x$Reduce the risk of cerebral vasospasm and secondary ischaemic injury$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Reduce the risk of cerebral vasospasm and secondary ischaemic injury
After SUBARACHNOID HAEMORRHAGE (SAH), blood around the brain arteries can cause them to narrow — CEREBRAL VASOSPASM — usually between days 4 and 14. This can starve parts of the brain of blood, causing DELAYED CEREBRAL ISCHAEMIA (new confusion, weakness, stroke). NIMODIPINE (a calcium channel blocker, 60 mg orally or by NG tube every 4 hours for 21 days) is given from admission because it IMPROVES OUTCOMES and reduces the risk of ischaemic damage. It acts mainly by protecting brain cells and reducing vasospasm effects.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lower blood pressure aggressively as the main goal — Lowering BP aggressively is NOT the goal — nimodipine can cause hypotension, which must be AVOIDED as it reduces brain blood flow.
• Reduce intracranial pressure directly — Nimodipine does not directly reduce ICP.
• Treat infection — Nimodipine is not an antibiotic.

⭐ Key point: If nimodipine causes hypotension, the dose may be split (30 mg every 2 hours) rather than stopped. Maintain normal blood volume and sodium.$x$, null),
(145, $x$A patient with known oesophageal varices develops sudden haematemesis and hypotension. The nurse's priority actions include:$x$, $x$Secure IV access, prepare for fluid/blood resuscitation, and avoid over-transfusion which can raise portal pressure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Secure IV access, prepare for fluid/blood resuscitation, and avoid over-transfusion which can raise portal pressure
OESOPHAGEAL VARICES are swollen veins in the oesophagus caused by high pressure in the portal vein (portal hypertension), usually from liver cirrhosis. When they bleed, the patient can lose large volumes of blood quickly (HAEMATEMESIS, HYPOTENSION). Priorities: secure the airway if needed, insert TWO LARGE-BORE IV cannulas, take blood (FBC, clotting, crossmatch), and start FLUID and BLOOD RESUSCITATION. However, AVOID OVER-TRANSFUSION: giving too much volume RAISES PORTAL PRESSURE and can restart or worsen bleeding. A restrictive transfusion target (Hb about 70–80 g/L) is used. Give terlipressin and IV antibiotics, and arrange urgent endoscopy for band ligation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Encourage oral fluids immediately — Oral fluids are dangerous (aspiration, vomiting) and delay endoscopy.
• Give a laxative to clear the bowel — Laxatives are not the priority; lactulose may be used later to prevent hepatic encephalopathy, but not in an emergency.
• Discharge home with outpatient follow-up — Discharge would be fatal — this is a life-threatening emergency.

⭐ Key point: Variceal bleeding has a high mortality; terlipressin and prophylactic antibiotics (e.g. ceftriaxone) reduce death.$x$, null),
(146, $x$A patient in labour has premature rupture of membranes with meconium-stained liquor. This finding is significant because it may indicate:$x$, $x$Possible fetal distress, requiring closer fetal monitoring$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Possible fetal distress, requiring closer fetal monitoring
MECONIUM is the baby's first stool. If it passes into the amniotic fluid before birth (MECONIUM-STAINED LIQUOR — green or brown fluid), it can indicate FETAL DISTRESS: low oxygen causes the fetal bowel to contract and the anal sphincter to relax. It is more common in post-term babies. It also carries a risk of MECONIUM ASPIRATION SYNDROME if the baby inhales it. So CLOSER FETAL MONITORING (continuous CTG), assessment of labour progress, and having a neonatal team ready at delivery are needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A sign of maternal infection only — Meconium staining is not mainly a sign of maternal infection (although infection should also be considered with prolonged rupture).
• A completely normal, reassuring finding with no significance — It is not a reassuring finding; it needs attention.
• Definite fetal death, requiring no further action — It does not mean the fetus is dead; the fetal heart should be monitored.

⭐ Key point: Thick meconium is more concerning than thin, light staining. Prolonged rupture of membranes also increases the risk of infection.$x$, null),
(147, $x$A patient with acute respiratory failure requires emergent intubation. Which pre-intubation nursing action is most important to optimize outcomes?$x$, $x$Pre-oxygenate with high-flow oxygen or bag-valve-mask prior to intubation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Pre-oxygenate with high-flow oxygen or bag-valve-mask prior to intubation
During intubation, the patient stops breathing for a period (apnoea). PRE-OXYGENATION — breathing 100% oxygen for 3–5 minutes (via a tight-fitting mask, high-flow oxygen, or non-invasive ventilation) before induction — fills the lungs with oxygen (replaces nitrogen in the functional residual capacity). This creates an oxygen "reservoir" that lets the patient tolerate apnoea for longer before oxygen levels fall dangerously. It is the most important step to prevent hypoxia and cardiac arrest during emergency intubation, especially in critically ill patients who desaturate quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sedate the patient heavily without any oxygen supplementation — Heavy sedation without oxygen causes severe hypoxia.
• Delay intubation until the patient is fully alert — Delaying intubation until the patient is fully alert is inappropriate in respiratory failure.
• Withhold oxygen until the tube is placed to avoid hyperoxia — Withholding oxygen is dangerous; avoiding hypoxia is far more important than avoiding temporary hyperoxia.

⭐ Key point: Also use apnoeic oxygenation (high-flow nasal oxygen during intubation), position the patient head-up, and have suction, airway adjuncts and a difficult airway plan ready.$x$, null),
(148, $x$A patient on long-term corticosteroid therapy for a chronic condition is admitted critically ill. The nurse should anticipate the need for:$x$, $x$Continued (often increased 'stress-dose') steroid coverage, since abrupt withdrawal risks adrenal crisis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Continued (often increased 'stress-dose') steroid coverage, since abrupt withdrawal risks adrenal crisis
Long-term CORTICOSTEROIDS (e.g. prednisolone for asthma, arthritis or autoimmune disease) SUPPRESS the body's own ADRENAL glands. When a patient becomes CRITICALLY ILL (surgery, sepsis, trauma), the body normally needs much more cortisol to cope with stress — but suppressed adrenals cannot produce it. If steroids are stopped suddenly, or not increased, the patient can develop an ADRENAL CRISIS: severe hypotension resistant to fluids and vasopressors, hypoglycaemia, hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia, and collapse. So the nurse should anticipate CONTINUED, often INCREASED "STRESS-DOSE" STEROIDS (e.g. IV hydrocortisone 50–100 mg every 6–8 hours, or 200 mg/day infusion).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Switching immediately to an unrelated drug class — Switching to an unrelated drug class does not replace cortisol.
• No special consideration regarding steroid dosing — Steroid dosing must be specially considered.
• Abrupt discontinuation of steroids immediately — Abrupt discontinuation risks adrenal crisis.

⭐ Key point: Patients on long-term steroids should carry a steroid card/alert and follow "sick day rules" (double the dose when ill).$x$, null),
(149, $x$A patient with a suspected overdose of unknown substance is unconscious with a GCS of 6 and shallow, slow respirations. What is the most immediate nursing priority?$x$, $x$Prepare for airway protection (e.g., positioning, suction, possible intubation) and provide ventilatory support$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Prepare for airway protection (e.g., positioning, suction, possible intubation) and provide ventilatory support
A GCS of 6 means the patient is deeply unconscious and CANNOT PROTECT THE AIRWAY (risk of obstruction by the tongue and aspiration of vomit). SHALLOW, SLOW BREATHING means RESPIRATORY DEPRESSION with high CO2 and low oxygen. The most immediate priority is AIRWAY and BREATHING: position the patient (head tilt/chin lift, recovery position if appropriate), suction, give oxygen, support breathing with bag-mask ventilation, and prepare for INTUBATION. Also check glucose and consider naloxone if opioid toxicity is possible.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Send the patient for an urgent CT scan of the head — A CT scan is important but must not happen before the airway is secured.
• Administer activated charcoal orally — Activated charcoal must NEVER be given to a patient who cannot protect their airway (aspiration risk).
• Obtain a detailed medication history from family — A medication history is helpful, but ABC comes first.

⭐ Key point: "GCS 8 — intubate": a GCS of 8 or less usually means the airway needs protection.$x$, null),
(150, $x$A patient's renal function tests show a rising creatinine and urea with oliguria following a major surgery complicated by hypotension. This presentation is most consistent with:$x$, $x$Acute kidney injury, likely pre-renal from hypoperfusion$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Acute kidney injury, likely pre-renal from hypoperfusion
ACUTE KIDNEY INJURY (AKI) is a sudden fall in kidney function, shown by a RISING CREATININE and UREA and/or REDUCED URINE OUTPUT (oliguria). After MAJOR SURGERY complicated by HYPOTENSION, the kidneys have not received enough blood flow (HYPOPERFUSION), so this is most likely PRE-RENAL AKI. If the hypoperfusion lasts long enough, it can progress to acute tubular necrosis (intrinsic AKI). Treatment: restore circulating volume and BP, stop nephrotoxic drugs, monitor fluid balance and electrolytes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diabetes insipidus — Diabetes insipidus causes POLYURIA (large volumes of dilute urine), not oliguria.
• Chronic kidney disease of long standing — Chronic kidney disease develops over months to years.
• Normal post-operative recovery — Rising creatinine with oliguria is not normal recovery.

⭐ Key point: KDIGO AKI criteria: creatinine rise ≥26.5 µmol/L within 48 hours, or ≥1.5 times baseline within 7 days, or urine output <0.5 mL/kg/hr for 6 hours.$x$, null),
(151, $x$A patient receiving IV nitroglycerin for cardiogenic pulmonary oedema develops a blood pressure of 82/50 mmHg. What is the nurse's priority action?$x$, $x$Stop or slow the infusion and reassess blood pressure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Stop or slow the infusion and reassess blood pressure
GLYCERYL TRINITRATE (GTN, nitroglycerin) dilates veins and arteries, which reduces preload and afterload — helpful in pulmonary oedema. But it also LOWERS BP. A BP of 82/50 mmHg (systolic below 90) is too low and may reduce blood flow to the heart, brain and kidneys. The priority is to STOP or SLOW the infusion and REASSESS the BP (it usually recovers quickly because GTN has a short half-life), then notify the doctor.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administer a fluid bolus and continue the infusion unchanged — A fluid bolus could worsen pulmonary oedema, and continuing the infusion unchanged would keep BP low.
• Switch to sublingual nitroglycerin — Sublingual GTN would lower BP further.
• Increase the infusion rate to improve cardiac output — Increasing the infusion would worsen hypotension.

⭐ Key point: Check BP every 5–15 minutes when starting or changing GTN. Avoid GTN in patients who have taken sildenafil or tadalafil.$x$, null),
(152, $x$A patient's SpO2 reads 99% but the patient appears cyanotic, confused, and has recently been exposed to carbon monoxide. The nurse should recognise that:$x$, $x$Standard pulse oximetry cannot distinguish oxyhaemoglobin from carboxyhaemoglobin, so a falsely reassuring high reading is possible in CO poisoning$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Standard pulse oximetry cannot distinguish oxyhaemoglobin from carboxyhaemoglobin, so a falsely reassuring high reading is possible in CO poisoning
CARBON MONOXIDE (CO) binds strongly to haemoglobin, forming CARBOXYHAEMOGLOBIN (COHb), which cannot carry oxygen. STANDARD PULSE OXIMETERS use only two wavelengths of light and CANNOT tell COHb from oxyhaemoglobin — both look "red" to the sensor. So the oximeter shows a FALSELY HIGH SpO2 (e.g. 99%), while the patient is actually severely hypoxic at the tissue level. Confusion is a sign of brain hypoxia. Measure COHb with a CO-oximeter (on a blood gas analyser), and give 100% oxygen via a non-rebreather mask.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The SpO2 reading is definitely accurate and reassuring — The SpO2 reading is NOT reliable in CO poisoning.
• No further assessment is needed given the good SpO2 — Further assessment and treatment are urgently needed.
• Cyanosis always confirms the oximeter is broken — Cyanosis does not mean the oximeter is broken; it shows the patient is unwell (CO victims may also appear cherry-red).

⭐ Key point: Generators, charcoal stoves and fires in closed spaces are common causes of CO poisoning in Nigeria. Consider hyperbaric oxygen for severe cases.$x$, null),
(153, $x$A patient recovering from a head injury is assessed to have decerebrate posturing (extension of all four limbs) in response to painful stimuli. This indicates:$x$, $x$More severe brainstem dysfunction, generally a worse prognosis than decorticate posturing$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: More severe brainstem dysfunction, generally a worse prognosis than decorticate posturing
DECEREBRATE POSTURING is EXTENSION of all four limbs to painful stimuli (arms straightened and rotated inwards, legs extended, toes pointed). It indicates damage at the level of the BRAINSTEM (midbrain/pons) — LOWER and MORE SEVERE than decorticate posturing (flexion of the arms, which indicates damage above the brainstem). Decerebrate posturing carries a WORSE PROGNOSIS. On the GCS, decerebrate = motor score 2; decorticate = 3.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A better prognosis than decorticate posturing — Decerebrate has a WORSE, not better, prognosis than decorticate.
• Normal neurological function — It is never normal.
• No clinical relevance — It is very clinically relevant — it may signal impending herniation.

⭐ Key point: A change from decorticate to decerebrate posturing is a sign of worsening brain injury — report immediately.$x$, null),
(154, $x$A patient receiving an intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP) suddenly develops a cold, pulseless left leg. The nurse should suspect:$x$, $x$Limb ischaemia from the IABP catheter, requiring urgent assessment and intervention$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Limb ischaemia from the IABP catheter, requiring urgent assessment and intervention
An INTRA-AORTIC BALLOON PUMP (IABP) catheter is inserted through the FEMORAL ARTERY into the descending aorta. It can BLOCK or reduce blood flow to the leg, cause clots, or dissect the artery, leading to LIMB ISCHAEMIA: a COLD, PALE, PULSELESS leg with pain, numbness and weakness. This is an emergency — check pulses (Doppler), colour, temperature, sensation and movement, and notify the doctor urgently (the IABP may need removal or repositioning).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An allergic reaction to the dressing — An allergic reaction to the dressing would cause local itching and rash, not a pulseless leg.
• Normal side effect requiring no action — A cold, pulseless leg is never a normal side effect.
• A simple positioning issue with no urgency — It is not a simple positioning issue; it needs urgent action.

⭐ Key point: Check distal pulses and limb perfusion (hourly) in patients with an IABP. Keep the leg straight and avoid hip flexion beyond 30°.$x$, null),
(155, $x$A patient's coagulation panel shows a markedly prolonged PT/INR with normal aPTT. This pattern most specifically suggests an abnormality in:$x$, $x$The extrinsic coagulation pathway (e.g. Factor VII deficiency or warfarin effect)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The extrinsic coagulation pathway (e.g. Factor VII deficiency or warfarin effect)
The PT/INR (prothrombin time) tests the EXTRINSIC pathway (Factor VII) and the common pathway (Factors X, V, II, fibrinogen). The aPTT tests the INTRINSIC pathway (Factors XII, XI, IX, VIII) and the common pathway. A PROLONGED PT/INR with NORMAL aPTT points to a problem in the EXTRINSIC pathway — e.g. FACTOR VII deficiency, WARFARIN effect (Factor VII has the shortest half-life, so it falls first), early VITAMIN K deficiency, or early liver disease.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The intrinsic coagulation pathway only — Intrinsic pathway problems prolong the aPTT (e.g. heparin, haemophilia).
• Platelet function only — Platelet function problems do not prolong the PT/INR.
• No coagulation abnormality — A markedly prolonged PT/INR IS a coagulation abnormality.

⭐ Key point: Warfarin is reversed with vitamin K and prothrombin complex concentrate (PCC).$x$, null),
(156, $x$A patient develops new left-sided facial droop, arm weakness, and slurred speech that resolves completely within 20 minutes. This presentation is most consistent with:$x$, $x$A Transient Ischaemic Attack (TIA), which still requires urgent evaluation given high subsequent stroke risk$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A Transient Ischaemic Attack (TIA), which still requires urgent evaluation given high subsequent stroke risk
A TRANSIENT ISCHAEMIC ATTACK (TIA) causes stroke-like symptoms (facial droop, arm weakness, slurred speech) that RESOLVE COMPLETELY, usually within minutes to an hour. Although symptoms go away, a TIA is a WARNING sign: the risk of a full stroke is high, especially in the next 48 hours to 7 days. It needs URGENT evaluation (brain imaging, carotid Doppler, ECG for AF, blood tests) and preventive treatment (antiplatelets, statins, BP control, anticoagulation if AF).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal ageing — Stroke-like symptoms are not normal ageing.
• A migraine with no further evaluation needed — Migraine can cause neurological symptoms, but this should not be assumed without evaluation.
• A completed stroke with permanent deficit — A completed stroke has permanent deficits, which are not present here.

⭐ Key point: Use the FAST test (Face, Arms, Speech, Time) to recognise stroke and TIA, and treat every episode as an emergency.$x$, null),
(157, $x$A patient with an infected surgical wound has cultures growing Pseudomonas aeruginosa resistant to multiple antibiotics. The nurse should anticipate:$x$, $x$Contact precautions in addition to standard precautions, given the multidrug-resistant organism$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Contact precautions in addition to standard precautions, given the multidrug-resistant organism
MULTIDRUG-RESISTANT (MDR) Pseudomonas aeruginosa can spread from patient to patient via hands, equipment and the environment (it survives in moist areas). The nurse should use CONTACT PRECAUTIONS in addition to standard precautions: single room or cohorting, gloves and gown/apron for contact with the patient or their surroundings, dedicated equipment, strict hand hygiene, and thorough environmental cleaning.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Standard precautions alone are sufficient with no additional measures — Standard precautions alone are not enough for MDR organisms.
• No precautions are necessary since it is only a wound infection — Precautions are necessary to protect other patients.
• Airborne precautions specifically — Pseudomonas is not airborne; airborne precautions are not specifically required.

⭐ Key point: Inform the infection prevention team, and follow antibiotic stewardship guidance.$x$, null),
(158, $x$A patient in the PACU has a pulse oximetry reading of 90% on room air, with shallow respirations and drowsiness after opioid analgesia. The nurse's priority action is to:$x$, $x$Stimulate the patient, apply supplemental oxygen, and consider naloxone per protocol while notifying the anaesthetist$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Stimulate the patient, apply supplemental oxygen, and consider naloxone per protocol while notifying the anaesthetist
After OPIOID analgesia, a DROWSY patient with SHALLOW breathing and SpO2 90% on room air has OPIOID-INDUCED RESPIRATORY DEPRESSION. Priority actions: STIMULATE the patient (call their name, gentle shake), encourage deep breaths, open the airway, APPLY SUPPLEMENTAL OXYGEN, monitor respiratory rate, SpO2 and sedation score, and CONSIDER NALOXONE (per protocol) if breathing does not improve, while NOTIFYING THE ANAESTHETIST.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase the opioid dose to help the patient rest — Increasing the opioid would worsen respiratory depression.
• Discharge the patient to the ward immediately — Discharging to the ward is unsafe until breathing and sedation improve.
• Document and reassess in one hour — Waiting an hour could allow respiratory arrest.

⭐ Key point: Naloxone may wear off before the opioid — observe closely after reversal.$x$, null),
(159, $x$A patient with suspected mushroom poisoning develops severe gastroenteritis 6-24 hours after ingestion, followed by signs of liver failure over the next several days. Which type of mushroom toxin is most likely responsible?$x$, $x$Amatoxin-containing mushrooms (e.g., Amanita phalloides)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Amatoxin-containing mushrooms (e.g., Amanita phalloides)
AMATOXIN-containing mushrooms (e.g. AMANITA PHALLOIDES, "death cap") cause a typical pattern: a symptom-free delay of 6–24 HOURS, then severe GASTROENTERITIS (vomiting, watery diarrhoea), an apparent improvement, then ACUTE LIVER FAILURE (jaundice, coagulopathy, encephalopathy) over the next few days. Amatoxins block protein synthesis in liver cells. It can be fatal; liver transplant may be needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ibotenic acid-containing mushrooms — Ibotenic acid mushrooms cause early (within 2 hours) neurological symptoms (confusion, hallucinations).
• Muscarine-containing mushrooms — Muscarine mushrooms cause early cholinergic symptoms (sweating, salivation).
• Psilocybin-containing mushrooms — Psilocybin causes hallucinations.

⭐ Key point: Late-onset gastrointestinal symptoms (after 6 hours) after eating wild mushrooms suggest dangerous toxins.$x$, null),
(160, $x$Primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn syndrome) classically presents with which combination of findings?$x$, $x$Hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis with suppressed plasma renin$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis with suppressed plasma renin
PRIMARY HYPERALDOSTERONISM (CONN SYNDROME) is excess ALDOSTERONE production by the adrenal glands (usually an adenoma). Aldosterone makes the kidneys retain SODIUM and water (causing HYPERTENSION) and excrete POTASSIUM and hydrogen ions (causing HYPOKALAEMIA and METABOLIC ALKALOSIS). The high BP and volume SUPPRESS RENIN.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypotension, hyperkalemia, and metabolic acidosis with elevated renin — Hypotension, hyperkalaemia and acidosis describe aldosterone DEFICIENCY (e.g. Addison's).
• Hypertension with elevated renin and elevated aldosterone — Hypertension with high renin and high aldosterone describes SECONDARY hyperaldosteronism (e.g. renal artery stenosis).
• Normal blood pressure with hyperkalemia — Normal BP with hyperkalaemia is not typical.

⭐ Key point: Suspect Conn syndrome in hypertensive patients with low potassium or resistant hypertension.$x$, null),
(161, $x$A patient with acute severe asthma has a silent chest on auscultation. The nurse should interpret this as:$x$, $x$An ominous sign of minimal air movement/impending respiratory failure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: An ominous sign of minimal air movement/impending respiratory failure
In ACUTE SEVERE ASTHMA, a SILENT CHEST means so little air is moving that wheeze can no longer be heard. It is an OMINOUS (very dangerous) sign of severe airway obstruction, exhaustion and IMPENDING RESPIRATORY FAILURE. The patient needs urgent senior help and ICU review.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal findings for an asthmatic patient — It is not normal in an asthmatic.
• A sign of over-treatment with bronchodilators — It is not a sign of over-treatment.
• A reassuring sign that the asthma is resolving — It is not reassuring.

⭐ Key point: Other life-threatening signs: SpO2 below 92%, PEF below 33%, cyanosis, exhaustion, confusion, hypotension.$x$, null),
(162, $x$A patient with acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema presents with pink frothy sputum, orthopnea, and bilateral crackles. Which hemodynamic mechanism best explains the fluid accumulation?$x$, $x$Increased pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure from left ventricular failure$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Increased pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure from left ventricular failure
In CARDIOGENIC PULMONARY OEDEMA, the LEFT VENTRICLE FAILS, so pressure builds up in the left atrium and the lung veins. This INCREASED PULMONARY CAPILLARY HYDROSTATIC PRESSURE pushes fluid out of the capillaries into the lung tissue and alveoli, causing PINK FROTHY SPUTUM, ORTHOPNOEA and BILATERAL CRACKLES.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increased pulmonary capillary permeability from sepsis — Increased capillary permeability is the mechanism in ARDS (non-cardiogenic).
• Decreased plasma oncotic pressure from hypoalbuminemia — Low oncotic pressure causes oedema in liver or kidney disease.
• Lymphatic obstruction from mediastinal malignancy — Lymphatic obstruction is a rare cause.

⭐ Key point: Treat with upright positioning, oxygen/CPAP, IV nitrates and diuretics.$x$, null),
(163, $x$A patient presents with dry flushed skin, dilated pupils, urinary retention, hyperthermia, tachycardia, and confusion after ingesting an unknown substance. This presentation is most consistent with which toxidrome?$x$, $x$Anticholinergic toxidrome$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Anticholinergic toxidrome
The ANTICHOLINERGIC TOXIDROME (e.g. from antihistamines, tricyclic antidepressants, atropine, datura plants) causes: DRY FLUSHED SKIN ("dry as a bone, red as a beet"), DILATED PUPILS ("blind as a bat"), HYPERTHERMIA ("hot as a hare"), CONFUSION ("mad as a hatter"), URINARY RETENTION ("full as a flask"), and TACHYCARDIA.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cholinergic toxidrome — Cholinergic toxidrome causes the OPPOSITE: secretions, small pupils, bradycardia.
• Sedative-hypnotic toxidrome — Sedative-hypnotic toxidrome causes CNS and respiratory depression.
• Opioid toxidrome — Opioid toxidrome causes pinpoint pupils and respiratory depression.

⭐ Key point: The key difference from the sympathomimetic toxidrome is DRY skin (no sweating).$x$, null),
(164, $x$A patient with severe acute pancreatitis has hypocalcaemia. This is primarily explained by:$x$, $x$Calcium being consumed in saponification (fat necrosis) reactions within the abdomen$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Calcium being consumed in saponification (fat necrosis) reactions within the abdomen
In SEVERE ACUTE PANCREATITIS, pancreatic enzymes digest fat around the pancreas (FAT NECROSIS). The released fatty acids bind CALCIUM to form insoluble "soaps" (SAPONIFICATION). This removes calcium from the blood, causing HYPOCALCAEMIA. It indicates severe disease.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A pre-existing unrelated condition — It is directly related to the pancreatitis.
• Excess dietary calcium intake — Excess dietary calcium does not cause hypocalcaemia.
• Overactive parathyroid glands — Overactive parathyroid glands cause HIGH calcium.

⭐ Key point: Monitor calcium and correct with IV calcium gluconate if symptomatic.$x$, null),
(165, $x$A patient presents with poisoning from ingestion of a substance containing methanol found in illicitly distilled alcohol ('moonshine'). Which clinical feature is a classic warning sign of methanol toxicity?$x$, $x$Visual disturbances or blindness due to optic nerve toxicity from formic acid accumulation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Visual disturbances or blindness due to optic nerve toxicity from formic acid accumulation
METHANOL (in illicitly distilled alcohol) is converted by the liver to FORMIC ACID, which damages the OPTIC NERVE and retina, causing VISUAL DISTURBANCES ("snowstorm vision", blurred vision) and BLINDNESS. It also causes severe high anion gap metabolic acidosis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Excessive salivation and lacrimation — Excessive salivation and lacrimation suggest cholinergic poisoning.
• Bright red skin discolouration — Bright red skin suggests cyanide or CO.
• Pinpoint pupils — Pinpoint pupils suggest opioids.

⭐ Key point: Treat with fomepizole or ethanol, bicarbonate, folic acid, and haemodialysis.$x$, null),
(166, $x$A patient with heart failure receiving digoxin has a potassium level of 3.0mmol/L. The nurse should recognise this combination as significant because it:$x$, $x$Increases the risk of digoxin toxicity and arrhythmia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Increases the risk of digoxin toxicity and arrhythmia
DIGOXIN competes with potassium for binding to the sodium-potassium pump in heart cells. When POTASSIUM is LOW (3.0 mmol/L), digoxin binds more strongly, INCREASING TOXICITY and the risk of ARRHYTHMIAS (e.g. ventricular ectopics, bigeminy, VT, heart block).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reduces the risk of digoxin toxicity — Low potassium increases, not reduces, toxicity.
• Only affects blood pressure, not cardiac rhythm — It affects heart rhythm.
• Has no bearing on digoxin's effect — Potassium has a major effect on digoxin.

⭐ Key point: Keep potassium above 4.0 mmol/L in patients on digoxin.$x$, null),
(167, $x$A patient with cardiac tamponade would be expected to show which combination of findings (Beck's Triad)?$x$, $x$Hypotension, jugular venous distension, muffled heart sounds$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hypotension, jugular venous distension, muffled heart sounds
BECK'S TRIAD indicates CARDIAC TAMPONADE (fluid compressing the heart): HYPOTENSION (low cardiac output), JUGULAR VENOUS DISTENSION (blood backs up), and MUFFLED HEART SOUNDS (fluid dampens sound).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fever, cough, wheeze — Fever, cough and wheeze suggest respiratory infection.
• Polyuria, polydipsia, weight loss — Polyuria, polydipsia and weight loss suggest diabetes.
• Hypertension, bradycardia, loud heart sounds — Hypertension, bradycardia and loud heart sounds are not tamponade signs.

⭐ Key point: Pulsus paradoxus (BP drop >10 mmHg on inspiration) is another sign. Treat with pericardiocentesis.$x$, null),
(168, $x$A patient six hours post-thyroidectomy develops increasing neck swelling and stridor. The nurse's priority action is to:$x$, $x$Call for immediate help, sit the patient upright, give high-flow oxygen, and prepare for emergency wound release if instructed$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Call for immediate help, sit the patient upright, give high-flow oxygen, and prepare for emergency wound release if instructed
INCREASING NECK SWELLING and STRIDOR after THYROIDECTOMY suggest a HAEMATOMA (bleeding) compressing the airway. This is a life-threatening emergency. CALL FOR IMMEDIATE HELP, SIT THE PATIENT UPRIGHT, give HIGH-FLOW OXYGEN, and prepare to RELEASE the wound (open skin and muscle sutures — "SCOOP": skin exposure, cut sutures, open skin, open muscles, pack wound) to evacuate the blood if instructed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reassure the patient this is normal post-operative swelling with no action needed — It is not normal swelling.
• Discharge the patient home for outpatient follow-up — Discharge would be fatal.
• Apply a tight neck dressing to control swelling — A tight dressing would worsen airway compression.

⭐ Key point: Keep a suture removal kit at the bedside after thyroid surgery.$x$, null),
(169, $x$A patient's spinal cord injury results in preserved diaphragmatic breathing but loss of intercostal muscle function and all function below the upper chest. This is most consistent with an injury at approximately which level?$x$, $x$C6-C8/T1$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: C6-C8/T1
The DIAPHRAGM is supplied by the phrenic nerve (C3–C5), so diaphragmatic breathing is PRESERVED in injuries below C5. INTERCOSTAL MUSCLES are supplied by thoracic nerves (T1–T12), so an injury around C6–C8/T1 causes LOSS OF INTERCOSTAL function and function below the upper chest.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• C1-C2 — C1–C2 injuries paralyse the diaphragm.
• T12-L1 — T12–L1 injuries preserve the intercostals.
• S2-S4 — S2–S4 injuries affect the bladder, bowel and sexual function.

⭐ Key point: Patients with lower cervical injuries breathe with the diaphragm alone and have a weak cough — monitor vital capacity.$x$, null),
(170, $x$A patient with severe liver disease develops ascites and peripheral oedema. The nurse understands this is partly explained by the liver's reduced synthesis of:$x$, $x$Albumin, reducing plasma oncotic pressure and allowing fluid to shift into tissues/peritoneal cavity$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Albumin, reducing plasma oncotic pressure and allowing fluid to shift into tissues/peritoneal cavity
The LIVER makes ALBUMIN, the main protein that holds fluid inside blood vessels (ONCOTIC PRESSURE). In severe liver disease, albumin falls, oncotic pressure drops, and fluid leaks into tissues (PERIPHERAL OEDEMA) and the abdomen (ASCITES). Portal hypertension also contributes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Insulin only — The liver doesn't make insulin (the pancreas does).
• Red blood cells — Red blood cells are made in bone marrow.
• White blood cells — White blood cells are made in bone marrow.

⭐ Key point: Treat ascites with salt restriction, diuretics (spironolactone), and paracentesis with albumin.$x$, null),
(171, $x$Primary hyperparathyroidism, most commonly caused by a solitary parathyroid adenoma, classically presents with which laboratory pattern?$x$, $x$Elevated serum calcium with elevated or inappropriately normal PTH$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Elevated serum calcium with elevated or inappropriately normal PTH
PRIMARY HYPERPARATHYROIDISM (usually a parathyroid adenoma) causes HIGH CALCIUM with HIGH or INAPPROPRIATELY NORMAL PTH. Normally, high calcium suppresses PTH; in this condition, PTH is not suppressed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Low serum calcium with low PTH — Low calcium with low PTH is hypoparathyroidism.
• Elevated serum calcium with suppressed PTH — High calcium with suppressed PTH suggests other causes (e.g. cancer).
• Low serum calcium with elevated PTH — Low calcium with high PTH is secondary hyperparathyroidism (e.g. kidney disease, vitamin D deficiency).

⭐ Key point: Symptoms: "bones, stones, groans, moans" — bone pain, kidney stones, abdominal pain, depression.$x$, null),
(172, $x$A patient with a severe brain injury is noted to have a rising temperature unrelated to infection. The nurse understands this can occur due to:$x$, $x$Hypothalamic dysfunction from the injury itself, sometimes called 'central fever'$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Hypothalamic dysfunction from the injury itself, sometimes called 'central fever'
The HYPOTHALAMUS controls body temperature. In severe brain injury, damage to the hypothalamus can cause FEVER without infection — "CENTRAL" (neurogenic) FEVER. However, infection must still be ruled out.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple dehydration only — Dehydration may contribute but is not the only cause.
• No relationship between brain injury and temperature regulation — Brain injury can affect temperature regulation.
• Always indicates infection with no other cause possible — Fever does not always indicate infection.

⭐ Key point: Fever worsens brain injury — keep temperature normal (cooling, antipyretics).$x$, null),
(173, $x$A patient's angina is unrelieved after 3 sublingual nitroglycerin tablets taken 5 minutes apart. The priority nursing action is to:$x$, $x$Notify the provider/activate emergency response and evaluate for acute MI$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Notify the provider/activate emergency response and evaluate for acute MI
If chest pain is NOT RELIEVED after 3 sublingual GTN doses 5 minutes apart, it may be an ACUTE MYOCARDIAL INFARCTION (heart attack). NOTIFY THE PROVIDER/ACTIVATE EMERGENCY RESPONSE, perform a 12-lead ECG, give aspirin (if not contraindicated), oxygen if hypoxic, and prepare for cardiac care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administer a 4th tablet and wait 15 more minutes — A 4th tablet and waiting delays treatment.
• Encourage the patient to walk to reduce anxiety — Walking increases heart workload.
• Document and reassess in 1 hour — Waiting an hour risks heart muscle death.

⭐ Key point: "Time is muscle" — rapid reperfusion saves heart tissue.$x$, null),
(174, $x$A patient with clear fluid draining from the nose after head trauma should be assessed for:$x$, $x$Cerebrospinal fluid rhinorrhoea, a sign of possible skull base fracture with dural tear$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Cerebrospinal fluid rhinorrhoea, a sign of possible skull base fracture with dural tear
CLEAR FLUID from the nose after head trauma may be CEREBROSPINAL FLUID (CSF RHINORRHOEA), indicating a SKULL BASE FRACTURE with a tear in the dura. This increases the risk of MENINGITIS. Test the fluid for glucose or beta-2 transferrin, and avoid nasal tubes and suctioning.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No clinical significance — It is clinically significant.
• A common cold unrelated to the trauma — It is unlikely to be a cold.
• Simple allergic rhinitis with no further concern — Allergic rhinitis is not the cause.

⭐ Key point: Other signs of skull base fracture: raccoon eyes, Battle's sign, haemotympanum, CSF otorrhoea.$x$, null),
(175, $x$A 55 year old male patient is admitted with a massive GI bleed. The patient is at risk for what type of acute kidney injury?$x$, $x$Pre-renal$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Pre-renal
A MASSIVE GI BLEED causes HYPOVOLAEMIA (loss of circulating blood). The kidneys receive less blood flow, so the glomerular filtration rate falls — this is PRE-RENAL AKI. At first the kidneys themselves are not damaged; urine output falls, urine is concentrated with low sodium (the kidneys are trying to save water and salt), and the urea rises more than creatinine (also from digested blood in the gut). If hypoperfusion continues, it can progress to acute tubular necrosis (intrinsic damage).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Intrinsic renal — Intrinsic renal AKI is direct damage to the kidney tissue (e.g. ATN, glomerulonephritis, nephrotoxins).
• Intra-renal — Intra-renal is another name for intrinsic renal AKI.
• Post-renal — Post-renal AKI is caused by obstruction to urine outflow (e.g. enlarged prostate, blocked catheter).

⭐ Key point: Rapid restoration of blood volume (fluids and blood transfusion) and stopping the bleeding can reverse pre-renal AKI before permanent damage occurs.$x$, null),
(176, $x$A patient on CRRT (Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy) develops a sudden drop in filter/circuit pressure with visible clotting in the circuit. The nurse's priority action is to:$x$, $x$Assess anticoagulation status, notify the team, and prepare for circuit change per protocol, since a clotted circuit stops effective filtration$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Assess anticoagulation status, notify the team, and prepare for circuit change per protocol, since a clotted circuit stops effective filtration
A CLOTTED CRRT circuit STOPS effective filtration and wastes blood (the blood in the circuit is lost). The nurse should ASSESS ANTICOAGULATION (e.g. citrate levels, heparin dose), NOTIFY the team, and PREPARE for a CIRCUIT CHANGE per protocol. Blood may be returned to the patient if possible before the circuit clots completely.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Disconnect the patient permanently from all renal support — Permanently disconnecting renal support is inappropriate.
• Continue therapy unchanged — Continuing unchanged wastes blood.
• Increase the blood flow rate significantly without assessment — Increasing blood flow without assessment is unsafe.

⭐ Key point: Prevent clotting with adequate anticoagulation, good catheter flow, and minimising interruptions.$x$, null),
(177, $x$A patient with a history of asthma receiving nebulized acetylcysteine should be monitored closely for:$x$, $x$Bronchospasm$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Bronchospasm
ACETYLCYSTEINE (N-acetylcysteine) given by NEBULISER breaks down thick mucus (mucolytic). However, it can IRRITATE the airways and trigger BRONCHOSPASM — especially in patients with ASTHMA or reactive airways. Signs: wheeze, chest tightness, increased work of breathing, falling SpO2. Many protocols give a bronchodilator (e.g. salbutamol) before or with nebulised acetylcysteine, and monitor closely.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypertension — Acetylcysteine does not typically cause hypertension.
• Hyperkalemia — It does not cause hyperkalaemia.
• Serotonin syndrome — It does not cause serotonin syndrome.

⭐ Key point: IV acetylcysteine (for paracetamol overdose) can cause anaphylactoid reactions (rash, bronchospasm, hypotension) — slow or pause the infusion if this occurs.$x$, null),
(178, $x$A patient with pneumonia develops persistent fever, pleuritic chest pain, and a loculated pleural fluid collection with low pH and low glucose on pleural fluid analysis. This is most consistent with:$x$, $x$Empyema (infected pleural effusion)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Empyema (infected pleural effusion)
An EMPYEMA is an INFECTED pleural effusion (pus in the pleural space), usually complicating PNEUMONIA. Features: PERSISTENT FEVER despite antibiotics, PLEURITIC CHEST PAIN, and a LOCULATED (walled-off, divided into pockets) fluid collection on imaging. Pleural fluid analysis shows LOW pH (below 7.2), LOW GLUCOSE (bacteria and white cells consume it), high LDH, and may show pus or organisms on Gram stain.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pulmonary embolism — Pulmonary embolism may cause a small effusion, but not an infected, loculated collection with low pH and glucose.
• A simple transudative effusion from heart failure — Heart failure causes a TRANSUDATE with normal pH and glucose.
• Chronic bronchitis — Chronic bronchitis does not cause pleural collections.

⭐ Key point: Treat with IV antibiotics AND drainage (chest tube); loculated empyema may need intrapleural fibrinolytics (tPA/DNase) or surgery (VATS).$x$, null),
(179, $x$A patient with a suspected benzodiazepine overdose is being considered for flumazenil administration. Which condition makes flumazenil relatively contraindicated?$x$, $x$Chronic benzodiazepine use or co-ingestion with a pro-convulsant drug such as a tricyclic antidepressant, due to seizure risk$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Chronic benzodiazepine use or co-ingestion with a pro-convulsant drug such as a tricyclic antidepressant, due to seizure risk
FLUMAZENIL reverses benzodiazepines, but can trigger SEIZURES in patients with CHRONIC BENZODIAZEPINE USE (sudden withdrawal) or co-ingestion of PRO-CONVULSANT drugs such as TRICYCLIC ANTIDEPRESSANTS (the benzodiazepine was suppressing seizures).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal renal function — Normal renal function is not a contraindication.
• Mild drowsiness without respiratory compromise — Mild drowsiness doesn't need flumazenil.
• Known allergy to opioids — Opioid allergy is unrelated.

⭐ Key point: Supportive care (airway, breathing) is usually safer than flumazenil in overdose.$x$, null),
(180, $x$A patient with a newly inserted temporary transvenous pacemaker shows pacer spikes with no captured QRS complex at times, worsening when the patient takes a deep breath. This is most likely due to:$x$, $x$Lead micro-displacement affecting capture with patient movement/position change, requiring evaluation$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Lead micro-displacement affecting capture with patient movement/position change, requiring evaluation
FAILURE TO CAPTURE (pacing spikes without QRS complexes) that WORSENS WITH BREATHING suggests the pacing LEAD has MICRO-DISPLACED — it moves away from the heart wall with breathing and position changes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Battery failure only — Battery failure would cause no spikes or reduced output.
• Improved lead contact — Improved contact would improve capture.
• Normal pacemaker function with no concern — Failure to capture is never normal.

⭐ Key point: Check connections, increase output per protocol, and arrange a chest X-ray.$x$, null),
(181, $x$A patient with suspected food poisoning from Clostridium botulinum presents with descending paralysis, diplopia, and dysphagia, with a clear sensorium. What is the priority nursing concern?$x$, $x$Close monitoring of respiratory function, as respiratory muscle paralysis can develop and require ventilatory support$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Close monitoring of respiratory function, as respiratory muscle paralysis can develop and require ventilatory support
BOTULISM causes DESCENDING PARALYSIS (eyes, face, throat, then breathing muscles). The patient remains conscious. The priority is to MONITOR RESPIRATORY FUNCTION (vital capacity, breathing pattern, SpO2), as RESPIRATORY MUSCLE PARALYSIS can develop and require ventilation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administering activated charcoal, since this is the priority treatment — Activated charcoal isn't the priority.
• Reassuring the patient that no monitoring is required since consciousness is preserved — Monitoring is essential.
• Restricting fluids to prevent worsening symptoms — Fluid restriction isn't indicated.

⭐ Key point: Give antitoxin early and notify public health.$x$, null),
(182, $x$A patient with a subarachnoid haemorrhage develops new confusion and weakness on day 7, distinct from their baseline post-bleed status. The nurse should have a high suspicion for:$x$, $x$Delayed cerebral ischaemia from vasospasm, a recognised complication in the days following subarachnoid haemorrhage$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Delayed cerebral ischaemia from vasospasm, a recognised complication in the days following subarachnoid haemorrhage
After SUBARACHNOID HAEMORRHAGE, blood around the brain arteries triggers VASOSPASM, usually starting around day 3–4, peaking at days 7–10, and resolving by about day 14–21. It can cause DELAYED CEREBRAL ISCHAEMIA (DCI): NEW CONFUSION, reduced consciousness, or FOCAL WEAKNESS that was not present before. On day 7, new confusion and weakness should raise high suspicion. Other causes to exclude: rebleeding, hydrocephalus, hyponatraemia, seizures and infection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal recovery progress — New deficits are not normal recovery.
• An unrelated finding — They are directly related to the SAH.
• Improvement in neurological status — New confusion and weakness show deterioration, not improvement.

⭐ Key point: Prevention: nimodipine for 21 days, maintain normal blood volume and sodium, avoid hypotension. Report any new neurological change immediately — urgent CT/CT angiography may be needed.$x$, null),
(183, $x$A patient is admitted 8 hours after a large intentional paracetamol (acetaminophen) overdose. Which investigation is essential to guide treatment decisions?$x$, $x$A serum paracetamol level plotted on the treatment nomogram against time since ingestion$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A serum paracetamol level plotted on the treatment nomogram against time since ingestion
For a paracetamol overdose presenting 8 HOURS after ingestion, a SERUM PARACETAMOL LEVEL (taken at least 4 hours after ingestion) is plotted on the TREATMENT NOMOGRAM against time since ingestion. If above the treatment line, give N-acetylcysteine. (If presenting late or uncertain, start NAC immediately.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A random blood glucose only — Glucose alone doesn't guide treatment.
• A chest X-ray — Chest X-ray doesn't guide treatment.
• A urine drug screen for opioids — Urine drug screen doesn't measure paracetamol risk.

⭐ Key point: NAC is most effective within 8 hours of ingestion.$x$, null),
(184, $x$A woman in active labour develops a sudden onset of severe abdominal pain, vaginal bleeding, and a change in fetal heart rate pattern to bradycardia after a previous caesarean section. This should raise suspicion for:$x$, $x$Uterine rupture at the site of the previous caesarean scar$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Uterine rupture at the site of the previous caesarean scar
UTERINE RUPTURE is a tear in the uterine wall, most commonly through a PREVIOUS CAESAREAN SECTION SCAR during labour (VBAC — vaginal birth after caesarean). Classic features: SUDDEN SEVERE ABDOMINAL PAIN (may persist between contractions), VAGINAL BLEEDING, FETAL HEART RATE ABNORMALITIES (especially sudden BRADYCARDIA — the most common sign), loss of contractions, the presenting part moving back up, and signs of maternal shock.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Braxton Hicks contractions — Braxton Hicks contractions are irregular, painless or mildly uncomfortable "practice" contractions — not this.
• Simple round ligament pain — Round ligament pain is a mild, sharp groin pain in pregnancy, not associated with bleeding and fetal bradycardia.
• Normal labour progress — This is not normal labour progress.

⭐ Key point: Uterine rupture is an emergency: call for help, resuscitate the mother (large-bore IV, blood), and arrange immediate laparotomy to deliver the baby and repair or remove the uterus.$x$, null),
(185, $x$A patient who has been admitted with a gastric ulcer now develops severe epigastric pain that spreads over the entire abdomen. He is very pale and is lying on his side. What should be the nurse's immediate action?$x$, $x$Notify the doctor and check the vitals.$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Notify the doctor and check the vitals.
SEVERE EPIGASTRIC PAIN spreading over the WHOLE ABDOMEN in a patient with a GASTRIC ULCER suggests PERFORATION and PERITONITIS. PALLOR suggests shock or bleeding. The immediate action is to NOTIFY THE DOCTOR and CHECK VITAL SIGNS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reposition the patient In Semi-Fowler's Position. — Repositioning is not the priority.
• Perform deep palpations on the abdomen. — Deep palpation would cause pain and is unnecessary.
• Immediately administer opioids. — Opioids without assessment may mask signs.

⭐ Key point: Prepare for IV access, fluids, nil by mouth and surgery.$x$, null),
(186, $x$A patient with beta-blocker overdose presents with severe bradycardia and hypotension refractory to atropine. Which agent is most appropriate as a specific treatment?$x$, $x$High-dose insulin with glucose (hyperinsulinaemia-euglycaemia therapy) and/or glucagon$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: High-dose insulin with glucose (hyperinsulinaemia-euglycaemia therapy) and/or glucagon
BETA-BLOCKER OVERDOSE blocks the beta receptors in the heart, causing severe BRADYCARDIA, heart block and HYPOTENSION (cardiogenic shock). Atropine often fails. Specific treatments include: GLUCAGON — it raises cyclic AMP in heart cells through its own receptors, BYPASSING the blocked beta receptors, increasing heart rate and contractility; and HIGH-DOSE INSULIN with GLUCOSE (hyperinsulinaemia–euglycaemia therapy, HIET) — it helps the stressed heart muscle use glucose for energy, improving contractility. Other options: calcium, vasopressors, pacing, and ECMO.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Naloxone — Naloxone reverses opioids, not beta-blockers.
• Flumazenil — Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines.
• Activated charcoal given IV — Activated charcoal is given ORALLY (not IV), and only early with a protected airway.

⭐ Key point: During HIET, check glucose every 15–30 minutes and potassium frequently (insulin drives potassium into cells).$x$, null),
(187, $x$A woman with an ectopic pregnancy presents with unilateral lower abdominal pain, vaginal bleeding, and signs of hypovolaemic shock. This presentation should be treated as:$x$, $x$A life-threatening gynaecological emergency requiring urgent assessment and likely surgical intervention$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: A life-threatening gynaecological emergency requiring urgent assessment and likely surgical intervention
An ECTOPIC PREGNANCY implants outside the uterus, usually in the fallopian tube. As it grows, the tube can RUPTURE, causing MASSIVE INTERNAL BLEEDING into the abdomen. Unilateral lower abdominal pain, vaginal bleeding and signs of HYPOVOLAEMIC SHOCK (tachycardia, hypotension, pallor, collapse, shoulder tip pain from blood irritating the diaphragm) mean a LIFE-THREATENING emergency. Actions: call for help, two large-bore IV cannulas, crossmatch blood, fluid and blood resuscitation, and URGENT SURGERY (laparoscopy or laparotomy).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A urinary tract infection — A UTI does not cause shock with vaginal bleeding in pregnancy.
• Normal early pregnancy discomfort — Shock is never normal pregnancy discomfort.
• A routine outpatient concern — This is not a routine outpatient concern; delay can be fatal.

⭐ Key point: Think of ectopic pregnancy in any woman of childbearing age with abdominal pain or collapse — do a pregnancy test.$x$, null),
(188, $x$A patient develops sudden chest pain and dyspnoea after central line insertion. A chest X-ray shows a hyperlucent area with absent lung markings on the same side. This confirms:$x$, $x$Pneumothorax, a recognised complication of central line insertion$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Pneumothorax, a recognised complication of central line insertion
During CENTRAL LINE insertion (especially SUBCLAVIAN and, less often, internal jugular), the needle can puncture the LUNG, letting air into the pleural space — a PNEUMOTHORAX. Symptoms: SUDDEN CHEST PAIN, BREATHLESSNESS, low SpO2, reduced breath sounds. The chest X-ray shows a HYPERLUCENT (darker) area with ABSENT LUNG MARKINGS beyond a visible lung edge on the same side.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Successful catheter placement with no complication — These findings indicate a complication, not successful placement.
• Pleural effusion — A pleural effusion shows WHITE (opaque) fluid, not a hyperlucent area.
• Normal post-procedure findings — These are not normal post-procedure findings.

⭐ Key point: Always obtain a chest X-ray after subclavian or jugular line insertion (to check tip position and exclude pneumothorax). Ultrasound-guided insertion reduces this risk.$x$, null),
(189, $x$A farm worker presents with excessive salivation, lacrimation, urination, diarrhoea, GI cramps, emesis (SLUDGE), miosis, bradycardia, and muscle fasciculations after exposure to a crop spray. Which class of toxin is most likely responsible?$x$, $x$Organophosphate (cholinergic) poisoning$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Organophosphate (cholinergic) poisoning
ORGANOPHOSPHATE pesticides (crop sprays) block the enzyme ACETYLCHOLINESTERASE, so ACETYLCHOLINE builds up at nerve endings. This causes CHOLINERGIC overstimulation: muscarinic effects — SLUDGE (Salivation, Lacrimation, Urination, Diarrhoea, GI cramps, Emesis), MIOSIS (pinpoint pupils), BRADYCARDIA, bronchorrhoea and bronchospasm ("killer Bs"); nicotinic effects — MUSCLE FASCICULATIONS, then weakness and paralysis; CNS effects — confusion, seizures, coma.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anticholinergic poisoning — Anticholinergic poisoning causes the opposite: dry skin, dilated pupils, tachycardia.
• Opioid overdose — Opioids cause pinpoint pupils and drowsiness, but not SLUDGE or fasciculations.
• Sympathomimetic overdose — Sympathomimetics cause dilated pupils, tachycardia and hypertension.

⭐ Key point: Treat with ATROPINE (in doubling doses until the chest is clear) and PRALIDOXIME; remove contaminated clothing and wash the skin; staff wear PPE.$x$, null),
(190, $x$A patient with a large pleural effusion undergoes therapeutic thoracentesis. As a general safety principle, fluid removal in a single session is often limited to approximately:$x$, $x$Around 1–1.5 litres, to reduce the risk of re-expansion pulmonary oedema$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Around 1–1.5 litres, to reduce the risk of re-expansion pulmonary oedema
Removing a large volume of pleural fluid too quickly allows the collapsed lung to RE-EXPAND suddenly. The damaged lung capillaries can then leak fluid, causing RE-EXPANSION PULMONARY OEDEMA — breathlessness, cough, frothy sputum and hypoxia. To reduce this risk, fluid removal in a SINGLE SESSION is usually limited to about 1–1.5 LITRES. The procedure should be stopped earlier if the patient develops cough, chest tightness, pain, or hypotension.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10 litres — Removing 10 litres at once would be very dangerous.
• No limit at all, regardless of volume — There is a recommended limit.
• Only 10ml — 10 mL is far too little to relieve symptoms (but enough for diagnostic analysis).

⭐ Key point: For large effusions, drain slowly over time (e.g. clamping the drain intermittently per protocol) and monitor the patient closely.$x$, null),
(191, $x$A patient with a PAC develops a new PA waveform that shows no dicrotic notch and looks like a damped, flattened line even with the balloon deflated. The FIRST nursing action should be to check:$x$, $x$The transducer connections, tubing for air/clots, and confirm the stopcocks are positioned correctly before escalating$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: The transducer connections, tubing for air/clots, and confirm the stopcocks are positioned correctly before escalating
A DAMPED PA waveform with no dicrotic notch, even with the balloon deflated, may be due to TECHNICAL problems: loose TRANSDUCER connections, AIR bubbles or CLOTS in the tubing, or STOPCOCKS in the wrong position. Check these FIRST before escalating (it may also indicate catheter migration).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The patient's temperature — Temperature is unrelated.
• The pacemaker settings — Pacemaker settings are unrelated.
• The patient's oral intake — Oral intake is unrelated.

⭐ Key point: Perform a fast-flush test to check the system.$x$, null),
(192, $x$A client on propylthiouracil develops a sore throat and fever. What should the nurse suspect?$x$, $x$Agranulocytosis$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Agranulocytosis
PROPYLTHIOURACIL (PTU) and carbimazole are antithyroid drugs. A rare but serious side effect is AGRANULOCYTOSIS — a severe drop in NEUTROPHILS (white cells that fight bacterial infection). It usually occurs in the first few months of therapy. The first signs are often SORE THROAT, FEVER, mouth ulcers or other infections. The nurse should suspect agranulocytosis, advise stopping the drug, and arrange an URGENT full blood count. If confirmed, the patient needs isolation precautions and possibly antibiotics and G-CSF.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Thyroid storm — Thyroid storm causes very high fever, tachycardia, agitation — not mainly a sore throat.
• Myxoedema — Myxoedema is severe HYPOthyroidism.
• Hypocalcaemia — Hypocalcaemia causes tingling and spasms, not sore throat and fever.

⭐ Key point: Teach all patients starting antithyroid drugs to report sore throat, fever or mouth ulcers immediately. PTU can also cause severe liver injury.$x$, null),
(193, $x$A patient with increased intracranial pressure is being nursed. Which positioning is generally recommended to help reduce ICP?$x$, $x$Head of bed elevated 30 degrees, head in neutral alignment$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Head of bed elevated 30 degrees, head in neutral alignment
To help REDUCE INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE (ICP), the head of the bed is ELEVATED to about 30 DEGREES and the HEAD is kept in NEUTRAL ALIGNMENT (not turned or flexed). This uses gravity to improve VENOUS DRAINAGE from the brain through the jugular veins, which lowers ICP, while still maintaining enough blood pressure to the brain. Also avoid tight ET tube ties, cervical collars that compress the neck, hip flexion above 90°, and sudden position changes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Prone position — Prone positioning raises intra-abdominal and intrathoracic pressure, which can raise ICP.
• Trendelenburg (head down) position — Trendelenburg (head-down) increases blood volume in the head and raises ICP.
• Head of bed flat, neck flexed — Lying flat with the neck flexed obstructs venous drainage and raises ICP.

⭐ Key point: Other ICP measures: adequate sedation and analgesia, avoid coughing and straining, keep PaCO2 normal, treat fever and seizures, osmotherapy.$x$, null),
(194, $x$A patient with methanol or ethylene glycol poisoning is being managed. Which agent competitively inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase and is used as an antidote?$x$, $x$Fomepizole (or ethanol as an alternative)$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Fomepizole (or ethanol as an alternative)
METHANOL and ETHYLENE GLYCOL are not very toxic themselves. The enzyme ALCOHOL DEHYDROGENASE in the liver converts them into TOXIC METABOLITES: methanol → formic acid (causes blindness and acidosis); ethylene glycol → glycolic and oxalic acid (causes acidosis and kidney failure). FOMEPIZOLE competitively INHIBITS alcohol dehydrogenase, preventing the formation of these toxic acids. ETHANOL (drinking alcohol) works the same way — the enzyme prefers ethanol — and is used if fomepizole is not available.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Atropine — Atropine treats organophosphate (cholinergic) poisoning.
• Naloxone — Naloxone reverses opioid overdose.
• Flumazenil — Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines.

⭐ Key point: HAEMODIALYSIS removes both the parent alcohols and their toxic acids. Give folic acid (methanol) or thiamine and pyridoxine (ethylene glycol) as co-factors.$x$, null),
(195, $x$A patient with tetanus develops trismus (lockjaw) as an early symptom. The nurse understands this occurs because tetanus toxin:$x$, $x$Blocks inhibitory neurotransmitters (glycine and GABA), causing sustained, uncontrolled muscle contraction$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Blocks inhibitory neurotransmitters (glycine and GABA), causing sustained, uncontrolled muscle contraction
TETANUS TOXIN blocks the release of INHIBITORY neurotransmitters (GLYCINE and GABA) in the spinal cord. Without inhibition, muscles CONTRACT continuously, causing TRISMUS (lockjaw), muscle spasms and rigidity.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only affects the peripheral sensory nerves — It affects motor, not sensory, nerves.
• Directly paralyses the diaphragm first — It doesn't paralyse the diaphragm first.
• Causes generalised sedation — It doesn't cause sedation.

⭐ Key point: Treat with tetanus immunoglobulin, wound care, metronidazole, muscle relaxants, and a quiet, dark environment.$x$, null),
(196, $x$A patient with obstructive uropathy from bilateral ureteral stones is treated with nephrostomy tube placement. What immediate postobstructive complication should the nurse monitor for?$x$, $x$Post-obstructive diuresis with large fluid and electrolyte losses$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Post-obstructive diuresis with large fluid and electrolyte losses
When a long-standing URINARY OBSTRUCTION is RELIEVED (e.g. by nephrostomy tubes or catheter), the kidneys may produce VERY LARGE VOLUMES of urine — POST-OBSTRUCTIVE DIURESIS (often above 200 mL/hr). Reasons: excretion of accumulated urea and salt, and the damaged tubules' temporary inability to concentrate urine. This can quickly cause HYPOVOLAEMIA, low BP and ELECTROLYTE LOSSES (sodium, potassium, magnesium).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Spontaneous resolution of chronic kidney disease — Chronic kidney disease does not resolve spontaneously.
• Immediate anuria — Anuria is unlikely immediately after relief of obstruction.
• Hypertension crisis only — Hypertensive crisis is not the main post-obstructive concern; hypovolaemia is.

⭐ Key point: Measure urine output hourly, check electrolytes frequently, and replace fluids (often a proportion of the output) as prescribed.$x$, null),
(197, $x$A patient with severe cirrhosis of the liver develops hepatorenal syndrome. Which of the following nursing assessment data would support this?$x$, $x$Oliguria and azotemia$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Oliguria and azotemia
HEPATORENAL SYNDROME (HRS) is kidney failure in a patient with severe liver disease (cirrhosis, ascites). The kidneys themselves are structurally normal, but severe VASOCONSTRICTION of the kidney blood vessels (caused by vasodilation elsewhere and activation of renin-angiotensin and sympathetic systems) reduces blood flow. Typical findings: OLIGURIA (low urine output) and AZOTAEMIA (rising urea and creatinine), with very low urine sodium, and no improvement after stopping diuretics and giving albumin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis is not a key feature of HRS.
• Decreased urinary concentration — Urine in HRS is concentrated (low sodium), not dilute.
• Weight gain of less than 1kg per week — Weight gain reflects ascites; slow weight gain is not a specific marker of HRS.

⭐ Key point: Treatment: terlipressin (or noradrenaline) plus IV albumin; liver transplant is the definitive cure.$x$, null),
(198, $x$A patient under spinal anaesthesia for a caesarean section develops sudden hypotension and nausea. The nurse should recognise this as likely due to:$x$, $x$Sympathetic blockade from the spinal anaesthesia causing vasodilation, typically managed with fluids and/or vasopressors$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Sympathetic blockade from the spinal anaesthesia causing vasodilation, typically managed with fluids and/or vasopressors
SPINAL ANAESTHESIA for caesarean section blocks the SYMPATHETIC nerves (which keep blood vessels constricted). The vessels in the lower body DILATE, blood pools, and BP falls (HYPOTENSION). Low BP reduces blood flow to the brain (causing NAUSEA, dizziness) and to the placenta (risking fetal distress). Management: LEFT UTERINE DISPLACEMENT (to relieve aortocaval compression), IV FLUIDS (co-loading), and VASOPRESSORS (phenylephrine first-line, or ephedrine).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An allergic reaction requiring adrenaline — An allergic reaction would typically cause rash, wheeze and swelling — adrenaline is not the first treatment here.
• A sign of successful anaesthesia with no action needed — Hypotension is common but must be treated, not ignored.
• Unrelated cardiac disease — It is not due to unrelated cardiac disease.

⭐ Key point: Nausea after spinal anaesthesia is often the first sign of hypotension — check the BP immediately.$x$, null),
(199, $x$A patient with severe theophylline toxicity develops refractory seizures and life-threatening arrhythmias. Beyond supportive care and multiple-dose activated charcoal, which additional treatment may be indicated in severe cases?$x$, $x$Haemodialysis or haemoperfusion$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Haemodialysis or haemoperfusion
THEOPHYLLINE (and aminophylline) has a NARROW THERAPEUTIC RANGE. Severe toxicity causes vomiting, tachycardia, life-threatening ARRHYTHMIAS, hypokalaemia, hyperglycaemia and REFRACTORY SEIZURES. Treatment: supportive care, MULTIPLE-DOSE ACTIVATED CHARCOAL (enhances elimination), and in severe cases (seizures, arrhythmias, very high levels), HAEMODIALYSIS or HAEMOPERFUSION, which rapidly remove theophylline from the blood.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Discontinuation of all monitoring — Discontinuing monitoring would be dangerous.
• Oral rehydration therapy alone — Oral rehydration alone is inadequate.
• Induced vomiting — Induced vomiting is not recommended (aspiration risk, seizures).

⭐ Key point: Many drugs (e.g. ciprofloxacin, erythromycin) increase theophylline levels; smoking reduces them. Monitor levels.$x$, null),
(200, $x$A patient with a pulmonary artery catheter (PAC) in place develops a persistently wedged waveform. The nurse's most appropriate immediate action is to:$x$, $x$Deflate the balloon immediately and reposition/notify the physician, as a permanently wedged catheter risks pulmonary infarction$x$, $x$✅ Correct answer: Deflate the balloon immediately and reposition/notify the physician, as a permanently wedged catheter risks pulmonary infarction
A PERSISTENTLY WEDGED PA catheter waveform means the catheter tip has migrated into a small pulmonary artery, blocking blood flow and risking PULMONARY INFARCTION or rupture. DEFLATE the balloon (if inflated), reposition/pull back per protocol, and NOTIFY THE PHYSICIAN.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Inflate the balloon further to confirm the wedge — Inflating the balloon further risks rupture.
• Ignore it since PAC waveforms fluctuate normally — Ignoring it risks infarction.
• Increase the IV fluid rate — Increasing IV fluids doesn't fix the catheter position.

⭐ Key point: Never leave the balloon inflated; limit wedge readings to brief periods.$x$, null)
),
upd as (
  update public.questions qq
     set exp = r.exp,
         ans_idx = coalesce((select (t.i - 1)::int from jsonb_array_elements_text(qq.opts) with ordinality t(v, i) where t.v = r.fixed_answer), qq.ans_idx)
    from r
   where qq.q = r.q
     and qq.opts ->> qq.ans_idx = r.answer
  returning r.n
)
insert into rationale_report select 5, count(distinct n), count(*) from upd
  on conflict (day) do update set questions_updated = excluded.questions_updated, rows_changed = excluded.rows_changed;

select day as "Day", questions_updated as "Questions updated (should be 200)", rows_changed as "Rows changed" from rationale_report order by day;
